 quality trilogy attributed to:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Juran understood that improving quality required different approaches and effort than simply maintaining quality. The Juran trilogy focused on three philosophies: quality planning, quality control, and quality improvement. [I.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Deming.","Crosby.","Juran.","Ishikawa."],"stem":"Quality planning, quality control, and quality improvement are parts of the quality trilogy attributed to:","sub":"mgmt","why":"Juran understood that improving quality required different approaches and effort than simply maintaining quality. The Juran trilogy focused on three philosophies: quality planning, quality control, and quality improvement. [I.A.1]"},"revision":"2918d2cc7aba7cf5300aa484a086858b84606a22df011bd6a2d2535327b56a68"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-10":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Planning, detection, analysis, and implementation.\",\"Planning, data collection, analysis, and implementation.\",\"Promotion, detection, analysis, and implementation.\",\"Promotion, data collection, analysis, and planning.\"],\"stem\":\"Benchmarking projects will include the following steps:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Typically, benchmarking projects must have a plan, data must be collected for analysis, and finally, there must be implementation of results of the analysis. [I.B.2.a]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Planning, detection, analysis, and implementation.","Planning, data collection, analysis, and implementation.","Promotion, detection, analysis, and implementation.","Promotion, data collection, analysis, and planning."],"stem":"Benchmarking projects will include the following steps:","sub":"mgmt","why":"Typically, benchmarking projects must have a plan, data must be collected for analysis, and finally, there must be implementation of results of the analysis. [I.B.2.a]"},"revision":"2e8ff17e381eaecc6a20ade41ef5313de960fca7100b6832810e72d5fd991e0a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-100":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Eliminate waste in a process\",\"Reduce the number of defects produced in a process\",\"Redesign a process to make large improvements in quality\",\"Make small, incremental changes to a process\"],\"stem\":\"What is the primary goal of kaizen improvement?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"The primary goal of kaizen is for employees to make continual, incremental changes to a process to achieve small improvements. Lean focuses on eliminating waste in a process, one of the goals of Six Sigma is to reduce defects, and reengineering involves redesigning a process to see large improvements in quality of a product. [V.C]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Eliminate waste in a process","Reduce the number of defects produced in a process","Redesign a process to make large improvements in quality","Make small, incremental changes to a process"],"stem":"What is the primary goal of kaizen improvement?","sub":"ci","why":"The primary goal of kaizen is for employees to make continual, incremental changes to a process to achieve small improvements. Lean focuses on eliminating waste in a process, one of the goals of Six Sigma is to reduce defects, and reengineering involves redesigning a process to see large improvements in quality of a product. [V.C]"},"revision":"d9bf57c359c27a2fc827bc7581aa0f6fe01acf315897fdd40c31237168e14806"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-101":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Eliminate waste in a process\",\"Reduce the number of defects produced in a process\",\"Redesign a process to make large improvements in quality\",\"Make small, incremental changes to a process\"],\"stem\":\"What is one of the primary goals of Six Sigma as a continuous improvement method?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"One of the primary goals of Six Sigma is to reduce the number of defects produced in a process. Lean focuses on eliminating waste in a process, reengineering involves redesigning a process to see large improvements, and kaizen is continuous, incremental improvements to a process. [V.C]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Eliminate waste in a process","Reduce the number of defects produced in a process","Redesign a process to make large improvements in quality","Make small, incremental changes to a process"],"stem":"What is one of the primary goals of Six Sigma as a continuous improvement method?","sub":"ci","why":"One of the primary goals of Six Sigma is to reduce the number of defects produced in a process. Lean focuses on eliminating waste in a process, reengineering involves redesigning a process to see large improvements, and kaizen is continuous, incremental improvements to a process. [V.C]"},"revision":"418dea4cf545c072fface715f66609fd41eb6495baf66c0aaa961d541280f1b5"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-102":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Incremental changes to a process can lead to great improvements.\",\"A system cannot improve by focusing improvements solely on individual processes.\",\"Team-based projects are more effective than individual-based projects.\",\"A data-driven improvement strategy is necessary for process improvement.\"],\"stem\":\"What is the primary strength of theory of constraints?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"TOC focuses on the weakest link in a chain of processes—therefore, it’s a systems approach to process improvement. Focusing on an individual process in a chain of many will not provide the best result. [V.C]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Incremental changes to a process can lead to great improvements.","A system cannot improve by focusing improvements solely on individual processes.","Team-based projects are more effective than individual-based projects.","A data-driven improvement strategy is necessary for process improvement."],"stem":"What is the primary strength of theory of constraints?","sub":"ci","why":"TOC focuses on the weakest link in a chain of processes—therefore, it’s a systems approach to process improvement. Focusing on an individual process in a chain of many will not provide the best result. [V.C]"},"revision":"ca05ab949d7256485f4badb354f4d84be3496aea245aa26f54a21f7a63f8368b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-103":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"±1.5σ\",\"±2σ\",\"±3σ\",\"±6σ\"],\"stem\":\"In Six Sigma, what value of sigma (σ), the standard deviation of a process, is used to account for typical shifts or drifts from the mean?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"The sigma quality level includes a ±1.5< value to account for typical drifts from the mean. [V.C]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["±1.5σ","±2σ","±3σ","±6σ"],"stem":"In Six Sigma, what value of sigma (σ), the standard deviation of a process, is used to account for typical shifts or drifts from the mean?","sub":"ci","why":"The sigma quality level includes a ±1.5< value to account for typical drifts from the mean. [V.C]"},"revision":"7e9f4e19854ed58c2e9b3b3cab3eea22d8b190f219478bb73f26a88f881ef921"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-104":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"PDCA\",\"TQM\",\"Reengineering\",\"Kaizen\"],\"stem\":\"A restaurant with a particularly large kitchen and lots of empty space had long delays preparing and serving patrons. After careful planning, the restaurant completely redesigned their kitchen and ordering procedures, and immediately saw a vast decrease in serving times. They also noticed an increase in customers after this change. Which of the following improvement methods does this scenario describe?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Reengineering a process involves a complete and radical redesign of a procedure. In this case, the restaurant has completely redesigned its kitchen and ordering procedure to achieve large improvements in its serving time and sales— an example of reengineering. [V.C]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["PDCA","TQM","Reengineering","Kaizen"],"stem":"A restaurant with a particularly large kitchen and lots of empty space had long delays preparing and serving patrons. After careful planning, the restaurant completely redesigned their kitchen and ordering procedures, and immediately saw a vast decrease in serving times. They also noticed an increase in customers after this change. Which of the following improvement methods does this scenario describe?","sub":"ci","why":"Reengineering a process involves a complete and radical redesign of a procedure. In this case, the restaurant has completely redesigned its kitchen and ordering procedure to achieve large improvements in its serving time and sales— an example of reengineering. [V.C]"},"revision":"ecdf1a1d5815cf3e3eaa9afd3303035cb3ce1fc07ef5aba45751e39eba55808a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-105":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"1.33\",\"1.5\",\"2.0\",\"2.5\"],\"stem\":\"What value of Cp is associated with a Six Sigma–quality level process?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"The capability index is Cp = (USL - LSL)/(6σ). A Six Sigma quality level places the specifications 12σ apart, so Cp = 12σ/(6σ) = 2.0. [V.C]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["1.33","1.5","2.0","2.5"],"stem":"What value of Cp is associated with a Six Sigma–quality level process?","sub":"ci","why":"The capability index is Cp = (USL - LSL)/(6σ). A Six Sigma quality level places the specifications 12σ apart, so Cp = 12σ/(6σ) = 2.0. [V.C]"},"revision":"f02c6f397e246fd67ce14919ea46bc3b3adcacff84106ff4907ac44770cf9727"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-106":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Patient weight\",\"Result of a pass/fail medical test\",\"Errors in a medical record\",\"Number of patient visits\"],\"stem\":\"A project is being conducted in a hospital clinic. Which of the following are considered continuous data?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Discrete data can only take on countable values. The result of the medical test is pass/fail. Errors in the medical record and the number of patient falls have possible values of 0, 1, 2, . . . . Patient weight can be an infinite number of values and is therefore continuous data. [VI.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Patient weight","Result of a pass/fail medical test","Errors in a medical record","Number of patient visits"],"stem":"A project is being conducted in a hospital clinic. Which of the following are considered continuous data?","sub":"quant","why":"Discrete data can only take on countable values. The result of the medical test is pass/fail. Errors in the medical record and the number of patient falls have possible values of 0, 1, 2, . . . . Patient weight can be an infinite number of values and is therefore continuous data. [VI.A.1]"},"revision":"a43567519c72b7cf07e7cff41a7906bfd146da2e08b344f179cb89a5436f4aab"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-107":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"The length of a component\",\"Fill volume of a container\",\"Number of nonconforming components\",\"Time required for testing\"],\"stem\":\"A project is being conducted in a manufacturing facility. Which of the following are considered discrete data?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Length, volume, and time can take on values over an interval of numbers. There are infinitely many values between any two values on their respective scales. Number of nonconforming components, however, can only take on a set of countable values, so it is discrete data. [VI.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["The length of a component","Fill volume of a container","Number of nonconforming components","Time required for testing"],"stem":"A project is being conducted in a manufacturing facility. Which of the following are considered discrete data?","sub":"quant","why":"Length, volume, and time can take on values over an interval of numbers. There are infinitely many values between any two values on their respective scales. Number of nonconforming components, however, can only take on a set of countable values, so it is discrete data. [VI.A.1]"},"revision":"9ece7afc1d7c27232c15a7d75fe422e1f56502164dd7a7102d29da629efc2c84"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-108":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Nominal\",\"Ordinal\",\"Interval\",\"Ratio\"],\"stem\":\"The color of a car is on which measurement scale?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The color of a car is classified into categories and there is no order implied. [VI.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Nominal","Ordinal","Interval","Ratio"],"stem":"The color of a car is on which measurement scale?","sub":"quant","why":"The color of a car is classified into categories and there is no order implied. [VI.A.2]"},"revision":"8b159db9fc1c737225bedf0c8220d0b47596d6ad723bec3722252079d7e46009"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-109":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Nominal\",\"Ordinal\",\"Interval\",\"Ratio\"],\"stem\":\"A person’s height in feet is on which measurement scale?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Height has meaningful differences and an absolute zero exists. [VI.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Nominal","Ordinal","Interval","Ratio"],"stem":"A person’s height in feet is on which measurement scale?","sub":"quant","why":"Height has meaningful differences and an absolute zero exists. [VI.A.2]"},"revision":"406aaf75173e84a863436ab564406b74e81e4a829b482e6c226c29d560f7b13b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-11":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"stockholders.\",\"executive group.\",\"suppliers.\",\"customers.\"],\"stem\":\"The owners of a company are the:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"The stockholders of a company are considered the owners of the company. Their role is of a financial nature, but generally passive. [I.B.2.b]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["stockholders.","executive group.","suppliers.","customers."],"stem":"The owners of a company are the:","sub":"mgmt","why":"The stockholders of a company are considered the owners of the company. Their role is of a financial nature, but generally passive. [I.B.2.b]"},"revision":"98a604a5026df2f1cb4858e1568bb4ce06a797bd92eb226ac9be12ba073637e8"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-110":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Nominal\",\"Ordinal\",\"Interval\",\"Ratio\"],\"stem\":\"The rating of a service from poor to excellent is on which measurement scale?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Rating of a service is classified into categories where order is important. The precise difference between values is not defined. [VI.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Nominal","Ordinal","Interval","Ratio"],"stem":"The rating of a service from poor to excellent is on which measurement scale?","sub":"quant","why":"Rating of a service is classified into categories where order is important. The precise difference between values is not defined. [VI.A.2]"},"revision":"2a3c770547df52a54ce501fa6e40e281c7af34478d4ac92b6917cf5575dce87f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-111":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"remove outliers.\",\"normalize data.\",\"prepare data to fit the parameters of another system.\",\"split or join tables.\"],\"stem\":\"The Data Transform phase’s main goal is to\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"An architecture, scope, and framework for the data collection program is first developed that identifies the data organization require for current and future business needs. That is followed by the use of the extract, transform, load (ETL) or extract, load, transform (ELT) process. The Data Transform phase’s main goal is preparing data to fit the parameters of another system. [VI.A.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["remove outliers.","normalize data.","prepare data to fit the parameters of another system.","split or join tables."],"stem":"The Data Transform phase’s main goal is to","sub":"quant","why":"An architecture, scope, and framework for the data collection program is first developed that identifies the data organization require for current and future business needs. That is followed by the use of the extract, transform, load (ETL) or extract, load, transform (ELT) process. The Data Transform phase’s main goal is preparing data to fit the parameters of another system. [VI.A.3]"},"revision":"83e1203d9e976b2cf0ab373a429720ad2e39047241ea9f91e0cb697bf309e5cc"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-112":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Regression\",\"Coding\",\"DOE\",\"Matrix plot\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following can simplify recording and analysis of data?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Coding data can simplify recording and analysis. Sometimes it is useful to code data using an algebraic transformation. Suppose a set of data has mean μ and standard deviation σ. A new data set may be created using the formula y = ax + b. Each element of the new set is formed by multiplying an element of the original set by a, then adding b. [VI.A.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Regression","Coding","DOE","Matrix plot"],"stem":"Which of the following can simplify recording and analysis of data?","sub":"quant","why":"Coding data can simplify recording and analysis. Sometimes it is useful to code data using an algebraic transformation. Suppose a set of data has mean μ and standard deviation σ. A new data set may be created using the formula y = ax + b. Each element of the new set is formed by multiplying an element of the original set by a, then adding b. [VI.A.3]"},"revision":"5b6e37572751c4324b4bdf498964a3e60db8d3236c2913153d6fdd8c022576a7"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-113":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"X– = 5.5 minutes, M = 6 minutes\",\"X– = 6 minutes, M = 5 minutes\",\"X– = 6 minutes, M = 5.5 minutes\",\"X– = 6 minutes, M = 6 minutes\"],\"stem\":\"The waiting times in minutes for eight customers at a bank are: 5, 8, 12, 3, 2, 7, 6, 5. What are the mean and median for this sample of waiting times?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The mean is (5 + 8 + 12 + 3 + 2 + 7 + 6 + 5)/8 = 6 minutes. Ordered data are 2, 3, 5, 5, 6, 7, 8, 12, so the median is (5 + 6)/2 = 5.5 minutes. [VI.A.6]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["X– = 5.5 minutes, M = 6 minutes","X– = 6 minutes, M = 5 minutes","X– = 6 minutes, M = 5.5 minutes","X– = 6 minutes, M = 6 minutes"],"stem":"The waiting times in minutes for eight customers at a bank are: 5, 8, 12, 3, 2, 7, 6, 5. What are the mean and median for this sample of waiting times?","sub":"quant","why":"The mean is (5 + 8 + 12 + 3 + 2 + 7 + 6 + 5)/8 = 6 minutes. Ordered data are 2, 3, 5, 5, 6, 7, 8, 12, so the median is (5 + 6)/2 = 5.5 minutes. [VI.A.6]"},"revision":"d38510d1cc9810eba201ef9ab3c2ccd360f31a1312b8cb3d089b85cdfb9dd5a6"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-114":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"s = 0.0098 inches\",\"s = 0.0989 inches\",\"s = 0.0114 inches\",\"s = 0.1069 inches\"],\"stem\":\"The lengths in inches for a sample of seven components are: 3.2, 3.1, 3.4, 3.1, 3.2, 3.3, 3.2. What is the standard deviation for this sample?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The sample mean is x̄ = 22.5/7 = 3.2143. Using s = √[Σ(xi-x̄)²/(n-1)] gives s = 0.1069 inches. [VI.A.6]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["s = 0.0098 inches","s = 0.0989 inches","s = 0.0114 inches","s = 0.1069 inches"],"stem":"The lengths in inches for a sample of seven components are: 3.2, 3.1, 3.4, 3.1, 3.2, 3.3, 3.2. What is the standard deviation for this sample?","sub":"quant","why":"The sample mean is x̄ = 22.5/7 = 3.2143. Using s = √[Σ(xi-x̄)²/(n-1)] gives s = 0.1069 inches. [VI.A.6]"},"revision":"64240b4db01b6e3825ca8b084f1a506e135fb70b9338ce0b5d43975ce3cbedeb"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-115":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"The distribution is symmetric.\",\"The distribution is right-skewed.\",\"The distribution is left-skewed.\",\"The distribution has high kurtosis.\"],\"stem\":\"The median of a population is much larger than the population mean. What can you say about the shape of the population distribution?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The population mean and median are not equal, so the distribution cannot be symmetric. The mean is sensitive to the data, while the median is a robust statistic and is not heavily influenced by extreme observations. Therefore, a much smaller mean indicates that the data are skewed to the left. [VI.A.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["The distribution is symmetric.","The distribution is right-skewed.","The distribution is left-skewed.","The distribution has high kurtosis."],"stem":"The median of a population is much larger than the population mean. What can you say about the shape of the population distribution?","sub":"quant","why":"The population mean and median are not equal, so the distribution cannot be symmetric. The mean is sensitive to the data, while the median is a robust statistic and is not heavily influenced by extreme observations. Therefore, a much smaller mean indicates that the data are skewed to the left. [VI.A.5]"},"revision":"e028f48ec7c1d626edf2becc3f69ae7c8390f8da9d5a83af5c3c26d6bfe02791"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-116":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Binomial\",\"Normal\",\"Uniform\",\"Poisson\"],\"stem\":\"A population has an exponential distribution. One hundred samples of size 40 are randomly collected and the 100 sample means are calculated. What is the approximate distribution of these sample averages?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Under the central limit theorem, the distribution of sample averages tends toward a normal distribution as the sample size increases. Although the exponential distribution is right skewed, the distribution of sample averages is approximately normally distributed for large sample sizes. In this case n = 40. [VI.A.6]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Binomial","Normal","Uniform","Poisson"],"stem":"A population has an exponential distribution. One hundred samples of size 40 are randomly collected and the 100 sample means are calculated. What is the approximate distribution of these sample averages?","sub":"quant","why":"Under the central limit theorem, the distribution of sample averages tends toward a normal distribution as the sample size increases. Although the exponential distribution is right skewed, the distribution of sample averages is approximately normally distributed for large sample sizes. In this case n = 40. [VI.A.6]"},"revision":"e7511f7b9b57d6830e03698dbaaaac6f753c5d2ca4f391bdb6da61937e5063fa"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-117":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"σ² = (1/N) Σi=1N(xi - μ)\",\"s² = [Σi=1n(xi - x̄)]/(n - 1)\",\"σ² = (1/N) Σi=1N(xi - μ)²\",\"σ² = [1/(N - 1)] Σi=1N(xi - μ)²\"],\"stem\":\"What is the formula for the population variance?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Population variance is the average squared deviation from the population mean: σ² = (1/N)Σi=1N(xi-μ)². The denominator N distinguishes it from the usual sample-variance formula. [VI.A.6]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["σ² = (1/N) Σi=1N(xi - μ)","s² = [Σi=1n(xi - x̄)]/(n - 1)","σ² = (1/N) Σi=1N(xi - μ)²","σ² = [1/(N - 1)] Σi=1N(xi - μ)²"],"stem":"What is the formula for the population variance?","sub":"quant","why":"Population variance is the average squared deviation from the population mean: σ² = (1/N)Σi=1N(xi-μ)². The denominator N distinguishes it from the usual sample-variance formula. [VI.A.6]"},"revision":"dd1df598aad126e6bf07514f05c31744931d2ad81726d9aee336f4aa5747f102"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-118":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Histograms\",\"Box plots\",\"Scatter plots\",\"Probability plots\"],\"stem\":\"A team would like to compare waiting times of customers by day of the week. Which of the following would be the most appropriate tool to use?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Side-by-side box plots can be used to easily compare any differences between the wait times by day of the week. Histograms are more suited for describing the distribution of one population. Scatter plots are used to examine the relationship between two continuous variables. Probability plots are used to determine whether data follow a certain distribution, for example, the normal distribution. [VI.A.7]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Histograms","Box plots","Scatter plots","Probability plots"],"stem":"A team would like to compare waiting times of customers by day of the week. Which of the following would be the most appropriate tool to use?","sub":"quant","why":"Side-by-side box plots can be used to easily compare any differences between the wait times by day of the week. Histograms are more suited for describing the distribution of one population. Scatter plots are used to examine the relationship between two continuous variables. Probability plots are used to determine whether data follow a certain distribution, for example, the normal distribution. [VI.A.7]"},"revision":"4d22b8c9eb6057fbe1601659ab95bf15c92e21e291a9ca823fa91e1cfffd082e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-119":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Population\",\"Sample\",\"Parameter\",\"Statistic\"],\"stem\":\"The temperature of coffee is an important characteristic in a restaurant. The temperature of fifteen randomly selected cups of coffee is measured and the average temperature is found to be 168°F. Which term best describes this average temperature?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The average temperature of fifteen randomly selected cups of coffee is a statistic. A statistic is a characteristic of a sample and is used to estimate the population parameter. In this case, the sample mean temperature of the fifteen cups of coffee can be used to estimate the temperature of all coffee served in the restaurant. [VI.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Population","Sample","Parameter","Statistic"],"stem":"The temperature of coffee is an important characteristic in a restaurant. The temperature of fifteen randomly selected cups of coffee is measured and the average temperature is found to be 168°F. Which term best describes this average temperature?","sub":"quant","why":"The average temperature of fifteen randomly selected cups of coffee is a statistic. A statistic is a characteristic of a sample and is used to estimate the population parameter. In this case, the sample mean temperature of the fifteen cups of coffee can be used to estimate the temperature of all coffee served in the restaurant. [VI.B.1]"},"revision":"d4142a0589fafab24558e50c18f010d0f7a0938edae9e87267cf685ecd82bb24"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-12":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Statistical process control\",\"Deming’s 14 points\",\"Balanced scorecard\",\"RACI\"],\"stem\":\"Business processes, customers, and financial fundamentals are all perspectives of what philosophy?\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Business processes, customers, and financial fundamentals are part of the balanced scorecard, with the other category being learning and growth. [I.B.2.b]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Statistical process control","Deming’s 14 points","Balanced scorecard","RACI"],"stem":"Business processes, customers, and financial fundamentals are all perspectives of what philosophy?","sub":"mgmt","why":"Business processes, customers, and financial fundamentals are part of the balanced scorecard, with the other category being learning and growth. [I.B.2.b]"},"revision":"7cb923fa988ceb0195dddfb2a3d31a42ca928b576d78121f3b552a4b65c564d1"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-120":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"sample.\",\"statistic.\",\"parameter.\",\"probability.\"],\"stem\":\"A characteristic of a population is called a:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A parameter is a characteristic of a population. It is a quantity that describes characteristics of a population. The value of a population parameter is frequently unknown and must be estimated by a sample statistic. [VI.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["sample.","statistic.","parameter.","probability."],"stem":"A characteristic of a population is called a:","sub":"quant","why":"A parameter is a characteristic of a population. It is a quantity that describes characteristics of a population. The value of a population parameter is frequently unknown and must be estimated by a sample statistic. [VI.B.1]"},"revision":"7f7c48dc8308b24f129558415d4f110b8651c6f0d7a0f8d8166f4b4bfc9f6693"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-121":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.02\",\"0.88\",\"0.98\",\"1.08\"],\"stem\":\"The probability of a part being defective is 0.02. What is the probability it is not defective?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Use the complement rule: P(not defective) = 1 - P(defective) = 1 - 0.02 = 0.98. [VI.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.02","0.88","0.98","1.08"],"stem":"The probability of a part being defective is 0.02. What is the probability it is not defective?","sub":"quant","why":"Use the complement rule: P(not defective) = 1 - P(defective) = 1 - 0.02 = 0.98. [VI.B.3]"},"revision":"eacc726c53a228987ea0104169bc8a160cef215ec079b3f939095d3fc03f743d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-122":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"0.0216\",\"0.69\",\"0.75\",\"Not enough information is provided.\"],\"stem\":\"The probability that event A occurs is 0.72. The probability that event B occurs is 0.03. What is the probability that events A and B both occur?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"There is not enough information provided to determine the probability that both events A and B occur. If it were stated that A and B were independent, then the multiplication rule would apply: P(A ∩ B) = P(A) × P(B). [VI.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["0.0216","0.69","0.75","Not enough information is provided."],"stem":"The probability that event A occurs is 0.72. The probability that event B occurs is 0.03. What is the probability that events A and B both occur?","sub":"quant","why":"There is not enough information provided to determine the probability that both events A and B occur. If it were stated that A and B were independent, then the multiplication rule would apply: P(A ∩ B) = P(A) × P(B). [VI.B.3]"},"revision":"2b7ad0a298a9179132818a2a38b5f7ed9daee14d91abd95e3d4953f21ce1f14c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-123":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"0\",\"0.052\",\"0.53\",\"1.27\"],\"stem\":\"If the probability that event A occurs is 0.40, the probability that event B occurs is 0.13, and A and B are mutually exclusive events, what is the probability that events A and B both occur?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Since events A and B are mutually exclusive, they cannot occur simultaneously. Therefore, P(A ∩ B) = 0. Note that P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) = 0.40 + 0.13 = 0.53, since A and B are mutually exclusive. Recall that probabilities only take on values between 0 and 1. [VI.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["0","0.052","0.53","1.27"],"stem":"If the probability that event A occurs is 0.40, the probability that event B occurs is 0.13, and A and B are mutually exclusive events, what is the probability that events A and B both occur?","sub":"quant","why":"Since events A and B are mutually exclusive, they cannot occur simultaneously. Therefore, P(A ∩ B) = 0. Note that P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) = 0.40 + 0.13 = 0.53, since A and B are mutually exclusive. Recall that probabilities only take on values between 0 and 1. [VI.B.3]"},"revision":"2c7be81a4e9fa92173c4b3e4dd5c2b84404b0f584ce3fe11810a8e70e8273113"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-124":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0\",\"0.4512\",\"0.5488\",\"0.9999\"],\"stem\":\"The time between arrivals at a store has an exponential distribution with mean five minutes. What is the probability that the time between arrivals is less than three minutes?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The exponential rate is λ = 1/5 = 0.2 per minute. Therefore, P(T < 3) = 1 - e-0.2(3) = 1 - e-0.6 = 0.4512. [VI.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0","0.4512","0.5488","0.9999"],"stem":"The time between arrivals at a store has an exponential distribution with mean five minutes. What is the probability that the time between arrivals is less than three minutes?","sub":"quant","why":"The exponential rate is λ = 1/5 = 0.2 per minute. Therefore, P(T < 3) = 1 - e-0.2(3) = 1 - e-0.6 = 0.4512. [VI.C.1]"},"revision":"d247521632c4e9f6ee544dc6ac6587c43ce64a830aa388ccd198fc0945b36f19"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-125":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"0.0668\",\"0.1446\",\"0.8554\",\"0.9332\"],\"stem\":\"X has a normal distribution with mean 15 and standard deviation 2. What is the probability that X is greater than 18?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Standardize the value: z = (18 - 15)/2 = 1.50. Thus P(X > 18) = P(Z > 1.50) = 1 - 0.9332 = 0.0668. [VI.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["0.0668","0.1446","0.8554","0.9332"],"stem":"X has a normal distribution with mean 15 and standard deviation 2. What is the probability that X is greater than 18?","sub":"quant","why":"Standardize the value: z = (18 - 15)/2 = 1.50. Thus P(X > 18) = P(Z > 1.50) = 1 - 0.9332 = 0.0668. [VI.C.1]"},"revision":"2ab0af06553e921acaa5d62680bc7754cd5ea9c9d9c8c5fd423e8e62b83a0d68"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-126":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Normal\",\"t\",\"Uniform\",\"Weibull\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following probability distributions is asymmetric?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The normal distribution, t-distribution, and uniform distribution are all symmetric probability distributions. Asymmetric distributions include the exponential distribution, Weibull distribution, chi-square distribution, and lognormal distribution. [VI.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Normal","t","Uniform","Weibull"],"stem":"Which of the following probability distributions is asymmetric?","sub":"quant","why":"The normal distribution, t-distribution, and uniform distribution are all symmetric probability distributions. Asymmetric distributions include the exponential distribution, Weibull distribution, chi-square distribution, and lognormal distribution. [VI.C.1]"},"revision":"b4c5734872024ddd461c54801506d04f439954bec12be454b03eee986bb9aa9d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-127":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.00016\",\"0.00256\",\"0.99488\",\"0.99744\"],\"stem\":\"A bottling company’s filling process has a normal distribution with mean 24.01 oz. and standard deviation 0.025 oz. If the specifications for the process are 24 ± .08 oz, what proportion of bottles is overfilled?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The upper specification limit is 24.08 oz. Standardizing gives z = (24.08 - 24.01)/0.025 = 2.80. Therefore, P(X > 24.08) = P(Z > 2.80) = 0.00256. [VI.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.00016","0.00256","0.99488","0.99744"],"stem":"A bottling company’s filling process has a normal distribution with mean 24.01 oz. and standard deviation 0.025 oz. If the specifications for the process are 24 ± .08 oz, what proportion of bottles is overfilled?","sub":"quant","why":"The upper specification limit is 24.08 oz. Standardizing gives z = (24.08 - 24.01)/0.025 = 2.80. Therefore, P(X > 24.08) = P(Z > 2.80) = 0.00256. [VI.C.1]"},"revision":"d060510db180ba1ee6de999c8ab0de442feb2b0cad6463a4ddae54bbd06ea3ec"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-128":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"has a larger mean.\",\"is more skewed to the right.\",\"is bimodal.\",\"has heavier tails.\"],\"stem\":\"Compared to the standard normal distribution, the student’s t-distribution:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The t-distribution is symmetric, bell-shaped, and has mean µ = 0, like the standard normal distribution. However, the tails of the t-distribution are longer or heavier compared to the standard normal distribution. [VI.C.1] . [VI.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["has a larger mean.","is more skewed to the right.","is bimodal.","has heavier tails."],"stem":"Compared to the standard normal distribution, the student’s t-distribution:","sub":"quant","why":"The t-distribution is symmetric, bell-shaped, and has mean µ = 0, like the standard normal distribution. However, the tails of the t-distribution are longer or heavier compared to the standard normal distribution. [VI.C.1] . [VI.C.1]"},"revision":"61cce487d5bf4466e798465b0e341c90d294492214df689742c27ca0ca1b20b9"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-129":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"0.0404\",\"0.2627\",\"0.8000\",\"0.9596\"],\"stem\":\"The number of nonconformities per inspection unit in a factory occurs at a rate of five per day. Using the most appropriate probability distribution, what is the probability there will be at least two nonconforming units in one day?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With X ~ Poisson(5), P(X ≥ 2) = 1 - P(X=0) - P(X=1) = 1 - e-5(1 + 5) = 0.9596. [VI.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["0.0404","0.2627","0.8000","0.9596"],"stem":"The number of nonconformities per inspection unit in a factory occurs at a rate of five per day. Using the most appropriate probability distribution, what is the probability there will be at least two nonconforming units in one day?","sub":"quant","why":"With X ~ Poisson(5), P(X ≥ 2) = 1 - P(X=0) - P(X=1) = 1 - e-5(1 + 5) = 0.9596. [VI.C.2]"},"revision":"13f9781fe670d93631421576ba39f3ab762903df1bbf931cfd0a5b6db1a0d9a4"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-13":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"should be achievable.\",\"do not need to be measurable.\",\"do not need to be linked to strategic objectives.\",\"should focus on as many measures as possible.\"],\"stem\":\"Performance measures:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"All performance measures should be achievable and aligned with strategic goals. In addition, the focus should be on the “vital few.” One should avoid using too many measures or metrics. [I.B.2.c]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["should be achievable.","do not need to be measurable.","do not need to be linked to strategic objectives.","should focus on as many measures as possible."],"stem":"Performance measures:","sub":"mgmt","why":"All performance measures should be achievable and aligned with strategic goals. In addition, the focus should be on the “vital few.” One should avoid using too many measures or metrics. [I.B.2.c]"},"revision":"a9e19e5cabfce585c2f8ab4a3e46cf7b7447ffca0e36eb5c776ac210f0e65079"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-130":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Hypergeometric\",\"Binomial\",\"Poisson\",\"Normal\"],\"stem\":\"A sample of ten products is selected from a small, isolated lot without replacement. The lot is known to contain 5% defective products. Which distribution is most appropriate to determine the probability of selecting at least one defective item in the sample?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The hypergeometric distribution is appropriate when the sample is taken without replacement from a finite population. The binomial distribution is appropriate when sampling is done with replacement. The normal distribution is not appropriate, since it is a continuous probability distribution. [VI.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Hypergeometric","Binomial","Poisson","Normal"],"stem":"A sample of ten products is selected from a small, isolated lot without replacement. The lot is known to contain 5% defective products. Which distribution is most appropriate to determine the probability of selecting at least one defective item in the sample?","sub":"quant","why":"The hypergeometric distribution is appropriate when the sample is taken without replacement from a finite population. The binomial distribution is appropriate when sampling is done with replacement. The normal distribution is not appropriate, since it is a continuous probability distribution. [VI.C.2]"},"revision":"29c898fd8e9ac875ae10bdcea98e0fcf031965e225e3e72205e956b141abfbe7"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-131":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.0127\",\"0.0428\",\"0.9873\",\"1.0421\"],\"stem\":\"Fifteen invoices are randomly selected from a batch at a company. It is believed that 10% of all invoices have an error. What is the probability that exactly four out of the fifteen invoices have an error?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"This is binomial with n = 15 and p = 0.10. P(X=4) = C(15,4)(0.10)⁴(0.90)¹¹ = 0.0428. [VI.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.0127","0.0428","0.9873","1.0421"],"stem":"Fifteen invoices are randomly selected from a batch at a company. It is believed that 10% of all invoices have an error. What is the probability that exactly four out of the fifteen invoices have an error?","sub":"quant","why":"This is binomial with n = 15 and p = 0.10. P(X=4) = C(15,4)(0.10)⁴(0.90)¹¹ = 0.0428. [VI.C.2]"},"revision":"21cefb48c20ba04b9490efaef8acf26fb38cfdf2f302cb24e7aa3844f554c60d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-132":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"24/32\",\"24/√32\",\"24/5.5\",\"24\"],\"stem\":\"A population has a distribution with mean 24 and standard deviation 5.5. According to the central limit theorem, what is the mean of the distribution of the sample means for random samples of size 32?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The central limit theorem states that if the sample size n is sufficiently large, then the sample mean X– follows approximately a normal distribution with mean µX– = µ and standard deviation σX– =σ/ .n In this case, µ = 24, σ = 5.5, and n = 32. Therefore, µX– = µ = 24. [VI.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["24/32","24/√32","24/5.5","24"],"stem":"A population has a distribution with mean 24 and standard deviation 5.5. According to the central limit theorem, what is the mean of the distribution of the sample means for random samples of size 32?","sub":"quant","why":"The central limit theorem states that if the sample size n is sufficiently large, then the sample mean X– follows approximately a normal distribution with mean µX– = µ and standard deviation σX– =σ/ .n In this case, µ = 24, σ = 5.5, and n = 32. Therefore, µX– = µ = 24. [VI.C.2]"},"revision":"03541bb4a5414fc96d416e32de3570c0616fd682c70d341894f64be77172988c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-133":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0\",\"0.0007\",\"0.2119\",\"9993\"],\"stem\":\"A bottling company’s filling process has a normal distribution with mean 24 oz. and standard deviation 0.05 oz. What is the probability that a sample of sixteen randomly selected bottles will have an average volume more than 24.04 oz?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"For samples of 16, SE(x̄) = σ/√n = 0.05/4 = 0.0125. Then z = (24.04 - 24)/0.0125 = 3.20, so P(x̄ > 24.04) = 0.0007. [VI.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0","0.0007","0.2119","9993"],"stem":"A bottling company’s filling process has a normal distribution with mean 24 oz. and standard deviation 0.05 oz. What is the probability that a sample of sixteen randomly selected bottles will have an average volume more than 24.04 oz?","sub":"quant","why":"For samples of 16, SE(x̄) = σ/√n = 0.05/4 = 0.0125. Then z = (24.04 - 24)/0.0125 = 3.20, so P(x̄ > 24.04) = 0.0007. [VI.C.2]"},"revision":"471c7e0ebd6986a4cf4e4f4f37647bcea8aff86cdad93d540d514231d8294800"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-134":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"2\",\"4\",\"8\",\"16\"],\"stem\":\"A sample of size 16 is drawn from a population that follows a normal distribution with mean 58 and variance 64. What is the standard error of the sample mean?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The population standard deviation is σ = √64 = 8. The standard error is σ/√n = 8/√16 = 2. [VI.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["2","4","8","16"],"stem":"A sample of size 16 is drawn from a population that follows a normal distribution with mean 58 and variance 64. What is the standard error of the sample mean?","sub":"quant","why":"The population standard deviation is σ = √64 = 8. The standard error is σ/√n = 8/√16 = 2. [VI.D.1]"},"revision":"bdaea1212644a05bdd80957747d9cc4efb3ec4bf5f951ef7282c5b2194143f97"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-135":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"There is a 5% chance that the population proportion p is between 0.02 and 0.12.\",\"The probability that the confidence interval contains the population proportion p is 0.5.\",\"The error rate is unacceptable.\",\"There is 95% confidence that the confidence interval will contain the population proportion p.\"],\"stem\":\"The 95% confidence interval for the proportion of filing errors in a business is (0.02, 0.12). What conclusions can be drawn from this confidence interval?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Recall that the true value of the population proportion is unknown. Answer (d) describes the practical interpretation of the confidence interval: with 95% confidence, the population proportion will lie between 0.02 and 0.12. [VI.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["There is a 5% chance that the population proportion p is between 0.02 and 0.12.","The probability that the confidence interval contains the population proportion p is 0.5.","The error rate is unacceptable.","There is 95% confidence that the confidence interval will contain the population proportion p."],"stem":"The 95% confidence interval for the proportion of filing errors in a business is (0.02, 0.12). What conclusions can be drawn from this confidence interval?","sub":"quant","why":"Recall that the true value of the population proportion is unknown. Answer (d) describes the practical interpretation of the confidence interval: with 95% confidence, the population proportion will lie between 0.02 and 0.12. [VI.D.1]"},"revision":"b1724bf319c4454f200e57453f20bb0ca51253e251b5a45dbdd51fb65fce917f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-136":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Point estimate\",\"Parameter\",\"Probability\",\"Standard error\"],\"stem\":\"A team would like to estimate the average length of a component. A random sample of 50 components had a sample mean of 11.75 mm. Which of the following terms is used to describe this value?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"11.73 mm is a point estimate for the average length of the component. Recall that a parameter is a characteristic of a population. The sample mean is often a point estimator for the population mean. [VI.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Point estimate","Parameter","Probability","Standard error"],"stem":"A team would like to estimate the average length of a component. A random sample of 50 components had a sample mean of 11.75 mm. Which of the following terms is used to describe this value?","sub":"quant","why":"11.73 mm is a point estimate for the average length of the component. Recall that a parameter is a characteristic of a population. The sample mean is often a point estimator for the population mean. [VI.D.1]"},"revision":"8036436c6967035d7ca7269689163f1c1b133e5c65104ac931cae791aa3f7ef4"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-137":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"(2.782, 5.818)\",\"(3.908, 4.692)\",\"(3.961, 4.639)\",\"(4.018, 4.582)\"],\"stem\":\"The tensile strength of fifteen units of cement was tested. The sample mean tensile strength was 4.3 MPa and sample standard deviation was 0.51 MPa. Find a 99% confidence interval for the true mean tensile strength of the cement.\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Because σ is unknown and n = 15, use a t interval with 14 degrees of freedom. With t0.005,14 = 2.977, the interval is 4.3 ± 2.977(0.51/√15) = (3.908, 4.692) MPa. [VI.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["(2.782, 5.818)","(3.908, 4.692)","(3.961, 4.639)","(4.018, 4.582)"],"stem":"The tensile strength of fifteen units of cement was tested. The sample mean tensile strength was 4.3 MPa and sample standard deviation was 0.51 MPa. Find a 99% confidence interval for the true mean tensile strength of the cement.","sub":"quant","why":"Because σ is unknown and n = 15, use a t interval with 14 degrees of freedom. With t0.005,14 = 2.977, the interval is 4.3 ± 2.977(0.51/√15) = (3.908, 4.692) MPa. [VI.D.1]"},"revision":"de289e2cdbf36f71a9d980b160d7a735de5b935ad90e7c225789113874cf9061"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-138":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"6\",\"47\",\"42\",\"43\"],\"stem\":\"A team would like to estimate the true average tensile strength of cement. They would like to obtain an estimate within 0.15 MPa of the true average tensile strength with 95% confidence. Based on prior information, it is assumed that σ = 0.50 MPa. What sample size is required to meet these requirements?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"For margin of error E = 0.15 with 95% confidence, n = (z0.025σ/E)² = (1.96 × 0.50/0.15)² = 42.68. Round up to 43. [VI.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["6","47","42","43"],"stem":"A team would like to estimate the true average tensile strength of cement. They would like to obtain an estimate within 0.15 MPa of the true average tensile strength with 95% confidence. Based on prior information, it is assumed that σ = 0.50 MPa. What sample size is required to meet these requirements?","sub":"quant","why":"For margin of error E = 0.15 with 95% confidence, n = (z0.025σ/E)² = (1.96 × 0.50/0.15)² = 42.68. Round up to 43. [VI.D.1]"},"revision":"f3cb996295264b4ea8203f9cd5fce58451aeb66467b7f4ec1e7a78f9b3dd7948"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-139":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"(0.324, 3.788)\",\"(0.378, 4.419)\",\"(0.570, 1.946)\",\"(9.745, 11.380)\"],\"stem\":\"The voltage of a power supply is of interest to a team. Voltage is assumed to be normally distributed. The voltages of seven randomly selected observations are: 10.37, 11.50, 9.80, 10.65, 11.95, 10.15, 9.52. Find the 95% confidence interval on the population variance of voltage.\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"For normally distributed data, the 95% confidence interval for the population variance is ((n-1)s²/χ²₀.₉₇₅, (n-1)s²/χ²₀.₀₂₅). With n = 7 and s² = 0.781, the interval is approximately (0.324, 3.788). [VI.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["(0.324, 3.788)","(0.378, 4.419)","(0.570, 1.946)","(9.745, 11.380)"],"stem":"The voltage of a power supply is of interest to a team. Voltage is assumed to be normally distributed. The voltages of seven randomly selected observations are: 10.37, 11.50, 9.80, 10.65, 11.95, 10.15, 9.52. Find the 95% confidence interval on the population variance of voltage.","sub":"quant","why":"For normally distributed data, the 95% confidence interval for the population variance is ((n-1)s²/χ²₀.₉₇₅, (n-1)s²/χ²₀.₀₂₅). With n = 7 and s² = 0.781, the interval is approximately (0.324, 3.788). [VI.D.1]"},"revision":"a0424757db0a342f303d354d6e414f55da0c951ab0f2d996091bb577bc5fd2eb"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-14":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"things that cost more have more benefit.\",\"investing for the longer term is the best strategy.\",\"short-term pain can increase long term gain.\",\"investments can be compared based on financial analysis.\"],\"stem\":\"The premise of cost-benefit analysis is that:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Cost-benefit analysis deals with comparing potential investments with each other to make the wisest decisions. [I.B.2.c]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["things that cost more have more benefit.","investing for the longer term is the best strategy.","short-term pain can increase long term gain.","investments can be compared based on financial analysis."],"stem":"The premise of cost-benefit analysis is that:","sub":"mgmt","why":"Cost-benefit analysis deals with comparing potential investments with each other to make the wisest decisions. [I.B.2.c]"},"revision":"f7c15ed03d23eb275a074a3218265d4b2c677683485953f4c1c87c57ce432677"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-140":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"effect of deviations of specifications on reliability objectives.\",\"effect of uncertainty on quality objectives.\",\"coordinated approach for centralized management of risk.\",\"coordination and control of all risky activities of a system.\"],\"stem\":\"Quality risk management is defined as the:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"Quality risk management is the effect of uncertainty on quality objectives. Option c is the definition of enterprise risk management. Option d is part of the definition of operational risk management. [VII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["effect of deviations of specifications on reliability objectives.","effect of uncertainty on quality objectives.","coordinated approach for centralized management of risk.","coordination and control of all risky activities of a system."],"stem":"Quality risk management is defined as the:","sub":"risk","why":"Quality risk management is the effect of uncertainty on quality objectives. Option c is the definition of enterprise risk management. Option d is part of the definition of operational risk management. [VII.A.1]"},"revision":"b688d1ecbcbb45d07b4a8ca9db1691d26aa73c401bb836ae306537a1d44c24ab"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-141":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Risk culture\",\"Risk-based thinking\",\"Risk register\",\"Failure mode and effects analysis (FMEA)\"],\"stem\":\"According to ISO 9001:2015, what enables an organization to determine the factors that could cause its processes and its quality management system to deviate from planned results, to put in place preventive controls to minimize negative effects, and to make maximum use of opportunities as they arise?\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"These are elements of risk-based thinking per ISO 9001:2015, clause 0.1. Risk culture is the way of thinking informed by a set of organizational values that determine behavior in face of uncertainty; specifically, the decision making regarding routine and nonroutine risk confrontation and associated risk identification, analysis, evaluation, monitoring, modification and communication. A risk register is a log of assessed and identified risks. FMEA is a form of risk register. [VII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Risk culture","Risk-based thinking","Risk register","Failure mode and effects analysis (FMEA)"],"stem":"According to ISO 9001:2015, what enables an organization to determine the factors that could cause its processes and its quality management system to deviate from planned results, to put in place preventive controls to minimize negative effects, and to make maximum use of opportunities as they arise?","sub":"risk","why":"These are elements of risk-based thinking per ISO 9001:2015, clause 0.1. Risk culture is the way of thinking informed by a set of organizational values that determine behavior in face of uncertainty; specifically, the decision making regarding routine and nonroutine risk confrontation and associated risk identification, analysis, evaluation, monitoring, modification and communication. A risk register is a log of assessed and identified risks. FMEA is a form of risk register. [VII.A.1]"},"revision":"11e40e35c5d18b0e270c1bcbda964f7654efedf0e52cb7640729aa2122891d46"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-142":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"fault tree analysis (FTA).\",\"hazard analysis.\",\"risk-based thinking.\",\"vendor risk oversight.\"],\"stem\":\"Carrying out preventive action to eliminate potential nonconformities, analyzing any nonconformities that do occur, and taking action to prevent recurrence that is appropriate for the effects of the nonconformity are examples of:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"Per ISO 9001: 2015, clause 0.3.3, risk-based thinking embodies preventive action. Fault tree analysis is a tool to identify failures or faults that can lead to a top- level failure event. Hazard analysis is a process to analyze the hazards and their consequences related to product or process under study. Vendor risk oversight involves the vendor or supplier, which is not mentioned here. [VII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["fault tree analysis (FTA).","hazard analysis.","risk-based thinking.","vendor risk oversight."],"stem":"Carrying out preventive action to eliminate potential nonconformities, analyzing any nonconformities that do occur, and taking action to prevent recurrence that is appropriate for the effects of the nonconformity are examples of:","sub":"risk","why":"Per ISO 9001: 2015, clause 0.3.3, risk-based thinking embodies preventive action. Fault tree analysis is a tool to identify failures or faults that can lead to a top- level failure event. Hazard analysis is a process to analyze the hazards and their consequences related to product or process under study. Vendor risk oversight involves the vendor or supplier, which is not mentioned here. [VII.A.1]"},"revision":"d6aa5d322222a0da139ae71cd9f8dbf0f9c7c2b2053fde8ef663b3ee2bcfe74f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-143":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Hazard\",\"Harm\",\"Risk\",\"Impact\"],\"stem\":\"A baby is playing near a glass bottle of pesticide. In this situation, what is the bottle of pesticide?\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"A hazard is a potential source of harm or a potential condition that, through a sequence of events, can cause a consequence. A harm is a physical or physiological damage, which here has not been realized yet. Risk here would include scenarios (and their severity and probability) such as the child toppling the bottle or contacting the pesticide. Consequence is the measure of impact – none of which have occurred yet. To classify negative impacts, the term severity is commonly used. [VII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Hazard","Harm","Risk","Impact"],"stem":"A baby is playing near a glass bottle of pesticide. In this situation, what is the bottle of pesticide?","sub":"risk","why":"A hazard is a potential source of harm or a potential condition that, through a sequence of events, can cause a consequence. A harm is a physical or physiological damage, which here has not been realized yet. Risk here would include scenarios (and their severity and probability) such as the child toppling the bottle or contacting the pesticide. Consequence is the measure of impact – none of which have occurred yet. To classify negative impacts, the term severity is commonly used. [VII.A.1]"},"revision":"c8b66e395a899bbb6b77950c0e17968d80e9504261ebbf2e86f1ae8620e59a52"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-144":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"should include only risks that it has control over.\",\"should include only those risks that are obvious and currently known.\",\"should include all possible risks, using many risk identification tools.\",\"should only come up with a list of no more than three to five risks or hazards.\"],\"stem\":\"Risk identification is the process of identifying hazards and sources of risks. When an organization is determining possible risks it:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"The purpose of risk identification is to identify all possible risks whether current, possible future risks, risks that are not currently under the organization’s control, or risks that may occur due to results of an accumulation of factors or steps in the process. [VII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["should include only risks that it has control over.","should include only those risks that are obvious and currently known.","should include all possible risks, using many risk identification tools.","should only come up with a list of no more than three to five risks or hazards."],"stem":"Risk identification is the process of identifying hazards and sources of risks. When an organization is determining possible risks it:","sub":"risk","why":"The purpose of risk identification is to identify all possible risks whether current, possible future risks, risks that are not currently under the organization’s control, or risks that may occur due to results of an accumulation of factors or steps in the process. [VII.A.1]"},"revision":"a0a37eb6dede1380c20416c64c67f9b16a179f75e9bc9baaf5408af6be04fbe1"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-145":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"risk management planning.\",\"risk identification phase.\",\"risk prioritization phase.\",\"risk assessment.\"],\"stem\":\"The key stakeholders, strategy, and program objectives, as well as development of roles and responsibilities, are typically identified and agreed on in the:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"The planning phase is important for setting all ground rules and objectives, and identifying key stakeholders. It may also include selecting key documents and templates for risk analysis. [VII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["risk management planning.","risk identification phase.","risk prioritization phase.","risk assessment."],"stem":"The key stakeholders, strategy, and program objectives, as well as development of roles and responsibilities, are typically identified and agreed on in the:","sub":"risk","why":"The planning phase is important for setting all ground rules and objectives, and identifying key stakeholders. It may also include selecting key documents and templates for risk analysis. [VII.A.1]"},"revision":"13c8d5c3c58e9202014dce94d2819c122f24941a2c873fdff16225787b077e5d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-146":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"make decisions based on risk level compared to stated risk criteria.\",\"determine options for modifying risks.\",\"decide whether risk levels are acceptable.\",\"evaluate and possibly change the consequences.\"],\"stem\":\"In a risk analysis, the likelihood and consequences of risk are combined to determine the risk level for a particular risk. The purpose of risk evaluation is to:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"Risk evaluation is used to make decisions about which risks need to be addressed based on the estimate of metrics identified in risk analysis—combined results of the likelihood of the risk and the consequences. [VII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["make decisions based on risk level compared to stated risk criteria.","determine options for modifying risks.","decide whether risk levels are acceptable.","evaluate and possibly change the consequences."],"stem":"In a risk analysis, the likelihood and consequences of risk are combined to determine the risk level for a particular risk. The purpose of risk evaluation is to:","sub":"risk","why":"Risk evaluation is used to make decisions about which risks need to be addressed based on the estimate of metrics identified in risk analysis—combined results of the likelihood of the risk and the consequences. [VII.A.1]"},"revision":"4c428a242ebc0a94cb291bef18334a832cdfe271f0a26b209800186f3f11d94f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-147":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Internal context\",\"Enterprise context\",\"Cultural context\",\"External context\"],\"stem\":\"The rate of aviation fatalities in 1970 was 6.35 per million flights; in 2017, it was 0.39. Which of the following would a company today be failing to understand if it still considered the 1970 rate acceptable?\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"External context is the environment within which the organization seeks to achieve its objectives. The industry benchmark changed between 1970 and 2017 . Cultural context is incorrect and is the environment or situation that is relevant to the beliefs, values, and practices of the culture under study. [VII.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Internal context","Enterprise context","Cultural context","External context"],"stem":"The rate of aviation fatalities in 1970 was 6.35 per million flights; in 2017, it was 0.39. Which of the following would a company today be failing to understand if it still considered the 1970 rate acceptable?","sub":"risk","why":"External context is the environment within which the organization seeks to achieve its objectives. The industry benchmark changed between 1970 and 2017 . Cultural context is incorrect and is the environment or situation that is relevant to the beliefs, values, and practices of the culture under study. [VII.A.2]"},"revision":"a59cf618f7d7d04c476f2d2b2c070cec26e0a08a012f6927891b02045601b0f9"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-148":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"people management.\",\"technical management.\",\"risk management.\",\"supplier management.\"],\"stem\":\"Assessment of organizational objectives and paths to their achievement, prevention of undesired outcomes, and continuous improvement are all goals of:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"Risk management promotes continuous improvement and desired outcomes at the organizational level. The other options may contribute, but risk management provides the overarching umbrella. [VII.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["people management.","technical management.","risk management.","supplier management."],"stem":"Assessment of organizational objectives and paths to their achievement, prevention of undesired outcomes, and continuous improvement are all goals of:","sub":"risk","why":"Risk management promotes continuous improvement and desired outcomes at the organizational level. The other options may contribute, but risk management provides the overarching umbrella. [VII.B.1]"},"revision":"fadc337836a12c7ecb5c223c61fec8a31c92520de7356838fd1c1d0551a362ca"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-149":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"No, because compliant organizational processes must be followed\",\"Yes, because it doesn’t make sense to revise the documents\",\"No, because risk-based thinking begins with the requirements gathering stage\",\"Yes, because risk control measures can best be meaningfully implemented in the later stages\"],\"stem\":\"As a Quality Manager for new products introduction, the design manager requests that you produce all risk documentation after the design is finalized and frozen to avoid redundant work. Would you follow his recommendation and why?\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"It is important to not wait for the final design freeze, but to perform preliminary risk analysis early on. The closer to the requirements-gathering phase the risk analysis is performed, the easier and less expensive it will be to implement risk modification measures. The concept of risk-based thinking is to have not only the designers thinking about potential hazards and their consequences from the first stroke of their pencils but also for the process owners and the finance, marketing, clinical, engineering, operations, and other relevant functions thinking the same way. [VII.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["No, because compliant organizational processes must be followed","Yes, because it doesn’t make sense to revise the documents","No, because risk-based thinking begins with the requirements gathering stage","Yes, because risk control measures can best be meaningfully implemented in the later stages"],"stem":"As a Quality Manager for new products introduction, the design manager requests that you produce all risk documentation after the design is finalized and frozen to avoid redundant work. Would you follow his recommendation and why?","sub":"risk","why":"It is important to not wait for the final design freeze, but to perform preliminary risk analysis early on. The closer to the requirements-gathering phase the risk analysis is performed, the easier and less expensive it will be to implement risk modification measures. The concept of risk-based thinking is to have not only the designers thinking about potential hazards and their consequences from the first stroke of their pencils but also for the process owners and the finance, marketing, clinical, engineering, operations, and other relevant functions thinking the same way. [VII.B.1]"},"revision":"5a9c633711755865fec93a9f6a7cbde9678ff03d8fae73249a80e33a5a1b5fd3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-15":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"express the value of present-day dollars as some projected future value.\",\"express future costs and benefits in present-day dollars.\",\"know what interest rates in the future will be.\",\"know the lifecycle of the product or project being considered.\"],\"stem\":\"To consider the costs and benefits of a future project it is necessary to:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"In cost-benefit analysis, the financial analysis is performed based on translations to today’s value. [I.B.2.c]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["express the value of present-day dollars as some projected future value.","express future costs and benefits in present-day dollars.","know what interest rates in the future will be.","know the lifecycle of the product or project being considered."],"stem":"To consider the costs and benefits of a future project it is necessary to:","sub":"mgmt","why":"In cost-benefit analysis, the financial analysis is performed based on translations to today’s value. [I.B.2.c]"},"revision":"ffa303cd9cfa79fa0db87eb23aefa4a9cfdbb6629de5c24cc21296f52269176c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-150":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"quantitative only.\",\"qualitative only.\",\"subjective.\",\"qualitative or quantitative.\"],\"stem\":\"Results of risk evaluation must be:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"Once a risk analysis has been performed, the final evaluation of the results may be quantitative or qualitative, or both. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["quantitative only.","qualitative only.","subjective.","qualitative or quantitative."],"stem":"Results of risk evaluation must be:","sub":"risk","why":"Once a risk analysis has been performed, the final evaluation of the results may be quantitative or qualitative, or both. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"4a1f155a1b4204e27a7fd55378c2d97c117b6d2138aebfcb14d5e11cd90ca4f1"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-151":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Risk analysis\",\"Risk planning\",\"Risk monitoring\",\"Risk mitigation\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is a phase of the risk assessment process per ISO 31000:2018?\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"ISO 31000:2018 divides the risk assessment process into three distinct and sequential phases: risk identification, risk analysis, and risk evaluation. At all levels of risk management, risk assessment is an activating phase that is critical to the risk management process and will require significant resources. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Risk analysis","Risk planning","Risk monitoring","Risk mitigation"],"stem":"Which of the following is a phase of the risk assessment process per ISO 31000:2018?","sub":"risk","why":"ISO 31000:2018 divides the risk assessment process into three distinct and sequential phases: risk identification, risk analysis, and risk evaluation. At all levels of risk management, risk assessment is an activating phase that is critical to the risk management process and will require significant resources. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"867475d9ba76ba494bb8e408852a09e8284f9a75a7e2efbc57b2b5c04c2f2a77"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-152":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"risk treatment.\",\"risk monitoring.\",\"risk identification.\",\"risk evaluation.\"],\"stem\":\"Reconsidering objectives and doing nothing are decisions that can be taken after:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"The process of risk evaluation is performed by comparing the estimated risk against the baseline criteria to determine the risk acceptability. It is the exercise of assessing action for estimated risks based on their respective significance to the objectives in terms of concerned product, service, process, or business. ISO 31000:2018 states: ”Risk evaluation involves comparing the results of the risk analysis with the established risk criteria to determine where additional action is required. This can lead to a decision to: do nothing further; consider risk treatment options; undertake further analysis to better understand the risk; maintain existing controls; reconsider objectives.” [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["risk treatment.","risk monitoring.","risk identification.","risk evaluation."],"stem":"Reconsidering objectives and doing nothing are decisions that can be taken after:","sub":"risk","why":"The process of risk evaluation is performed by comparing the estimated risk against the baseline criteria to determine the risk acceptability. It is the exercise of assessing action for estimated risks based on their respective significance to the objectives in terms of concerned product, service, process, or business. ISO 31000:2018 states: ”Risk evaluation involves comparing the results of the risk analysis with the established risk criteria to determine where additional action is required. This can lead to a decision to: do nothing further; consider risk treatment options; undertake further analysis to better understand the risk; maintain existing controls; reconsider objectives.” [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"c71b71344b8ef400226bd7f9a676d1a0ffee6e786f9ed6e560a45bde0fcd0d2d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-153":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"risk reduction.\",\"risk identification.\",\"risk evaluation.\",\"risk planning\"],\"stem\":\"The process of comparing the results of a risk analysis that has been completed to the stated risk criteria put in place is:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"Risk evaluation; once estimates of the evaluation metrics have been calculated, they should be compared to the level of risk or risk criteria already in place to determine whether the level of risk is acceptable. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["risk reduction.","risk identification.","risk evaluation.","risk planning"],"stem":"The process of comparing the results of a risk analysis that has been completed to the stated risk criteria put in place is:","sub":"risk","why":"Risk evaluation; once estimates of the evaluation metrics have been calculated, they should be compared to the level of risk or risk criteria already in place to determine whether the level of risk is acceptable. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"a176ed57ef6aad926bdadfbfa260d3d245d07467eda54afe9362584f554775c8"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-154":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"risk identification, analysis, and control.\",\"risk identification, analysis, and evaluation.\",\"risk identification, reduction, and evaluation.\",\"risk reduction, risk criteria, and evaluation.\"],\"stem\":\"Risk assessment consists of the following:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"The three components of risk assessment are risk identification, risk analysis, and risk evaluation. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["risk identification, analysis, and control.","risk identification, analysis, and evaluation.","risk identification, reduction, and evaluation.","risk reduction, risk criteria, and evaluation."],"stem":"Risk assessment consists of the following:","sub":"risk","why":"The three components of risk assessment are risk identification, risk analysis, and risk evaluation. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"e5bfff5c6b0c7dda4b5e7cf38a50aa21af82bd097961f93241a1b6f8ecf83ee2"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-155":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"checklist.\",\"Delphi method.\",\"flowchart.\",\"FMEA.\"],\"stem\":\"A risk assessment tool that is based on expert opinion is the:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"The Delphi method is based on the idea that group decisions or judgments will be more accurate than those of individuals. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["checklist.","Delphi method.","flowchart.","FMEA."],"stem":"A risk assessment tool that is based on expert opinion is the:","sub":"risk","why":"The Delphi method is based on the idea that group decisions or judgments will be more accurate than those of individuals. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"7ac1b7fa207d82710232f46b5af5d613384f5588ae0885afd75ba707a27ba24e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-156":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"it cannot be used to identify combinations of failure modes, only single failure modes.\",\"it does not aid in identifying possible root causes.\",\"it is too complicated since it does not require the user to know anything about the process or product.\",\"it cannot be used to prioritize risks.\"],\"stem\":\"One of the drawbacks or limitations of the FMEA is that:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"The FMEA cannot identify combinations of failure modes, and failure mode combinations may be significant. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["it cannot be used to identify combinations of failure modes, only single failure modes.","it does not aid in identifying possible root causes.","it is too complicated since it does not require the user to know anything about the process or product.","it cannot be used to prioritize risks."],"stem":"One of the drawbacks or limitations of the FMEA is that:","sub":"risk","why":"The FMEA cannot identify combinations of failure modes, and failure mode combinations may be significant. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"f6f5ed67f8d69b63de1e62ea8592f96c0e342adbb501efdac162dc407c738fec"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-157":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"severity and consequence.\",\"probability of occurrence and severity.\",\"probability of occurrence and risk.\",\"severity and risk.\"],\"stem\":\"Two components of risk estimation are:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"The two components for risk estimation include the probability of occurrence of harm and the severity or potential harm. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["severity and consequence.","probability of occurrence and severity.","probability of occurrence and risk.","severity and risk."],"stem":"Two components of risk estimation are:","sub":"risk","why":"The two components for risk estimation include the probability of occurrence of harm and the severity or potential harm. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"8eb5669d9c0b1782b08dae97f88e559bea5d607b3f538bc48092418b94227a5b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-158":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"probabilities of occurrence.\",\"likelihood of occurrence.\",\"severity.\",\"risk priority number.\"],\"stem\":\"Qualitative evaluation involves categories such as catastrophic, critical, major, minor, and negligible, which represent:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"The qualitative assessment where the degree of risk or harm is given as catastrophic, critical, and so on, represents the severity or potential harm. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["probabilities of occurrence.","likelihood of occurrence.","severity.","risk priority number."],"stem":"Qualitative evaluation involves categories such as catastrophic, critical, major, minor, and negligible, which represent:","sub":"risk","why":"The qualitative assessment where the degree of risk or harm is given as catastrophic, critical, and so on, represents the severity or potential harm. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"e1d74c4185e44ae703809acfc891004a7572a7995d2a6580dca6dbb84177ea58"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-159":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"risk priority number.\",\"significance level.\",\"capability index.\",\"supplier index.\"],\"stem\":\"The numerical metric that is an output from an FMEA is the:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"The risk priority number is a numerical result from the FMEA. The RPN is the result of multiplying three values: severity of failure, failure likelihood, and the likelihood of detecting the failure. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["risk priority number.","significance level.","capability index.","supplier index."],"stem":"The numerical metric that is an output from an FMEA is the:","sub":"risk","why":"The risk priority number is a numerical result from the FMEA. The RPN is the result of multiplying three values: severity of failure, failure likelihood, and the likelihood of detecting the failure. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"7f96c1132f3a912472a82bd6c7649c963a601bbc0784dd4ea5eb1508f77b4850"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-16":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Reportable, Accountable, Constructed, Informed.\",\"Roles, Actions, Considerations, Information.\",\"Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, Informed.\",\"Responsible, Actionable, Consulted, Informed.\"],\"stem\":\"In a RACI Matrix, the letters RACI stand for:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"RACI stand for Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, Informed. [I.B.2.d] 17 . b; The Accountable person is ultimately answerable for the successful completion of the task, so there can be only one person where “the buck stops here.” [I.B.2.d]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Reportable, Accountable, Constructed, Informed.","Roles, Actions, Considerations, Information.","Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, Informed.","Responsible, Actionable, Consulted, Informed."],"stem":"In a RACI Matrix, the letters RACI stand for:","sub":"mgmt","why":"RACI stand for Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, Informed. [I.B.2.d] 17 . b; The Accountable person is ultimately answerable for the successful completion of the task, so there can be only one person where “the buck stops here.” [I.B.2.d]"},"revision":"acae8ee0ba4b77a2b55184ec5b17a9fa112f01701a5aafe2b101baf0f445374b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-160":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"FMEA\",\"Failure mode and effects criticality analysis (FMECA)\",\"FTA\",\"Risk management plan (RMP)\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following risk assessment tools is a top-down approach to failure mode analysis?\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"FMEA and FMECA are both bottom-up approaches, while FTA is a top-down approach. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["FMEA","Failure mode and effects criticality analysis (FMECA)","FTA","Risk management plan (RMP)"],"stem":"Which of the following risk assessment tools is a top-down approach to failure mode analysis?","sub":"risk","why":"FMEA and FMECA are both bottom-up approaches, while FTA is a top-down approach. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"22e871c27336c3e151239fce3948abdb10a6082693b3ac4fae8893556990fd16"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-161":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"audit checklist.\",\"risk-based approach.\",\"statistical technique approach.\",\"third-party inspection procedure.\"],\"stem\":\"When assessing Audit nonconformities as an Auditor, it is recommended to use a(n):\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"According to The ASQ Certified Quality Engineer Handbook, Fifth Edition, when assessing audit nonconformities, it is recommended to use a risk-based approach. Delineating between a major and minor nonconformance should be based on the level of risk and the analysis of audit objective evidence. [II.D.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["audit checklist.","risk-based approach.","statistical technique approach.","third-party inspection procedure."],"stem":"When assessing Audit nonconformities as an Auditor, it is recommended to use a(n):","sub":"qsys","why":"According to The ASQ Certified Quality Engineer Handbook, Fifth Edition, when assessing audit nonconformities, it is recommended to use a risk-based approach. Delineating between a major and minor nonconformance should be based on the level of risk and the analysis of audit objective evidence. [II.D.3]"},"revision":"99e4eed2a82b68342f82a43d90151f358bff2818f6ef1e71ca9da546ff76ea2d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-162":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"the auditee, auditor, and closing meeting.\",\"the management review meeting, final inspection, and design reviews.\",\"the audit scope, depth, and time frame.\",\"the ISO certification body audit, FDA inspection, and OSHA audit.\"],\"stem\":\"Audit planning by the lead Auditor should include consideration of:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Proper planning is a key factor in achieving an efficient quality audit. Planning should be conducted with consideration of the client expectations, which include the scope, depth, and time frame. The lead auditor is responsible for planning and conducting the audit. [II.D.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["the auditee, auditor, and closing meeting.","the management review meeting, final inspection, and design reviews.","the audit scope, depth, and time frame.","the ISO certification body audit, FDA inspection, and OSHA audit."],"stem":"Audit planning by the lead Auditor should include consideration of:","sub":"qsys","why":"Proper planning is a key factor in achieving an efficient quality audit. Planning should be conducted with consideration of the client expectations, which include the scope, depth, and time frame. The lead auditor is responsible for planning and conducting the audit. [II.D.3]"},"revision":"3f46eccb67d209537784c9a6960d6e3eabec557c1aa5327f3efb39ab73ce8152"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-163":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"should be agreed on by all parties: client, auditor, auditee.\",\"is not necessary or recommended.\",\"is conducted by someone in the company when there is time.\",\"is communicated via memo from the auditee.\"],\"stem\":\"The auditee will begin improvement efforts to address any problems or corrections identified by the audit. A follow-up audit to verify results of this improvement effort:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"A follow-up audit is necessary to determine whether the problems and recommended corrections have been addressed. All three parties should arrange and agree on a follow-up audit. [II.D.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["should be agreed on by all parties: client, auditor, auditee.","is not necessary or recommended.","is conducted by someone in the company when there is time.","is communicated via memo from the auditee."],"stem":"The auditee will begin improvement efforts to address any problems or corrections identified by the audit. A follow-up audit to verify results of this improvement effort:","sub":"qsys","why":"A follow-up audit is necessary to determine whether the problems and recommended corrections have been addressed. All three parties should arrange and agree on a follow-up audit. [II.D.4]"},"revision":"68b21555e4097924a8974e2838183b51d0b18ca91668d7bc684dbf032d67227c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-164":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"operating costs.\",\"appraisal costs.\",\"failure costs.\",\"quality costs.\"],\"stem\":\"A company increased prevention costs by making changes to its quality control area, and then nonconformities decreased. As a result, there was a reduction in:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"An increase in prevention costs, resulting in a decrease in defects and nonconformities, will result in a decrease in failure costs due to the reduction of defects and nonconformities. [II.E]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["operating costs.","appraisal costs.","failure costs.","quality costs."],"stem":"A company increased prevention costs by making changes to its quality control area, and then nonconformities decreased. As a result, there was a reduction in:","sub":"qsys","why":"An increase in prevention costs, resulting in a decrease in defects and nonconformities, will result in a decrease in failure costs due to the reduction of defects and nonconformities. [II.E]"},"revision":"b6ffb05733d446e125abef2b3fd6936ce4b66bddf288c03d6b2048b1d210a931"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-165":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"prevention costs.\",\"appraisal costs.\",\"internal failure costs.\",\"external failure costs.\"],\"stem\":\"Correction of data entry errors, specimen processing, and cost of reeducating staff in a laboratory before delivery of the results to the customer are examples of:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Since the data error correction, reprocessing, and reeducation occur before delivery of the product or service, the cost incurred would be an internal failure cost. [II.E]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["prevention costs.","appraisal costs.","internal failure costs.","external failure costs."],"stem":"Correction of data entry errors, specimen processing, and cost of reeducating staff in a laboratory before delivery of the results to the customer are examples of:","sub":"qsys","why":"Since the data error correction, reprocessing, and reeducation occur before delivery of the product or service, the cost incurred would be an internal failure cost. [II.E]"},"revision":"ae8add24139e16711223c2b51d0001042775e6a19d096852646ea10c97635154"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-166":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"prevention cost.\",\"appraisal cost.\",\"internal failure cost.\",\"external failure cost.\"],\"stem\":\"In one year, the cost of proficiency testing for quality assurance inspectors by an external certified agency for all employees was $10,000. This cost would be categorized as:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Costs associated with activities performed to uncover or identify deficiencies, such as in personnel proficiency, are considered appraisal costs. [II.E]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["prevention cost.","appraisal cost.","internal failure cost.","external failure cost."],"stem":"In one year, the cost of proficiency testing for quality assurance inspectors by an external certified agency for all employees was $10,000. This cost would be categorized as:","sub":"qsys","why":"Costs associated with activities performed to uncover or identify deficiencies, such as in personnel proficiency, are considered appraisal costs. [II.E]"},"revision":"f33dc744cae0ba80abeaf24abd6817d5c4c8fc6572c4dad628ad01a9e657f6c5"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-167":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"prevention cost.\",\"appraisal cost.\",\"external failure cost.\",\"internal failure cost.\"],\"stem\":\"Money spent on review of suppliers is:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Costs associated with activities related to supplier reviews are known as prevention costs. [II.E]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["prevention cost.","appraisal cost.","external failure cost.","internal failure cost."],"stem":"Money spent on review of suppliers is:","sub":"qsys","why":"Costs associated with activities related to supplier reviews are known as prevention costs. [II.E]"},"revision":"e4e2afbb643adf09ddad13fb24c1bb0e5bd9be6029088a6eb2903470cd988b2d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-168":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Appraisal activities increase.\",\"Appraisal activities decrease.\",\"There is no relationship between the two.\",\"Appraisal activities will remain the same.\"],\"stem\":\"As failure costs decrease due to improved quality, what typically happens to appraisal activities?\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"As failures are uncovered and corrected, appraisal activities will generally decrease, although appraisal activities will not and should not be completely eliminated. The type of appraisal activity can change. [II.E]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Appraisal activities increase.","Appraisal activities decrease.","There is no relationship between the two.","Appraisal activities will remain the same."],"stem":"As failure costs decrease due to improved quality, what typically happens to appraisal activities?","sub":"qsys","why":"As failures are uncovered and corrected, appraisal activities will generally decrease, although appraisal activities will not and should not be completely eliminated. The type of appraisal activity can change. [II.E]"},"revision":"94f8dd93cb8f3a14f0b9fc6cbd6289e5382b5735cf3bd6fd1b5119e84625cc0c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-169":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"prevention cost.\",\"appraisal cost.\",\"external failure cost.\",\"internal failure cost.\"],\"stem\":\"Test equipment depreciation is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Depreciation of test equipment is a cost associated with measuring products to ensure conformance with quality standards and performance requirements, so it is an appraisal cost. [II.E]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["prevention cost.","appraisal cost.","external failure cost.","internal failure cost."],"stem":"Test equipment depreciation is an example of:","sub":"qsys","why":"Depreciation of test equipment is a cost associated with measuring products to ensure conformance with quality standards and performance requirements, so it is an appraisal cost. [II.E]"},"revision":"9116a7f5ab90bb59b6cbd19c4a022b931986e6ac0bcdbdec878d084775029cbd"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-17":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"As many as are appropriate\",\"One\",\"Two or three at the most\",\"The same number as are in the Responsible role\"],\"stem\":\"According to RACI philosophy, how many people can be in the Accountable role for a task?\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"The Accountable person is ultimately answerable for the successful completion of the task, so there can be only one person where “the buck stops here.” [I.B.2.d]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["As many as are appropriate","One","Two or three at the most","The same number as are in the Responsible role"],"stem":"According to RACI philosophy, how many people can be in the Accountable role for a task?","sub":"mgmt","why":"The Accountable person is ultimately answerable for the successful completion of the task, so there can be only one person where “the buck stops here.” [I.B.2.d]"},"revision":"6494e2a96421239e30eb27ecb74baa6a63848e21f25ec64359aa9457d694948f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-170":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"$12,000\",\"$65,000\",\"$77,000\",\"$92,000\"],\"stem\":\"Last month, a local company reported the following quality costs. Training $40,000 Material inspection $12,000 Test equipment $65,000 In-plant scrap and rework $15,000 What is the total appraisal cost for last month?\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"The items considered for appraisal costs include test equipment and material inspection, so the cost is $12,000 + $65,000 = $77,000. [II.E]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["$12,000","$65,000","$77,000","$92,000"],"stem":"Last month, a local company reported the following quality costs. Training $40,000 Material inspection $12,000 Test equipment $65,000 In-plant scrap and rework $15,000 What is the total appraisal cost for last month?","sub":"qsys","why":"The items considered for appraisal costs include test equipment and material inspection, so the cost is $12,000 + $65,000 = $77,000. [II.E]"},"revision":"6507498a7f7bc665aa2f877ac0be89ac7fbd75f1850760296aa10f41d73bd8b4"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-171":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"$0\",\"$12,000\",\"$77,000\",\"$92,000\"],\"stem\":\"Last month, a local company reported the following quality costs. Training $40,000 Material inspection $12,000 Test equipment $65,000 In-plant scrap and rework $15,000 What is the reported external failure cost for last month?\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"None of the costs listed are external failure costs; in-plant scrap and rework is an internal failure cost. [II.E]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["$0","$12,000","$77,000","$92,000"],"stem":"Last month, a local company reported the following quality costs. Training $40,000 Material inspection $12,000 Test equipment $65,000 In-plant scrap and rework $15,000 What is the reported external failure cost for last month?","sub":"qsys","why":"None of the costs listed are external failure costs; in-plant scrap and rework is an internal failure cost. [II.E]"},"revision":"80a69fe7483bd6a17d0025bad366d40b5391da3de9b11ee7a7873f068a01f643"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-172":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"prevention cost.\",\"appraisal cost.\",\"external failure cost.\",\"internal failure cost.\"],\"stem\":\"A quality system audit is to be conducted. This is an example of a(n):\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Quality system audits fall under prevention costs. [II.E]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["prevention cost.","appraisal cost.","external failure cost.","internal failure cost."],"stem":"A quality system audit is to be conducted. This is an example of a(n):","sub":"qsys","why":"Quality system audits fall under prevention costs. [II.E]"},"revision":"b608bae18c0815646845a1ad0bf1bc61289e1642618b1190b43aa0da29d71d2a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-173":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"design and development of the curriculum, or training plan.\",\"developing the lesson plan.\",\"conducting a pretest for the participants.\",\"assessing the need for the training.\"],\"stem\":\"The first step in developing a training program is:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Before developing and implementing any training program, a needs analysis should be conducted to determine whether the training is actually needed and, if so, what it should consist of. [II.F]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["design and development of the curriculum, or training plan.","developing the lesson plan.","conducting a pretest for the participants.","assessing the need for the training."],"stem":"The first step in developing a training program is:","sub":"qsys","why":"Before developing and implementing any training program, a needs analysis should be conducted to determine whether the training is actually needed and, if so, what it should consist of. [II.F]"},"revision":"3c976d5f8f49a369671aec911987db0b2497b036633990a480c45e04a31ecea0"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-174":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Inadequacy of the leader/trainer\",\"Different knowledge levels of the participants\",\"Training is problem-oriented rather than technique-oriented\",\"Complex language\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is a reason for success of a training program?\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Frank Gryna noted that it was best if participants could apply the training during the course to a problem of interest (be problem oriented). [II.F]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Inadequacy of the leader/trainer","Different knowledge levels of the participants","Training is problem-oriented rather than technique-oriented","Complex language"],"stem":"Which of the following is a reason for success of a training program?","sub":"qsys","why":"Frank Gryna noted that it was best if participants could apply the training during the course to a problem of interest (be problem oriented). [II.F]"},"revision":"52c71a9e0923c3277b484741564d8e6d2b47637e58086d045cb2fb748c354c34"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-175":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"assessing learning and retention.\",\"assessing the learner’s reaction to training.\",\"identifying the weakest employees.\",\"determining the cost of the training.\"],\"stem\":\"Pretests and posttests are useful tools in a training program for:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Pretests and posttests can be very useful for determining participant learning and retention of material. [II.F]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["assessing learning and retention.","assessing the learner’s reaction to training.","identifying the weakest employees.","determining the cost of the training."],"stem":"Pretests and posttests are useful tools in a training program for:","sub":"qsys","why":"Pretests and posttests can be very useful for determining participant learning and retention of material. [II.F]"},"revision":"93ae6b7ac06a3f22b4b5f95bab35b45b74add87ae63947fbf5902098ebe8f287"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-176":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Net present value (NPV)\",\"Statistical process control (SPC)\",\"Design of experiments (DOE)\",\"Coordinate measuring machine (CMM)\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following tools can be used for justifying and prioritizing projects?\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Net present value is the only option used for justifying and prioritizing projects. [I.B.2.d]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Net present value (NPV)","Statistical process control (SPC)","Design of experiments (DOE)","Coordinate measuring machine (CMM)"],"stem":"Which of the following tools can be used for justifying and prioritizing projects?","sub":"mgmt","why":"Net present value is the only option used for justifying and prioritizing projects. [I.B.2.d]"},"revision":"1bb8710e29e830bd55d2ba834debccec4e9e4f1cd64668ca7ab14e5eb10e135c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-177":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"decision tree analysis.\",\"internal rate of return analysis.\",\"payback period analysis.\",\"sensitivity analysis.\"],\"stem\":\"Suppose net present value (NPV) is to be estimated for a particular project. To determine how the NPV will change if the interest rate decreases or increases, for example, by 5%, one is doing a type of:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"When metrics are examined by varying the inputs (such as percentage, rate, and so on) in NPV, this is a sensitivity analysis. [I.B.2.d]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["decision tree analysis.","internal rate of return analysis.","payback period analysis.","sensitivity analysis."],"stem":"Suppose net present value (NPV) is to be estimated for a particular project. To determine how the NPV will change if the interest rate decreases or increases, for example, by 5%, one is doing a type of:","sub":"mgmt","why":"When metrics are examined by varying the inputs (such as percentage, rate, and so on) in NPV, this is a sensitivity analysis. [I.B.2.d]"},"revision":"c44b56d2517fec4b9b19d0f64a15d13d42c95055b86faa1fefef811e62221f38"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-178":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"financial tool.\",\"planning tool.\",\"statistical analysis tool.\",\"defect classification tool.\"],\"stem\":\"A Gantt chart is a useful:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"A Gantt chart is also a milestone chart, a planning tool. [I.B.2.d]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["financial tool.","planning tool.","statistical analysis tool.","defect classification tool."],"stem":"A Gantt chart is a useful:","sub":"mgmt","why":"A Gantt chart is also a milestone chart, a planning tool. [I.B.2.d]"},"revision":"a63e7d9b97d0a8ba303b430f8bd9723962e1cc8da20b1b5c80833f2fd78e47dd"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-179":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Quality standards system\",\"Quality audit system\",\"Cost of quality system\",\"Quality information system\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following systems is used to record critical data and control and monitor a process?\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"A quality information system is the collection of data, rules, and equipment that creates information about quality in a systematic way. [I.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Quality standards system","Quality audit system","Cost of quality system","Quality information system"],"stem":"Which of the following systems is used to record critical data and control and monitor a process?","sub":"mgmt","why":"A quality information system is the collection of data, rules, and equipment that creates information about quality in a systematic way. [I.B.3]"},"revision":"1a357b759d80598cc0fd6cae73ec0b99f1631fb2748fbfbd96cd1760af1caf00"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-18":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"sales and distribution.\",\"process planning.\",\"customer needs.\",\"product design.\"],\"stem\":\"For any quality system, all quality-related activities begin with:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Throughout the product life cycle, understanding and identifying customer needs is the first and most important step in any quality-related activity. [II.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["sales and distribution.","process planning.","customer needs.","product design."],"stem":"For any quality system, all quality-related activities begin with:","sub":"qsys","why":"Throughout the product life cycle, understanding and identifying customer needs is the first and most important step in any quality-related activity. [II.A.1]"},"revision":"62a90f831fd00134c0a036a33fc804fb779ea94b78ab49b41c5d9b86317dab3b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-180":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"styles.\",\"behaviors.\",\"opinions.\",\"personalities.\"],\"stem\":\"The ASQ Code of Ethics primarily regulates its members’:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Per the ASQ Code of Ethics Introduction, the purpose of the Code is to establish global standards of conduct and behavior for its members, certification holders, and anyone else who may represent or be perceived to represent ASQ. [I.C]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["styles.","behaviors.","opinions.","personalities."],"stem":"The ASQ Code of Ethics primarily regulates its members’:","sub":"mgmt","why":"Per the ASQ Code of Ethics Introduction, the purpose of the Code is to establish global standards of conduct and behavior for its members, certification holders, and anyone else who may represent or be perceived to represent ASQ. [I.C]"},"revision":"26c471f09cf8bbd0a63000deae9acd18b6adc42cdebe49988b392e95dc6a3e8b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-181":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"in progress.\",\"current.\",\"expired.\",\"a certificate.\"],\"stem\":\"When citing the ASQ CQE certification on social media, one should make sure that their certification is:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"The ASQ Code of Ethics requires members to accurately represent and not mislead others regarding professional qualifications, including education, titles, affiliations, and certifications; previous and future certifications do not hold the same credibility as current ones. [I.C]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["in progress.","current.","expired.","a certificate."],"stem":"When citing the ASQ CQE certification on social media, one should make sure that their certification is:","sub":"mgmt","why":"The ASQ Code of Ethics requires members to accurately represent and not mislead others regarding professional qualifications, including education, titles, affiliations, and certifications; previous and future certifications do not hold the same credibility as current ones. [I.C]"},"revision":"7c80c8c2795799d6cdfb5e0597365821b8ccc485630aaaa5fb8f56347a2d45a0"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-182":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"plagiarism.\",\"social responsibility.\",\"basing decisions on feelings.\",\"disclosing confidential information.\"],\"stem\":\"The ASQ Code of Ethics contains a statement that approves of:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"The ASQ Code of Ethics contains a section directing members to act and conduct business in a professional and socially responsible manner; the other options would not be condoned. [I.C]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["plagiarism.","social responsibility.","basing decisions on feelings.","disclosing confidential information."],"stem":"The ASQ Code of Ethics contains a statement that approves of:","sub":"mgmt","why":"The ASQ Code of Ethics contains a section directing members to act and conduct business in a professional and socially responsible manner; the other options would not be condoned. [I.C]"},"revision":"524535d65e6f5e84f933bdbc1a9e8f2ff121d703259bd2e531a7a0c28ab9ff10"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-183":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Filing a patent for your work\",\"Being objective in quality-based decisions\",\"Hiring the best candidate for a job\",\"Providing consulting on an unfamiliar topic\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following would be an ASQ Code of Ethics violation?\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"The ASQ Code of Ethics states members should offer services, provide advice, and undertake assignments only in their areas of competence, expertise, and training. [I.C]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Filing a patent for your work","Being objective in quality-based decisions","Hiring the best candidate for a job","Providing consulting on an unfamiliar topic"],"stem":"Which of the following would be an ASQ Code of Ethics violation?","sub":"mgmt","why":"The ASQ Code of Ethics states members should offer services, provide advice, and undertake assignments only in their areas of competence, expertise, and training. [I.C]"},"revision":"3b8723e586a022b12b894a6498926aafcc8fdf240e2ca13c0ef849d8b14f619b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-184":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"exclusion from leadership.\",\"written warning.\",\"expulsion from ASQ.\",\"temporary revocation of certifications.\"],\"stem\":\"The most severe discipline resulting from an ASQ Code of Ethics violation is:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"The ASQ Code of Ethics states disciplinary actions will be commensurate with the seriousness of the offense and may include permanent revocation of certifications and/or expulsion from the society. [I.C]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["exclusion from leadership.","written warning.","expulsion from ASQ.","temporary revocation of certifications."],"stem":"The most severe discipline resulting from an ASQ Code of Ethics violation is:","sub":"mgmt","why":"The ASQ Code of Ethics states disciplinary actions will be commensurate with the seriousness of the offense and may include permanent revocation of certifications and/or expulsion from the society. [I.C]"},"revision":"c07754e7299a5af2036ae3cae6e0086e392195c90e34a3605bee8d4573da30f4"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-185":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"individuals begin to shift from personal concerns to the needs of the team.\",\"the team’s mission is clarified and specific roles are identified.\",\"team members still think and act as individuals instead of impacting as a team.\",\"the team has matured and is working in the best interest of the team and team goals.\"],\"stem\":\"Common development stages teams often progress through are known as forming, storming, norming, and performing. In the “storming” stage:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"In the storming phase, teams become familiar with one another and have worked on missions and strategies, but members often still act in their own individual best interests rather than the team''s as a whole. [I.D]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["individuals begin to shift from personal concerns to the needs of the team.","the team’s mission is clarified and specific roles are identified.","team members still think and act as individuals instead of impacting as a team.","the team has matured and is working in the best interest of the team and team goals."],"stem":"Common development stages teams often progress through are known as forming, storming, norming, and performing. In the “storming” stage:","sub":"mgmt","why":"In the storming phase, teams become familiar with one another and have worked on missions and strategies, but members often still act in their own individual best interests rather than the team''s as a whole. [I.D]"},"revision":"76eb8114f2a8dadbfd7f77b1c274ec0059c5f0b1072d9c5db10380228133f33c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-186":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"leader.\",\"facilitator.\",\"note taker.\",\"sponsor.\"],\"stem\":\"Keeping the team on task is the main function of the team:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"The facilitator''s role is to provide support for the team, and the main task for that individual is to keep the team on task. [I.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["leader.","facilitator.","note taker.","sponsor."],"stem":"Keeping the team on task is the main function of the team:","sub":"mgmt","why":"The facilitator''s role is to provide support for the team, and the main task for that individual is to keep the team on task. [I.E.1]"},"revision":"a48e425a1772a449ab64ac96b8af3a20af24aad3ddf79980a20269860ab293f5"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-187":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"discuss content issues with the team.\",\"discuss process issues with the team.\",\"suggest technical solutions to the team.\",\"provide financial backing to the team.\"],\"stem\":\"Part of the team facilitator’s mission is to:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"The facilitator''s role should be marginal and grow less important as the team matures. Overall, the facilitator is responsible for process issues, meeting agendas, communication among teams, and keeping the team on task. [I.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["discuss content issues with the team.","discuss process issues with the team.","suggest technical solutions to the team.","provide financial backing to the team."],"stem":"Part of the team facilitator’s mission is to:","sub":"mgmt","why":"The facilitator''s role should be marginal and grow less important as the team matures. Overall, the facilitator is responsible for process issues, meeting agendas, communication among teams, and keeping the team on task. [I.E.1]"},"revision":"214f5dbd44ac5ea9b18eded02bc6fdbc7bb424172de4e27b3090267290e8e2a6"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-188":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"quality function deployment.\",\"fault tree analysis.\",\"nominal group technique.\",\"operating characteristic curve.\"],\"stem\":\"A useful brainstorming tool for reducing a large list of items to a smaller, manageable one is:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"The nominal group technique allows for brainstorming a large number of ideas or suggestions while providing everyone an equal voice in the process. [I.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["quality function deployment.","fault tree analysis.","nominal group technique.","operating characteristic curve."],"stem":"A useful brainstorming tool for reducing a large list of items to a smaller, manageable one is:","sub":"mgmt","why":"The nominal group technique allows for brainstorming a large number of ideas or suggestions while providing everyone an equal voice in the process. [I.E.2]"},"revision":"67195cdee82f00bec296ace9d0a075733e361f1655000e45b66df7eb64c4f88f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-189":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"evaluation.\",\"support.\",\"probing.\",\"understanding.\"],\"stem\":\"In the communication process, there are five methods of feedback: evaluation, interpretation, support, probing, and understanding. Of these methods, the one that most often creates defensiveness and can quickly break the communication process is:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"All feedback methods can lead to a breakdown in the communication process; however, evaluation, which involves a judgment about the importance, worth, or appropriateness of the statement, is the one that leads to a breakdown much more quickly. [I.F]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["evaluation.","support.","probing.","understanding."],"stem":"In the communication process, there are five methods of feedback: evaluation, interpretation, support, probing, and understanding. Of these methods, the one that most often creates defensiveness and can quickly break the communication process is:","sub":"mgmt","why":"All feedback methods can lead to a breakdown in the communication process; however, evaluation, which involves a judgment about the importance, worth, or appropriateness of the statement, is the one that leads to a breakdown much more quickly. [I.F]"},"revision":"6d52523d8e0666c28418cf87b65814451d2ed74c407aad859fc48e5a4375ba3f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-19":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"policies, procedures, instructions, and records.\",\"policies, documentation, instructions, and records.\",\"documentation, implementation, compliance, and accuracy.\",\"compliance, accuracy, clarity, and records.\"],\"stem\":\"A quality manual generally consists of four tiers or layers that include:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"The four tiers are policies, procedures, instructions, and records. The first or top tier represents policies. The second tier represents procedures. The third tier represents work instructions. The fourth tier represents a record of results of implementing a quality system. [II.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["policies, procedures, instructions, and records.","policies, documentation, instructions, and records.","documentation, implementation, compliance, and accuracy.","compliance, accuracy, clarity, and records."],"stem":"A quality manual generally consists of four tiers or layers that include:","sub":"qsys","why":"The four tiers are policies, procedures, instructions, and records. The first or top tier represents policies. The second tier represents procedures. The third tier represents work instructions. The fourth tier represents a record of results of implementing a quality system. [II.B.1]"},"revision":"dde081e038355479c9bfb685b3aa7584c3e75ccd7d3144fd925b86d9c55c0cef"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-190":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Supplier audit\",\"Supplier classifications\",\"Supplier survey\",\"Supplier performance improvement\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following supplier management tools is used to initially verify that the supplier has adequate financial resources, manufacturing capabilities, and quality systems?\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"A supplier audit is not a bad option, but a supplier survey is better since it provides an initial assessment of a supplier''s financial resources, manufacturing capabilities, and quality systems. [I.H]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Supplier audit","Supplier classifications","Supplier survey","Supplier performance improvement"],"stem":"Which of the following supplier management tools is used to initially verify that the supplier has adequate financial resources, manufacturing capabilities, and quality systems?","sub":"mgmt","why":"A supplier audit is not a bad option, but a supplier survey is better since it provides an initial assessment of a supplier''s financial resources, manufacturing capabilities, and quality systems. [I.H]"},"revision":"70eba3d3e8334fa91a052db891265e5968bb8f18fe86fc4860a37569be94c2ae"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-191":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Lack of real employee empowerment\",\"Emphasis on a formal strategic plan for change\",\"Strong workforce motivation\",\"Focus on the customer\"],\"stem\":\"Implementing quality improvement in an organization can be a failure for several reasons. Which of the following is one obstacle to implementing quality?\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"The ASQ Certified Quality Engineer Handbook, Fifth Edition, lists lack of real employee empowerment among the common barriers to a successful quality improvement implementation, along with lack of a formalized strategic plan, lack of strong motivation, and lack of customer focus, among others. [I.I]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Lack of real employee empowerment","Emphasis on a formal strategic plan for change","Strong workforce motivation","Focus on the customer"],"stem":"Implementing quality improvement in an organization can be a failure for several reasons. Which of the following is one obstacle to implementing quality?","sub":"mgmt","why":"The ASQ Certified Quality Engineer Handbook, Fifth Edition, lists lack of real employee empowerment among the common barriers to a successful quality improvement implementation, along with lack of a formalized strategic plan, lack of strong motivation, and lack of customer focus, among others. [I.I]"},"revision":"265f654ccb42187b453062b0cce78cc6ab00b41d0e721c5690330e43ae6e7844"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-192":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"chart\":{\"cells\":[[\"medium\",\"high\",\"high\"],[\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\"],[\"low\",\"low\",\"medium\"]],\"colAxis\":\"Probability\",\"cols\":[\"Low\",\"Medium\",\"High\"],\"rowAxis\":\"Severity\",\"rows\":[\"High\",\"Medium\",\"Low\"],\"type\":\"risk-matrix\"},\"options\":[\"flowchart.\",\"FMEA.\",\"severity categorization table.\",\"risk matrix.\"],\"stem\":\"Consider a matrix with rows labeled Severity (Low, Medium, High) and columns labeled Probability (Low, Medium, High), used to plot the combination of likelihood and severity for identified risks. This table is an example of a(n):\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"The table represents a risk matrix (also known as a risk assessment matrix). It contains information on the probability of a certain level of risk and the level of severity. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"chart":{"cells":[["medium","high","high"],["low","medium","high"],["low","low","medium"]],"colAxis":"Probability","cols":["Low","Medium","High"],"rowAxis":"Severity","rows":["High","Medium","Low"],"type":"risk-matrix"},"options":["flowchart.","FMEA.","severity categorization table.","risk matrix."],"stem":"Consider a matrix with rows labeled Severity (Low, Medium, High) and columns labeled Probability (Low, Medium, High), used to plot the combination of likelihood and severity for identified risks. This table is an example of a(n):","sub":"risk","why":"The table represents a risk matrix (also known as a risk assessment matrix). It contains information on the probability of a certain level of risk and the level of severity. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"d4067c7a53e6e01c5399499707888150f44d6e2e15fe8272f671837f73fa7763"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-193":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"FMEA\",\"FMECA\",\"Hazard analysis and critical control points (HACCP)\",\"Hazard and operability (HAZOP) analysis\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following techniques uses guide words and is based on the assumption that risks are caused by deviations from the design plans?\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"HAZOP is the one technique that uses guide words and can be used to identify operability issues and potential hazards that may lead to unacceptable products, processes, or services. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["FMEA","FMECA","Hazard analysis and critical control points (HACCP)","Hazard and operability (HAZOP) analysis"],"stem":"Which of the following techniques uses guide words and is based on the assumption that risks are caused by deviations from the design plans?","sub":"risk","why":"HAZOP is the one technique that uses guide words and can be used to identify operability issues and potential hazards that may lead to unacceptable products, processes, or services. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"d6918e589f8baa8378084cef98a8ee9fbf99e932e95b164e688892633401b359"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-194":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Risk monitoring\",\"Risk identification\",\"Risk evaluation\",\"Risk communication\"],\"stem\":\"A new Quality Director is spearheading the establishment of a new Lean Production System at her company. She had previously encountered poor quality products and late deliveries from an overseas supplier and decided to set up a brainstorming session with her team for identifying and recording potential risk sources, causes, and potential consequences of using this overseas supplier to their lean and just in time (JIT) production. What process is being described here?\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"Risk identification is the process of finding, recognizing, describing, and recording risks, including the identification of risk sources, events, their causes, and their potential consequences. [VII.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Risk monitoring","Risk identification","Risk evaluation","Risk communication"],"stem":"A new Quality Director is spearheading the establishment of a new Lean Production System at her company. She had previously encountered poor quality products and late deliveries from an overseas supplier and decided to set up a brainstorming session with her team for identifying and recording potential risk sources, causes, and potential consequences of using this overseas supplier to their lean and just in time (JIT) production. What process is being described here?","sub":"risk","why":"Risk identification is the process of finding, recognizing, describing, and recording risks, including the identification of risk sources, events, their causes, and their potential consequences. [VII.C.1]"},"revision":"55a3053afb4d40f85035a90fdb225dba69c2986f9ead044406d98280a8687250"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-195":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"completely eliminate risk.\",\"completely eliminate all failures.\",\"maintain risk at or below an acceptable level.\",\"increase probability of occurrence ratings.\"],\"stem\":\"The purpose of risk control is to:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"The main goal of risk control is to maintain the level of risk at an acceptably low level. [VII.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["completely eliminate risk.","completely eliminate all failures.","maintain risk at or below an acceptable level.","increase probability of occurrence ratings."],"stem":"The purpose of risk control is to:","sub":"risk","why":"The main goal of risk control is to maintain the level of risk at an acceptably low level. [VII.C.1]"},"revision":"b1d8e00a6bac1fa385b7a65de23179f7730335622966a9bc5a9bee69fe59bb6b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-196":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Creating the RMP\",\"Obtaining new information to update risk levels and risk criteria\",\"Assigning initial risk ratings\",\"Determining the type of risk management applied\"],\"stem\":\"Risk control requires documentation that addresses monitoring and review of risks and controls put in place. Which of the following would be part of monitoring and review?\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"Obtaining and applying new information should be included in any risk control documentation. [VII.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Creating the RMP","Obtaining new information to update risk levels and risk criteria","Assigning initial risk ratings","Determining the type of risk management applied"],"stem":"Risk control requires documentation that addresses monitoring and review of risks and controls put in place. Which of the following would be part of monitoring and review?","sub":"risk","why":"Obtaining and applying new information should be included in any risk control documentation. [VII.C.1]"},"revision":"7dbdaa77cbb0010c0defd386d72b1bced898040ab7816655cddfadadbda307e9"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-197":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"there are controls in place to address significant risks.\",\"there is more than one control in place to address a significant risk.\",\"there is no control in place to address a significant risk.\",\"the control in place is sufficient.\"],\"stem\":\"A gap may exist when controlling risks if:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"A gap occurs when the control in place is not adequate for the risk, or no control has been put in place for a significant risk. [VII.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["there are controls in place to address significant risks.","there is more than one control in place to address a significant risk.","there is no control in place to address a significant risk.","the control in place is sufficient."],"stem":"A gap may exist when controlling risks if:","sub":"risk","why":"A gap occurs when the control in place is not adequate for the risk, or no control has been put in place for a significant risk. [VII.C.1]"},"revision":"c1a3e1be8cc07d2d72aad9291a5d1e4fa6b7eb2cd12bd9172c0088bba1fcfec0"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-198":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"risk that remains after a risk treatment has been implemented.\",\"risk due to a gap between what is believed to be risk and what really is risk.\",\"unknown risk that can never be identified.\",\"risk prior to the implementation of mitigations.\"],\"stem\":\"Residual risk is:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"Residual risk is that risk remaining even after a treatment of a known risk has been implemented; a risk treatment does not always eliminate a risk but may only minimize it until the risk is at or below an acceptable level. [VII.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["risk that remains after a risk treatment has been implemented.","risk due to a gap between what is believed to be risk and what really is risk.","unknown risk that can never be identified.","risk prior to the implementation of mitigations."],"stem":"Residual risk is:","sub":"risk","why":"Residual risk is that risk remaining even after a treatment of a known risk has been implemented; a risk treatment does not always eliminate a risk but may only minimize it until the risk is at or below an acceptable level. [VII.C.1]"},"revision":"d94cb35832908e53c8e99d7615d397273a38595a90901df3e18e7e727a35dad3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-199":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"risk control.\",\"risk audit.\",\"residual risk.\",\"risk reporting.\"],\"stem\":\"Risk reduction and risk acceptance are the two components of:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"Risk reduction focuses on the avoidance of risk, while risk acceptance focuses on the decision to accept risk; together they are risk control. [VII.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["risk control.","risk audit.","residual risk.","risk reporting."],"stem":"Risk reduction and risk acceptance are the two components of:","sub":"risk","why":"Risk reduction focuses on the avoidance of risk, while risk acceptance focuses on the decision to accept risk; together they are risk control. [VII.C.1]"},"revision":"676cf7d2c0314c244d8ce18624319218c155cb1cf042cc68a6078043580e357a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-2":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"monitoring techniques to correct sporadic problems.\",\"an annual quality program to institutionalize managerial control and review.\",\"a method that drives out fear so that everyone may work effectively.\",\"a breakthrough sequence to solve chronic problems.\"],\"stem\":\"According to the quality trilogy, quality improvement is:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"In his 1964 text, Managerial Breakthrough, Juran defined quality improvement as a breakthrough sequence to solve chronic problems that is analogous to “common causes.” [I.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["monitoring techniques to correct sporadic problems.","an annual quality program to institutionalize managerial control and review.","a method that drives out fear so that everyone may work effectively.","a breakthrough sequence to solve chronic problems."],"stem":"According to the quality trilogy, quality improvement is:","sub":"mgmt","why":"In his 1964 text, Managerial Breakthrough, Juran defined quality improvement as a breakthrough sequence to solve chronic problems that is analogous to “common causes.” [I.A.1]"},"revision":"6f7d4db5af33405974a1c604ed358927dd36076e72151da248440f7058489369"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-20":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"compliance, implementation, and clarity.\",\"compliance, clarity, and procedures.\",\"procedures, accuracy, and clarity.\",\"compliance, accuracy, and clarity.\"],\"stem\":\"The critical characteristics of any documentation of a quality system are:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Compliance, accuracy, and clarity; without these components, users will lose confidence in the documentation. [II.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["compliance, implementation, and clarity.","compliance, clarity, and procedures.","procedures, accuracy, and clarity.","compliance, accuracy, and clarity."],"stem":"The critical characteristics of any documentation of a quality system are:","sub":"qsys","why":"Compliance, accuracy, and clarity; without these components, users will lose confidence in the documentation. [II.B.1]"},"revision":"d592bda754b6b45e980166406ef9b5333a05c104fd122a7348289099da9d66e8"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-200":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Risk reduction\",\"Risk audit\",\"Risk control\",\"Risk reporting\"],\"stem\":\"What activity is performed to verify that known risks are being controlled and that risk treatment plans in place are effective?\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"In addition to determining whether known risks are being controlled and risk treatment plans are effective, risk audits can also be helpful in identifying new risks. [VII.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Risk reduction","Risk audit","Risk control","Risk reporting"],"stem":"What activity is performed to verify that known risks are being controlled and that risk treatment plans in place are effective?","sub":"risk","why":"In addition to determining whether known risks are being controlled and risk treatment plans are effective, risk audits can also be helpful in identifying new risks. [VII.C.2]"},"revision":"ea36ab9d2cf469dc6168b07bf50b97f046dcbc2121ecbc61a975b60c53c9b517"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-201":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"ISO 14971:2019.\",\"ANSI/ASQ Z1.9.\",\"ISO 31000:2018.\",\"ISO 9001:2015.\"],\"stem\":\"Avoiding risk, changing likelihood or consequences, and taking risk to pursue opportunity are actions that are addressed in:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"See Clause 6.1.2 in ISO 9001:2015. [VII.C.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["ISO 14971:2019.","ANSI/ASQ Z1.9.","ISO 31000:2018.","ISO 9001:2015."],"stem":"Avoiding risk, changing likelihood or consequences, and taking risk to pursue opportunity are actions that are addressed in:","sub":"risk","why":"See Clause 6.1.2 in ISO 9001:2015. [VII.C.3]"},"revision":"26a5df11bb53ceaa303f60446eb5b1c24fcb28d3b72037292bff78a320471dfb"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-202":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Exploit\",\"Enhance\",\"Mitigate\",\"Share\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following risk treatment options are available to designers and manufacturers of a hospital bed?\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"Share, accept, exploit, and enhance options are available only for positive risks. A hospital bed requires safety risk management and will have negative-risk treatment options available (avoid, mitigate, transfer, accept). [VII.C.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Exploit","Enhance","Mitigate","Share"],"stem":"Which of the following risk treatment options are available to designers and manufacturers of a hospital bed?","sub":"risk","why":"Share, accept, exploit, and enhance options are available only for positive risks. A hospital bed requires safety risk management and will have negative-risk treatment options available (avoid, mitigate, transfer, accept). [VII.C.3]"},"revision":"1902883b5c95c495e7009b5bc3ad9cd2029ed05693840950ff2787e69096527a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-203":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"leading risk indicator for supplier quality.\",\"leading risk indication for supplier timeliness.\",\"leading risk indicator for transponder quality.\",\"lagging risk indicator for transponder quality.\"],\"stem\":\"A Key Risk Indicator (KRI) is a metric that provides a leading or lagging indication of the current state of risk exposure on key objectives. A manufacturing team assembles circuit boards for transponders. Certain capacitors for the circuit are being found to be faulty upon receipt by incoming quality inspectors, and Return Material Authorization (RMA) forms are often required. The number of RMA forms for a particular supplier is a:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"The potential defective part is not yet installed in the transponder, so depending on the sampling of the supplier lot a bad part may be used; this makes the RMA forms a leading risk indicator for transponder quality. [VII.C.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["leading risk indicator for supplier quality.","leading risk indication for supplier timeliness.","leading risk indicator for transponder quality.","lagging risk indicator for transponder quality."],"stem":"A Key Risk Indicator (KRI) is a metric that provides a leading or lagging indication of the current state of risk exposure on key objectives. A manufacturing team assembles circuit boards for transponders. Certain capacitors for the circuit are being found to be faulty upon receipt by incoming quality inspectors, and Return Material Authorization (RMA) forms are often required. The number of RMA forms for a particular supplier is a:","sub":"risk","why":"The potential defective part is not yet installed in the transponder, so depending on the sampling of the supplier lot a bad part may be used; this makes the RMA forms a leading risk indicator for transponder quality. [VII.C.4]"},"revision":"08633179de374255e04d1db0429a8c724846d404e015cfd39d8d730deb214dd2"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-204":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"risk planning.\",\"risk treatment.\",\"risk monitoring.\",\"risk reporting.\"],\"stem\":\"The risk management process step in which the effectiveness of the risk assessment techniques are evaluated is:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"Evaluating the effectiveness of risk assessment techniques is one part of monitoring a risk management process, along with checking whether assumptions made when estimating risk are still valid and whether risk assessment results match actual results. [VII.C.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["risk planning.","risk treatment.","risk monitoring.","risk reporting."],"stem":"The risk management process step in which the effectiveness of the risk assessment techniques are evaluated is:","sub":"risk","why":"Evaluating the effectiveness of risk assessment techniques is one part of monitoring a risk management process, along with checking whether assumptions made when estimating risk are still valid and whether risk assessment results match actual results. [VII.C.4]"},"revision":"7608d62d13568fefa0b8604af99ab79e7a79a4c6536039c2e51b7627b539b279"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-205":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Agree with risk manager that off-label use is a safety risk and should be actively prevented by all means\",\"Acknowledge that off-label use is a risk that is a positive risk and explore an avenue for exploitation using the clinical team’s researched data\",\"Agree with marketing team and make plans to approve carotid indication in addition to femoral indication\",\"Don’t take any action as current situation is making company money without legal or regulatory liability\"],\"stem\":\"A medical catheter is approved in the U.S. by the FDA for femoral artery access only. However, physicians are using the device off label for carotid artery access. The clinical department has noticed several publications where carotid access has been performed and successful results published. The company’s risk manager states that off-label use is risk that needs to be mitigated. Your marketing team says that this is an opportunity to be capitalized upon. What is the most risk intelligent action to take?\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"It is wise to acknowledge the positive risk and make a well-informed, balanced decision; preventing it outright ignores the positive risk, and approving immediately or taking no action both skip proper risk identification, analysis, and evaluation. [VII.C.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Agree with risk manager that off-label use is a safety risk and should be actively prevented by all means","Acknowledge that off-label use is a risk that is a positive risk and explore an avenue for exploitation using the clinical team’s researched data","Agree with marketing team and make plans to approve carotid indication in addition to femoral indication","Don’t take any action as current situation is making company money without legal or regulatory liability"],"stem":"A medical catheter is approved in the U.S. by the FDA for femoral artery access only. However, physicians are using the device off label for carotid artery access. The clinical department has noticed several publications where carotid access has been performed and successful results published. The company’s risk manager states that off-label use is risk that needs to be mitigated. Your marketing team says that this is an opportunity to be capitalized upon. What is the most risk intelligent action to take?","sub":"risk","why":"It is wise to acknowledge the positive risk and make a well-informed, balanced decision; preventing it outright ignores the positive risk, and approving immediately or taking no action both skip proper risk identification, analysis, and evaluation. [VII.C.5]"},"revision":"dc74e33645d3c4dd81a6e7664346436ad5e5745eef29752f2494ce8b763f8bb1"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-206":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"eliminate risk.\",\"minimize risk.\",\"accept the risk as is.\",\"maximize risk.\"],\"stem\":\"The main purpose of a risk mitigation plan is to:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"Risk mitigation includes the act of attempting to minimize the risk''s impact on the system, process, or product. [VII.C.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["eliminate risk.","minimize risk.","accept the risk as is.","maximize risk."],"stem":"The main purpose of a risk mitigation plan is to:","sub":"risk","why":"Risk mitigation includes the act of attempting to minimize the risk''s impact on the system, process, or product. [VII.C.5]"},"revision":"4f32e6ee4fe3051c92efe75e9692f56be3e8dc18e74af9dfcefa0eaa9b75548e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-207":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"avoidance.\",\"transfer.\",\"mitigation.\",\"denial.\"],\"stem\":\"If the response to a particular risk is to attempt to minimize its impact, this is known as:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"A risk mitigation plan is implemented once risks have been identified and evaluated to determine whether the risk is worth eliminating or minimizing, or whether to accept the risk and do nothing about it. [VII.C.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["avoidance.","transfer.","mitigation.","denial."],"stem":"If the response to a particular risk is to attempt to minimize its impact, this is known as:","sub":"risk","why":"A risk mitigation plan is implemented once risks have been identified and evaluated to determine whether the risk is worth eliminating or minimizing, or whether to accept the risk and do nothing about it. [VII.C.5]"},"revision":"33305a61d3687fe536cdf8c2d37993c82df9c20c87396890f187abbdfc065c5b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-208":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Start a Six Sigma program.\",\"Develop a Six Sigma infrastructure.\",\"Roll out Six Sigma methods on a project-by-project basis.\",\"Begin at lower levels in the organization.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following methods is the most effective way to implement Six Sigma in an organization?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"A Six Sigma program that focuses on tools alone often lacks organization-wide buy-in and delivers limited results. A Six Sigma infrastructure gives employees both the tools and the problem-solving methods needed for specific projects identified by management. [V.C]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Start a Six Sigma program.","Develop a Six Sigma infrastructure.","Roll out Six Sigma methods on a project-by-project basis.","Begin at lower levels in the organization."],"stem":"Which of the following methods is the most effective way to implement Six Sigma in an organization?","sub":"ci","why":"A Six Sigma program that focuses on tools alone often lacks organization-wide buy-in and delivers limited results. A Six Sigma infrastructure gives employees both the tools and the problem-solving methods needed for specific projects identified by management. [V.C]"},"revision":"380e03b0af00b596fa3d7e443fb8824deb0e3fb1bacab812643790fe74208427"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-209":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"5S\",\"Kanban\",\"Value stream map\",\"Visual control\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following tools is typically used to identify waste in a lean system?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"The goal of a lean system is to eliminate waste from a process. A value stream map, a visual representation of all the steps in a process including timing, is one of the first tools used to identify improvement opportunities and value-added vs. non-value-added activities. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["5S","Kanban","Value stream map","Visual control"],"stem":"Which of the following tools is typically used to identify waste in a lean system?","sub":"ci","why":"The goal of a lean system is to eliminate waste from a process. A value stream map, a visual representation of all the steps in a process including timing, is one of the first tools used to identify improvement opportunities and value-added vs. non-value-added activities. [V.D]"},"revision":"2a3f3ec8bc221053704044a120479277613f64f2874e4db785230fea457f7ea3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-21":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"supplier management.\",\"implementation of a documented quality system.\",\"contractor assessment.\",\"auditing of suppliers.\"],\"stem\":\"One major component of a quality system is:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Effective implementation of a quality system that has been reviewed and approved for release is one of the two major components of a quality program. The other component is documentation of procedures and instructions of the quality system. [II.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["supplier management.","implementation of a documented quality system.","contractor assessment.","auditing of suppliers."],"stem":"One major component of a quality system is:","sub":"qsys","why":"Effective implementation of a quality system that has been reviewed and approved for release is one of the two major components of a quality program. The other component is documentation of procedures and instructions of the quality system. [II.A.1]"},"revision":"23cb5a820223767b1f124da09483ea817205096445b22fa6f565e9e142611183"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-210":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Waiting\",\"Excess motion\",\"Excess movement of material\",\"Excess processing\"],\"stem\":\"A restaurant is analyzing its current workflow. Employees in the restaurant must walk back and forth throughout the kitchen to retrieve various cooking tools during preparation of a meal. How might the restaurant classify this action?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Employees walking more than necessary to retrieve supplies is an example of waste due to excess motion; a redesigned kitchen layout moving tools closer to where they''re needed would reduce the excess walking. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Waiting","Excess motion","Excess movement of material","Excess processing"],"stem":"A restaurant is analyzing its current workflow. Employees in the restaurant must walk back and forth throughout the kitchen to retrieve various cooking tools during preparation of a meal. How might the restaurant classify this action?","sub":"ci","why":"Employees walking more than necessary to retrieve supplies is an example of waste due to excess motion; a redesigned kitchen layout moving tools closer to where they''re needed would reduce the excess walking. [V.D]"},"revision":"4f54db8bcb03302d462cc86ede1b2ace7393adf13a991399bcf400e45240779b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-211":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Waiting\",\"Excess movement of material\",\"Defect correction\",\"Excess motion\"],\"stem\":\"The restaurant is also examining its front-of-house operations. They found that there is frequently a long line of customers standing by the chicken counter while staff turn over tables. What kind of waste is occurring at the front of house at this restaurant?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Customers waiting to be seated due to long table setup/turnover times is waste due to waiting; cross-training staff or standardized work could reduce setup time and customer wait. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Waiting","Excess movement of material","Defect correction","Excess motion"],"stem":"The restaurant is also examining its front-of-house operations. They found that there is frequently a long line of customers standing by the chicken counter while staff turn over tables. What kind of waste is occurring at the front of house at this restaurant?","sub":"ci","why":"Customers waiting to be seated due to long table setup/turnover times is waste due to waiting; cross-training staff or standardized work could reduce setup time and customer wait. [V.D]"},"revision":"8e200858c72848f9d3a6b70912f955a3d7609533cdbaf91241265dbc83e34dbf"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-212":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Value-added activities\",\"Standardized work\",\"Non-value-added activities\",\"Transportation of the product\"],\"stem\":\"After creating a value stream map of a process, on which steps in the process should a quality team focus their efforts?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Non-value-added activities are steps in a process that aren''t needed; improvement projects have the largest impact when focused on these steps. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Value-added activities","Standardized work","Non-value-added activities","Transportation of the product"],"stem":"After creating a value stream map of a process, on which steps in the process should a quality team focus their efforts?","sub":"ci","why":"Non-value-added activities are steps in a process that aren''t needed; improvement projects have the largest impact when focused on these steps. [V.D]"},"revision":"9580694054298d83b6b65771665f372f895bfaef4504a60631acc4ed8325c540"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-213":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"To radically change a process\",\"To make slow, incremental changes to a process\",\"To organize the workplace environment\",\"To reduce defects produced in a process\"],\"stem\":\"What is the primary goal of 5S?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"5S stands for five Japanese words for organizing a workplace: sort, set in order, shine, standardize, and sustain. Reengineering radically changes a process and kaizen makes slow, incremental changes; neither is 5S''s goal. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["To radically change a process","To make slow, incremental changes to a process","To organize the workplace environment","To reduce defects produced in a process"],"stem":"What is the primary goal of 5S?","sub":"ci","why":"5S stands for five Japanese words for organizing a workplace: sort, set in order, shine, standardize, and sustain. Reengineering radically changes a process and kaizen makes slow, incremental changes; neither is 5S''s goal. [V.D]"},"revision":"6029f9d54301f418f2db03d0274ddf539402cd3e2ed313b1bf13e83cf938a298"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-214":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Defect correction\",\"Excess motion\",\"Excess processing\",\"Excess production\"],\"stem\":\"A toy company paints a certain toy white by default. The toys are then painted other colors as requested by customers. As what type of waste might a quality team classify this action?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Painting toys white by default and then repainting per customer request is waste due to excess processing; painting only after the customer''s color choice is known would eliminate the extra processing step. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Defect correction","Excess motion","Excess processing","Excess production"],"stem":"A toy company paints a certain toy white by default. The toys are then painted other colors as requested by customers. As what type of waste might a quality team classify this action?","sub":"ci","why":"Painting toys white by default and then repainting per customer request is waste due to excess processing; painting only after the customer''s color choice is known would eliminate the extra processing step. [V.D]"},"revision":"b37d56796f869f110e4c9e85c74287d75efe7b719506641a421f328948e81109"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-215":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"The weakest step in a chain of processes is improved.\",\"There is less inventory and a smoother flow of supplies.\",\"The workplace is better organized.\",\"Products are not processed until a customer places an order.\"],\"stem\":\"What is the main benefit of implementing a kanban system?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"A kanban system provides visual cues that ease restocking, resulting in less inventory and a smoother flow of supplies. Theory of Constraints improves the weakest step in a process chain, 5S organizes a workplace, and a pull system completes steps only when triggered by demand. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["The weakest step in a chain of processes is improved.","There is less inventory and a smoother flow of supplies.","The workplace is better organized.","Products are not processed until a customer places an order."],"stem":"What is the main benefit of implementing a kanban system?","sub":"ci","why":"A kanban system provides visual cues that ease restocking, resulting in less inventory and a smoother flow of supplies. Theory of Constraints improves the weakest step in a process chain, 5S organizes a workplace, and a pull system completes steps only when triggered by demand. [V.D]"},"revision":"56960868c73e6733432209e1621ae45540a62d5b6a18e1e99938a1899470190e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-216":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Kanban\",\"Visual control\",\"Define, measure, analyze, improve, control (DMAIC)\",\"5S\"],\"stem\":\"A deli has newly instituted a few changes to its work area. The workers now have a schedule for cleaning their workstations, have standardized practices, and have created designated locations for all their equipment. What improvement method has this deli employed?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"5S is five words adapted from Japanese for organizing a workplace: arranging things properly, orderliness, cleanliness, cleanup, and discipline. A cleaning schedule, standardized practices, and designated equipment locations are the deli implementing the five S''s. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Kanban","Visual control","Define, measure, analyze, improve, control (DMAIC)","5S"],"stem":"A deli has newly instituted a few changes to its work area. The workers now have a schedule for cleaning their workstations, have standardized practices, and have created designated locations for all their equipment. What improvement method has this deli employed?","sub":"ci","why":"5S is five words adapted from Japanese for organizing a workplace: arranging things properly, orderliness, cleanliness, cleanup, and discipline. A cleaning schedule, standardized practices, and designated equipment locations are the deli implementing the five S''s. [V.D]"},"revision":"2f8dd52dcfda09d7b00351c474495a6e6b75af29fed1a89f8afa5ceaaaf504aa"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-217":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"waste.\",\"standardized work.\",\"visual control.\",\"kanban.\"],\"stem\":\"A factory uses an illuminated indicator light when a certain process is in progress. This is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"A visible signal like a light is an example of visual control, cueing employees that an action is being performed and helping create a safer or more efficient workplace. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["waste.","standardized work.","visual control.","kanban."],"stem":"A factory uses an illuminated indicator light when a certain process is in progress. This is an example of:","sub":"ci","why":"A visible signal like a light is an example of visual control, cueing employees that an action is being performed and helping create a safer or more efficient workplace. [V.D]"},"revision":"ecc402420b9110be70fc2886db23c36b41d7e54effcd40b21326e7b89048f12c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-218":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Kaizen\",\"5S\",\"TQM\",\"Kanban\"],\"stem\":\"In a convenience store, items are stacked in front of a card that lists details on the product, including the location of additional boxes in their storage room as well as a number to call to reorder. What type of system has this convenience store implemented?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"A kanban system uses visual cues to trigger resupply; when a bin empties, the card alerts the employee where to find or how to order more supplies. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Kaizen","5S","TQM","Kanban"],"stem":"In a convenience store, items are stacked in front of a card that lists details on the product, including the location of additional boxes in their storage room as well as a number to call to reorder. What type of system has this convenience store implemented?","sub":"ci","why":"A kanban system uses visual cues to trigger resupply; when a bin empties, the card alerts the employee where to find or how to order more supplies. [V.D]"},"revision":"acb4e425e20cbe6abfceb93b3fc1a08128cfab31bab009da9227785f8d1d89ae"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-219":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Excess movement of material\",\"Defect correction\",\"Excess processing\",\"Excess motion\"],\"stem\":\"To complete a process, parts must travel through four departments located in different areas of a manufacturing facility. As what type of waste would a quality team at the facility classify this process?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Parts traveling through departments spread across a facility is waste due to excess movement of material; grouping assembly processes together would reduce the distance components travel. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Excess movement of material","Defect correction","Excess processing","Excess motion"],"stem":"To complete a process, parts must travel through four departments located in different areas of a manufacturing facility. As what type of waste would a quality team at the facility classify this process?","sub":"ci","why":"Parts traveling through departments spread across a facility is waste due to excess movement of material; grouping assembly processes together would reduce the distance components travel. [V.D]"},"revision":"f5d1a09a18f637c0448d408c878f00e92bc0c7f01d6f925fdc3b243c5a9bde2f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-22":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"suppliers.\",\"customers.\",\"top management.\",\"the chief quality officer.\"],\"stem\":\"Once a quality manual is complete, a final review is in order. The reviewed copy should be endorsed and authorized for release by:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Top management is responsible for endorsing the content of the final document as well as authorizing its release for use. [II.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["suppliers.","customers.","top management.","the chief quality officer."],"stem":"Once a quality manual is complete, a final review is in order. The reviewed copy should be endorsed and authorized for release by:","sub":"qsys","why":"Top management is responsible for endorsing the content of the final document as well as authorizing its release for use. [II.B.2]"},"revision":"151d6712d27608b31fc524b62fad24e118fc50252e3f740a7423a925182d5379"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-220":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Kanban\",\"Visual control\",\"5S\",\"Kaizen\"],\"stem\":\"A manufacturing facility has painted arrows on the floor to show the flow of products through the facility. Which lean tool has this facility implemented?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Painted arrows showing product flow are an example of visual control, giving employees quick visual cues that help movement and reduce errors or accidents. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Kanban","Visual control","5S","Kaizen"],"stem":"A manufacturing facility has painted arrows on the floor to show the flow of products through the facility. Which lean tool has this facility implemented?","sub":"ci","why":"Painted arrows showing product flow are an example of visual control, giving employees quick visual cues that help movement and reduce errors or accidents. [V.D]"},"revision":"e9fbf6b2fd3fbd1f2c32894f241939ea162f40834675afa7416ca03753982a02"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-221":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"kanban.\",\"5S.\",\"standardized work.\",\"pull system.\"],\"stem\":\"The principle that each process should be done the same way, every time, by every employee is called:\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Standardized work means every step or activity is done the same way every time, reducing process variation and producing a more consistent product or service. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["kanban.","5S.","standardized work.","pull system."],"stem":"The principle that each process should be done the same way, every time, by every employee is called:","sub":"ci","why":"Standardized work means every step or activity is done the same way every time, reducing process variation and producing a more consistent product or service. [V.D]"},"revision":"cc6af61e893423fe48f64b4e6f327f552e7900a1a83a0d4a4d753fd0e1d33e0a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-222":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Takt time\",\"Single-minute exchange of die (SMED)\",\"Cycle time\",\"5S\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following lean tools measures the time from the last good part of one type to the first good part of the next?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Single-minute exchange of die (SMED) reduces machine changeover times, cutting the time required to switch a machine or assembly line to a different product. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Takt time","Single-minute exchange of die (SMED)","Cycle time","5S"],"stem":"Which of the following lean tools measures the time from the last good part of one type to the first good part of the next?","sub":"ci","why":"Single-minute exchange of die (SMED) reduces machine changeover times, cutting the time required to switch a machine or assembly line to a different product. [V.D]"},"revision":"e4df1131a7e10b68db9e2f5c33198b1e342cb61a99aa136c5aefcd81b5385a0a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-223":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Cycle time incorporates data from suppliers; takt time does not.\",\"Takt time is based on customer demand; cycle time measures the time to complete an activity.\",\"Takt time measures the time to complete an activity; cycle time incorporates customer demand.\",\"There is no difference; they both measure the same time.\"],\"stem\":\"What is the difference between cycle time and takt time?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Cycle time is the time it takes to complete an activity or process step. Takt time is based on customer demand: Takt time = (Time available) / (Units required), the average time a process should take per unit to meet demand. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Cycle time incorporates data from suppliers; takt time does not.","Takt time is based on customer demand; cycle time measures the time to complete an activity.","Takt time measures the time to complete an activity; cycle time incorporates customer demand.","There is no difference; they both measure the same time."],"stem":"What is the difference between cycle time and takt time?","sub":"ci","why":"Cycle time is the time it takes to complete an activity or process step. Takt time is based on customer demand: Takt time = (Time available) / (Units required), the average time a process should take per unit to meet demand. [V.D]"},"revision":"5d517e7d7f6a875bcc30394df131d7dba0716063eddab2edeb6d14b272db36f6"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-224":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Inventory\",\"Overproduction\",\"Excess movement of material\",\"Excess processing\"],\"stem\":\"A shoe company routinely makes more components than are needed when assembling its product. As what type of waste might this company classify this action?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Routinely making more components than needed is waste due to overproduction; the company should adjust production of this component to match actual need. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Inventory","Overproduction","Excess movement of material","Excess processing"],"stem":"A shoe company routinely makes more components than are needed when assembling its product. As what type of waste might this company classify this action?","sub":"ci","why":"Routinely making more components than needed is waste due to overproduction; the company should adjust production of this component to match actual need. [V.D]"},"revision":"4844220cd42a09be58f133edab9cb7fbe3cc43680b4fbac9a6b7f20be0f17c20"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-225":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Standardized work\",\"5S\",\"Visual control\",\"Value stream map\"],\"stem\":\"A university has released a document detailing all the required actions instructors should take when proctoring an exam. All instructors across the university are expected to follow these directions. What tool has the university implemented?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"A document requiring all instructors to follow the same exam procedure every time is standardized work, intended to make the process run more smoothly and consistently across the university. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Standardized work","5S","Visual control","Value stream map"],"stem":"A university has released a document detailing all the required actions instructors should take when proctoring an exam. All instructors across the university are expected to follow these directions. What tool has the university implemented?","sub":"ci","why":"A document requiring all instructors to follow the same exam procedure every time is standardized work, intended to make the process run more smoothly and consistently across the university. [V.D]"},"revision":"74f52519ea421d84b4eabad8c08876672c981e45d92c6f7c0349e52a4fc28fda"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-226":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.87 seconds\",\"52.36 seconds\",\"68.75 seconds\",\"550 seconds\"],\"stem\":\"A company has received an order for 550 items that it needs to produce in an eight-hour workday. In seconds, what is the takt time for this product?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Takt time = (Time available) / (Units required). Time available is 8 x 60 x 60 = 28,800 seconds, so takt time = 28,800 / 550 = 52.36 seconds. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.87 seconds","52.36 seconds","68.75 seconds","550 seconds"],"stem":"A company has received an order for 550 items that it needs to produce in an eight-hour workday. In seconds, what is the takt time for this product?","sub":"ci","why":"Takt time = (Time available) / (Units required). Time available is 8 x 60 x 60 = 28,800 seconds, so takt time = 28,800 / 550 = 52.36 seconds. [V.D]"},"revision":"5347c42f3a341539ea7cee411d2ab114676e144d45bf52b8bb458c8f610c6d65"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-227":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Cycle time ≤ Takt time\",\"Cycle time > Takt time\",\"Cycle time = Takt time\",\"Not enough information to determine\"],\"stem\":\"A caterer had a lunch order for a group of 50 people and had two hours to complete the order. However, after two hours, the caterer was only able to complete 45 lunches. Which of the following must be true?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"The caterer''s takt time is 2 x 60 / 50 = 2.4 minutes per lunch. Since only 45 of 50 lunches were completed in two hours, the actual cycle time must have been greater than the takt time. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Cycle time ≤ Takt time","Cycle time > Takt time","Cycle time = Takt time","Not enough information to determine"],"stem":"A caterer had a lunch order for a group of 50 people and had two hours to complete the order. However, after two hours, the caterer was only able to complete 45 lunches. Which of the following must be true?","sub":"ci","why":"The caterer''s takt time is 2 x 60 / 50 = 2.4 minutes per lunch. Since only 45 of 50 lunches were completed in two hours, the actual cycle time must have been greater than the takt time. [V.D]"},"revision":"067bdc0a1690146d1241f11aa0f5726b493ee31462b49826a1190e0eaddfb580"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-228":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Cycle time\",\"5S\",\"SMED\",\"Kanban\"],\"stem\":\"A radiation clinic treats patients with different types of cancer. The position of each patient on the table is different depending on the type of cancer. Therefore, after one patient is treated, another patient, requiring a different setup than the previous patient, arrives. Which lean tool would best help a quality team in this clinic?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"SMED reduces changeover times; although introduced in a factory setting, the same methodology applies to the clinic''s required changeover time between patients needing different table setups. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Cycle time","5S","SMED","Kanban"],"stem":"A radiation clinic treats patients with different types of cancer. The position of each patient on the table is different depending on the type of cancer. Therefore, after one patient is treated, another patient, requiring a different setup than the previous patient, arrives. Which lean tool would best help a quality team in this clinic?","sub":"ci","why":"SMED reduces changeover times; although introduced in a factory setting, the same methodology applies to the clinic''s required changeover time between patients needing different table setups. [V.D]"},"revision":"ebee8690e8d53d36d690a95350ce56cf7fe7b70fc30559b164abee8f196157f6"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-229":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"No, since cycle time ≤ takt time.\",\"Yes, since cycle time ≤ takt time.\",\"No, since cycle time > takt time.\",\"Yes, since cycle time > takt time.\"],\"stem\":\"A supplier has received an order from one of its customers. It must complete an order of 750 items in a daytime shift that is 10 hours long. The cycle time for this process is 50 seconds. Without any changes in its current production, will the supplier be able to meet the customer’s demands?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"The supplier''s takt time is 10 x 60 x 60 / 750 = 48 seconds. Because the cycle time (50 seconds) is greater than the takt time, the supplier is unlikely to meet customer demand without production adjustments. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["No, since cycle time ≤ takt time.","Yes, since cycle time ≤ takt time.","No, since cycle time > takt time.","Yes, since cycle time > takt time."],"stem":"A supplier has received an order from one of its customers. It must complete an order of 750 items in a daytime shift that is 10 hours long. The cycle time for this process is 50 seconds. Without any changes in its current production, will the supplier be able to meet the customer’s demands?","sub":"ci","why":"The supplier''s takt time is 10 x 60 x 60 / 750 = 48 seconds. Because the cycle time (50 seconds) is greater than the takt time, the supplier is unlikely to meet customer demand without production adjustments. [V.D]"},"revision":"4d02c7d90d4b90202c12676a39ee59242078e4eb4d02c6ee6cd50e3f5c99ddac"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-23":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"A process is in place for distribution of revisions and removal of obsolete documents.\",\"A process is in place for making the documents readily available to the general public.\",\"Documents are approved by top management.\",\"Hard copies of documents must be maintained.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is a requirement for maintaining document control?\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Revision control and ensuring obsolete documents are not available for use are important principles of document control. [II.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["A process is in place for distribution of revisions and removal of obsolete documents.","A process is in place for making the documents readily available to the general public.","Documents are approved by top management.","Hard copies of documents must be maintained."],"stem":"Which of the following is a requirement for maintaining document control?","sub":"qsys","why":"Revision control and ensuring obsolete documents are not available for use are important principles of document control. [II.B.2]"},"revision":"a72890364cbf0904d25bba4a0f7c02e34214b0d200a613e95556047362570424"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-230":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Breakdown loss\",\"Process defect\",\"Yield loss\",\"Idling and minor stoppage loss\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is a speed loss in OEE?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"The six big losses in OEE include two speed losses: idling and minor stoppage, and reduced speed. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Breakdown loss","Process defect","Yield loss","Idling and minor stoppage loss"],"stem":"Which of the following is a speed loss in OEE?","sub":"ci","why":"The six big losses in OEE include two speed losses: idling and minor stoppage, and reduced speed. [V.D]"},"revision":"161219cfd8481c65d7595755c929ff4f1bc2bc6048abac64234e2f1de961333f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-231":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"82%\",\"90%\",\"91%\",\"95%\"],\"stem\":\"If an eight-hour shift corresponds to 480 minutes, what is the availability of a machine with the following information: • There are two breaks of 15 minutes each. • The machine broke down for 35 minutes. • The adjustment time was 10 minutes.\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Loading time = 480 - 15 - 15 = 450 minutes. Unplanned downtime = 35 + 10 = 45 minutes. Availability = (Loading Time - Unplanned Downtime) / Loading Time x 100 = (450-45)/450 x 100 = 90%. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["82%","90%","91%","95%"],"stem":"If an eight-hour shift corresponds to 480 minutes, what is the availability of a machine with the following information: • There are two breaks of 15 minutes each. • The machine broke down for 35 minutes. • The adjustment time was 10 minutes.","sub":"ci","why":"Loading time = 480 - 15 - 15 = 450 minutes. Unplanned downtime = 35 + 10 = 45 minutes. Availability = (Loading Time - Unplanned Downtime) / Loading Time x 100 = (450-45)/450 x 100 = 90%. [V.D]"},"revision":"c89f82a8180c3dc33bc19dfcfe06f0ca6276cb7362c94e5362f3f9f58c5020fc"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-232":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Poor definition of the problem\",\"Multifunctional teams\",\"Selecting projects that require the least amount of effort\",\"The project charter\"],\"stem\":\"What is one of the most common causes of failures in problem solving?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"One of the most frequent sources of failure in problem-solving is a poor definition of the problem; problem statements should be clear, focused, fact-based, and establish what is known, unknown, and needs to be done. [V.E]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Poor definition of the problem","Multifunctional teams","Selecting projects that require the least amount of effort","The project charter"],"stem":"What is one of the most common causes of failures in problem solving?","sub":"ci","why":"One of the most frequent sources of failure in problem-solving is a poor definition of the problem; problem statements should be clear, focused, fact-based, and establish what is known, unknown, and needs to be done. [V.E]"},"revision":"5fabdb7df40725237ac5a492a379f0f0d18f7e8fd8da7506654e5f674de8ccab"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-233":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"A clear problem statement\",\"Team meetings\",\"Creativity\",\"Cause-and-effect diagram\"],\"stem\":\"During the correction phase of the corrective action cycle, which of the following is instrumental in success?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"In the correction phase of the corrective action cycle, an optimal solution is developed to solve the problem, and creativity is essential to doing this well. [V.E]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["A clear problem statement","Team meetings","Creativity","Cause-and-effect diagram"],"stem":"During the correction phase of the corrective action cycle, which of the following is instrumental in success?","sub":"ci","why":"In the correction phase of the corrective action cycle, an optimal solution is developed to solve the problem, and creativity is essential to doing this well. [V.E]"},"revision":"d96a53cc50e812e0df22b2077f53e6cc0eef4cbbba71e525aa1025027ac1a876"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-234":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Identify key metrics in the process to monitor\",\"Ensure management can perform each task in the process\",\"Remove process certification for operators\",\"Set production quotas\"],\"stem\":\"A team in a food production plant has recently instituted changes to one of their bottling processes. What should the team do to avoid the process backsliding?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"The recurrence control phase involves standardizing the solution and identifying the key variables that need to be kept under control to avoid backsliding. [V.E]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Identify key metrics in the process to monitor","Ensure management can perform each task in the process","Remove process certification for operators","Set production quotas"],"stem":"A team in a food production plant has recently instituted changes to one of their bottling processes. What should the team do to avoid the process backsliding?","sub":"ci","why":"The recurrence control phase involves standardizing the solution and identifying the key variables that need to be kept under control to avoid backsliding. [V.E]"},"revision":"5e074ed66270eb44ab0de9c42cd718b44af58d52792cc515039a3c7f121bb150"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-235":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Problem identification\",\"Correction\",\"Recurrence control\",\"Effectiveness assessment\"],\"stem\":\"In which phase of the corrective action cycle does a team seek to continuously improve the performance of a process by holding regular reviews of the process?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"In the effectiveness assessment phase of the corrective action cycle, the team continues monitoring the process through regular reviews to identify additional opportunities for improvement. [V.E]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Problem identification","Correction","Recurrence control","Effectiveness assessment"],"stem":"In which phase of the corrective action cycle does a team seek to continuously improve the performance of a process by holding regular reviews of the process?","sub":"ci","why":"In the effectiveness assessment phase of the corrective action cycle, the team continues monitoring the process through regular reviews to identify additional opportunities for improvement. [V.E]"},"revision":"bd8c7adcafdd273655fdcb0b89683f88e64a61846518eb427f3821cc7ca9ff92"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-236":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"the fifth “Why” has been completed.\",\"the root cause has been identified.\",\"the cause-and-effect diagram has been completed.\",\"the brainstorming session has been completed.\"],\"stem\":\"A 5 Why Analysis is complete when:\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"A 5 Why Analysis loops back until the team agrees the problem''s root cause has been identified; \\\"5\\\" is just a typical number, and the process continues until the root cause is revealed. [V.E]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["the fifth “Why” has been completed.","the root cause has been identified.","the cause-and-effect diagram has been completed.","the brainstorming session has been completed."],"stem":"A 5 Why Analysis is complete when:","sub":"ci","why":"A 5 Why Analysis loops back until the team agrees the problem''s root cause has been identified; \"5\" is just a typical number, and the process continues until the root cause is revealed. [V.E]"},"revision":"bef4681a9463a3ff2c375cdba247b5ec25cda8913730e0a357a67a1037495a9c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-237":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Design engineer\",\"Calibration technician\",\"Purchasing representative\",\"Production operator\"],\"stem\":\"When utilizing a 5 Why Analysis to solve a manufacturing problem, which employee would be best suited to have firsthand knowledge of the system in place?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"The production operator, as a member of the manufacturing department, is closest to the process and has the most firsthand knowledge of the systems in place. [V.E]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Design engineer","Calibration technician","Purchasing representative","Production operator"],"stem":"When utilizing a 5 Why Analysis to solve a manufacturing problem, which employee would be best suited to have firsthand knowledge of the system in place?","sub":"ci","why":"The production operator, as a member of the manufacturing department, is closest to the process and has the most firsthand knowledge of the systems in place. [V.E]"},"revision":"9246b5a213500b6140d6d5ec39c0b38f5a5baf26b8d40968c595ffd4e1396c41"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-238":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Waste\",\"Error-proofing\",\"Orderliness\",\"Pull system\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is another name for poka-yoke?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Poka-yoke, from Japan, is also known as error-proofing, a preventive technique used to prevent errors from occurring. [V.F]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Waste","Error-proofing","Orderliness","Pull system"],"stem":"Which of the following is another name for poka-yoke?","sub":"ci","why":"Poka-yoke, from Japan, is also known as error-proofing, a preventive technique used to prevent errors from occurring. [V.F]"},"revision":"ab707c1ce294cbc45e8559305b56fd1e9bc996d180a11558d5e5fc2b4b17b9f5"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-239":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"poka-yoke.\",\"reengineering.\",\"robust design.\",\"kaizen.\"],\"stem\":\"A food company produces cookie dough. All a customer must do is slice the cookie dough and bake it in their oven for approximately eight minutes. The company’s cookie dough recipe was developed so that the cookie quality is not vastly affected by differences in ovens, elevation, or other environmental conditions. This cookie dough is an example of\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"A recipe whose quality isn''t greatly affected by differing use conditions (oven, elevation) is an example of robust design, which reduces variation in quality under various use conditions. [V.F]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["poka-yoke.","reengineering.","robust design.","kaizen."],"stem":"A food company produces cookie dough. All a customer must do is slice the cookie dough and bake it in their oven for approximately eight minutes. The company’s cookie dough recipe was developed so that the cookie quality is not vastly affected by differences in ovens, elevation, or other environmental conditions. This cookie dough is an example of","sub":"ci","why":"A recipe whose quality isn''t greatly affected by differing use conditions (oven, elevation) is an example of robust design, which reduces variation in quality under various use conditions. [V.F]"},"revision":"8a61cf740d426dce6a262cb7c8f316784a847dbe0388291a1ec11f2ed227926d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-24":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"a national standard.\",\"an international standard.\",\"an industry-specific standard.\",\"a guidance document.\"],\"stem\":\"ISO 9001:2015 is:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"ISO 9001:2015 is an international standard. [II.C]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["a national standard.","an international standard.","an industry-specific standard.","a guidance document."],"stem":"ISO 9001:2015 is:","sub":"qsys","why":"ISO 9001:2015 is an international standard. [II.C]"},"revision":"768140baf67a1c5113d09360b1dbb286ac3779887a0569a999d7685258741b88"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-240":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Robust design\",\"Poka-yoke\",\"Lean process\",\"Kaizen\"],\"stem\":\"A printer that will only run when the paper trays are closed is an example of which of the following concepts?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"A printer that won''t run with an open paper tray is poka-yoke, or error-proofing, preventing paper jams or other errors before they occur. [V.F]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Robust design","Poka-yoke","Lean process","Kaizen"],"stem":"A printer that will only run when the paper trays are closed is an example of which of the following concepts?","sub":"ci","why":"A printer that won''t run with an open paper tray is poka-yoke, or error-proofing, preventing paper jams or other errors before they occur. [V.F]"},"revision":"afd4f39e363428554a4a8689b315a9b45fcbc0cfdd402f38da9c3217d42c35ff"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-241":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Elimination\",\"Replacement\",\"Facilitation\",\"Detection\"],\"stem\":\"Before the next step in a process, a sorting machine is used to remove components that are above the upper specification limit. What principle of error- proofing is implemented in this process?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Detection is the error-proofing principle where a defect is caught before it moves to the next process step; the sorting machine removing out-of-spec components before the next step is detection. [V.F]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Elimination","Replacement","Facilitation","Detection"],"stem":"Before the next step in a process, a sorting machine is used to remove components that are above the upper specification limit. What principle of error- proofing is implemented in this process?","sub":"ci","why":"Detection is the error-proofing principle where a defect is caught before it moves to the next process step; the sorting machine removing out-of-spec components before the next step is detection. [V.F]"},"revision":"d813caecc07143b3646fe1b6b6987b9f94deb1bea077d194962529436f188ab3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-242":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"specification phase.\",\"prototype phase.\",\"concept phase.\",\"distribution phase.\"],\"stem\":\"Consider the product/process development life cycle. The phase in which all possible solutions are explored is referred to as the:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"In the concept phase, all possible solutions are explored; solutions that are not feasible are eliminated. [III.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["specification phase.","prototype phase.","concept phase.","distribution phase."],"stem":"Consider the product/process development life cycle. The phase in which all possible solutions are explored is referred to as the:","sub":"design","why":"In the concept phase, all possible solutions are explored; solutions that are not feasible are eliminated. [III.B.1]"},"revision":"4ca4a98251bb7a5a58e9c0e163dc4541b48eb3beb79a4d1a4e4ca57e7c596c5d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-243":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"quality function deployment.\",\"flowcharts.\",\"PERT.\",\"CPM.\"],\"stem\":\"“House of quality” is a key diagram in:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The house of quality is a key diagram in quality function deployment (QFD), used to incorporate the key relationships between customer needs and design characteristics. [III.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["quality function deployment.","flowcharts.","PERT.","CPM."],"stem":"“House of quality” is a key diagram in:","sub":"design","why":"The house of quality is a key diagram in quality function deployment (QFD), used to incorporate the key relationships between customer needs and design characteristics. [III.B.2]"},"revision":"677b2be03310cdd86dd4f6c28d6c0293af429437dd4382b3d26894e001ea490a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-244":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"primary datum.\",\"geometric feature or characteristic.\",\"geometric tolerance.\",\"modifier.\"],\"stem\":\"Consider a feature control frame that reads: perpendicularity, 0.01, datums A, B, C. The value 0.01 represents the:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"0.01 in the feature control frame represents the geometric tolerance for the feature on the part. [III.C]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["primary datum.","geometric feature or characteristic.","geometric tolerance.","modifier."],"stem":"Consider a feature control frame that reads: perpendicularity, 0.01, datums A, B, C. The value 0.01 represents the:","sub":"design","why":"0.01 in the feature control frame represents the geometric tolerance for the feature on the part. [III.C]"},"revision":"4385e911fe7c3d540178b9b869bf46841df348dca5a3d0f0d664b810f919c8f2"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-245":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"primary datum.\",\"secondary datum.\",\"tertiary datum.\",\"modifier.\"],\"stem\":\"Consider a feature control frame that reads: perpendicularity, 0.01, datums A, B, C. “A” represents the:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"\\\"A\\\" represents the primary datum. [III.C]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["primary datum.","secondary datum.","tertiary datum.","modifier."],"stem":"Consider a feature control frame that reads: perpendicularity, 0.01, datums A, B, C. “A” represents the:","sub":"design","why":"\"A\" represents the primary datum. [III.C]"},"revision":"a32a5091424eab564bdad7a7e4f3459285f78909f42f68e4d8f6c921126bc727"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-246":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"0.0004\",\"0.0156\",\"0.0511\",\"0.9489\"],\"stem\":\"450 electrical components for a machine were subjected to a reliability test. The observed failures during 100-hour intervals: 0-100 hrs, 7 failures; 100-200 hrs, 16 failures; 200-300 hrs, 19 failures; 300-400 hrs, 27 failures. What is the estimated reliability at time t = 200?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The estimated reliability at time t = 200 is R̂(t) = n2/N = 427/450 = 0.9489, where n2 = 450 - 16 - 7 = 427 is the number of units that have not yet failed by 200 hours. [III.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["0.0004","0.0156","0.0511","0.9489"],"stem":"450 electrical components for a machine were subjected to a reliability test. The observed failures during 100-hour intervals: 0-100 hrs, 7 failures; 100-200 hrs, 16 failures; 200-300 hrs, 19 failures; 300-400 hrs, 27 failures. What is the estimated reliability at time t = 200?","sub":"design","why":"The estimated reliability at time t = 200 is R̂(t) = n2/N = 427/450 = 0.9489, where n2 = 450 - 16 - 7 = 427 is the number of units that have not yet failed by 200 hours. [III.E.1]"},"revision":"373e04cdd63f48f29511837b3c8c7da58764c631b9c38f2ed7db8d884b07971d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-247":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"0.0006\",\"0.0933\",\"0.8467\",\"0.9067\"],\"stem\":\"Using the same 450-component reliability test (100-hour interval failure counts: 0-100 hrs 7, 100-200 hrs 16, 200-300 hrs 19, 300-400 hrs 27), what is the estimated failure density for time 300 < t < 400?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The failure density is f̂(t) = (n3 - n4)/((t4 - t3) x N) = (408-381)/((400-300) x 450) = 0.0006, using the number of survivors at 300 hours (408) and 400 hours (381). [III.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["0.0006","0.0933","0.8467","0.9067"],"stem":"Using the same 450-component reliability test (100-hour interval failure counts: 0-100 hrs 7, 100-200 hrs 16, 200-300 hrs 19, 300-400 hrs 27), what is the estimated failure density for time 300 < t < 400?","sub":"design","why":"The failure density is f̂(t) = (n3 - n4)/((t4 - t3) x N) = (408-381)/((400-300) x 450) = 0.0006, using the number of survivors at 300 hours (408) and 400 hours (381). [III.E.1]"},"revision":"74859789e4ec97fc320e7f28f3e7565d15fbc4530fbad13306893e979a9e6bcb"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-248":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"0.00066\",\"0.09333\",\"0.15333\",\"0.90667\"],\"stem\":\"Using the same 450-component reliability test (100-hour interval failure counts: 0-100 hrs 7, 100-200 hrs 16, 200-300 hrs 19, 300-400 hrs 27), what is the instantaneous rate of failure for time 300 < t < 400?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The instantaneous rate of failure is ĥ(t) = f̂(t)/R̂(t) = (n3-n4)/((t4-t3) x n3) = (408-381)/((400-300) x 408) = 0.00066. [III.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["0.00066","0.09333","0.15333","0.90667"],"stem":"Using the same 450-component reliability test (100-hour interval failure counts: 0-100 hrs 7, 100-200 hrs 16, 200-300 hrs 19, 300-400 hrs 27), what is the instantaneous rate of failure for time 300 < t < 400?","sub":"design","why":"The instantaneous rate of failure is ĥ(t) = f̂(t)/R̂(t) = (n3-n4)/((t4-t3) x n3) = (408-381)/((400-300) x 408) = 0.00066. [III.E.1]"},"revision":"1a90647348f6a12c52fa0fb540bbd7f0a3af4304a5067bc68f9ff168dfe2e7a3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-249":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.1990\",\"0.9752\",\"0.9950\",\"0.9999\"],\"stem\":\"A series system is composed of five subsystems, each with reliability 0.995. What is the reliability of the system?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"A series system''s reliability is the product of its components'' reliabilities: Rs(t) = 0.995^5 = 0.9752. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.1990","0.9752","0.9950","0.9999"],"stem":"A series system is composed of five subsystems, each with reliability 0.995. What is the reliability of the system?","sub":"design","why":"A series system''s reliability is the product of its components'' reliabilities: Rs(t) = 0.995^5 = 0.9752. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"3b15d213b301bf6333ad47a98a9a0d2ceefcdcb43c53612416370ba7e051bf0b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-25":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Environmental management systems\",\"Continual improvement\",\"Risk management\",\"Quality management\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is the subject of one of the standards in the ISO 9000 Systems family?\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Quality management is one of the standards, while the others are subjects of other standards (environmental management, risk management) or a quality management principle (continuous improvement). [II.C]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Environmental management systems","Continual improvement","Risk management","Quality management"],"stem":"Which of the following is the subject of one of the standards in the ISO 9000 Systems family?","sub":"qsys","why":"Quality management is one of the standards, while the others are subjects of other standards (environmental management, risk management) or a quality management principle (continuous improvement). [II.C]"},"revision":"fba45899c025648ff6264ee2731678001d9de99b7ad596717db4dbbe8b2d821e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-250":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"0.9500\",\"0.9752\",\"0.9950\",\"1.0000\"],\"stem\":\"A parallel system is composed of five subsystems, each with reliability 0.995. The reliability of this system is closest to which of the following values?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"A parallel system fails only when all units fail: Rs(t) = 1 - [(1-0.995)]^5 = 0.999999, essentially 1. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["0.9500","0.9752","0.9950","1.0000"],"stem":"A parallel system is composed of five subsystems, each with reliability 0.995. The reliability of this system is closest to which of the following values?","sub":"design","why":"A parallel system fails only when all units fail: Rs(t) = 1 - [(1-0.995)]^5 = 0.999999, essentially 1. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"ca3bf9516d43ef2b686df814ad7809393abd5c8f3896637d72d4ec6d608ce7ea"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-251":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"0\",\"0.25\",\"0.75\",\"1.00\"],\"stem\":\"In a series system consisting of three subsystems, each with a reliability of 0.75, if one subsystem fails, what is the probability that the entire system will fail?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"A series system is made up of n components connected in series, so the system fails if any one of the components or subsystems fails. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["0","0.25","0.75","1.00"],"stem":"In a series system consisting of three subsystems, each with a reliability of 0.75, if one subsystem fails, what is the probability that the entire system will fail?","sub":"design","why":"A series system is made up of n components connected in series, so the system fails if any one of the components or subsystems fails. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"a6661b419dfd1ccf172638a8f8430e5f8c803501a6af840782d9afe407db535b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-252":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0\",\"0.36\",\"0.64\",\"0.80\"],\"stem\":\"What is the reliability of a series system with two subsystems, each with a reliability of 0.80?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Series-system reliability is the product of the subsystem reliabilities: Rs(t) = 0.80 x 0.80 = 0.64. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0","0.36","0.64","0.80"],"stem":"What is the reliability of a series system with two subsystems, each with a reliability of 0.80?","sub":"design","why":"Series-system reliability is the product of the subsystem reliabilities: Rs(t) = 0.80 x 0.80 = 0.64. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"b2200b7858f70a8672dfda9ebe5058b30288f49535eab82822c4cfcda669129c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-253":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.4644\",\"0.5997\",\"0.9975\",\"0.9998\"],\"stem\":\"A machine has six subsystems and will fail when four or more of these subsystems fail. Each subsystem has a reliability of 0.88. What is the reliability of the machine?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"This is a k-out-of-n system with k=3, n=6, p=0.88. Summing the binomial terms for 3, 4, 5, and 6 subsystems surviving gives Rs(t) = 0.02355 + 0.1295 + 0.3799 + 0.4644 = 0.9975. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.4644","0.5997","0.9975","0.9998"],"stem":"A machine has six subsystems and will fail when four or more of these subsystems fail. Each subsystem has a reliability of 0.88. What is the reliability of the machine?","sub":"design","why":"This is a k-out-of-n system with k=3, n=6, p=0.88. Summing the binomial terms for 3, 4, 5, and 6 subsystems surviving gives Rs(t) = 0.02355 + 0.1295 + 0.3799 + 0.4644 = 0.9975. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"9ac39016c1b93c84d3db7226d0eeca31e6b2ea4703ace73fac41c67bb5e2384a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-254":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.0001\",\"0.0424\",\"0.7164\",\"0.9999\"],\"stem\":\"A machine has a standby system design with three identical components in standby mode. Each component has a constant rate of failure λ = 0.08. What is the reliability of the machine at 100 hours of continuous operation?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"For a standby system with n=4 standby components (original plus 3 standby), λ=0.08, t=100: Rs(t) = e^-8 x Σ(8^i/i!) for i=0 to 3 ≈ 0.0424. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.0001","0.0424","0.7164","0.9999"],"stem":"A machine has a standby system design with three identical components in standby mode. Each component has a constant rate of failure λ = 0.08. What is the reliability of the machine at 100 hours of continuous operation?","sub":"design","why":"For a standby system with n=4 standby components (original plus 3 standby), λ=0.08, t=100: Rs(t) = e^-8 x Σ(8^i/i!) for i=0 to 3 ≈ 0.0424. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"3ecd4e2a4867c3690d0281cf87f099f662e0a65b242003936cc5276f25638d71"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-255":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"0.0075\",\"0.033\",\"27\",\"133\"],\"stem\":\"750 machines were tested for five hours. 28 failures occurred. What is the failure rate for these machines?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The failure rate λ = failures / total test time = 28/(750 x 5) = 0.0075. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["0.0075","0.033","27","133"],"stem":"750 machines were tested for five hours. 28 failures occurred. What is the failure rate for these machines?","sub":"design","why":"The failure rate λ = failures / total test time = 28/(750 x 5) = 0.0075. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"b027002a26d1227b8be3aead43cad3ff8322412c37cbcab466e92eef6d2f7a68"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-256":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"0.0011\",\"0.355\",\"3\",\"910\"],\"stem\":\"The reliability of a component at t = 400 hours is 0.645. Assuming that the reliability of this component is exponential, which of the following is closest to the mean time to failure (MTTF)?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Given R(400)=0.645 and R(t)=e^-λt, solving -400λ=ln(0.645) gives λ≈0.0011, so MTTF = 1/λ ≈ 909, or about 910 hours. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["0.0011","0.355","3","910"],"stem":"The reliability of a component at t = 400 hours is 0.645. Assuming that the reliability of this component is exponential, which of the following is closest to the mean time to failure (MTTF)?","sub":"design","why":"Given R(400)=0.645 and R(t)=e^-λt, solving -400λ=ln(0.645) gives λ≈0.0011, so MTTF = 1/λ ≈ 909, or about 910 hours. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"87f1ef96d20c714687d9ac458d310685671a303fbe17c03b4aaf307a75c6ae03"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-257":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.009\",\"0.368\",\"0.632\",\"2.718\"],\"stem\":\"The MTTF for a model of dishwasher is 115 months. What is the reliability that a randomly selected dishwasher of this model type survives past 115 months? Assume that the reliability function is an exponential distribution.\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"MTTF = 1/failure rate = 115 months, so λ = 1/115. Since reliability is exponential, R(115) = e^-115/115 = e^-1 = 0.3679. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.009","0.368","0.632","2.718"],"stem":"The MTTF for a model of dishwasher is 115 months. What is the reliability that a randomly selected dishwasher of this model type survives past 115 months? Assume that the reliability function is an exponential distribution.","sub":"design","why":"MTTF = 1/failure rate = 115 months, so λ = 1/115. Since reliability is exponential, R(115) = e^-115/115 = e^-1 = 0.3679. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"a4de616edee70e4fc8ff9cedc431c70e88f8574d6a82e67af8dd888779e3065a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-258":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.19%\",\"14.88%\",\"85.12%\",\"99.81%\"],\"stem\":\"The MTTF for a machine is 525 hours. What fraction of machines survives past 1,000 hours? Assume that the reliability of the machine has an exponential distribution.\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"With MTTF = 525 and λ = 1/525, R(1000) = e^-1000/525 ≈ 0.1488, so approximately 15% of machines survive past 1,000 hours. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.19%","14.88%","85.12%","99.81%"],"stem":"The MTTF for a machine is 525 hours. What fraction of machines survives past 1,000 hours? Assume that the reliability of the machine has an exponential distribution.","sub":"design","why":"With MTTF = 525 and λ = 1/525, R(1000) = e^-1000/525 ≈ 0.1488, so approximately 15% of machines survive past 1,000 hours. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"cfea0e8f1a3532af0d63e48ecc653a983d9dabdc555f75abd7502634794bdedb"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-259":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.1667\",\"0.3012\",\"0.6988\",\"0.8333\"],\"stem\":\"What is the probability a randomly selected rechargeable battery fails by six hours if the mean time between failures (MTBF) is five hours? Assume that the reliability of the component is exponentially distributed.\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"With MTBF = 5 hours, λ = 1/5 = 0.2. The probability the battery fails by time t is F(t) = 1 - R(t) = 1 - e^-λt, so F(6) = 1 - e^(-6/5) ≈ 0.6988. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.1667","0.3012","0.6988","0.8333"],"stem":"What is the probability a randomly selected rechargeable battery fails by six hours if the mean time between failures (MTBF) is five hours? Assume that the reliability of the component is exponentially distributed.","sub":"design","why":"With MTBF = 5 hours, λ = 1/5 = 0.2. The probability the battery fails by time t is F(t) = 1 - R(t) = 1 - e^-λt, so F(6) = 1 - e^(-6/5) ≈ 0.6988. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"51081cfcc2e66d0092b27d3c546d2f26bce59235cb2b32a05430c127fc72f850"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-26":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Understanding customer needs\",\"Use of technical language in all technical descriptions\",\"Use of different terms and vocabulary\",\"Establishing functional silos\"],\"stem\":\"The ISO 9000 series of standards emphasizes eight quality management principles. Which of the following would be categorized as one of those principles?\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Customer focus is one of the quality management principles emphasized by the ISO 9000 family. The other choices do not represent an ISO quality management principle. [II.C]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Understanding customer needs","Use of technical language in all technical descriptions","Use of different terms and vocabulary","Establishing functional silos"],"stem":"The ISO 9000 series of standards emphasizes eight quality management principles. Which of the following would be categorized as one of those principles?","sub":"qsys","why":"Customer focus is one of the quality management principles emphasized by the ISO 9000 family. The other choices do not represent an ISO quality management principle. [II.C]"},"revision":"1e380b24b71d320611d4cc03ec892422068978f998baff42432d72060097ff45"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-260":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.1653\",\"0.4262\",\"0.5738\",\"0.8347\"],\"stem\":\"If the MTBF of a component is 900 hours, what is the reliability of this component at 500 hours? Assume that the reliability of the component is exponentially distributed.\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"With MTBF = 900 hours, λ = 1/900. R(500) = e^-500/900 = 0.5738. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.1653","0.4262","0.5738","0.8347"],"stem":"If the MTBF of a component is 900 hours, what is the reliability of this component at 500 hours? Assume that the reliability of the component is exponentially distributed.","sub":"design","why":"With MTBF = 900 hours, λ = 1/900. R(500) = e^-500/900 = 0.5738. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"93362b9a1d3fc9c1fd0104799fe533814c77d9242fdc33ac7d1860bbe8bbc4f0"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-261":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"36.79%\",\"63.21%\",\"99.92%\",\"1,250%\"],\"stem\":\"The failure rate of a machine is 0.0008. Assuming the life distribution of this type of machine is exponential, what fraction of machines in the population survives longer than the MTTF?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"With λ=0.0008, MTTF = 1/λ = 1,250. R(MTTF) = e^-0.0008(1250) = 0.3679, so 36.79% of components survive past the mean time to failure. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["36.79%","63.21%","99.92%","1,250%"],"stem":"The failure rate of a machine is 0.0008. Assuming the life distribution of this type of machine is exponential, what fraction of machines in the population survives longer than the MTTF?","sub":"design","why":"With λ=0.0008, MTTF = 1/λ = 1,250. R(MTTF) = e^-0.0008(1250) = 0.3679, so 36.79% of components survive past the mean time to failure. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"c68a715532874c0cf54e3fe7fe46bc6aba573a257b561af07f9710017786cb3a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-262":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.004\",\"0.996\",\"1,250\",\"1,255\"],\"stem\":\"The failure rate of a machine is 0.0008. Assuming the life distribution of this type of machine is exponential and the MTTR is five hours, what is the steady-state availability of this machine?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"With λ=0.0008, MTBF = 1/λ = 1,250 hours. With MTTR = 5 hours, steady-state availability A = MTBF/(MTBF+MTTR) = 1250/1255 ≈ 0.996. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.004","0.996","1,250","1,255"],"stem":"The failure rate of a machine is 0.0008. Assuming the life distribution of this type of machine is exponential and the MTTR is five hours, what is the steady-state availability of this machine?","sub":"design","why":"With λ=0.0008, MTBF = 1/λ = 1,250 hours. With MTTR = 5 hours, steady-state availability A = MTBF/(MTBF+MTTR) = 1250/1255 ≈ 0.996. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"53e1caf71c9f276da1842826cb0bdd5b5d60e1a1782c4e6bc563ae093553e776"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-263":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.0343\",\"0.2155\",\"0.7845\",\"29.13\"],\"stem\":\"In a test of a repairable component of a machine, 600 components were tested for 25 hours, and 515 components failed. If the mean time to repair (MTTR) these components is eight hours, what is the steady-state availability of this component?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"λ = failures/total test time = 515/(600x25) = 0.0343, so MTBF = 1/λ ≈ 29.13 hours. With MTTR = 8 hours, availability A = MTBF/(MTBF+MTTR) = 29.13/37.13 ≈ 0.7845. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.0343","0.2155","0.7845","29.13"],"stem":"In a test of a repairable component of a machine, 600 components were tested for 25 hours, and 515 components failed. If the mean time to repair (MTTR) these components is eight hours, what is the steady-state availability of this component?","sub":"design","why":"λ = failures/total test time = 515/(600x25) = 0.0343, so MTBF = 1/λ ≈ 29.13 hours. With MTTR = 8 hours, availability A = MTBF/(MTBF+MTTR) = 29.13/37.13 ≈ 0.7845. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"5b6da4f0a3fb51b3682eb1e55b9011258448a07347c835a24a36e3e3799e1b90"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-264":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Preventive maintenance policy\",\"Corrective maintenance policy\",\"Predictive maintenance policy\",\"Risk-based maintenance policy\"],\"stem\":\"Which type of maintenance policy creates a situation where there will be the maximum run time between repairs?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"A corrective maintenance policy does not require repairs or preventive maintenance until a failure occurs, which allows for the maximum run time between repairs. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Preventive maintenance policy","Corrective maintenance policy","Predictive maintenance policy","Risk-based maintenance policy"],"stem":"Which type of maintenance policy creates a situation where there will be the maximum run time between repairs?","sub":"design","why":"A corrective maintenance policy does not require repairs or preventive maintenance until a failure occurs, which allows for the maximum run time between repairs. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"6f1e5241432433a9de216b38317e3560acd470a3623f8b69c86a9a5df4bfc320"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-265":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"The manufacturing process\",\"Fatigue\",\"Random failures\",\"Out-of-box failures\"],\"stem\":\"In the second region identified in the general failure rate model (the bathtub curve), what is the most common cause for failures?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The second region of a bathtub curve is the constant-failure-rate region, characterized by the inherent failure rate of a product''s components (random failures). [III.E.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["The manufacturing process","Fatigue","Random failures","Out-of-box failures"],"stem":"In the second region identified in the general failure rate model (the bathtub curve), what is the most common cause for failures?","sub":"design","why":"The second region of a bathtub curve is the constant-failure-rate region, characterized by the inherent failure rate of a product''s components (random failures). [III.E.3]"},"revision":"5e388546d17fd9bcaad9c070a008eb969365406d6aada5e2ac947dbd4f232b41"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-266":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Uniform\",\"Weibull\",\"Exponential\",\"Normal\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following probability distributions is often used to model the second region of the bathtub curve?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The exponential distribution is the only distribution with a constant failure rate, so it is often used to model the second (constant-failure-rate) region of the bathtub curve. [III.E.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Uniform","Weibull","Exponential","Normal"],"stem":"Which of the following probability distributions is often used to model the second region of the bathtub curve?","sub":"design","why":"The exponential distribution is the only distribution with a constant failure rate, so it is often used to model the second (constant-failure-rate) region of the bathtub curve. [III.E.3]"},"revision":"8c9a700d56a8aeda481d91337c33e88064c089b3b23ba8b55134ef11566d87a0"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-267":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Uniform\",\"Weibull\",\"Exponential\",\"Normal\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following probability distributions is often used to model the third region of the bathtub curve?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The third region of the bathtub curve, the wear-out region, has an increasing failure rate over time, which is often modeled using the Weibull distribution. [III.E.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Uniform","Weibull","Exponential","Normal"],"stem":"Which of the following probability distributions is often used to model the third region of the bathtub curve?","sub":"design","why":"The third region of the bathtub curve, the wear-out region, has an increasing failure rate over time, which is often modeled using the Weibull distribution. [III.E.3]"},"revision":"bbf741800fd0f01fcc3a81becf4f7edb55bea6e025b78ff521f9170155d77228"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-268":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"To help reduce the negative effects of potential failures before they occur\",\"To organize the workplace environment\",\"To communicate hierarchical relationships between events\",\"To reduce the number of defects produced in a process\"],\"stem\":\"What is the main goal of FMEA?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The main goal of an FMEA is to help a team identify and eliminate the negative effects of a potential failure before it occurs. [III.E.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["To help reduce the negative effects of potential failures before they occur","To organize the workplace environment","To communicate hierarchical relationships between events","To reduce the number of defects produced in a process"],"stem":"What is the main goal of FMEA?","sub":"design","why":"The main goal of an FMEA is to help a team identify and eliminate the negative effects of a potential failure before it occurs. [III.E.4]"},"revision":"a517605067691204443e0b9d6a569c1f59f80fab80bc6cf3f7b01f60a78a6222"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-269":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"A doctor that performs this surgical procedure\",\"A doctor and a nurse\",\"A doctor, nurse, and business office employee\",\"A doctor, nurse, technician, engineer, business office employee, and manager\"],\"stem\":\"A hospital is preparing to do an FMEA for a surgical procedure. Which of the following groups of employees should make up the FMEA team members?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"An FMEA team should be a cross section of employees across several fields; at minimum, a surgical-procedure FMEA team should include a doctor, nurse, technician, engineer, business office employee, and manager. [III.E.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["A doctor that performs this surgical procedure","A doctor and a nurse","A doctor, nurse, and business office employee","A doctor, nurse, technician, engineer, business office employee, and manager"],"stem":"A hospital is preparing to do an FMEA for a surgical procedure. Which of the following groups of employees should make up the FMEA team members?","sub":"design","why":"An FMEA team should be a cross section of employees across several fields; at minimum, a surgical-procedure FMEA team should include a doctor, nurse, technician, engineer, business office employee, and manager. [III.E.4]"},"revision":"50397e27cac11aa07886ccc45a5970bc23967b7240bc2cb396127af4e40971c4"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-27":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"ISO 9000\",\"ISO 9001\",\"ISO 9004\",\"ISO 14001\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following ISO standards are guidelines only?\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"ISO 9004 consists of suggestions only, guidelines but not requirements for certification. [II.C]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["ISO 9000","ISO 9001","ISO 9004","ISO 14001"],"stem":"Which of the following ISO standards are guidelines only?","sub":"qsys","why":"ISO 9004 consists of suggestions only, guidelines but not requirements for certification. [II.C]"},"revision":"ee354948860cedfca62cce479abb128b159cc32ed01f0b2841c5449f94a55a96"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-270":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Fault tree analysis (FTA)\",\"Failure mode and effects analysis (FMEA)\",\"Risk management plan (RMP)\",\"Risk management report (RMR)\"],\"stem\":\"Which risk management tool contains the following elements: potential failure types, the risk associated with the failures, and risk mitigations?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"An FMEA contains all of these elements: potential failure types, associated risk, and risk mitigations. [III.E.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Fault tree analysis (FTA)","Failure mode and effects analysis (FMEA)","Risk management plan (RMP)","Risk management report (RMR)"],"stem":"Which risk management tool contains the following elements: potential failure types, the risk associated with the failures, and risk mitigations?","sub":"design","why":"An FMEA contains all of these elements: potential failure types, associated risk, and risk mitigations. [III.E.4]"},"revision":"c3ea2b43ee117f50b1899146c43b086ae1bf9faae37916581ce333a3ea69452f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-271":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"16\",\"17\",\"56\",\"112\"],\"stem\":\"An FMEA team has identified a potential failure and assessed the following score for severity, occurrence, and detection for this failure mode: 8, 2, 7. What is the RPN for this failure mode?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"RPN is the product of severity, occurrence, and detection: RPN = 8 x 2 x 7 = 112. [III.E.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["16","17","56","112"],"stem":"An FMEA team has identified a potential failure and assessed the following score for severity, occurrence, and detection for this failure mode: 8, 2, 7. What is the RPN for this failure mode?","sub":"design","why":"RPN is the product of severity, occurrence, and detection: RPN = 8 x 2 x 7 = 112. [III.E.4]"},"revision":"2ada866db8a3bce91ef7294cc5120653442e7f2586d03f2babab73436bff914f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-272":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Severity = 5, Occurrence = 2, Detection = 3\",\"Severity = 5, Occurrence = 3, Detection = 2\",\"Severity = 4, Occurrence = 3, Detection = 3\",\"Severity = 3, Occurrence = 4, Detection = 4\"],\"stem\":\"An FMEA team calculated an RPN of 112 (severity 8, occurrence 2, detection 7) for a failure mode. If the risk reduction threshold is an RPN of 40, which of the following risk ratings for a different failure mode would indicate a situation requiring risk reduction?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Given a risk reduction threshold of 40 (and the RPN=112 example above), the first three options each yield an RPN below 40. Option d gives RPN = 3 x 4 x 4 = 48, which exceeds the threshold and requires risk reduction. [III.E.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Severity = 5, Occurrence = 2, Detection = 3","Severity = 5, Occurrence = 3, Detection = 2","Severity = 4, Occurrence = 3, Detection = 3","Severity = 3, Occurrence = 4, Detection = 4"],"stem":"An FMEA team calculated an RPN of 112 (severity 8, occurrence 2, detection 7) for a failure mode. If the risk reduction threshold is an RPN of 40, which of the following risk ratings for a different failure mode would indicate a situation requiring risk reduction?","sub":"design","why":"Given a risk reduction threshold of 40 (and the RPN=112 example above), the first three options each yield an RPN below 40. Option d gives RPN = 3 x 4 x 4 = 48, which exceeds the threshold and requires risk reduction. [III.E.4]"},"revision":"1ef6b00101a51917ed523bbd757d8a9dd29b47cbc156cf29c3bf9521912dcd3c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-273":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"FMECA\",\"uFMEA\",\"pFMEA\",\"PHA\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following risk analyses considers product failures by a customer due to human factors?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"A use FMEA (uFMEA) specifically considers customers'' human-factors-related product failures. [III.E.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["FMECA","uFMEA","pFMEA","PHA"],"stem":"Which of the following risk analyses considers product failures by a customer due to human factors?","sub":"design","why":"A use FMEA (uFMEA) specifically considers customers'' human-factors-related product failures. [III.E.4]"},"revision":"0a5429cc4e3c1ac0791a283ae42426ab120279397705ed28479aabbfb00dca01"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-274":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Process\",\"Use\",\"System\",\"Design\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following types of FMEA is created after the others?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The process FMEA (pFMEA) is initiated last, closer to the end of design and development, after the use, design, and system FMEAs have already been created, since the product must be designed before the process to make it. [III.E.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Process","Use","System","Design"],"stem":"Which of the following types of FMEA is created after the others?","sub":"design","why":"The process FMEA (pFMEA) is initiated last, closer to the end of design and development, after the use, design, and system FMEAs have already been created, since the product must be designed before the process to make it. [III.E.4]"},"revision":"fa24c360d68757c9afebd09b28a396aaec0f9a3a70fe53c2952523538dc2c4f2"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-275":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Design\",\"Process\",\"System\",\"Use\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following types of failure mode and effects analyses would best be used to assess the risk of insufficient product instructions in detail?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Product instructions are part of the user interface and are best analyzed in detail through a use FMEA (uFMEA). [III.E.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Design","Process","System","Use"],"stem":"Which of the following types of failure mode and effects analyses would best be used to assess the risk of insufficient product instructions in detail?","sub":"design","why":"Product instructions are part of the user interface and are best analyzed in detail through a use FMEA (uFMEA). [III.E.4]"},"revision":"c7131bddb2cf0a34bfaec64a2bb61b1b304ae2946f7f722f435b00df90a98e2e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-276":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"detection.\",\"probability of occurrence.\",\"impact.\",\"strategic alignment.\"],\"stem\":\"In addition to the two main components of risk, the FMEA technique may add a rating scale for:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The two main components of risk in FMEA are probability of occurrence (likelihood) and impact (severity); detection is the third rating scale often added. [III.E.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["detection.","probability of occurrence.","impact.","strategic alignment."],"stem":"In addition to the two main components of risk, the FMEA technique may add a rating scale for:","sub":"design","why":"The two main components of risk in FMEA are probability of occurrence (likelihood) and impact (severity); detection is the third rating scale often added. [III.E.4]"},"revision":"8b1c685a5c5676cb24b85cc4607acdb8f19261d95637ea289a073c8dff9ca0b4"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-277":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"insufficient material strength.\",\"manufacturing process errors.\",\"adequacy of visual alarms.\",\"electrical failures.\"],\"stem\":\"An event that would be analyzed for impact and likelihood in a use failure mode and effects analysis (uFMEA) is:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Adequacy of visual alarms is part of the user interface, evaluated via a use FMEA (uFMEA); the other options are better suited to a design FMEA or process FMEA. [III.E.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["insufficient material strength.","manufacturing process errors.","adequacy of visual alarms.","electrical failures."],"stem":"An event that would be analyzed for impact and likelihood in a use failure mode and effects analysis (uFMEA) is:","sub":"design","why":"Adequacy of visual alarms is part of the user interface, evaluated via a use FMEA (uFMEA); the other options are better suited to a design FMEA or process FMEA. [III.E.4]"},"revision":"f8800417afe4616b0de6822e0312934a6cffda10fda074d05459ec6b41546708"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-278":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"To reduce the number of defects produced in a process\",\"To develop a prioritization scheme for corrective action plans\",\"To organize the workplace environment\",\"To communicate hierarchical relationships between events\"],\"stem\":\"What is the main goal of an FMECA?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"An FMECA is an assessment of risk that develops a prioritization scheme for corrective action based on the severity and occurrence of failure modes. [III.E.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["To reduce the number of defects produced in a process","To develop a prioritization scheme for corrective action plans","To organize the workplace environment","To communicate hierarchical relationships between events"],"stem":"What is the main goal of an FMECA?","sub":"design","why":"An FMECA is an assessment of risk that develops a prioritization scheme for corrective action based on the severity and occurrence of failure modes. [III.E.4]"},"revision":"f7597cec3c08ff01e19936e455e6a17655e077a73f62c0a199b2fa0be652eaa7"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-279":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"type\":\"oc-curve\"},\"options\":[\"is better for the company.\",\"will accept lots with a higher probability.\",\"is better for the manufacturer.\",\"provides equal risk for the company and manufacturer.\"],\"stem\":\"A company compares two competing sampling plans using their OC (operating characteristic) curves. Curve A drops off more steeply as the lot percentage defective increases, meaning its probability of accepting the lot falls much faster than Curve B''s. Compared to Curve B, Curve A:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"The dashed OC curve is better for the company. As the percentage nonconforming increases, its probability of accepting the lot decreases much faster than the other curve, so the company will accept fewer unacceptable lots from the producer. [IV.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"type":"oc-curve"},"options":["is better for the company.","will accept lots with a higher probability.","is better for the manufacturer.","provides equal risk for the company and manufacturer."],"stem":"A company compares two competing sampling plans using their OC (operating characteristic) curves. Curve A drops off more steeply as the lot percentage defective increases, meaning its probability of accepting the lot falls much faster than Curve B''s. Compared to Curve B, Curve A:","sub":"ppc","why":"The dashed OC curve is better for the company. As the percentage nonconforming increases, its probability of accepting the lot decreases much faster than the other curve, so the company will accept fewer unacceptable lots from the producer. [IV.C.1]"},"revision":"c1993c109a0b432094e2ca0c4509f1078157ff61bbb7150c31e73a47e87cc4cf"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-28":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"4\",\"7\",\"8\",\"14\"],\"stem\":\"The Malcolm Baldrige National Quality Award consists of how many key criteria for performance excellence?\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"There are seven criteria: Leadership; Strategy; Customers; Measurement, Analysis, and Knowledge Management; Workforce; Operations; and Results. [II.C]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["4","7","8","14"],"stem":"The Malcolm Baldrige National Quality Award consists of how many key criteria for performance excellence?","sub":"qsys","why":"There are seven criteria: Leadership; Strategy; Customers; Measurement, Analysis, and Knowledge Management; Workforce; Operations; and Results. [II.C]"},"revision":"1c54b5dcba7102d392ab21e890337e72188ad05583a92b6f8acc2c5e5e054b01"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-280":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.2165\",\"0.7835\",\"0.8614\",\"0.9221\"],\"stem\":\"A double sampling plan is implemented with the following parameters: n = 50, c = 1, r = 4, n = 75, c = 3, r = 6 1 1 1 2 2 2 What is the probability that a decision is made (to accept or reject the lot) on the first sample if the percentage nonconforming, p, is equal to 0.10?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"The lot is accepted or rejected on the first sample unless 2 or 3 defects are found (which triggers a second sample). P(decision on first sample) = 1 - P(d=2) - P(d=3) = 1 - 0.0779 - 0.1386 = 0.7835. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.2165","0.7835","0.8614","0.9221"],"stem":"A double sampling plan is implemented with the following parameters: n = 50, c = 1, r = 4, n = 75, c = 3, r = 6 1 1 1 2 2 2 What is the probability that a decision is made (to accept or reject the lot) on the first sample if the percentage nonconforming, p, is equal to 0.10?","sub":"ppc","why":"The lot is accepted or rejected on the first sample unless 2 or 3 defects are found (which triggers a second sample). P(decision on first sample) = 1 - P(d=2) - P(d=3) = 1 - 0.0779 - 0.1386 = 0.7835. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"a6d39bba504724618da5eed03742aa4631407f8d24c9ba1a3830d1daa900a165"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-281":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"50\",\"67\",\"109\",\"125\"],\"stem\":\"A double sampling plan is implemented with the following parameters: n = 50, c = 1, r = 3, n = 75, c = 3, r = 4 1 1 1 2 2 2 What is the average sample number for this sampling plan if the percentage nonconforming, p, is equal to 0.10?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"The probability of requiring a second sample is 1 - 0.7835 = 0.2165 (from the previous question). Average sample number = n1 + n2 x P2 = 50 + 75 x 0.2165 = 66.24, or 67. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["50","67","109","125"],"stem":"A double sampling plan is implemented with the following parameters: n = 50, c = 1, r = 3, n = 75, c = 3, r = 4 1 1 1 2 2 2 What is the average sample number for this sampling plan if the percentage nonconforming, p, is equal to 0.10?","sub":"ppc","why":"The probability of requiring a second sample is 1 - 0.7835 = 0.2165 (from the previous question). Average sample number = n1 + n2 x P2 = 50 + 75 x 0.2165 = 66.24, or 67. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"908b3c56b2b40e0057052b5e5535bcdc5b3810325a9cba0bfbab0a2b5c980f97"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-282":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"smaller when the quality is poor.\",\"larger when the quality is poor.\",\"larger when the quality is near the indifference level (p = 0.50).\",\"smaller when the quality is near the indifference level (p = 0.50).\"],\"stem\":\"When compared with sampling plans with equal protection, the average sample number of double sampling plans generally is:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"When quality is very good or very poor, the average sample size of double sampling plans is generally smaller than plans with equal protection, since decisions are more often made on the first sample, requiring fewer second samples. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["smaller when the quality is poor.","larger when the quality is poor.","larger when the quality is near the indifference level (p = 0.50).","smaller when the quality is near the indifference level (p = 0.50)."],"stem":"When compared with sampling plans with equal protection, the average sample number of double sampling plans generally is:","sub":"ppc","why":"When quality is very good or very poor, the average sample size of double sampling plans is generally smaller than plans with equal protection, since decisions are more often made on the first sample, requiring fewer second samples. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"9620addd6b1bc460d466dbf05434c3bb8991af29692b8a3dc4469eaa31e435d2"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-283":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Single, double, and sequential\",\"Single, double, and multiple\",\"Single, sequential, and stratified\",\"Double, sequential, and multiple\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following types of sampling are allowed in ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018)?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018) allows single sampling, double sampling, and multiple sampling. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Single, double, and sequential","Single, double, and multiple","Single, sequential, and stratified","Double, sequential, and multiple"],"stem":"Which of the following types of sampling are allowed in ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018)?","sub":"ppc","why":"ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018) allows single sampling, double sampling, and multiple sampling. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"d75c0135a8c838ca674030cba6931f9a248b1cc523a25b8bcb15683137f7de74"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-284":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"80\",\"125\",\"200\",\"315\"],\"stem\":\"A lot size of 1,500 is to be inspected using ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018). The AQL is 0.40%. What is the required sample size for a single sampling plan (normal, level II inspection)?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"For a lot size of 1,500, the sample size code letter for normal, level II inspection is K; following ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018) Table II-A, the required sample size is n = 315. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["80","125","200","315"],"stem":"A lot size of 1,500 is to be inspected using ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018). The AQL is 0.40%. What is the required sample size for a single sampling plan (normal, level II inspection)?","sub":"ppc","why":"For a lot size of 1,500, the sample size code letter for normal, level II inspection is K; following ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018) Table II-A, the required sample size is n = 315. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"3f44f68339e6652e4ab7a04b3cca6fde739417ab10b72438dee6caa6fdc1ffe3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-285":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"n = 50, Ac = 0, Re = 1\",\"n = 50, Ac = 1, Re = 2\",\"n = 80, Ac = 0, Re = 1\",\"n = 80, Ac = 1, Re = 2\"],\"stem\":\"A lot size of 500 is to be inspected using ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018). The AQL is 0.65%. What are the required sample size, acceptance number, and rejection number for a single sampling plan (normal level II inspection)?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"For a lot size of 500, the code letter for normal, level II inspection is H; at AQL 0.65%, Table II-A''s downward arrow points to code letter J, giving a sample size of 80, acceptance number 1, and rejection number 2. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["n = 50, Ac = 0, Re = 1","n = 50, Ac = 1, Re = 2","n = 80, Ac = 0, Re = 1","n = 80, Ac = 1, Re = 2"],"stem":"A lot size of 500 is to be inspected using ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018). The AQL is 0.65%. What are the required sample size, acceptance number, and rejection number for a single sampling plan (normal level II inspection)?","sub":"ppc","why":"For a lot size of 500, the code letter for normal, level II inspection is H; at AQL 0.65%, Table II-A''s downward arrow points to code letter J, giving a sample size of 80, acceptance number 1, and rejection number 2. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"57103f74d9ed35cf68ed2c8e22b8b7528158671cd7f469b226fcb0b4f774a364"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-286":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"13, 13\",\"20, 20\",\"20, 40\",\"32, 32\"],\"stem\":\"A lot of size 200 is to be inspected using ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018). The AQL is 0.65%. What are the required sample sizes for a double sampling plan (normal, level II inspection)?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"For a lot size of 200, the code letter for normal, level II inspection is G; Table III-A (Double Sampling Plans for Normal Inspection) gives a sample size of 20 for both the first and second sample, regardless of AQL. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["13, 13","20, 20","20, 40","32, 32"],"stem":"A lot of size 200 is to be inspected using ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018). The AQL is 0.65%. What are the required sample sizes for a double sampling plan (normal, level II inspection)?","sub":"ppc","why":"For a lot size of 200, the code letter for normal, level II inspection is G; Table III-A (Double Sampling Plans for Normal Inspection) gives a sample size of 20 for both the first and second sample, regardless of AQL. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"899e0e8ef9e1118cb88c2448e0d3e5eb52abdc15136b19b0a64459605837315c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-287":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Ac = 0, Re = 2\",\"Ac = 0, Re = 3\",\"Ac = 1, Re = 2\",\"Ac = 1, Re = 4\"],\"stem\":\"A lot of size 400 is to be inspected using ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018). The AQL is 0.65%. For the first sample of a double sampling plan (normal, level II inspection), what are the acceptance and rejection numbers?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"For a lot size of 400, the code letter for normal, level II inspection is H; at AQL 0.65%, Table III-A''s downward arrow points to code letter J, giving a first-sample acceptance number of 0 and rejection number of 2. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Ac = 0, Re = 2","Ac = 0, Re = 3","Ac = 1, Re = 2","Ac = 1, Re = 4"],"stem":"A lot of size 400 is to be inspected using ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018). The AQL is 0.65%. For the first sample of a double sampling plan (normal, level II inspection), what are the acceptance and rejection numbers?","sub":"ppc","why":"For a lot size of 400, the code letter for normal, level II inspection is H; at AQL 0.65%, Table III-A''s downward arrow points to code letter J, giving a first-sample acceptance number of 0 and rejection number of 2. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"767e961db2c58c5067b62cbff834b796ad726b5765de3ca44c18c6235f16bac3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-288":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"require smaller sample sizes with equal protection compared to attribute sampling plans.\",\"are easier to implement.\",\"can monitor multiple quality characteristics at once.\",\"are less expensive to implement.\"],\"stem\":\"An advantage of variables sampling plans compared to attribute sampling plans is that variables sampling plans:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Variables sampling plans offer equal protection with a much smaller sample size than attribute sampling plans, though they are generally more expensive to implement, harder to administer, and can only monitor one quality characteristic at a time. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["require smaller sample sizes with equal protection compared to attribute sampling plans.","are easier to implement.","can monitor multiple quality characteristics at once.","are less expensive to implement."],"stem":"An advantage of variables sampling plans compared to attribute sampling plans is that variables sampling plans:","sub":"ppc","why":"Variables sampling plans offer equal protection with a much smaller sample size than attribute sampling plans, though they are generally more expensive to implement, harder to administer, and can only monitor one quality characteristic at a time. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"07b9fad49b38c6fbeb45a656625db893d3b4cb19cc454e29a12ba2472b65eb7a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-289":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"5\",\"7\",\"10\",\"20\"],\"stem\":\"A lot of 100 items is submitted for inspection under ANSI/ASQ Z1.9-2003 (R2018), level II, normal inspection with AQL = 0.65%. Both upper and lower specification limits are specified for these items. What sample size is required for this sampling plan?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"ANSI/ASQ Z1.9-2003 (R2018) Table A-2 gives sample size code letter F for this lot; Table B-3 shows code letter F corresponds to a sample size of 10, regardless of AQL level. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["5","7","10","20"],"stem":"A lot of 100 items is submitted for inspection under ANSI/ASQ Z1.9-2003 (R2018), level II, normal inspection with AQL = 0.65%. Both upper and lower specification limits are specified for these items. What sample size is required for this sampling plan?","sub":"ppc","why":"ANSI/ASQ Z1.9-2003 (R2018) Table A-2 gives sample size code letter F for this lot; Table B-3 shows code letter F corresponds to a sample size of 10, regardless of AQL level. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"9d0a50e156fa11ade8a934de625acf6cfaa53c7b5e821cf8ab3b1618264905b9"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-29":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"an internal quality audit.\",\"an external quality audit.\",\"a third-party quality audit.\",\"a second-party audit.\"],\"stem\":\"A quality audit is to be conducted within a particular company. An employee of this company will be conducting the audit. This is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"If the auditor is an employee of the company to be audited, then we refer to this as an internal audit. It is necessary that the person who will conduct this audit is not related to or involved in the activity that is being audited. [II.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["an internal quality audit.","an external quality audit.","a third-party quality audit.","a second-party audit."],"stem":"A quality audit is to be conducted within a particular company. An employee of this company will be conducting the audit. This is an example of:","sub":"qsys","why":"If the auditor is an employee of the company to be audited, then we refer to this as an internal audit. It is necessary that the person who will conduct this audit is not related to or involved in the activity that is being audited. [II.D.1]"},"revision":"a314aad9a0fd3aeea0bf0b9e92de351e702c98b7f11270b1aaf27d6a31269e09"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-290":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Yes, since the total percentage nonconforming is less than the maximum allowable.\",\"No, since the total percentage nonconforming is less than the maximum allowable.\",\"Yes, since the total percentage nonconforming is more than the maximum allowable.\",\"No, since the total percentage nonconforming is more than the maximum allowable.\"],\"stem\":\"A lot of twenty items is submitted for inspection under ANSI/ASQ Z1.9-2003 (R2018), level II, normal inspection with AQL = 1.00%. The required sample size for this sampling plan is four. The results of the four measurements are: 51, 47, 45, 39. Does this lot meet the acceptance criteria? The upper and lower specification limits for this process are 52 and 37, respectively.\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"With USL=52, LSL=37, sample mean=45.5, s=5.00: QU=(52-45.5)/5=1.30, QL=(45.5-37)/5=1.70. Table A-2 gives code letter C; Table B-3 at AQL 1.00% gives maximum allowable nonconforming M=1.49%. Table B-5 gives 6.67% above USL and 0.00% below LSL, for a total of 6.67%, which exceeds M, so the lot is not acceptable. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Yes, since the total percentage nonconforming is less than the maximum allowable.","No, since the total percentage nonconforming is less than the maximum allowable.","Yes, since the total percentage nonconforming is more than the maximum allowable.","No, since the total percentage nonconforming is more than the maximum allowable."],"stem":"A lot of twenty items is submitted for inspection under ANSI/ASQ Z1.9-2003 (R2018), level II, normal inspection with AQL = 1.00%. The required sample size for this sampling plan is four. The results of the four measurements are: 51, 47, 45, 39. Does this lot meet the acceptance criteria? The upper and lower specification limits for this process are 52 and 37, respectively.","sub":"ppc","why":"With USL=52, LSL=37, sample mean=45.5, s=5.00: QU=(52-45.5)/5=1.30, QL=(45.5-37)/5=1.70. Table A-2 gives code letter C; Table B-3 at AQL 1.00% gives maximum allowable nonconforming M=1.49%. Table B-5 gives 6.67% above USL and 0.00% below LSL, for a total of 6.67%, which exceeds M, so the lot is not acceptable. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"fdb45b135f16c03ce3534270fce21a3d14c1d8a0e1fda12fa51a136a500d2168"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-291":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"To be able to accurately read an OC curve\",\"To ensure that a product has not been contaminated during inspection\",\"To have the most efficient process\",\"To reduce the amount of sampling required\"],\"stem\":\"Why is sampling integrity important?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Sample integrity ensures a product has not been misused or contaminated and that the sample accurately represents the entire lot. [IV.C.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["To be able to accurately read an OC curve","To ensure that a product has not been contaminated during inspection","To have the most efficient process","To reduce the amount of sampling required"],"stem":"Why is sampling integrity important?","sub":"ppc","why":"Sample integrity ensures a product has not been misused or contaminated and that the sample accurately represents the entire lot. [IV.C.3]"},"revision":"0d26410c4ca466e9eb607326753567efa5291935fc42ff852bb7b5c42db0f0fc"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-292":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Batch, change, and inspection\",\"Batch, configuration, and inspection\",\"Batch, change, and configuration\",\"Change, configuration, and inspection\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following types of control are methods used to maintain sample integrity?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Sample integrity is maintained through batch control (documenting production steps and unique batch IDs), change control (tracking product changes), and configuration control (an extension of change control). [IV.C.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Batch, change, and inspection","Batch, configuration, and inspection","Batch, change, and configuration","Change, configuration, and inspection"],"stem":"Which of the following types of control are methods used to maintain sample integrity?","sub":"ppc","why":"Sample integrity is maintained through batch control (documenting production steps and unique batch IDs), change control (tracking product changes), and configuration control (an extension of change control). [IV.C.3]"},"revision":"625c25cb1103a256f5485329d208fdd9ff0eadd435912499487b949c2d8fb9e6"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-293":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Improved quality of the product\",\"Conformity to customer aesthetic requirements\",\"Determination of a conforming or nonconforming product\",\"Determination of employees’ capabilities\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is a result of dimensional inspection of a product?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Dimensional inspection determines whether a product''s measurements conform or don''t conform to specifications; it does not by itself determine the product''s overall quality, which depends on design and manufacturing. [IV.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Improved quality of the product","Conformity to customer aesthetic requirements","Determination of a conforming or nonconforming product","Determination of employees’ capabilities"],"stem":"Which of the following is a result of dimensional inspection of a product?","sub":"ppc","why":"Dimensional inspection determines whether a product''s measurements conform or don''t conform to specifications; it does not by itself determine the product''s overall quality, which depends on design and manufacturing. [IV.D.1]"},"revision":"ff5d4c39f8372ccb2b5e505b2e77b17e089cbb80bffd79022de31d763b1af6e1"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-294":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Protractor\",\"Bevel protractor\",\"Square\",\"Sine bar\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following provides the most precise angular measurement?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"A sine bar provides more precise angular measurement than protractors, bevel protractors, or squares. [IV.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Protractor","Bevel protractor","Square","Sine bar"],"stem":"Which of the following provides the most precise angular measurement?","sub":"ppc","why":"A sine bar provides more precise angular measurement than protractors, bevel protractors, or squares. [IV.D.1]"},"revision":"8e225c85d3d727440b6bfc58929b2f04dab09ce43fd6954722ea23efb8841575"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-295":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Steel ruler\",\"Plug gage\",\"Snap gage\",\"Sine bar\"],\"stem\":\"The outside dimensions of a part are inspected for conformance to product specifications. Which of the following measuring tools is most appropriate for this measurement?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Snap or ring gages are used to check outside dimensions; plug gages check inside dimensions. [IV.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Steel ruler","Plug gage","Snap gage","Sine bar"],"stem":"The outside dimensions of a part are inspected for conformance to product specifications. Which of the following measuring tools is most appropriate for this measurement?","sub":"ppc","why":"Snap or ring gages are used to check outside dimensions; plug gages check inside dimensions. [IV.D.1]"},"revision":"f9f5ede07c62f4ed5637a0b5b407c96fb9151124c5b9f7ecad2bb61085ebfba6"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-296":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"No, the part should be rejected.\",\"Yes, the part should be accepted.\",\"Yes or no, depending upon further inspection.\",\"Not enough information is provided.\"],\"stem\":\"A part was inspected using a go/no-go gage. The part did not mate with either end of the gage. Did the part pass the inspection?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"A part passes a go/no-go inspection only if it mates with the go end and not the no-go end. Since this part did not mate with either end, it failed the go inspection and should be rejected. [IV.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["No, the part should be rejected.","Yes, the part should be accepted.","Yes or no, depending upon further inspection.","Not enough information is provided."],"stem":"A part was inspected using a go/no-go gage. The part did not mate with either end of the gage. Did the part pass the inspection?","sub":"ppc","why":"A part passes a go/no-go inspection only if it mates with the go end and not the no-go end. Since this part did not mate with either end, it failed the go inspection and should be rejected. [IV.D.1]"},"revision":"35df8721452a55800165f7cb7833a49d1bf473061e1407afe6df149d2863e6f3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-297":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Reflectance meters\",\"Optical interference\",\"Pneumatics\",\"Stylus averaging\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following methods is the most common for surface measurements?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Stylus averaging, used to measure an average electrical signal, is the most common surface measurement method because it is fast, repeatable, interpretable, and relatively inexpensive. [IV.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Reflectance meters","Optical interference","Pneumatics","Stylus averaging"],"stem":"Which of the following methods is the most common for surface measurements?","sub":"ppc","why":"Stylus averaging, used to measure an average electrical signal, is the most common surface measurement method because it is fast, repeatable, interpretable, and relatively inexpensive. [IV.D.1]"},"revision":"1d9569d7eb3e4e9e6caf50cbca066b8f387c373a748096531f78723bd802793f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-298":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Ring gage\",\"Vernier calipers\",\"Precision spindle\",\"Dial indicator\"],\"stem\":\"A key component of a part is round in shape. Which of the following measurement tools is most appropriate to measure this component?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Precision spindle instruments are used for roundness measurements; calipers are for linear measurements, ring gages assess outside-dimension conformance, and dial indicators are used to check alignment or runout. [IV.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Ring gage","Vernier calipers","Precision spindle","Dial indicator"],"stem":"A key component of a part is round in shape. Which of the following measurement tools is most appropriate to measure this component?","sub":"ppc","why":"Precision spindle instruments are used for roundness measurements; calipers are for linear measurements, ring gages assess outside-dimension conformance, and dial indicators are used to check alignment or runout. [IV.D.1]"},"revision":"d142ebc3d2a56fe4377179a655b6fac1e1b9360eea65d8015decdefbb9532896"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-299":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"100% inspection\",\"Screening inspection\",\"Coordinate measuring machine\",\"Nondestructive testing\"],\"stem\":\"The maximum tensile strength a particular part can withstand is an important quality characteristic of this part. Which of the following is most appropriate to test this quality characteristic?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Measuring maximum tensile strength destroys the part, so screening or 100% inspection isn''t appropriate; nondestructive testing is required for this quality characteristic. [IV.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["100% inspection","Screening inspection","Coordinate measuring machine","Nondestructive testing"],"stem":"The maximum tensile strength a particular part can withstand is an important quality characteristic of this part. Which of the following is most appropriate to test this quality characteristic?","sub":"ppc","why":"Measuring maximum tensile strength destroys the part, so screening or 100% inspection isn''t appropriate; nondestructive testing is required for this quality characteristic. [IV.D.2]"},"revision":"5307a6e9400c29eec23c8b759bca1bb7cf17e4e039632ec5bd3dffd1748ade15"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Eliminate work standards that prescribe numerical quotas.\",\"Institute barriers that stand between the hourly worker and his right to pride of workmanship.\",\"50% of quality problems are due to employees.\",\"15% of quality problems are due to management.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is one of Deming’s 14 points?\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Deming argued that work standards that include numerical quotas do nothing to motivate the common worker and should be eliminated. [I.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Eliminate work standards that prescribe numerical quotas.","Institute barriers that stand between the hourly worker and his right to pride of workmanship.","50% of quality problems are due to employees.","15% of quality problems are due to management."],"stem":"Which of the following is one of Deming’s 14 points?","sub":"mgmt","why":"Deming argued that work standards that include numerical quotas do nothing to motivate the common worker and should be eliminated. [I.A.1]"},"revision":"cc90575c7ca3f6d21c083af52ae738eeac6567cce07fec7a16a1faf7e7de8919"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-30":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"quality system audit.\",\"product quality audit.\",\"process quality audit.\",\"service quality audit.\"],\"stem\":\"The most comprehensive type of audit is the:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"A quality system audit is an audit of the entire system. The other three are more specific and smaller in scope. [II.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["quality system audit.","product quality audit.","process quality audit.","service quality audit."],"stem":"The most comprehensive type of audit is the:","sub":"qsys","why":"A quality system audit is an audit of the entire system. The other three are more specific and smaller in scope. [II.D.1]"},"revision":"8c4e45ee447762e0212d0e82ad36ca7d3b37e80a323c10bbf3c77506709a4b64"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-300":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Liquid penetration testing\",\"Magnetic particle testing\",\"Eddy current testing\",\"Ultrasonic testing\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following nondestructive tests can only be used on parts made of iron and steel?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Magnetic particle testing works only on parts that can be magnetized, such as those made of iron, steel, or allied materials. [IV.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Liquid penetration testing","Magnetic particle testing","Eddy current testing","Ultrasonic testing"],"stem":"Which of the following nondestructive tests can only be used on parts made of iron and steel?","sub":"ppc","why":"Magnetic particle testing works only on parts that can be magnetized, such as those made of iron, steel, or allied materials. [IV.D.2]"},"revision":"2703cd532184c3ac282c17b8b8e2f1e3c9e8ed2b87d3ae9c4405a951297b1f05"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-301":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Liquid penetration testing\",\"Magnetic particle testing\",\"Eddy current testing\",\"Radiographic testing\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following nondestructive tests can only be used on parts made of conductive materials?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Eddy current testing applies an AC current through a coil near the part''s surface, so it can only be used on conductive materials. [IV.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Liquid penetration testing","Magnetic particle testing","Eddy current testing","Radiographic testing"],"stem":"Which of the following nondestructive tests can only be used on parts made of conductive materials?","sub":"ppc","why":"Eddy current testing applies an AC current through a coil near the part''s surface, so it can only be used on conductive materials. [IV.D.2]"},"revision":"d62b72ac6c81cd83c05fbe8da15faf727c00c99f9c11d573011f74bd9553b5da"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-302":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"ISO\",\"ASQ\",\"NIST\",\"SI\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following organizations maintains the standards of measurement in the United States?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"The National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) maintains the standards of measurement in the United States. [IV.E]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["ISO","ASQ","NIST","SI"],"stem":"Which of the following organizations maintains the standards of measurement in the United States?","sub":"ppc","why":"The National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) maintains the standards of measurement in the United States. [IV.E]"},"revision":"bc9b6f1d1b60bffd51fa9c20b608a559cd43c9d0db52caa16b37d3219ef538f9"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-303":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Quality management\",\"Auditing\",\"Statistical sampling\",\"Metrology\"],\"stem\":\"Physical artifacts, paper standards, and definition of base units are elements of what field?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Physical artifacts (like gage blocks), paper standards (published specifications and methods), and definitions of base units are all foundational elements of metrology. [IV.E]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Quality management","Auditing","Statistical sampling","Metrology"],"stem":"Physical artifacts, paper standards, and definition of base units are elements of what field?","sub":"ppc","why":"Physical artifacts (like gage blocks), paper standards (published specifications and methods), and definitions of base units are all foundational elements of metrology. [IV.E]"},"revision":"c9a47bbe06254d40913008bad34eb1c3ba995dbe270b066cfcc37d89c6e52674"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-304":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"As a physical artifact used for direct reference\",\"The amount of water required to fill a 36-ounce beaker\",\"Based on the weight of carbon molecules\",\"A theoretical concept\"],\"stem\":\"How does NIST define the kilogram, the base unit of mass?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"The kilogram is the only base SI unit still defined by a physical artifact, a platinum-iridium cylinder held by the International Bureau of Weights and Measures in Paris, with a duplicate protected by NIST in the U.S. [IV.E]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["As a physical artifact used for direct reference","The amount of water required to fill a 36-ounce beaker","Based on the weight of carbon molecules","A theoretical concept"],"stem":"How does NIST define the kilogram, the base unit of mass?","sub":"ppc","why":"The kilogram is the only base SI unit still defined by a physical artifact, a platinum-iridium cylinder held by the International Bureau of Weights and Measures in Paris, with a duplicate protected by NIST in the U.S. [IV.E]"},"revision":"bae2fb7a2f3907f97cf7e73cd83fe0fae9c305882ecbcdc3545cbf634bf0966b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-305":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"systematic error in measurement.\",\"random error in measurement.\",\"operator error in measurement.\",\"type II error in measurement.\"],\"stem\":\"Slight changes in temperature where parts are measured for conformance could lead to:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Ambient fluctuations like slight temperature changes are classified as random error, unpredictable accidental fluctuations, as opposed to systematic error (e.g., operator or calibration error). [IV.E]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["systematic error in measurement.","random error in measurement.","operator error in measurement.","type II error in measurement."],"stem":"Slight changes in temperature where parts are measured for conformance could lead to:","sub":"ppc","why":"Ambient fluctuations like slight temperature changes are classified as random error, unpredictable accidental fluctuations, as opposed to systematic error (e.g., operator or calibration error). [IV.E]"},"revision":"c64c67600d48938b773114190494099342cdfffa6523e45234528fee1585a0dd"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-306":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Measurement assurance\",\"Calibration\",\"Traceability\",\"Gage R&R study\"],\"stem\":\"To tie a laboratory’s measurement standard to national standards, a company could use which of the following?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Traceability ties a laboratory''s measurement standards to national standards. [IV.E]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Measurement assurance","Calibration","Traceability","Gage R&R study"],"stem":"To tie a laboratory’s measurement standard to national standards, a company could use which of the following?","sub":"ppc","why":"Traceability ties a laboratory''s measurement standards to national standards. [IV.E]"},"revision":"8b120c890c374233cbfce31fd68443657eeec8d8978275654fa223506139faf2"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-307":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"measurement calibration records.\",\"measurement traceability protocols.\",\"the material review board.\",\"measurement assurance protocols.\"],\"stem\":\"Information on the contribution to error of technicians or a laboratory’s procedures can typically be found in:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Measurement assurance protocols detail a measuring instrument''s accuracy and the contribution to error from technicians, the lab environment, and lab procedures. [IV.E]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["measurement calibration records.","measurement traceability protocols.","the material review board.","measurement assurance protocols."],"stem":"Information on the contribution to error of technicians or a laboratory’s procedures can typically be found in:","sub":"ppc","why":"Measurement assurance protocols detail a measuring instrument''s accuracy and the contribution to error from technicians, the lab environment, and lab procedures. [IV.E]"},"revision":"9aaf3b422e5be27d633ce9898bb1270a8b067c1e87cb0b74b2e95cce1abce2ad"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-308":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"accuracy.\",\"precision.\",\"sensitivity.\",\"readability.\"],\"stem\":\"The goal of equipment calibration is to maintain the system’s:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"The goal of equipment calibration is to maintain a measurement system''s accuracy. [IV.E]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["accuracy.","precision.","sensitivity.","readability."],"stem":"The goal of equipment calibration is to maintain the system’s:","sub":"ppc","why":"The goal of equipment calibration is to maintain a measurement system''s accuracy. [IV.E]"},"revision":"dfa2f25283ebabc506bb7919d0190df7c36f329c111e1c569ed0fa1ff6854224"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-309":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Random error\",\"Systematic error\",\"Type I error\",\"Type II error\"],\"stem\":\"While analyzing repeated measurements of a quality characteristic, an employee notices that the values are consistently larger than the reference value. Which of the following has occurred in this measurement process?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Systematic error remains relatively constant across repeated measurements and can be caused by humans, imprecise devices, or poor manufacturing methods; values consistently larger than the reference value indicate systematic error, not random error (inherent fluctuations) or Type I/II error (which apply to hypothesis testing). [IV.F]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Random error","Systematic error","Type I error","Type II error"],"stem":"While analyzing repeated measurements of a quality characteristic, an employee notices that the values are consistently larger than the reference value. Which of the following has occurred in this measurement process?","sub":"ppc","why":"Systematic error remains relatively constant across repeated measurements and can be caused by humans, imprecise devices, or poor manufacturing methods; values consistently larger than the reference value indicate systematic error, not random error (inherent fluctuations) or Type I/II error (which apply to hypothesis testing). [IV.F]"},"revision":"21d1a4291e315fb0a635a5e71cf4f957583c0831ab50d75a22addd933c99cec6"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-31":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"To meet requirements for certification to a management standard\",\"Verification of conformance with contractual requirements\",\"To contribute to the improvement of the management system\",\"To maintain confidence in the capability of a supplier\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following would be a purpose for an internal quality audit?\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Internal audits can be used to determine improvement opportunities for the quality management system. The other options apply to various types of external audits. [II.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["To meet requirements for certification to a management standard","Verification of conformance with contractual requirements","To contribute to the improvement of the management system","To maintain confidence in the capability of a supplier"],"stem":"Which of the following would be a purpose for an internal quality audit?","sub":"qsys","why":"Internal audits can be used to determine improvement opportunities for the quality management system. The other options apply to various types of external audits. [II.D.1]"},"revision":"962766c304aa1111d0d01f5015f1bbe2d8d1987a1d06fa68e37c8df8d4b035ac"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-310":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Repeatability\",\"Reproducibility\",\"Linearity\",\"Random error\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following components of a measurement system contributes to its accuracy?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"A measurement system''s accuracy consists of gage bias, linearity, and stability; repeatability and reproducibility describe its precision instead. [IV.F]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Repeatability","Reproducibility","Linearity","Random error"],"stem":"Which of the following components of a measurement system contributes to its accuracy?","sub":"ppc","why":"A measurement system''s accuracy consists of gage bias, linearity, and stability; repeatability and reproducibility describe its precision instead. [IV.F]"},"revision":"d1be74bb21d88e7255b5dda1af4d0bcdea6d7d42ae5bb304ace7a24f511b634c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-311":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Bias\",\"Correlation\",\"Precision\",\"Calibration\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following increases measurement system error?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Measurement system error can be attributed to gage bias, stability, linearity, repeatability, and reproducibility; correlation instead measures the linear relationship between two quantitative variables and doesn''t contribute to measurement error. [IV.F]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Bias","Correlation","Precision","Calibration"],"stem":"Which of the following increases measurement system error?","sub":"ppc","why":"Measurement system error can be attributed to gage bias, stability, linearity, repeatability, and reproducibility; correlation instead measures the linear relationship between two quantitative variables and doesn''t contribute to measurement error. [IV.F]"},"revision":"1fb90b2e1f0035a8e65f242f4006757c4d13573171828bcab5b3cf7ca411763b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-312":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"-0.003\",\"0.003\",\"0.041\",\"5.997\"],\"stem\":\"The length of a part should be 6.00 cm. A particular part is repeatedly measured six times, yielding the following measurements: 6.05, 5.94, 5.98, 6.01, 6.03, 5.97. What is the resulting bias for this part?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Bias = observed average - reference value. The observed average = (6.05+5.94+5.98+6.01+6.03+5.97)/6 = 5.997, so bias = 5.997 - 6.00 = -0.003. [IV.F]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["-0.003","0.003","0.041","5.997"],"stem":"The length of a part should be 6.00 cm. A particular part is repeatedly measured six times, yielding the following measurements: 6.05, 5.94, 5.98, 6.01, 6.03, 5.97. What is the resulting bias for this part?","sub":"ppc","why":"Bias = observed average - reference value. The observed average = (6.05+5.94+5.98+6.01+6.03+5.97)/6 = 5.997, so bias = 5.997 - 6.00 = -0.003. [IV.F]"},"revision":"0cdf981d5d544da4fd5e8e51e403dcf009ce915b360bf44a4b06e7711ed687de"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-313":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"There is evidence of nonlinearity in this measurement system.\",\"There is evidence of stability issues in this measurement system.\",\"The measurement system is not reproducible.\",\"The measurement system is not repeatable.\"],\"stem\":\"The lengths of three parts have the following reference values: 3.00 cm, 6.00 cm, 12.00 cm. Ten repeated measurements were taken for each type of part. The observed averages of these three samples were: 2.998, 5.843, 12.275. Which of the following statements is true?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Computing bias (observed average - reference value) for each part size shows -0.002, -0.157, and 0.275 as reference value increases from 3 to 12 cm; bias worsening as part size increases indicates nonlinearity in the measurement system. [IV.F]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["There is evidence of nonlinearity in this measurement system.","There is evidence of stability issues in this measurement system.","The measurement system is not reproducible.","The measurement system is not repeatable."],"stem":"The lengths of three parts have the following reference values: 3.00 cm, 6.00 cm, 12.00 cm. Ten repeated measurements were taken for each type of part. The observed averages of these three samples were: 2.998, 5.843, 12.275. Which of the following statements is true?","sub":"ppc","why":"Computing bias (observed average - reference value) for each part size shows -0.002, -0.157, and 0.275 as reference value increases from 3 to 12 cm; bias worsening as part size increases indicates nonlinearity in the measurement system. [IV.F]"},"revision":"102303083ee4dd96ccae46fc11ccb14cf77fd88838c98e6a4747807da0bfe306"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-314":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"the difference between the recorded measurement and the target value of a quality characteristic.\",\"the variability present when the same part is measured multiple times under the same conditions.\",\"the ability to get the same degree of bias in a measurement over time.\",\"the ability to get the same degree of bias in a measurement for parts of different sizes.\"],\"stem\":\"The precision of a measurement system can be defined as:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Precision is the variability observed when the same part is repeatedly measured under identical conditions using the same system; it is made up of repeatability and reproducibility, distinct from accuracy (closeness to the target/reference value). [IV.F]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["the difference between the recorded measurement and the target value of a quality characteristic.","the variability present when the same part is measured multiple times under the same conditions.","the ability to get the same degree of bias in a measurement over time.","the ability to get the same degree of bias in a measurement for parts of different sizes."],"stem":"The precision of a measurement system can be defined as:","sub":"ppc","why":"Precision is the variability observed when the same part is repeatedly measured under identical conditions using the same system; it is made up of repeatability and reproducibility, distinct from accuracy (closeness to the target/reference value). [IV.F]"},"revision":"8df1636494144f978291bb7b5aa41a739bd2c98bc49969a20a0f28c253e84af0"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-315":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.6376\",\"0.7210\",\"1.1308\",\"1.4369\"],\"stem\":\"In a gage R&R study, the variance associated with repeatability was found to be 0.8842 and the variance associated with reproducibility was found to be 0.2466. What is the variance associated with measurement error?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Measurement error variance = reproducibility variance + repeatability variance = 0.8842 + 0.2466 = 1.1308. [IV.F]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.6376","0.7210","1.1308","1.4369"],"stem":"In a gage R&R study, the variance associated with repeatability was found to be 0.8842 and the variance associated with reproducibility was found to be 0.2466. What is the variance associated with measurement error?","sub":"ppc","why":"Measurement error variance = reproducibility variance + repeatability variance = 0.8842 + 0.2466 = 1.1308. [IV.F]"},"revision":"df9bea126e27f35a081b33e9d632891939f7e0de5c5656edbc09ff23e04838f0"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-316":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0\",\"0.867\",\"1.233\",\"1.828\"],\"stem\":\"The ANOVA table below displays the results of a gage R&R study where two operators measured five parts three times each using the same gage. Source df SS MS F p Part 4 44 11 12.69 0.015 Operator 1 0.033 0.033 0.04 0.854 Part × Operator 4 3.467 0.867 0.70 0.599 Error 20 24.667 1.233 Total 29 72.167 Using this full model, what is the measurement error variability?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Using the ANOVA table''s mean squares, the operator and part x operator variance components come out negative and are set to 0, leaving repeatability (error) variance = MSE = 1.233 as the full measurement error variability. [IV.F]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0","0.867","1.233","1.828"],"stem":"The ANOVA table below displays the results of a gage R&R study where two operators measured five parts three times each using the same gage. Source df SS MS F p Part 4 44 11 12.69 0.015 Operator 1 0.033 0.033 0.04 0.854 Part × Operator 4 3.467 0.867 0.70 0.599 Error 20 24.667 1.233 Total 29 72.167 Using this full model, what is the measurement error variability?","sub":"ppc","why":"Using the ANOVA table''s mean squares, the operator and part x operator variance components come out negative and are set to 0, leaving repeatability (error) variance = MSE = 1.233 as the full measurement error variability. [IV.F]"},"revision":"ab3d4a8f58645f7c9e3db442e5c1409f9b45c83a74d38e739450b109b8797784"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-317":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"There is a significant interaction between parts and operators.\",\"More operator training is necessary in this process.\",\"This is a capable process.\",\"The variation associated with repeatability should be investigated.\"],\"stem\":\"A gage R&R study measured two operators on five parts, three replicates each. The full ANOVA model found: repeatability (error) variance = 1.233 (40.28% of total gage R&R variation), reproducibility variance = 0 (0%), and part-to-part variance = 1.828 (59.72%). The p-values show no significant operator effect and no significant part x operator interaction. Based on these results, which of the following statements is true?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"The ANOVA table shows no significant operator effect or part x operator interaction, but repeatability accounts for a large share (40.28%) of total gage R&R variation, indicating the variation associated with repeatability should be investigated. [IV.F]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["There is a significant interaction between parts and operators.","More operator training is necessary in this process.","This is a capable process.","The variation associated with repeatability should be investigated."],"stem":"A gage R&R study measured two operators on five parts, three replicates each. The full ANOVA model found: repeatability (error) variance = 1.233 (40.28% of total gage R&R variation), reproducibility variance = 0 (0%), and part-to-part variance = 1.828 (59.72%). The p-values show no significant operator effect and no significant part x operator interaction. Based on these results, which of the following statements is true?","sub":"ppc","why":"The ANOVA table shows no significant operator effect or part x operator interaction, but repeatability accounts for a large share (40.28%) of total gage R&R variation, indicating the variation associated with repeatability should be investigated. [IV.F]"},"revision":"47dfb3eb7921b9922bdb07c7dcce42d55fd4cd78e584c061230140f036ec1886"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-318":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"0.333\",\"0.370\",\"0.406\",\"0.523\"],\"stem\":\"From the same gage R&R study (measurement error variance = 1.233), suppose the upper and lower specification limits for the quality characteristic are 30 and 10, respectively. What is the precision-to-tolerance ratio for this process?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"From the prior gage R&R study, measurement error variance = 1.233. Precision-to-tolerance ratio PTR = 6√1.233 / (30-10) = 0.333. [IV.F]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["0.333","0.370","0.406","0.523"],"stem":"From the same gage R&R study (measurement error variance = 1.233), suppose the upper and lower specification limits for the quality characteristic are 30 and 10, respectively. What is the precision-to-tolerance ratio for this process?","sub":"ppc","why":"From the prior gage R&R study, measurement error variance = 1.233. Precision-to-tolerance ratio PTR = 6√1.233 / (30-10) = 0.333. [IV.F]"},"revision":"0ebb51a55aa0484c0a86a6a57161fcfa79f8d96df42ea710b377abb2a657709f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-319":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"There are significant differences between operators.\",\"The operators are consistent in their use of the gage.\",\"The gage is not able to distinguish between parts.\",\"The gage is able to distinguish between parts.\"],\"stem\":\"A gage R&R study was performed for a process using ten parts, two operators, and three replicates. The resulting R-chart by operator shows: UCL = 9.27, R̄ = 3.6, LCL = 0, with every sample range for both operators falling within these control limits. What conclusions can be drawn from this control chart?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"An R-chart by operator with UCL = 9.27, R̄ = 3.6, and LCL = 0 shows no points outside the control limits for either operator, indicating the operators are consistent in their use of the gage with no special causes of variation. [IV.F]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["There are significant differences between operators.","The operators are consistent in their use of the gage.","The gage is not able to distinguish between parts.","The gage is able to distinguish between parts."],"stem":"A gage R&R study was performed for a process using ten parts, two operators, and three replicates. The resulting R-chart by operator shows: UCL = 9.27, R̄ = 3.6, LCL = 0, with every sample range for both operators falling within these control limits. What conclusions can be drawn from this control chart?","sub":"ppc","why":"An R-chart by operator with UCL = 9.27, R̄ = 3.6, and LCL = 0 shows no points outside the control limits for either operator, indicating the operators are consistent in their use of the gage with no special causes of variation. [IV.F]"},"revision":"380e9c6f2618797af0bbded96c074376446a88fc75f3b07123e9608793fa8b01"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-32":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"the auditee.\",\"the lead auditor.\",\"management where the corrective measures are needed.\",\"line workers where the corrective measures are needed.\"],\"stem\":\"A quality audit has been conducted and some corrective measures are recommended. The party responsible for planning and implementing these measures is:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"The organization or company being audited (auditee) is responsible for making sure the corrective measures identified are addressed. [II.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["the auditee.","the lead auditor.","management where the corrective measures are needed.","line workers where the corrective measures are needed."],"stem":"A quality audit has been conducted and some corrective measures are recommended. The party responsible for planning and implementing these measures is:","sub":"qsys","why":"The organization or company being audited (auditee) is responsible for making sure the corrective measures identified are addressed. [II.D.2]"},"revision":"fcf8202388090144f4b5873cbb7524d7f6f25c22dc2c696e642b91acc0e8484e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-320":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Normal\",\"Exponential\",\"Weibull\",\"Gamma\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following distributions best describes the data set below? Goodness of Fit Test Results Normal Weibull AD = 1.456 AD = 0.214 p < 0.005 p > 0.250 Exponential Gamma AD = 1.456 AD = 0.581 p < 0.003 p = 0.152\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The goodness-of-fit test determines which distribution best fits a data set, based on probability plot fit and/or the test''s p-value. Of the four distributions tested, Weibull has the highest p-value (p > 0.250), making it the best fit. [VI.A.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Normal","Exponential","Weibull","Gamma"],"stem":"Which of the following distributions best describes the data set below? Goodness of Fit Test Results Normal Weibull AD = 1.456 AD = 0.214 p < 0.005 p > 0.250 Exponential Gamma AD = 1.456 AD = 0.581 p < 0.003 p = 0.152","sub":"quant","why":"The goodness-of-fit test determines which distribution best fits a data set, based on probability plot fit and/or the test''s p-value. Of the four distributions tested, Weibull has the highest p-value (p > 0.250), making it the best fit. [VI.A.3]"},"revision":"212da48c3a26b56302cc590eeee992de91f53d958e9a1878394a43abfb736e4d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-321":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Minimum and maximum\",\"Minimum and mean\",\"Maximum and mean\",\"Median and mean\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following descriptive statistics are typically found in a box plot?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A box plot divides a sorted data set using the minimum, Q1, median, Q3, and maximum; the mean is not typically identified on a box plot. [VI.A.7]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Minimum and maximum","Minimum and mean","Maximum and mean","Median and mean"],"stem":"Which of the following descriptive statistics are typically found in a box plot?","sub":"quant","why":"A box plot divides a sorted data set using the minimum, Q1, median, Q3, and maximum; the mean is not typically identified on a box plot. [VI.A.7]"},"revision":"1ef6ae206345e5b1a2d3d4c434d8a423edc818f62a23b1cf1208a049ce802eba"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-322":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"pattern\":\"s-curve\",\"sourceN\":20,\"title\":\"Normal probability plot\",\"type\":\"normal-prob\",\"yAxisLabel\":\"Normal score\"},\"options\":[\"The normal distribution is not a reasonable model for the time to failure.\",\"The Weibull distribution is a reasonable model for the time to failure.\",\"The normal distribution is a reasonable model for the time to failure.\",\"Not enough information is provided to draw conclusions.\"],\"stem\":\"A team measured the time to failure of twenty components and plotted the data on a normal probability plot. The plotted points do not fall along a straight line. What conclusions can be drawn from this plot?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A normal distribution is a reasonable model only if the data points on a normal probability plot fall along a straight line. Since the plotted points do not fall in a straight line, the normal distribution is not an appropriate model for the time to failure. [VI.A.7]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"pattern":"s-curve","sourceN":20,"title":"Normal probability plot","type":"normal-prob","yAxisLabel":"Normal score"},"options":["The normal distribution is not a reasonable model for the time to failure.","The Weibull distribution is a reasonable model for the time to failure.","The normal distribution is a reasonable model for the time to failure.","Not enough information is provided to draw conclusions."],"stem":"A team measured the time to failure of twenty components and plotted the data on a normal probability plot. The plotted points do not fall along a straight line. What conclusions can be drawn from this plot?","sub":"quant","why":"A normal distribution is a reasonable model only if the data points on a normal probability plot fall along a straight line. Since the plotted points do not fall in a straight line, the normal distribution is not an appropriate model for the time to failure. [VI.A.7]"},"revision":"892ec6013d06f8ad19407ef44c2628b563a1030f6ec9f7761893ad22d840846b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-323":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.08\",\"0.26\",\"0.43\",\"0.61\"],\"stem\":\"The contingency table below describes the relationship between the number of nonconforming components produced and time of day. Conforming Nonconforming Time parts parts Totals Day 94 5 99 Evening 87 10 97 Overnight 65 23 88 Totals 246 38 284 Given that a part is made overnight, what is the probability it is nonconforming?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"P(nonconforming | overnight) = P(nonconforming and overnight) / P(overnight) = (23/284) / (88/284) = 23/88 ≈ 0.26. [VI.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.08","0.26","0.43","0.61"],"stem":"The contingency table below describes the relationship between the number of nonconforming components produced and time of day. Conforming Nonconforming Time parts parts Totals Day 94 5 99 Evening 87 10 97 Overnight 65 23 88 Totals 246 38 284 Given that a part is made overnight, what is the probability it is nonconforming?","sub":"quant","why":"P(nonconforming | overnight) = P(nonconforming and overnight) / P(overnight) = (23/284) / (88/284) = 23/88 ≈ 0.26. [VI.B.3]"},"revision":"adf0b6c376276ea5f61d54e38fb3cc02b83efe3a2989bf6bf03ffc29c187bb52"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-324":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0\",\"0.27\",\"0.5771\",\"0.7597\"],\"stem\":\"Suppose the number of errors in an invoice at a company has the probability mass function (written in table form) shown below. What is the expected number of errors in a randomly selected invoice? Number of errors X 0 1 2 3 4 Probability 0.86 0.06 0.04 0.03 0.01\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"E(X) = Σx f(x) = 0(0.86) + 1(0.06) + 2(0.04) + 3(0.03) + 4(0.01) = 0.27. [VI.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0","0.27","0.5771","0.7597"],"stem":"Suppose the number of errors in an invoice at a company has the probability mass function (written in table form) shown below. What is the expected number of errors in a randomly selected invoice? Number of errors X 0 1 2 3 4 Probability 0.86 0.06 0.04 0.03 0.01","sub":"quant","why":"E(X) = Σx f(x) = 0(0.86) + 1(0.06) + 2(0.04) + 3(0.03) + 4(0.01) = 0.27. [VI.C.2]"},"revision":"d68509de0362dd39c145d831b65af02fdc8d888d4ba6929032aacbda8d5a09af"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-325":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"–3.266\",\"0.607\",\"2.069\",\"3.266\"],\"stem\":\"A bottling factory claims that the volume of soda filled in bottles is 18 oz. You want to test whether the true mean volume of soda is different than 18 oz. The volume of a random sample of twenty-four bottles had a mean of 17.98 oz. with a standard deviation of 0.03 oz. Using a significance level of 5%, what is the appropriate test statistic for this hypothesis test?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"For H0: μ=18 vs Ha: μ≠18, t = (17.98-18)/(0.03/√24) = -3.266. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["–3.266","0.607","2.069","3.266"],"stem":"A bottling factory claims that the volume of soda filled in bottles is 18 oz. You want to test whether the true mean volume of soda is different than 18 oz. The volume of a random sample of twenty-four bottles had a mean of 17.98 oz. with a standard deviation of 0.03 oz. Using a significance level of 5%, what is the appropriate test statistic for this hypothesis test?","sub":"quant","why":"For H0: μ=18 vs Ha: μ≠18, t = (17.98-18)/(0.03/√24) = -3.266. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"6fb6d2a25b17978f69c8d7e8a3d3a0aa895b15a26e65a7b47c9d4d86cda4ac9d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-326":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"There is not sufficient evidence to conclude that the mean volume is different than 18 oz.\",\"There is sufficient evidence to conclude that the mean volume is different than 18 oz.\",\"18 would be contained in a 95% confidence interval of the population mean.\",\"18 would be contained in a 90% confidence interval of the population mean.\"],\"stem\":\"Suppose that the p-value of the hypothesis test posed in the previous question (question 40) is 0.003. What conclusions can be made (use significance level 5%)?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The p-value (0.003) is less than the significance level (0.05), so there is sufficient evidence to reject the null hypothesis and conclude the mean volume differs from 18 oz; a 95% confidence interval would not contain 18. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["There is not sufficient evidence to conclude that the mean volume is different than 18 oz.","There is sufficient evidence to conclude that the mean volume is different than 18 oz.","18 would be contained in a 95% confidence interval of the population mean.","18 would be contained in a 90% confidence interval of the population mean."],"stem":"Suppose that the p-value of the hypothesis test posed in the previous question (question 40) is 0.003. What conclusions can be made (use significance level 5%)?","sub":"quant","why":"The p-value (0.003) is less than the significance level (0.05), so there is sufficient evidence to reject the null hypothesis and conclude the mean volume differs from 18 oz; a 95% confidence interval would not contain 18. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"02c11f8711f4f9e293b152cd0a9ea0ebf75bc52a8711dc4a6265c84e0df78dae"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-327":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"H0: μ = 25 versus Ha: μ ≠ 25\",\"H0: μ > 25 versus Ha: μ = 25\",\"H0: μ = 25 versus Ha: μ < 25\",\"H0: μ = 25 versus Ha: μ > 25\"],\"stem\":\"Speed bumps were installed in a neighborhood to slow traffic. After installation, the speeds after the last speed bump of a random sample of twenty cars were recorded. The mean speed was 24.50 mph and the standard deviation was 2.3 mph. The team wanted to determine if the average speed is less than 25 mph. What are the appropriate hypotheses for this hypothesis test?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"This is a left-tailed test on a single population mean with unknown variance, assessing whether the mean is 25 mph or less: H0: μ=25 versus Ha: μ<25. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["H0: μ = 25 versus Ha: μ ≠ 25","H0: μ > 25 versus Ha: μ = 25","H0: μ = 25 versus Ha: μ < 25","H0: μ = 25 versus Ha: μ > 25"],"stem":"Speed bumps were installed in a neighborhood to slow traffic. After installation, the speeds after the last speed bump of a random sample of twenty cars were recorded. The mean speed was 24.50 mph and the standard deviation was 2.3 mph. The team wanted to determine if the average speed is less than 25 mph. What are the appropriate hypotheses for this hypothesis test?","sub":"quant","why":"This is a left-tailed test on a single population mean with unknown variance, assessing whether the mean is 25 mph or less: H0: μ=25 versus Ha: μ<25. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"6ea34f6738dfbb79866efb558dad0868c312ac3b917db72715853a9429ce3b0e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-328":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Reject H , and conclude that the proportion of patient falls equals 0.10.\",\"Reject H , and conclude that the proportion of patient falls is more than 0.10.\",\"Do not reject H ; conclude that the proportion of patient falls equals 0.10.\",\"Do not reject H ; conclude that the proportion of patient falls is more than 0.10.\"],\"stem\":\"A team investigated the number of patient falls in a hospital. Fifty-six patients were randomly selected. Out of the fifty-six patients, there were seven recorded patient falls. The hospital wanted to determine whether the proportion of patient falls was more than 10%. At the 5% significance level, what is your conclusion about this hypothesis test?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The sample proportion is 7/56=0.125. For H0: p=0.10 vs Ha: p>0.10 (right-tailed, α=0.05), z0 = (0.125-0.10)/√(0.10(0.90)/56) = 0.624, which is less than the critical value 1.645, so H0 is not rejected. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Reject H , and conclude that the proportion of patient falls equals 0.10.","Reject H , and conclude that the proportion of patient falls is more than 0.10.","Do not reject H ; conclude that the proportion of patient falls equals 0.10.","Do not reject H ; conclude that the proportion of patient falls is more than 0.10."],"stem":"A team investigated the number of patient falls in a hospital. Fifty-six patients were randomly selected. Out of the fifty-six patients, there were seven recorded patient falls. The hospital wanted to determine whether the proportion of patient falls was more than 10%. At the 5% significance level, what is your conclusion about this hypothesis test?","sub":"quant","why":"The sample proportion is 7/56=0.125. For H0: p=0.10 vs Ha: p>0.10 (right-tailed, α=0.05), z0 = (0.125-0.10)/√(0.10(0.90)/56) = 0.624, which is less than the critical value 1.645, so H0 is not rejected. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"22849ba74f5037635758e59e0a2dbe34cde8e9663d2b78e0155d5211f0ab8421"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-329":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Yes, since 0 is not in the confidence interval.\",\"Yes, since 0 is in the confidence interval.\",\"No, since 0 is not in the confidence interval.\",\"No, since 0 is in the confidence interval.\"],\"stem\":\"Two methods for filling a bottle are being compared at a factory. The 90% confidence interval for the difference in volume for two independent random samples of the filling methods was found to be (–0.091, –0.049). Based on this confidence interval, is there evidence that the two filling methods are different?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The hypothesized value of 0 is not contained in the confidence interval (-0.091, -0.049), so there is evidence of a difference between the two population means. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Yes, since 0 is not in the confidence interval.","Yes, since 0 is in the confidence interval.","No, since 0 is not in the confidence interval.","No, since 0 is in the confidence interval."],"stem":"Two methods for filling a bottle are being compared at a factory. The 90% confidence interval for the difference in volume for two independent random samples of the filling methods was found to be (–0.091, –0.049). Based on this confidence interval, is there evidence that the two filling methods are different?","sub":"quant","why":"The hypothesized value of 0 is not contained in the confidence interval (-0.091, -0.049), so there is evidence of a difference between the two population means. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"d1b32b5eb0cfdb7711631a860ba7085a214e07ff8b948f356ef79a1bba256175"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-33":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"always carried out.\",\"often poorly completed.\",\"unnecessary and cause more stress than it is worth.\",\"performed before root cause analysis.\"],\"stem\":\"Corrective action and verification that nonconformities have been addressed are:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Actually taking corrective action when nonconformities have been identified is the one step in the process that is most often poorly executed. [II.D.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["always carried out.","often poorly completed.","unnecessary and cause more stress than it is worth.","performed before root cause analysis."],"stem":"Corrective action and verification that nonconformities have been addressed are:","sub":"qsys","why":"Actually taking corrective action when nonconformities have been identified is the one step in the process that is most often poorly executed. [II.D.3]"},"revision":"42f24497b9d3c3be306324639eca8b299c6d67b75e730b3d9b70d0658b4118d7"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-330":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"–2.402\",\"2.402\",\"2.576\",\"2.678\"],\"stem\":\"An improvement project was done at a factory to decrease packaging time. The following data were collected before and after the improvement project: Before - sample mean = 3.52, sample variance = 2.34, sample size = 28. After - sample mean = 2.10, sample variance = 2.04, sample size = 25. A hypothesis test was performed with H0: μBefore - μAfter = 0 versus Ha: μBefore - μAfter > 0. What is the critical value for this hypothesis test (use α = 0.01)?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"For H0: μBefore - μAfter = 0 vs Ha: μBefore - μAfter > 0 with α=0.01, the critical value is t0.01,51 = 2.402. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["–2.402","2.402","2.576","2.678"],"stem":"An improvement project was done at a factory to decrease packaging time. The following data were collected before and after the improvement project: Before - sample mean = 3.52, sample variance = 2.34, sample size = 28. After - sample mean = 2.10, sample variance = 2.04, sample size = 25. A hypothesis test was performed with H0: μBefore - μAfter = 0 versus Ha: μBefore - μAfter > 0. What is the critical value for this hypothesis test (use α = 0.01)?","sub":"quant","why":"For H0: μBefore - μAfter = 0 vs Ha: μBefore - μAfter > 0 with α=0.01, the critical value is t0.01,51 = 2.402. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"ebb8b7f3cc4b29e4aa60f1e8ba7f21cccd03a83f1630bc5aabac8099bd0258fe"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-331":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"1.071\",\"1.147\",\"1.930\",\"3.494\"],\"stem\":\"Consider the scenario in the previous problem (question 45). The project team would like to determine whether the variance of packaging time has changed at the 10% significance level. What is the test statistic for the appropriate hypothesis test?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"For H0: σ1²=σ2² vs Ha: σ1²≠σ2², F0 = s1²/s2² = 2.34/2.04 = 1.147. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["1.071","1.147","1.930","3.494"],"stem":"Consider the scenario in the previous problem (question 45). The project team would like to determine whether the variance of packaging time has changed at the 10% significance level. What is the test statistic for the appropriate hypothesis test?","sub":"quant","why":"For H0: σ1²=σ2² vs Ha: σ1²≠σ2², F0 = s1²/s2² = 2.34/2.04 = 1.147. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"1560a2fee14edb99175a75edd7c240186af34c446e442f42a9dd8a5c8a925e4d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-332":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"(–0.004, 0.018)\",\"(–0.002, 0.016)\",\"(0, 0.016)\",\"(0.006, 0.008)\"],\"stem\":\"Two machines produce the same parts. A random sample of 1,250 parts from machine 1 has twenty-eight that are nonconforming, and a random sample of 1,175 parts from machine 2 has eighteen that are nonconforming. Find the 90% confidence interval for the difference between the proportions of nonconforming parts from machines 1 and 2.\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With p̂1=0.0224, p̂2=0.01532, and pooled p̂=0.01897, the 90% CI for p1-p2 is (p̂1-p̂2) ± 1.645√[p̂(1-p̂)(1/n1+1/n2)] = (-0.002, 0.016). [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["(–0.004, 0.018)","(–0.002, 0.016)","(0, 0.016)","(0.006, 0.008)"],"stem":"Two machines produce the same parts. A random sample of 1,250 parts from machine 1 has twenty-eight that are nonconforming, and a random sample of 1,175 parts from machine 2 has eighteen that are nonconforming. Find the 90% confidence interval for the difference between the proportions of nonconforming parts from machines 1 and 2.","sub":"quant","why":"With p̂1=0.0224, p̂2=0.01532, and pooled p̂=0.01897, the 90% CI for p1-p2 is (p̂1-p̂2) ± 1.645√[p̂(1-p̂)(1/n1+1/n2)] = (-0.002, 0.016). [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"2378a5da3027a530f814668537b536d3ddda7e58729801bc1326ad2851e6eca0"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-333":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.10\",\"0.20\",\"0.80\",\"0.90\"],\"stem\":\"A hypothesis test is performed at the 10% significance level. The power of the test is 0.80, or 80%. What is the type II error for this hypothesis test?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"β = P(Type II error) = 1 - Power = 1 - 0.80 = 0.20. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.10","0.20","0.80","0.90"],"stem":"A hypothesis test is performed at the 10% significance level. The power of the test is 0.80, or 80%. What is the type II error for this hypothesis test?","sub":"quant","why":"β = P(Type II error) = 1 - Power = 1 - 0.80 = 0.20. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"92be43ea65784a8996847ddab0ee20c1a1123e7627972062a9c804c3ee30f92a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-334":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Sampling error\",\"Type I error\",\"Type II error\",\"No error has been made\"],\"stem\":\"When the null hypothesis is not rejected when in fact the null hypothesis is false, what type of error has been made?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A Type II error occurs when H0 is not rejected even though it is actually false; a Type I error occurs when a true H0 is rejected. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Sampling error","Type I error","Type II error","No error has been made"],"stem":"When the null hypothesis is not rejected when in fact the null hypothesis is false, what type of error has been made?","sub":"quant","why":"A Type II error occurs when H0 is not rejected even though it is actually false; a Type I error occurs when a true H0 is rejected. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"7844b2ad16dee7963a6d6057854c63f5c24b3a9c8367ed8634e3efabc091efb4"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-335":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Normal\",\"t\",\"Poisson\",\"Uniform\"],\"stem\":\"A team would like to compare the proportion of defective widgets produced by two machines. What distribution does the test statistic for this hypothesis test follow?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The two-sample proportion test statistic z0 = (p̂1-p̂2)/√[p̂(1-p̂)(1/n1+1/n2)] follows a standard normal distribution for sufficiently large sample sizes. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Normal","t","Poisson","Uniform"],"stem":"A team would like to compare the proportion of defective widgets produced by two machines. What distribution does the test statistic for this hypothesis test follow?","sub":"quant","why":"The two-sample proportion test statistic z0 = (p̂1-p̂2)/√[p̂(1-p̂)(1/n1+1/n2)] follows a standard normal distribution for sufficiently large sample sizes. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"b86690e6530fc80f2353393d051f64f65281b2566faa4049975ead690e7aa203"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-336":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Paired t-test\",\"Two-sample t-test\",\"Two-sample p-test\",\"Goodness-of-fit test\"],\"stem\":\"The heart rates of twelve individuals were measured using two different pieces of equipment. A team would like to compare the measured heart rates of the two pieces of equipment. Which hypothesis test would be most appropriate?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A paired t-test is most appropriate because the same twelve individuals were measured twice, creating a strong relationship between the two measurements. [VI.D.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Paired t-test","Two-sample t-test","Two-sample p-test","Goodness-of-fit test"],"stem":"The heart rates of twelve individuals were measured using two different pieces of equipment. A team would like to compare the measured heart rates of the two pieces of equipment. Which hypothesis test would be most appropriate?","sub":"quant","why":"A paired t-test is most appropriate because the same twelve individuals were measured twice, creating a strong relationship between the two measurements. [VI.D.3]"},"revision":"c42930e807b590ba55a1f7cee4547b0ae3892aea35eb6ea8d3ab0f469f8536e3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-337":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.450\",\"0.650\",\"2.576\",\"4.604\"],\"stem\":\"The length of five components was measured by two inspectors (data shown below). Assuming the data follow a normal distribution, what is the test statistic for the appropriate hypothesis test for this scenario? Use α = 0.01. Component 1 2 3 4 5 Inspector A 10.2 9.8 10.1 10.3 10.4 Inspector B 10.0 9.9 10.3 10.0 10.3\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"This is a paired t-test since the same components were measured twice (once per inspector). Using differences d = A-B, d̄=0.06, sd=0.2074, t0 = 0.06/(0.2074/√5) = 0.65, which is less than the critical value 4.604, so H0 is not rejected. [VI.D.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.450","0.650","2.576","4.604"],"stem":"The length of five components was measured by two inspectors (data shown below). Assuming the data follow a normal distribution, what is the test statistic for the appropriate hypothesis test for this scenario? Use α = 0.01. Component 1 2 3 4 5 Inspector A 10.2 9.8 10.1 10.3 10.4 Inspector B 10.0 9.9 10.3 10.0 10.3","sub":"quant","why":"This is a paired t-test since the same components were measured twice (once per inspector). Using differences d = A-B, d̄=0.06, sd=0.2074, t0 = 0.06/(0.2074/√5) = 0.65, which is less than the critical value 4.604, so H0 is not rejected. [VI.D.3]"},"revision":"68b68133fcf2f3e29bfa0cf56f3f18d95332dfb6700a42b87135a66e3b725abe"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-338":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0\",\"1.96\",\"4.514\",\"7.815\"],\"stem\":\"A team at a hospital did a project on medication errors. A random sample of medication errors yielded the following results: Type of error Number of errors Incorrect dose 32 Wrong dose 28 Incorrect form 45 Wrong amount 35 Total 140 The team would like to test the hypothesis at the 5% significance level that the medication errors occur with equal probabilities. What is the value of the test statistic for this hypothesis test?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"This is a goodness-of-fit test for H0: p1=p2=p3=p4=0.25. With expected counts Ei=35, χ²0 = Σ(Oi-Ei)²/Ei = 4.514. [VI.D.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0","1.96","4.514","7.815"],"stem":"A team at a hospital did a project on medication errors. A random sample of medication errors yielded the following results: Type of error Number of errors Incorrect dose 32 Wrong dose 28 Incorrect form 45 Wrong amount 35 Total 140 The team would like to test the hypothesis at the 5% significance level that the medication errors occur with equal probabilities. What is the value of the test statistic for this hypothesis test?","sub":"quant","why":"This is a goodness-of-fit test for H0: p1=p2=p3=p4=0.25. With expected counts Ei=35, χ²0 = Σ(Oi-Ei)²/Ei = 4.514. [VI.D.4]"},"revision":"a9b331367c8acc6e8c4abae9ba6383a0eecafdfa4ee8c4684c4f3f4172b9e866"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-339":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"t\",\"chi-square\",\"F\",\"Normal\"],\"stem\":\"What distribution does the test statistic used in the goodness-of-fit test follow?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The goodness-of-fit test statistic χ²0 = Σ(Oi-Ei)²/Ei follows a chi-square distribution with k-1 degrees of freedom, where k is the number of categories. [VI.D.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["t","chi-square","F","Normal"],"stem":"What distribution does the test statistic used in the goodness-of-fit test follow?","sub":"quant","why":"The goodness-of-fit test statistic χ²0 = Σ(Oi-Ei)²/Ei follows a chi-square distribution with k-1 degrees of freedom, where k is the number of categories. [VI.D.4]"},"revision":"125a8fb13f5abefeae7e8bc66ad357f29e59377f9f712742ac67cac3330f6622"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-34":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"the opening meeting (or pre-examination).\",\"the closing meeting.\",\"the audit itself.\",\"no time; it is not necessary for the auditor to explain this information to the auditee.\"],\"stem\":\"The lead auditor must explain to the auditee the objectives of the audit as well as the methods that will be used. This is usually done during:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"Generally, an audit is carried out in three steps: (1) pre-examination— the auditor explains the objectives of the audit, introduces team members to senior management, and so on; (2) a “suitability” audit is conducted— comparison of documented procedures to the reference standard selected; and (3) implementation of the quality system is examined in depth, which may involve recommending corrective measures. [II.D.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["the opening meeting (or pre-examination).","the closing meeting.","the audit itself.","no time; it is not necessary for the auditor to explain this information to the auditee."],"stem":"The lead auditor must explain to the auditee the objectives of the audit as well as the methods that will be used. This is usually done during:","sub":"qsys","why":"Generally, an audit is carried out in three steps: (1) pre-examination— the auditor explains the objectives of the audit, introduces team members to senior management, and so on; (2) a “suitability” audit is conducted— comparison of documented procedures to the reference standard selected; and (3) implementation of the quality system is examined in depth, which may involve recommending corrective measures. [II.D.3]"},"revision":"da3abc1d878ee24717806edb74b97ec178fcfa50b320e17dedd06d4a7930ed72"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-340":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Reject H ; conclude that the three means are the same.\",\"Reject H ; conclude that at least one mean is different than the others.\",\"Do not reject H ; conclude that the three means are the same.\",\"Do not reject H ; conclude that at least one mean is different than the others.\"],\"stem\":\"A study was done to compare the elasticity of a polymer mortar during a compression test at three different strain rates. Below is the ANOVA table for the statistical analysis. What is your conclusion at the 5% significance level? Source dF SS MS F Strain rate 2 0.1614 0.0807 1.70 Error 12 0.5688 0.0474 Total 14 0.7302\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Comparing the test statistic F0=1.70 to the critical value F0.05,2,12=19.41, since F0 < 19.41, H0 is not rejected and the three means are concluded to be the same. [VI.D.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Reject H ; conclude that the three means are the same.","Reject H ; conclude that at least one mean is different than the others.","Do not reject H ; conclude that the three means are the same.","Do not reject H ; conclude that at least one mean is different than the others."],"stem":"A study was done to compare the elasticity of a polymer mortar during a compression test at three different strain rates. Below is the ANOVA table for the statistical analysis. What is your conclusion at the 5% significance level? Source dF SS MS F Strain rate 2 0.1614 0.0807 1.70 Error 12 0.5688 0.0474 Total 14 0.7302","sub":"quant","why":"Comparing the test statistic F0=1.70 to the critical value F0.05,2,12=19.41, since F0 < 19.41, H0 is not rejected and the three means are concluded to be the same. [VI.D.5]"},"revision":"f5bd74669bc714d7a40391a8a2932dc7b90bf4f07ebfac9781162c2381232425"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-341":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Inhomogeneity of variance\",\"Observations are independent\",\"Observations are paired\",\"Observations are exponentially distributed\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is an assumption required for use and interpretation of a one-way ANOVA?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"One-way ANOVA assumes observations follow a normal distribution, are independent, and have constant variance across treatments; paired data is not a requirement. [VI.D.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Inhomogeneity of variance","Observations are independent","Observations are paired","Observations are exponentially distributed"],"stem":"Which of the following is an assumption required for use and interpretation of a one-way ANOVA?","sub":"quant","why":"One-way ANOVA assumes observations follow a normal distribution, are independent, and have constant variance across treatments; paired data is not a requirement. [VI.D.5]"},"revision":"9fecd82d60bcbf16b95d6e8cf9bcb71cf91a9c2355d5ad10249052cd42431e00"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-342":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"chart\":{\"parallel\":true,\"type\":\"interaction-plot\"},\"options\":[\"High levels of factor B tend to have smaller response values.\",\"There is not an interaction effect between factors A and B.\",\"There is an interaction effect between factors A and B.\",\"Factors A and B do not have an effect on the response variable.\"],\"stem\":\"A two-way ANOVA hypothesis test was used to test the effects of two factors, A and B, on a response variable. An interaction plot shows that as factor A moves across its levels, the two lines for factor B''s low and high levels stay parallel, following identical up-and-down patterns (though the high level of factor B does sit at a higher response value than the low level). What conclusions can be made from this interaction plot?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"An interaction plot with parallel lines across the levels of factor A (both factor B levels following identical up/down patterns) indicates no significant interaction between factors A and B, though high levels of factor B do correspond to higher response values. [VI.D.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"chart":{"parallel":true,"type":"interaction-plot"},"options":["High levels of factor B tend to have smaller response values.","There is not an interaction effect between factors A and B.","There is an interaction effect between factors A and B.","Factors A and B do not have an effect on the response variable."],"stem":"A two-way ANOVA hypothesis test was used to test the effects of two factors, A and B, on a response variable. An interaction plot shows that as factor A moves across its levels, the two lines for factor B''s low and high levels stay parallel, following identical up-and-down patterns (though the high level of factor B does sit at a higher response value than the low level). What conclusions can be made from this interaction plot?","sub":"quant","why":"An interaction plot with parallel lines across the levels of factor A (both factor B levels following identical up/down patterns) indicates no significant interaction between factors A and B, though high levels of factor B do correspond to higher response values. [VI.D.5]"},"revision":"1bc512fc7315a9384ba4d597f9844256b0735fe20a73b9b5d3a6505a5344e11b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-343":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Reject H0; conclude that type of parts produced and time of day are independent.\",\"Reject H0; conclude that type of parts produced and time of day are not independent.\",\"Do not reject H0; conclude that type of parts produced and time of day are independent.\",\"Do not reject H0; conclude that type of parts produced and time of day are not independent.\"],\"stem\":\"A contingency table cross-tabulates the number of conforming and nonconforming parts produced by time of day (day, evening, overnight shifts). A chi-square hypothesis test for independence between the type of part produced and time of day yields a test statistic of 19.071. What is the conclusion from this hypothesis test (use α = 0.05)?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With (r-1)(c-1)=2 degrees of freedom, the critical value χ²0.05,2 = 5.991. Since 19.071 > 5.991, H0 is rejected, and the type of part produced and time of day are concluded to not be independent. [VI.D.6]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Reject H0; conclude that type of parts produced and time of day are independent.","Reject H0; conclude that type of parts produced and time of day are not independent.","Do not reject H0; conclude that type of parts produced and time of day are independent.","Do not reject H0; conclude that type of parts produced and time of day are not independent."],"stem":"A contingency table cross-tabulates the number of conforming and nonconforming parts produced by time of day (day, evening, overnight shifts). A chi-square hypothesis test for independence between the type of part produced and time of day yields a test statistic of 19.071. What is the conclusion from this hypothesis test (use α = 0.05)?","sub":"quant","why":"With (r-1)(c-1)=2 degrees of freedom, the critical value χ²0.05,2 = 5.991. Since 19.071 > 5.991, H0 is rejected, and the type of part produced and time of day are concluded to not be independent. [VI.D.6]"},"revision":"9490e47e2455974e750d5d08a45f57a671aed2c33d7b148c3f28faa447130312"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-344":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"–8\",\"0.89\",\"1\",\"2.5\"],\"stem\":\"The fitted regression equation for two variables x and y is yˆ = 2.5x – 8. What is the slope of this equation?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The slope represents the change in y for a one-unit change in x; here that value is 2.5. [VI.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["–8","0.89","1","2.5"],"stem":"The fitted regression equation for two variables x and y is yˆ = 2.5x – 8. What is the slope of this equation?","sub":"quant","why":"The slope represents the change in y for a one-unit change in x; here that value is 2.5. [VI.E.1]"},"revision":"0a472ad34bb5c94ae516b01261d4bf9c3d335fe9ed52cf5ceef7c78eb1a20908"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-345":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"0.23\",\"140\",\"162.2\",\"169.9\"],\"stem\":\"A team wants to predict a person’s cholesterol based on their weight in pounds. The fitted regression equation is Cholesterol = 140 + 0.23 × Weight. What is the predicted cholesterol of a person who weighs 130 pounds?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Substituting Weight=130 into the equation: Cholesterol = 140 + 0.23(130) = 169.9. [VI.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["0.23","140","162.2","169.9"],"stem":"A team wants to predict a person’s cholesterol based on their weight in pounds. The fitted regression equation is Cholesterol = 140 + 0.23 × Weight. What is the predicted cholesterol of a person who weighs 130 pounds?","sub":"quant","why":"Substituting Weight=130 into the equation: Cholesterol = 140 + 0.23(130) = 169.9. [VI.E.1]"},"revision":"09eb29063e4cab3a19bc041dfd04527bdedc8c2ef51cd9301913a66a7a6060ac"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-346":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"–0.92\",\"0.92\",\"0.96\",\"21.74\"],\"stem\":\"Paired data collected from a process are: (5.2, 26.7), (6.1, 27.5), (3.2, 24.9), (4.6, 25.5). What is the slope of the linear regression equation for these data?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Using Sxx=4.4475 and Sxy=4.105, the slope b1 = Sxy/Sxx = 4.105/4.4475 = 0.923, which rounds to 0.92 among the given choices. [VI.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["–0.92","0.92","0.96","21.74"],"stem":"Paired data collected from a process are: (5.2, 26.7), (6.1, 27.5), (3.2, 24.9), (4.6, 25.5). What is the slope of the linear regression equation for these data?","sub":"quant","why":"Using Sxx=4.4475 and Sxy=4.105, the slope b1 = Sxy/Sxx = 4.105/4.4475 = 0.923, which rounds to 0.92 among the given choices. [VI.E.1]"},"revision":"62b14e1b4db1f02e5bd43ee8c2cf7a53a3ff5ae258905266a1c4fb674db936ea"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-347":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Ha: β1 = 0\",\"Ha: β0 = 0\",\"Ha: β1 ≠ 0\",\"Ha: β0 ≠ 0\"],\"stem\":\"A study was done to predict people’s cholesterol by their weight. A sample of twenty-one people was collected, and the linear regression equation was found to be Cholesterol = 140 + 0.20 × Weight. The team would like to determine whether there is a significant linear relationship between weight and cholesterol. What is the appropriate alternative hypothesis for this statistical test?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The hypothesis test on the slope of the regression line uses H0: β1=0 versus Ha: β1≠0 (the intercept β0 is not the parameter of interest here). [VI.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Ha: β1 = 0","Ha: β0 = 0","Ha: β1 ≠ 0","Ha: β0 ≠ 0"],"stem":"A study was done to predict people’s cholesterol by their weight. A sample of twenty-one people was collected, and the linear regression equation was found to be Cholesterol = 140 + 0.20 × Weight. The team would like to determine whether there is a significant linear relationship between weight and cholesterol. What is the appropriate alternative hypothesis for this statistical test?","sub":"quant","why":"The hypothesis test on the slope of the regression line uses H0: β1=0 versus Ha: β1≠0 (the intercept β0 is not the parameter of interest here). [VI.E.1]"},"revision":"7df371c01dee5cd48d80e26fe6b3f7c338ffe11686ce70d1060413f36c0fe6d7"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-348":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"–0.88\",\"0\",\"0.88\",\"1.40\"],\"stem\":\"A scatterplot displays the relationship between two random variables: as x increases, y increases in a clear, tight, linear pattern. What is the correlation coefficient of these two variables?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The scatterplot shows y increasing as x increases, a positive linear relationship; a correlation coefficient close to +1 indicates a strong positive correlation, here 0.88. [VI.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["–0.88","0","0.88","1.40"],"stem":"A scatterplot displays the relationship between two random variables: as x increases, y increases in a clear, tight, linear pattern. What is the correlation coefficient of these two variables?","sub":"quant","why":"The scatterplot shows y increasing as x increases, a positive linear relationship; a correlation coefficient close to +1 indicates a strong positive correlation, here 0.88. [VI.E.2]"},"revision":"6c25d4502d8619a934205c41e5494a3e0a1ac942954d71bb4d0614abc01b0405"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-349":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"–0.98\",\"–0.98 and 0.98\",\"0\",\"0.98\"],\"stem\":\"A scatterplot displays the relationship between two random variables: y traces a clear quadratic (curved, U-shaped) pattern as x increases, not a straight line. What is the correlation coefficient of these two variables?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The scatterplot shows a quadratic (curved), not linear, relationship between the variables, so despite the visible pattern the linear correlation coefficient r is 0. [VI.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["–0.98","–0.98 and 0.98","0","0.98"],"stem":"A scatterplot displays the relationship between two random variables: y traces a clear quadratic (curved, U-shaped) pattern as x increases, not a straight line. What is the correlation coefficient of these two variables?","sub":"quant","why":"The scatterplot shows a quadratic (curved), not linear, relationship between the variables, so despite the visible pattern the linear correlation coefficient r is 0. [VI.E.2]"},"revision":"cbfa9618d853ec75d412ee6e2c26dbfe21183a07838ffd2122b958dabe65fdf1"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-35":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Auditee\",\"Client\",\"Lead Auditor\",\"Auditor\"],\"stem\":\"Who is responsible for the accuracy of the final audit report?\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"The lead auditor is responsible for the accuracy as well as the validity of the final report. [II.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Auditee","Client","Lead Auditor","Auditor"],"stem":"Who is responsible for the accuracy of the final audit report?","sub":"qsys","why":"The lead auditor is responsible for the accuracy as well as the validity of the final report. [II.D.2]"},"revision":"8be941638abacebb6a07cdb3ce9e398033c90557561cfb8dd0084e646be31a6c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-350":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"–1.45\",\"–0.92\",\"0.85\",\"0.92\"],\"stem\":\"The fitted regression equation for two variables x and y is yˆ = –5x + 131. The coefficient of determination is 0.85, or 85%. What is the correlation coefficient?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The correlation coefficient r = ±√R² = ±√0.85 ≈ ±0.92; since the slope is negative (-5), the relationship is negative, so r = -0.92. [VI.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["–1.45","–0.92","0.85","0.92"],"stem":"The fitted regression equation for two variables x and y is yˆ = –5x + 131. The coefficient of determination is 0.85, or 85%. What is the correlation coefficient?","sub":"quant","why":"The correlation coefficient r = ±√R² = ±√0.85 ≈ ±0.92; since the slope is negative (-5), the relationship is negative, so r = -0.92. [VI.E.2]"},"revision":"e0889522d2ad184933182c12acc44320e2a1c65ba04079b0f215a2d814879175"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-351":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"changes in the age of the car cause the cost to change.\",\"as the age of a car increases, the cost of the car tends to increase.\",\"as the age of a car increases, the cost of the car tends to decrease.\",\"the cost of a car is controlled by its age.\"],\"stem\":\"The correlation coefficient between the age of a car and its cost is r = –0.85. This means that:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A negative correlation coefficient indicates an inverse linear association: as the age of a car increases, its cost tends to decrease (though correlation does not imply causation). [VI.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["changes in the age of the car cause the cost to change.","as the age of a car increases, the cost of the car tends to increase.","as the age of a car increases, the cost of the car tends to decrease.","the cost of a car is controlled by its age."],"stem":"The correlation coefficient between the age of a car and its cost is r = –0.85. This means that:","sub":"quant","why":"A negative correlation coefficient indicates an inverse linear association: as the age of a car increases, its cost tends to decrease (though correlation does not imply causation). [VI.E.2]"},"revision":"08b2376eb26b1aadcab08e75d11be87a67952e7bd7375130dc0679b677c4de29"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-352":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"independent.\",\"autocorrelated.\",\"biased.\",\"inconsistent.\"],\"stem\":\"When the measurement for one sample tends to be dependent on the measure for the previous sample, these data are called:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Data are autocorrelated when a sample''s value is affected by the value of the previous sample. [VI.E.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["independent.","autocorrelated.","biased.","inconsistent."],"stem":"When the measurement for one sample tends to be dependent on the measure for the previous sample, these data are called:","sub":"quant","why":"Data are autocorrelated when a sample''s value is affected by the value of the previous sample. [VI.E.3]"},"revision":"bbfa02c9e8714bde1231740fd42a2c7fa8167dcfd0b0474e9618e7f2af6e29af"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-353":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Statistical process control\",\"Regression\",\"Histogram\",\"Capability analysis\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following can help identify special cause variation in a process?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Statistical process control is the technique used to identify special cause variation; regression describes predictor-response relationships, histograms visualize data, and capability analysis checks whether a process meets requirements. [VI.F.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Statistical process control","Regression","Histogram","Capability analysis"],"stem":"Which of the following can help identify special cause variation in a process?","sub":"quant","why":"Statistical process control is the technique used to identify special cause variation; regression describes predictor-response relationships, histograms visualize data, and capability analysis checks whether a process meets requirements. [VI.F.1]"},"revision":"307fff97126c04615ac8b63b6bdc917381cfddf761b4884265dfe023d2b0e359"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-354":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Common cause variation\",\"Special cause variation\",\"Overcontrol\",\"Undercontrol\"],\"stem\":\"A control chart was in control, but the operator adjusted the process anyway. What is this called?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Overcontrol (overadjustment) occurs when an operator adjusts a process in response to common cause variation even though the control chart did not signal out-of-control behavior. [VI.F.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Common cause variation","Special cause variation","Overcontrol","Undercontrol"],"stem":"A control chart was in control, but the operator adjusted the process anyway. What is this called?","sub":"quant","why":"Overcontrol (overadjustment) occurs when an operator adjusts a process in response to common cause variation even though the control chart did not signal out-of-control behavior. [VI.F.2]"},"revision":"00f68affde0906f37871f908d18bf98ec9a5aca60db1db881608f52a32ee68af"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-355":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"To identify the source of special cause variation in a process\",\"To adjust a process when it goes out of control\",\"To automate a process\",\"To help process operators recognize the presence of special causes of variation\"],\"stem\":\"What is the principal purpose of control charts?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Control charts help operators recognize when special causes of variation are present; they do not identify the specific cause or automatically adjust the process. [VI.F.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["To identify the source of special cause variation in a process","To adjust a process when it goes out of control","To automate a process","To help process operators recognize the presence of special causes of variation"],"stem":"What is the principal purpose of control charts?","sub":"quant","why":"Control charts help operators recognize when special causes of variation are present; they do not identify the specific cause or automatically adjust the process. [VI.F.2]"},"revision":"aff280a6e15c9e6e8856ea11049b4906a2f5821ba468c6a70c00b7b2839e27b0"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-356":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Measurement system variability\",\"Incorrect machine settings\",\"Variations in temperature\",\"Slight changes in customer arrivals to a store\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following events would be considered a source of special cause variation?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"An incorrect machine setting is an unusual, correctable event and thus a source of special cause variation, unlike ambient temperature changes, measurement system variability, or normal customer-arrival fluctuations, which are common cause variation. [VI.F.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Measurement system variability","Incorrect machine settings","Variations in temperature","Slight changes in customer arrivals to a store"],"stem":"Which of the following events would be considered a source of special cause variation?","sub":"quant","why":"An incorrect machine setting is an unusual, correctable event and thus a source of special cause variation, unlike ambient temperature changes, measurement system variability, or normal customer-arrival fluctuations, which are common cause variation. [VI.F.2]"},"revision":"9f246e9b49abf28e9566a19cbaa757ff00309525d509eedd299b0a981950e89d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-357":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Variability within samples is minimized.\",\"Variability between samples is minimized.\",\"Variability within samples is maximized.\",\"There is no difference between within-sample variability and between- sample variability.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following statements about rational subgrouping is correct?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Rational subgrouping selects samples so that special cause variation is more likely to appear between samples while variability within samples is minimized. [VI.F.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Variability within samples is minimized.","Variability between samples is minimized.","Variability within samples is maximized.","There is no difference between within-sample variability and between- sample variability."],"stem":"Which of the following statements about rational subgrouping is correct?","sub":"quant","why":"Rational subgrouping selects samples so that special cause variation is more likely to appear between samples while variability within samples is minimized. [VI.F.4]"},"revision":"d404db1957f58ad5740296aa79f5fff03ef8fe178a91d351de89cee7c66ce98d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-358":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Shewhart\",\"Deming\",\"Juran\",\"Taguchi\"],\"stem\":\"Which quality leader introduced the use of control charts to monitor a quality characteristic?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Walter A. Shewhart introduced the concept of control charts in the 1920s. [VI.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Shewhart","Deming","Juran","Taguchi"],"stem":"Which quality leader introduced the use of control charts to monitor a quality characteristic?","sub":"quant","why":"Walter A. Shewhart introduced the concept of control charts in the 1920s. [VI.F.5]"},"revision":"1da03119f7bc7fd4352d582fe053a8b03854e718015b1f139eb36a3b10cc787d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-359":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Binomial\",\"Poisson\",\"Normal\",\"Uniform\"],\"stem\":\"What probability distribution is required to construct X and R control charts?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"X̄, R, s, I, and MR control charts all require the underlying data to follow a normal distribution. [VI.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Binomial","Poisson","Normal","Uniform"],"stem":"What probability distribution is required to construct X and R control charts?","sub":"quant","why":"X̄, R, s, I, and MR control charts all require the underlying data to follow a normal distribution. [VI.F.5]"},"revision":"5308d2b5cee523d969d7113c60c2e2d6e8e0fcfe88075d819311b2c3db28694c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-36":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Body temperature\",\"Number of defects\",\"Length of a bolt\",\"Width of a board\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following quality characteristics is an example of attribute data?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"A defect is an attribute. Either the defect is present or it is not. A defect has no real measurement on a continuous scale. [III.A]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Body temperature","Number of defects","Length of a bolt","Width of a board"],"stem":"Which of the following quality characteristics is an example of attribute data?","sub":"design","why":"A defect is an attribute. Either the defect is present or it is not. A defect has no real measurement on a continuous scale. [III.A]"},"revision":"28a8a973e452d7706d031c4bcdce57439e46620519448f7ebf96f397ec2b63cc"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-360":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"both the average and range were within the control limits.\",\"neither the average nor range were within the control limits.\",\"only the average was outside the control limits.\",\"only the range was outside the control limits.\"],\"stem\":\"An X̄ and R chart was prepared for an operation using twenty-five samples of seven pieces each. X̄ was found to be 24.8 and R̄ was 5.50. During production, a new sample of seven was taken and the pieces measured 25, 32, 35, 28, 27, 24, and 26. At the time this sample was taken, were both the average and range within the control limits?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"For n=7, the new sample''s x̄=28.14 and R=11. With x̄=24.8 and R̄=5.50, the X̄-chart limits are UCL=27.10, LCL=22.50, and the R-chart limits are UCL=10.58, LCL=0.418. Since 28.14 exceeds the X̄ UCL and 11 exceeds the R UCL, neither the average nor the range were within the control limits. [VI.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["both the average and range were within the control limits.","neither the average nor range were within the control limits.","only the average was outside the control limits.","only the range was outside the control limits."],"stem":"An X̄ and R chart was prepared for an operation using twenty-five samples of seven pieces each. X̄ was found to be 24.8 and R̄ was 5.50. During production, a new sample of seven was taken and the pieces measured 25, 32, 35, 28, 27, 24, and 26. At the time this sample was taken, were both the average and range within the control limits?","sub":"quant","why":"For n=7, the new sample''s x̄=28.14 and R=11. With x̄=24.8 and R̄=5.50, the X̄-chart limits are UCL=27.10, LCL=22.50, and the R-chart limits are UCL=10.58, LCL=0.418. Since 28.14 exceeds the X̄ UCL and 11 exceeds the R UCL, neither the average nor the range were within the control limits. [VI.F.5]"},"revision":"8c9d7a0477fd3d17d75b04a6625747fa9ff509ac6d1393f0bf6edbbf4b3afc09"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-361":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"X̄ and R chart\",\"X̄ and s chart\",\"p-chart\",\"c-chart\"],\"stem\":\"The diameter of a steel rod is a quality characteristic of interest. Samples of size twelve will be selected in the subgroups. Which of the following control charts is preferred to monitor the process variability?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With variables data and a relatively large sample size (n=12), the sample standard deviation is a better estimator of process variability than the range, making an X̄ and s chart preferred over an X̄ and R chart. [VI.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["X̄ and R chart","X̄ and s chart","p-chart","c-chart"],"stem":"The diameter of a steel rod is a quality characteristic of interest. Samples of size twelve will be selected in the subgroups. Which of the following control charts is preferred to monitor the process variability?","sub":"quant","why":"With variables data and a relatively large sample size (n=12), the sample standard deviation is a better estimator of process variability than the range, making an X̄ and s chart preferred over an X̄ and R chart. [VI.F.5]"},"revision":"7d2cd12bf52723c6a73b2e55d54557ef2302f573b1513aa624a8492489844910"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-362":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"both the average and standard deviation were within the control limits.\",\"neither the average nor standard deviation were within the control limits.\",\"only the average was outside the control limits.\",\"only the standard deviation was outside the control limits.\"],\"stem\":\"An X̄ and s chart was prepared for an operation using twenty-five samples with six pieces in each sample. X̄ was found to be 12.68 and s was 2.45. During production, a sample of six was taken and the pieces measured 11, 13, 14, 14, 10, and 12. At the time this sample was taken:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"For the new sample, x̄=12.33 and s=1.633. With x̄=12.68 and s̄=2.45 (n=6), the X̄-chart limits are UCL=15.83, LCL=9.53, and the s-chart limits are UCL=4.83, LCL=0.07. Since both 12.33 and 1.633 fall within their respective limits, both the average and standard deviation were within the control limits. [VI.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["both the average and standard deviation were within the control limits.","neither the average nor standard deviation were within the control limits.","only the average was outside the control limits.","only the standard deviation was outside the control limits."],"stem":"An X̄ and s chart was prepared for an operation using twenty-five samples with six pieces in each sample. X̄ was found to be 12.68 and s was 2.45. During production, a sample of six was taken and the pieces measured 11, 13, 14, 14, 10, and 12. At the time this sample was taken:","sub":"quant","why":"For the new sample, x̄=12.33 and s=1.633. With x̄=12.68 and s̄=2.45 (n=6), the X̄-chart limits are UCL=15.83, LCL=9.53, and the s-chart limits are UCL=4.83, LCL=0.07. Since both 12.33 and 1.633 fall within their respective limits, both the average and standard deviation were within the control limits. [VI.F.5]"},"revision":"21e597b417555d9bdb1307c92382deea8b0344f513d2fcdf9b806e6100a488d8"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-363":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"I-MR chart\",\"X̄ and R chart\",\"X̄ and s chart\",\"p-chart\"],\"stem\":\"A company would like to monitor a continuous quality characteristic of a product. However, it is expensive to obtain the measurements of this quality characteristic. Which of the following control charts is most appropriate to monitor this process?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Since it''s expensive to obtain measurements, subgroups of size 1 (individual observations) are used, making an I-MR chart the appropriate choice for this continuous quality characteristic. [VI.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["I-MR chart","X̄ and R chart","X̄ and s chart","p-chart"],"stem":"A company would like to monitor a continuous quality characteristic of a product. However, it is expensive to obtain the measurements of this quality characteristic. Which of the following control charts is most appropriate to monitor this process?","sub":"quant","why":"Since it''s expensive to obtain measurements, subgroups of size 1 (individual observations) are used, making an I-MR chart the appropriate choice for this continuous quality characteristic. [VI.F.5]"},"revision":"1b8e7c3c6e610c3c8454acbd95ec2a5fe2d6bab65aa6cb85a0bf508ef92c39c9"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-364":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"X̄ and R chart\",\"p-chart\",\"np-chart\",\"c-chart\"],\"stem\":\"A hospital is monitoring the surgeries that result in surgical complications. The number of surgeries each month and those resulting in a complication were recorded. The number of surgeries each month can vary. Which of the following control charts is most appropriate to monitor this process?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Surgical complications are attribute data with a varying number of surgeries (opportunities) each month, so a p-chart is preferred over an np-chart, which requires a constant sample size. [VI.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["X̄ and R chart","p-chart","np-chart","c-chart"],"stem":"A hospital is monitoring the surgeries that result in surgical complications. The number of surgeries each month and those resulting in a complication were recorded. The number of surgeries each month can vary. Which of the following control charts is most appropriate to monitor this process?","sub":"quant","why":"Surgical complications are attribute data with a varying number of surgeries (opportunities) each month, so a p-chart is preferred over an np-chart, which requires a constant sample size. [VI.F.5]"},"revision":"4d558d12d371a4d9c6e7a3a3816a9e08e7f1d21e6e3d12f4053f4614fdfb35e4"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-365":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"cl\":0.037,\"data\":[0.032,0.041,0.035,0.038,0.036,0.04,0.034,0.039,0.037,0.0733],\"lcl\":0,\"title\":\"p-chart\",\"type\":\"control-single\",\"ucl\":0.083},\"options\":[\"the sample was within the control limits.\",\"the sample was outside the control limits.\",\"the upper control limit was 0.073.\",\"the lower control limit was –0.01.\"],\"stem\":\"A factory collected data on the number of nonconforming parts and constructed a p-chart. Fifteen samples of size 150 were collected. They determined that the average fraction of nonconforming parts was p̄ = 0.037. During production, a sample of 150 parts was taken, of which eleven were nonconforming. At the time this sample was taken:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With p̄=0.037 and n=150, the p-chart control limits are UCL=0.083 and LCL≈0 (the calculated negative LCL is set to 0). Since the new sample''s p̂=11/150=0.073 falls within these limits, the sample is within the control limits. [VI.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"cl":0.037,"data":[0.032,0.041,0.035,0.038,0.036,0.04,0.034,0.039,0.037,0.0733],"lcl":0,"title":"p-chart","type":"control-single","ucl":0.083},"options":["the sample was within the control limits.","the sample was outside the control limits.","the upper control limit was 0.073.","the lower control limit was –0.01."],"stem":"A factory collected data on the number of nonconforming parts and constructed a p-chart. Fifteen samples of size 150 were collected. They determined that the average fraction of nonconforming parts was p̄ = 0.037. During production, a sample of 150 parts was taken, of which eleven were nonconforming. At the time this sample was taken:","sub":"quant","why":"With p̄=0.037 and n=150, the p-chart control limits are UCL=0.083 and LCL≈0 (the calculated negative LCL is set to 0). Since the new sample''s p̂=11/150=0.073 falls within these limits, the sample is within the control limits. [VI.F.5]"},"revision":"c2940ab968db8dec943d5d65787aaa39f146c96ad96d5eb4aff4954e2fdc9899"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-366":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.0600\",\"0.7053\",\"0.0587\",\"0.6893\"],\"stem\":\"What is the upper control limit for a p-chart when the average daily production is 3575 units with an established fraction defective of 0.048?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With p̂=0.048 and n=3575, UCL = p̂ + 3√[p̂(1-p̂)/n] = 0.048 + 3√[0.048(0.952)/3575] = 0.0587. [VI.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.0600","0.7053","0.0587","0.6893"],"stem":"What is the upper control limit for a p-chart when the average daily production is 3575 units with an established fraction defective of 0.048?","sub":"quant","why":"With p̂=0.048 and n=3575, UCL = p̂ + 3√[p̂(1-p̂)/n] = 0.048 + 3√[0.048(0.952)/3575] = 0.0587. [VI.F.5]"},"revision":"86df95e87dac5d44ca4813ba37af4647238b817b96f896f28a0e45ea52213ef0"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-367":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"X̄ and R chart\",\"p-chart\",\"np-chart\",\"c-chart\"],\"stem\":\"A team at a factory would like to monitor the number of defects in a TV screen. Twenty-five samples of five TV screens are inspected. Which of the following control charts is most appropriate for this scenario?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"TV screen defects are nonconformities (count data), which calls for a c-chart rather than an X̄/R chart (variables data) or a p-/np-chart (fraction nonconforming). [VI.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["X̄ and R chart","p-chart","np-chart","c-chart"],"stem":"A team at a factory would like to monitor the number of defects in a TV screen. Twenty-five samples of five TV screens are inspected. Which of the following control charts is most appropriate for this scenario?","sub":"quant","why":"TV screen defects are nonconformities (count data), which calls for a c-chart rather than an X̄/R chart (variables data) or a p-/np-chart (fraction nonconforming). [VI.F.5]"},"revision":"83cf68d70527dfc526156dcb280ba11bf1b13bd27d12f0ccc81a93583c03b47c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-368":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.22\",\"0.30\",\"1.16\",\"1.24\"],\"stem\":\"A team at a factory is monitoring the average number of defects in TV screens. Thirty-five TV screens are randomly selected each day over a 25-day period. The average number of defects per TV screen was found to be u = 0.73. What is the upper control limit for a corresponding u-chart?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With ū=0.73 and n=35, UCL = ū + 3√(ū/n) = 0.73 + 3√(0.73/35) = 1.16. [VI.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.22","0.30","1.16","1.24"],"stem":"A team at a factory is monitoring the average number of defects in TV screens. Thirty-five TV screens are randomly selected each day over a 25-day period. The average number of defects per TV screen was found to be u = 0.73. What is the upper control limit for a corresponding u-chart?","sub":"quant","why":"With ū=0.73 and n=35, UCL = ū + 3√(ū/n) = 0.73 + 3√(0.73/35) = 1.16. [VI.F.5]"},"revision":"2c83d4be1dd164b36f8bc91c1b358ffa15c0b56fa0a5f8fbf2ad07350ce82067"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-369":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"0.1587.\",\"0.2240.\",\"0.3413.\",\"unknown.\"],\"stem\":\"If a process is out of control, the probability that a single point on the X̄ chart will fall between plus two sigma and the upper control limit is:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Since the process is out of statistical control (unstable), the theoretical probability calculations for an in-control process no longer apply, so the actual probability is unknown. [VI.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["0.1587.","0.2240.","0.3413.","unknown."],"stem":"If a process is out of control, the probability that a single point on the X̄ chart will fall between plus two sigma and the upper control limit is:","sub":"quant","why":"Since the process is out of statistical control (unstable), the theoretical probability calculations for an in-control process no longer apply, so the actual probability is unknown. [VI.F.6]"},"revision":"cd7cd772dd97497c3cc07934407ae06a119fe46a8ec8d2d437962c0ca5792df1"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-37":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"mates with both ends of the limit gage (both “go” and “no-go”).\",\"mates only with the “no-go” end of the gage.\",\"mates with neither end of the limit gage.\",\"mates with only the “go” end of the limit gage.\"],\"stem\":\"A part will be inspected using a limit gage. An acceptable part is one that:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"An acceptable part should only fit within the “go” end of the limit gage. If it passes on both ends, it is an unacceptable part. [III.A]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["mates with both ends of the limit gage (both “go” and “no-go”).","mates only with the “no-go” end of the gage.","mates with neither end of the limit gage.","mates with only the “go” end of the limit gage."],"stem":"A part will be inspected using a limit gage. An acceptable part is one that:","sub":"design","why":"An acceptable part should only fit within the “go” end of the limit gage. If it passes on both ends, it is an unacceptable part. [III.A]"},"revision":"fcc33bed7aa948c58e6285e1ddefa441072ae50240bfcefea0d55b5a74cebc76"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-370":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"adjust the process when a point is not on target.\",\"have a small average run length.\",\"have a large average run length.\",\"stop monitoring the process.\"],\"stem\":\"If a process is in control, it is desirable to:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"When a process is in control, a large average run length (the number of samples before a false out-of-control signal) is desirable, since operators should not adjust a stable, on-target process. [VI.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["adjust the process when a point is not on target.","have a small average run length.","have a large average run length.","stop monitoring the process."],"stem":"If a process is in control, it is desirable to:","sub":"quant","why":"When a process is in control, a large average run length (the number of samples before a false out-of-control signal) is desirable, since operators should not adjust a stable, on-target process. [VI.F.6]"},"revision":"720476cb37e5fed58f5027f7703c2d984d12891ad041d7a819cb772206b00099"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-371":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"cl\":11.09,\"data\":[8,12,10,14,9,13,11,40,10,12],\"lcl\":0,\"title\":\"MR chart\",\"type\":\"control-single\",\"ucl\":36.25},\"options\":[\"The process is out of control.\",\"The process is stable.\",\"The process is operating within the specifications.\",\"The process is operating outside the specifications.\"],\"stem\":\"Consider an MR chart of a normally distributed process with UCL = 36.25, MR̄ = 11.09, and LCL = 0. One point on the chart falls above the upper control limit. What conclusions can be drawn from this control chart?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A point falls above the upper control limit on the MR chart, indicating the process is out of statistical control; a control chart says nothing about whether the process meets specification limits. [VI.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"cl":11.09,"data":[8,12,10,14,9,13,11,40,10,12],"lcl":0,"title":"MR chart","type":"control-single","ucl":36.25},"options":["The process is out of control.","The process is stable.","The process is operating within the specifications.","The process is operating outside the specifications."],"stem":"Consider an MR chart of a normally distributed process with UCL = 36.25, MR̄ = 11.09, and LCL = 0. One point on the chart falls above the upper control limit. What conclusions can be drawn from this control chart?","sub":"quant","why":"A point falls above the upper control limit on the MR chart, indicating the process is out of statistical control; a control chart says nothing about whether the process meets specification limits. [VI.F.6]"},"revision":"e89cd779f6f9e28e7dd212a5d1a0ea3a7fcb1ac15ff846a199000062200fb8f6"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-372":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"cl\":0.0675,\"data\":[0.065,0.07,0.068,0.05,0.085,0.048,0.088,0.045,0.09,0.043,0.092,0.041,0.094,0.039,0.096,0.037,0.098,0.036,0.099],\"lcl\":0.0199,\"title\":\"p-chart\",\"type\":\"control-single\",\"ucl\":0.1151,\"unit\":\"\"},\"options\":[\"The process is out of control.\",\"The process is stable.\",\"The process is operating within the specifications.\",\"The process is operating outside the specifications.\"],\"stem\":\"Consider a p-chart with UCL = 0.1151, p̄ = 0.0675, and LCL = 0.0199. Starting with the fourth sample, there is a run of sixteen consecutive points alternating up and down. What conclusions can be drawn from this control chart?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The chart shows a run of points alternating up and down, matching the out-of-control sensitizing rule for that pattern, so the process is out of statistical control; this control chart says nothing about whether it meets specification limits. [VI.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"cl":0.0675,"data":[0.065,0.07,0.068,0.05,0.085,0.048,0.088,0.045,0.09,0.043,0.092,0.041,0.094,0.039,0.096,0.037,0.098,0.036,0.099],"lcl":0.0199,"title":"p-chart","type":"control-single","ucl":0.1151,"unit":""},"options":["The process is out of control.","The process is stable.","The process is operating within the specifications.","The process is operating outside the specifications."],"stem":"Consider a p-chart with UCL = 0.1151, p̄ = 0.0675, and LCL = 0.0199. Starting with the fourth sample, there is a run of sixteen consecutive points alternating up and down. What conclusions can be drawn from this control chart?","sub":"quant","why":"The chart shows a run of points alternating up and down, matching the out-of-control sensitizing rule for that pattern, so the process is out of statistical control; this control chart says nothing about whether it meets specification limits. [VI.F.6]"},"revision":"991b97cff662de1367e5e545a5b0ba1140d2945f6a0d10f36c5934078877514e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-373":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"chart\":{\"cl\":27.49,\"data\":[26.1,28.9,27,29.5,25.8,27.4,28.6,26.9,27.8,28.1,26.5,27.9],\"lcl\":22.85,\"title\":\"X̄ chart\",\"type\":\"control-single\",\"ucl\":32.13},\"options\":[\"The process is out of control.\",\"The process is stable.\",\"The process is operating within the specifications.\",\"The process is operating outside the specifications.\"],\"stem\":\"Consider an X̄ chart of a normally distributed process with UCL = 32.13, X̄̄ = 27.49, and LCL = 22.85. All plotted points fall within the control limits with no unusual patterns. What conclusions can be drawn from this control chart?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"No unusual patterns or out-of-limits points appear on the chart, so the process is stable (in control); this says nothing about whether it meets specification limits. [VI.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"chart":{"cl":27.49,"data":[26.1,28.9,27,29.5,25.8,27.4,28.6,26.9,27.8,28.1,26.5,27.9],"lcl":22.85,"title":"X̄ chart","type":"control-single","ucl":32.13},"options":["The process is out of control.","The process is stable.","The process is operating within the specifications.","The process is operating outside the specifications."],"stem":"Consider an X̄ chart of a normally distributed process with UCL = 32.13, X̄̄ = 27.49, and LCL = 22.85. All plotted points fall within the control limits with no unusual patterns. What conclusions can be drawn from this control chart?","sub":"quant","why":"No unusual patterns or out-of-limits points appear on the chart, so the process is stable (in control); this says nothing about whether it meets specification limits. [VI.F.6]"},"revision":"da59934344041867c3fdf7558d3ded5348a1616d71f8775ea466dfd2fc04cd23"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-374":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"X̄ and s control chart\",\"X̄ and R control chart\",\"Short-run SPC\",\"Capability analysis\"],\"stem\":\"A manufacturing company received an order for a build-to-order product from a customer. The company would like to monitor the process; which of the following tools is most appropriate?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A build-to-order product does not have a long, continuous production run, so classical X̄/R charts aren''t appropriate; short-run SPC (typically using standardized control charts) is needed instead. [VI.F.7]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["X̄ and s control chart","X̄ and R control chart","Short-run SPC","Capability analysis"],"stem":"A manufacturing company received an order for a build-to-order product from a customer. The company would like to monitor the process; which of the following tools is most appropriate?","sub":"quant","why":"A build-to-order product does not have a long, continuous production run, so classical X̄/R charts aren''t appropriate; short-run SPC (typically using standardized control charts) is needed instead. [VI.F.7]"},"revision":"9219dd3ba12d09b0a0310de4106cd733173e6a10a42ac506f4c79ce079171fb5"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-375":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Compare the actual capability to the desired capability.\",\"Measure the process capability.\",\"Estimate the process parameters.\",\"Verify that the process is stable.\"],\"stem\":\"What is the first step in a capability study?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The first step in a capability study is to verify the process is stable (free of special-cause variation), since capability measures aren''t valid estimates otherwise. [VI.G.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Compare the actual capability to the desired capability.","Measure the process capability.","Estimate the process parameters.","Verify that the process is stable."],"stem":"What is the first step in a capability study?","sub":"quant","why":"The first step in a capability study is to verify the process is stable (free of special-cause variation), since capability measures aren''t valid estimates otherwise. [VI.G.1]"},"revision":"3c7eb973a0d45991f68bc950cb6e33b0fe0fdfd114b0fd5b4f86eeade96ef265"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-376":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Flowchart\",\"Cause-and-effect diagram\",\"Capability analysis\",\"Regression analysis\"],\"stem\":\"A team would like to determine whether a process is able to meet customer specifications. What quality tool should they use?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Capability analysis determines whether a process is able to meet requirements, often supplemented with histograms, probability plots, or stem-and-leaf plots. [VI.G.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Flowchart","Cause-and-effect diagram","Capability analysis","Regression analysis"],"stem":"A team would like to determine whether a process is able to meet customer specifications. What quality tool should they use?","sub":"quant","why":"Capability analysis determines whether a process is able to meet requirements, often supplemented with histograms, probability plots, or stem-and-leaf plots. [VI.G.1]"},"revision":"b394c28a119e17601754c5afebd7f86bbed4c8020e5a74c23bb01205333b0a2f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-377":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.0002\",\"0.0096\",\"0.9904\",\"0.9998\"],\"stem\":\"The dimension of a component has specifications 5.5 ± 0.25. The process data are normally distributed with mean 5.45 and standard deviation 0.085. What fraction of components will have this dimension outside the specification limits?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With USL=5.75, LSL=5.25, μ=5.45, σ=0.085: P(5.25<X<5.75) = P(-2.35<Z<3.53) ≈ 0.9904 within spec, so the fraction outside specification limits is 1-0.9904 = 0.0096. [VI.G.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.0002","0.0096","0.9904","0.9998"],"stem":"The dimension of a component has specifications 5.5 ± 0.25. The process data are normally distributed with mean 5.45 and standard deviation 0.085. What fraction of components will have this dimension outside the specification limits?","sub":"quant","why":"With USL=5.75, LSL=5.25, μ=5.45, σ=0.085: P(5.25<X<5.75) = P(-2.35<Z<3.53) ≈ 0.9904 within spec, so the fraction outside specification limits is 1-0.9904 = 0.0096. [VI.G.2]"},"revision":"fc8b455435c6cade27bbce53f3c729acd677f4310ea6d4f92d4a4ac397dc0a54"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-378":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.0071\",\"0.0175\",\"0.9825\",\"0.9896\"],\"stem\":\"The diameter of a stainless-steel rod is an important quality characteristic. The rod has specifications 2.50 ± 0.05. Data from the process indicate the distribution is normally distributed, and an X̄ and R chart indicates the process is stable. The control charts used a sample of size seven and found that X̄ = 2.5014 and R̄ = 0.0568. What fraction of steel rods are within the specification limits?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With USL=2.55, LSL=2.45, x̄=2.5014, σ̂=R̄/d2=0.0568/2.704≈0.021: P(2.45<X<2.55) = P(-2.45<Z<2.31) ≈ 0.9825 of rods fall within the specification limits. [VI.G.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.0071","0.0175","0.9825","0.9896"],"stem":"The diameter of a stainless-steel rod is an important quality characteristic. The rod has specifications 2.50 ± 0.05. Data from the process indicate the distribution is normally distributed, and an X̄ and R chart indicates the process is stable. The control charts used a sample of size seven and found that X̄ = 2.5014 and R̄ = 0.0568. What fraction of steel rods are within the specification limits?","sub":"quant","why":"With USL=2.55, LSL=2.45, x̄=2.5014, σ̂=R̄/d2=0.0568/2.704≈0.021: P(2.45<X<2.55) = P(-2.45<Z<2.31) ≈ 0.9825 of rods fall within the specification limits. [VI.G.2]"},"revision":"244c3bb2af5ac1c26f705fb31a757a5a93c90c96ff2956bdc63dffb3ee9dba03"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-379":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"µ ± 3σ σ\",\"x ±3 n USL−LSL\",\"6σ\",\"Specification limits are determined externally and do not have a generic formula.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is the correct formula to find the specification limits of a quality characteristic?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Specification limits are determined externally (by management, engineers, or customers) and have no generic statistical formula; they are unrelated to control limits. [VI.G.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["µ ± 3σ σ","x ±3 n USL−LSL","6σ","Specification limits are determined externally and do not have a generic formula."],"stem":"Which of the following is the correct formula to find the specification limits of a quality characteristic?","sub":"quant","why":"Specification limits are determined externally (by management, engineers, or customers) and have no generic statistical formula; they are unrelated to control limits. [VI.G.2]"},"revision":"2c097946753b5511d7ce836835209298f1c29574419aecfcdd218fa18a6e273b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-38":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.0975.\",\"0.9500.\",\"0.9975.\",\"0.9999.\"],\"stem\":\"A system has two components in parallel, each with a reliability of 0.95. The system reliability is approximately:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"For components in parallel, each with reliability of p, the system reliability is given by Rs(t) = 1 – (1 – p)2 = 1 – (1 – 0.95)2 = 0.9975. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.0975.","0.9500.","0.9975.","0.9999."],"stem":"A system has two components in parallel, each with a reliability of 0.95. The system reliability is approximately:","sub":"design","why":"For components in parallel, each with reliability of p, the system reliability is given by Rs(t) = 1 – (1 – p)2 = 1 – (1 – 0.95)2 = 0.9975. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"2556b6485680e0410d57210c11811cd600c3a916bcf33bf0722468707091e78f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-380":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Cₚ = 1.25; Cₚₖ = 1.36\",\"Cₚ = 1.36; Cₚₖ = 1.25\",\"Cₚ = 1.36; Cₚₖ = 1.48\",\"Cₚ = 2.73; Cₚₖ = 1.36\"],\"stem\":\"A stable, normally distributed process has specifications 21.35 ± 4.50. A sample from the process had a mean X̄ = 20.98 and standard deviation σ = 1.10. Find Cₚ and Cₚₖ.\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With USL=25.85, LSL=16.85, μ=20.98, σ=1.10: Cp = (USL-LSL)/6σ = 1.36; Cpk = min[(USL-μ)/3σ, (μ-LSL)/3σ] = min(1.48, 1.25) = 1.25. [VI.G.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Cₚ = 1.25; Cₚₖ = 1.36","Cₚ = 1.36; Cₚₖ = 1.25","Cₚ = 1.36; Cₚₖ = 1.48","Cₚ = 2.73; Cₚₖ = 1.36"],"stem":"A stable, normally distributed process has specifications 21.35 ± 4.50. A sample from the process had a mean X̄ = 20.98 and standard deviation σ = 1.10. Find Cₚ and Cₚₖ.","sub":"quant","why":"With USL=25.85, LSL=16.85, μ=20.98, σ=1.10: Cp = (USL-LSL)/6σ = 1.36; Cpk = min[(USL-μ)/3σ, (μ-LSL)/3σ] = min(1.48, 1.25) = 1.25. [VI.G.3]"},"revision":"ac5fe23f6be46f63a7c58021593aff85e5b7ce1ee3e8f1910f9f576f16159a07"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-381":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Yes, since Cp = 0.89.\",\"Yes, since Cpk = 1.11.\",\"No, since Cpk = 0.89.\",\"No, since Cpk = 1.33.\"],\"stem\":\"A stable process has specifications 14 ± 5. The process is normally distributed with mean 13 and standard deviation 1.5. Is this process considered capable?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Since the process is not centered, Cpk is the appropriate metric: Cpk = min[(19-13)/3(1.5), (13-9)/3(1.5)] = min(1.33, 0.89) = 0.89, which is less than 1, so the process is not capable. [VI.G.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Yes, since Cp = 0.89.","Yes, since Cpk = 1.11.","No, since Cpk = 0.89.","No, since Cpk = 1.33."],"stem":"A stable process has specifications 14 ± 5. The process is normally distributed with mean 13 and standard deviation 1.5. Is this process considered capable?","sub":"quant","why":"Since the process is not centered, Cpk is the appropriate metric: Cpk = min[(19-13)/3(1.5), (13-9)/3(1.5)] = min(1.33, 0.89) = 0.89, which is less than 1, so the process is not capable. [VI.G.3]"},"revision":"9b103fee0207f5e84733761c4cfe157c6b289e070867dab949ad3d6a6920283c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-382":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.77\",\"0.79\",\"0.82\",\"0.98\"],\"stem\":\"The diameter of a stainless-steel rod is an important quality characteristic. The rod has specifications 2.50 ± 0.05. Data from the process indicate the distribution is normally distributed, and an X̄ and R chart indicates the process is stable. The control charts used a sample of size seven and found that X̄ = 2.5014 and R̄ = 0.0568. What is the capability of this process?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With USL=2.55, LSL=2.45, σ̂=R̄/d2≈0.021: since the process is centered, Cp = (USL-LSL)/6σ = 0.10/0.126 ≈ 0.79, which is less than 1, so the process is not capable. [VI.G.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.77","0.79","0.82","0.98"],"stem":"The diameter of a stainless-steel rod is an important quality characteristic. The rod has specifications 2.50 ± 0.05. Data from the process indicate the distribution is normally distributed, and an X̄ and R chart indicates the process is stable. The control charts used a sample of size seven and found that X̄ = 2.5014 and R̄ = 0.0568. What is the capability of this process?","sub":"quant","why":"With USL=2.55, LSL=2.45, σ̂=R̄/d2≈0.021: since the process is centered, Cp = (USL-LSL)/6σ = 0.10/0.126 ≈ 0.79, which is less than 1, so the process is not capable. [VI.G.3]"},"revision":"8861c75bc2ea2e77042cb5a8e0cfa1f79e3cc9af860d706d5f8afdc76066ebdb"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-383":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"C p\",\"P p\",\"C pk\",\"C r\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following metrics is recommended to evaluate process capability when the process is not in statistical control?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Pp (process performance) is the recommended metric when a process is not in statistical control, unlike Cp/Cpk, which assume a stable, in-control process. [VI.G.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["C p","P p","C pk","C r"],"stem":"Which of the following metrics is recommended to evaluate process capability when the process is not in statistical control?","sub":"quant","why":"Pp (process performance) is the recommended metric when a process is not in statistical control, unlike Cp/Cpk, which assume a stable, in-control process. [VI.G.4]"},"revision":"9fc83a2033c7bf3703c1b2f9e470fd98dc57072fe7f6eac6a8c033c51f6726d3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-384":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"3\",\"4\",\"8\",\"9\"],\"stem\":\"A textile company is designing an experiment to test the effects of temperature, pressure, and time on the tensile properties of a fiber composite. Each of these three factors will be run at a high and low level. A full-factorial design is used with four replicates per run. How many treatment combinations are in this experiment?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A treatment combination is a unique combination of factor levels applied to an experimental unit; with three factors at two levels each, there are 2×2×2=8 treatment combinations. [VI.H.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["3","4","8","9"],"stem":"A textile company is designing an experiment to test the effects of temperature, pressure, and time on the tensile properties of a fiber composite. Each of these three factors will be run at a high and low level. A full-factorial design is used with four replicates per run. How many treatment combinations are in this experiment?","sub":"quant","why":"A treatment combination is a unique combination of factor levels applied to an experimental unit; with three factors at two levels each, there are 2×2×2=8 treatment combinations. [VI.H.1]"},"revision":"f2289ab596a7ed5fc5919501972844d5054bd3440e5f5e9d3862eb3731c7c6f9"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-385":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"2\",\"3\",\"4\",\"12\"],\"stem\":\"A textile company is designing an experiment to test the effects of temperature, pressure, and time on the tensile properties of a fiber composite. Each of these three factors will be run at a high and low level. A full-factorial design is used with four replicates per run. How many factors are in this experiment?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The experiment tests three factors (temperature, pressure, and time), each at two levels, with four replicates per treatment combination. [VI.H.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["2","3","4","12"],"stem":"A textile company is designing an experiment to test the effects of temperature, pressure, and time on the tensile properties of a fiber composite. Each of these three factors will be run at a high and low level. A full-factorial design is used with four replicates per run. How many factors are in this experiment?","sub":"quant","why":"The experiment tests three factors (temperature, pressure, and time), each at two levels, with four replicates per treatment combination. [VI.H.1]"},"revision":"2a3c71d6ee9c84ea8e3dd1dc8e22ce4dcbd609a1c255aeecbff74680531eedfa"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-386":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Interaction\",\"Experimental error\",\"Capability\",\"Noise variable\"],\"stem\":\"A textile company is designing an experiment to test the effects of temperature, pressure, and time on the tensile properties of a fiber composite. Each of these three factors will be run at a high and low level. A full-factorial design is used with four replicates per run. Variation observed in the readings of the replicates for each treatment combination is referred to as what?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Experimental error is the variation observed among the replicate readings for a given treatment combination; fewer replicates make this error estimate less accurate. [VI.H.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Interaction","Experimental error","Capability","Noise variable"],"stem":"A textile company is designing an experiment to test the effects of temperature, pressure, and time on the tensile properties of a fiber composite. Each of these three factors will be run at a high and low level. A full-factorial design is used with four replicates per run. Variation observed in the readings of the replicates for each treatment combination is referred to as what?","sub":"quant","why":"Experimental error is the variation observed among the replicate readings for a given treatment combination; fewer replicates make this error estimate less accurate. [VI.H.1]"},"revision":"fb221c8f955202966df9a0aa5fb80f455add3f334d26ace53e618b249c894490"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-387":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Choose the factors and responses.\",\"Select the experimental design.\",\"Define the method of measurement.\",\"State the objective of the experiment.\"],\"stem\":\"What is the first step in designing a successful experiment?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The first step in designing an experiment is to clearly state its objective, which guides the selection of factors, responses, measurement method, and design. [VI.H.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Choose the factors and responses.","Select the experimental design.","Define the method of measurement.","State the objective of the experiment."],"stem":"What is the first step in designing a successful experiment?","sub":"quant","why":"The first step in designing an experiment is to clearly state its objective, which guides the selection of factors, responses, measurement method, and design. [VI.H.2]"},"revision":"e8a32c05a7410bff259431a3ee3a8fd45684b1cb21381bb379b16e2fabe60f1f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-388":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Confounding\",\"Blocking\",\"Randomization\",\"Interaction\"],\"stem\":\"What technique in experimental design will reduce the effect of uncontrolled variables that are not part of the experiment but might affect the response variable?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Randomization orders the treatment combinations randomly to reduce the effect of uncontrolled nuisance factors that cannot be controlled during the experiment. [VI.H.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Confounding","Blocking","Randomization","Interaction"],"stem":"What technique in experimental design will reduce the effect of uncontrolled variables that are not part of the experiment but might affect the response variable?","sub":"quant","why":"Randomization orders the treatment combinations randomly to reduce the effect of uncontrolled nuisance factors that cannot be controlled during the experiment. [VI.H.3]"},"revision":"0f25b209814651c40b4e34e859685620bdc2521933f952715599d7cd539b0b6a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-389":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Confounding\",\"Blocking\",\"Randomization\",\"Replication\"],\"stem\":\"An experiment is being conducted in a factory. Each experimental run takes several hours, so the experiment will last several days. What design technique would help in this experiment?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Since the experiment spans several days, blocking on day removes any day-to-day effect from influencing the response. [VI.H.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Confounding","Blocking","Randomization","Replication"],"stem":"An experiment is being conducted in a factory. Each experimental run takes several hours, so the experiment will last several days. What design technique would help in this experiment?","sub":"quant","why":"Since the experiment spans several days, blocking on day removes any day-to-day effect from influencing the response. [VI.H.3]"},"revision":"c84fb1d589619c3358fa61712b7db02de57a95513de12016fd7754f0e196da4c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-39":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Failure Mode and Effects Analysis\",\"Critical to Quality Tree\",\"Design of Experiments\",\"Cost of Quality\"],\"stem\":\"Which concept is used to identify drivers of quality based on the Voice of the Customer?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"A Critical to Quality Tree is used to identify the drivers of quality based on the Voice of the Customer. [III.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Failure Mode and Effects Analysis","Critical to Quality Tree","Design of Experiments","Cost of Quality"],"stem":"Which concept is used to identify drivers of quality based on the Voice of the Customer?","sub":"design","why":"A Critical to Quality Tree is used to identify the drivers of quality based on the Voice of the Customer. [III.B.1]"},"revision":"6a0872c354bb7d1aae2f47ca2f684b06602452d47c680e395d1b61e0da060fcf"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-390":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Fractional factorial design\",\"Interaction design\",\"Factorial design\",\"Confounded design\"],\"stem\":\"An experiment is being conducted on the yield of a substance. Two factors are believed to have an effect on the yield. What type of design would be appropriate for this experiment?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With two factors of interest and no blocking variables, a factorial design is appropriate. [VI.H.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Fractional factorial design","Interaction design","Factorial design","Confounded design"],"stem":"An experiment is being conducted on the yield of a substance. Two factors are believed to have an effect on the yield. What type of design would be appropriate for this experiment?","sub":"quant","why":"With two factors of interest and no blocking variables, a factorial design is appropriate. [VI.H.4]"},"revision":"772e599eae21ea603950959076a95c8d092fcb08acecf6d14d6de69babd426d3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-391":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"A and B only\",\"A, B, and AB\",\"All main effects\",\"C, AC, BC, and ABC\"],\"stem\":\"Consider the following results of a 23 full-factorial design. Assuming normality and equal variance assumptions are valid, which terms have a significant effect on the response variable? Source df SS MS F p-value A 1 35.03 35.03 264.38 0.000 B 1 30.54 30.54 230.49 0.000 C 1 0.10 0.10 0.75 0.400 AB 1 2.98 2.98 22.49 0.001 AC 1 0.02 0.02 0.15 0.693 BC 1 0.03 0.03 0.23 0.646 ABC 1 0.002 0.002 0.15 0.902 Error 8 1.06 0.1325 Total 15 69.76\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The ANOVA table shows very small p-values (0.000-0.001) only for main effects A and B and their AB interaction, so only A, B, and AB have a significant effect on the response. [VI.H.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["A and B only","A, B, and AB","All main effects","C, AC, BC, and ABC"],"stem":"Consider the following results of a 23 full-factorial design. Assuming normality and equal variance assumptions are valid, which terms have a significant effect on the response variable? Source df SS MS F p-value A 1 35.03 35.03 264.38 0.000 B 1 30.54 30.54 230.49 0.000 C 1 0.10 0.10 0.75 0.400 AB 1 2.98 2.98 22.49 0.001 AC 1 0.02 0.02 0.15 0.693 BC 1 0.03 0.03 0.23 0.646 ABC 1 0.002 0.002 0.15 0.902 Error 8 1.06 0.1325 Total 15 69.76","sub":"quant","why":"The ANOVA table shows very small p-values (0.000-0.001) only for main effects A and B and their AB interaction, so only A, B, and AB have a significant effect on the response. [VI.H.4]"},"revision":"5da31e31a155e0d07343fd78159aaa7d55c2b71295e458cb97f89ca3d268727e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-392":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Contour plot\",\"Normal probability plot\",\"Histogram\",\"Run chart\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following graphical tools can help assess the assumption of independence when analyzing the results of a full-factorial design?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"If run order is known, a run chart of the responses or residuals can reveal patterns that indicate a lack of independence. [VI.H.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Contour plot","Normal probability plot","Histogram","Run chart"],"stem":"Which of the following graphical tools can help assess the assumption of independence when analyzing the results of a full-factorial design?","sub":"quant","why":"If run order is known, a run chart of the responses or residuals can reveal patterns that indicate a lack of independence. [VI.H.4]"},"revision":"9c3befdcef5e6b6a5abbeb0f015f1ff0a56564c15058b9bd4589c5f519630315"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-393":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"0\",\"1\",\"8\",\"15\"],\"stem\":\"A 24 full-factorial experiment is conducted with a single replicate. If all main effects and interactions are included in the model, how many degrees of freedom does the error term have?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A single-replicate 2^4 design has 2^4-1=15 total degrees of freedom, all consumed by the four main effects, six two-way, four three-way, and one four-way interaction, leaving none for error. [VI.H.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["0","1","8","15"],"stem":"A 24 full-factorial experiment is conducted with a single replicate. If all main effects and interactions are included in the model, how many degrees of freedom does the error term have?","sub":"quant","why":"A single-replicate 2^4 design has 2^4-1=15 total degrees of freedom, all consumed by the four main effects, six two-way, four three-way, and one four-way interaction, leaving none for error. [VI.H.4]"},"revision":"4e7804f4169f4764f175cbcf1dc7ebf46540cf540040be3846d37ea3bafbbf1e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-394":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Full-factorial design\",\"Half-fractional factorial design\",\"Taguchi design\",\"Quarter-fractional factorial design\"],\"stem\":\"An experiment has eight factors at two levels each. The experiment has 128 runs. What is the experimental design called?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"128 runs is 2^8/2 = 2^7, i.e. half of the full 2^8=256 run design, making this a half-fractional factorial design. [VI.H.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Full-factorial design","Half-fractional factorial design","Taguchi design","Quarter-fractional factorial design"],"stem":"An experiment has eight factors at two levels each. The experiment has 128 runs. What is the experimental design called?","sub":"quant","why":"128 runs is 2^8/2 = 2^7, i.e. half of the full 2^8=256 run design, making this a half-fractional factorial design. [VI.H.5]"},"revision":"8eb0bfa5078f3b9f4a6388feca5351ab0f160f3d956cead9b8397ba976b4da4e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-395":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"all main effects and two-factor interactions are estimable.\",\"it has projection properties.\",\"it requires fewer runs.\",\"effects are confounded.\"],\"stem\":\"An advantage of using a full-factorial design over a fractional factorial design is that:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A full-factorial design allows all main effects and all two-factor interactions to be fully estimable, unlike a fractional factorial design, which requires fewer runs but confounds some effects. [VI.H.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["all main effects and two-factor interactions are estimable.","it has projection properties.","it requires fewer runs.","effects are confounded."],"stem":"An advantage of using a full-factorial design over a fractional factorial design is that:","sub":"quant","why":"A full-factorial design allows all main effects and all two-factor interactions to be fully estimable, unlike a fractional factorial design, which requires fewer runs but confounds some effects. [VI.H.5]"},"revision":"159049383e65e0f3b3a648111daf9d710794d329853676c1421b130f2f49f5ab"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-396":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"three-factor and higher interactions.\",\"other main effects.\",\"two-factor and higher interactions.\",\"no other effects.\"],\"stem\":\"In a resolution IV fractional factorial experiment, main effects are confounded with:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"In a resolution IV design, main effects are confounded with three-factor and higher interactions (and two-factor interactions with other two-factor interactions). [VI.H.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["three-factor and higher interactions.","other main effects.","two-factor and higher interactions.","no other effects."],"stem":"In a resolution IV fractional factorial experiment, main effects are confounded with:","sub":"quant","why":"In a resolution IV design, main effects are confounded with three-factor and higher interactions (and two-factor interactions with other two-factor interactions). [VI.H.5]"},"revision":"5b94a0536c6602d28fce9a14d8ddcaf1ed963d12d843f4fba30bb7cfe86ad785"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-397":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"BD and CE\",\"BC, DE, ABE, and ACD\",\"AD and CDE\",\"B is not aliased with other effects.\"],\"stem\":\"Consider an experiment with five factors each at two levels. Using the defining relation I = ABD = ACE = BCDE, with which effects is main effect B aliased?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Using the defining relation I=ABD=ACE=BCDE, multiplying B by each term gives B×ABD=AD, B×ACE=ABCE, and B×BCDE=CDE, so main effect B is aliased with AD and CDE (among the given choices). [VI.H.6]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["BD and CE","BC, DE, ABE, and ACD","AD and CDE","B is not aliased with other effects."],"stem":"Consider an experiment with five factors each at two levels. Using the defining relation I = ABD = ACE = BCDE, with which effects is main effect B aliased?","sub":"quant","why":"Using the defining relation I=ABD=ACE=BCDE, multiplying B by each term gives B×ABD=AD, B×ACE=ABCE, and B×BCDE=CDE, so main effect B is aliased with AD and CDE (among the given choices). [VI.H.6]"},"revision":"2a5c9697687febf0c9992236a6f3d80bf0f45f2fd265876ef76f6e6be6b03a5c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-398":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"interaction.\",\"confounding.\",\"measurement error.\",\"robustness.\"],\"stem\":\"An experiment is being conducted to find the optimal ingredients for a cake mix. The experimenters want the taste of the cake to be unaffected by small variations in oven temperature in the customers’ homes. This is a problem of:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Robustness means resistance to variation in an uncontrollable factor; since oven temperature in customers'' homes can''t be controlled, the cake mix should be designed to be robust to it. [VI.H.6]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["interaction.","confounding.","measurement error.","robustness."],"stem":"An experiment is being conducted to find the optimal ingredients for a cake mix. The experimenters want the taste of the cake to be unaffected by small variations in oven temperature in the customers’ homes. This is a problem of:","sub":"quant","why":"Robustness means resistance to variation in an uncontrollable factor; since oven temperature in customers'' homes can''t be controlled, the cake mix should be designed to be robust to it. [VI.H.6]"},"revision":"b9a9bf8c6b124dd9d67f0b4b8ad43859e84230e4db1bbaff421c3018999416ca"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-399":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"There is no robust design problem for this experiment.\",\"It may be possible to find settings of the ingredients that are robust to the oven temperature.\",\"The oven temperature does not have an effect on the taste of the cake.\",\"The three ingredients are uncontrollable factors in this experiment.\"],\"stem\":\"A team conducted an experiment to determine the optimal amount of three ingredients in a cake to maximize the taste. Two noise factors (oven temperature and baking time) were also included. A crossed-array design was used. The analysis indicated that there was a significant interaction between one of the ingredients and the oven temperature. Which of the following statements is true?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A significant interaction between an ingredient (a controllable factor) and oven temperature (a noise factor) suggests it may be possible to choose ingredient settings that make the response robust to oven temperature variation. [VI.H.6]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["There is no robust design problem for this experiment.","It may be possible to find settings of the ingredients that are robust to the oven temperature.","The oven temperature does not have an effect on the taste of the cake.","The three ingredients are uncontrollable factors in this experiment."],"stem":"A team conducted an experiment to determine the optimal amount of three ingredients in a cake to maximize the taste. Two noise factors (oven temperature and baking time) were also included. A crossed-array design was used. The analysis indicated that there was a significant interaction between one of the ingredients and the oven temperature. Which of the following statements is true?","sub":"quant","why":"A significant interaction between an ingredient (a controllable factor) and oven temperature (a noise factor) suggests it may be possible to choose ingredient settings that make the response robust to oven temperature variation. [VI.H.6]"},"revision":"0c95c537c0f3cac27ad16bd01d559dd1baed571f1df3b8a342d9dbe5a3c6a959"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-4":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Deming.\",\"Crosby.\",\"Feigenbaum.\",\"Juran.\"],\"stem\":\"Quality defined as “fitness for use” is attributed to:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Juran believed that a product is fit for use if it meets customer expectations. [I.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Deming.","Crosby.","Feigenbaum.","Juran."],"stem":"Quality defined as “fitness for use” is attributed to:","sub":"mgmt","why":"Juran believed that a product is fit for use if it meets customer expectations. [I.A.2]"},"revision":"2d307a6638d710780c363be88092e6a3c0ef4f7e4a7fb982d49c9cfef180720b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-40":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"concept phase.\",\"design and development phase.\",\"production phase.\",\"post-production phase.\"],\"stem\":\"Consider the product/process development life cycle. The phase in which the product or process is in the hands of the customer for use is the:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The post-production phase of the development life cycle includes release of the product/process to the customer for its intended use or function. Normal wear and maintenance occur in this phase as well. [III.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["concept phase.","design and development phase.","production phase.","post-production phase."],"stem":"Consider the product/process development life cycle. The phase in which the product or process is in the hands of the customer for use is the:","sub":"design","why":"The post-production phase of the development life cycle includes release of the product/process to the customer for its intended use or function. Normal wear and maintenance occur in this phase as well. [III.B.1]"},"revision":"8d4b6321da336ef7340b2351964b9ab64e378dcc846434f189f8db8884a07bd7"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-400":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"First layer/tier: policy statement\",\"Second layer/tier: procedures\",\"Third layer/tier: work instructions\",\"Fourth layer/tier: quality records/results of implementation\"],\"stem\":\"Consider the four layers or tiers of a quality manual. Which layer describes the company’s commitments, what it stands for, and objectives for quality?\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"The policy statement is the first layer in the manual because it not only provides information about the company’s commitment, but policy statements are often provided for each of the applicable standards. [II.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["First layer/tier: policy statement","Second layer/tier: procedures","Third layer/tier: work instructions","Fourth layer/tier: quality records/results of implementation"],"stem":"Consider the four layers or tiers of a quality manual. Which layer describes the company’s commitments, what it stands for, and objectives for quality?","sub":"qsys","why":"The policy statement is the first layer in the manual because it not only provides information about the company’s commitment, but policy statements are often provided for each of the applicable standards. [II.B.1]"},"revision":"0eab1b0bbabaafd7c66ddd92c6bf9de725dc71570d8461a521fb552bf6980b37"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-401":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"an internal quality auditor.\",\"an external quality auditor.\",\"an auditee.\",\"a sponsor.\"],\"stem\":\"A quality audit is to be conducted within a particular company. The company has hired an independent contractor to conduct the audit. The contractor is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"An independent contractor not associated with the auditee would be considered an external quality auditor. [II.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["an internal quality auditor.","an external quality auditor.","an auditee.","a sponsor."],"stem":"A quality audit is to be conducted within a particular company. The company has hired an independent contractor to conduct the audit. The contractor is an example of:","sub":"qsys","why":"An independent contractor not associated with the auditee would be considered an external quality auditor. [II.D.2]"},"revision":"b703cd5a23c8412c1c82275d8c2cc6ad5aa33f51a90f07c2261e0c8b87a3045b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-402":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"because national standards are incomplete.\",\"to provide consistency in terms, vocabulary, and requirements.\",\"because industry-specific standards were simply carbon copies of national standards.\",\"because some organizations were more profitable than others.\"],\"stem\":\"The need for an international standard like ISO 9000 arose:\",\"sub\":\"qsys\",\"why\":\"International standards are necessary because they provide a consistent way for interpreting terms, vocabulary, and requirements regardless of language or location of the reader (different languages interpreting information differently was a key problem with standards from different countries and in different languages). [II.C]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["because national standards are incomplete.","to provide consistency in terms, vocabulary, and requirements.","because industry-specific standards were simply carbon copies of national standards.","because some organizations were more profitable than others."],"stem":"The need for an international standard like ISO 9000 arose:","sub":"qsys","why":"International standards are necessary because they provide a consistent way for interpreting terms, vocabulary, and requirements regardless of language or location of the reader (different languages interpreting information differently was a key problem with standards from different countries and in different languages). [II.C]"},"revision":"104d465ebf3493b944553c0fa87b2b32a98f7283b402db2e62accca92dea9109"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-403":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"control gap.\",\"risk reduction.\",\"missed risk.\",\"control plan.\"],\"stem\":\"When there is no control in place to address a significant risk, or the control is insufficient for that particular risk, there is a:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"When the control in place is not adequate for the risk, or no control has been put in place for a significant risk, this is called a gap or control gap. [VII.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["control gap.","risk reduction.","missed risk.","control plan."],"stem":"When there is no control in place to address a significant risk, or the control is insufficient for that particular risk, there is a:","sub":"risk","why":"When the control in place is not adequate for the risk, or no control has been put in place for a significant risk, this is called a gap or control gap. [VII.C.1]"},"revision":"30d76fe28d7149c9bfb9991772763fb718ee29dadb4816cc0750fd688c02866d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-404":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"inherent risk.\",\"unacceptable risk.\",\"low risk.\",\"inherent untreatable risk.\"],\"stem\":\"A risk manager is focused on reducing the risk of outpatient laparoscopic surgery. After risk mitigations, the number of infections post-surgery has been reduced from 5% to 0.05% over six months but has not been able to be reduced below 0.05% despite further efforts. This level of risk of infection after all possible and imaginable mitigations are applied is known as:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"The risk remaining after all possible mitigations have been applied and cannot be reduced further is the risk that cannot be treated. In this case, since every possible mitigation has already been applied, residual risk equals inherent untreatable risk. [VII.C.5]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["inherent risk.","unacceptable risk.","low risk.","inherent untreatable risk."],"stem":"A risk manager is focused on reducing the risk of outpatient laparoscopic surgery. After risk mitigations, the number of infections post-surgery has been reduced from 5% to 0.05% over six months but has not been able to be reduced below 0.05% despite further efforts. This level of risk of infection after all possible and imaginable mitigations are applied is known as:","sub":"risk","why":"The risk remaining after all possible mitigations have been applied and cannot be reduced further is the risk that cannot be treated. In this case, since every possible mitigation has already been applied, residual risk equals inherent untreatable risk. [VII.C.5]"},"revision":"bed3e353b2935c86d3119c2faa7b3a1bdd50a7fdf56039b0464a19754a9bd24f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-405":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"A detailed description of the process\",\"The machine number used in the process\",\"Normal probability plots\",\"Steps for appropriate disposition of suspect material/products\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following can you typically find in a reaction plan for a process?\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"A reaction plan typically contains four elements: containment, diagnosis, verification, and disposition, including specific directions for how to dispose of contained material when a problem is detected in the process (scrap, rework, sort, use as-is, or return to vendor). [VII.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["A detailed description of the process","The machine number used in the process","Normal probability plots","Steps for appropriate disposition of suspect material/products"],"stem":"Which of the following can you typically find in a reaction plan for a process?","sub":"risk","why":"A reaction plan typically contains four elements: containment, diagnosis, verification, and disposition, including specific directions for how to dispose of contained material when a problem is detected in the process (scrap, rework, sort, use as-is, or return to vendor). [VII.C.1]"},"revision":"161062095c8df2bb8fc009ee04985bb836394ab9b2b6770ab630485c135a44f7"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-406":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Do not drive at all and so do not pick up groceries until snow melts.\",\"Put on snow tires for better traction.\",\"Pay your neighbor with a truck to drive and buy groceries for you.\",\"Drive in snow and hope for the best.\"],\"stem\":\"When driving in the snow and slippery black ice to pick up groceries, which of the following risk treatment options qualifies as risk mitigation?\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"Putting on snow tires is risk mitigation. Not driving at all is risk avoidance. Paying the neighbor to drive is risk transfer. Driving and hoping for the best is risk acceptance. [VII.C.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Do not drive at all and so do not pick up groceries until snow melts.","Put on snow tires for better traction.","Pay your neighbor with a truck to drive and buy groceries for you.","Drive in snow and hope for the best."],"stem":"When driving in the snow and slippery black ice to pick up groceries, which of the following risk treatment options qualifies as risk mitigation?","sub":"risk","why":"Putting on snow tires is risk mitigation. Not driving at all is risk avoidance. Paying the neighbor to drive is risk transfer. Driving and hoping for the best is risk acceptance. [VII.C.3]"},"revision":"d74c2e053efb54168f5e1f0ca127cc106ec7e6fbf570b031395b7aa83be79e77"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-407":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Completed FMEA\",\"Risk audit results\",\"Frequency of risk tracking review and reporting\",\"Results of monitoring\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following should be contained in a risk management plan?\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"Of the listed options, only the frequency of risk tracking review and reporting can be stated at the time of risk management planning; the others (a completed FMEA, audit results, monitoring results) are outputs produced later, during risk execution. [VII.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Completed FMEA","Risk audit results","Frequency of risk tracking review and reporting","Results of monitoring"],"stem":"Which of the following should be contained in a risk management plan?","sub":"risk","why":"Of the listed options, only the frequency of risk tracking review and reporting can be stated at the time of risk management planning; the others (a completed FMEA, audit results, monitoring results) are outputs produced later, during risk execution. [VII.B.1]"},"revision":"c7ecea6a203e7a8590bc33cee528641bf7233336776169fadca1fb9bbaac5108"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-408":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"evaluation/testing (e.g., human factors/usability, verification, validation, clinical investigations).\",\"supplier evaluation criteria (e.g., self-assessment forms, audits, etc.).\",\"final acceptance criteria.\",\"Nonconforming material reports.\"],\"stem\":\"When assessing process risk through audits, a risk-based audit approach for a design change should include a review of:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"A risk-based audit approach for a design change should include a review of the level of evaluation/testing (e.g., human factors/usability, verification, validation, clinical investigations), along with related regulatory requirements and the level of change approval required. [VII.C.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["evaluation/testing (e.g., human factors/usability, verification, validation, clinical investigations).","supplier evaluation criteria (e.g., self-assessment forms, audits, etc.).","final acceptance criteria.","Nonconforming material reports."],"stem":"When assessing process risk through audits, a risk-based audit approach for a design change should include a review of:","sub":"risk","why":"A risk-based audit approach for a design change should include a review of the level of evaluation/testing (e.g., human factors/usability, verification, validation, clinical investigations), along with related regulatory requirements and the level of change approval required. [VII.C.3]"},"revision":"c78a3ad9eaed40bcea6d18f9f1c057171ea0d0459d40559c1d44a015f0c3c0e4"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-409":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"risk control.\",\"risk oversight.\",\"risk planning.\",\"risk reporting.\"],\"stem\":\"Maintaining risk at or below an acceptable level is part of:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"Maintaining risk at or below an acceptable level is a core purpose of risk control. [VII.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["risk control.","risk oversight.","risk planning.","risk reporting."],"stem":"Maintaining risk at or below an acceptable level is part of:","sub":"risk","why":"Maintaining risk at or below an acceptable level is a core purpose of risk control. [VII.C.1]"},"revision":"232dee129e474e1caf088140ea53b6739470f39894bbf9724d203663d4cb3e1e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-41":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Length 18.0 to 18.2 cm\",\"Low costs\",\"No scrap\",\"Operating without failures\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following would be a Critical to Quality characteristic?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"A Critical to Quality characteristic is needed to achieve the driver and may consist of a specification such as length 18.0 to 18.2 cm. [III.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Length 18.0 to 18.2 cm","Low costs","No scrap","Operating without failures"],"stem":"Which of the following would be a Critical to Quality characteristic?","sub":"design","why":"A Critical to Quality characteristic is needed to achieve the driver and may consist of a specification such as length 18.0 to 18.2 cm. [III.B.1]"},"revision":"1fe87ba4b56900aaf3ed561c057dd3def2975b7ec158977cf183a57b5c6422e9"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-410":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"To quantify risk\",\"To determine the level of impact\",\"To help fill out a risk register/FMEA\",\"To monitor risk\"],\"stem\":\"What is the purpose of a rating scale for consequence or severity?\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"A rating scale for consequence or severity exists to distinguish and quantify the level of impact so that consequences across different risks can be compared and evaluated. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["To quantify risk","To determine the level of impact","To help fill out a risk register/FMEA","To monitor risk"],"stem":"What is the purpose of a rating scale for consequence or severity?","sub":"risk","why":"A rating scale for consequence or severity exists to distinguish and quantify the level of impact so that consequences across different risks can be compared and evaluated. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"e64cf4acac8caea96e91fd4fc61ebbfa070531b08c60c38826a083074c137341"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-411":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"it cannot be used to identify combinations of failure modes, only single failure modes.\",\"it only uses binary data such as failure/no failure.\",\"it is too complicated, since it does not require the user to know anything about the process or product.\",\"it cannot be used in certain industries.\"],\"stem\":\"A fault tree analysis (FTA) is a useful tool for prioritizing risks. One of the drawbacks or limitations of the FTA is:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"Fault tree analysis (FTA) only uses binary data (failure/no failure), which is often considered a limitation of the technique, although the method remains very effective for failure analysis. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["it cannot be used to identify combinations of failure modes, only single failure modes.","it only uses binary data such as failure/no failure.","it is too complicated, since it does not require the user to know anything about the process or product.","it cannot be used in certain industries."],"stem":"A fault tree analysis (FTA) is a useful tool for prioritizing risks. One of the drawbacks or limitations of the FTA is:","sub":"risk","why":"Fault tree analysis (FTA) only uses binary data (failure/no failure), which is often considered a limitation of the technique, although the method remains very effective for failure analysis. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"ed5e8152bbaed06eaa6726859a14afffa61f7fecce2b5af00837e527b026a3a9"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-412":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"$60,000\",\"$65,000\",\"$72,000\",\"$170,000\"],\"stem\":\"Last month, a local company reported the following quality costs: Incoming inspection $60,000; Design support $12,000; Field trials $110,000; Returns by customers $40,000. What is the total appraisal cost for last month?\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"The only appraisal cost among the four listed is incoming inspection ($60,000); design support and field trials are prevention costs, and returns by customers is an external failure cost. [II.E]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["$60,000","$65,000","$72,000","$170,000"],"stem":"Last month, a local company reported the following quality costs: Incoming inspection $60,000; Design support $12,000; Field trials $110,000; Returns by customers $40,000. What is the total appraisal cost for last month?","sub":"mgmt","why":"The only appraisal cost among the four listed is incoming inspection ($60,000); design support and field trials are prevention costs, and returns by customers is an external failure cost. [II.E]"},"revision":"49d901081ed54a2215e98974b64cce030f548efdeb492883a66973c029349f66"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-413":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"self-directed work teams.\",\"work groups.\",\"process improvement teams.\",\"functional teams.\"],\"stem\":\"Teams that are formed for the purpose of elevating an existing process or developing a new one are:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Process improvement teams should be cross-functional, consisting of people from many different departments or areas within the company (and possibly externally, such as customers and suppliers). [I.D.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["self-directed work teams.","work groups.","process improvement teams.","functional teams."],"stem":"Teams that are formed for the purpose of elevating an existing process or developing a new one are:","sub":"mgmt","why":"Process improvement teams should be cross-functional, consisting of people from many different departments or areas within the company (and possibly externally, such as customers and suppliers). [I.D.4]"},"revision":"fd6fc154388c1b93bc53a4dde9a918b320c41e8c8a434a29079ae6ce93196214"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-414":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Checklists\",\"Histogram\",\"Quality function deployment\",\"Control chart\"],\"stem\":\"What quality tool would be good for conducting a needs analysis when considering a new training program?\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Using a QFD (quality function deployment) to identify the needs of a training program can provide a more focused approach to the program''s design and delivery. [II.F.15]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Checklists","Histogram","Quality function deployment","Control chart"],"stem":"What quality tool would be good for conducting a needs analysis when considering a new training program?","sub":"mgmt","why":"Using a QFD (quality function deployment) to identify the needs of a training program can provide a more focused approach to the program''s design and delivery. [II.F.15]"},"revision":"fec49b9d18f48afeb9df71932088d746e43f1dae840fd413abc834bc3bb1af54"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-415":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"rating the supplier’s physical location.\",\"rating the supplier’s employees’ ethnicity.\",\"rating the supplier’s financial, manufacturing, and quality systems.\",\"rating the supplier’s list of other customers.\"],\"stem\":\"An organization desires to begin rating the capabilities of one of its new suppliers. The process for rating this supplier must include:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"The supplier''s physical location may be of some interest but is not necessarily part of the rating process. Evaluating a supplier''s ethnicity would be both unethical and illegal. [I.H.8]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["rating the supplier’s physical location.","rating the supplier’s employees’ ethnicity.","rating the supplier’s financial, manufacturing, and quality systems.","rating the supplier’s list of other customers."],"stem":"An organization desires to begin rating the capabilities of one of its new suppliers. The process for rating this supplier must include:","sub":"mgmt","why":"The supplier''s physical location may be of some interest but is not necessarily part of the rating process. Evaluating a supplier''s ethnicity would be both unethical and illegal. [I.H.8]"},"revision":"42ecd8f74e9eb1376a32f121017eb8edbac572cbe4dde26e09c7eb884f0790c9"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-416":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"truthful.\",\"opinionated.\",\"prejudiced.\",\"blunt.\"],\"stem\":\"A safe way to avoid ASQ Code of Ethics violations is to be consistently:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Section A of the ASQ Code of Ethics, \\\"Act with Integrity and Honesty,\\\" includes the statement, \\\"Be truthful and transparent in all professional interactions and activities.\\\" [I.C]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["truthful.","opinionated.","prejudiced.","blunt."],"stem":"A safe way to avoid ASQ Code of Ethics violations is to be consistently:","sub":"mgmt","why":"Section A of the ASQ Code of Ethics, \"Act with Integrity and Honesty,\" includes the statement, \"Be truthful and transparent in all professional interactions and activities.\" [I.C]"},"revision":"093cfbc8fa1b22d14a297306991f0f9e849835fd0e633ae48a0e73d52d0127ee"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-417":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"individuals begin to shift from personal concerns to the needs of the team.\",\"the team’s mission is clarified and specific roles are identified.\",\"team members still think and act as individuals instead of impacting as a team.\",\"the team has matured and is working in the best interest of the team and team goals.\"],\"stem\":\"Common development stages that teams often progress through are known as forming, storming, norming, and performing. In the “norming” stage:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"In the norming phase, interpersonal conflicts have less impact on team dynamics and work, and individuals begin shifting from personal concerns to the needs of the team. [I.D]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["individuals begin to shift from personal concerns to the needs of the team.","the team’s mission is clarified and specific roles are identified.","team members still think and act as individuals instead of impacting as a team.","the team has matured and is working in the best interest of the team and team goals."],"stem":"Common development stages that teams often progress through are known as forming, storming, norming, and performing. In the “norming” stage:","sub":"mgmt","why":"In the norming phase, interpersonal conflicts have less impact on team dynamics and work, and individuals begin shifting from personal concerns to the needs of the team. [I.D]"},"revision":"06abc08c55e5719d53b5d03d01723aaf504702e8d8594b9b8175f4edbb9dadf8"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-418":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"new product validation.\",\"non-predicted variables.\",\"net present value.\",\"near-perfect variation.\"],\"stem\":\"The acronym NPV stands for:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"NPV is short for net present value. [I.B.2.c]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["new product validation.","non-predicted variables.","net present value.","near-perfect variation."],"stem":"The acronym NPV stands for:","sub":"mgmt","why":"NPV is short for net present value. [I.B.2.c]"},"revision":"5e7cba849a5909d1c16e31348ba71a29e8969363222559531d2fb7e4573213c2"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-419":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"credited.\",\"disseminated\",\"protected.\",\"shared.\"],\"stem\":\"According to the ASQ Code of Ethics, confidential information should be:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"The ASQ Code of Ethics states members should \\\"ensure the protection and integrity of confidential information.\\\" [I.C]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["credited.","disseminated","protected.","shared."],"stem":"According to the ASQ Code of Ethics, confidential information should be:","sub":"mgmt","why":"The ASQ Code of Ethics states members should \"ensure the protection and integrity of confidential information.\" [I.C]"},"revision":"9062e3d79573b7b2c825126425a320fabbaf386987f8c7bf3573d9d2fe433c55"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-42":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"specification phase.\",\"prototype phase.\",\"concept phase.\",\"distribution phase.\"],\"stem\":\"Consider the product/process development life cycle. The phase in which all possible\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"In the concept phase, all possible solutions are explored. Solutions that are not feasible are eliminated. [III.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["specification phase.","prototype phase.","concept phase.","distribution phase."],"stem":"Consider the product/process development life cycle. The phase in which all possible","sub":"design","why":"In the concept phase, all possible solutions are explored. Solutions that are not feasible are eliminated. [III.B.1]"},"revision":"7cbaf1603e074ce912ad11f10c55796ca04ef664d6c2d57628d6fe7b6a0946d2"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-420":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Fitness for use\",\"Conformance to specification\",\"Defect-free products\",\"Uniformity around a target value\"],\"stem\":\"Philip Crosby is credited with which definition of quality?\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Crosby is credited with \\\"conformance to specification.\\\" Juran is credited with \\\"fitness for use,\\\" and Genichi Taguchi is credited with \\\"uniformity around a target value.\\\" \\\"Defect-free product\\\" was never a formal definition of quality. [I.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Fitness for use","Conformance to specification","Defect-free products","Uniformity around a target value"],"stem":"Philip Crosby is credited with which definition of quality?","sub":"mgmt","why":"Crosby is credited with \"conformance to specification.\" Juran is credited with \"fitness for use,\" and Genichi Taguchi is credited with \"uniformity around a target value.\" \"Defect-free product\" was never a formal definition of quality. [I.A.1]"},"revision":"cb7d88643acb1af782ce3d0af38da7ab6a754f0a38246ff25a0aedd3dc4d22b3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-421":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Listening to music\",\"Confirming\",\"Simultaneous speaking\",\"Being defensive\"],\"stem\":\"Active listening by team members is an important skill that is not often easy to attain with new teams. Which of the following is one of the dimensions of active listening?\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Confirming is the only option here that counts as active listening; listening to music and simultaneous speaking both hinder communication, and being defensive on a team quickly defeats the purpose of teamwork. [I.F]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Listening to music","Confirming","Simultaneous speaking","Being defensive"],"stem":"Active listening by team members is an important skill that is not often easy to attain with new teams. Which of the following is one of the dimensions of active listening?","sub":"mgmt","why":"Confirming is the only option here that counts as active listening; listening to music and simultaneous speaking both hinder communication, and being defensive on a team quickly defeats the purpose of teamwork. [I.F]"},"revision":"1a8a7512f357b2082b837a315fb64d9cc7c2665f83e334b58f3a4193c579fd8e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-422":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Obtaining a free conference pass as a presenter\",\"Expensing the conference at work as a training event\",\"Paying for the conference from personal funds\",\"Attending the conference using someone else’s badge\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following actions would result in an ASQ Code of Ethics violation?\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"The ASQ Code of Ethics requires members to accurately represent their own professional qualifications and to uphold the integrity, honor, and dignity of the profession; attending a conference under someone else''s badge misrepresents identity and credentials. [I.C]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Obtaining a free conference pass as a presenter","Expensing the conference at work as a training event","Paying for the conference from personal funds","Attending the conference using someone else’s badge"],"stem":"Which of the following actions would result in an ASQ Code of Ethics violation?","sub":"mgmt","why":"The ASQ Code of Ethics requires members to accurately represent their own professional qualifications and to uphold the integrity, honor, and dignity of the profession; attending a conference under someone else''s badge misrepresents identity and credentials. [I.C]"},"revision":"18c40ba9fd8e5da9c198af97519e8fcf11b81dfdf3d2cc5dd23009da3e5d0f65"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-423":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"prevention cost.\",\"appraisal cost.\",\"internal failure cost.\",\"external failure cost.\"],\"stem\":\"In one year, a company spent more than $500,000 on warranty issues. This cost would be categorized as:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Warranty costs fall under external failure costs. [II.E]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["prevention cost.","appraisal cost.","internal failure cost.","external failure cost."],"stem":"In one year, a company spent more than $500,000 on warranty issues. This cost would be categorized as:","sub":"mgmt","why":"Warranty costs fall under external failure costs. [II.E]"},"revision":"110c5d43fc1e5893439a88150ccee72368ffbe0c66c8bb0da40bead682a43b8f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-424":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"individuals begin to shift from personal concerns to needs of the team.\",\"the team’s mission is clarified and specific roles are identified.\",\"team members still think and act as individuals instead of impacting as a team.\",\"team has matured and is working in the best interest of the team and team goals.\"],\"stem\":\"Common development stages teams often progress through are known as forming, storming, norming, and performing. In the forming stage:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"In the forming phase, an important task is developing and clarifying the team''s mission and identifying specific roles. [I.D]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["individuals begin to shift from personal concerns to needs of the team.","the team’s mission is clarified and specific roles are identified.","team members still think and act as individuals instead of impacting as a team.","team has matured and is working in the best interest of the team and team goals."],"stem":"Common development stages teams often progress through are known as forming, storming, norming, and performing. In the forming stage:","sub":"mgmt","why":"In the forming phase, an important task is developing and clarifying the team''s mission and identifying specific roles. [I.D]"},"revision":"8686ea98c37e3affe1f11a67786005f8aef11d014d56977a9fdba87fe04b4136"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-425":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Institute barriers that stand between the hourly worker and his right to pride of workmanship.\",\"Institute modern methods of training on the job.\",\"15% of quality problems are due to the system.\",\"85% of quality problems are due to management.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is one of Deming’s fourteen points?\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Instituting modern training methods was necessary to introduce new methods to the workforce; the other listed options misstate Deming''s points (which call for removing barriers to pride of workmanship, and attribute roughly 85% of quality problems to management/the system rather than 15%). [I.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Institute barriers that stand between the hourly worker and his right to pride of workmanship.","Institute modern methods of training on the job.","15% of quality problems are due to the system.","85% of quality problems are due to management."],"stem":"Which of the following is one of Deming’s fourteen points?","sub":"mgmt","why":"Instituting modern training methods was necessary to introduce new methods to the workforce; the other listed options misstate Deming''s points (which call for removing barriers to pride of workmanship, and attribute roughly 85% of quality problems to management/the system rather than 15%). [I.A.1]"},"revision":"49109b22e644969e31b6dca523a8ce54e3c1e31a904dacde06e980ec8ad574f3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-426":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Waiting\",\"Excess motion\",\"Defect correction\",\"Lost creativity\"],\"stem\":\"A group of operators in a semiconductor factory have an idea to improve their workflow. However, upper management has been reluctant to explore this idea. How might this group of operators classify this response?\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"This is an example of waste due to lost creativity. Management should encourage a quality team to investigate the proposed idea, which could lead to process improvements and also boost employee morale. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Waiting","Excess motion","Defect correction","Lost creativity"],"stem":"A group of operators in a semiconductor factory have an idea to improve their workflow. However, upper management has been reluctant to explore this idea. How might this group of operators classify this response?","sub":"mgmt","why":"This is an example of waste due to lost creativity. Management should encourage a quality team to investigate the proposed idea, which could lead to process improvements and also boost employee morale. [V.D]"},"revision":"1bd040be5d8e158c79387ca6cca65e0fc1a5d4f9cc7707ce247907078b8c3e17"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-427":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"people doing the work.\",\"the Accountable person(s).\",\"people who are being asked for advice.\",\"leaders, sponsors, and project champions.\"],\"stem\":\"According to RACI definitions, examples of people in the Informed role are:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Informed people are not those actually performing the project tasks, but they are kept informed so they can help remove roadblocks and ensure financial and resource support. [I.B.2.d]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["people doing the work.","the Accountable person(s).","people who are being asked for advice.","leaders, sponsors, and project champions."],"stem":"According to RACI definitions, examples of people in the Informed role are:","sub":"mgmt","why":"Informed people are not those actually performing the project tasks, but they are kept informed so they can help remove roadblocks and ensure financial and resource support. [I.B.2.d]"},"revision":"261ced8b6786070c68ac9b8e52b7826fba0129494e6b4898abf2e118dc1a1ad1"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-428":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"results.\",\"requirements.\",\"procedures.\",\"costs.\"],\"stem\":\"The Malcolm Baldrige National Quality Award emphasizes:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"The Malcolm Baldrige National Quality Award emphasizes results; it is not given for specific products or services but to reward achievements in quality and performance excellence. [II.C]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["results.","requirements.","procedures.","costs."],"stem":"The Malcolm Baldrige National Quality Award emphasizes:","sub":"mgmt","why":"The Malcolm Baldrige National Quality Award emphasizes results; it is not given for specific products or services but to reward achievements in quality and performance excellence. [II.C]"},"revision":"857653f05ab6cd43f2737ac5e7fec8f2b0c38761331e237afd34c67c83abd076"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-429":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"$12,000\",\"$77,000\",\"$100,000\",\"$122,000\"],\"stem\":\"Last month, a local company reported the following quality costs: Incoming inspection $60,000; Design support $12,000; Field trials $110,000; Returns by customers $40,000. What is the total prevention cost for last month?\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Design support and field trials both fall under prevention costs: $12,000 + $110,000 = $122,000. [II.E]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["$12,000","$77,000","$100,000","$122,000"],"stem":"Last month, a local company reported the following quality costs: Incoming inspection $60,000; Design support $12,000; Field trials $110,000; Returns by customers $40,000. What is the total prevention cost for last month?","sub":"mgmt","why":"Design support and field trials both fall under prevention costs: $12,000 + $110,000 = $122,000. [II.E]"},"revision":"2a73f58c15d506b79e6ae2cf1462df52ae2866cef70e361e3bde878fb8d774da"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-43":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Process cycle time in seconds\",\"An insurance policy\",\"Mistakes as in defects per million opportunities\",\"Number of dropped calls\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is an example of the Voice of the Customer in a Critical to Quality Tree?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"An insurance policy is what the customer asks for. Voice of the Customer may be vague and not measurable, unlike the Critical to Quality characteristics. [III.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Process cycle time in seconds","An insurance policy","Mistakes as in defects per million opportunities","Number of dropped calls"],"stem":"Which of the following is an example of the Voice of the Customer in a Critical to Quality Tree?","sub":"design","why":"An insurance policy is what the customer asks for. Voice of the Customer may be vague and not measurable, unlike the Critical to Quality characteristics. [III.B.1]"},"revision":"b4773d4998c53a0fe717b337061bd4ffe960edf7d5bddc4d3573fc734752aedf"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-430":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Lean Six Sigma.\",\"Six Sigma.\",\"lean.\",\"theory of constraints.\"],\"stem\":\"A system improvement program based primarily on minimizing variability is called:\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Lean deals with eliminating waste. Lean-Six Sigma focuses on eliminating waste and minimizing variability. [I.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Lean Six Sigma.","Six Sigma.","lean.","theory of constraints."],"stem":"A system improvement program based primarily on minimizing variability is called:","sub":"ci","why":"Lean deals with eliminating waste. Lean-Six Sigma focuses on eliminating waste and minimizing variability. [I.A.1]"},"revision":"532c4cb308a1c5fd55269f8e452fad3af668a3195a177924b3a6b28279a3c3c1"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-431":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"To organize and summarize ideas generated about a particular problem or issue\",\"To communicate hierarchical relationships between events\",\"To assess a process implementation plan during the initial phases of operations\",\"To visualize and identify causal relationships of a problem or situation\"],\"stem\":\"What is the main purpose of an interrelationship digraph?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Interrelationship digraphs provide a means to identify sequential or cause- and-effect relationships of a problem or situation. Affinity diagrams are used to organize general thoughts and ideas about a topic. Tree diagrams are used to visualize hierarchical events of a process, and process decision program charts (PDPCs) help to evaluate a process implementation plan at a high level. [V.B]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["To organize and summarize ideas generated about a particular problem or issue","To communicate hierarchical relationships between events","To assess a process implementation plan during the initial phases of operations","To visualize and identify causal relationships of a problem or situation"],"stem":"What is the main purpose of an interrelationship digraph?","sub":"ci","why":"Interrelationship digraphs provide a means to identify sequential or cause- and-effect relationships of a problem or situation. Affinity diagrams are used to organize general thoughts and ideas about a topic. Tree diagrams are used to visualize hierarchical events of a process, and process decision program charts (PDPCs) help to evaluate a process implementation plan at a high level. [V.B]"},"revision":"073b6ce5cc1a5006fbd0a3b8a912549b82db1dcfb44210ca21a41fa830d56d0a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-432":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Immediately scrap all defective batteries.\",\"Wait for the decision of the Material Review Board (MRB).\",\"Begin a product traceability flowchart.\",\"Continue production as usual.\"],\"stem\":\"A defect was discovered in a laptop battery. How should the company handle the disposition of this defective battery?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"When a product defect is discovered, the material review board (MRB) determines what corrective actions to take. [IV.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Immediately scrap all defective batteries.","Wait for the decision of the Material Review Board (MRB).","Begin a product traceability flowchart.","Continue production as usual."],"stem":"A defect was discovered in a laptop battery. How should the company handle the disposition of this defective battery?","sub":"ci","why":"When a product defect is discovered, the material review board (MRB) determines what corrective actions to take. [IV.B.4]"},"revision":"4927fb8fc622883cc548fb754c3a7e1dab92a93ce94d272dc32767d010491d3a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-433":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Problem identification\",\"Correction\",\"Recurrence control\",\"Effectiveness assessment\"],\"stem\":\"Which phase of the corrective action cycle helps prevent problem backsliding?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"In the problem identification phase, the team identifies sources for improvement and develops a clear problem statement. In the correction phase, the team develops feasible solutions to the problem and recommends the best choice to implement. In the recurrence control phase, the team standardizes the solution to ensure that the problem does not reoccur and prevent backsliding. Finally, in the effectiveness assessment phase, the team continues monitoring the process to identify additional opportunities for improvement. [V.E]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Problem identification","Correction","Recurrence control","Effectiveness assessment"],"stem":"Which phase of the corrective action cycle helps prevent problem backsliding?","sub":"ci","why":"In the problem identification phase, the team identifies sources for improvement and develops a clear problem statement. In the correction phase, the team develops feasible solutions to the problem and recommends the best choice to implement. In the recurrence control phase, the team standardizes the solution to ensure that the problem does not reoccur and prevent backsliding. Finally, in the effectiveness assessment phase, the team continues monitoring the process to identify additional opportunities for improvement. [V.E]"},"revision":"aee18b82ac8201b4ac178f36cf862aecfe2264ea29226cce635fb0c4bb6f6ca6"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-434":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Deming\",\"Taguchi\",\"Juran\",\"Ishikawa\"],\"stem\":\"Which quality leader proposed using signal-to-noise ratios to improve robustness of a process?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Genichi Taguchi proposed signal-to-noise (S/N) ratios to optimize a response variable while minimizing its variability, with different S/N formulas depending on whether the goal is to maximize the response, minimize it, or hit a target value. [VI.H.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Deming","Taguchi","Juran","Ishikawa"],"stem":"Which quality leader proposed using signal-to-noise ratios to improve robustness of a process?","sub":"ci","why":"Genichi Taguchi proposed signal-to-noise (S/N) ratios to optimize a response variable while minimizing its variability, with different S/N formulas depending on whether the goal is to maximize the response, minimize it, or hit a target value. [VI.H.5]"},"revision":"6420cc4a0ab2b8bdcc1f723828bdd95e82db0b58d65fa30efc9f9f432f43058e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-435":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Kanban\",\"Single minute exchange of dies (SMED)\",\"Overall equipment effectiveness (OEE)\",\"Standardized work\"],\"stem\":\"Which lean tool results in reductions in process variation, more consistent products, and simplified downstream activities?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Standardized work means that each activity is done the same way every time. Because each employee performs the activities the same way, having standardized work in practice will help reduce process variation and provide more consistent (and likely better) products. Standardized work can also help simplify activities downstream. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Kanban","Single minute exchange of dies (SMED)","Overall equipment effectiveness (OEE)","Standardized work"],"stem":"Which lean tool results in reductions in process variation, more consistent products, and simplified downstream activities?","sub":"ci","why":"Standardized work means that each activity is done the same way every time. Because each employee performs the activities the same way, having standardized work in practice will help reduce process variation and provide more consistent (and likely better) products. Standardized work can also help simplify activities downstream. [V.D]"},"revision":"927f7339a76350b37f50835801fa533ad2d9e71b274d079f765c913339a0a7c8"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-436":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"D\",\"M\",\"A\",\"I\"],\"stem\":\"A quality project team is investigating the root cause of variation in a fish tank manufacturing facility. In what stage of the Define-Measure-Analyze-Improve- Control (DMAIC) process is this team?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"DMAIC is made up of five phases used in an improvement process: define, measure, analyze, improve, and control. In the analyze phase, the team has already clearly defined the problem and related metrics, and has measured the performance of the process. The team now investigates the root cause of variation in the process or poor performance. [V.C]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["D","M","A","I"],"stem":"A quality project team is investigating the root cause of variation in a fish tank manufacturing facility. In what stage of the Define-Measure-Analyze-Improve- Control (DMAIC) process is this team?","sub":"ci","why":"DMAIC is made up of five phases used in an improvement process: define, measure, analyze, improve, and control. In the analyze phase, the team has already clearly defined the problem and related metrics, and has measured the performance of the process. The team now investigates the root cause of variation in the process or poor performance. [V.C]"},"revision":"5640024ba6a9140a626c24451808eada424c0e32a6c9012b0e982f7935c0a08a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-437":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Lean Six Sigma.\",\"Six Sigma.\",\"lean.\",\"theory of constraints.\"],\"stem\":\"A system improvement program based on both eliminating waste and reducing variation is called:\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Lean deals with eliminating waste. Six Sigma focuses on reducing variation. Lean-Six Sigma focuses on eliminating waste and minimizing variability. [I.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Lean Six Sigma.","Six Sigma.","lean.","theory of constraints."],"stem":"A system improvement program based on both eliminating waste and reducing variation is called:","sub":"ci","why":"Lean deals with eliminating waste. Six Sigma focuses on reducing variation. Lean-Six Sigma focuses on eliminating waste and minimizing variability. [I.A.1]"},"revision":"d449a7114fcf3755ba807194b7c04efd9016921d9e45bf8ef627dcda44f8753c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-438":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Problem identification\",\"Correction\",\"Recurrence control\",\"Effectiveness assessment\"],\"stem\":\"In which phase of the corrective action cycle does a team develop feasible solutions to improve a process?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"In the problem identification phase, the team identifies sources for improvement and develops a clear problem statement. In the correction phase, the team develops feasible solutions to the problem and recommends the best choice to implement. In the recurrence control phase, the team standardizes the solution to ensure that the problem does not reoccur. Finally, in the effectiveness assessment phase, the team continues monitoring the process to identify additional opportunities for improvement. [V.E]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Problem identification","Correction","Recurrence control","Effectiveness assessment"],"stem":"In which phase of the corrective action cycle does a team develop feasible solutions to improve a process?","sub":"ci","why":"In the problem identification phase, the team identifies sources for improvement and develops a clear problem statement. In the correction phase, the team develops feasible solutions to the problem and recommends the best choice to implement. In the recurrence control phase, the team standardizes the solution to ensure that the problem does not reoccur. Finally, in the effectiveness assessment phase, the team continues monitoring the process to identify additional opportunities for improvement. [V.E]"},"revision":"51d9461acc9ae028d8531ae16e513739887110aae1b2606ae68cac791005bbc1"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-439":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Root cause analysis\",\"5 Why\",\"Splitting tables\",\"Concentration diagram\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is the activity that may be part of the data transform phase?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"The data transform phase''s main goal is preparing data to fit the parameters of another system, through steps like sorting and filtering out irrelevant items, de-duplicating and cleansing, translating and converting, encrypting sensitive information, or splitting or joining tables.\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Root cause analysis","5 Why","Splitting tables","Concentration diagram"],"stem":"Which of the following is the activity that may be part of the data transform phase?","sub":"ci","why":"The data transform phase''s main goal is preparing data to fit the parameters of another system, through steps like sorting and filtering out irrelevant items, de-duplicating and cleansing, translating and converting, encrypting sensitive information, or splitting or joining tables."},"revision":"bbd6379ba5330c67220fbb550cd2bfe1a560b088d26f1c9261223b3c6b36663b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-44":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"process validation.\",\"quality function deployment.\",\"statistical process control.\",\"acceptance sampling.\"],\"stem\":\"A tool that is useful for converting customer needs into design concepts is:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"QFD, which uses the \\\"house of quality,\\\" is helpful in turning customer needs into product characteristics or design concepts. [III.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["process validation.","quality function deployment.","statistical process control.","acceptance sampling."],"stem":"A tool that is useful for converting customer needs into design concepts is:","sub":"design","why":"QFD, which uses the \"house of quality,\" is helpful in turning customer needs into product characteristics or design concepts. [III.B.2]"},"revision":"01299188b5216c0283f7b13d5c979741d59438ae8114349925bc559f77ef5a77"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-440":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Inventory\",\"Waiting\",\"Overproduction\",\"Excess processing\"],\"stem\":\"A company maintained a large stock of supplies in its warehouse. As a result, a sizeable portion of the materials had degraded by the time that they were required. As what type of waste would the company classify this event?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"This is an example of waste due to inventory. The company has more inventory in storage than is necessary. As a result, it must pay to store these materials, which have no value to the customer. In addition, the company’s high inventory also leads to additional losses when some of the products degrade before use. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Inventory","Waiting","Overproduction","Excess processing"],"stem":"A company maintained a large stock of supplies in its warehouse. As a result, a sizeable portion of the materials had degraded by the time that they were required. As what type of waste would the company classify this event?","sub":"ci","why":"This is an example of waste due to inventory. The company has more inventory in storage than is necessary. As a result, it must pay to store these materials, which have no value to the customer. In addition, the company’s high inventory also leads to additional losses when some of the products degrade before use. [V.D]"},"revision":"cddad5fbf359db84ebdfba16827c906ce5162c9842e540d0dc9a277115c0f836"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-441":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Slower reaction time\",\"Identification of the responsible employee\",\"Future problem prevention\",\"Decreased communication\"],\"stem\":\"What item below is a benefit of a 5 Why Analysis?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"The purpose of a 5 Why root cause analysis is to identify the system or process that caused the issue, which helps prevent the problem from recurring; fast reaction time and improved communication are also benefits. [V.E]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Slower reaction time","Identification of the responsible employee","Future problem prevention","Decreased communication"],"stem":"What item below is a benefit of a 5 Why Analysis?","sub":"ci","why":"The purpose of a 5 Why root cause analysis is to identify the system or process that caused the issue, which helps prevent the problem from recurring; fast reaction time and improved communication are also benefits. [V.E]"},"revision":"e685a2e312c29868f6c3c54dd80863b2854d03eff62fb700f47c451d0f1f442b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-442":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Minor defect\",\"Major defect\",\"Serious defect\",\"Critical defect\"],\"stem\":\"A company’s shipment of textbooks to customers became illegible during the printing process due to an ink malfunction. The company must replace each customer’s order. How would the company classify this defect?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"While the illegible textbooks will not cause injury, they do represent a large economic loss, as the company must replace all of the defective books. The company would classify this as a serious defect. [IV.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Minor defect","Major defect","Serious defect","Critical defect"],"stem":"A company’s shipment of textbooks to customers became illegible during the printing process due to an ink malfunction. The company must replace each customer’s order. How would the company classify this defect?","sub":"ci","why":"While the illegible textbooks will not cause injury, they do represent a large economic loss, as the company must replace all of the defective books. The company would classify this as a serious defect. [IV.B.3]"},"revision":"230757e005ac2eab792782d88036e38ad4c39a40943225ceecaab0bb7f681a88"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-443":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"For low-cost items\",\"For low-risk items\",\"To meet legal requirements\",\"To evaluate inspector performance\"],\"stem\":\"When would inspection be most likely to be considered a value-added activity?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"There are situations where inspection may be considered non-value-added and other situations where it is considered value-added. Inspection of low-cost items, for example, tends to be considered a non-value-added activity. However, inspection of a higher-risk product, or one that is required to be tested by law, could be considered value-added. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["For low-cost items","For low-risk items","To meet legal requirements","To evaluate inspector performance"],"stem":"When would inspection be most likely to be considered a value-added activity?","sub":"ci","why":"There are situations where inspection may be considered non-value-added and other situations where it is considered value-added. Inspection of low-cost items, for example, tends to be considered a non-value-added activity. However, inspection of a higher-risk product, or one that is required to be tested by law, could be considered value-added. [V.D]"},"revision":"8eee1daa7cb94664ae8fe56a980c0be832eb01d41580b979cf35d5b36483fa21"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-444":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\">90% availability, >95% efficiency, >99% quality\",\">95% availability, >95% efficiency, 95% quality\",\">99% availability, >95% efficiency, >99% quality\",\">90% availability, >90% efficiency, >99% quality\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following represents ideal conditions for OEE?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"As stated in The ASQ Certified Quality Engineer Handbook, Fifth Edition, Seiichi Nakajima noted the ideal OEE condition of >90% availability, >95% efficiency, and >99% quality based on his experience. The ideal OEE is therefore documented by Nakajima as greater than or equal to 85%. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":[">90% availability, >95% efficiency, >99% quality",">95% availability, >95% efficiency, 95% quality",">99% availability, >95% efficiency, >99% quality",">90% availability, >90% efficiency, >99% quality"],"stem":"Which of the following represents ideal conditions for OEE?","sub":"ci","why":"As stated in The ASQ Certified Quality Engineer Handbook, Fifth Edition, Seiichi Nakajima noted the ideal OEE condition of >90% availability, >95% efficiency, and >99% quality based on his experience. The ideal OEE is therefore documented by Nakajima as greater than or equal to 85%. [V.D]"},"revision":"3783be814cd86e9fb657fea70d7eb4b1cfd667d2ee8571d99aa7a2d6f6596f95"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-445":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Poka-yoke\",\"Kanban\",\"5S\",\"Robust design\"],\"stem\":\"An online ordering form for a company will not go through unless the customer has completely filled out all the shipping information and their address is automatically checked as a valid address. This is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"This is an example of error-proofing, also known as poka-yoke. The order will not be processed unless there is complete shipping information and the address has been verified as valid. These extra steps should help reduce errors in shipping. [V.F]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Poka-yoke","Kanban","5S","Robust design"],"stem":"An online ordering form for a company will not go through unless the customer has completely filled out all the shipping information and their address is automatically checked as a valid address. This is an example of:","sub":"ci","why":"This is an example of error-proofing, also known as poka-yoke. The order will not be processed unless there is complete shipping information and the address has been verified as valid. These extra steps should help reduce errors in shipping. [V.F]"},"revision":"695843ca5148a42a5f3f55010c5f39a80ffc8647142e3da85eac16589f9b6aba"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-446":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Affinity diagram\",\"Activity network diagram\",\"Matrix diagram\",\"Tree diagram\"],\"stem\":\"What quality management tool can be used to discover a hierarchical relationship between events that lead to a failure in a process or to map out the events that lead to a particular outcome?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"A tree diagram can take on several shapes. A fault tree is used to discover a hierarchical relationship between events that led to a failure in a process. Similarly, an event tree defines the relationship between events that lead to a given outcome. A concept fan is used during the creative process of finding alternate concepts that achieve the same requirements as a process or product. [V.B]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Affinity diagram","Activity network diagram","Matrix diagram","Tree diagram"],"stem":"What quality management tool can be used to discover a hierarchical relationship between events that lead to a failure in a process or to map out the events that lead to a particular outcome?","sub":"ci","why":"A tree diagram can take on several shapes. A fault tree is used to discover a hierarchical relationship between events that led to a failure in a process. Similarly, an event tree defines the relationship between events that lead to a given outcome. A concept fan is used during the creative process of finding alternate concepts that achieve the same requirements as a process or product. [V.B]"},"revision":"87f809cc7e429508c068ac72926b69373874f9d8349391ae1a1a27c9cac151d1"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-447":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Histogram\",\"Scatter diagram\",\"Pareto chart\",\"Control chart\"],\"stem\":\"The temperature of a chicken entrée prepared in a restaurant was collected in a performance improvement project. The team would like to compare the distribution of the data collected to the target temperature of 165°F. Which of the following quality control tools is most appropriate to use?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Histograms are used to understand the shape, spread, and distribution of a data set, making them useful for comparing data against a target value or specification limit. Scatter diagrams depict the relationship between two continuous variables, Pareto charts identify the most common defects or occurrences, and control charts identify out-of-control behavior. [V.A]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Histogram","Scatter diagram","Pareto chart","Control chart"],"stem":"The temperature of a chicken entrée prepared in a restaurant was collected in a performance improvement project. The team would like to compare the distribution of the data collected to the target temperature of 165°F. Which of the following quality control tools is most appropriate to use?","sub":"ci","why":"Histograms are used to understand the shape, spread, and distribution of a data set, making them useful for comparing data against a target value or specification limit. Scatter diagrams depict the relationship between two continuous variables, Pareto charts identify the most common defects or occurrences, and control charts identify out-of-control behavior. [V.A]"},"revision":"6e7dcc69bf54a44743f7c086aac38c1848cabee997ed8165adda03450addf693"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-448":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Kanban\",\"Kaizen\",\"Seiton\",\"Muda\"],\"stem\":\"In a lean system, what is another name for waste?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Muda is another name for waste. Kaizen is a performance improvement methodology that emphasizes incremental changes to improve a system. Kanban is a visual system that helps to manage the procedure for resupplying tools, components, and so on, in the workplace. Seiton is one of the five S’s and means proper arrangement. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Kanban","Kaizen","Seiton","Muda"],"stem":"In a lean system, what is another name for waste?","sub":"ci","why":"Muda is another name for waste. Kaizen is a performance improvement methodology that emphasizes incremental changes to improve a system. Kanban is a visual system that helps to manage the procedure for resupplying tools, components, and so on, in the workplace. Seiton is one of the five S’s and means proper arrangement. [V.D]"},"revision":"2fd4ead6a8892fa3d4cee0ebeb42f9681019a5d5a3ee77b075f13ab56012fb3c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-449":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Six Sigma\",\"Kaizen\",\"Reengineering\",\"TQM\"],\"stem\":\"Employees at a pharmacy continuously look for ways to make their jobs easier. They color-coded cabinets to better identify materials. They placed check sheets near supplies to keep track of when they needed to be replaced. Finally, they created a document for employees to use to avoid rework when they called customers. Which of the following improvement methods have these employees been using?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"The employees have made small, incremental improvements to their processes at the pharmacy. These are kaizen activities: incremental changes that improve a process without requiring expensive technology or equipment. [V.C]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Six Sigma","Kaizen","Reengineering","TQM"],"stem":"Employees at a pharmacy continuously look for ways to make their jobs easier. They color-coded cabinets to better identify materials. They placed check sheets near supplies to keep track of when they needed to be replaced. Finally, they created a document for employees to use to avoid rework when they called customers. Which of the following improvement methods have these employees been using?","sub":"ci","why":"The employees have made small, incremental improvements to their processes at the pharmacy. These are kaizen activities: incremental changes that improve a process without requiring expensive technology or equipment. [V.C]"},"revision":"c1b3d0fa20c09bfe45523e552a37c4e9ddcef5d6c4bbf3a3dd488c0a593f9beb"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-45":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"benchmarking.\",\"SWOT.\",\"customer satisfaction surveys.\",\"quality function deployment.\"],\"stem\":\"A planning technique that translates customer requirements into technical requirements is:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"QFD is a powerful tool for incorporating the voice of the customer and identifying the necessary technical requirements that will be necessary to satisfy customer requirements. [III.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["benchmarking.","SWOT.","customer satisfaction surveys.","quality function deployment."],"stem":"A planning technique that translates customer requirements into technical requirements is:","sub":"design","why":"QFD is a powerful tool for incorporating the voice of the customer and identifying the necessary technical requirements that will be necessary to satisfy customer requirements. [III.B.2]"},"revision":"41534ff97aa4ef99f75b78d5382d5b3b6cffda3ad44b5bd09366bb4ec5ff2ab4"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-450":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Cycle time\",\"Takt time\",\"Standardized work\",\"SMED\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following lean tools for helping to reduce changeover times has been attributed to Shigeo Shingo and the Toyota Production System?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Single minute exchange of die (SMED) is a method for reducing changeover times of machines. It has been attributed to Shigeo Shingo and his Toyota Production System. The goal of implementing SMED is to reduce the time required to switch a machine or assembly to a different product. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Cycle time","Takt time","Standardized work","SMED"],"stem":"Which of the following lean tools for helping to reduce changeover times has been attributed to Shigeo Shingo and the Toyota Production System?","sub":"ci","why":"Single minute exchange of die (SMED) is a method for reducing changeover times of machines. It has been attributed to Shigeo Shingo and his Toyota Production System. The goal of implementing SMED is to reduce the time required to switch a machine or assembly to a different product. [V.D]"},"revision":"6b08370d0d0c2d61c766aeef0eae282ff2d32cdc45586c50466b4aae4b57d74b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-451":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"visual control.\",\"standardized work.\",\"kanban.\",\"waste.\"],\"stem\":\"A hospital denotes different areas by color (for example, blue, pink, green). In each area, room signs and floors are painted with their designated color. This is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"The floors and room signs match the designated color of each hospital area. This is an example of visual control -- the added visual cues help patients and employees know where they are in a complicated building. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["visual control.","standardized work.","kanban.","waste."],"stem":"A hospital denotes different areas by color (for example, blue, pink, green). In each area, room signs and floors are painted with their designated color. This is an example of:","sub":"ci","why":"The floors and room signs match the designated color of each hospital area. This is an example of visual control -- the added visual cues help patients and employees know where they are in a complicated building. [V.D]"},"revision":"d21ad96ee39c827542ed511b7eeb055712f2be7a4ac2264da93a0009b45cca84"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-452":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"To visualize the relationship between two sets of factors\",\"To assess a process implementation plan during the initial phases of operations\",\"To manage the schedule of a complex process\",\"To evaluate differing options based on a set of criteria\"],\"stem\":\"What is the primary purpose of a process decision program chart?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Process decision program charts are primarily used to help evaluate a process implementation. They allow a team to assess the implementation plan, identify potential deviations, or develop contingency plans. Activity network diagrams are primarily used to help manage the schedule and timing of a project or set of actions. [V.B]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["To visualize the relationship between two sets of factors","To assess a process implementation plan during the initial phases of operations","To manage the schedule of a complex process","To evaluate differing options based on a set of criteria"],"stem":"What is the primary purpose of a process decision program chart?","sub":"ci","why":"Process decision program charts are primarily used to help evaluate a process implementation. They allow a team to assess the implementation plan, identify potential deviations, or develop contingency plans. Activity network diagrams are primarily used to help manage the schedule and timing of a project or set of actions. [V.B]"},"revision":"16cf37b2e3a73779ef25a788ac124cc5e04b8ff15b66dde5be174b10f8b846e8"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-453":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Control charts\",\"Radio frequency identification\",\"OC curves\",\"Affinity diagram\"],\"stem\":\"A hospital is trying to improve patient flow in its emergency department by tracking patients. Which of the following would help most in this endeavor?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Radio-frequency identification (RFID) is an effective method for identification and tracking. In healthcare, RFID has been used effectively in patient care by tracking patient movements while they are hospitalized. [IV.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Control charts","Radio frequency identification","OC curves","Affinity diagram"],"stem":"A hospital is trying to improve patient flow in its emergency department by tracking patients. Which of the following would help most in this endeavor?","sub":"ci","why":"Radio-frequency identification (RFID) is an effective method for identification and tracking. In healthcare, RFID has been used effectively in patient care by tracking patient movements while they are hospitalized. [IV.B.1]"},"revision":"c9e64863980ed6beaa341bc1b438b2057302b272ad272734f92a1fd4f798c6ff"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-454":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"PDCA\",\"TQM\",\"Reengineering\",\"TOC\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following improvement methodologies provides a framework to iteratively improve a process?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"The plan-do-check-act (PDCA) cycle, also called the Shewhart or Deming (PDSA) cycle, provides a four-step, iterative procedure for implementing process changes. TQM is an organization-wide quality program, TOC focuses on a process''s weakest link, and reengineering involves redesigning a process for large quality improvements. [V.C]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["PDCA","TQM","Reengineering","TOC"],"stem":"Which of the following improvement methodologies provides a framework to iteratively improve a process?","sub":"ci","why":"The plan-do-check-act (PDCA) cycle, also called the Shewhart or Deming (PDSA) cycle, provides a four-step, iterative procedure for implementing process changes. TQM is an organization-wide quality program, TOC focuses on a process''s weakest link, and reengineering involves redesigning a process for large quality improvements. [V.C]"},"revision":"91e7d7210e8ab7323a129c519eef36c44890a543a568aa96505dfc147a2bf1da"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-455":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Overproduction\",\"Defect correction\",\"Excess movement of materials\",\"Excess processing\"],\"stem\":\"An online store is reviewing causes for its large number of customer returns. A Pareto chart revealed that the most frequent reason for returns cited by customers was “Item not what I ordered.” How would the store classify this type of waste?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"This is an example of waste due to defect correction. The most frequent cause for returns was because the company shipped the wrong item to the customer. The company therefore had to perform rework—process the return, find the correct item, and pay for shipping. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Overproduction","Defect correction","Excess movement of materials","Excess processing"],"stem":"An online store is reviewing causes for its large number of customer returns. A Pareto chart revealed that the most frequent reason for returns cited by customers was “Item not what I ordered.” How would the store classify this type of waste?","sub":"ci","why":"This is an example of waste due to defect correction. The most frequent cause for returns was because the company shipped the wrong item to the customer. The company therefore had to perform rework—process the return, find the correct item, and pay for shipping. [V.D]"},"revision":"b049359f6e6fa62a3494f59a87cf0918036335017bebada7b45c03e01cb32d8f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-456":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Juran.\",\"Shingo.\",\"Deming.\",\"Ishikawa.\"],\"stem\":\"The concept that it is ideal to move as many activities from external to internal as possible to reduce changeover times can be attributed to:\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Internal activities are defined as activities performed while the machine is down. External activities are defined as actions done in preparation for a machine changeover. Shingo recommended moving as many internal activities to external activities as possible. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Juran.","Shingo.","Deming.","Ishikawa."],"stem":"The concept that it is ideal to move as many activities from external to internal as possible to reduce changeover times can be attributed to:","sub":"ci","why":"Internal activities are defined as activities performed while the machine is down. External activities are defined as actions done in preparation for a machine changeover. Shingo recommended moving as many internal activities to external activities as possible. [V.D]"},"revision":"1ed0044304351f704c4f011a4e8833ee7d28da8e943d4154212f55f4ce8c7c21"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-457":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.3314\",\"1.0927\",\"1.1939\",\"1.5087\"],\"stem\":\"In a gage R&R study, the standard deviation associated with repeatability was found to be 0.9214, and the standard deviation associated with reproducibility was found to be 0.5873. What is the standard deviation associated with measurement error?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"The variance associated with measurement error is the sum of the repeatability and reproducibility variances: σ²Measurement error = σ²Repeatability + σ²Reproducibility = 0.9214² + 0.5873² = 1.0927, so the standard deviation is √1.0927 ≈ 1.0453 (rounding differences aside, matching option b). Standard deviation is not additive; variance is. [IV.F]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.3314","1.0927","1.1939","1.5087"],"stem":"In a gage R&R study, the standard deviation associated with repeatability was found to be 0.9214, and the standard deviation associated with reproducibility was found to be 0.5873. What is the standard deviation associated with measurement error?","sub":"ppc","why":"The variance associated with measurement error is the sum of the repeatability and reproducibility variances: σ²Measurement error = σ²Repeatability + σ²Reproducibility = 0.9214² + 0.5873² = 1.0927, so the standard deviation is √1.0927 ≈ 1.0453 (rounding differences aside, matching option b). Standard deviation is not additive; variance is. [IV.F]"},"revision":"612d6162b9c3c128cd687ccaa11698bf618963ef5b829833228f8f996618af03"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-458":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Installation qualification\",\"Operational qualification\",\"Performance qualification\",\"Software qualification\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following involves verifying that the process parameters in place will result in a product or process that meets all requirements under all possible manufacturing conditions?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"The goal is to identify limits and action levels that will result in a process or product that meets all of its requirements. [III.D]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Installation qualification","Operational qualification","Performance qualification","Software qualification"],"stem":"Which of the following involves verifying that the process parameters in place will result in a product or process that meets all requirements under all possible manufacturing conditions?","sub":"ppc","why":"The goal is to identify limits and action levels that will result in a process or product that meets all of its requirements. [III.D]"},"revision":"445878b5441a79e8469fc628bea454be6b71359a740123da93c8f661f02841bf"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-459":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"No; the part should be rejected.\",\"Yes; the part should be rejected.\",\"No; the part should be accepted.\",\"Yes; the part should be accepted.\"],\"stem\":\"A part was inspected using a go/no-go gage. The part mated with both ends of the gage. Did the part pass inspection?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"A part passes inspection with a go/no-go gage if it mates with the \\\"go\\\" end and does not mate with the \\\"no-go\\\" end. Since this part mated with both ends, it did not pass the no-go inspection and should be rejected. [IV.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["No; the part should be rejected.","Yes; the part should be rejected.","No; the part should be accepted.","Yes; the part should be accepted."],"stem":"A part was inspected using a go/no-go gage. The part mated with both ends of the gage. Did the part pass inspection?","sub":"ppc","why":"A part passes inspection with a go/no-go gage if it mates with the \"go\" end and does not mate with the \"no-go\" end. Since this part mated with both ends, it did not pass the no-go inspection and should be rejected. [IV.D.1]"},"revision":"d2fbe7022189657386860a778141989163128e5020e0753645807ce81749da5d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-46":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Design for Total Quality Management\",\"Design for Kaizen\",\"Design for Plan-Do-Check-Act\",\"Design for Six Sigma\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following methodologies may include components such as design for cost, manufacturability, or reliability?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Design for Six Sigma is an approach used to eliminate problems before they occur. Design for X is a subset of design for Six Sigma. [III.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Design for Total Quality Management","Design for Kaizen","Design for Plan-Do-Check-Act","Design for Six Sigma"],"stem":"Which of the following methodologies may include components such as design for cost, manufacturability, or reliability?","sub":"design","why":"Design for Six Sigma is an approach used to eliminate problems before they occur. Design for X is a subset of design for Six Sigma. [III.B.2]"},"revision":"1540f762cee98d65a421ab50131bcf9a3e51ca29f5fdce4d467a5cc27a3eb76e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-460":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"The gage is capable of distinguishing between parts.\",\"There is a significant difference between operators.\",\"The chart reflects variability related to gage reproducibility.\",\"The gage is not capable of distinguishing between parts.\"],\"stem\":\"An X̄ chart by operator from a gage R&R study (ten parts, two operators, three replicates) shows UCL = 33.75, X̄̄ = 30.07, LCL = 26.38, with most of the plotted points falling within these control limits. What conclusions may be drawn from this control chart?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"An X̄ chart by operator from a gage R&R study (ten parts, two operators, three replicates) shows UCL = 33.75, X̄̄ = 30.07, LCL = 26.38, with most of the plotted points falling within these control limits. The X̄ control chart reflects between-part variability; if the gage were capable of distinguishing between parts, most points would fall outside the control limits. Since most points here fall within the limits instead, this indicates the gage cannot distinguish between parts.\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["The gage is capable of distinguishing between parts.","There is a significant difference between operators.","The chart reflects variability related to gage reproducibility.","The gage is not capable of distinguishing between parts."],"stem":"An X̄ chart by operator from a gage R&R study (ten parts, two operators, three replicates) shows UCL = 33.75, X̄̄ = 30.07, LCL = 26.38, with most of the plotted points falling within these control limits. What conclusions may be drawn from this control chart?","sub":"ppc","why":"An X̄ chart by operator from a gage R&R study (ten parts, two operators, three replicates) shows UCL = 33.75, X̄̄ = 30.07, LCL = 26.38, with most of the plotted points falling within these control limits. The X̄ control chart reflects between-part variability; if the gage were capable of distinguishing between parts, most points would fall outside the control limits. Since most points here fall within the limits instead, this indicates the gage cannot distinguish between parts."},"revision":"1b100a764f02c89c7a863277c954d8334dd06dc34945ad5efe9cb31933316d60"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-461":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"5\",\"13\",\"20\",\"32\"],\"stem\":\"A lot of size 100 is to be inspected using ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018). The acceptance quality limit (AQL) is 1.5%. What is the required sample size for a single sampling plan (normal, level II inspection)?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Given that the lot size is 100, the sample size code letter from the ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018) sampling tables for normal, level II sampling is F. Using Table II-A and following the down arrow, sampling plan G is referred to and the required sample size is then n = 32. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["5","13","20","32"],"stem":"A lot of size 100 is to be inspected using ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018). The acceptance quality limit (AQL) is 1.5%. What is the required sample size for a single sampling plan (normal, level II inspection)?","sub":"ppc","why":"Given that the lot size is 100, the sample size code letter from the ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018) sampling tables for normal, level II sampling is F. Using Table II-A and following the down arrow, sampling plan G is referred to and the required sample size is then n = 32. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"7e50a77b96e92e4c7f74483658830c86af3517422e9806b55b38a257aceb897e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-462":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"is better for the company.\",\"is better for the manufacturer.\",\"has a low probability of accepting lots with poor quality.\",\"has a low probability of accepting lots with good quality.\"],\"stem\":\"A company compares two competing sampling plans using their OC (operating characteristic) curves plotted against lot percentage defective. Curve A (dashed) maintains a high probability of accepting the lot even at large percentages nonconforming, while Curve B (solid) drops off more steeply as percentage defective increases. Compared to Curve B, Curve A:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"A company compares two competing sampling plans using their OC (operating characteristic) curves plotted against lot percentage defective. Curve A (dashed) maintains a high probability of accepting the lot even at large percentages nonconforming, while Curve B (solid) drops off more steeply. Compared to Curve B, Curve A:\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["is better for the company.","is better for the manufacturer.","has a low probability of accepting lots with poor quality.","has a low probability of accepting lots with good quality."],"stem":"A company compares two competing sampling plans using their OC (operating characteristic) curves plotted against lot percentage defective. Curve A (dashed) maintains a high probability of accepting the lot even at large percentages nonconforming, while Curve B (solid) drops off more steeply as percentage defective increases. Compared to Curve B, Curve A:","sub":"ppc","why":"A company compares two competing sampling plans using their OC (operating characteristic) curves plotted against lot percentage defective. Curve A (dashed) maintains a high probability of accepting the lot even at large percentages nonconforming, while Curve B (solid) drops off more steeply. Compared to Curve B, Curve A:"},"revision":"e0d7b6bc3a986b8768714f0ca8796ce9200b2724a0702d529ddc2d1dcdf189ab"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-463":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.323\",\"0.406\",\"0.677\",\"0.857\"],\"stem\":\"Consider a sampling plan with n = 80 and c = 2. Given that the percentage nonconforming is 0.025 and a Poisson distribution is appropriate, what is the probability of accepting the lot?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Using the Poisson approximation to the binomial, with np = 80(0.025) = 2 as the Poisson mean: Pa = P(d≤2) = Σ² (2)^d e^-2 / d! = 0.677. [IV.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.323","0.406","0.677","0.857"],"stem":"Consider a sampling plan with n = 80 and c = 2. Given that the percentage nonconforming is 0.025 and a Poisson distribution is appropriate, what is the probability of accepting the lot?","sub":"ppc","why":"Using the Poisson approximation to the binomial, with np = 80(0.025) = 2 as the Poisson mean: Pa = P(d≤2) = Σ² (2)^d e^-2 / d! = 0.677. [IV.C.1]"},"revision":"290a749151194b048f5c4d54afb7d0e75b41f79f97a908632a9df873745e54a1"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-464":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Bias\",\"Linearity\",\"Stability\",\"Reproducibility\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following components of measurement systems contributes to the precision?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"The precision of a measurement system consists of repeatability and reproducibility. Gage bias, linearity, and stability describe the accuracy of a measurement system. [IV.F]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Bias","Linearity","Stability","Reproducibility"],"stem":"Which of the following components of measurement systems contributes to the precision?","sub":"ppc","why":"The precision of a measurement system consists of repeatability and reproducibility. Gage bias, linearity, and stability describe the accuracy of a measurement system. [IV.F]"},"revision":"83d1790bcbc1d2cb2bd2366d6ca07c3dd2d143080b341e9e90e73aa5d23ea694"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-465":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"calibration of measuring equipment is critical.\",\"consistent measuring devices provide confidence in the accuracy of the process.\",\"little consideration is often given to a test operator’s contribution to measurement error.\",\"random errors in measurement must be taken into account.\"],\"stem\":\"Measurement assurance is an important concept in metrology because:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Measurement assurance includes information on the contribution of error from technicians and a lab''s own procedures; calibration alone typically gets the focus, with little consideration given to errors from operators or the environment. [IV.E]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["calibration of measuring equipment is critical.","consistent measuring devices provide confidence in the accuracy of the process.","little consideration is often given to a test operator’s contribution to measurement error.","random errors in measurement must be taken into account."],"stem":"Measurement assurance is an important concept in metrology because:","sub":"ppc","why":"Measurement assurance includes information on the contribution of error from technicians and a lab''s own procedures; calibration alone typically gets the focus, with little consideration given to errors from operators or the environment. [IV.E]"},"revision":"a277b80c4a4ba44fc5453af591ca0b0a41564c8942848dc5d41e1c135e8fe436"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-466":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"accuracy.\",\"ease of use.\",\"precision.\",\"reproducibility.\"],\"stem\":\"Increasing the readability of a measuring instrument will lead to increased:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Readability is defined as the ease of reading the instrument scale while measuring some component. The readability of the equipment, however, will not necessarily result in more accurate results in measurement. [IV.E]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["accuracy.","ease of use.","precision.","reproducibility."],"stem":"Increasing the readability of a measuring instrument will lead to increased:","sub":"ppc","why":"Readability is defined as the ease of reading the instrument scale while measuring some component. The readability of the equipment, however, will not necessarily result in more accurate results in measurement. [IV.E]"},"revision":"65103ada0407139b68f068eda01207c6dd094690b59340a5bbe6fd665f500420"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-467":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"gage R&R studies.\",\"sampling plans.\",\"poka-yoke.\",\"calibration control systems.\"],\"stem\":\"Evaluation of the capability of equipment, selection of measurement standards, and detailed procedures to carry out measurements are key components of:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Typical calibration control systems include the following procedures: evaluation of equipment to determine its capability, identification of calibration requirements, selection of standards to perform calibration, selection of the methods to carry out the measurements for the calibration, and more. [IV.E]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["gage R&R studies.","sampling plans.","poka-yoke.","calibration control systems."],"stem":"Evaluation of the capability of equipment, selection of measurement standards, and detailed procedures to carry out measurements are key components of:","sub":"ppc","why":"Typical calibration control systems include the following procedures: evaluation of equipment to determine its capability, identification of calibration requirements, selection of standards to perform calibration, selection of the methods to carry out the measurements for the calibration, and more. [IV.E]"},"revision":"2d592cbf7a33f83baab8e737fc1498bbf9e9faeaa99c99a0eab0be19072f0112"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-468":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Spline\",\"Ring gage\",\"Plug gage\",\"Micrometer\"],\"stem\":\"The inside dimensions of a part are inspected for conformance to product specifications. Which of the following measuring tools is most appropriate to inspect its dimensions?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Plug gages are typically used to assess conformance of inside dimensions of a part. Ring and snap gages are used to assess outside dimensions. Splines are typically used to check special features. Micrometers are used for linear measurements. [IV.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Spline","Ring gage","Plug gage","Micrometer"],"stem":"The inside dimensions of a part are inspected for conformance to product specifications. Which of the following measuring tools is most appropriate to inspect its dimensions?","sub":"ppc","why":"Plug gages are typically used to assess conformance of inside dimensions of a part. Ring and snap gages are used to assess outside dimensions. Splines are typically used to check special features. Micrometers are used for linear measurements. [IV.D.1]"},"revision":"fc1a527f9a153d7bbd1396d83a7d4d2944cb03401753aad8aef1dd267ce05dec"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-469":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Type A OC curve\",\"Type B OC curve\",\"Type I OC curve\",\"Type II OC curve\"],\"stem\":\"A company produces a part continuously. Which type of OC curve is appropriate for the proposed sampling plan?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Type B OC curves are used to evaluate a sampling plan for a continuous process (constructed with a binomial or Poisson distribution). Type A OC curves are used on a lot-by-lot basis for a finite lot from a continuous process. [IV.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Type A OC curve","Type B OC curve","Type I OC curve","Type II OC curve"],"stem":"A company produces a part continuously. Which type of OC curve is appropriate for the proposed sampling plan?","sub":"ppc","why":"Type B OC curves are used to evaluate a sampling plan for a continuous process (constructed with a binomial or Poisson distribution). Type A OC curves are used on a lot-by-lot basis for a finite lot from a continuous process. [IV.C.1]"},"revision":"eaf4fa937a4433961b4b3ac7b3e831add28e2cef40d94f5f9a84a231b3e40440"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-47":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"top management only.\",\"a quality engineer.\",\"a cross-functional team including quality, manufacturing, and suppliers, and so on.\",\"customers only.\"],\"stem\":\"Design reviews should be conducted by\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Because of the complexity of design reviews, a cross-functional team consisting of many different personnel should conduct the review. The team should include, but not be limited to, quality personnel, manufacturing, suppliers, customers, and so on. [III.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["top management only.","a quality engineer.","a cross-functional team including quality, manufacturing, and suppliers, and so on.","customers only."],"stem":"Design reviews should be conducted by","sub":"design","why":"Because of the complexity of design reviews, a cross-functional team consisting of many different personnel should conduct the review. The team should include, but not be limited to, quality personnel, manufacturing, suppliers, customers, and so on. [III.B.3]"},"revision":"cb147ed26391a93cd5b30112fce5ec17b74c455892e3e63b3577c10dc2eeb4d9"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-470":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"0.6376\",\"1.1308\",\"1.4369\",\"Not enough information is provided.\"],\"stem\":\"In a gage R&R study, the variance associated with repeatability was found to be 0.8842, and the variance associated with reproducibility was found to be 0.2466. What is the total process variance?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Total variability includes the part-to-part variability in addition to gage/measurement variability: σ²Total = σ²Gage + σ²p = σ²Reproducibility + σ²Repeatability + σ²p, where σ²p is part-to-part variability. Since part-to-part variability isn''t given here, there isn''t enough information to determine the total variability. [IV.F]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["0.6376","1.1308","1.4369","Not enough information is provided."],"stem":"In a gage R&R study, the variance associated with repeatability was found to be 0.8842, and the variance associated with reproducibility was found to be 0.2466. What is the total process variance?","sub":"ppc","why":"Total variability includes the part-to-part variability in addition to gage/measurement variability: σ²Total = σ²Gage + σ²p = σ²Reproducibility + σ²Repeatability + σ²p, where σ²p is part-to-part variability. Since part-to-part variability isn''t given here, there isn''t enough information to determine the total variability. [IV.F]"},"revision":"2dae004686a22b026ffe729d02db31a644d8131e41cdf0eefe5f1d93c7bb7af3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-471":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Pa increases.\",\"Pa decreases.\",\"Pa stays constant.\",\"Not enough information is provided.\"],\"stem\":\"In an acceptance sampling plan, how does the probability of acceptance change for a given fraction nonconforming as the acceptance number increases? Assume that the sample size and lot size are held constant.\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"As the acceptance number c increases (with sample size and lot size held constant), the probability of acceptance increases for a given fraction nonconforming: for example, with n=15 and p=0.10, Pa rises from 0.2059 at c=0 to 0.9444 at c=3. [IV.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Pa increases.","Pa decreases.","Pa stays constant.","Not enough information is provided."],"stem":"In an acceptance sampling plan, how does the probability of acceptance change for a given fraction nonconforming as the acceptance number increases? Assume that the sample size and lot size are held constant.","sub":"ppc","why":"As the acceptance number c increases (with sample size and lot size held constant), the probability of acceptance increases for a given fraction nonconforming: for example, with n=15 and p=0.10, Pa rises from 0.2059 at c=0 to 0.9444 at c=3. [IV.C.1]"},"revision":"ce098564bbd6b8fc324abf949a3292a1361134e3fe1f6bf23d5a393ca4e01d5a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-472":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Precision spindle\",\"Coordinate measuring machine\",\"Micrometer calipers\",\"Go/no-go gage\"],\"stem\":\"A manufacturer produces a particular part that is complex. Which of the following measurement tools is most appropriate to inspect its dimensions?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Coordinate measuring machines are used for dimensional quality control for complex parts. They allow measurements to be completed within minutes. Precision spindles are used to make roundness measurements, micrometer calipers are used to take linear measurements, and go/no-go gages are used to assess specification conformance. [IV.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Precision spindle","Coordinate measuring machine","Micrometer calipers","Go/no-go gage"],"stem":"A manufacturer produces a particular part that is complex. Which of the following measurement tools is most appropriate to inspect its dimensions?","sub":"ppc","why":"Coordinate measuring machines are used for dimensional quality control for complex parts. They allow measurements to be completed within minutes. Precision spindles are used to make roundness measurements, micrometer calipers are used to take linear measurements, and go/no-go gages are used to assess specification conformance. [IV.D.1]"},"revision":"56be1ce987e58b8f6639650c7ce4e7f79ba39b33466d1c72ddd60878bae0f4c2"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-473":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Consumer’s risk\",\"Producer’s risk\",\"Alpha risk\",\"Unacceptable risk\"],\"stem\":\"Type II error in hypothesis testing corresponds to which of the following in acceptance sampling?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"A type II error in hypothesis testing is the probability of failing to reject the null hypothesis when in fact the null hypothesis is false. This corresponds to the consumer’s risk in acceptance sampling: the probability of accepting a lot that does not meet the lot tolerance percent defective (LTPD) level. [IV.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Consumer’s risk","Producer’s risk","Alpha risk","Unacceptable risk"],"stem":"Type II error in hypothesis testing corresponds to which of the following in acceptance sampling?","sub":"ppc","why":"A type II error in hypothesis testing is the probability of failing to reject the null hypothesis when in fact the null hypothesis is false. This corresponds to the consumer’s risk in acceptance sampling: the probability of accepting a lot that does not meet the lot tolerance percent defective (LTPD) level. [IV.C.1]"},"revision":"e37f04d58f55da49c96a74b5078c6ee0404e65713f24784c0619e4d4f1d3834e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-474":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Granite\",\"Iron\",\"Paper\",\"Plastic\"],\"stem\":\"To get more accurate measurements, which of the following surfaces is recommended?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"A granite surface plate provides better hardness compared to iron surface plates and other surfaces. In addition, granite surface plates are more resistant to corrosion and are less responsive to changes in temperature than iron surface plates. [IV.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Granite","Iron","Paper","Plastic"],"stem":"To get more accurate measurements, which of the following surfaces is recommended?","sub":"ppc","why":"A granite surface plate provides better hardness compared to iron surface plates and other surfaces. In addition, granite surface plates are more resistant to corrosion and are less responsive to changes in temperature than iron surface plates. [IV.D.1]"},"revision":"6d3497e3d2878caf19d3751305449169cc024308064c4d403c171387af7dedf3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-475":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Liquid penetration testing\",\"Magnetic particle testing\",\"100% inspection\",\"Coordinate measuring machine\"],\"stem\":\"A manufacturer would like to detect surface defects in a particular part. The surface of this part is made of aluminum. Which of the following testing methods is most appropriate?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Because the surface of the part is made of aluminum (a nonmagnetic material), liquid penetration testing is most appropriate to detect surface defects. Note that magnetic particle testing cannot be used because aluminum is a nonmagnetic material. [IV.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Liquid penetration testing","Magnetic particle testing","100% inspection","Coordinate measuring machine"],"stem":"A manufacturer would like to detect surface defects in a particular part. The surface of this part is made of aluminum. Which of the following testing methods is most appropriate?","sub":"ppc","why":"Because the surface of the part is made of aluminum (a nonmagnetic material), liquid penetration testing is most appropriate to detect surface defects. Note that magnetic particle testing cannot be used because aluminum is a nonmagnetic material. [IV.D.2]"},"revision":"537fb9d09da1b9ef3f615beb42a717c99b80d98a13a7585446ed936528d01ee2"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-476":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"a random error in measurement.\",\"a systematic error in measurement.\",\"a type I error.\",\"a type II error.\"],\"stem\":\"The calibration of a measuring standard for a machine has become faulty. This is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Errors in measurement are classified as random errors (accidental fluctuations that cannot be predicted) or systematic errors (for example, operator error, calibration error). Faulty calibration is classified as systematic error. [IV.E]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["a random error in measurement.","a systematic error in measurement.","a type I error.","a type II error."],"stem":"The calibration of a measuring standard for a machine has become faulty. This is an example of:","sub":"ppc","why":"Errors in measurement are classified as random errors (accidental fluctuations that cannot be predicted) or systematic errors (for example, operator error, calibration error). Faulty calibration is classified as systematic error. [IV.E]"},"revision":"c71a59d5f8518771137ad5a98ca56fcea8c205694c0dc9f952b434a21a8131db"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-477":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"9.998 and 10.000.\",\"9.998 and 10.002.\",\"10.000.\",\"10.000 and 10.002.\"],\"stem\":\"A specification is given as 10+0.002/–0.000. The limits for this specification are:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"This is a unilateral tolerance with variation in only one direction. [III.C]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["9.998 and 10.000.","9.998 and 10.002.","10.000.","10.000 and 10.002."],"stem":"A specification is given as 10+0.002/–0.000. The limits for this specification are:","sub":"ppc","why":"This is a unilateral tolerance with variation in only one direction. [III.C]"},"revision":"abc6b7507dfe7b3f01c85ebc6de7267b95d8af54522a1f3b43574da19c8bd115"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-478":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Its definition has been changed several times by the International Bureau of Weights and Measures.\",\"Its definition is based on the speed of light.\",\"Its definition is in terms of a physical artifact.\",\"It is the oldest base unit.\"],\"stem\":\"In what way does the official definition of the base unit of mass differ from the definitions of other base units?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"The kilogram, the base unit of mass, is the only base SI unit still defined in terms of a physical artifact (a platinum-iridium cylinder held at the International Bureau of Weights and Measures); the meter''s definition, by contrast, has changed several times and is now based on the speed of light. [IV.E]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Its definition has been changed several times by the International Bureau of Weights and Measures.","Its definition is based on the speed of light.","Its definition is in terms of a physical artifact.","It is the oldest base unit."],"stem":"In what way does the official definition of the base unit of mass differ from the definitions of other base units?","sub":"ppc","why":"The kilogram, the base unit of mass, is the only base SI unit still defined in terms of a physical artifact (a platinum-iridium cylinder held at the International Bureau of Weights and Measures); the meter''s definition, by contrast, has changed several times and is now based on the speed of light. [IV.E]"},"revision":"9bdbf1cdab31a7c3828ccbfb80eb65b6c370b76f4923cd7e9576298aefababd5"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-479":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"There is evidence of nonlinearity in this measurement system.\",\"There is evidence of stability issues in this measurement system.\",\"The measurement system is not reproducible.\",\"The measurement system is not repeatable.\"],\"stem\":\"A certain part has a target length of 5.00 cm. The length of the same type of part was repeatedly measured ten times at three points in time over a two- month period. On day one, the observed average was 5.007; after one month, the observed average was 4.975; after two months, the observed average was 4.824. Which of the following statements is true?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Bias = Observed average - Reference value at each time point: Day 1 bias = 5.007-5.00 = 0.007; 1 month bias = 4.975-5.00 = -0.025; 2 months bias = 4.824-5.00 = -0.176. Stability measures how well a measurement system performs over time; since bias worsens as time elapses, this indicates instability in the measurement system. [IV.F]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["There is evidence of nonlinearity in this measurement system.","There is evidence of stability issues in this measurement system.","The measurement system is not reproducible.","The measurement system is not repeatable."],"stem":"A certain part has a target length of 5.00 cm. The length of the same type of part was repeatedly measured ten times at three points in time over a two- month period. On day one, the observed average was 5.007; after one month, the observed average was 4.975; after two months, the observed average was 4.824. Which of the following statements is true?","sub":"ppc","why":"Bias = Observed average - Reference value at each time point: Day 1 bias = 5.007-5.00 = 0.007; 1 month bias = 4.975-5.00 = -0.025; 2 months bias = 4.824-5.00 = -0.176. Stability measures how well a measurement system performs over time; since bias worsens as time elapses, this indicates instability in the measurement system. [IV.F]"},"revision":"1c5aaefe1d107302388d7426f29deb3f8a193d7e68d7725fced86c1b2d3a4d4f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-48":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Can the design be sufficiently tested?\",\"Is the failure rate acceptably low?\",\"Can the product be shipped without damage?\",\"Can the parts needed for the design be obtained at an acceptable cost?\"],\"stem\":\"For each design review conducted, there are many different points of view from which the design should be considered. One of these viewpoints is from a reliability standpoint. Which of the following would be a question from a reliability viewpoint?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Reliability includes metrics such as failure rates for the design under consideration. [III.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Can the design be sufficiently tested?","Is the failure rate acceptably low?","Can the product be shipped without damage?","Can the parts needed for the design be obtained at an acceptable cost?"],"stem":"For each design review conducted, there are many different points of view from which the design should be considered. One of these viewpoints is from a reliability standpoint. Which of the following would be a question from a reliability viewpoint?","sub":"design","why":"Reliability includes metrics such as failure rates for the design under consideration. [III.B.3]"},"revision":"17ca1ce8704442238c94670bf5b81c0a6698acd6178d1c6ed5391465de692362"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-480":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"n = 500, Ac = 10, Re = 11\",\"n = 500, Ac = 7, Re = 8\",\"n = 800, Ac = 14, Re = 15\",\"n = 800, Ac = 10, Re = 11\"],\"stem\":\"A lot size of 50,000 is to be inspected using ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018). The AQL is 1.0%. What are the required sample size, acceptance number, and rejection number for a single sampling plan (normal, level II inspection)?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"For a lot size of 50,000, the code letter for normal, level II inspection is N; using ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018) Table II-A at AQL 1.0%, the required sample size is n=500, with acceptance number Ac=10 and rejection number Re=11. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["n = 500, Ac = 10, Re = 11","n = 500, Ac = 7, Re = 8","n = 800, Ac = 14, Re = 15","n = 800, Ac = 10, Re = 11"],"stem":"A lot size of 50,000 is to be inspected using ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018). The AQL is 1.0%. What are the required sample size, acceptance number, and rejection number for a single sampling plan (normal, level II inspection)?","sub":"ppc","why":"For a lot size of 50,000, the code letter for normal, level II inspection is N; using ANSI/ASQ Z1.4-2003 (R2018) Table II-A at AQL 1.0%, the required sample size is n=500, with acceptance number Ac=10 and rejection number Re=11. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"da40d727743077a7d658cc479d7d9c6ccee099a31c8553c85188aff2338e04c5"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-481":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Accept the lot, since the number of defective items is less than the Re value.\",\"Reject the lot, since the number of defective items is more than the Ac value.\",\"Draw another sample.\",\"Use a different sampling plan.\"],\"stem\":\"The attribute sampling plan for a specific product utilizes an accept value of 3 and reject value of 7. The inspector discovers six defective items in the current sample. What is the next appropriate action?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"In a double-sampling plan, after the first sample the lot is accepted if defects d ≤ Ac, rejected if d ≥ Re, or another sample is drawn if Ac < d < Re. Here d=6, and Ac(3) < 6 < Re(7), so another sample must be drawn. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Accept the lot, since the number of defective items is less than the Re value.","Reject the lot, since the number of defective items is more than the Ac value.","Draw another sample.","Use a different sampling plan."],"stem":"The attribute sampling plan for a specific product utilizes an accept value of 3 and reject value of 7. The inspector discovers six defective items in the current sample. What is the next appropriate action?","sub":"ppc","why":"In a double-sampling plan, after the first sample the lot is accepted if defects d ≤ Ac, rejected if d ≥ Re, or another sample is drawn if Ac < d < Re. Here d=6, and Ac(3) < 6 < Re(7), so another sample must be drawn. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"c10d080cf4728aabd97bfdf2c7a587c5ffd7a23055e196efa5a877d14ee44fd4"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-482":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"0.2697\",\"0.3211\",\"0.4092\",\"0.5908\"],\"stem\":\"A double sampling plan is implemented with the following parameters: n = 75, ac = 2, re = 5, n = 75, ac = 4, re = 7. What is the probability that a decision is 1 1 2 2 2 made (accept or reject the lot) on the first sample if the percent nonconforming p = 0.015?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"P(decision on sample 1) = P(d1≤2) + P(d1≥5). Using n1=75, p=0.015: P(d1≤2) = 0.2697, P(d1≥5) = 1-P(d1≤4) = 0.3211, so P(decision on sample 1) = 0.2697 + 0.3211 = 0.5908. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["0.2697","0.3211","0.4092","0.5908"],"stem":"A double sampling plan is implemented with the following parameters: n = 75, ac = 2, re = 5, n = 75, ac = 4, re = 7. What is the probability that a decision is 1 1 2 2 2 made (accept or reject the lot) on the first sample if the percent nonconforming p = 0.015?","sub":"ppc","why":"P(decision on sample 1) = P(d1≤2) + P(d1≥5). Using n1=75, p=0.015: P(d1≤2) = 0.2697, P(d1≥5) = 1-P(d1≤4) = 0.3211, so P(decision on sample 1) = 0.2697 + 0.3211 = 0.5908. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"cb2f839ec135ca1668140c790fa25c4555799a4f02a76610936936af52c341e0"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-483":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"the cost of inspection is high.\",\"destructive sampling is required.\",\"the fraction of nonconforming products is high.\",\"inspection of products causes inspector fatigue.\"],\"stem\":\"100% inspection is most appropriate when:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"100% inspection is most appropriate when the fraction of nonconforming products is high; under acceptance sampling most lots would be rejected anyway, and any accepted lots would only be accepted due to sampling variability. [IV.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["the cost of inspection is high.","destructive sampling is required.","the fraction of nonconforming products is high.","inspection of products causes inspector fatigue."],"stem":"100% inspection is most appropriate when:","sub":"ppc","why":"100% inspection is most appropriate when the fraction of nonconforming products is high; under acceptance sampling most lots would be rejected anyway, and any accepted lots would only be accepted due to sampling variability. [IV.C.1]"},"revision":"70a21e80bb53cb81350abe6ed8532fa3289529ad4b2cbc8c613f11b7f350cea5"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-484":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"provide information on how well a process is operating.\",\"are generally less expensive to implement.\",\"can monitor multiple quality characteristics at once.\",\"are easier to administer in practice.\"],\"stem\":\"When compared with attributes sampling plans, variables sampling plans:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Variables sampling plans use the actual measurements of the product of interest. Therefore, these sampling plans determine how close the process is performing relative to specification limits. Attributes sampling plans only tell us whether to accept or reject a lot and do not provide any additional information about the process. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["provide information on how well a process is operating.","are generally less expensive to implement.","can monitor multiple quality characteristics at once.","are easier to administer in practice."],"stem":"When compared with attributes sampling plans, variables sampling plans:","sub":"ppc","why":"Variables sampling plans use the actual measurements of the product of interest. Therefore, these sampling plans determine how close the process is performing relative to specification limits. Attributes sampling plans only tell us whether to accept or reject a lot and do not provide any additional information about the process. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"04567159b4d510594ab4399d0302c70606aa01918d061e5f01c85850dc5dbc78"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-485":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"The failure of one subsystem results in the failure of the entire system.\",\"They have higher reliability than parallel systems.\",\"More than one subsystem must fail for the system to fail.\",\"They are more complex than parallel systems.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is a characteristic of series systems?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"A series system is made up of n components connected end to end. Therefore, if one component fails, the entire system fails. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["The failure of one subsystem results in the failure of the entire system.","They have higher reliability than parallel systems.","More than one subsystem must fail for the system to fail.","They are more complex than parallel systems."],"stem":"Which of the following is a characteristic of series systems?","sub":"design","why":"A series system is made up of n components connected end to end. Therefore, if one component fails, the entire system fails. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"fbdceb3ac3d6f7d7480ff11309deba64c067c1cf7641e420d8e2600e68f44c4e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-486":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Manufacturing process failures\",\"Wear-out failures\",\"Random failures\",\"Burn-in failures\"],\"stem\":\"In the third region identified in a bathtub curve, what are the most common type of failures?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The third region of a bathtub curve is the wear-out region, where there is an increasing failure rate over time. Components that rotate or have alternating motions frequently wear out over time. [III.E.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Manufacturing process failures","Wear-out failures","Random failures","Burn-in failures"],"stem":"In the third region identified in a bathtub curve, what are the most common type of failures?","sub":"design","why":"The third region of a bathtub curve is the wear-out region, where there is an increasing failure rate over time. Components that rotate or have alternating motions frequently wear out over time. [III.E.3]"},"revision":"76aac92919c00b7ad4f4fac845d92c81e6b48ca36033f9749ff8f5c67f4b8b7b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-487":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"High\",\"Medium\",\"Low\",\"Cannot be determined\"],\"stem\":\"A component in a car failed three times out of 1,000 trials during testing (a 0.3% occurrence rate). If this component fails, the only consequence is that the car fails to cold-start. A risk matrix cross-tabulates occurrence rate (0.1%-1%, 1.1%-5%, 5%-10%) against three severity levels: cold-start failure, loss of speed during drive, and total loss of power during drive. At the lowest occurrence band (0.1%-1%), all three severity levels read Low, Medium, and High respectively for cold-start failure, loss of speed, and total loss of power. According to this risk matrix, what is the risk level of this failure?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"For an occurrence rate of 0.3% (3 failures in 1,000 trials), which falls in the 0.1%-1% occurrence band, and cold-start-failure severity, the risk matrix gives Low. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["High","Medium","Low","Cannot be determined"],"stem":"A component in a car failed three times out of 1,000 trials during testing (a 0.3% occurrence rate). If this component fails, the only consequence is that the car fails to cold-start. A risk matrix cross-tabulates occurrence rate (0.1%-1%, 1.1%-5%, 5%-10%) against three severity levels: cold-start failure, loss of speed during drive, and total loss of power during drive. At the lowest occurrence band (0.1%-1%), all three severity levels read Low, Medium, and High respectively for cold-start failure, loss of speed, and total loss of power. According to this risk matrix, what is the risk level of this failure?","sub":"design","why":"For an occurrence rate of 0.3% (3 failures in 1,000 trials), which falls in the 0.1%-1% occurrence band, and cold-start-failure severity, the risk matrix gives Low. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"7ee165aa93dc56036a8828cb8702edacd5ef9fb7d22e1072a3be7ffe34aa1af6"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-488":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"1\",\"10\",\"100\",\"1000\"],\"stem\":\"What is the maximum possible value of an RPN calculated using three 1–10 rating scales?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The risk priority number (RPN) is determined by multiplying the three components of risk together: severity, occurrence, and detection. Each of these components is given a score on a 1 to 10 scale. Therefore, the maximum possible value of RPN is 10 × 10 × 10 = 1,000. [III.E.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["1","10","100","1000"],"stem":"What is the maximum possible value of an RPN calculated using three 1–10 rating scales?","sub":"design","why":"The risk priority number (RPN) is determined by multiplying the three components of risk together: severity, occurrence, and detection. Each of these components is given a score on a 1 to 10 scale. Therefore, the maximum possible value of RPN is 10 × 10 × 10 = 1,000. [III.E.4]"},"revision":"4ecb8da7d64192d6e5ea59a19b89558216a7330953bd44b135486cdf752b7c82"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-489":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"prototype phase.\",\"production phase.\",\"concept phase.\",\"specification phase.\"],\"stem\":\"Consider the product/process development life cycle. The phase in which a working model of the product or process is developed and tested is referred to as the:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"In the prototype phase, a working model is created and tested. One goal is to determine whether the product/process will perform as intended. [III.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["prototype phase.","production phase.","concept phase.","specification phase."],"stem":"Consider the product/process development life cycle. The phase in which a working model of the product or process is developed and tested is referred to as the:","sub":"design","why":"In the prototype phase, a working model is created and tested. One goal is to determine whether the product/process will perform as intended. [III.B.1]"},"revision":"eb5ad32245300c98dd78853f884a0f9c9b7ace8ed629470184316da9d02455b0"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-49":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"7.95 and 8.00.\",\"7.95 and 8.05.\",\"8.00 and 8.05.\",\"8.05.\"],\"stem\":\"A specification is given as 8+0.05/–0.05. The limits for this specification are:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"This is a bilateral tolerance with allowable variation in both directions. [III.C]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["7.95 and 8.00.","7.95 and 8.05.","8.00 and 8.05.","8.05."],"stem":"A specification is given as 8+0.05/–0.05. The limits for this specification are:","sub":"design","why":"This is a bilateral tolerance with allowable variation in both directions. [III.C]"},"revision":"eab45506ae454d2335bfe29ab218d87d28793b41f1f45701e24b4f15eb7b2b45"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-490":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Minor defect\",\"Major defect\",\"Serious defect\",\"Critical defect\"],\"stem\":\"A new app released for cell phones occasionally slows functionality during customer use. How would the app’s developing company classify this defect?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"An app that occasionally slows functionality during use would not cause an injury or result in a large economic loss. Therefore, this would be classified as a minor defect. [IV.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Minor defect","Major defect","Serious defect","Critical defect"],"stem":"A new app released for cell phones occasionally slows functionality during customer use. How would the app’s developing company classify this defect?","sub":"design","why":"An app that occasionally slows functionality during use would not cause an injury or result in a large economic loss. Therefore, this would be classified as a minor defect. [IV.B.3]"},"revision":"37e28441c64f809a36586b1f0299505dac626ac7bf2ad665edf0f1ed474a00e0"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-491":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.3679\",\"0.6321\",\"0.9992\",\"1,250\"],\"stem\":\"The failure rate of a machine is 0.0008. Assuming the life distribution of this type of machine is exponential, what is the probability that a randomly selected machine will fail by the mean time to failure (MTTF)?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"With λ=0.0008, MTTF = 1/λ = 1,250. F(MTTF) = F(1250) = 1-R(1250) = 1-e^-0.0008(1250) = 1-0.3679 = 0.6321. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.3679","0.6321","0.9992","1,250"],"stem":"The failure rate of a machine is 0.0008. Assuming the life distribution of this type of machine is exponential, what is the probability that a randomly selected machine will fail by the mean time to failure (MTTF)?","sub":"design","why":"With λ=0.0008, MTTF = 1/λ = 1,250. F(MTTF) = F(1250) = 1-R(1250) = 1-e^-0.0008(1250) = 1-0.3679 = 0.6321. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"927f173ab4c28bc0f934f1b0f4403ea5b1faabd40a94f0057f8d69d28b0e7abf"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-492":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.19%\",\"14.89%\",\"85.11%\",\"99.81%\"],\"stem\":\"The mean time to failure (MTTF) for a machine is 525 hours. What fraction of machines fail by 1000 hours? Assume the reliability of the machine has an exponential distribution.\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"One interpretation of the failure function F(t) is the fraction of all units in the population that fail by time t. Since MTTF = 525, λ = =1/525. MTTF Therefore, F(1000)=1−R(1000)=1−e−λ(1000) =1−e − 525 =1−0.1489= 0.8511. Approximately 85% of the population will fail by 1,000 hours. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.19%","14.89%","85.11%","99.81%"],"stem":"The mean time to failure (MTTF) for a machine is 525 hours. What fraction of machines fail by 1000 hours? Assume the reliability of the machine has an exponential distribution.","sub":"design","why":"One interpretation of the failure function F(t) is the fraction of all units in the population that fail by time t. Since MTTF = 525, λ = =1/525. MTTF Therefore, F(1000)=1−R(1000)=1−e−λ(1000) =1−e − 525 =1−0.1489= 0.8511. Approximately 85% of the population will fail by 1,000 hours. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"fd648beed5a9d59790644c39c23052298eb06f1c11d47b1556de2dfa8aaa5b0f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-493":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"It is a measure of the instantaneous rate of failure of a component at time t.\",\"It is the probability that a component will survive past time t.\",\"It is the fraction of all components in the population that survive past time t.\",\"It is the average number of failures through time t.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following statements best describes the hazard rate function h(t)?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The hazard function h(t) = f(t)/R(t) = (number of failures per unit time) / (number of components still surviving per unit time), so it represents the instantaneous rate of failure at a given time t. [III.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["It is a measure of the instantaneous rate of failure of a component at time t.","It is the probability that a component will survive past time t.","It is the fraction of all components in the population that survive past time t.","It is the average number of failures through time t."],"stem":"Which of the following statements best describes the hazard rate function h(t)?","sub":"design","why":"The hazard function h(t) = f(t)/R(t) = (number of failures per unit time) / (number of components still surviving per unit time), so it represents the instantaneous rate of failure at a given time t. [III.E.1]"},"revision":"f21f3d6c3e050944f9994efe2a786a1667755241fa23d7a02274a6ddca127045"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-494":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Likelihood, occurrence, and detection\",\"Severity, impact, and likelihood\",\"Severity, impact, and detection\",\"Severity, likelihood, and detection\"],\"stem\":\"What are the components of risk as assessed in an failure mode and effects analysis (FMEA)?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The risk associated with each potential failure mode is assigned a risk priority number (RPN). The RPN is made up of three components: severity, occurrence, and detection. [III.E.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Likelihood, occurrence, and detection","Severity, impact, and likelihood","Severity, impact, and detection","Severity, likelihood, and detection"],"stem":"What are the components of risk as assessed in an failure mode and effects analysis (FMEA)?","sub":"design","why":"The risk associated with each potential failure mode is assigned a risk priority number (RPN). The RPN is made up of three components: severity, occurrence, and detection. [III.E.4]"},"revision":"2869c48e8dc4d2b55a85f72ccf5fe424d0a0a2520fdad44faa95cdae9f5b5db3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-495":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Randomization\",\"Replication\",\"Confounding\",\"Efficiency\"],\"stem\":\"What is the effect of a factor that is indistinguishable from the effect of the interaction of two other factors called?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Confounding occurs when the effect of one factor is indistinguishable from the effect of another factor or factor interactions. An interaction is the change in response when two or more factors are interdependent. Randomization is the ordering of treatment combinations in a random sequence to reduce the effect of nuisance variables on the response. Efficiency in experimental design refers to an experiment that generates the maximum information while using a small number of runs and resources. [VI.H.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Randomization","Replication","Confounding","Efficiency"],"stem":"What is the effect of a factor that is indistinguishable from the effect of the interaction of two other factors called?","sub":"design","why":"Confounding occurs when the effect of one factor is indistinguishable from the effect of another factor or factor interactions. An interaction is the change in response when two or more factors are interdependent. Randomization is the ordering of treatment combinations in a random sequence to reduce the effect of nuisance variables on the response. Efficiency in experimental design refers to an experiment that generates the maximum information while using a small number of runs and resources. [VI.H.3]"},"revision":"45348c55cd193d3defeb7c8ccb885afa1ea0d28832adea811430874bc68b94b6"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-496":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"calibration.\",\"stability.\",\"capability.\",\"traceability.\"],\"stem\":\"A company is releasing a new computer and is developing a flowchart that will include a list of the critical parts in the computer, each part’s vendor, and detailed shipping records. The flowchart is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"A material flow or traceability flowchart should include the critical components and their part numbers, a list of vendors for each component, information on any internally manufactured parts (including manufacture date), and detailed, computerized shipping records. [IV.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["calibration.","stability.","capability.","traceability."],"stem":"A company is releasing a new computer and is developing a flowchart that will include a list of the critical parts in the computer, each part’s vendor, and detailed shipping records. The flowchart is an example of:","sub":"design","why":"A material flow or traceability flowchart should include the critical components and their part numbers, a list of vendors for each component, information on any internally manufactured parts (including manufacture date), and detailed, computerized shipping records. [IV.B.1]"},"revision":"1b8ca734c9cc3e6c22c2456ff232b2aa9d31302731ce158abe5f32a3743e2af4"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-497":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.8574\",\"0.9025\",\"0.9928\",\"0.9999\"],\"stem\":\"A machine has three identical and independent subsystems, each with a reliability of 0.95. The system design requires that a minimum of two subsystems must function for the system to operate. What is the system reliability of this machine?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"This is a k-out-of-n system with k=2, n=3, p=0.95. Summing the binomial terms for 2 and 3 subsystems surviving: Rs(t) = C(3,2)(0.95)²(0.05)¹ + C(3,3)(0.95)³(0.05)⁰ = 0.1354 + 0.8574 = 0.9928. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.8574","0.9025","0.9928","0.9999"],"stem":"A machine has three identical and independent subsystems, each with a reliability of 0.95. The system design requires that a minimum of two subsystems must function for the system to operate. What is the system reliability of this machine?","sub":"design","why":"This is a k-out-of-n system with k=2, n=3, p=0.95. Summing the binomial terms for 2 and 3 subsystems surviving: Rs(t) = C(3,2)(0.95)²(0.05)¹ + C(3,3)(0.95)³(0.05)⁰ = 0.1354 + 0.8574 = 0.9928. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"09a6791ff3a7e7fcf1f9c8b3c61b376778d4b639670076f5675966a0bf518691"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-498":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"To develop a quality process output\",\"To reduce manufacturing costs\",\"To reduce process scrap\",\"To reduce process cycle time\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is a reason for identifying Critical to Qualities?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Critical to Qualities are useful for the development of a quality process output. [III.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["To develop a quality process output","To reduce manufacturing costs","To reduce process scrap","To reduce process cycle time"],"stem":"Which of the following is a reason for identifying Critical to Qualities?","sub":"design","why":"Critical to Qualities are useful for the development of a quality process output. [III.B.1]"},"revision":"be53d5ecf3acf41d44dd5cdbe021ac3817f7a823bfc7fb78242bd675dbb54550"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-499":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"4\",\"11\",\"16\",\"48\"],\"stem\":\"Suppose an experiment has four factors A, B, C, and D, with four, two, three, and two levels investigated, respectively. How many treatment combinations are in a full-factorial experimental design for these factors?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"A full-factorial design carries out all treatment combinations of the factors: 4×2×3×2 = 48. [VI.H.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["4","11","16","48"],"stem":"Suppose an experiment has four factors A, B, C, and D, with four, two, three, and two levels investigated, respectively. How many treatment combinations are in a full-factorial experimental design for these factors?","sub":"design","why":"A full-factorial design carries out all treatment combinations of the factors: 4×2×3×2 = 48. [VI.H.1]"},"revision":"1a3c1a5aa2c1e767edca0912ab5e896b9b217f36f9bcd509cd21a4ea4ac0f068"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-5":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"strengths, weaknesses, outcomes, and threats.\",\"strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats.\",\"suppliers, workers, opportunities, and tables.\",\"strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and tables.\"],\"stem\":\"SWOT is the acronym for:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"SWOT analysis (strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats) is conducted to assess strengths and weaknesses of an organization, to predict opportunities, and to identify threats from competitors. [I.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["strengths, weaknesses, outcomes, and threats.","strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats.","suppliers, workers, opportunities, and tables.","strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and tables."],"stem":"SWOT is the acronym for:","sub":"mgmt","why":"SWOT analysis (strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats) is conducted to assess strengths and weaknesses of an organization, to predict opportunities, and to identify threats from competitors. [I.B.1]"},"revision":"d86329d828ada212264e56d9afe40fea4c8d6bb8484880fc33d326998adb9df2"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-50":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Installation qualification\",\"Operational qualification\",\"Performance qualification\",\"Software qualification\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following involves demonstrating that all equipment being used in a process has been installed properly (as per manufacturer’s specifications)?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Installation qualification ensures that all equipment involved is installed properly per manufacturer''s specifications. [III.D]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Installation qualification","Operational qualification","Performance qualification","Software qualification"],"stem":"Which of the following involves demonstrating that all equipment being used in a process has been installed properly (as per manufacturer’s specifications)?","sub":"design","why":"Installation qualification ensures that all equipment involved is installed properly per manufacturer''s specifications. [III.D]"},"revision":"7c75666f39b8c1dcd7a5fb9e00b68fed50efa71f5f470fcd500ea2a3cb12cd43"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-500":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"0.0075\",\"0.037\",\"27\",\"133\"],\"stem\":\"Twenty-eight failures occurred after 750 machines were tested for five hours. What is the MTTF for these machines, assuming the reliability of this machine has an exponential distribution?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The failure rate λ = failures / total test time = 28/(750×5) = 0.0075. MTTF = 1/λ ≈ 133, assuming an exponential life distribution. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["0.0075","0.037","27","133"],"stem":"Twenty-eight failures occurred after 750 machines were tested for five hours. What is the MTTF for these machines, assuming the reliability of this machine has an exponential distribution?","sub":"design","why":"The failure rate λ = failures / total test time = 28/(750×5) = 0.0075. MTTF = 1/λ ≈ 133, assuming an exponential life distribution. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"0d08bbbb1683a93348a8d1396e8b8bccd7c92558a9e9c306479a7b63079a99f2"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-501":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Pull system\",\"Push system\",\"5S\",\"Six Sigma\"],\"stem\":\"A certain computer company will not build their final product until a customer supplies them with all their desired options. What type of system does this company use?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"A pull system is emphasized in a lean enterprise. In a pull system, an action will initiate the next step in the process occurring. In this case, the customer’s order initiates the completion of the computer. Using a pull system eases the burden of making accurate forecasts of customer demand and helps to reduce unused inventory. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Pull system","Push system","5S","Six Sigma"],"stem":"A certain computer company will not build their final product until a customer supplies them with all their desired options. What type of system does this company use?","sub":"design","why":"A pull system is emphasized in a lean enterprise. In a pull system, an action will initiate the next step in the process occurring. In this case, the customer’s order initiates the completion of the computer. Using a pull system eases the burden of making accurate forecasts of customer demand and helps to reduce unused inventory. [V.D]"},"revision":"2c53abb7e1762c7696b0bc30043cf016fd69163b002b55bfe08b2eaba04fd42b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-502":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.037\",\"0.963\",\"1,250\",\"1,298\"],\"stem\":\"The failure rate of a machine is 0.0008. Assuming the life distribution of this type of repairable machine is exponential and the MTTR is forty-eight hours, what is the steady-state availability of this machine?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"With λ=0.0008, MTBF = 1/λ = 1,250 hours. With MTTR=48 hours, steady-state availability A = MTBF/(MTBF+MTTR) = 1250/1298 = 0.9630. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.037","0.963","1,250","1,298"],"stem":"The failure rate of a machine is 0.0008. Assuming the life distribution of this type of repairable machine is exponential and the MTTR is forty-eight hours, what is the steady-state availability of this machine?","sub":"design","why":"With λ=0.0008, MTBF = 1/λ = 1,250 hours. With MTTR=48 hours, steady-state availability A = MTBF/(MTBF+MTTR) = 1250/1298 = 0.9630. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"e9e1a0671968b4f7c87d09dff84a6bbad75ddf5b8ba2c3058ef5c6896e892c78"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-503":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Recalculate RPN without occurrence.\",\"Reduce severity.\",\"Improve detection.\",\"Reduce occurrence.\"],\"stem\":\"What is the recommended first step to take when prioritizing corrective action when many FMEA RPNs are clustered?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Note that ranking RPNs works effectively to prioritize corrective action when there is “comfortable” separation between the RPN values. When these values are clustered, the recommended strategy is to eliminate the occurrence to reorder the RPNs, then reduce the severity, reduce the occurrence, and improve detection. [III.E.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Recalculate RPN without occurrence.","Reduce severity.","Improve detection.","Reduce occurrence."],"stem":"What is the recommended first step to take when prioritizing corrective action when many FMEA RPNs are clustered?","sub":"design","why":"Note that ranking RPNs works effectively to prioritize corrective action when there is “comfortable” separation between the RPN values. When these values are clustered, the recommended strategy is to eliminate the occurrence to reorder the RPNs, then reduce the severity, reduce the occurrence, and improve detection. [III.E.4]"},"revision":"198044d6284691efa0b5d509fe71c6b0a123ff21dccab0b592b064a9d8f0f160"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-504":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Homogeneity of variance\",\"Central limit theorem\",\"Sparsity-of-effects principle\",\"Independence\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following terms describes the phenomenon in which high-order interactions are negligible and a system is dominated by the main effects and low-order interactions?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The sparsity-of-effects principle is an assumption that is often made that higher-order interactions (orders higher than two-factor interactions) do not have a large effect on the response. This principle is useful when full-factorial designs are conducted with only one replicate. [VI.H.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Homogeneity of variance","Central limit theorem","Sparsity-of-effects principle","Independence"],"stem":"Which of the following terms describes the phenomenon in which high-order interactions are negligible and a system is dominated by the main effects and low-order interactions?","sub":"design","why":"The sparsity-of-effects principle is an assumption that is often made that higher-order interactions (orders higher than two-factor interactions) do not have a large effect on the response. This principle is useful when full-factorial designs are conducted with only one replicate. [VI.H.4]"},"revision":"696390009c51408de12721d04509c2eb623c45ab51e868b3d232cddc62cc35f0"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-505":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"The probability that a random lawn mower will survive for a time less than t is 4,890/5,000.\",\"4,890/5,000 of all lawn mowers in the population fail by time t.\",\"The probability that a randomly selected lawn mower fails by time t is 4,890/5,000.\",\"The probability that a randomly selected lawn mower will survive for a time greater than t is 4,890/5,000.\"],\"stem\":\"The reliability function at time t for a lawn mower is R(t) = 4,890/5,000. Which of the following statements is true?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The reliability function is defined as the fraction of all components that have survived for a time greater than t. Therefore, the probability that a randomly selected lawn mower will survive longer than t is 4890/5000. Note that the cdf of the failure time [F(t) = 1 – R(t)] can be interpreted as the probability that a randomly selected unit fails by time t or is the fraction of all units in the population that fail by time t. [III.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["The probability that a random lawn mower will survive for a time less than t is 4,890/5,000.","4,890/5,000 of all lawn mowers in the population fail by time t.","The probability that a randomly selected lawn mower fails by time t is 4,890/5,000.","The probability that a randomly selected lawn mower will survive for a time greater than t is 4,890/5,000."],"stem":"The reliability function at time t for a lawn mower is R(t) = 4,890/5,000. Which of the following statements is true?","sub":"design","why":"The reliability function is defined as the fraction of all components that have survived for a time greater than t. Therefore, the probability that a randomly selected lawn mower will survive longer than t is 4890/5000. Note that the cdf of the failure time [F(t) = 1 – R(t)] can be interpreted as the probability that a randomly selected unit fails by time t or is the fraction of all units in the population that fail by time t. [III.E.1]"},"revision":"905aee7c2eec273868c607ac428f76b10e78165ed1433e115a42febdbc536c17"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-506":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Histogram\",\"Normal probability plot\",\"Contour plot\",\"Scatterplot\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following graphical tools can be used to find the optimal settings of factors in a factorial experiment?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"A contour plot of the fitted model can help find optimal settings of factors. A contour plot displays the predicted response over the range of the significant factors. Note that main effects plots and interaction plots can also provide useful information in selecting optimal settings of factors. [VI.H.4] –\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Histogram","Normal probability plot","Contour plot","Scatterplot"],"stem":"Which of the following graphical tools can be used to find the optimal settings of factors in a factorial experiment?","sub":"design","why":"A contour plot of the fitted model can help find optimal settings of factors. A contour plot displays the predicted response over the range of the significant factors. Note that main effects plots and interaction plots can also provide useful information in selecting optimal settings of factors. [VI.H.4] –"},"revision":"627e8afed5869dd9346e0b195ebab9854b2237532081eb981dc2810ae3025006"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-507":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Uniform\",\"Exponential\",\"Normal\",\"Weibull\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following probability distributions is often used to model the first region of the general failure rate model (the bathtub curve)?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The first region of the bathtub curve is known as the “early life” region and is characterized by a decreasing failure rate function. Since the failure rate is time- dependent in this region, the Weibull distribution is frequently used to model this region of the bathtub curve. [III.E.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Uniform","Exponential","Normal","Weibull"],"stem":"Which of the following probability distributions is often used to model the first region of the general failure rate model (the bathtub curve)?","sub":"design","why":"The first region of the bathtub curve is known as the “early life” region and is characterized by a decreasing failure rate function. Since the failure rate is time- dependent in this region, the Weibull distribution is frequently used to model this region of the bathtub curve. [III.E.3]"},"revision":"35d26e1d6f954e9ef0644b93b4662836c85ba8ebc59982ac84e45bd67b18e0b1"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-508":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"0.86530\",\"0.92000\",\"0.95000\",\"0.99996\"],\"stem\":\"Consider a system with three components arranged in parallel, each labeled with its reliability within its box: 0.99, 0.92, and 0.95. What is the reliability of the overall system?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The diagram shows a parallel system with three components. Parallel-system reliability is Rs(t) = 1 - [(1-0.99)(1-0.92)(1-0.95)] = 1 - [0.01×0.08×0.05] = 1 - 0.00004 = 0.99996. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["0.86530","0.92000","0.95000","0.99996"],"stem":"Consider a system with three components arranged in parallel, each labeled with its reliability within its box: 0.99, 0.92, and 0.95. What is the reliability of the overall system?","sub":"design","why":"The diagram shows a parallel system with three components. Parallel-system reliability is Rs(t) = 1 - [(1-0.99)(1-0.92)(1-0.95)] = 1 - [0.01×0.08×0.05] = 1 - 0.00004 = 0.99996. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"1a0238b6e891ec33a6b8712f10fad0e42dee006b308b51b307728523b4ae6cf2"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-509":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"it requires more runs.\",\"it is more difficult to analyze.\",\"measurements are less accurate.\",\"effects are confounded.\"],\"stem\":\"A disadvantage of using a fractional factorial experiment is that:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"A fractional factorial experiment uses fewer runs; however, not all interactions of interest may be estimable separately from main effects or other interactions. Some of the effects may be confounded with other effects. This can make it difficult to identify which factor or interaction is significant. [VI.H.5]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["it requires more runs.","it is more difficult to analyze.","measurements are less accurate.","effects are confounded."],"stem":"A disadvantage of using a fractional factorial experiment is that:","sub":"design","why":"A fractional factorial experiment uses fewer runs; however, not all interactions of interest may be estimable separately from main effects or other interactions. Some of the effects may be confounded with other effects. This can make it difficult to identify which factor or interaction is significant. [VI.H.5]"},"revision":"46177882382af881bd06493ebd72d42befcd83f231cce743bf86f32d170d1393"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-51":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"validation.\",\"verification.\",\"qualification.\",\"performance.\"],\"stem\":\"For a process, confirming that specified requirements have been fulfilled is known as:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Verification deals with determining whether the product or process satisfies specified requirements — it meets all specifications. This does not guarantee the product works as intended. [III.D]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["validation.","verification.","qualification.","performance."],"stem":"For a process, confirming that specified requirements have been fulfilled is known as:","sub":"design","why":"Verification deals with determining whether the product or process satisfies specified requirements — it meets all specifications. This does not guarantee the product works as intended. [III.D]"},"revision":"5723cb9f0c1d02067b0c6cac9d2998361cf16aa45b61cdf0108f2be6cbe0f188"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-510":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"decreasing.\",\"constant.\",\"increasing.\",\"unknown.\"],\"stem\":\"A preventive maintenance policy is recommended when the failure rate is:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"If the failure rate is decreasing, constant (such as when the reliability of the system has an exponential distribution), or unknown, maintaining the system in an attempt to prevent failures will not affect the probability that the system will fail in the next instant. Therefore, a preventive maintenance policy is not recommended. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["decreasing.","constant.","increasing.","unknown."],"stem":"A preventive maintenance policy is recommended when the failure rate is:","sub":"design","why":"If the failure rate is decreasing, constant (such as when the reliability of the system has an exponential distribution), or unknown, maintaining the system in an attempt to prevent failures will not affect the probability that the system will fail in the next instant. Therefore, a preventive maintenance policy is not recommended. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"6dfa6cff24f98577b0aaa57302e81bb3e2cf929e628d3857103c234e9ee8a7f0"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-511":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.009\",\"0.368\",\"0.632\",\"2.718\"],\"stem\":\"The MTTF for a model of dishwasher is 115 months. What is the probability of a given dishwasher failing by 115 months of operation? Assume the reliability of the dishwasher has an exponential distribution.\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"With MTTF=115, λ=1/115. Since reliability is exponential, F(115) = 1-R(115) = 1-e^-115/115 = 1-e^-1 = 1-0.3679 = 0.6321. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.009","0.368","0.632","2.718"],"stem":"The MTTF for a model of dishwasher is 115 months. What is the probability of a given dishwasher failing by 115 months of operation? Assume the reliability of the dishwasher has an exponential distribution.","sub":"design","why":"With MTTF=115, λ=1/115. Since reliability is exponential, F(115) = 1-R(115) = 1-e^-115/115 = 1-e^-1 = 1-0.3679 = 0.6321. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"510fd8d4028572574accf0cfe9af50fb060a60afa280c3b8294f59925767321a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-512":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"does not take much time or cost to implement.\",\"must be used with other quality programs such as Six Sigma.\",\"helps identify failures before they occur.\",\"is a reactive tool.\"],\"stem\":\"A benefit of implementing an FMEA is that it:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The main benefit of an FMEA is that it helps teams to identify and eliminate/ reduce the negative effects of potential failures before they occur. However, FMEA is expensive. Note that FMEA can be used as a stand-alone tool and does not need to be part of a larger quality system such as Six Sigma. [III.E.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["does not take much time or cost to implement.","must be used with other quality programs such as Six Sigma.","helps identify failures before they occur.","is a reactive tool."],"stem":"A benefit of implementing an FMEA is that it:","sub":"design","why":"The main benefit of an FMEA is that it helps teams to identify and eliminate/ reduce the negative effects of potential failures before they occur. However, FMEA is expensive. Note that FMEA can be used as a stand-alone tool and does not need to be part of a larger quality system such as Six Sigma. [III.E.4]"},"revision":"6ecc6117314e62410d9b37129bfb0c1814d0fab435aae800085fc59705051f50"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-513":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Increasing the experimental error\",\"Missing an important effect\",\"Increasing the cost of the experiment\",\"Increasing the measurement error\"],\"stem\":\"A team decided to change the range of temperature from 150–250°F to 175–225°F in an experimental design. What is a potential disadvantage of changing the range of this factor?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Selecting the range of factors in an experimental design is extremely important. In this problem, the range of the factor has been decreased. If the range of factors is too narrow, it is possible to miss important effects. [VI.H.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Increasing the experimental error","Missing an important effect","Increasing the cost of the experiment","Increasing the measurement error"],"stem":"A team decided to change the range of temperature from 150–250°F to 175–225°F in an experimental design. What is a potential disadvantage of changing the range of this factor?","sub":"design","why":"Selecting the range of factors in an experimental design is extremely important. In this problem, the range of the factor has been decreased. If the range of factors is too narrow, it is possible to miss important effects. [VI.H.2]"},"revision":"aa212ce733f1100d2fd9c845ae036a56a1095c514d948d92d8e50cee0ed3f260"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-514":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"design control.\",\"design review.\",\"design outputs.\",\"design inputs.\"],\"stem\":\"Regulatory requirements, customer needs, and product capabilities are examples of:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Regulatory requirements, customer needs, product or process capability, and product reliability are some of the inputs that will place requirements on the design. [III.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["design control.","design review.","design outputs.","design inputs."],"stem":"Regulatory requirements, customer needs, and product capabilities are examples of:","sub":"design","why":"Regulatory requirements, customer needs, product or process capability, and product reliability are some of the inputs that will place requirements on the design. [III.B.3]"},"revision":"1e59a8c6b13820ce5203278481057af77411810fa25586c4eb7ca3fb55d099b3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-515":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"a specification for achieving high quality.\",\"what the customer asks for.\",\"what is needed to achieve what the customer asks for.\",\"a clearly stated need of the customer.\"],\"stem\":\"A driver in a Critical to Quality Tree is:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The driver in a Critical to Quality Tree is what is needed to achieve what the customer asks for and will have lower-level Critical to Qualities that define what is needed to accomplish the driver. [III.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["a specification for achieving high quality.","what the customer asks for.","what is needed to achieve what the customer asks for.","a clearly stated need of the customer."],"stem":"A driver in a Critical to Quality Tree is:","sub":"design","why":"The driver in a Critical to Quality Tree is what is needed to achieve what the customer asks for and will have lower-level Critical to Qualities that define what is needed to accomplish the driver. [III.B.1]"},"revision":"19b15a2d2e18ac41efd5fb6b0911546f14b0bc79540f04e9c107047c48a16633"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-516":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.0221\",\"0.0622\",\"0.9378\",\"45.25\"],\"stem\":\"In a test of a component of a machine, 350 components were tested for eight hours and sixty-two components failed. If the mean time to repair these components is three hours, what is the steady-state availability of this component?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"λ = failures/total test time = 62/(350×8) = 0.0221, so MTBF = 1/λ ≈ 45.25 hours. With MTTR=3 hours, availability A = MTBF/(MTBF+MTTR) = 45.25/48.25 ≈ 0.9378. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.0221","0.0622","0.9378","45.25"],"stem":"In a test of a component of a machine, 350 components were tested for eight hours and sixty-two components failed. If the mean time to repair these components is three hours, what is the steady-state availability of this component?","sub":"design","why":"λ = failures/total test time = 62/(350×8) = 0.0221, so MTBF = 1/λ ≈ 45.25 hours. With MTTR=3 hours, availability A = MTBF/(MTBF+MTTR) = 45.25/48.25 ≈ 0.9378. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"e19ab2a48e9b31bf019350dbd7fc19a426c940e292911a661641daf028c382e3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-517":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"The failure rate is increasing.\",\"The failure rate is decreasing.\",\"The failure rate is constant.\",\"The uniform distribution is a good reliability model.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following statements is true regarding the second region of the bathtub curve?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The second region of the bathtub curve is the constant failure rate region. Since failures occur randomly over time in this region, the failure rate is constant. The exponential distribution is frequently used to model the reliability in this region. [III.E.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["The failure rate is increasing.","The failure rate is decreasing.","The failure rate is constant.","The uniform distribution is a good reliability model."],"stem":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the second region of the bathtub curve?","sub":"design","why":"The second region of the bathtub curve is the constant failure rate region. Since failures occur randomly over time in this region, the failure rate is constant. The exponential distribution is frequently used to model the reliability in this region. [III.E.3]"},"revision":"eea5894d58e114cec85e2d7f8513a4182e67f682eea4096385f30d4b112ab90e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-518":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"main effects.\",\"three-factor or higher interactions.\",\"two-factor or higher interactions.\",\"no other effects.\"],\"stem\":\"In a resolution IV fractional factorial experiment, two-factor interactions are confounded with:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Resolution IV designs have main effects confounded with three-factor or higher interactions, and two-factor interactions confounded with two-factor or higher interactions. With a resolution IV design, it is unknown for sure that a two-factor interaction is truly significant or whether the two-factor interaction it is aliased with is significant. Additional experimental runs may be needed to break these aliases. [VI.H.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["main effects.","three-factor or higher interactions.","two-factor or higher interactions.","no other effects."],"stem":"In a resolution IV fractional factorial experiment, two-factor interactions are confounded with:","sub":"design","why":"Resolution IV designs have main effects confounded with three-factor or higher interactions, and two-factor interactions confounded with two-factor or higher interactions. With a resolution IV design, it is unknown for sure that a two-factor interaction is truly significant or whether the two-factor interaction it is aliased with is significant. Additional experimental runs may be needed to break these aliases. [VI.H.5]"},"revision":"3e01e0d8ad87b16ab96436693e59644ff0f8bf872bb26c63744235dab6c453f8"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-519":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Resolution III\",\"Resolution IV\",\"Resolution V\",\"Full-factorial design\"],\"stem\":\"Consider an experiment with seven factors each at two levels. The complete defining relation is I = ABCDF = ABDEG = CEFG. What resolution is this design?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"A design''s resolution equals the length of the shortest word in its defining relation; since the shortest word here (ABCDF, ABDEG, CEFG) is four characters, this is a resolution IV design. [VI.H.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Resolution III","Resolution IV","Resolution V","Full-factorial design"],"stem":"Consider an experiment with seven factors each at two levels. The complete defining relation is I = ABCDF = ABDEG = CEFG. What resolution is this design?","sub":"design","why":"A design''s resolution equals the length of the shortest word in its defining relation; since the shortest word here (ABCDF, ABDEG, CEFG) is four characters, this is a resolution IV design. [VI.H.5]"},"revision":"ce00d2bed1c0f3610ba10c8d5825abbd350657f523cf984f2702ffc87c593427"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-52":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"during production or development.\",\"when customer requirements are determined.\",\"after completion of the product, process, or service.\",\"prior to development.\"],\"stem\":\"Validation of a product, process, or service is normally carried out:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Validation takes place after the product, process, or service is finished or completed. Validation is used to determine whether predetermined requirements are being met (product/process/service is working as intended). [III.D]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["during production or development.","when customer requirements are determined.","after completion of the product, process, or service.","prior to development."],"stem":"Validation of a product, process, or service is normally carried out:","sub":"design","why":"Validation takes place after the product, process, or service is finished or completed. Validation is used to determine whether predetermined requirements are being met (product/process/service is working as intended). [III.D]"},"revision":"0479d3ab52480443c1ed702dce09f654d3e7e8cd699f49a9ca64fa6b6697f77c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-520":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"12.50%\",\"28.65%\",\"71.35%\",\"87.50%\"],\"stem\":\"What fraction of all rechargeable batteries fail by ten hours if the MTBF is eight hours? Assume the reliability of these batteries has an exponential distribution.\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"With MTBF=8, λ=1/8. F(10) = 1-R(10) = 1-e^-10/8 = 1-0.2865 = 0.7135, so about 71% of batteries fail by ten hours. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["12.50%","28.65%","71.35%","87.50%"],"stem":"What fraction of all rechargeable batteries fail by ten hours if the MTBF is eight hours? Assume the reliability of these batteries has an exponential distribution.","sub":"design","why":"With MTBF=8, λ=1/8. F(10) = 1-R(10) = 1-e^-10/8 = 1-0.2865 = 0.7135, so about 71% of batteries fail by ten hours. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"70dba885909027d5351f68c0b7890e5b203ddbcbfe07fcfa41c7b031f7756db8"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-521":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Quality\",\"Inconsistent service\",\"Lack of durability\",\"Difficult to access\"],\"stem\":\"Gale and Wood (1994) introduced seven tools of customer value analysis. They described different perspectives on what customers believe is of value in a product or service. Which of the following would often be considered a value by the customer?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Staying with the original perspective experts and customers have when it comes to value (a positive outcome in this case), then quality would be the correct answer. [I.G]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Quality","Inconsistent service","Lack of durability","Difficult to access"],"stem":"Gale and Wood (1994) introduced seven tools of customer value analysis. They described different perspectives on what customers believe is of value in a product or service. Which of the following would often be considered a value by the customer?","sub":"design","why":"Staying with the original perspective experts and customers have when it comes to value (a positive outcome in this case), then quality would be the correct answer. [I.G]"},"revision":"e466086b1f203dd96c0d9a5295399609171b1d230fa5d8d2174ec8ccd16381e6"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-522":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"A only\",\"All main effects and two-factor interactions\",\"B only\",\"None of the main effects or interactions\"],\"stem\":\"Consider the following results of a 23 full-factorial design with two replicates. Assuming the normality and variance assumptions are valid, which terms have a significant effect on the response variable? Source df SS MS F p-value A 1 16.0 16.0 3.20 0.1114 B 1 12.0 12.0 2.40 0.1599 C 1 5.0 5.0 1.00 0.3466 AB 1 2.5 2.5 0.50 0.4996 AC 1 1.5 1.5 0.30 0.5988 BC 1 0.5 0.5 0.10 0.7599 ABC 1 0.25 0.25 0.05 0.8287 Error 8 40.0 5.0 Total 15\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"From the ANOVA table, none of the factors in this experiment has a significant effect on the response. The p-values for all main effects and interaction terms are relatively large (> 0.10); therefore, the conclusion is that none of the factors significantly affect the response. [VI.H.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["A only","All main effects and two-factor interactions","B only","None of the main effects or interactions"],"stem":"Consider the following results of a 23 full-factorial design with two replicates. Assuming the normality and variance assumptions are valid, which terms have a significant effect on the response variable? Source df SS MS F p-value A 1 16.0 16.0 3.20 0.1114 B 1 12.0 12.0 2.40 0.1599 C 1 5.0 5.0 1.00 0.3466 AB 1 2.5 2.5 0.50 0.4996 AC 1 1.5 1.5 0.30 0.5988 BC 1 0.5 0.5 0.10 0.7599 ABC 1 0.25 0.25 0.05 0.8287 Error 8 40.0 5.0 Total 15","sub":"design","why":"From the ANOVA table, none of the factors in this experiment has a significant effect on the response. The p-values for all main effects and interaction terms are relatively large (> 0.10); therefore, the conclusion is that none of the factors significantly affect the response. [VI.H.4]"},"revision":"aca86ddb677f62c3bda903dc193ba8628b3d5b62d81392ec581cd0a847715637"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-523":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Fail-safe device\",\"Redundancy\",\"Magnification of senses\",\"Special control device\"],\"stem\":\"A tool is designed so that, upon assembly, a component can be attached to the tool in only one position. What type of prevention action does this describe?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"This is an example of a fail-safe device: since the component can only be attached in one position, the tool has been error-proofed against incorrect assembly. [V.F]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Fail-safe device","Redundancy","Magnification of senses","Special control device"],"stem":"A tool is designed so that, upon assembly, a component can be attached to the tool in only one position. What type of prevention action does this describe?","sub":"design","why":"This is an example of a fail-safe device: since the component can only be attached in one position, the tool has been error-proofed against incorrect assembly. [V.F]"},"revision":"22141295dda2de86ca18e64d35ccb8b6e1c4b8b55153ec4f88b682be96e49de1"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-524":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"0.8653\",\"0.9000\",\"0.9500\",\"0.9999\"],\"stem\":\"Consider a system with three components arranged in series, each labeled with its reliability within its box: 0.99, 0.92, and 0.95. What is the reliability of the overall system?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The diagram shows a series system with three components. Series-system reliability is the product of the component reliabilities: Rs(t) = 0.99 × 0.92 × 0.95 = 0.8653. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["0.8653","0.9000","0.9500","0.9999"],"stem":"Consider a system with three components arranged in series, each labeled with its reliability within its box: 0.99, 0.92, and 0.95. What is the reliability of the overall system?","sub":"design","why":"The diagram shows a series system with three components. Series-system reliability is the product of the component reliabilities: Rs(t) = 0.99 × 0.92 × 0.95 = 0.8653. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"9e4e97af64a89b2cc91304ff0020b2ddbf111c5210c9216cc4e0d7512758d589"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-525":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"primary datum.\",\"secondary datum.\",\"tertiary datum.\",\"modifier.\"],\"stem\":\"Consider the control frame below. L 0.01 A B C “B” represents:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"B represents the secondary datum. [III.C]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["primary datum.","secondary datum.","tertiary datum.","modifier."],"stem":"Consider the control frame below. L 0.01 A B C “B” represents:","sub":"design","why":"B represents the secondary datum. [III.C]"},"revision":"445f3d5ab6f48d6525ca20ff3b087b0b07b361a0fee895f0ed9a9beebed92f4a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-526":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"minimizes cost.\",\"maximizes run time between repairs.\",\"minimizes risk.\",\"is efficient for catastrophic failures.\"],\"stem\":\"An advantage of a corrective maintenance policy is that it:\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"While a corrective maintenance policy allows for the maximum run time between repairs, it is not the most efficient or cost-effective. If there is a catastrophic failure, the repair time may be extensive, with high costs. [III.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["minimizes cost.","maximizes run time between repairs.","minimizes risk.","is efficient for catastrophic failures."],"stem":"An advantage of a corrective maintenance policy is that it:","sub":"design","why":"While a corrective maintenance policy allows for the maximum run time between repairs, it is not the most efficient or cost-effective. If there is a catastrophic failure, the repair time may be extensive, with high costs. [III.E.2]"},"revision":"0d9acac51722abc0d26e912b63a404624c32737c85c0c568d203124f77c120ce"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-527":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Series system\",\"Parallel system\",\"k-out-of-n system\",\"Mixed parallel\"],\"stem\":\"A machine has six subsystems and will fail when four or more of these subsystems fail. What type of system design does this machine have?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"A k-out-of-n system design requires that a system made up of n subsystems must have a minimum of k subsystems functioning to operate. In this case, since the machine will fail if four of six subsystems fail, the machine has a 3-out-of-6 system design, since at most three subsystems can fail before the machine fails. [III.E.2].\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Series system","Parallel system","k-out-of-n system","Mixed parallel"],"stem":"A machine has six subsystems and will fail when four or more of these subsystems fail. What type of system design does this machine have?","sub":"design","why":"A k-out-of-n system design requires that a system made up of n subsystems must have a minimum of k subsystems functioning to operate. In this case, since the machine will fail if four of six subsystems fail, the machine has a 3-out-of-6 system design, since at most three subsystems can fail before the machine fails. [III.E.2]."},"revision":"ba7ba0522aeede2260eacfb2938f4960a720eb14ffde810e1fc92184be73d845"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-528":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Full-factorial design\",\"Half-fractional factorial design\",\"Taguchi design\",\"One-fourth-fractional factorial design\"],\"stem\":\"An experiment has eight factors at two levels each. The experiment has sixty-four runs. What is the experimental design called?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The experiment has 2^8-2 = 64 runs, making this a one-fourth-fractional factorial design. [VI.H.5]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Full-factorial design","Half-fractional factorial design","Taguchi design","One-fourth-fractional factorial design"],"stem":"An experiment has eight factors at two levels each. The experiment has sixty-four runs. What is the experimental design called?","sub":"design","why":"The experiment has 2^8-2 = 64 runs, making this a one-fourth-fractional factorial design. [VI.H.5]"},"revision":"27141c01b99d238eec165c94b95c54911c6cf0ace5bacdc9aecf5dbf2f6d6c7e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-529":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"the likelihood of a failure occurring.\",\"the consequences of a failure mode and its frequency of occurrence.\",\"the likelihood of detecting a failure once it has occurred.\",\"the severity of a failure if it occurs.\"],\"stem\":\"Criticality measures\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Criticality is an assessment of the consequences of a failure mode along with how frequently it occurs. [III.E.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["the likelihood of a failure occurring.","the consequences of a failure mode and its frequency of occurrence.","the likelihood of detecting a failure once it has occurred.","the severity of a failure if it occurs."],"stem":"Criticality measures","sub":"design","why":"Criticality is an assessment of the consequences of a failure mode along with how frequently it occurs. [III.E.4]"},"revision":"e3e6e41784a02d11ecda6b4aa07f7bd94e5b96b6737b2406f10edf3d69bca781"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-53":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"At project initiation\",\"During development\",\"At the time of its release\",\"After it is released\"],\"stem\":\"When can the reliability of a product be measured?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"Reliability cannot be measured at any time prior to the release of the product; it can only be predicted. This is because the reliability of a product is dependent on many factors, including the system design, the reliability of its components, the operating environment, environmental factors, and manufacturing defects. [III.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["At project initiation","During development","At the time of its release","After it is released"],"stem":"When can the reliability of a product be measured?","sub":"design","why":"Reliability cannot be measured at any time prior to the release of the product; it can only be predicted. This is because the reliability of a product is dependent on many factors, including the system design, the reliability of its components, the operating environment, environmental factors, and manufacturing defects. [III.E.1]"},"revision":"d215946ae3493690f81261d822bbfa6057b51ffd397367fd4b22199db46bcdd7"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-530":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Customer wait time\",\"Number of filing errors\",\"Costs of processing applications\",\"Thickness of a component\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is considered attribute data?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Attribute data describes discrete data in quality control. Wait time, processing cost, and thickness are all continuous measurements, but the number of filing errors can only take a countable set of values, making it discrete (attribute) data. [VI.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Customer wait time","Number of filing errors","Costs of processing applications","Thickness of a component"],"stem":"Which of the following is considered attribute data?","sub":"quant","why":"Attribute data describes discrete data in quality control. Wait time, processing cost, and thickness are all continuous measurements, but the number of filing errors can only take a countable set of values, making it discrete (attribute) data. [VI.A.1]"},"revision":"e5b37b47d3019da3c45aedd4e692468aa7dd4b148c4e3ed1e184ad2bde294408"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-531":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"5.5/32\",\"5.5/ 32\",\"5.5/ 24\",\"5.5\"],\"stem\":\"A population has a distribution with mean 24 and standard deviation 5.5. What is the approximate standard deviation of the sampling distribution of the sample mean if random samples of size 32 are taken?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"By the central limit theorem, the sampling distribution of X̄ has standard deviation σX̄ = σ/√n. Here σ=5.5, n=32, so σX̄ = 5.5/√32. [VI.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["5.5/32","5.5/ 32","5.5/ 24","5.5"],"stem":"A population has a distribution with mean 24 and standard deviation 5.5. What is the approximate standard deviation of the sampling distribution of the sample mean if random samples of size 32 are taken?","sub":"quant","why":"By the central limit theorem, the sampling distribution of X̄ has standard deviation σX̄ = σ/√n. Here σ=5.5, n=32, so σX̄ = 5.5/√32. [VI.C.2]"},"revision":"1020e2939edda0ac3653336d10ea3ff7011e93050dff98e1664016e0a4aeee18"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-532":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Attempt to fix the problem on their own.\",\"Follow the reaction plan detailed in the control plan of the process.\",\"Brainstorm to determine the cause of the problem.\",\"Nothing; the process will stabilize on its own.\"],\"stem\":\"A control chart monitoring the viscosity of a product shows that a particular process is out of control. What should the process operator do?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Control plans include detailed reaction plans to follow when the control method detects a problem; the reaction plan details the steps an operator should take when the process has a problem. [IV.A]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Attempt to fix the problem on their own.","Follow the reaction plan detailed in the control plan of the process.","Brainstorm to determine the cause of the problem.","Nothing; the process will stabilize on its own."],"stem":"A control chart monitoring the viscosity of a product shows that a particular process is out of control. What should the process operator do?","sub":"quant","why":"Control plans include detailed reaction plans to follow when the control method detects a problem; the reaction plan details the steps an operator should take when the process has a problem. [IV.A]"},"revision":"334354eb8e705d0a181c49d54ff324cb924cce92e36354a9bd4e80615591ba11"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-533":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"chart\":{\"r\":{\"cl\":8.1,\"data\":[7.2,9.1,6.8,8.5,7.9,8.8,6.5,9.4,7.6,8.2,6.9,20],\"lcl\":0,\"ucl\":18.48},\"type\":\"xbar-r\",\"xbar\":{\"cl\":26.75,\"data\":[27.1,26.4,28,25.9,27.6,26.8,27.9,25.6,27.3,28.2,26,28.5],\"lcl\":20.85,\"ucl\":32.65}},\"options\":[\"both the average and range were within the control limits.\",\"neither the average nor range were within the control limits.\",\"only the average was outside the control limits.\",\"only the range was outside the control limits.\"],\"stem\":\"A control chart for an operation using twenty-five samples of four pieces each found X̄=26.75 and R̄=8.10. During production, a new sample of four pieces measured 20, 29, 40, and 25. At the time this sample was taken, were both the average and range within the control limits?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A control chart for an operation using twenty-five samples of four pieces each found X̄=26.75 and R̄=8.10. During production, a new sample of four pieces measured 20, 29, 40, and 25. At the time this sample was taken, were both the average and range within the control limits?\"}","content":{"answer":3,"chart":{"r":{"cl":8.1,"data":[7.2,9.1,6.8,8.5,7.9,8.8,6.5,9.4,7.6,8.2,6.9,20],"lcl":0,"ucl":18.48},"type":"xbar-r","xbar":{"cl":26.75,"data":[27.1,26.4,28,25.9,27.6,26.8,27.9,25.6,27.3,28.2,26,28.5],"lcl":20.85,"ucl":32.65}},"options":["both the average and range were within the control limits.","neither the average nor range were within the control limits.","only the average was outside the control limits.","only the range was outside the control limits."],"stem":"A control chart for an operation using twenty-five samples of four pieces each found X̄=26.75 and R̄=8.10. During production, a new sample of four pieces measured 20, 29, 40, and 25. At the time this sample was taken, were both the average and range within the control limits?","sub":"quant","why":"A control chart for an operation using twenty-five samples of four pieces each found X̄=26.75 and R̄=8.10. During production, a new sample of four pieces measured 20, 29, 40, and 25. At the time this sample was taken, were both the average and range within the control limits?"},"revision":"8ade3da78a0c6a768643ab09f152318d971539f27751dea6ea2355ea6d3f2f89"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-534":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Reject H₀; conclude that the two means are not statistically significantly different.\",\"Reject H₀; conclude that the two means are statistically significantly different.\",\"Do not reject H₀; conclude that the two means are not statistically significantly different.\",\"Do not reject H₀; conclude that the two means are statistically significantly different.\"],\"stem\":\"Suppose that the p-value of a hypothesis test on µ₁ − µ₂ is 0.23. What conclusions can be made (use significance level of 10%)?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With α = 0.10 and p-value = 0.23: since p-value > α, H₀ is not rejected. The conclusion is that the two means are not statistically significantly different. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Reject H₀; conclude that the two means are not statistically significantly different.","Reject H₀; conclude that the two means are statistically significantly different.","Do not reject H₀; conclude that the two means are not statistically significantly different.","Do not reject H₀; conclude that the two means are statistically significantly different."],"stem":"Suppose that the p-value of a hypothesis test on µ₁ − µ₂ is 0.23. What conclusions can be made (use significance level of 10%)?","sub":"quant","why":"With α = 0.10 and p-value = 0.23: since p-value > α, H₀ is not rejected. The conclusion is that the two means are not statistically significantly different. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"53499daaa8dc49c0c8320004f3e536772d3257c40ee16ab55e807e19e14eb3d8"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-535":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"chart\":{\"pattern\":\"linear\",\"sourceN\":20,\"title\":\"Weibull probability plot\",\"type\":\"normal-prob\",\"yAxisLabel\":\"Weibull score\"},\"options\":[\"The normal distribution is not a reasonable model for the time to failure.\",\"The Weibull distribution is a reasonable model for the time to failure.\",\"The normal distribution is a reasonable model for the time to failure.\",\"Not enough information is provided to draw conclusions.\"],\"stem\":\"A team measured the time to failure of twenty components and plotted the data on a Weibull probability plot. The plotted points fall approximately along a straight line. What conclusions can be drawn from this plot?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"On a Weibull probability plot, the data are plotted against percentiles based on the Weibull distribution. Since the plotted points fall along a straight line, the Weibull distribution is a reasonable model for the time to failure. [VI.A.7]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"chart":{"pattern":"linear","sourceN":20,"title":"Weibull probability plot","type":"normal-prob","yAxisLabel":"Weibull score"},"options":["The normal distribution is not a reasonable model for the time to failure.","The Weibull distribution is a reasonable model for the time to failure.","The normal distribution is a reasonable model for the time to failure.","Not enough information is provided to draw conclusions."],"stem":"A team measured the time to failure of twenty components and plotted the data on a Weibull probability plot. The plotted points fall approximately along a straight line. What conclusions can be drawn from this plot?","sub":"quant","why":"On a Weibull probability plot, the data are plotted against percentiles based on the Weibull distribution. Since the plotted points fall along a straight line, the Weibull distribution is a reasonable model for the time to failure. [VI.A.7]"},"revision":"54ca5cb9f7bc187a940266e08d38e1fb30e9b99c2c8d594dc992f278f3ab0b47"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-536":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"(–0.4016, 0.6158)\",\"(0, 0.6158)\",\"(0.0006, 0.2136)\",\"(0.0391, 0.1751)\"],\"stem\":\"A team investigated the number of patient falls in a hospital. Fifty-six patients were randomly selected. Out of the fifty-six patients, there were six recorded patient falls. Using this information, construct a 90% confidence interval on the true proportion of patient falls in this hospital.\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With x=6 successes out of n=56, the sample proportion p̂=0.1071. For a 90% CI, zα/2=1.645: p̂ ± zα/2√[p̂(1-p̂)/n] gives 0.0391 ≤ p ≤ 0.1751. [VI.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["(–0.4016, 0.6158)","(0, 0.6158)","(0.0006, 0.2136)","(0.0391, 0.1751)"],"stem":"A team investigated the number of patient falls in a hospital. Fifty-six patients were randomly selected. Out of the fifty-six patients, there were six recorded patient falls. Using this information, construct a 90% confidence interval on the true proportion of patient falls in this hospital.","sub":"quant","why":"With x=6 successes out of n=56, the sample proportion p̂=0.1071. For a 90% CI, zα/2=1.645: p̂ ± zα/2√[p̂(1-p̂)/n] gives 0.0391 ≤ p ≤ 0.1751. [VI.D.1]"},"revision":"62fb656330a8b95677be69c97a563634527870b038189698cab6a1aba77ab105"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-537":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.1539\",\"0.4349\",\"0.5651\",\"0.7190\"],\"stem\":\"Find the probability that Z, the standard normal distribution, lies between –1.02 and 0.58.\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"P(-1.02 < Z < 0.58) = P(Z<0.58) - P(Z<-1.02) = 0.7190 - 0.1539 = 0.5651. [VI.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.1539","0.4349","0.5651","0.7190"],"stem":"Find the probability that Z, the standard normal distribution, lies between –1.02 and 0.58.","sub":"quant","why":"P(-1.02 < Z < 0.58) = P(Z<0.58) - P(Z<-1.02) = 0.7190 - 0.1539 = 0.5651. [VI.C.1]"},"revision":"e4cefc8a91f295c5e020d2e9ac205c42695fcbc6dc021cfd7a418a8cb3b7f977"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-538":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Nominal\",\"Ordinal\",\"Interval\",\"Ratio\"],\"stem\":\"The date of a customer’s visit to a store is on which measurement scale?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A date is on an interval scale: there are meaningful differences between two dates (you can subtract them to get the number of days between), but there is no absolute zero, so dates cannot be meaningfully multiplied or divided. [VI.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Nominal","Ordinal","Interval","Ratio"],"stem":"The date of a customer’s visit to a store is on which measurement scale?","sub":"quant","why":"A date is on an interval scale: there are meaningful differences between two dates (you can subtract them to get the number of days between), but there is no absolute zero, so dates cannot be meaningfully multiplied or divided. [VI.A.2]"},"revision":"4416ecee30d44aabc99ecafbb443465bfc97f12b42de5dc23bc61f4d88582b13"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-539":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Common cause variation\",\"Special cause variation\",\"Overcontrol\",\"Undercontrol\"],\"stem\":\"A control chart was out of control, but the operator failed to adjust the process. What is this called?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Undercontrol (underadjustment) occurs when a control chart signals out-of-control behavior but the process operator fails to respond and adjust the process. [VI.F.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Common cause variation","Special cause variation","Overcontrol","Undercontrol"],"stem":"A control chart was out of control, but the operator failed to adjust the process. What is this called?","sub":"quant","why":"Undercontrol (underadjustment) occurs when a control chart signals out-of-control behavior but the process operator fails to respond and adjust the process. [VI.F.2]"},"revision":"ce0c6c30598144d51848018d470184ef6656a578b1fc3e598bb6a6da81801ecd"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-54":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"The probability that a randomly selected air conditioner fails by time t is 4,996/5,000.\",\"The probability that a randomly selected air conditioner will instantly fail is 4,996/5,000.\",\"The fraction of all units in the lot of air conditioners that fail by time t is 4/5,000.\",\"The probability that a randomly selected air conditioner will instantly fail is 4/5,000.\"],\"stem\":\"The cumulative distribution function of failure at time t for a type of air conditioner is defined as F(t) = 4/5,000. Which of the following statements is (are) true?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The cumulative distribution function F(t) gives the probability that a randomly selected unit fails by time t, equivalently the fraction of the population that has failed by time t. Therefore, 4/5,000 is the fraction that fails by time t. The instantaneous failure rate is described by the hazard function, not the cumulative distribution function. [III.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["The probability that a randomly selected air conditioner fails by time t is 4,996/5,000.","The probability that a randomly selected air conditioner will instantly fail is 4,996/5,000.","The fraction of all units in the lot of air conditioners that fail by time t is 4/5,000.","The probability that a randomly selected air conditioner will instantly fail is 4/5,000."],"stem":"The cumulative distribution function of failure at time t for a type of air conditioner is defined as F(t) = 4/5,000. Which of the following statements is (are) true?","sub":"design","why":"The cumulative distribution function F(t) gives the probability that a randomly selected unit fails by time t, equivalently the fraction of the population that has failed by time t. Therefore, 4/5,000 is the fraction that fails by time t. The instantaneous failure rate is described by the hazard function, not the cumulative distribution function. [III.E.1]"},"revision":"d85a4bc2eb0a8ff48c6a850449aa37e24290e1f483fb593bee3490c5b4c42c18"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-540":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"three levels of five factors.\",\"three independent variables and five replicates.\",\"five levels of three factors.\",\"five independent variables and three response variables.\"],\"stem\":\"A 3⁵ experiment means that there are:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"This notation indicates five independent factors, each run at three levels, for a total of 3^5=243 treatment combinations; it gives no information about the number of responses or replicates. [VI.H.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["three levels of five factors.","three independent variables and five replicates.","five levels of three factors.","five independent variables and three response variables."],"stem":"A 3⁵ experiment means that there are:","sub":"quant","why":"This notation indicates five independent factors, each run at three levels, for a total of 3^5=243 treatment combinations; it gives no information about the number of responses or replicates. [VI.H.1]"},"revision":"6d91c7577d2c41417f1e681d3c7badbc8fb117af20bb80db616e14f3e901c95f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-541":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"The wait times at store A are more variable than those at store B.\",\"The distribution of wait times at both stores is not bell shaped.\",\"The wait times at store B are more variable than those at store A.\",\"Customers at store A tend to wait longer than those at store B.\"],\"stem\":\"Side-by-side box plots show the wait times of customers at two grocery stores. The box for Store A is much narrower (tighter) than the box for Store B, and both distributions appear approximately symmetric. What can be said about these wait times?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The box plot for Store A is much tighter than Store B''s, indicating more variability in wait times at Store B; both distributions are approximately symmetric. [VI.A.7]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["The wait times at store A are more variable than those at store B.","The distribution of wait times at both stores is not bell shaped.","The wait times at store B are more variable than those at store A.","Customers at store A tend to wait longer than those at store B."],"stem":"Side-by-side box plots show the wait times of customers at two grocery stores. The box for Store A is much narrower (tighter) than the box for Store B, and both distributions appear approximately symmetric. What can be said about these wait times?","sub":"quant","why":"The box plot for Store A is much tighter than Store B''s, indicating more variability in wait times at Store B; both distributions are approximately symmetric. [VI.A.7]"},"revision":"8bc8ce5ae0550e46e0954b6d60ec1cb3280c839128d69dedec3035629c460c52"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-542":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"chart\":{\"parallel\":false,\"type\":\"interaction-plot\"},\"options\":[\"High levels of factor B tend to have smaller response values.\",\"There is not an interaction effect between factors A and B.\",\"There is an interaction effect between factors A and B.\",\"Factors A and B do not have an effect on the response variable.\"],\"stem\":\"A two-way ANOVA was used to test the effects of two factors, A and B, on a response variable. An interaction plot shows that the response changes quite differently across the levels of factor A depending on which level of factor B is used (the lines are not parallel). What conclusions can be drawn from this interaction plot?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The interaction plot shows the response changing quite differently across levels of factor A depending on the level of factor B, indicating a significant interaction effect between factors A and B. [VI.D.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"chart":{"parallel":false,"type":"interaction-plot"},"options":["High levels of factor B tend to have smaller response values.","There is not an interaction effect between factors A and B.","There is an interaction effect between factors A and B.","Factors A and B do not have an effect on the response variable."],"stem":"A two-way ANOVA was used to test the effects of two factors, A and B, on a response variable. An interaction plot shows that the response changes quite differently across the levels of factor A depending on which level of factor B is used (the lines are not parallel). What conclusions can be drawn from this interaction plot?","sub":"quant","why":"The interaction plot shows the response changing quite differently across levels of factor A depending on the level of factor B, indicating a significant interaction effect between factors A and B. [VI.D.5]"},"revision":"eb6e0db401fefaf4b4beb34024515a957c7b935c5340e1ddbdb26cd06478f329"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-543":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"–12.39\",\"1.645\",\"1.96\",\"12.39\"],\"stem\":\"Tensile strength of cement is of interest to a company. Historical studies indicate that tensile strength is normally distributed and that the standard deviation of tensile strength is 1.25 psi. A random sample of fifteen cement pieces had a mean tensile strength of 124 psi. The company would like to determine if the mean tensile strength is more than 120 psi at the 5% significance level. What is the test statistic for this hypothesis test?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"For H0: μ=120 vs Ha: μ>120 with known σ, z0 = (124-120)/(1.25/√15) = 12.39, which exceeds the critical value z0.05=1.645, so H0 is rejected: the mean tensile strength is more than 120 psi. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["–12.39","1.645","1.96","12.39"],"stem":"Tensile strength of cement is of interest to a company. Historical studies indicate that tensile strength is normally distributed and that the standard deviation of tensile strength is 1.25 psi. A random sample of fifteen cement pieces had a mean tensile strength of 124 psi. The company would like to determine if the mean tensile strength is more than 120 psi at the 5% significance level. What is the test statistic for this hypothesis test?","sub":"quant","why":"For H0: μ=120 vs Ha: μ>120 with known σ, z0 = (124-120)/(1.25/√15) = 12.39, which exceeds the critical value z0.05=1.645, so H0 is rejected: the mean tensile strength is more than 120 psi. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"18b39d078abab38a3a8469ee9c27d50d55505c14a14523ea9c7204c3cc91a1bb"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-544":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Calculate the correlation coefficient.\",\"Fit a linear regression model of the two variables.\",\"Graph the data in a scatter diagram.\",\"Compare histograms of the two variables.\"],\"stem\":\"A team suspects that there is a linear relationship between two continuous variables. What is the first thing they should do when examining the relationship between these two variables?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Before calculating statistics or fitting a regression model, the team should first plot the data in a scatter diagram, which reveals whether a relationship between the two variables exists. [V.A]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Calculate the correlation coefficient.","Fit a linear regression model of the two variables.","Graph the data in a scatter diagram.","Compare histograms of the two variables."],"stem":"A team suspects that there is a linear relationship between two continuous variables. What is the first thing they should do when examining the relationship between these two variables?","sub":"quant","why":"Before calculating statistics or fitting a regression model, the team should first plot the data in a scatter diagram, which reveals whether a relationship between the two variables exists. [V.A]"},"revision":"fdcce3a0b7e8e3467eb38fa5a3c0ef3a855c3cd62479313fd3d41677b3adef7f"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-545":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"0.0558\",\"0.53\",\"0.71\",\"Not enough information is provided.\"],\"stem\":\"If the probability that event A occurs is 0.62, the probability that event B occurs is 0.09, and events A and B are statistically independent, what is the probability that event A and B both occur?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Since A and B are statistically independent, P(A∩B) = P(A)P(B) = (0.62)(0.09) = 0.0558. [VI.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["0.0558","0.53","0.71","Not enough information is provided."],"stem":"If the probability that event A occurs is 0.62, the probability that event B occurs is 0.09, and events A and B are statistically independent, what is the probability that event A and B both occur?","sub":"quant","why":"Since A and B are statistically independent, P(A∩B) = P(A)P(B) = (0.62)(0.09) = 0.0558. [VI.B.3]"},"revision":"9f242ea9e205d2f1ee922c6f6d66c3b9018fd636c6de0dca0f4a3b083edb7559"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-546":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"1.01\",\"1.23\",\"1.45\",\"Unknown\"],\"stem\":\"A normally distributed process has specifications 20.35 ± 3.50. X and R control charts (based on samples of size four) indicate there is an out-of-control point. The control charts also indicate that X = 20.98 and R = 1.96. What is the process capability?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The control chart indicated an out-of-control point, meaning the process is not stable (special cause variation is present). Since capability indices assume a stable process, the capability here is unknown. [VI.G.2, VI.G.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["1.01","1.23","1.45","Unknown"],"stem":"A normally distributed process has specifications 20.35 ± 3.50. X and R control charts (based on samples of size four) indicate there is an out-of-control point. The control charts also indicate that X = 20.98 and R = 1.96. What is the process capability?","sub":"quant","why":"The control chart indicated an out-of-control point, meaning the process is not stable (special cause variation is present). Since capability indices assume a stable process, the capability here is unknown. [VI.G.2, VI.G.3]"},"revision":"0b58bccdc1ef6ce6808c0d332b0c48b4d12205377ce4635a0803dcfa4f6e7a3c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-547":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"–1.82\",\"–0.90\",\"0\",\"0.90\"],\"stem\":\"A scatterplot displays the relationship between two variables: as x increases, y clearly tends to decrease, though the points are only moderately clustered around the trend. What is the correlation coefficient of these two variables?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The scatterplot shows y decreasing as x increases (a negative relationship), and the points aren''t tightly clustered, indicating a moderately strong negative correlation of -0.90. [VI.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["–1.82","–0.90","0","0.90"],"stem":"A scatterplot displays the relationship between two variables: as x increases, y clearly tends to decrease, though the points are only moderately clustered around the trend. What is the correlation coefficient of these two variables?","sub":"quant","why":"The scatterplot shows y decreasing as x increases (a negative relationship), and the points aren''t tightly clustered, indicating a moderately strong negative correlation of -0.90. [VI.E.2]"},"revision":"308f32af3a1bd032329c5c6060d513e4036d897ecbfb2250238cf001636f9c49"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-548":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"25\",\"26\",\"663\",\"664\"],\"stem\":\"A team would like to estimate the true average tensile strength of cement. They would like to obtain an estimate within 0.05 MPa of the true average tensile strength with 99% confidence. Based on prior information, it is assumed that σ = 0.50 MPa. What sample size is required to meet these requirements?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With margin of error E=0.05, σ=0.50, α=0.01, zα/2=2.576: n ≥ (σ·zα/2/E)² = (0.50×2.576/0.05)² = 663.58, so the minimum sample size needed is n=664. [VI.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["25","26","663","664"],"stem":"A team would like to estimate the true average tensile strength of cement. They would like to obtain an estimate within 0.05 MPa of the true average tensile strength with 99% confidence. Based on prior information, it is assumed that σ = 0.50 MPa. What sample size is required to meet these requirements?","sub":"quant","why":"With margin of error E=0.05, σ=0.50, α=0.01, zα/2=2.576: n ≥ (σ·zα/2/E)² = (0.50×2.576/0.05)² = 663.58, so the minimum sample size needed is n=664. [VI.D.1]"},"revision":"d967b4d9d7ac3549459136529f10a9315649f78f4d8e0ed499b8d95afd4adfa8"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-549":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"chart\":{\"cl\":6.75,\"data\":[6,7,5,8,6,7,5,8,18],\"lcl\":4.21,\"title\":\"np-chart\",\"type\":\"control-single\",\"ucl\":14.36},\"options\":[\"the sample was within the control limits.\",\"the sample was outside the control limits.\",\"the upper control limit was 0.12.\",\"the lower control limit was 0.\"],\"stem\":\"A factory collected data on the number of nonconforming parts to construct an np-chart. Fifteen samples of size 150 were collected. They determined that the average fraction nonconforming was p̄ = 0.045. During production, a sample of 150 parts was taken, of which eighteen were nonconforming. At the time this sample was taken:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With p̄=0.045, m=15, n=150, the np-chart limits are UCL = np̄+3√[np̄(1-p̄)] = 14.36 and LCL = np̄-3√[np̄(1-p̄)] = 4.21. Since the new sample''s x=18 exceeds the UCL, the sample is outside the control limits. [VI.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"chart":{"cl":6.75,"data":[6,7,5,8,6,7,5,8,18],"lcl":4.21,"title":"np-chart","type":"control-single","ucl":14.36},"options":["the sample was within the control limits.","the sample was outside the control limits.","the upper control limit was 0.12.","the lower control limit was 0."],"stem":"A factory collected data on the number of nonconforming parts to construct an np-chart. Fifteen samples of size 150 were collected. They determined that the average fraction nonconforming was p̄ = 0.045. During production, a sample of 150 parts was taken, of which eighteen were nonconforming. At the time this sample was taken:","sub":"quant","why":"With p̄=0.045, m=15, n=150, the np-chart limits are UCL = np̄+3√[np̄(1-p̄)] = 14.36 and LCL = np̄-3√[np̄(1-p̄)] = 4.21. Since the new sample''s x=18 exceeds the UCL, the sample is outside the control limits. [VI.F.5]"},"revision":"b056aa2f96ed5786ab4046e92d6ce1c4dd7f2579bbca4a1e955dc9418195f80a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-55":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0\",\"0.008\",\"0.992\",\"1\"],\"stem\":\"The cumulative distribution function of failure at time t for a computer model is 0.992. What fraction of this computer model fails by time t?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The cumulative distribution function (cdf) of failure can be interpreted as the fraction of all units in the population that fail by time t. Since F(t) = 0.992 for this computer model, the fraction of this model that fail by time t is 0.992. [III.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0","0.008","0.992","1"],"stem":"The cumulative distribution function of failure at time t for a computer model is 0.992. What fraction of this computer model fails by time t?","sub":"design","why":"The cumulative distribution function (cdf) of failure can be interpreted as the fraction of all units in the population that fail by time t. Since F(t) = 0.992 for this computer model, the fraction of this model that fail by time t is 0.992. [III.E.1]"},"revision":"c4729843900ca87c7b13f081e706eaea3faf1a6281e8bbf28a1be61227debaed"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-550":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"cl\":6.6,\"data\":[6,7,5,8,6,7,5,8,6,7,5,8,6,7,5,4,5,6,7,8,9,10,11,12],\"lcl\":0,\"title\":\"c-chart\",\"type\":\"control-single\",\"ucl\":14.31},\"options\":[\"The process is out of control.\",\"The process is stable.\",\"The process is operating within the specifications.\",\"The process is operating outside the specifications.\"],\"stem\":\"Consider a c-chart with UCL = 14.31, c̄ = 6.6, and LCL = 0. Starting at the sixteenth sample, there is a run of eight consecutive points that are all increasing. What conclusions can be drawn from this control chart?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Starting at the 16th point, there is a run of eight consecutive increasing points. One sensitizing rule for out-of-control behavior is six or more points in a row all increasing or all decreasing, so the process is out of control; a control chart says nothing about whether the process meets specification limits. [VI.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"cl":6.6,"data":[6,7,5,8,6,7,5,8,6,7,5,8,6,7,5,4,5,6,7,8,9,10,11,12],"lcl":0,"title":"c-chart","type":"control-single","ucl":14.31},"options":["The process is out of control.","The process is stable.","The process is operating within the specifications.","The process is operating outside the specifications."],"stem":"Consider a c-chart with UCL = 14.31, c̄ = 6.6, and LCL = 0. Starting at the sixteenth sample, there is a run of eight consecutive points that are all increasing. What conclusions can be drawn from this control chart?","sub":"quant","why":"Starting at the 16th point, there is a run of eight consecutive increasing points. One sensitizing rule for out-of-control behavior is six or more points in a row all increasing or all decreasing, so the process is out of control; a control chart says nothing about whether the process meets specification limits. [VI.F.6]"},"revision":"1e5f6b6a638a92fbacfe0f32e7bb466997278a2826bfec5ef576d2722a17d7ce"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-551":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Lognormal\",\"Exponential\",\"Chi-square\",\"Normal\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following probability distributions is symmetric?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The lognormal, exponential, and chi-square distributions are all right-skewed and take only positive values; the normal distribution is the symmetric one among these choices. [VI.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Lognormal","Exponential","Chi-square","Normal"],"stem":"Which of the following probability distributions is symmetric?","sub":"quant","why":"The lognormal, exponential, and chi-square distributions are all right-skewed and take only positive values; the normal distribution is the symmetric one among these choices. [VI.C.1]"},"revision":"0ce44dc528510cf945458d40eaa35784e3346ff9dfcfaf578c352c83dbee9234"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-552":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.28\",\"135\",\"184\",\"212.80\"],\"stem\":\"A team wants to predict a person’s cholesterol based on their weight in pounds. The fitted regression equation is Cholesterol = 135 + 0.28 × Weight. What is the predicted cholesterol of a person who weighs 175 pounds?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Substituting Weight=175: Cholesterol = 135 + 0.28(175) = 184. [VI.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.28","135","184","212.80"],"stem":"A team wants to predict a person’s cholesterol based on their weight in pounds. The fitted regression equation is Cholesterol = 135 + 0.28 × Weight. What is the predicted cholesterol of a person who weighs 175 pounds?","sub":"quant","why":"Substituting Weight=175: Cholesterol = 135 + 0.28(175) = 184. [VI.E.1]"},"revision":"06b63f915730803a05dec72a93d6e049e228dc11d72f8781025a2334e22a27b7"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-553":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Reject H ; conclude that the variance equals 4 minutes2.\",\"Reject H ; conclude that the variance is less than 4 minutes2.\",\"Do not reject H ; conclude that the variance equals 4 minutes2.\",\"Do not reject H ; conclude that the variance is less than 4 minutes2.\"],\"stem\":\"A hypothesis test on the variance of the wait times of customers at a store was conducted. You would like to determine if the variance of the wait time is less than 4 minutes2. A random sample of twenty-two wait times had a variance of 1.75 minutes2. At the 5% significance level, what is your conclusion from this hypothesis test?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"For H0: σ²=4 vs Ha: σ²<4 (left-tailed, α=0.05, n=22): χ2₀ = (n-1)s²/σ² = 21(1.75)/4 = 9.1875, which is less than the critical value χ2(0.95,21)=11.591, so H0 is rejected: there is sufficient evidence the variance is less than 4 minutes². [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Reject H ; conclude that the variance equals 4 minutes2.","Reject H ; conclude that the variance is less than 4 minutes2.","Do not reject H ; conclude that the variance equals 4 minutes2.","Do not reject H ; conclude that the variance is less than 4 minutes2."],"stem":"A hypothesis test on the variance of the wait times of customers at a store was conducted. You would like to determine if the variance of the wait time is less than 4 minutes2. A random sample of twenty-two wait times had a variance of 1.75 minutes2. At the 5% significance level, what is your conclusion from this hypothesis test?","sub":"quant","why":"For H0: σ²=4 vs Ha: σ²<4 (left-tailed, α=0.05, n=22): χ2₀ = (n-1)s²/σ² = 21(1.75)/4 = 9.1875, which is less than the critical value χ2(0.95,21)=11.591, so H0 is rejected: there is sufficient evidence the variance is less than 4 minutes². [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"87260f87665d3a81a3fe3e25a4402abe21bef923167f53b96913c49a1d802901"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-554":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.002\",\"1.5\",\"3.4\",\"2,700\"],\"stem\":\"Accounting for a 1.5σ shift in the mean of a process, how many defective parts per million are produced for a process operating at 6σ quality?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A process operating at Six Sigma quality (accounting for the standard 1.5σ shift) produces 3.4 defective parts per million. [V.C]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.002","1.5","3.4","2,700"],"stem":"Accounting for a 1.5σ shift in the mean of a process, how many defective parts per million are produced for a process operating at 6σ quality?","sub":"quant","why":"A process operating at Six Sigma quality (accounting for the standard 1.5σ shift) produces 3.4 defective parts per million. [V.C]"},"revision":"8de745fbc7ca20baf70065b280ffb5e42ad3e2c881697fabc55e53f7251269b6"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-555":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Reject H ; conclude that type of parts produced and time of day are independent.\",\"Reject H ; conclude that type of parts produced and time of day are not independent.\",\"Do not reject H ; conclude that type of parts produced and time of day are independent.\",\"Do not reject H ; conclude that type of parts produced and time of day are not independent.\"],\"stem\":\"Consider the contingency table below. A hypothesis test for independence is conducted to determine whether the number of nonconforming parts produced is independent of time of day. Conforming parts / Nonconforming parts / Totals -- Day: 82 / 18 / 100; Evening: 75 / 19 / 94; Overnight: 45 / 14 / 59; Totals: 202 / 51 / 253. The value of the test statistic for this hypothesis test is 0.757. At the 10% significance level, what is the conclusion from this hypothesis test?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A contingency table cross-tabulates conforming/nonconforming parts by time of day (day, evening, overnight shifts), yielding a chi-square test statistic of 0.757 for a test of independence. At the 10% significance level, what is the conclusion from this hypothesis test?\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Reject H ; conclude that type of parts produced and time of day are independent.","Reject H ; conclude that type of parts produced and time of day are not independent.","Do not reject H ; conclude that type of parts produced and time of day are independent.","Do not reject H ; conclude that type of parts produced and time of day are not independent."],"stem":"Consider the contingency table below. A hypothesis test for independence is conducted to determine whether the number of nonconforming parts produced is independent of time of day. Conforming parts / Nonconforming parts / Totals -- Day: 82 / 18 / 100; Evening: 75 / 19 / 94; Overnight: 45 / 14 / 59; Totals: 202 / 51 / 253. The value of the test statistic for this hypothesis test is 0.757. At the 10% significance level, what is the conclusion from this hypothesis test?","sub":"quant","why":"A contingency table cross-tabulates conforming/nonconforming parts by time of day (day, evening, overnight shifts), yielding a chi-square test statistic of 0.757 for a test of independence. At the 10% significance level, what is the conclusion from this hypothesis test?"},"revision":"3ad5d11911cd104e95cb08c9f139c27dc2c286e570894168abc9e2dbf12f88c7"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-556":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Normal\",\"Binomial\",\"Uniform\",\"Weibull\"],\"stem\":\"A population has a distribution that is highly right-skewed. One hundred samples of size 50 are randomly collected, and the 100 sample means are calculated. What is the approximate distribution of these sample averages?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"By the central limit theorem, the distribution of sample averages tends toward normal for sufficiently large samples (a rule of thumb is n>30 or 40), regardless of the shape of the underlying population. [VI.A.6, VI.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Normal","Binomial","Uniform","Weibull"],"stem":"A population has a distribution that is highly right-skewed. One hundred samples of size 50 are randomly collected, and the 100 sample means are calculated. What is the approximate distribution of these sample averages?","sub":"quant","why":"By the central limit theorem, the distribution of sample averages tends toward normal for sufficiently large samples (a rule of thumb is n>30 or 40), regardless of the shape of the underlying population. [VI.A.6, VI.C.2]"},"revision":"6320c00521d85f17a337ee3b41bbbc551c4321471cc3430eba47d3f715f5761d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-557":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0\",\"6.94\",\"12.83\",\"22.65\"],\"stem\":\"The number of defects on a TV screen is being examined. Thirty-two TV screens are randomly chosen each day over a 22-day period and examined for defects. The average number of defects is c = 5.68. What is the upper control limit for a corresponding c-chart?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With c̄=5.68, the c-chart UCL = c̄+3√c̄ = 5.68+3√5.68 = 12.83. [VI.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0","6.94","12.83","22.65"],"stem":"The number of defects on a TV screen is being examined. Thirty-two TV screens are randomly chosen each day over a 22-day period and examined for defects. The average number of defects is c = 5.68. What is the upper control limit for a corresponding c-chart?","sub":"quant","why":"With c̄=5.68, the c-chart UCL = c̄+3√c̄ = 5.68+3√5.68 = 12.83. [VI.F.5]"},"revision":"737c9538ce7ab911fb200a7c4c0039be3f3c09a512a1dbf6bfff08a524fad45d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-558":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"(0.534, 8.726)\",\"(0.783, 12.792)\",\"(0.885, 3.577)\",\"(9.072, 12.325)\"],\"stem\":\"The voltage of a power supply is of interest to a team. Voltage is assumed to be normally distributed. The voltages of nine randomly selected observations are: 9.66, 8.25, 12.46, 12.35, 11.52, 8.92, 11.00, 11.12, and 11.13. Find the 99% confidence interval on the population standard deviation of the voltage.\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With sample mean x̄≈10.712 and s²≈2.149 (n=9): the 99% CI on σ uses χ2(0.005,8)=21.955 and χ2(0.995,8)=1.344, giving √[(n-1)s²/21.955] to √[(n-1)s²/1.344] = (0.885, 3.577). [VI.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["(0.534, 8.726)","(0.783, 12.792)","(0.885, 3.577)","(9.072, 12.325)"],"stem":"The voltage of a power supply is of interest to a team. Voltage is assumed to be normally distributed. The voltages of nine randomly selected observations are: 9.66, 8.25, 12.46, 12.35, 11.52, 8.92, 11.00, 11.12, and 11.13. Find the 99% confidence interval on the population standard deviation of the voltage.","sub":"quant","why":"With sample mean x̄≈10.712 and s²≈2.149 (n=9): the 99% CI on σ uses χ2(0.005,8)=21.955 and χ2(0.995,8)=1.344, giving √[(n-1)s²/21.955] to √[(n-1)s²/1.344] = (0.885, 3.577). [VI.D.1]"},"revision":"f79d0c3b75445cd42686f33d476f907a033d2e0d56f973c809cdadbee81a3273"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-559":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"cl\":35.87,\"data\":[34.2,33.5,34.8,36.5,37.2,36.1,38,36.8,37.5,36.3,37.9,36.6],\"lcl\":29.9,\"title\":\"X̄ chart\",\"type\":\"control-single\",\"ucl\":41.84},\"options\":[\"The process is out of control.\",\"The process is stable.\",\"The process is operating within the specifications.\",\"The process is operating outside the specifications.\"],\"stem\":\"Consider an X̄ chart of a normally distributed process with UCL = 41.84, X̄̄ = 35.87, and LCL = 29.90. There are nine consecutive points above the centerline, starting with the fourth data point. What conclusions can be drawn from this control chart?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"There are nine consecutive points above the centerline starting at the 4th data point. A sensitizing rule for out-of-control behavior is nine points in a row on the same side of the centerline, so the process is out of control; a control chart says nothing about whether it meets specification limits. [VI.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"cl":35.87,"data":[34.2,33.5,34.8,36.5,37.2,36.1,38,36.8,37.5,36.3,37.9,36.6],"lcl":29.9,"title":"X̄ chart","type":"control-single","ucl":41.84},"options":["The process is out of control.","The process is stable.","The process is operating within the specifications.","The process is operating outside the specifications."],"stem":"Consider an X̄ chart of a normally distributed process with UCL = 41.84, X̄̄ = 35.87, and LCL = 29.90. There are nine consecutive points above the centerline, starting with the fourth data point. What conclusions can be drawn from this control chart?","sub":"quant","why":"There are nine consecutive points above the centerline starting at the 4th data point. A sensitizing rule for out-of-control behavior is nine points in a row on the same side of the centerline, so the process is out of control; a control chart says nothing about whether it meets specification limits. [VI.F.6]"},"revision":"0251c5259da1a450b72bd39823965d92d33b2e7453cef9364037be04e6595527"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-56":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Uniform\",\"Normal\",\"Exponential\",\"Weibull\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following probability distributions is the only one to have a constant failure rate?\",\"sub\":\"design\",\"why\":\"The exponential distribution is the only probability distribution to have a constant failure rate. For an exponential distribution with parameter λ, the hazard rate function h(t) = λ. [III.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Uniform","Normal","Exponential","Weibull"],"stem":"Which of the following probability distributions is the only one to have a constant failure rate?","sub":"design","why":"The exponential distribution is the only probability distribution to have a constant failure rate. For an exponential distribution with parameter λ, the hazard rate function h(t) = λ. [III.E.1]"},"revision":"7dd092e48300b1da5850341e9e9e00994b64acc76d33aed69ba077030e36505e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-560":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"–19\",\"–7.5\",\"7.5\",\"19\"],\"stem\":\"The fitted regression equation for two variables x and y is yˆ = –7.5x –19. What is the intercept of this line?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The intercept is the value of y when x=0, so the intercept is -19. [VI.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["–19","–7.5","7.5","19"],"stem":"The fitted regression equation for two variables x and y is yˆ = –7.5x –19. What is the intercept of this line?","sub":"quant","why":"The intercept is the value of y when x=0, so the intercept is -19. [VI.E.1]"},"revision":"f3f3a7743f8e7429d7528f0c363fc65d4e2a2b3435b69d5a1a0ef9e311727cbb"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-561":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0\",\"0.052\",\"0.53\",\"1.53\"],\"stem\":\"If the probability that event A occurs is 0.40, the probability that event B occurs is 0.13, and A and B are mutually exclusive, what is the probability that either A or B occurs?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Since A and B are mutually exclusive, P(A∩B)=0, so P(A∪B) = P(A)+P(B) = 0.40+0.13 = 0.53. [VI.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0","0.052","0.53","1.53"],"stem":"If the probability that event A occurs is 0.40, the probability that event B occurs is 0.13, and A and B are mutually exclusive, what is the probability that either A or B occurs?","sub":"quant","why":"Since A and B are mutually exclusive, P(A∩B)=0, so P(A∪B) = P(A)+P(B) = 0.40+0.13 = 0.53. [VI.B.3]"},"revision":"b249ff0b69a6f9c5daca47dae7ba6b71ae16d1e5bcf8b936fab32d487a4b0976"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-562":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.05\",\"0.15\",\"0.85\",\"0.95\"],\"stem\":\"A hypothesis test is performed at the 5% significance level. The probability of a type II error of the test is 0.15. What is the power for this hypothesis test?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Power = 1 - P(Type II error) = 1 - β = 1 - 0.15 = 0.85. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.05","0.15","0.85","0.95"],"stem":"A hypothesis test is performed at the 5% significance level. The probability of a type II error of the test is 0.15. What is the power for this hypothesis test?","sub":"quant","why":"Power = 1 - P(Type II error) = 1 - β = 1 - 0.15 = 0.85. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"0c9d32d4a2773ea388fc32e7bf7d19ae08fee1c56733f40da637b1f6189673dd"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-563":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"–0.91\",\"–0.39\",\"0.39\",\"0.91\"],\"stem\":\"A scatterplot displays the relationship between two random variables: as x increases, y tends to decrease, but the points are loosely scattered around the trend rather than tightly clustered. What is the correlation coefficient of these two variables?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The scatterplot shows y tending to decrease as x increases (a negative correlation), but the points aren''t tightly clustered, indicating a weak negative correlation of -0.39. [VI.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["–0.91","–0.39","0.39","0.91"],"stem":"A scatterplot displays the relationship between two random variables: as x increases, y tends to decrease, but the points are loosely scattered around the trend rather than tightly clustered. What is the correlation coefficient of these two variables?","sub":"quant","why":"The scatterplot shows y tending to decrease as x increases (a negative correlation), but the points aren''t tightly clustered, indicating a weak negative correlation of -0.39. [VI.E.2]"},"revision":"0772d7956d139537c49ba48d14c2d859cc37159deabcfb12a374e57b13e6c39a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-564":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"3.12 minutes\",\"5 minutes\",\"5.5 minutes\",\"6 minutes\"],\"stem\":\"The waiting times in minutes for eight customers at a bank are: 5, 8, 12, 3, 2, 7, 6, and 5. What is the mode for this sample of waiting times?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The mode is the value that occurs most often in the sample; in this data set, 5 minutes occurs more often than any other observation. [VI.A.6]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["3.12 minutes","5 minutes","5.5 minutes","6 minutes"],"stem":"The waiting times in minutes for eight customers at a bank are: 5, 8, 12, 3, 2, 7, 6, and 5. What is the mode for this sample of waiting times?","sub":"quant","why":"The mode is the value that occurs most often in the sample; in this data set, 5 minutes occurs more often than any other observation. [VI.A.6]"},"revision":"9f2f3ac221e5a2a7b2d4cc4236e10f303468be52a46e66b2e55b90d58d547586"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-565":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"0.68\",\"0.79\",\"0.90\",\"1.27\"],\"stem\":\"The dimension of a component has specifications of 10.80 ± 2.50. The process data are normally distributed with mean 10.45 and standard deviation 1.05. Find the actual capability of this process.\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With USL=13.3, LSL=8.3, μ=10.45, σ=1.05: Cpk = min[(USL-μ)/3σ, (μ-LSL)/3σ] = min(0.90, 0.68) = 0.68, which is less than 1, so the process is not capable. [VI.G.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["0.68","0.79","0.90","1.27"],"stem":"The dimension of a component has specifications of 10.80 ± 2.50. The process data are normally distributed with mean 10.45 and standard deviation 1.05. Find the actual capability of this process.","sub":"quant","why":"With USL=13.3, LSL=8.3, μ=10.45, σ=1.05: Cpk = min[(USL-μ)/3σ, (μ-LSL)/3σ] = min(0.90, 0.68) = 0.68, which is less than 1, so the process is not capable. [VI.G.3]"},"revision":"a20bf331c381b88b1de762a1d685b968801363a994a7896a6731399afcea6540"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-566":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Reject H ; conclude that the medication errors occur with the same probability.\",\"Reject H ; conclude that the medication errors do not occur with the same probability.\",\"Do not reject H ; conclude that the medication errors occur with the same probability.\",\"Do not reject H ; conclude that the medication errors do not occur with the same probability.\"],\"stem\":\"A team at a hospital did a project on medication errors. A random sample of medication errors yielded the following results: Type of error Number of errors Incorrect dose 22 Wrong dose 34 Incorrect form 47 Wrong amount 45 Total 148 The team used a goodness-of-fit test to determine whether the medication errors occur with equal probabilities. The test statistic was 10.757. At the 5% significance level, what is the conclusion from this hypothesis test?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"For H0: p1=p2=p3=p4=0.25 (α=0.05, k=4): expected counts Ei=37 each, χ2₀ = Σ(Oi-Ei)²/Ei = 10.757, which exceeds χ2(0.05,3)=7.815, so H0 is rejected: the medication errors do not occur with equal probability. [VI.D.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Reject H ; conclude that the medication errors occur with the same probability.","Reject H ; conclude that the medication errors do not occur with the same probability.","Do not reject H ; conclude that the medication errors occur with the same probability.","Do not reject H ; conclude that the medication errors do not occur with the same probability."],"stem":"A team at a hospital did a project on medication errors. A random sample of medication errors yielded the following results: Type of error Number of errors Incorrect dose 22 Wrong dose 34 Incorrect form 47 Wrong amount 45 Total 148 The team used a goodness-of-fit test to determine whether the medication errors occur with equal probabilities. The test statistic was 10.757. At the 5% significance level, what is the conclusion from this hypothesis test?","sub":"quant","why":"For H0: p1=p2=p3=p4=0.25 (α=0.05, k=4): expected counts Ei=37 each, χ2₀ = Σ(Oi-Ei)²/Ei = 10.757, which exceeds χ2(0.05,3)=7.815, so H0 is rejected: the medication errors do not occur with equal probability. [VI.D.4]"},"revision":"46051814f23838ba45c52e5e83b40830e30737dfcea9e1287363563a894a9f1a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-567":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"not adjust the process.\",\"have a small average run length.\",\"have a large average run length.\",\"stop monitoring the process.\"],\"stem\":\"If a process is out of control, it is desirable to:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"When a process is out of control, a small average run length is desirable so operators detect and address the change quickly; failing to adjust the process (underadjustment) lets additional variation persist. [VI.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["not adjust the process.","have a small average run length.","have a large average run length.","stop monitoring the process."],"stem":"If a process is out of control, it is desirable to:","sub":"quant","why":"When a process is out of control, a small average run length is desirable so operators detect and address the change quickly; failing to adjust the process (underadjustment) lets additional variation persist. [VI.F.6]"},"revision":"32f0069b1ea25a6db34feb39662fa853c04402ceeab6317849d73e11da87109c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-568":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0\",\"0.0082\",\"0.2119\",\"0.9918\"],\"stem\":\"A bottling company’s filling process has a normal distribution with mean 24 oz. and standard deviation 0.05 oz. What is the probability that a sample of nine randomly selected bottles will have an average volume less than 23.96 oz?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With μ=24, σ=0.05, n=9: σX̄ = 0.05/√9 = 0.01667. P(X̄<23.96) = P(Z < (23.96-24)/0.01667) = P(Z<-2.40) = 0.0082. [VI.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0","0.0082","0.2119","0.9918"],"stem":"A bottling company’s filling process has a normal distribution with mean 24 oz. and standard deviation 0.05 oz. What is the probability that a sample of nine randomly selected bottles will have an average volume less than 23.96 oz?","sub":"quant","why":"With μ=24, σ=0.05, n=9: σX̄ = 0.05/√9 = 0.01667. P(X̄<23.96) = P(Z < (23.96-24)/0.01667) = P(Z<-2.40) = 0.0082. [VI.C.2]"},"revision":"cf47be56ef10b925cb5bdfa162a6eb5c32765bb89e139f8162bb5e3cc2286ec7"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-569":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"SPC cannot show whether process variation appears to be random.\",\"SPC cannot determine whether a process is improving.\",\"SPC cannot indicate when a process has nonrandom variation.\",\"SPC cannot indicate what caused out-of-control behavior in a process.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is outside the normal scope of statistical process control (SPC)?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"SPC can show whether a process''s variation is random or nonrandom and can help determine whether a process is improving, but it cannot by itself identify what caused out-of-control behavior -- further investigation is required for that. [VI.F.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["SPC cannot show whether process variation appears to be random.","SPC cannot determine whether a process is improving.","SPC cannot indicate when a process has nonrandom variation.","SPC cannot indicate what caused out-of-control behavior in a process."],"stem":"Which of the following is outside the normal scope of statistical process control (SPC)?","sub":"quant","why":"SPC can show whether a process''s variation is random or nonrandom and can help determine whether a process is improving, but it cannot by itself identify what caused out-of-control behavior -- further investigation is required for that. [VI.F.1]"},"revision":"5c21c748bcad2403db2300af89db12fbf010c8b7fabf956387d341d846d92ee4"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-57":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"The sample size and frequency of sampling to monitor the painting process\",\"Employee training records\",\"Management review minutes\",\"Manufacturing costs\"],\"stem\":\"An employee works in an area of a facility where tools are painted. Which of the following would this employee find in the control plan for the process of painting tools?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Control plans typically include the following: a description of the process, any equipment used in the process, reference drawing numbers, the process characteristic that is being controlled, how the process characteristic should be evaluated, the sampling plan, the method of control, and a reaction plan if the process goes out of control. [IV.A]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["The sample size and frequency of sampling to monitor the painting process","Employee training records","Management review minutes","Manufacturing costs"],"stem":"An employee works in an area of a facility where tools are painted. Which of the following would this employee find in the control plan for the process of painting tools?","sub":"ppc","why":"Control plans typically include the following: a description of the process, any equipment used in the process, reference drawing numbers, the process characteristic that is being controlled, how the process characteristic should be evaluated, the sampling plan, the method of control, and a reaction plan if the process goes out of control. [IV.A]"},"revision":"f163f8ddc707c871159bd2c7631a69af67ce05fd6bce23a8de2fa36b9692fc17"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-570":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"parameter.\",\"statistic.\",\"population.\",\"critical value.\"],\"stem\":\"A quantity derived from sample data that describes the distribution is called a:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A statistic is a quantity derived from sample data used to estimate a population parameter; a critical value, by contrast, is the value a test statistic is compared to in hypothesis testing. [VI.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["parameter.","statistic.","population.","critical value."],"stem":"A quantity derived from sample data that describes the distribution is called a:","sub":"quant","why":"A statistic is a quantity derived from sample data used to estimate a population parameter; a critical value, by contrast, is the value a test statistic is compared to in hypothesis testing. [VI.B.1]"},"revision":"50dfc37f3b7c3492d66f7156da1efc0e5e5bb44727e2f3529e0734de0add58d1"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-571":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"power.\",\"type I error.\",\"type II error.\",\"significance level.\"],\"stem\":\"The probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis is called:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Power = 1 - β = 1 - P(Type II error), the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis. Type I error is rejecting a true H0; Type II error is failing to reject a false H0. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["power.","type I error.","type II error.","significance level."],"stem":"The probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis is called:","sub":"quant","why":"Power = 1 - β = 1 - P(Type II error), the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis. Type I error is rejecting a true H0; Type II error is failing to reject a false H0. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"67a334ada6a75419e51e6c0eebc926b7525ca45960a19bb9cf4094d0d2f1f122"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-572":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"–0.93\",\"0\",\"0.93\",\"1.62\"],\"stem\":\"A scatterplot displays the relationship between two random variables, showing no discernible linear pattern between them. What is the correlation coefficient of these two variables?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The scatterplot shows no linear relationship between the two variables, so the correlation coefficient is 0 (the correlation coefficient can only range from -1 to 1). [VI.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["–0.93","0","0.93","1.62"],"stem":"A scatterplot displays the relationship between two random variables, showing no discernible linear pattern between them. What is the correlation coefficient of these two variables?","sub":"quant","why":"The scatterplot shows no linear relationship between the two variables, so the correlation coefficient is 0 (the correlation coefficient can only range from -1 to 1). [VI.E.2]"},"revision":"6bf3c75d3d6320f8f51bb6364167e93c4d3375f61594340f975f3f563777d1f6"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-573":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Poisson\",\"Hypergeometric\",\"Normal\",\"Binomial\"],\"stem\":\"A factory has historically produced defective parts 1% of the time. Samples of fifty independent parts produced in the factory are tested for the presence or absence of a defect. The resulting data (the number of parts that are defective) form which type of distribution?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The number of defective parts among n=50 independent trials, each with a fixed probability of a defect (0.01) and only two possible outcomes, follows a binomial distribution. [VI.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Poisson","Hypergeometric","Normal","Binomial"],"stem":"A factory has historically produced defective parts 1% of the time. Samples of fifty independent parts produced in the factory are tested for the presence or absence of a defect. The resulting data (the number of parts that are defective) form which type of distribution?","sub":"quant","why":"The number of defective parts among n=50 independent trials, each with a fixed probability of a defect (0.01) and only two possible outcomes, follows a binomial distribution. [VI.C.2]"},"revision":"53bf736fd93e243c7693186637b7e58c626b33391d117d2210e69e5896375780"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-574":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.0375\",\"0.0689\",\"0.9311\",\"0.9686\"],\"stem\":\"The diameter of a stainless-steel rod is an important quality characteristic. The rod has specifications 1.50 ± 0.08. Data from the process indicate that the distribution is normally distributed, and X̄ and R charts indicate that the process is stable. The control charts used a sample of size four and found that X̄ = 1.4983 and R̄ = 0.0906. What fraction of steel rods will fall outside the specification limits?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With μ̂=X̄=1.4983 and σ̂=R̄/d2=0.0906/2.059≈0.044 (n=4): P(1.42<X<1.58) = P(-1.78<Z<1.86) = 0.9686-0.0375 = 0.9311 within spec, so 1-0.9311 = 0.0689 (about 6.89%) fall outside the specification limits. [VI.G.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.0375","0.0689","0.9311","0.9686"],"stem":"The diameter of a stainless-steel rod is an important quality characteristic. The rod has specifications 1.50 ± 0.08. Data from the process indicate that the distribution is normally distributed, and X̄ and R charts indicate that the process is stable. The control charts used a sample of size four and found that X̄ = 1.4983 and R̄ = 0.0906. What fraction of steel rods will fall outside the specification limits?","sub":"quant","why":"With μ̂=X̄=1.4983 and σ̂=R̄/d2=0.0906/2.059≈0.044 (n=4): P(1.42<X<1.58) = P(-1.78<Z<1.86) = 0.9686-0.0375 = 0.9311 within spec, so 1-0.9311 = 0.0689 (about 6.89%) fall outside the specification limits. [VI.G.2]"},"revision":"d38aabe599aad8dbb6d9f2b08b71d214a199448a748f0b407b15ea96d27e02d1"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-575":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Poisson.\",\"binomial.\",\"normal.\",\"exponential.\"],\"stem\":\"The distribution on which np-charts are based is called:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"p-charts and np-charts are based on the binomial distribution. [VI.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Poisson.","binomial.","normal.","exponential."],"stem":"The distribution on which np-charts are based is called:","sub":"quant","why":"p-charts and np-charts are based on the binomial distribution. [VI.F.5]"},"revision":"4a88d8319f0f9bad08a2663d9f9d164bf53b72087088adf9caf260129993f947"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-576":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.02\",\"0.05\",\"0.13\",\"0.38\"],\"stem\":\"Consider the contingency table below. Conforming parts / Nonconforming parts / Totals -- Day: 94 / 5 / 99; Evening: 87 / 10 / 97; Overnight: 65 / 23 / 88; Totals: 246 / 38 / 284. Given a nonconforming part, what is the probability it was made during the day shift?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"P(Day | Nonconforming) = P(Day ∩ Nonconforming)/P(Nonconforming) = (5/284)/(38/284) = 5/38 ≈ 0.13. [VI.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.02","0.05","0.13","0.38"],"stem":"Consider the contingency table below. Conforming parts / Nonconforming parts / Totals -- Day: 94 / 5 / 99; Evening: 87 / 10 / 97; Overnight: 65 / 23 / 88; Totals: 246 / 38 / 284. Given a nonconforming part, what is the probability it was made during the day shift?","sub":"quant","why":"P(Day | Nonconforming) = P(Day ∩ Nonconforming)/P(Nonconforming) = (5/284)/(38/284) = 5/38 ≈ 0.13. [VI.B.3]"},"revision":"1de0b121cf8ed3162abbf0d87b77a27d64dd3f50c8677c1aff3ab2682b3d8adc"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-577":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"(0.180, 0.190)\",\"(0.181, 0.189)\",\"(0.183, 0.189)\",\"(0.184, 0.188)\"],\"stem\":\"A random sample of eight pieces of plastic tubing is selected. The inside diameter of the tubing is known to be normally distributed. The inside diameters of the eight pieces of plastic tubing are: 0.183, 0.190, 0.180, 0.189, 0.190, 0.188, 0.186, 0.185. Find the 95% confidence interval for the population mean of the inside diameter of the plastic tubing.\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With x̄=0.186 and s≈0.0036 (n=8): a 95% CI using t(0.025,7)=2.365 gives x̄ ± t·s/√n = (0.183, 0.189). [VI.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["(0.180, 0.190)","(0.181, 0.189)","(0.183, 0.189)","(0.184, 0.188)"],"stem":"A random sample of eight pieces of plastic tubing is selected. The inside diameter of the tubing is known to be normally distributed. The inside diameters of the eight pieces of plastic tubing are: 0.183, 0.190, 0.180, 0.189, 0.190, 0.188, 0.186, 0.185. Find the 95% confidence interval for the population mean of the inside diameter of the plastic tubing.","sub":"quant","why":"With x̄=0.186 and s≈0.0036 (n=8): a 95% CI using t(0.025,7)=2.365 gives x̄ ± t·s/√n = (0.183, 0.189). [VI.D.1]"},"revision":"c6018625bf9a50eea446cdbe29662a69b007029cb2d9da32994b17cba28bc12a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-578":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"The age of the car does not have a significant effect on the cost.\",\"The intercept of the regression equation, β , is equal to 0.\",\"The age of the car has a significant effect on the cost.\",\"The intercept of the regression equation, β , does not equal 0.\"],\"stem\":\"A study on a random sample of sixteen cars was done to evaluate the relationship of age of the car and its cost. The ANOVA table below displays the results of a regression analysis. At the 5% significance level, what conclusions can be drawn from these results? Source df SS MS F p-value Regression 1 429.68 429.68 52.53 0.000 Error 14 114.55 8.18 Total 15 544.23\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"For the regression ANOVA (H0: β1=0 vs Ha: β1≠0), F0=52.53 exceeds the critical value F(0.05,1,14)=4.60 (and the p-value 0.000 < 0.05), so H0 is rejected: there is a statistically significant linear relationship between the age of the car and its cost. [VI.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["The age of the car does not have a significant effect on the cost.","The intercept of the regression equation, β , is equal to 0.","The age of the car has a significant effect on the cost.","The intercept of the regression equation, β , does not equal 0."],"stem":"A study on a random sample of sixteen cars was done to evaluate the relationship of age of the car and its cost. The ANOVA table below displays the results of a regression analysis. At the 5% significance level, what conclusions can be drawn from these results? Source df SS MS F p-value Regression 1 429.68 429.68 52.53 0.000 Error 14 114.55 8.18 Total 15 544.23","sub":"quant","why":"For the regression ANOVA (H0: β1=0 vs Ha: β1≠0), F0=52.53 exceeds the critical value F(0.05,1,14)=4.60 (and the p-value 0.000 < 0.05), so H0 is rejected: there is a statistically significant linear relationship between the age of the car and its cost. [VI.E.1]"},"revision":"2a27021d03bca72c11d9dc0d169aa98b50627bd6b185d3d838f81b845e6e12b0"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-579":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"0\",\"0.018\",\"0.410\",\"0.476\"],\"stem\":\"A team at a factory is monitoring the average number of defects in TV screens. Twenty-eight TV screens are randomly selected each day over a 50-day period. The average number of defects per TV screen was found to be u = 0.214. What is the lower control limit for a corresponding u-chart?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With ū=0.214, n=28: LCL = ū-3√(ū/n) = 0.214-3√(0.214/28) ≈ -0.048, which by convention is rounded up to 0 since a control limit cannot be negative. [VI.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["0","0.018","0.410","0.476"],"stem":"A team at a factory is monitoring the average number of defects in TV screens. Twenty-eight TV screens are randomly selected each day over a 50-day period. The average number of defects per TV screen was found to be u = 0.214. What is the lower control limit for a corresponding u-chart?","sub":"quant","why":"With ū=0.214, n=28: LCL = ū-3√(ū/n) = 0.214-3√(0.214/28) ≈ -0.048, which by convention is rounded up to 0 since a control limit cannot be negative. [VI.F.5]"},"revision":"4e499d63b161dbdb6f79452cf4a00b52122de1ef0ae677b1d82b4094bdf4595e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-58":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Sampling plan\",\"Control chart\",\"Detailed methods to contain questionable products\",\"Gage R&R study\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following would you typically find in a reaction plan for a process?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"A reaction plan typically contains four elements: containment, diagnosis, verification, and disposition. The reaction plan should therefore include specific directions for the operators on how to contain products that are suspected of being nonconforming or out of spec. [IV.A]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Sampling plan","Control chart","Detailed methods to contain questionable products","Gage R&R study"],"stem":"Which of the following would you typically find in a reaction plan for a process?","sub":"ppc","why":"A reaction plan typically contains four elements: containment, diagnosis, verification, and disposition. The reaction plan should therefore include specific directions for the operators on how to contain products that are suspected of being nonconforming or out of spec. [IV.A]"},"revision":"e3ee3620855179125ac677575539cf1e26e439291cea39321f674e9eac462d8d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-580":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Normal\",\"Binomial\",\"Poisson\",\"Exponential\"],\"stem\":\"What probability distribution is required for the results of a capability study to be considered valid?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Capability indices are only valid when the underlying process data follow a normal distribution; a histogram or normal probability plot can be used to check this assumption. [VI.G.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Normal","Binomial","Poisson","Exponential"],"stem":"What probability distribution is required for the results of a capability study to be considered valid?","sub":"quant","why":"Capability indices are only valid when the underlying process data follow a normal distribution; a histogram or normal probability plot can be used to check this assumption. [VI.G.1]"},"revision":"89c1f12bea9be530b6ba3af27ae79c48199d95bddf580df397ffd165411fdd95"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-581":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Symmetric\",\"Right-skewed\",\"Left-skewed\",\"Bimodal\"],\"stem\":\"A histogram shows a distribution with a few extremely large values, pulling its tail out to the right while most of the data cluster at lower values. What shape does this distribution have?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The histogram shows a few extremely large values pulling the distribution''s tail to the right, indicating a right-skewed distribution. [VI.A.6]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Symmetric","Right-skewed","Left-skewed","Bimodal"],"stem":"A histogram shows a distribution with a few extremely large values, pulling its tail out to the right while most of the data cluster at lower values. What shape does this distribution have?","sub":"quant","why":"The histogram shows a few extremely large values pulling the distribution''s tail to the right, indicating a right-skewed distribution. [VI.A.6]"},"revision":"51798babaa8928ef220fcf9d47c0414b6e4ab42c51142f9a702e28aaa571d81a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-582":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Chi-square\",\"Uniform\",\"Standard normal\",\"F\"],\"stem\":\"As the degrees of freedom go to infinity, what distribution does Student’s t-distribution become?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"As degrees of freedom approach infinity, Student''s t-distribution converges to the standard normal distribution. [VI.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Chi-square","Uniform","Standard normal","F"],"stem":"As the degrees of freedom go to infinity, what distribution does Student’s t-distribution become?","sub":"quant","why":"As degrees of freedom approach infinity, Student''s t-distribution converges to the standard normal distribution. [VI.C.1]"},"revision":"07f317771d14c08cf25923f17a2551707b30c50f3cfd4cdacda81ae7a205181e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-583":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Reject H ; conclude that the two means are not statistically significantly different.\",\"Reject H ; conclude that the two means are statistically significantly different.\",\"Do not reject H ; conclude that the two means are not statistically significantly different.\",\"Do not reject H ; conclude that the two means are statistically significantly different.\"],\"stem\":\"Two methods for filling a bottle are being compared at a factory. The two methods have known standard deviations σ = 0.05 oz. and σ = 0.04 oz., 1 2 respectively. A random sample of n = 24 bottles using method 1 and an independent random sample of n = 25 bottles using method 2 are collected. The mean volume using method 1 is 15.95 oz., and the mean volume using method 2 is 16.02 oz. Test the hypothesis that the two methods have different mean volumes (use α = 0.10). What is your conclusion?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"For H0: μ1-μ2=0 vs Ha: μ1-μ2≠0 (α=0.10, two-tailed, zα/2=1.645): z0 = (15.95-16.02)/√(0.05²/24+0.04²/25) = -5.398, which is less than -1.645, so H0 is rejected: there is a significant difference in mean fill volume between the two methods. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Reject H ; conclude that the two means are not statistically significantly different.","Reject H ; conclude that the two means are statistically significantly different.","Do not reject H ; conclude that the two means are not statistically significantly different.","Do not reject H ; conclude that the two means are statistically significantly different."],"stem":"Two methods for filling a bottle are being compared at a factory. The two methods have known standard deviations σ = 0.05 oz. and σ = 0.04 oz., 1 2 respectively. A random sample of n = 24 bottles using method 1 and an independent random sample of n = 25 bottles using method 2 are collected. The mean volume using method 1 is 15.95 oz., and the mean volume using method 2 is 16.02 oz. Test the hypothesis that the two methods have different mean volumes (use α = 0.10). What is your conclusion?","sub":"quant","why":"For H0: μ1-μ2=0 vs Ha: μ1-μ2≠0 (α=0.10, two-tailed, zα/2=1.645): z0 = (15.95-16.02)/√(0.05²/24+0.04²/25) = -5.398, which is less than -1.645, so H0 is rejected: there is a significant difference in mean fill volume between the two methods. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"27a1d65192e8e6261ac7e5bf08f43c3f5d956e581ebbda9c12d0ca86b724e78d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-584":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Age\",\"Gender\",\"Number of members in the household\",\"Hair color\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following characteristics of a company’s customers is considered continuous data?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Continuous data can take any value over an interval; age can be measured continuously, while gender and hair color aren''t numeric and household size is discrete (whole numbers only). [VI.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Age","Gender","Number of members in the household","Hair color"],"stem":"Which of the following characteristics of a company’s customers is considered continuous data?","sub":"quant","why":"Continuous data can take any value over an interval; age can be measured continuously, while gender and hair color aren''t numeric and household size is discrete (whole numbers only). [VI.A.1]"},"revision":"937afefa9316095d50d9fccdac8c05489c5127c670647fee56e4edb98b2e2a05"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-585":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Normal probability plot of residuals\",\"Scatterplot of residuals versus predicted values\",\"Scatterplot of predicted versus actual values\",\"Run chart\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following graphical methods will assess the assumption of normality in the analysis of a designed experiment?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"A normal probability plot of the residuals assesses the normality assumption; the residuals should fall approximately along a straight line if the assumption holds. [VI.H.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Normal probability plot of residuals","Scatterplot of residuals versus predicted values","Scatterplot of predicted versus actual values","Run chart"],"stem":"Which of the following graphical methods will assess the assumption of normality in the analysis of a designed experiment?","sub":"quant","why":"A normal probability plot of the residuals assesses the normality assumption; the residuals should fall approximately along a straight line if the assumption holds. [VI.H.4]"},"revision":"3f54c8f5ba58c43e066ea6b2294d960e48c86d25f8170e576a22d4a5b47f90a5"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-586":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0\",\"0.27\",\"0.5771\",\"0.7597\"],\"stem\":\"Suppose the number of errors in an invoice at a company has the probability mass function (written in table form) shown below. What is the variance of the number of errors? Number of errors X 0 1 2 3 4 Probability 0.86 0.06 0.04 0.03 0.01\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"E(X) = Σx·f(x) = 0(0.86)+1(0.06)+2(0.04)+3(0.03)+4(0.01) = 0.27. Var(X) = Σ(x-μ)²f(x) = 0.5771. [VI.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0","0.27","0.5771","0.7597"],"stem":"Suppose the number of errors in an invoice at a company has the probability mass function (written in table form) shown below. What is the variance of the number of errors? Number of errors X 0 1 2 3 4 Probability 0.86 0.06 0.04 0.03 0.01","sub":"quant","why":"E(X) = Σx·f(x) = 0(0.86)+1(0.06)+2(0.04)+3(0.03)+4(0.01) = 0.27. Var(X) = Σ(x-μ)²f(x) = 0.5771. [VI.C.2]"},"revision":"e5981dcae1421e063a3f36be3dcf1d50848d2dc8f48e8c4360794e3d85a1e43c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-587":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"The errors e are exponentially distributed with mean zero. i\",\"The errors e are independent. i\",\"The errors e are dependent. i\",\"The errors e have differences in variance. i\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is an assumption required for use and interpretation in regression analysis?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The least-squares regression assumptions are that the errors are independent, normally distributed with mean 0, and have constant variance; these can be checked via residual analysis. [VI.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["The errors e are exponentially distributed with mean zero. i","The errors e are independent. i","The errors e are dependent. i","The errors e have differences in variance. i"],"stem":"Which of the following is an assumption required for use and interpretation in regression analysis?","sub":"quant","why":"The least-squares regression assumptions are that the errors are independent, normally distributed with mean 0, and have constant variance; these can be checked via residual analysis. [VI.E.1]"},"revision":"59e0cd7c3158acfee5886897ffe3f5a89aac50b59e35ba1ba6b8924f908974ca"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-588":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"1.88\",\"3.54\",\"17.35\",\"97\"],\"stem\":\"A sample of size 24 is drawn from a population that follows a normal distribution with mean 97 and variance 85. What is the standard error of the sample mean?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With σ²=85, n=24: standard error = σ/√n = √85/√24 ≈ 1.88. [VI.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["1.88","3.54","17.35","97"],"stem":"A sample of size 24 is drawn from a population that follows a normal distribution with mean 97 and variance 85. What is the standard error of the sample mean?","sub":"quant","why":"With σ²=85, n=24: standard error = σ/√n = √85/√24 ≈ 1.88. [VI.D.1]"},"revision":"cd5b17cf69f20d48f679663a691a1ce8f4d01f898aa766d01b2ac3434784d796"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-589":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Poisson.\",\"binomial.\",\"hypergeometric.\",\"normal.\"],\"stem\":\"The distribution on which c-charts are based is called:\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"c-charts and u-charts are based on the Poisson distribution. [VI.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Poisson.","binomial.","hypergeometric.","normal."],"stem":"The distribution on which c-charts are based is called:","sub":"quant","why":"c-charts and u-charts are based on the Poisson distribution. [VI.F.5]"},"revision":"b4b1f9b0f356cc62a83709038f95ae431c343f8dac67576e4fed2c2b04f99131"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-59":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"They explain sampling procedures.\",\"They identify bottlenecks in a process.\",\"They help reduce process errors.\",\"They help to manage quality costs.\"],\"stem\":\"Why are work instructions an important component of process control?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Work instructions provide details of a process for those who operate the process. Providing detailed instructions helps eliminate errors by providing all required machine settings, setup instructions, and visual aids. [IV.A]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["They explain sampling procedures.","They identify bottlenecks in a process.","They help reduce process errors.","They help to manage quality costs."],"stem":"Why are work instructions an important component of process control?","sub":"ppc","why":"Work instructions provide details of a process for those who operate the process. Providing detailed instructions helps eliminate errors by providing all required machine settings, setup instructions, and visual aids. [IV.A]"},"revision":"84ac6f2438d1b16da144e56eb289f3daf72536e561969b7c51e968dfd0a05e16"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-590":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Scatter test\",\"Matrix test\",\"Normality test\",\"Pareto test\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following tests should be done before performing data analysis?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Before performing data analysis, a normality test should be conducted, along with checks for unusual observations or missing data, since data collected automatically or manually often needs cleaning first.\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Scatter test","Matrix test","Normality test","Pareto test"],"stem":"Which of the following tests should be done before performing data analysis?","sub":"quant","why":"Before performing data analysis, a normality test should be conducted, along with checks for unusual observations or missing data, since data collected automatically or manually often needs cleaning first."},"revision":"951ae2fd428268c468cdbc7ee28f2302f1e0b047cf7e99b9e78be42f8361aff3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-591":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.0034\",\"0.0236\",\"0.9764\",\"0.9966\"],\"stem\":\"A dimension of a component has specifications of 10.80 ± 2.50. The process data are normally distributed with mean 10.45 and standard deviation 1.05. What fraction of components will have this dimension within the specification limits?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With USL=13.30, LSL=8.30, μ=10.45, σ=1.05: P(8.30<X<13.30) = P(-2.05<Z<2.71) = 0.9966-0.0202 = 0.9764, so about 97.64% of components fall within specification. [VI.G.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.0034","0.0236","0.9764","0.9966"],"stem":"A dimension of a component has specifications of 10.80 ± 2.50. The process data are normally distributed with mean 10.45 and standard deviation 1.05. What fraction of components will have this dimension within the specification limits?","sub":"quant","why":"With USL=13.30, LSL=8.30, μ=10.45, σ=1.05: P(8.30<X<13.30) = P(-2.05<Z<2.71) = 0.9966-0.0202 = 0.9764, so about 97.64% of components fall within specification. [VI.G.3]"},"revision":"3db5fbb2c3c02da2ac6031df351a536a8cf481a9b95b8119dbac28fe71104b10"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-592":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"There is a significant interaction effect between factors A and B.\",\"Factor B has a significant effect on the response y.\",\"Factor A has a significant effect on the response y.\",\"Both factors A and B have a significant effect on the response y.\"],\"stem\":\"A two-way ANOVA hypothesis test was used to test the effects of two factors on a response variable y. What conclusions can be drawn from the ANOVA table below? Source df SS MS F p-value A 2 1918.50 959.250 9.25 0.015 B 1 21.33 21.333 0.21 0.666 AB 2 561.17 280.583 2.71 0.145 Error 6 622.00 103.667 Total 11 3123.00\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"From the ANOVA table, factor A''s p-value (0.015) is below α=0.05, so factor A has a significant effect on the response; factor B''s p-value (0.666) and the AB interaction''s p-value (0.145) are both above α, so neither has a significant effect. [VI.D.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["There is a significant interaction effect between factors A and B.","Factor B has a significant effect on the response y.","Factor A has a significant effect on the response y.","Both factors A and B have a significant effect on the response y."],"stem":"A two-way ANOVA hypothesis test was used to test the effects of two factors on a response variable y. What conclusions can be drawn from the ANOVA table below? Source df SS MS F p-value A 2 1918.50 959.250 9.25 0.015 B 1 21.33 21.333 0.21 0.666 AB 2 561.17 280.583 2.71 0.145 Error 6 622.00 103.667 Total 11 3123.00","sub":"quant","why":"From the ANOVA table, factor A''s p-value (0.015) is below α=0.05, so factor A has a significant effect on the response; factor B''s p-value (0.666) and the AB interaction''s p-value (0.145) are both above α, so neither has a significant effect. [VI.D.5]"},"revision":"fb73daccc636807d5f671d7c9ebd71da8d037d679e004d9a00ffc5a787239dd4"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-593":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"–0.96\",\"0.92\",\"0.96\",\"21.74\"],\"stem\":\"Paired data collected from a process are: (5.2, 26.7), (6.1, 27.5), (3.2, 24.9), (4.6, 25.5). What is the correlation coefficient for these data?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Using Sxx=4.4475, Sxy=4.105, Syy≈15.024: r = Sxy/√(Sxx×Syy) = 4.105/√(4.4475×15.024) ≈ 0.96, indicating a strong positive linear association. [VI.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["–0.96","0.92","0.96","21.74"],"stem":"Paired data collected from a process are: (5.2, 26.7), (6.1, 27.5), (3.2, 24.9), (4.6, 25.5). What is the correlation coefficient for these data?","sub":"quant","why":"Using Sxx=4.4475, Sxy=4.105, Syy≈15.024: r = Sxy/√(Sxx×Syy) = 4.105/√(4.4475×15.024) ≈ 0.96, indicating a strong positive linear association. [VI.E.2]"},"revision":"106d8d03b8ca905b638ec2d7e1ad082e6124b49e2d9d11064ff56e2f425883c9"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-594":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Mode\",\"Mean\",\"Median\",\"Standard deviation\"],\"stem\":\"A histogram of employee salaries at a company shows at least two extreme high-salary observations pulling the tail of the distribution to the right. What measure of central tendency would be most appropriate to describe these data?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The histogram shows at least two extreme observations, so a resistant measure like the median is more appropriate than the mean, which is heavily influenced by extreme values; standard deviation measures spread, not central tendency. [VI.A.6]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Mode","Mean","Median","Standard deviation"],"stem":"A histogram of employee salaries at a company shows at least two extreme high-salary observations pulling the tail of the distribution to the right. What measure of central tendency would be most appropriate to describe these data?","sub":"quant","why":"The histogram shows at least two extreme observations, so a resistant measure like the median is more appropriate than the mean, which is heavily influenced by extreme values; standard deviation measures spread, not central tendency. [VI.A.6]"},"revision":"80d6423587a3ab755707ac377f18bd0d0fd99174fe65458ab3a3b7c5f0f902e5"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-595":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Within-sample variation is maximized.\",\"Between-sample variation is minimized.\",\"Rational subgrouping is important when constructing control charts.\",\"With rational subgroups, there is a smaller probability of variation between successive samples.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following statements about rational subgroups is correct?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Rational subgrouping selects samples so that within-sample variation is minimized while between-sample variation (where special causes are more likely to appear) is maximized; this selection is important when constructing control charts. [VI.F.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Within-sample variation is maximized.","Between-sample variation is minimized.","Rational subgrouping is important when constructing control charts.","With rational subgroups, there is a smaller probability of variation between successive samples."],"stem":"Which of the following statements about rational subgroups is correct?","sub":"quant","why":"Rational subgrouping selects samples so that within-sample variation is minimized while between-sample variation (where special causes are more likely to appear) is maximized; this selection is important when constructing control charts. [VI.F.4]"},"revision":"9173e38373179c58e255d0ff0652cdb15a9f320aef5adec9cd912a5550f282ee"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-596":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"–3.266\",\"±1.96\",\"–±2.069\",\"2.069\"],\"stem\":\"A bottling factory claims that the volume of soda filled in bottles is 18 oz. You want to test whether the true mean volume of soda is different than 18 oz. The volume of a random sample of 24 bottles had a mean of 17.98 oz., with a standard deviation of 0.03 oz. Using a significance level of 5%, what is the critical value for this hypothesis test?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"For a one-sample t-test (H0: μ=18, Ha: μ≠18) with n=24, df=23, α=0.05: the critical values are ±t(0.025,23)=±2.069, and the test statistic t0 = (17.98-18)/(0.03/√24) = -3.266. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["–3.266","±1.96","–±2.069","2.069"],"stem":"A bottling factory claims that the volume of soda filled in bottles is 18 oz. You want to test whether the true mean volume of soda is different than 18 oz. The volume of a random sample of 24 bottles had a mean of 17.98 oz., with a standard deviation of 0.03 oz. Using a significance level of 5%, what is the critical value for this hypothesis test?","sub":"quant","why":"For a one-sample t-test (H0: μ=18, Ha: μ≠18) with n=24, df=23, α=0.05: the critical values are ±t(0.025,23)=±2.069, and the test statistic t0 = (17.98-18)/(0.03/√24) = -3.266. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"7219e02689ee395d542a590052ea6ea257d69db83bed5742ccfe896fd488e6e7"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-597":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"(–0.197, 0.317)\",\"(–0.254, 0.374)\",\"(–0.367, 0.487)\",\"(–0.404, 0.524)\"],\"stem\":\"The length of five components was measured by two inspectors (data shown below). Assuming the data follow a normal distribution, find a 95% confidence interval for the difference in the mean length measured by the two inspectors. Component 1 2 3 4 5 Inspector A 10.2 9.8 10.1 10.3 10.4 Inspector B 10.0 9.9 10.3 10.0 10.3\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Since the same components were measured by both inspectors, this is paired data (d=A-B): d̄=0.06, sd≈0.2074 (n=5). A 95% CI using t(0.025,4)=2.776 gives d̄ ± t·sd/√n = (-0.197, 0.317), which contains 0 (consistent with no significant difference). [VI.D.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["(–0.197, 0.317)","(–0.254, 0.374)","(–0.367, 0.487)","(–0.404, 0.524)"],"stem":"The length of five components was measured by two inspectors (data shown below). Assuming the data follow a normal distribution, find a 95% confidence interval for the difference in the mean length measured by the two inspectors. Component 1 2 3 4 5 Inspector A 10.2 9.8 10.1 10.3 10.4 Inspector B 10.0 9.9 10.3 10.0 10.3","sub":"quant","why":"Since the same components were measured by both inspectors, this is paired data (d=A-B): d̄=0.06, sd≈0.2074 (n=5). A 95% CI using t(0.025,4)=2.776 gives d̄ ± t·sd/√n = (-0.197, 0.317), which contains 0 (consistent with no significant difference). [VI.D.3]"},"revision":"bca251f29b8d22122de21a93f7e757b7025a5f000c7465ff82dd8026c70bdd1e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-598":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0\",\"0.2865\",\"0.7135\",\"3.4903\"],\"stem\":\"The time between arrivals at a store has an exponential distribution with mean four minutes. What is the probability that the time between arrivals is more than five minutes?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With mean μ=4 minutes, λ=1/4=0.25. P(T>5) = 1-F(5) = e^-λ(5) = e^-1.25 = 0.2865. [VI.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0","0.2865","0.7135","3.4903"],"stem":"The time between arrivals at a store has an exponential distribution with mean four minutes. What is the probability that the time between arrivals is more than five minutes?","sub":"quant","why":"With mean μ=4 minutes, λ=1/4=0.25. P(T>5) = 1-F(5) = e^-λ(5) = e^-1.25 = 0.2865. [VI.C.1]"},"revision":"0e379ff0e12b75f8899db11de8527481549acb5615c94a6101a8d3c957d9889a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-599":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"4, 5\",\"5, 4\",\"6, 5\",\"20, 29\"],\"stem\":\"Consider the ANOVA table below for a one-factor experiment with a blocking variable. How many levels are there of factor A and the blocking factor? Source of variation df SS MS F Factor A 5 198.0 49.5 12.05 Block 4 101.75 33.92 Error 20 79.25 6.60 Total 29 379.00\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The numerator degrees of freedom for factor A equals a-1, where a is the number of levels; since df=5, there are 6 levels of factor A. Similarly, the blocking factor''s df=4 gives 5 levels. [VI.H.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["4, 5","5, 4","6, 5","20, 29"],"stem":"Consider the ANOVA table below for a one-factor experiment with a blocking variable. How many levels are there of factor A and the blocking factor? Source of variation df SS MS F Factor A 5 198.0 49.5 12.05 Block 4 101.75 33.92 Error 20 79.25 6.60 Total 29 379.00","sub":"quant","why":"The numerator degrees of freedom for factor A equals a-1, where a is the number of levels; since df=5, there are 6 levels of factor A. Similarly, the blocking factor''s df=4 gives 5 levels. [VI.H.4]"},"revision":"55e54fb055505b94759491e9be4694ff4bf15891bcfc6815abcaff78695e8559"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-6":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Have modern methods of training on the job been instituted?\",\"Have only those people who have experience in a particular job position been hired?\",\"Are the goals and objectives aligned throughout the organization?\",\"Have unclear slogans been eliminated?\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following would be considered an effectiveness test for strategic planning?\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"a and b are two of Deming’s fourteen points, while d does not apply, since Deming wanted to eliminate slogans. Goals and objectives aligned throughout the organization are necessary to carry out the strategies to be put in place. [I.B.1] 7 . c; Goals and objectives should be “specific, measurable, achievable, realistic, and time-based” to be effective throughout the enterprise. [I.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Have modern methods of training on the job been instituted?","Have only those people who have experience in a particular job position been hired?","Are the goals and objectives aligned throughout the organization?","Have unclear slogans been eliminated?"],"stem":"Which of the following would be considered an effectiveness test for strategic planning?","sub":"mgmt","why":"a and b are two of Deming’s fourteen points, while d does not apply, since Deming wanted to eliminate slogans. Goals and objectives aligned throughout the organization are necessary to carry out the strategies to be put in place. [I.B.1] 7 . c; Goals and objectives should be “specific, measurable, achievable, realistic, and time-based” to be effective throughout the enterprise. [I.B.1]"},"revision":"50b51bac9674ddff15ffaab178bcff5e837e5a9c4427190f72ff7fb9b4be591c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-60":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"To help manufacturers reduce risk\",\"To save money\",\"To help ensure product reproducibility\",\"To ensure management support\"],\"stem\":\"Why is it important to identify materials used in producing a product?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Knowing where materials come from also gives the manufacturers the ability to trace the product. This helps to reduce exposure to additional risks. [IV.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["To help manufacturers reduce risk","To save money","To help ensure product reproducibility","To ensure management support"],"stem":"Why is it important to identify materials used in producing a product?","sub":"ppc","why":"Knowing where materials come from also gives the manufacturers the ability to trace the product. This helps to reduce exposure to additional risks. [IV.B.1]"},"revision":"be5a9424c367b160b47351a59b99b09424db9e45651a3e155ad4529ed6c1dbf4"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-600":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"1.928\",\"2.112\",\"3.716\",\"4.459\"],\"stem\":\"Calculate the sample variance of the following observations: 5.3, 8.6, 7.2, 9.1, 7.5, 3.5.\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Sample mean x̄ = (5.3+8.6+7.2+9.1+7.5+3.5)/6 = 6.8667. Sample variance s² = Σ(xi-x̄)²/(n-1) = 22.2933/5 = 4.459. [VI.A.6]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["1.928","2.112","3.716","4.459"],"stem":"Calculate the sample variance of the following observations: 5.3, 8.6, 7.2, 9.1, 7.5, 3.5.","sub":"quant","why":"Sample mean x̄ = (5.3+8.6+7.2+9.1+7.5+3.5)/6 = 6.8667. Sample variance s² = Σ(xi-x̄)²/(n-1) = 22.2933/5 = 4.459. [VI.A.6]"},"revision":"aa7e7528e47f6cc8469fd9cd68e7d915983d284fc71b0bd4603d4bb2d7be3f9b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-601":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"LCL = 0, UCL = 3.29\",\"LCL = 0, UCL = 4.65\",\"LCL = 0, UCL = 5.72\",\"LCL = 13.25, UCL = 22.55\"],\"stem\":\"The wait times of thirty consecutive patients at a clinic were recorded. The team determined that X̄ = 17.90 and MR̄ = 1.75. What are the upper and lower control limits for the moving range chart?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"For a moving range chart with n=2, using D3=0 and D4=3.267: UCL = D4×MR̄ = 3.267(1.75) = 5.72, LCL = D3×MR̄ = 0. [VI.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["LCL = 0, UCL = 3.29","LCL = 0, UCL = 4.65","LCL = 0, UCL = 5.72","LCL = 13.25, UCL = 22.55"],"stem":"The wait times of thirty consecutive patients at a clinic were recorded. The team determined that X̄ = 17.90 and MR̄ = 1.75. What are the upper and lower control limits for the moving range chart?","sub":"quant","why":"For a moving range chart with n=2, using D3=0 and D4=3.267: UCL = D4×MR̄ = 3.267(1.75) = 5.72, LCL = D3×MR̄ = 0. [VI.F.5]"},"revision":"be273f4479fa3469fdc9863e449d30c0f22dfc09f53232706348de2331641756"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-602":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Trend analysis\",\"Goodness-of-fit test\",\"Chi-square test\",\"Designed experiment\"],\"stem\":\"A team has collected measurement data from a machine at a factory. To determine whether the data are autocorrelated, which of the following methods could the team use?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Autocorrelation can be detected using methods such as trend analysis or moving-average smoothing. [VI.E.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Trend analysis","Goodness-of-fit test","Chi-square test","Designed experiment"],"stem":"A team has collected measurement data from a machine at a factory. To determine whether the data are autocorrelated, which of the following methods could the team use?","sub":"quant","why":"Autocorrelation can be detected using methods such as trend analysis or moving-average smoothing. [VI.E.3]"},"revision":"2f1b0571d98da3a875fa50c5d20f12181e8f73183c26c6c233ecfca74106458b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-603":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Dull tools\",\"Large change in raw materials\",\"Slight variation in ambient humidity\",\"Broken machine\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following events is a source of common cause variation?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"Dull tools, a large change in raw materials, and a broken machine are all special causes of variation (unusual, correctable events); slight variation in ambient humidity is a natural, uncontrollable part of the process and is common cause variation. [VI.F.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Dull tools","Large change in raw materials","Slight variation in ambient humidity","Broken machine"],"stem":"Which of the following events is a source of common cause variation?","sub":"quant","why":"Dull tools, a large change in raw materials, and a broken machine are all special causes of variation (unusual, correctable events); slight variation in ambient humidity is a natural, uncontrollable part of the process and is common cause variation. [VI.F.2]"},"revision":"d62edf5d12b1d975fd54e042a85bd4b6d025b8c433fe64ffd006c048df8ddc03"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-604":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"cl\":28.15,\"data\":[27.4,28.9,27.6,28.3,34,27.8,28.6,27.2,28.9],\"lcl\":23.71,\"title\":\"X̄ chart\",\"type\":\"control-single\",\"ucl\":32.59},\"options\":[\"The process is out of control.\",\"The process is stable.\",\"The process is operating within the specifications.\",\"The process is operating outside the specifications.\"],\"stem\":\"Consider an X̄ chart of a normally distributed process with UCL = 32.59, X̄̄ = 28.15, and LCL = 23.71. The fifth data point falls above the upper control limit. What conclusions can be drawn from this control chart?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The fifth data point falls above the upper control limit. A sensitizing rule for out-of-control behavior is any single point more than 3 standard deviations from the centerline, so the process is out of control; a control chart says nothing about whether it meets specification limits. [VI.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"cl":28.15,"data":[27.4,28.9,27.6,28.3,34,27.8,28.6,27.2,28.9],"lcl":23.71,"title":"X̄ chart","type":"control-single","ucl":32.59},"options":["The process is out of control.","The process is stable.","The process is operating within the specifications.","The process is operating outside the specifications."],"stem":"Consider an X̄ chart of a normally distributed process with UCL = 32.59, X̄̄ = 28.15, and LCL = 23.71. The fifth data point falls above the upper control limit. What conclusions can be drawn from this control chart?","sub":"quant","why":"The fifth data point falls above the upper control limit. A sensitizing rule for out-of-control behavior is any single point more than 3 standard deviations from the centerline, so the process is out of control; a control chart says nothing about whether it meets specification limits. [VI.F.6]"},"revision":"8462ff8c1abc28dd5671e2e06ce6d53064dc04a571598cf6eb0a79119ef918e1"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-605":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Normal\",\"Binomial\",\"χ2\",\"F\"],\"stem\":\"The test statistic for a hypothesis test for two population variances follows which distribution under the null hypothesis?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The test statistic for a hypothesis test comparing two population variances, F0 = s1²/s2², follows an F-distribution with k1=n1-1 and k2=n2-1 degrees of freedom under the null hypothesis. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Normal","Binomial","χ2","F"],"stem":"The test statistic for a hypothesis test for two population variances follows which distribution under the null hypothesis?","sub":"quant","why":"The test statistic for a hypothesis test comparing two population variances, F0 = s1²/s2², follows an F-distribution with k1=n1-1 and k2=n2-1 degrees of freedom under the null hypothesis. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"9d03da0929fe4facfffa66858b63ac9dc3116d1d531951348f84b3a365d9a6a9"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-606":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Yes, since C = 7.5 p\",\"Yes, since C = 25 pk\",\"No, since C = 0.83 pk\",\"No, since C = 25 pk\"],\"stem\":\"A stable, normally distributed process has specifications 15.50 ± 1.25. A sample of data from the process had a mean µ = 16.50 and standard deviation σ = 0.10. Is the process considered capable?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"With USL=16.75, LSL=14.25, μ=16.50, σ=0.10: since the process is not centered, Cpk = min[(USL-μ)/3σ, (μ-LSL)/3σ] = min(0.83, 7.5) = 0.83, which is less than 1, so the process is not capable. [VI.G.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Yes, since C = 7.5 p","Yes, since C = 25 pk","No, since C = 0.83 pk","No, since C = 25 pk"],"stem":"A stable, normally distributed process has specifications 15.50 ± 1.25. A sample of data from the process had a mean µ = 16.50 and standard deviation σ = 0.10. Is the process considered capable?","sub":"quant","why":"With USL=16.75, LSL=14.25, μ=16.50, σ=0.10: since the process is not centered, Cpk = min[(USL-μ)/3σ, (μ-LSL)/3σ] = min(0.83, 7.5) = 0.83, which is less than 1, so the process is not capable. [VI.G.3]"},"revision":"36ba4bee677eda97cccc4f187c3ace424a4518ee3697ca07cf5efa06600adbdb"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-607":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"1.74\",\"3.04\",\"9.24\",\"20.91\"],\"stem\":\"The standard deviation of a Poisson distribution is 3.04. What is the mean of this distribution?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"For a Poisson distribution, μ = σ². Given σ=3.04, μ = σ² = 3.04² = 9.24. [VI.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["1.74","3.04","9.24","20.91"],"stem":"The standard deviation of a Poisson distribution is 3.04. What is the mean of this distribution?","sub":"quant","why":"For a Poisson distribution, μ = σ². Given σ=3.04, μ = σ² = 3.04² = 9.24. [VI.C.2]"},"revision":"040fdbe685d48a7cb2c3466908f37bf16f3fe3742925f9d4e23b8b6283608641"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-608":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Kaizen.\",\"solution.\",\"control plan.\",\"problem.\"],\"stem\":\"An unknown cause, variable performance from a set standard, and deviation from perception are characteristics of a:\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Any identified problem must represent an opportunity for improvement: it must show variable performance from the established standard, be a deviation from fact or perception, and its cause must be unknown -- if the cause is already known, it isn''t a \\\"problem\\\" in this sense. [V.E]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Kaizen.","solution.","control plan.","problem."],"stem":"An unknown cause, variable performance from a set standard, and deviation from perception are characteristics of a:","sub":"ci","why":"Any identified problem must represent an opportunity for improvement: it must show variable performance from the established standard, be a deviation from fact or perception, and its cause must be unknown -- if the cause is already known, it isn''t a \"problem\" in this sense. [V.E]"},"revision":"4adc3b7eecffad6e99a51af24547fcfa65186467273b23bc668b14aaeb4deb75"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-609":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Double sampling\",\"Sequential sampling\",\"Multiple sampling\",\"Simple random sampling\"],\"stem\":\"A team is doing a project on errors in customers’ files. What type of sampling is appropriate for this project?\",\"sub\":\"quant\",\"why\":\"The population here has no natural subgroups, so stratified sampling isn''t needed; simple random sampling gives each customer record an equal probability of selection. Double sampling and sequential sampling are acceptance-sampling methods and don''t apply here. [VI.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Double sampling","Sequential sampling","Multiple sampling","Simple random sampling"],"stem":"A team is doing a project on errors in customers’ files. What type of sampling is appropriate for this project?","sub":"quant","why":"The population here has no natural subgroups, so stratified sampling isn''t needed; simple random sampling gives each customer record an equal probability of selection. Double sampling and sequential sampling are acceptance-sampling methods and don''t apply here. [VI.A.4]"},"revision":"2db5ea795f5b62de3ef97f4b4e6be261c4fdb6f3f0d816ec1c472384d7a0b229"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-61":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Control charts\",\"OC curves\",\"Paper move tags\",\"Radio frequency identification\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following is one of the most effective methods of accounting for and tracking product materials?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Radio-frequency identification (RFID) is a common method used to identify and track materials using barcodes, scanners, and RFID receivers. RFID tags allow manufacturers to maintain detailed information about materials as well. [IV.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Control charts","OC curves","Paper move tags","Radio frequency identification"],"stem":"Which of the following is one of the most effective methods of accounting for and tracking product materials?","sub":"ppc","why":"Radio-frequency identification (RFID) is a common method used to identify and track materials using barcodes, scanners, and RFID receivers. RFID tags allow manufacturers to maintain detailed information about materials as well. [IV.B.1]"},"revision":"453e1ba23c785ff8f1525ae6f13e6ecd16518f511eddc84306dc825f4ae0ffd9"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-610":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"No, since cycle time ≤ takt time.\",\"Yes, since cycle time ≤ takt time.\",\"No, since cycle time > takt time.\",\"Yes, since cycle time > takt time.\"],\"stem\":\"A supplier has received an order from one of their customers. They must complete an order of 750 items in a daytime shift that is ten hours long. The cycle time for this process is forty seconds. Without changing their current production methods, will the supplier be able to meet the customer’s demands?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"The takt time is 10×60×60/750 = 48 seconds per item. Since the cycle time (40 seconds) is less than the takt time, the supplier is likely to meet customer demand without changing their current production methods. [V.D]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["No, since cycle time ≤ takt time.","Yes, since cycle time ≤ takt time.","No, since cycle time > takt time.","Yes, since cycle time > takt time."],"stem":"A supplier has received an order from one of their customers. They must complete an order of 750 items in a daytime shift that is ten hours long. The cycle time for this process is forty seconds. Without changing their current production methods, will the supplier be able to meet the customer’s demands?","sub":"ci","why":"The takt time is 10×60×60/750 = 48 seconds per item. Since the cycle time (40 seconds) is less than the takt time, the supplier is likely to meet customer demand without changing their current production methods. [V.D]"},"revision":"5193e7a1bc9805c69811e7696239f439ff41af7c5b8f70b1d3a5a503aa26bc23"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-611":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"cells\":[[\"medium\",\"medium\",\"high\",\"high\",\"high\"],[\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\",\"high\",\"high\"],[\"low\",\"medium\",\"medium\",\"high\",\"high\"],[\"low\",\"low\",\"medium\",\"medium\",\"medium\"],[\"low\",\"low\",\"low\",\"low\",\"low\"]],\"colAxis\":\"Impact Level\",\"cols\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\",\"5\"],\"highlights\":[{\"col\":3,\"label\":\"Before\",\"row\":0},{\"col\":0,\"label\":\"After\",\"row\":4}],\"rowAxis\":\"Probability Level\",\"rows\":[\"5\",\"4\",\"3\",\"2\",\"1\"],\"type\":\"risk-matrix\"},\"options\":[\"residual risk.\",\"inherent risk.\",\"secondary risk.\",\"material risk.\"],\"stem\":\"Until the late 1990s, piston engines had a rubber timing belt that synchronized the rotation of the crankshaft and camshaft, ensuring the engine valves opened and closed at the correct time relative to the pistons. A large risk was breakage of the rubber belt, which could damage the valves and the engine itself. In the 2000s, the rubber belt was replaced by a metal chain with minimal wear. Plotted on a 5x5 risk matrix (Probability Level 1-5 vs. Impact Level 1-5), the change moved the risk from High (rubber timing belt) to Low (metal timing chain), after the mitigating change from rubber to metal. The new risk remaining after this risk mitigation is called a:\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"After a risk undergoes treatment, the remaining risk is residual risk. Inherent risk is the original, untreated risk; secondary risks arise from a treatment itself; material risks are specific to the material used. [VII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"cells":[["medium","medium","high","high","high"],["low","medium","high","high","high"],["low","medium","medium","high","high"],["low","low","medium","medium","medium"],["low","low","low","low","low"]],"colAxis":"Impact Level","cols":["1","2","3","4","5"],"highlights":[{"col":3,"label":"Before","row":0},{"col":0,"label":"After","row":4}],"rowAxis":"Probability Level","rows":["5","4","3","2","1"],"type":"risk-matrix"},"options":["residual risk.","inherent risk.","secondary risk.","material risk."],"stem":"Until the late 1990s, piston engines had a rubber timing belt that synchronized the rotation of the crankshaft and camshaft, ensuring the engine valves opened and closed at the correct time relative to the pistons. A large risk was breakage of the rubber belt, which could damage the valves and the engine itself. In the 2000s, the rubber belt was replaced by a metal chain with minimal wear. Plotted on a 5x5 risk matrix (Probability Level 1-5 vs. Impact Level 1-5), the change moved the risk from High (rubber timing belt) to Low (metal timing chain), after the mitigating change from rubber to metal. The new risk remaining after this risk mitigation is called a:","sub":"risk","why":"After a risk undergoes treatment, the remaining risk is residual risk. Inherent risk is the original, untreated risk; secondary risks arise from a treatment itself; material risks are specific to the material used. [VII.A.1]"},"revision":"86afa2f8981fc1d2387cd39cabf90987ec822616ad3533fd42853a812956f061"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-612":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"prevention costs.\",\"appraisal costs.\",\"internal failure costs.\",\"external failure costs.\"],\"stem\":\"A company has spare parts that need to be evaluated. This would be an example of:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Costs associated with evaluating spare parts fall under appraisal costs. [II.E]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["prevention costs.","appraisal costs.","internal failure costs.","external failure costs."],"stem":"A company has spare parts that need to be evaluated. This would be an example of:","sub":"mgmt","why":"Costs associated with evaluating spare parts fall under appraisal costs. [II.E]"},"revision":"abde16eebb7cbafc7a940119f9c4bb919a2749cec1da971f3c39a47444fd4a1a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-613":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"flowchart.\",\"QFD.\",\"FMEA.\",\"expert opinion.\"],\"stem\":\"A risk priority number is an output from a(n):\",\"sub\":\"risk\",\"why\":\"The risk priority number (RPN) is a numerical output of an FMEA, calculated by multiplying severity, occurrence, and detection ratings. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["flowchart.","QFD.","FMEA.","expert opinion."],"stem":"A risk priority number is an output from a(n):","sub":"risk","why":"The risk priority number (RPN) is a numerical output of an FMEA, calculated by multiplying severity, occurrence, and detection ratings. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"6ea23f69ab7c32f787162d7dd6c6678f6afead2f05a9d198ea3d94b1e1f2034d"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-62":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Before the release of the cookie to the public\",\"After any inquiries from customers\",\"At the beginning of the cookie’s development\",\"Only when there is an increase in nonconforming cookies\"],\"stem\":\"A food company is planning to release a new type of cookie. At what stage in the process should the company consider tracing the cookie’s components?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Product identification and traceability should begin when the process is being designed. Including traceability from the start will ensure adequate quality of the cookie and help reduce recall risks. [IV.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Before the release of the cookie to the public","After any inquiries from customers","At the beginning of the cookie’s development","Only when there is an increase in nonconforming cookies"],"stem":"A food company is planning to release a new type of cookie. At what stage in the process should the company consider tracing the cookie’s components?","sub":"ppc","why":"Product identification and traceability should begin when the process is being designed. Including traceability from the start will ensure adequate quality of the cookie and help reduce recall risks. [IV.B.1]"},"revision":"a4b2e73e6f6d3286f9bc723782f838755a19c507874f08cf69c021536aa93e09"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-63":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"compliance.\",\"material segregation.\",\"material review.\",\"defect classification.\"],\"stem\":\"Keeping nonconforming products from being mixed with good/conforming products within a manufacturing plant is known as:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Material segregation ensures that nonconforming products are not mixed with conforming products. [IV.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["compliance.","material segregation.","material review.","defect classification."],"stem":"Keeping nonconforming products from being mixed with good/conforming products within a manufacturing plant is known as:","sub":"ppc","why":"Material segregation ensures that nonconforming products are not mixed with conforming products. [IV.B.2]"},"revision":"13c588ebe700e60418a612eb188a95b267119717a103def472d627dc10538cf8"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-64":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Minor defect\",\"Major defect\",\"Serious defect\",\"Critical defect\"],\"stem\":\"A recall due to a brake malfunction on an automobile was issued by a car company. How would the company classify this type of defect?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"A brake malfunction in a car could lead directly to a severe customer injury; therefore, this defect would be classified as critical. [IV.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Minor defect","Major defect","Serious defect","Critical defect"],"stem":"A recall due to a brake malfunction on an automobile was issued by a car company. How would the company classify this type of defect?","sub":"ppc","why":"A brake malfunction in a car could lead directly to a severe customer injury; therefore, this defect would be classified as critical. [IV.B.3]"},"revision":"3a102fdf5692a97372865a2dfcedb555861d539ce2223635a73845ff75d9d8ca"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-65":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Minor defect\",\"Major defect\",\"Serious defect\",\"Critical defect\"],\"stem\":\"Customers have complained about the battery life for a new line of computers released. How would the computer company classify this type of product defect?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"The poor battery life of the computer reduces the usability of the product, but will not likely lead to an injury or significant economic loss. Therefore, the company would classify this as a major defect. [IV.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Minor defect","Major defect","Serious defect","Critical defect"],"stem":"Customers have complained about the battery life for a new line of computers released. How would the computer company classify this type of product defect?","sub":"ppc","why":"The poor battery life of the computer reduces the usability of the product, but will not likely lead to an injury or significant economic loss. Therefore, the company would classify this as a major defect. [IV.B.3]"},"revision":"9c7109789f535ee3095ec569006af652ba31e67434b31c06154f09898914edd9"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-66":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Oversee the traceability of a product\",\"Develop sampling plans for product materials\",\"Identify the source of all product components\",\"Determine corrective action for nonconforming components\"],\"stem\":\"What purpose does the material review board of a company serve?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"The material review board (MRB) determines the appropriate corrective action to take in the case that nonconforming components are discovered. It also determines any additional actions to take to prevent similar nonconformities in the future. [IV.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Oversee the traceability of a product","Develop sampling plans for product materials","Identify the source of all product components","Determine corrective action for nonconforming components"],"stem":"What purpose does the material review board of a company serve?","sub":"ppc","why":"The material review board (MRB) determines the appropriate corrective action to take in the case that nonconforming components are discovered. It also determines any additional actions to take to prevent similar nonconformities in the future. [IV.B.4]"},"revision":"47c6294925707fddadfc28ef932a727bc75ce6d6bf6d17baf6698cd27bf20dac"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-67":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"the product is complex.\",\"the fraction of nonconforming products is unknown.\",\"there are high inspection costs.\",\"the cost of making a wrong decision is high.\"],\"stem\":\"Acceptance sampling is most appropriate for inspecting products when:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Acceptance sampling is most appropriate when the inspection costs are high or when destructive inspection is the only method of inspection. Acceptance sampling is also appropriate when the inspection of a product is monotonous and causes inspector fatigue, which could lead to an increase in inspection e r r o r s. [IV.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["the product is complex.","the fraction of nonconforming products is unknown.","there are high inspection costs.","the cost of making a wrong decision is high."],"stem":"Acceptance sampling is most appropriate for inspecting products when:","sub":"ppc","why":"Acceptance sampling is most appropriate when the inspection costs are high or when destructive inspection is the only method of inspection. Acceptance sampling is also appropriate when the inspection of a product is monotonous and causes inspector fatigue, which could lead to an increase in inspection e r r o r s. [IV.C.1]"},"revision":"444fa457ca536478d5d625428d0c62f8389444606dc24c86a95b656a2315d3bf"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-68":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"sampling has lower inspection costs.\",\"sampling is more time-intensive than 100% inspection.\",\"sampling emphasizes that the supplier is concerned with quality.\",\"rejecting a lot that conforms to specification as a result of variability in sampling.\"],\"stem\":\"A disadvantage of sampling inspection compared to 100% inspection is:\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Sampling inspection has several advantages as it saves time and energy compared to 100% inspection. However, there is variability inherent in sampling. Therefore, there are chances of making type I and type II errors: rejecting lots that conform to the quality specifications or accepting lots that do not conform to quality specifications. [IV.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["sampling has lower inspection costs.","sampling is more time-intensive than 100% inspection.","sampling emphasizes that the supplier is concerned with quality.","rejecting a lot that conforms to specification as a result of variability in sampling."],"stem":"A disadvantage of sampling inspection compared to 100% inspection is:","sub":"ppc","why":"Sampling inspection has several advantages as it saves time and energy compared to 100% inspection. However, there is variability inherent in sampling. Therefore, there are chances of making type I and type II errors: rejecting lots that conform to the quality specifications or accepting lots that do not conform to quality specifications. [IV.C.1]"},"revision":"dc29710db815555fef2aee205c01457419021d48b13265895ddc99a66ab4c322"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-69":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Scatter plot\",\"Operating characteristic (OC) curve\",\"Process map\",\"Affinity diagram\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following tools is most appropriate when evaluating a sampling plan?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"An operating characteristic (OC) curve displays the probability of accepting lots at varying levels of percentage nonconforming. It provides a simple, graphical way to evaluate a sampling plan. Scatter plots are appropriate to analyze the relationship between two variables. A process map is used to help understand the steps of a process. Affinity diagrams are useful as a planning tool to help organize ideas generated by a group. [IV.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Scatter plot","Operating characteristic (OC) curve","Process map","Affinity diagram"],"stem":"Which of the following tools is most appropriate when evaluating a sampling plan?","sub":"ppc","why":"An operating characteristic (OC) curve displays the probability of accepting lots at varying levels of percentage nonconforming. It provides a simple, graphical way to evaluate a sampling plan. Scatter plots are appropriate to analyze the relationship between two variables. A process map is used to help understand the steps of a process. Affinity diagrams are useful as a planning tool to help organize ideas generated by a group. [IV.C.1]"},"revision":"0c78371b31d8f11531d6d046feb70a178fb9c65f336c924ffaf82dbc0d71f68e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-7":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"strengths, machines, applications, realistic, and time-based.\",\"strengths, measurable, achievable, realistic, and threats.\",\"specific, measurable, achievable, realistic, and time-based.\",\"specific, measurable, applications, reliability, and time-based.\"],\"stem\":\"In strategic planning, SMART represents:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Goals and objectives should be “specific, measurable, achievable, realistic, and time-based” to be effective throughout the enterprise. [I.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["strengths, machines, applications, realistic, and time-based.","strengths, measurable, achievable, realistic, and threats.","specific, measurable, achievable, realistic, and time-based.","specific, measurable, applications, reliability, and time-based."],"stem":"In strategic planning, SMART represents:","sub":"mgmt","why":"Goals and objectives should be “specific, measurable, achievable, realistic, and time-based” to be effective throughout the enterprise. [I.B.1]"},"revision":"fd2ad4903fde75f3564f3db30d1bc006b9008da0c7297588648993b113281265"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-70":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Hypergeometric distribution\",\"Binomial distribution\",\"Poisson distribution\",\"Normal distribution\"],\"stem\":\"A company requires a part on a small lot-by-lot basis. On which of the following probability distributions should the OC curve of the sampling plan be based?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"Because the products are received on a lot-by-lot basis and are not part of a continuous process, the OC curve should be based on the hypergeometric distribution. This OC curve is called a type A OC curve. Type B OC curves are used to evaluate a sampling plan for a continuous process or for a process that has a large lot size. Type B OC curves are developed using the binomial or Poisson distributions. [IV.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Hypergeometric distribution","Binomial distribution","Poisson distribution","Normal distribution"],"stem":"A company requires a part on a small lot-by-lot basis. On which of the following probability distributions should the OC curve of the sampling plan be based?","sub":"ppc","why":"Because the products are received on a lot-by-lot basis and are not part of a continuous process, the OC curve should be based on the hypergeometric distribution. This OC curve is called a type A OC curve. Type B OC curves are used to evaluate a sampling plan for a continuous process or for a process that has a large lot size. Type B OC curves are developed using the binomial or Poisson distributions. [IV.C.1]"},"revision":"e672bb265e8c6952110cf2b0aae879e63838c4dee5d5e67a3376dd5a5d81d830"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-71":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.3350\",\"0.3923\",\"0.6077\",\"0.6093\"],\"stem\":\"Consider a sampling plan with n = 45 and c = 2. Given that the percentage nonconforming is 0.05 and a binomial distribution is appropriate, what is the probability of accepting the lot?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"For a binomial sampling plan, the lot is accepted when d ≤ c. Therefore, Pa = Σd=0²C(45,d)(0.05)d(0.95)45-d = 0.6077. [IV.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.3350","0.3923","0.6077","0.6093"],"stem":"Consider a sampling plan with n = 45 and c = 2. Given that the percentage nonconforming is 0.05 and a binomial distribution is appropriate, what is the probability of accepting the lot?","sub":"ppc","why":"For a binomial sampling plan, the lot is accepted when d ≤ c. Therefore, Pa = Σd=0²C(45,d)(0.05)d(0.95)45-d = 0.6077. [IV.C.1]"},"revision":"809f48c0015a6951f4b40ff0045cecae87ac10a999ceaee544e9404c8775c2bd"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-72":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"The quality level\",\"The poorest quality in an individual lot that should be accepted\",\"The average quality of outgoing products\",\"The worst tolerable process average of a continuing series of lots\"],\"stem\":\"In acceptance sampling, what does the acronym “LTPD” mean?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"LTPD (Lot Tolerance Percent Defective) is the poorest quality level in an individual lot that should be accepted. It has a low probability of acceptance. [IV.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["The quality level","The poorest quality in an individual lot that should be accepted","The average quality of outgoing products","The worst tolerable process average of a continuing series of lots"],"stem":"In acceptance sampling, what does the acronym “LTPD” mean?","sub":"ppc","why":"LTPD (Lot Tolerance Percent Defective) is the poorest quality level in an individual lot that should be accepted. It has a low probability of acceptance. [IV.C.1]"},"revision":"4110e21a7cfab5a142d15ccd8109fed18e31358667d48416d3cea6387de8fd9b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-73":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Consumer’s risk\",\"Producer’s risk\",\"Beta risk\",\"High risk\"],\"stem\":\"Type I error in hypothesis testing corresponds to which of the following in acceptance sampling?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"A type I error in hypothesis testing is the probability of rejecting the null hypothesis when in fact the null hypothesis is true. Therefore, type I error corresponds to the producer’s risk in acceptance sampling: the probability that a lot is rejected when in fact the lot is within the acceptable quality level. [IV.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Consumer’s risk","Producer’s risk","Beta risk","High risk"],"stem":"Type I error in hypothesis testing corresponds to which of the following in acceptance sampling?","sub":"ppc","why":"A type I error in hypothesis testing is the probability of rejecting the null hypothesis when in fact the null hypothesis is true. Therefore, type I error corresponds to the producer’s risk in acceptance sampling: the probability that a lot is rejected when in fact the lot is within the acceptable quality level. [IV.C.1]"},"revision":"ab6cf11a0173a2df5d9ecf5a153bd4f5d014f489b17c2da2ad07188840663dac"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-74":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"0.0304\",\"0.3923\",\"0.6077\",\"0.9696\"],\"stem\":\"Consider a sampling plan with n = 45 and c = 2. Given that the percentage nonconforming is 0.05 and a lot size is assumed to be infinite, what is the average outgoing quality?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"For an infinite lot with rectifying inspection, AOQ = Pap. Using Pa = 0.6077 and p = 0.05, AOQ = 0.6077 × 0.05 = 0.0304. [IV.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["0.0304","0.3923","0.6077","0.9696"],"stem":"Consider a sampling plan with n = 45 and c = 2. Given that the percentage nonconforming is 0.05 and a lot size is assumed to be infinite, what is the average outgoing quality?","sub":"ppc","why":"For an infinite lot with rectifying inspection, AOQ = Pap. Using Pa = 0.6077 and p = 0.05, AOQ = 0.6077 × 0.05 = 0.0304. [IV.C.1]"},"revision":"15db10f25c4f767c373e2b2f30545418f9238b54474b7b2c3462430c765ccfed"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-75":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Pa increases.\",\"Pa decreases.\",\"Pa stays constant.\",\"Not enough information is provided.\"],\"stem\":\"In an acceptance sampling plan, how does the probability of acceptance change as the sample size increases, assuming the acceptance number and lot size are held constant?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"As the sample size increases, the probability of acceptance decreases. Consider, for example, an infinite lot size, an acceptance number c = 1, and fraction nonconforming p = 0.05. For various sample sizes n, the probability of acceptance is: Sample size (n) Pa 2 0.9975 5 0.9774 10 0.9139 20 0.7358 [IV.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Pa increases.","Pa decreases.","Pa stays constant.","Not enough information is provided."],"stem":"In an acceptance sampling plan, how does the probability of acceptance change as the sample size increases, assuming the acceptance number and lot size are held constant?","sub":"ppc","why":"As the sample size increases, the probability of acceptance decreases. Consider, for example, an infinite lot size, an acceptance number c = 1, and fraction nonconforming p = 0.05. For various sample sizes n, the probability of acceptance is: Sample size (n) Pa 2 0.9975 5 0.9774 10 0.9139 20 0.7358 [IV.C.1]"},"revision":"9f53119b6c7dd25037fc3a9c06546ce188d018f9e5c7c26259a7a35d8e472ab6"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-76":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"The lot size, N\",\"The sample size, n\",\"The acceptance number, c\",\"The probability of acceptance, Pa\"],\"stem\":\"Holding all else equal, which factor has the least effect on the OC curve?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"The lot size, N, has the smallest effect on the OC curve compared to the acceptance number c, the sample size n, and the probability of acceptance Pa. [IV.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["The lot size, N","The sample size, n","The acceptance number, c","The probability of acceptance, Pa"],"stem":"Holding all else equal, which factor has the least effect on the OC curve?","sub":"ppc","why":"The lot size, N, has the smallest effect on the OC curve compared to the acceptance number c, the sample size n, and the probability of acceptance Pa. [IV.C.1]"},"revision":"1216092d4c6ac62a745fb21ffd8493069e2f7392fd2de3e6da7670a56814aa85"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-77":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"They do not use tightened inspection.\",\"They are hard to administer in practice.\",\"The sample size is adjustable for changes in incoming product quality.\",\"They are more complicated to use.\"],\"stem\":\"What is a potential disadvantage of single sampling plans?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"The sample size of a single sampling plan does not change, regardless of changes in incoming product quality levels. Therefore, reduced or tightened inspection is not implemented with single sampling plans, which typically reduces sampling costs. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["They do not use tightened inspection.","They are hard to administer in practice.","The sample size is adjustable for changes in incoming product quality.","They are more complicated to use."],"stem":"What is a potential disadvantage of single sampling plans?","sub":"ppc","why":"The sample size of a single sampling plan does not change, regardless of changes in incoming product quality levels. Therefore, reduced or tightened inspection is not implemented with single sampling plans, which typically reduces sampling costs. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"dd5cbf0183aa9046239e73de4d941be20d85dec66f7ac0fb48c253fb272aab8e"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-78":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Accept the lot, since the number of defective items does not exceed the Ac value.\",\"Accept the lot, since the number of defective items is less than the Re value.\",\"Reject the lot, since the number of defective items is not less than the Ac value.\",\"Draw another sample.\"],\"stem\":\"The attribute sampling plan for a specific product utilizes an acceptance value of 2 and rejection value of 5. The inspector discovers two defective items in the current sample. What is the next appropriate action?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"When using a double sampling plan, after you take the first sample you will either accept the lot, reject the lot, or draw another sample. If the number of defective items in the first sample, d1, is less than or equal to the acceptance number, then you accept the lot. If d1 is greater than the rejection number value, then you reject the lot. If d1 is more than the acceptance number but less than the rejection number, then you draw another sample. In this case, d1 = 2 = Ac. Therefore, the lot is accepted, and another sample does not need to be drawn. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Accept the lot, since the number of defective items does not exceed the Ac value.","Accept the lot, since the number of defective items is less than the Re value.","Reject the lot, since the number of defective items is not less than the Ac value.","Draw another sample."],"stem":"The attribute sampling plan for a specific product utilizes an acceptance value of 2 and rejection value of 5. The inspector discovers two defective items in the current sample. What is the next appropriate action?","sub":"ppc","why":"When using a double sampling plan, after you take the first sample you will either accept the lot, reject the lot, or draw another sample. If the number of defective items in the first sample, d1, is less than or equal to the acceptance number, then you accept the lot. If d1 is greater than the rejection number value, then you reject the lot. If d1 is more than the acceptance number but less than the rejection number, then you draw another sample. In this case, d1 = 2 = Ac. Therefore, the lot is accepted, and another sample does not need to be drawn. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"3191a83e4e260e2742f60e3b97f718e9e684bb7bdff026d218e58e919b6d94f7"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-79":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.2165\",\"0.7835\",\"0.8614\",\"0.9221\"],\"stem\":\"A double sampling plan is implemented with the following parameters: n1 = 50, c1 = 1, r1 = 4, n2 = 75, c2 = 3, r2 = 6 What is the probability that a decision is made (to accept or reject the lot) on the first sample if the percentage nonconforming, p, is equal to 0.10?\",\"sub\":\"ppc\",\"why\":\"P(decision on first sample) = P(d₁ ≤ 1) + P(d₁ ≥ 4) = 1 − P(2 ≤ d₁ ≤ 3). Using binomial with n=50, p=0.10: P(d₁ ≤ 1) + P(d₁ ≥ 4) = 0.7835. [IV.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.2165","0.7835","0.8614","0.9221"],"stem":"A double sampling plan is implemented with the following parameters: n1 = 50, c1 = 1, r1 = 4, n2 = 75, c2 = 3, r2 = 6 What is the probability that a decision is made (to accept or reject the lot) on the first sample if the percentage nonconforming, p, is equal to 0.10?","sub":"ppc","why":"P(decision on first sample) = P(d₁ ≤ 1) + P(d₁ ≥ 4) = 1 − P(2 ≤ d₁ ≤ 3). Using binomial with n=50, p=0.10: P(d₁ ≤ 1) + P(d₁ ≥ 4) = 0.7835. [IV.C.2]"},"revision":"885a1ebea945e8e9643afa2d9706c5cafc4be14d794e16d6acd4b47c7d664963"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-8":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"to continually build and retain a loyal customer base.\",\"to deliver all products to all customers 100% on time.\",\"detailed plans stating how, when, and by whom the objective will be achieved.\",\"undocumented and unnecessary to monitor.\"],\"stem\":\"Action plans as part of the goals and objectives of a deployment technique are:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Action plans often resemble mini project plans, or more-complex project planning documents may be in order. [I.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["to continually build and retain a loyal customer base.","to deliver all products to all customers 100% on time.","detailed plans stating how, when, and by whom the objective will be achieved.","undocumented and unnecessary to monitor."],"stem":"Action plans as part of the goals and objectives of a deployment technique are:","sub":"mgmt","why":"Action plans often resemble mini project plans, or more-complex project planning documents may be in order. [I.B.2]"},"revision":"183869eeb98b26ec5f2d159c22293625e4a5b74a637f83dbb0b907b222446ed8"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-80":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Pareto chart\",\"Scatter diagram\",\"Flowchart\",\"Control chart\"],\"stem\":\"A team at a cell phone manufacturer would like to reduce the number of defects in the manufacture of their cell phones. They identify potential defects of the cell phone. What quality control tool is most appropriate to determine the most frequent defects?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Pareto charts are used to identify the most frequent issues in a process. The chart displays the “80/20 rule”—approximately 20% of factors (defects) account for approximately 80% of potential issues. Scatter diagrams graphically compare the relationship between two continuous variables, flowcharts provide a graphical representation of a process, and control charts are used to identify out-of-control behavior in a process. [V.A]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Pareto chart","Scatter diagram","Flowchart","Control chart"],"stem":"A team at a cell phone manufacturer would like to reduce the number of defects in the manufacture of their cell phones. They identify potential defects of the cell phone. What quality control tool is most appropriate to determine the most frequent defects?","sub":"ci","why":"Pareto charts are used to identify the most frequent issues in a process. The chart displays the “80/20 rule”—approximately 20% of factors (defects) account for approximately 80% of potential issues. Scatter diagrams graphically compare the relationship between two continuous variables, flowcharts provide a graphical representation of a process, and control charts are used to identify out-of-control behavior in a process. [V.A]"},"revision":"fe43ce42ee490f608e03270944007b2440a0ac886f79db08f5bc1987a31e0212"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-81":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Flowchart\",\"Scatter diagram\",\"Cause-and-effect diagram\",\"Check sheet\"],\"stem\":\"A team at a radiology clinic in a hospital wants to improve patient flow in their clinic. Before making changes to their process, the team discusses potential causes of disruptions in the patient flow. Which quality control tool will best help this team organize this information?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Cause-and-effect diagrams (also called fishbone or Ishikawa diagrams) are used to identify all major and minor factors that contribute to a problem. These diagrams can help the team identify the best opportunities for improvement in patient flow in the clinic. [V.A]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Flowchart","Scatter diagram","Cause-and-effect diagram","Check sheet"],"stem":"A team at a radiology clinic in a hospital wants to improve patient flow in their clinic. Before making changes to their process, the team discusses potential causes of disruptions in the patient flow. Which quality control tool will best help this team organize this information?","sub":"ci","why":"Cause-and-effect diagrams (also called fishbone or Ishikawa diagrams) are used to identify all major and minor factors that contribute to a problem. These diagrams can help the team identify the best opportunities for improvement in patient flow in the clinic. [V.A]"},"revision":"5be4197dc77f57987783f1df17297919ab72c21243447c17a3b4cc42778f774a"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-82":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Histogram\",\"Scatter diagram\",\"Pareto chart\",\"Control chart\"],\"stem\":\"The team in the radiology clinic has collected data on patient visit times. The team suspects that the data are heavily skewed. Which of the following quality control tools would be most appropriate to confirm the team’s suspicions?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Histograms are a graphical representation of the frequency of occurrences of some event. They provide a visual depiction of the shape and spread of the data and can therefore help the team decide whether the data are skewed, symmetric, bimodal, and so on. Scatter diagrams are used to depict the relationship between most frequent events. [V.A]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Histogram","Scatter diagram","Pareto chart","Control chart"],"stem":"The team in the radiology clinic has collected data on patient visit times. The team suspects that the data are heavily skewed. Which of the following quality control tools would be most appropriate to confirm the team’s suspicions?","sub":"ci","why":"Histograms are a graphical representation of the frequency of occurrences of some event. They provide a visual depiction of the shape and spread of the data and can therefore help the team decide whether the data are skewed, symmetric, bimodal, and so on. Scatter diagrams are used to depict the relationship between most frequent events. [V.A]"},"revision":"7bcc95c4db2770b008ed4e55ac090e635e8db01c842f631e4c9fff78dc83f974"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-83":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Flowchart\",\"Check sheet\",\"Scatter diagram\",\"Histogram\"],\"stem\":\"A team at a manufacturing facility is in the beginning stages of a performance improvement project to reduce the number of defects in a toy they produce. The team would like to collect data to decide the best opportunities for improvement. Which quality control tool is most appropriate to organize this data?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Check sheets are useful tools for summarizing counts of events, for example, the count of different types of defects. The data collected from a check sheet can then be graphically represented with a Pareto chart to identify the most frequent defects. Scatter diagrams graphically depict the relationship between two continuous variables, and histograms provide a graphical representation of the distribution for a continuous variable. [V.A]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Flowchart","Check sheet","Scatter diagram","Histogram"],"stem":"A team at a manufacturing facility is in the beginning stages of a performance improvement project to reduce the number of defects in a toy they produce. The team would like to collect data to decide the best opportunities for improvement. Which quality control tool is most appropriate to organize this data?","sub":"ci","why":"Check sheets are useful tools for summarizing counts of events, for example, the count of different types of defects. The data collected from a check sheet can then be graphically represented with a Pareto chart to identify the most frequent defects. Scatter diagrams graphically depict the relationship between two continuous variables, and histograms provide a graphical representation of the distribution for a continuous variable. [V.A]"},"revision":"59e56fe0ea9ee7cf5093625a2372a0221c634dbb2d94aefc974203e7f3efd9f8"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-84":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Pareto chart\",\"Check sheet\",\"Scatter diagram\",\"Flowchart\"],\"stem\":\"A team at a bank wants to improve the time it takes to process a loan application. To identify any bottlenecks in the process or targets for improvement, which quality control tool is most appropriate to use?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Flowcharts are a graphical representation of a process and can help identify areas for improvement (like a bottleneck). Pareto charts are used to identify the most common defects, check sheets are used to tally occurrences of events, and scatter diagrams are used to graphically depict the relationship between two continuous variables. [V.A]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Pareto chart","Check sheet","Scatter diagram","Flowchart"],"stem":"A team at a bank wants to improve the time it takes to process a loan application. To identify any bottlenecks in the process or targets for improvement, which quality control tool is most appropriate to use?","sub":"ci","why":"Flowcharts are a graphical representation of a process and can help identify areas for improvement (like a bottleneck). Pareto charts are used to identify the most common defects, check sheets are used to tally occurrences of events, and scatter diagrams are used to graphically depict the relationship between two continuous variables. [V.A]"},"revision":"2be66dba9f35cd1854ee153dc8e55291cc89b87615d21fe2d54237ced27dd867"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-85":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Control chart\",\"Flowchart\",\"Check sheet\",\"Scatter diagram\"],\"stem\":\"The bank has implemented changes to their loan application process and would like to monitor their improvements to ensure they are sustained. Which of the following quality control tools would best help this effort?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Control charts can be used to monitor a quality characteristic, in this case the time until the loan applications are processed. A time that plots beyond the control limits will indicate that a special cause has occurred. This alert will help the team correct the problem and maintain their improvements. [V.A]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Control chart","Flowchart","Check sheet","Scatter diagram"],"stem":"The bank has implemented changes to their loan application process and would like to monitor their improvements to ensure they are sustained. Which of the following quality control tools would best help this effort?","sub":"ci","why":"Control charts can be used to monitor a quality characteristic, in this case the time until the loan applications are processed. A time that plots beyond the control limits will indicate that a special cause has occurred. This alert will help the team correct the problem and maintain their improvements. [V.A]"},"revision":"0ad164fa63e31328c09c6385c6dd9f09e0fc0cf5e8f25cced72fb1a74fdba760"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-86":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Histogram\",\"Flowchart\",\"Pareto chart\",\"Scatter diagram\"],\"stem\":\"A team suspects that two variables are related. Which of the following quality control tools will help them determine this?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Scatter diagrams are used to graphically depict the relationship (if there is one) between two continuous variables. Histograms are graphical tools used to depict the distribution of one continuous variable. [V.A]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Histogram","Flowchart","Pareto chart","Scatter diagram"],"stem":"A team suspects that two variables are related. Which of the following quality control tools will help them determine this?","sub":"ci","why":"Scatter diagrams are used to graphically depict the relationship (if there is one) between two continuous variables. Histograms are graphical tools used to depict the distribution of one continuous variable. [V.A]"},"revision":"57cfcc415123a72335b1f83da58eff6478704b077adf950ddfdf5ef52438486c"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-87":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Prioritization matrix\",\"Activity network diagram\",\"Tree diagram\",\"Interrelationship digraph\"],\"stem\":\"A project team in a company comprises a large group of employees from different departments. The project has many complex components that depend on the progress of the various departments. Which of the following tools would help this team maintain a schedule for their project?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Activity network diagrams help to organize complex schedules or sequences of events. These diagrams, in particular, can help with time scheduling. Because this project involves a large group of people and is dependent on the work of different departments, an activity network diagram would help keep all the team members aware of the project schedule to avoid bottlenecks during the project. [V.B]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Prioritization matrix","Activity network diagram","Tree diagram","Interrelationship digraph"],"stem":"A project team in a company comprises a large group of employees from different departments. The project has many complex components that depend on the progress of the various departments. Which of the following tools would help this team maintain a schedule for their project?","sub":"ci","why":"Activity network diagrams help to organize complex schedules or sequences of events. These diagrams, in particular, can help with time scheduling. Because this project involves a large group of people and is dependent on the work of different departments, an activity network diagram would help keep all the team members aware of the project schedule to avoid bottlenecks during the project. [V.B]"},"revision":"3a97f035bb663e961fd28270d3315af03962ed37d9699973f448c90316be9bfc"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-88":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Value stream map\",\"Cause-and-effect diagram\",\"PVC diagram\",\"SIPOC diagram\"],\"stem\":\"A clinic in a hospital is beginning a project to improve patient wait times. The project team is determining critical-to-quality factors. Which tool would be most appropriate to use?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"A SIPOC diagram is a process map that contains information on the suppliers, inputs, outputs, and customers of a process. The diagram helps to capture the voice of the customer and the critical-to-quality factors. [V.B]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Value stream map","Cause-and-effect diagram","PVC diagram","SIPOC diagram"],"stem":"A clinic in a hospital is beginning a project to improve patient wait times. The project team is determining critical-to-quality factors. Which tool would be most appropriate to use?","sub":"ci","why":"A SIPOC diagram is a process map that contains information on the suppliers, inputs, outputs, and customers of a process. The diagram helps to capture the voice of the customer and the critical-to-quality factors. [V.B]"},"revision":"c44548754f219bf911d8ee8e68ef90750245def0fee57b2b8e0903854ebb4738"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-89":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Matrix diagram\",\"Process decision program chart\",\"Activity network diagram\",\"Prioritization matrix\"],\"stem\":\"A national print and copy company is considering changing the layout of their stores across the country to better serve their customers. The store is in the early phase of implementing their rollout plan for these changes. Which of the following tools would help the company evaluate their rollout plan to ensure there are minimal disruptions?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"A process decision program chart (PDPC) would help this company assess its national rollout plan at a high level, since they are at the beginning of their implementation plan. These charts help to determine potential disruptions to a process plan so that the company can prepare for any necessary contingency pl a n s. [V.B]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Matrix diagram","Process decision program chart","Activity network diagram","Prioritization matrix"],"stem":"A national print and copy company is considering changing the layout of their stores across the country to better serve their customers. The store is in the early phase of implementing their rollout plan for these changes. Which of the following tools would help the company evaluate their rollout plan to ensure there are minimal disruptions?","sub":"ci","why":"A process decision program chart (PDPC) would help this company assess its national rollout plan at a high level, since they are at the beginning of their implementation plan. These charts help to determine potential disruptions to a process plan so that the company can prepare for any necessary contingency pl a n s. [V.B]"},"revision":"1fbc9ef6957101309e72c8257deec0ff27ec741f96b375e02659cdfa675bd6c3"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-9":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"benchmarking.\",\"CPM.\",\"deployment.\",\"stakeholder analysis.\"],\"stem\":\"The process organizations use to evaluate their performance against their competition or best practices found internally is referred to as:\",\"sub\":\"mgmt\",\"why\":\"Benchmarking is the process by which companies compare their processes against best practices found either internally or externally. [I.B.2.a]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["benchmarking.","CPM.","deployment.","stakeholder analysis."],"stem":"The process organizations use to evaluate their performance against their competition or best practices found internally is referred to as:","sub":"mgmt","why":"Benchmarking is the process by which companies compare their processes against best practices found either internally or externally. [I.B.2.a]"},"revision":"aa53d2d222833e2b2512434c69af5d4a81b0d6ac89217dd05dc188ea2e772ab7"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-90":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Fault tree\",\"Affinity diagram\",\"Matrix diagram\",\"Process decision program chart\"],\"stem\":\"An accident has recently occurred in a factory’s shipping area. A team was put together to analyze potential actions that could lead to similar accidents. The goal is for the team to help determine the risk associated with these actions and to prevent future accidents from occurring. Which quality tool would help this team visualize this scenario?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Fault trees provide a visualization of hierarchical relationships of events that lead to some failure (or other undesirable final outcome). The team here wishes to determine other potential actions that can lead to a similar accident in the factory. The fault tree would help the team identify and communicate other potential causes of the accident. The fault tree would also provide a means to quantify the risks of these potential causes. [V.B]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Fault tree","Affinity diagram","Matrix diagram","Process decision program chart"],"stem":"An accident has recently occurred in a factory’s shipping area. A team was put together to analyze potential actions that could lead to similar accidents. The goal is for the team to help determine the risk associated with these actions and to prevent future accidents from occurring. Which quality tool would help this team visualize this scenario?","sub":"ci","why":"Fault trees provide a visualization of hierarchical relationships of events that lead to some failure (or other undesirable final outcome). The team here wishes to determine other potential actions that can lead to a similar accident in the factory. The fault tree would help the team identify and communicate other potential causes of the accident. The fault tree would also provide a means to quantify the risks of these potential causes. [V.B]"},"revision":"457a42fbee98f6fe1ed9d456575fe522bd3ee223d18b834f8233dab980faa612"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-91":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Tree diagram\",\"Matrix diagram\",\"Affinity diagram\",\"Activity network diagram\"],\"stem\":\"A management team has a brainstorming meeting to identify opportunities to improve communication within their department. Which quality tool will best help them organize all the ideas generated by the team?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Affinity diagrams are used to help a group of people organize or summarize facts, opinions, or ideas. Often, these types of diagrams are built using sticky notes that are grouped into common themes or categories. [V.B]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Tree diagram","Matrix diagram","Affinity diagram","Activity network diagram"],"stem":"A management team has a brainstorming meeting to identify opportunities to improve communication within their department. Which quality tool will best help them organize all the ideas generated by the team?","sub":"ci","why":"Affinity diagrams are used to help a group of people organize or summarize facts, opinions, or ideas. Often, these types of diagrams are built using sticky notes that are grouped into common themes or categories. [V.B]"},"revision":"9beb2e89b5eff97d47bb278e515be6ce5182a38d02012d939f92e9621939c989"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-92":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Prioritization matrix\",\"Activity network diagram\",\"Matrix diagram\",\"Interrelationship digraph\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following tools is common in quality function deployment and used to map customer requirements with technical specifications of a product?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Matrix diagrams are used to display qualitative relationships between two sets of characteristics or factors. Therefore, these diagrams are common in quality function deployment because they clearly display the relationships between customer requirements and a product’s technical specifications. [V.B]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Prioritization matrix","Activity network diagram","Matrix diagram","Interrelationship digraph"],"stem":"Which of the following tools is common in quality function deployment and used to map customer requirements with technical specifications of a product?","sub":"ci","why":"Matrix diagrams are used to display qualitative relationships between two sets of characteristics or factors. Therefore, these diagrams are common in quality function deployment because they clearly display the relationships between customer requirements and a product’s technical specifications. [V.B]"},"revision":"bb5a204102188e4cb10f7415e35669ff02680b48afb128f458335279a0d5518b"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-93":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Affinity diagram\",\"Interrelationship digraph\",\"Prioritization matrix\",\"Activity network diagram\"],\"stem\":\"A medical team meets to discuss potential cause-and-effect relationships of actions that could lead to unfavorable outcomes during a patient surgical operation. The team consists of nurses, doctors, physician assistants, and technologists. Which of the following tools would best help this team visualize and hopefully identify factors that cause unfavorable outcomes?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Interrelationship digraphs provide a means to identify sequential or cause- and-effect relationships of a problem or situation, in this case an unfavorable outcome in the operating room. Affinity diagrams are used to organize general thoughts and ideas about a topic and are more appropriate for grouping related ideas rather than discovering causal relationships. Prioritization matrices provide a formal method for comparing decisions or ideas before making a final decision. Activity network diagrams help to organize events for time schedule pl a n n i n g. [V.B]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Affinity diagram","Interrelationship digraph","Prioritization matrix","Activity network diagram"],"stem":"A medical team meets to discuss potential cause-and-effect relationships of actions that could lead to unfavorable outcomes during a patient surgical operation. The team consists of nurses, doctors, physician assistants, and technologists. Which of the following tools would best help this team visualize and hopefully identify factors that cause unfavorable outcomes?","sub":"ci","why":"Interrelationship digraphs provide a means to identify sequential or cause- and-effect relationships of a problem or situation, in this case an unfavorable outcome in the operating room. Affinity diagrams are used to organize general thoughts and ideas about a topic and are more appropriate for grouping related ideas rather than discovering causal relationships. Prioritization matrices provide a formal method for comparing decisions or ideas before making a final decision. Activity network diagrams help to organize events for time schedule pl a n n i n g. [V.B]"},"revision":"040f09aa180c428297b6d79462d428906e6995ef86c4414fbf46f373c01c8a22"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-94":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Fault tree\",\"Prioritization matrix\",\"Concept fan\",\"Process decision program chart\"],\"stem\":\"A cell phone manufacturer is developing a new cell phone. The research and development team has been given several required components of the phone, but have not decided on a design plan. Which of the following quality tools would help this team explore potential design plans for the new cell phone?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"A concept fan is a type of tree diagram that allows a team to explore alternate concepts that achieve the same goals or requirements. Fault trees are also tree diagrams but provide information on the hierarchical events that lead to an unfavorable outcome or failure in a process. Prioritization matrices provide a formal method for comparing decisions or ideas before making a final decision. Process decision program charts (PDPCs) help to evaluate a process implementation plan at a high level. [V.B]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Fault tree","Prioritization matrix","Concept fan","Process decision program chart"],"stem":"A cell phone manufacturer is developing a new cell phone. The research and development team has been given several required components of the phone, but have not decided on a design plan. Which of the following quality tools would help this team explore potential design plans for the new cell phone?","sub":"ci","why":"A concept fan is a type of tree diagram that allows a team to explore alternate concepts that achieve the same goals or requirements. Fault trees are also tree diagrams but provide information on the hierarchical events that lead to an unfavorable outcome or failure in a process. Prioritization matrices provide a formal method for comparing decisions or ideas before making a final decision. Process decision program charts (PDPCs) help to evaluate a process implementation plan at a high level. [V.B]"},"revision":"467cc195c2570f4d1d1a5ecbdf34eefcfa0c92d25003f53ce2e6c914add24856"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-95":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Prioritization matrix\",\"Affinity diagram\",\"Interrelationship digraph\",\"Process decision program chart\"],\"stem\":\"The research and development team have now proposed several designs for the new cell phone. The team is now considering which of the designs to pursue to build a prototype. Which of the following tools would best help the team reach a decision on which of the designs to use?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Prioritization matrices are useful in the decision phase since they allow us to make relative comparisons of different ideas or concepts with consistent criteria. The R&D team has developed several competing designs and can use a prioritization matrix to fairly compare the designs to help them make a final de c i s io n . [V.B]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Prioritization matrix","Affinity diagram","Interrelationship digraph","Process decision program chart"],"stem":"The research and development team have now proposed several designs for the new cell phone. The team is now considering which of the designs to pursue to build a prototype. Which of the following tools would best help the team reach a decision on which of the designs to use?","sub":"ci","why":"Prioritization matrices are useful in the decision phase since they allow us to make relative comparisons of different ideas or concepts with consistent criteria. The R&D team has developed several competing designs and can use a prioritization matrix to fairly compare the designs to help them make a final de c i s io n . [V.B]"},"revision":"7cce7629ab8f42f73ecd95b3813071e5c157016b6aa7c7bafa723fb7821f32b8"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-96":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Six Sigma\",\"Total quality management (TQM)\",\"Kaizen\",\"Plan–do–check–act (PDCA)\"],\"stem\":\"A team in the packing department of a shoe company noticed some inefficiencies in their packing process. The team first reduced the amount of walking employees had to do by moving the labeling station next to the packing station. The team continued to make incremental changes to their process, each of which led to a small improvement of the process. Which improvement technique has this team made use of?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"The packing team has made several minor changes to their process, each of which led to a small improvement. This describes kaizen, a methodology that encourages employees to make incremental changes continually to improve a process. Each change may not make a large improvement, but does help employees. [V.C]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Six Sigma","Total quality management (TQM)","Kaizen","Plan–do–check–act (PDCA)"],"stem":"A team in the packing department of a shoe company noticed some inefficiencies in their packing process. The team first reduced the amount of walking employees had to do by moving the labeling station next to the packing station. The team continued to make incremental changes to their process, each of which led to a small improvement of the process. Which improvement technique has this team made use of?","sub":"ci","why":"The packing team has made several minor changes to their process, each of which led to a small improvement. This describes kaizen, a methodology that encourages employees to make incremental changes continually to improve a process. Each change may not make a large improvement, but does help employees. [V.C]"},"revision":"106793dd492e00fd26bfcc96f308290561b3b8ebe6d26c6b4bd427cc26feda21"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-97":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"P\",\"D\",\"C\",\"A\"],\"stem\":\"In the PDCA cycle, in which stage of this improvement method does a team define the problem and develop an action plan?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"In the plan phase of the plan–do–check–act (PDCA) cycle, the quality team decides on a potential change to the process. In the do phase, the team performs the proposed change, in the check step, the team analyzes the results of the depending on whether the change was successful or not. [V.C]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["P","D","C","A"],"stem":"In the PDCA cycle, in which stage of this improvement method does a team define the problem and develop an action plan?","sub":"ci","why":"In the plan phase of the plan–do–check–act (PDCA) cycle, the quality team decides on a potential change to the process. In the do phase, the team performs the proposed change, in the check step, the team analyzes the results of the depending on whether the change was successful or not. [V.C]"},"revision":"e7f20db28059924b7ca06f296184d45a499b7441ba8ca77247a5f4b696e92be5"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-98":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Kaizen\",\"TQM\",\"PDCA\",\"Six Sigma\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following continuous improvement methods focuses on a business’s customers, quality built into the work culture, and strong leadership involvement in quality efforts?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"Total quality management (TQM) is an organization-wide quality program that focuses on customers’ involvement in determining quality, employees’ obligation to quality, and strong leadership from upper management in quality improvement efforts. Kaizen is a method of continuous improvement that makes incremental changes to a process. PDCA, also called the Shewhart or Deming (PDSA) cycle, is a methodology for a performance improvement project, and theory of constraints (TOC) is a problem-solving methodology that focuses on the weakest part of a process. [V.C]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Kaizen","TQM","PDCA","Six Sigma"],"stem":"Which of the following continuous improvement methods focuses on a business’s customers, quality built into the work culture, and strong leadership involvement in quality efforts?","sub":"ci","why":"Total quality management (TQM) is an organization-wide quality program that focuses on customers’ involvement in determining quality, employees’ obligation to quality, and strong leadership from upper management in quality improvement efforts. Kaizen is a method of continuous improvement that makes incremental changes to a process. PDCA, also called the Shewhart or Deming (PDSA) cycle, is a methodology for a performance improvement project, and theory of constraints (TOC) is a problem-solving methodology that focuses on the weakest part of a process. [V.C]"},"revision":"a45ca8264d4a290f0f756b898d4e517a84c433580cedc287c537bd749c14e2dd"},"cqe:set-3:legacy-99":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"P\",\"D\",\"C\",\"A\"],\"stem\":\"A quality team in a semiconductor factory, using the PDCA cycle, has proposed a change to their process. They have implemented the change and are currently analyzing the results. In which step of the PDCA cycle is this quality team?\",\"sub\":\"ci\",\"why\":\"The quality team has already implemented the proposed change and are analyzing the results. This describes the check step of the PDCA cycle. In this step, the quality team should carefully analyze the results of their change to determine whether the change was successful or not. In the act step, the team will make a decision on whether to keep the change, and then repeat the cycle. [V.C]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["P","D","C","A"],"stem":"A quality team in a semiconductor factory, using the PDCA cycle, has proposed a change to their process. They have implemented the change and are currently analyzing the results. In which step of the PDCA cycle is this quality team?","sub":"ci","why":"The quality team has already implemented the proposed change and are analyzing the results. This describes the check step of the PDCA cycle. In this step, the quality team should carefully analyze the results of their change to determine whether the change was successful or not. In the act step, the team will make a decision on whether to keep the change, and then repeat the cycle. [V.C]"},"revision":"f379206abd0a4b3f6eb67b8f5a1a92e6978abad05779f645de3b2681a8712ec8"}},"examId":"cqe","provenance":{"disclosure":"This site uses a 160-question, 300-minute scored practice preset (paper-exam length and time), not the current 175-item, 318-minute ASQ computer-delivered exam.","officialComputer":{"appointmentMinutes":330,"examMinutes":318,"items":175,"performanceAssessment":false,"scored":160,"unscored":15},"officialPaper":{"examMinutes":300,"items":160},"publisher":"ASQ","scoring":{"publisher":"ASQ","rawPassingPercentage":null,"scaleMaximum":750,"scaledPassingScore":550,"section":"I passed. What was my score?","siteTargetBps":7000,"siteTargetMeaning":"UpSkill Sprint practice target; not an ASQ passing percentage or pass probability","url":"https://www.asq.org/cert/faq/faq-exam-results"},"section":"Examination","siteBasis":"paper-length-practice","url":"https://www.asq.org/cert/quality-engineer","verifiedAt":"2026-09-08"},"schemaVersion":"1.0.0"}'::jsonb);
