t is the approximate 95% confidence interval for the population mean?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"For known σ, use x̄ ± 1.96σ/√n. Margin=1.96×12/√64=2.94; CI=107±2.94.\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["95 to 119","106.63 to 107.37","104.06 to 109.94","105.53 to 108.47"],"stem":"A sample mean is 107, population σ is known to be 12, and n=64. What is the approximate 95% confidence interval for the population mean?","sub":"ana","why":"For known σ, use x̄ ± 1.96σ/√n. Margin=1.96×12/√64=2.94; CI=107±2.94."},"revision":"ea4d043f607a2896fa831aab552c5da5d9b317c910369f12e31203d49ea5fe65"},"cssbb:set-2:legacy-98":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"54\",\"7.7\",\"23\",\"432\"],\"stem\":\"An FMEA rates severity=9, occurrence=6, and detection=8. What is the traditional RPN?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"RPN = severity × occurrence × detection. RPN=9×6×8=432.\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["54","7.7","23","432"],"stem":"An FMEA rates severity=9, occurrence=6, and detection=8. What is the traditional RPN?","sub":"ana","why":"RPN = severity × occurrence × detection. RPN=9×6×8=432."},"revision":"50a72d4fff7d22eb7dd57318fabc31b7431e1889e3ba1a07f4ed9f81085dc4d9"},"cssbb:set-2:legacy-99":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"57.99999999999999% of the observed variation in the response is explained by the fitted model.\",\"The correlation is necessarily 0.58\",\"The model is correct for exactly 57.99999999999999% of future observations\",\"The remaining variation is entirely measurement error\"],\"stem\":\"A regression model reports R² = 0.58. Which statement is correct?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"R² is the proportion of response variation explained by the model in the sample.\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["57.99999999999999% of the observed variation in the response is explained by the fitted model.","The correlation is necessarily 0.58","The model is correct for exactly 57.99999999999999% of future observations","The remaining variation is entirely measurement error"],"stem":"A regression model reports R² = 0.58. Which statement is correct?","sub":"ana","why":"R² is the proportion of response variation explained by the model in the sample."},"revision":"2168292c7951f51fe6bf853cccd5b4c1fedc1e1955b49033995fd67a19cdaee1"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-1":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"separation of Six Sigma and business strategy.\",\"minimal use of statistical techniques.\",\"emphasis on quick-turnaround projects that have the highest payoff.\",\"use of teams assigned to well-defined projects that impact the bottom line.\"],\"stem\":\"A common thread in the use of the Six Sigma approach across organizations is the:\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"The Six Sigma approach emphasizes assigning teams to tackle well-defined projects that bring financial reward to the organization. The projects should mesh with the organization’s business strategy and can run anywhere from two to nine months. Decisions are made based on data, and statistical techniques are often used for analysis. [I.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["separation of Six Sigma and business strategy.","minimal use of statistical techniques.","emphasis on quick-turnaround projects that have the highest payoff.","use of teams assigned to well-defined projects that impact the bottom line."],"stem":"A common thread in the use of the Six Sigma approach across organizations is the:","sub":"p1","why":"The Six Sigma approach emphasizes assigning teams to tackle well-defined projects that bring financial reward to the organization. The projects should mesh with the organization’s business strategy and can run anywhere from two to nine months. Decisions are made based on data, and statistical techniques are often used for analysis. [I.A.1]"},"revision":"6b6058590bc1ecef804af6ffa0c5a6ccd0281c0a122132740d830e2e6cc02d13"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-10":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Based on criteria such as customer impact and availability of resources\",\"According to the opinions of top management\",\"Only if there is a known solution\",\"Based on public relations feedback\"],\"stem\":\"How should Six Sigma projects be selected?\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"Six Sigma projects should be chosen based on an agreed-upon set of weighted criteria, such as customer impact and availability of resources, among others. [I.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Based on criteria such as customer impact and availability of resources","According to the opinions of top management","Only if there is a known solution","Based on public relations feedback"],"stem":"How should Six Sigma projects be selected?","sub":"p1","why":"Six Sigma projects should be chosen based on an agreed-upon set of weighted criteria, such as customer impact and availability of resources, among others. [I.A.2]"},"revision":"a7e56f127991941604716cee6461347835c7218dd52ac25b1ca9d8863a5c28f8"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-100":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Alpha\",\"Beta\",\"Gamma\",\"No error; it is a correct decision.\"],\"stem\":\"What type of error occurs when the null hypothesis is rejected when H₀ is true?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Rejecting a true null hypothesis is a Type I, or alpha, error. [VI.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Alpha","Beta","Gamma","No error; it is a correct decision."],"stem":"What type of error occurs when the null hypothesis is rejected when H₀ is true?","sub":"ana","why":"Rejecting a true null hypothesis is a Type I, or alpha, error. [VI.B.1]"},"revision":"f80e8b15714855df99a97df1b6722fad4b9727e80db3215f76466305c8ea7026"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-101":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"The second call center had a larger decrease in mean call time.\",\"The training program worked better in the first center.\",\"The training program significantly decreased call times in each call center.\",\"The training program had an equal effect on call times for each center.\"],\"stem\":\"Two hypothesis tests were run to test whether a new training program decreased mean call times for two call centers in different cities. The p-value of the test for the first center was equal to 0.04 and the p-value for the second center was equal to 0.001. At α = 0.05, what can be said about the mean call times for the centers?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Both p-values are below 0.05, so the null hypothesis is rejected for each center. The tests establish statistical decreases at both centers but do not establish which decrease was larger. [VI.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["The second call center had a larger decrease in mean call time.","The training program worked better in the first center.","The training program significantly decreased call times in each call center.","The training program had an equal effect on call times for each center."],"stem":"Two hypothesis tests were run to test whether a new training program decreased mean call times for two call centers in different cities. The p-value of the test for the first center was equal to 0.04 and the p-value for the second center was equal to 0.001. At α = 0.05, what can be said about the mean call times for the centers?","sub":"ana","why":"Both p-values are below 0.05, so the null hypothesis is rejected for each center. The tests establish statistical decreases at both centers but do not establish which decrease was larger. [VI.B.1]"},"revision":"d60a7db6a36e3951972571d3770604bd82f0946e8b7fceb26f052f3075e1a954"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-102":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"The null hypothesis is true with a probability equal to 0.045.\",\"The alternative hypothesis is true with a probability equal to 0.045.\",\"The probability of getting the current result or something more extreme, assuming the null hypothesis is true, is 0.045.\",\"The probability of getting the current result or something more extreme, assuming the alternative hypothesis is true, is 0.045.\"],\"stem\":\"A Black Belt performs a hypothesis test, and the analysis returns a p-value equal to 0.045. What is the interpretation of the p-value?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"By definition, p = 0.045 means the probability of obtaining the observed test statistic or something more extreme, given that H₀ is true, is 4.5%. It is not the probability that H₀ is true. [VI.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["The null hypothesis is true with a probability equal to 0.045.","The alternative hypothesis is true with a probability equal to 0.045.","The probability of getting the current result or something more extreme, assuming the null hypothesis is true, is 0.045.","The probability of getting the current result or something more extreme, assuming the alternative hypothesis is true, is 0.045."],"stem":"A Black Belt performs a hypothesis test, and the analysis returns a p-value equal to 0.045. What is the interpretation of the p-value?","sub":"ana","why":"By definition, p = 0.045 means the probability of obtaining the observed test statistic or something more extreme, given that H₀ is true, is 4.5%. It is not the probability that H₀ is true. [VI.B.1]"},"revision":"92b04c56d7de5a5c78c43c20584cc394770b29d27a84db3276cecbf6b38d1e44"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-103":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"H₀: μ_d = 0 vs. H₁: μ_d ≠ 0\",\"H₀: μ₁ − μ₂ = 0 vs. H₁: μ₁ − μ₂ ≤ 0\",\"H₀: μ₁ − μ₂ = 0 vs. H₁: μ₁ − μ₂ ≠ 0\",\"H₀: x̄₁ − x̄₂ = 0 vs. H₁: x̄₁ − x̄₂ ≤ 0\"],\"stem\":\"Which pair of hypotheses is appropriate for a two-sided test comparing two independent population means?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"A two-sided test of two population means is H₀: μ₁ − μ₂ = 0 versus H₁: μ₁ − μ₂ ≠ 0. [VI.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["H₀: μ_d = 0 vs. H₁: μ_d ≠ 0","H₀: μ₁ − μ₂ = 0 vs. H₁: μ₁ − μ₂ ≤ 0","H₀: μ₁ − μ₂ = 0 vs. H₁: μ₁ − μ₂ ≠ 0","H₀: x̄₁ − x̄₂ = 0 vs. H₁: x̄₁ − x̄₂ ≤ 0"],"stem":"Which pair of hypotheses is appropriate for a two-sided test comparing two independent population means?","sub":"ana","why":"A two-sided test of two population means is H₀: μ₁ − μ₂ = 0 versus H₁: μ₁ − μ₂ ≠ 0. [VI.B.1]"},"revision":"1c8dc12a624b6fb204cd2229cda4a165ff2216614cd7526bc99546d0777216d2"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-104":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"a false claim.\",\"a result being statistically insignificant.\",\"a result being statistically insignificant but practically significant.\",\"a result being statistically significant, but not practically significant.\"],\"stem\":\"A training company finds a statistically significant increase in mean score on a 100-point certification exam: 80.4 for program users versus 80.2 for nonusers. The organization defines a practically meaningful improvement as at least 5 points. This is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"The 0.2-point difference is statistically detectable but below the defined 5-point practical-significance threshold, so it is statistically significant but not practically significant. [VI.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["a false claim.","a result being statistically insignificant.","a result being statistically insignificant but practically significant.","a result being statistically significant, but not practically significant."],"stem":"A training company finds a statistically significant increase in mean score on a 100-point certification exam: 80.4 for program users versus 80.2 for nonusers. The organization defines a practically meaningful improvement as at least 5 points. This is an example of:","sub":"ana","why":"The 0.2-point difference is statistically detectable but below the defined 5-point practical-significance threshold, so it is statistically significant but not practically significant. [VI.B.2]"},"revision":"433c99b5f5ffbbb07ca9b0ee67370de5d2f51adcfe3f351d5ec0ea578e90e0c4"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-105":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"48\",\"49\",\"96\",\"97\"],\"stem\":\"After adjusting a filling process, a Six Sigma professional wants to estimate the new mean fill weight with a 95% confidence interval having a margin of error of 0.5 g. The historical process standard deviation is σ = 2.5 g. Use z* = 1.96. What minimum sample size is required?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"With known historical σ, n = (z*σ/E)² = (1.96 × 2.5/0.5)² = 96.04. Round up to ensure the requested margin of error, giving 97. [VI.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["48","49","96","97"],"stem":"After adjusting a filling process, a Six Sigma professional wants to estimate the new mean fill weight with a 95% confidence interval having a margin of error of 0.5 g. The historical process standard deviation is σ = 2.5 g. Use z* = 1.96. What minimum sample size is required?","sub":"ana","why":"With known historical σ, n = (z*σ/E)² = (1.96 × 2.5/0.5)² = 96.04. Round up to ensure the requested margin of error, giving 97. [VI.B.3]"},"revision":"b9c376aeb6c0732301198988e5421a811d5b0399fa0ab06a2caa13e301ff578a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-106":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"[5.098, 5.902]\",\"[5.108, 5.588]\",\"[5.171, 5.829]\",\"[5.412, 5.588]\"],\"stem\":\"A process has an average of 5.50 for the last 20 independent parts and a known historical standard deviation σ = 0.20. Assume individual part values are normally distributed. Use z* = 1.96 to find the 95% prediction interval for the next part produced.\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"For a future individual observation with known historical σ, use x̄ ± z*σ√(1 + 1/n). This gives 5.50 ± 1.96(0.20)√(1.05) = [5.098, 5.902]. [VI.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["[5.098, 5.902]","[5.108, 5.588]","[5.171, 5.829]","[5.412, 5.588]"],"stem":"A process has an average of 5.50 for the last 20 independent parts and a known historical standard deviation σ = 0.20. Assume individual part values are normally distributed. Use z* = 1.96 to find the 95% prediction interval for the next part produced.","sub":"ana","why":"For a future individual observation with known historical σ, use x̄ ± z*σ√(1 + 1/n). This gives 5.50 ± 1.96(0.20)√(1.05) = [5.098, 5.902]. [VI.B.4]"},"revision":"2d7b059bfb38de508d82989467f17649f3559db688dd78e148a13c12f943977e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-107":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Input\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"Sample size, n\",\"100\"],[\"Confidence\",\"99%\"],[\"Population coverage\",\"95%\"],[\"Two-sided tolerance factor, K\",\"2.350\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"[5.580, 7.920]\",\"[5.678, 7.822]\",\"[5.810, 7.690]\",\"[5.927, 7.573]\"],\"stem\":\"A process output is normally distributed with a sample mean equal to 6.75 and a sample standard deviation of 0.40. If 100 parts are produced, what is the two-sided tolerance interval that we can be 99% confident will contain 95% of the products produced?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Using the supplied two-sided normal tolerance factor K = 2.350, the interval is x̄ ± Ks = 6.75 ± 2.350(0.40) = [5.810, 7.690]. [VI.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"chart":{"columns":["Input","Value"],"rows":[["Sample size, n","100"],["Confidence","99%"],["Population coverage","95%"],["Two-sided tolerance factor, K","2.350"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["[5.580, 7.920]","[5.678, 7.822]","[5.810, 7.690]","[5.927, 7.573]"],"stem":"A process output is normally distributed with a sample mean equal to 6.75 and a sample standard deviation of 0.40. If 100 parts are produced, what is the two-sided tolerance interval that we can be 99% confident will contain 95% of the products produced?","sub":"ana","why":"Using the supplied two-sided normal tolerance factor K = 2.350, the interval is x̄ ± Ks = 6.75 ± 2.350(0.40) = [5.810, 7.690]. [VI.B.4]"},"revision":"74936b1adb3c34f83b8000433145c64758031370f709dd6117f9317871903f71"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-108":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Input\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"Sample size, n\",\"35\"],[\"Sample mean, x̄\",\"2.15\"],[\"Sample standard deviation, s\",\"0.80\"],[\"90% two-sided critical value, t*\",\"1.691\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"[1.875, 2.425]\",\"[1.921, 2.379]\",\"[1.925, 2.375]\",\"[1.928, 2.372]\"],\"stem\":\"A sample of n = 35 has x̄ = 2.15 and s = 0.80. Calculate the 90% confidence interval for the mean. Use t* = t(0.05, 34) = 1.691.\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"90% CI: x̄ ± t(0.05, 34)(s/√n) = 2.15 ± 1.691(0.80/√35) = 2.15 ± 1.691(0.1353) = 2.15 ± 0.229 = [1.921, 2.379]. [VI.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"chart":{"columns":["Input","Value"],"rows":[["Sample size, n","35"],["Sample mean, x̄","2.15"],["Sample standard deviation, s","0.80"],["90% two-sided critical value, t*","1.691"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["[1.875, 2.425]","[1.921, 2.379]","[1.925, 2.375]","[1.928, 2.372]"],"stem":"A sample of n = 35 has x̄ = 2.15 and s = 0.80. Calculate the 90% confidence interval for the mean. Use t* = t(0.05, 34) = 1.691.","sub":"ana","why":"90% CI: x̄ ± t(0.05, 34)(s/√n) = 2.15 ± 1.691(0.80/√35) = 2.15 ± 1.691(0.1353) = 2.15 ± 0.229 = [1.921, 2.379]. [VI.B.4]"},"revision":"9137e0e46d8d4fad0f3044acd45bb17176ba416616fbfcc6a975b61c0c4cd925"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-109":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Exactly 95% of individual observations in the population fall within this interval.\",\"The population mean changes from sample to sample and falls within the interval 95% of the time.\",\"We are 95% confident that the interval covers the true mean.\",\"We are 95% confident that the sample mean equals the true mean.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following statements is a correct interpretation of a 95% confidence interval on the mean?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"The mean does not try to fall into an interval; it is the interval that tries to cover the mean. The correct interpretation is: “We are 95% confident that the interval covers the true mean.” [VI.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Exactly 95% of individual observations in the population fall within this interval.","The population mean changes from sample to sample and falls within the interval 95% of the time.","We are 95% confident that the interval covers the true mean.","We are 95% confident that the sample mean equals the true mean."],"stem":"Which of the following statements is a correct interpretation of a 95% confidence interval on the mean?","sub":"ana","why":"The mean does not try to fall into an interval; it is the interval that tries to cover the mean. The correct interpretation is: “We are 95% confident that the interval covers the true mean.” [VI.B.4]"},"revision":"040e25ae7238795e25bff425c4580bb80bb5e72e2e71f7d338225323f939c2fa"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-11":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"reduces variation and waste.\",\"only succeeds in manufacturing environments.\",\"creates an excessive training burden.\",\"fosters an “us” versus “them” working environment.\"],\"stem\":\"Using lean and Six Sigma together:\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"Using lean and Six Sigma together reduces variation and waste. Six Sigma concentrates on reduced variation, while lean emphasizes elimination of waste. [I.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["reduces variation and waste.","only succeeds in manufacturing environments.","creates an excessive training burden.","fosters an “us” versus “them” working environment."],"stem":"Using lean and Six Sigma together:","sub":"p1","why":"Using lean and Six Sigma together reduces variation and waste. Six Sigma concentrates on reduced variation, while lean emphasizes elimination of waste. [I.A.1]"},"revision":"6722dd7584a327fdef259af8a35c820f134d89a3d2bba7761a6264e267659967"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-110":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Input\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"df₁, df₂\",\"24, 24\"],[\"F(0.975; 24,24)\",\"2.2693\"],[\"F(0.025; 24,24)\",\"0.4407\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"[0.679, 3.740]\",\"[0.703, 3.621]\",\"[0.716, 3.557]\",\"[0.805, 3.165]\"],\"stem\":\"Independent random samples were taken from two normal populations. Construct a 95% two-sided confidence interval for σ₁²/σ₂² given n₁ = 25, s₁ = 48.0, n₂ = 25, and s₂ = 38.0. Use F(0.975; 24,24) = 2.2693 and F(0.025; 24,24) = 0.4407.\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"The variance ratio is 48²/38² = 1.5956. The 95% interval is [1.5956/2.2693, 1.5956/0.4407] = [0.703, 3.621]. [VI.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"chart":{"columns":["Input","Value"],"rows":[["df₁, df₂","24, 24"],["F(0.975; 24,24)","2.2693"],["F(0.025; 24,24)","0.4407"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["[0.679, 3.740]","[0.703, 3.621]","[0.716, 3.557]","[0.805, 3.165]"],"stem":"Independent random samples were taken from two normal populations. Construct a 95% two-sided confidence interval for σ₁²/σ₂² given n₁ = 25, s₁ = 48.0, n₂ = 25, and s₂ = 38.0. Use F(0.975; 24,24) = 2.2693 and F(0.025; 24,24) = 0.4407.","sub":"ana","why":"The variance ratio is 48²/38² = 1.5956. The 95% interval is [1.5956/2.2693, 1.5956/0.4407] = [0.703, 3.621]. [VI.B.4]"},"revision":"e3c671a499a38f2a2d8a33d8f0167a8f43605b8977e69ab56c31479b1f95f74c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-111":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"The interval proves that σ₁ is greater than σ₂.\",\"The interval proves that σ₂ is greater than σ₁.\",\"Because the interval excludes zero, the two standard deviations are different.\",\"There is insufficient evidence that σ₁ is greater than σ₂.\"],\"stem\":\"A 90% one-sided confidence interval for the ratio σ₁/σ₂ is [0.657, ∞). What can be concluded about whether σ₁ is greater than σ₂?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"To conclude that σ₁ exceeds σ₂, the one-sided lower bound for σ₁/σ₂ must exceed 1. Because 0.657 is below 1, there is insufficient evidence that σ₁ is greater than σ₂. [VI.B.5]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["The interval proves that σ₁ is greater than σ₂.","The interval proves that σ₂ is greater than σ₁.","Because the interval excludes zero, the two standard deviations are different.","There is insufficient evidence that σ₁ is greater than σ₂."],"stem":"A 90% one-sided confidence interval for the ratio σ₁/σ₂ is [0.657, ∞). What can be concluded about whether σ₁ is greater than σ₂?","sub":"ana","why":"To conclude that σ₁ exceeds σ₂, the one-sided lower bound for σ₁/σ₂ must exceed 1. Because 0.657 is below 1, there is insufficient evidence that σ₁ is greater than σ₂. [VI.B.5]"},"revision":"7efa874be6d1575481c739074d327173c3241840ec7e839f2f839713cd74604b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-112":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"23\",\"25\",\"32\",\"33\"],\"stem\":\"A hypothesis test will compare the means of two independent normally distributed groups whose variances cannot be assumed equal. What degrees of freedom should be used given s₁ = 10.0, n₁ = 20, s₂ = 15.0, and n₂ = 15?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Welch df = (s₁²/n₁ + s₂²/n₂)² / [(s₁²/n₁)²/(n₁ − 1) + (s₂²/n₂)²/(n₂ − 1)] = (5 + 15)² / [25/19 + 225/14] = 400/(1.316 + 16.07) = 400/17.39 ≈ 23. [VI.B.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["23","25","32","33"],"stem":"A hypothesis test will compare the means of two independent normally distributed groups whose variances cannot be assumed equal. What degrees of freedom should be used given s₁ = 10.0, n₁ = 20, s₂ = 15.0, and n₂ = 15?","sub":"ana","why":"Welch df = (s₁²/n₁ + s₂²/n₂)² / [(s₁²/n₁)²/(n₁ − 1) + (s₂²/n₂)²/(n₂ − 1)] = (5 + 15)² / [25/19 + 225/14] = 400/(1.316 + 16.07) = 400/17.39 ≈ 23. [VI.B.5]"},"revision":"bd52370108e72419e10c6a72df1fb5fbcc0bede675874462a964f9fe3f38e2b5"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-113":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"correlated.\",\"normally distributed.\",\"categorized.\",\"uniformly distributed.\"],\"stem\":\"To use the chi-square distribution to test a population variance, the data must be independent and:\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"The chi-square test for a single population variance requires independent observations from a normally distributed population. [VI.B.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["correlated.","normally distributed.","categorized.","uniformly distributed."],"stem":"To use the chi-square distribution to test a population variance, the data must be independent and:","sub":"ana","why":"The chi-square test for a single population variance requires independent observations from a normally distributed population. [VI.B.5]"},"revision":"f5ee288b5ea669455189638711d17420c958c46fad469cae0494cc35337c1546"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-114":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Test statistic = 3.24; do not reject H₀\",\"Test statistic = 3.24; reject H₀\",\"Test statistic = 2.28; do not reject H₀\",\"Test statistic = 2.28; reject H₀\"],\"stem\":\"A Six Sigma team installs a new drilling-machine guide to reduce hole-location variation. Before improvement, σ = 0.005. Ten independent, normally distributed measurements after the change have s = 0.003. Test H₀: σ = 0.005 versus H₁: σ is less than 0.005 at α = 0.05. Use the lower-tail critical value χ²(0.05, 9) = 3.325.\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"The test statistic is χ² = (n − 1)s²/σ₀² = 9(0.003)²/(0.005)² = 3.24. Because 3.24 is below the lower-tail critical value 3.325, reject H₀; the data provide evidence that variation decreased. [VI.B.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Test statistic = 3.24; do not reject H₀","Test statistic = 3.24; reject H₀","Test statistic = 2.28; do not reject H₀","Test statistic = 2.28; reject H₀"],"stem":"A Six Sigma team installs a new drilling-machine guide to reduce hole-location variation. Before improvement, σ = 0.005. Ten independent, normally distributed measurements after the change have s = 0.003. Test H₀: σ = 0.005 versus H₁: σ is less than 0.005 at α = 0.05. Use the lower-tail critical value χ²(0.05, 9) = 3.325.","sub":"ana","why":"The test statistic is χ² = (n − 1)s²/σ₀² = 9(0.003)²/(0.005)² = 3.24. Because 3.24 is below the lower-tail critical value 3.325, reject H₀; the data provide evidence that variation decreased. [VI.B.5]"},"revision":"9635be4bb0c3a5a29bda5e542956ac7a3525245899c671720807feaae65d278d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-115":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"z₀ = −0.90; reject H₀\",\"z₀ = −0.90; do not reject H₀\",\"z₀ = −1.06; reject H₀\",\"z₀ = −1.06; do not reject H₀\"],\"stem\":\"Before an improvement project, the defective rate for a milling process was 5.7%. After the changes, 150 parts were run and 4.0% were defective. Using the normal approximation, test H₀: p = 0.057 versus H₁: p is less than 0.057 at α = 0.05. Use critical z = −1.645.\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"z₀ = (0.040 − 0.057)/√[0.057(0.943)/150] = −0.90. Since −0.90 is not below the lower-tail critical value −1.645, do not reject H₀; there is insufficient evidence that the defective rate decreased. [VI.B.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["z₀ = −0.90; reject H₀","z₀ = −0.90; do not reject H₀","z₀ = −1.06; reject H₀","z₀ = −1.06; do not reject H₀"],"stem":"Before an improvement project, the defective rate for a milling process was 5.7%. After the changes, 150 parts were run and 4.0% were defective. Using the normal approximation, test H₀: p = 0.057 versus H₁: p is less than 0.057 at α = 0.05. Use critical z = −1.645.","sub":"ana","why":"z₀ = (0.040 − 0.057)/√[0.057(0.943)/150] = −0.90. Since −0.90 is not below the lower-tail critical value −1.645, do not reject H₀; there is insufficient evidence that the defective rate decreased. [VI.B.5]"},"revision":"cd7dec6c16d217ebb8667d3eb4434e4c9efb78259e0e82a3f122db74974e62ac"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-116":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"z₀ = 2.89; reject H₀\",\"z₀ = 2.89; do not reject H₀\",\"t₀ = 2.89; reject H₀\",\"t₀ = 2.89; do not reject H₀\"],\"stem\":\"A new electric vehicle is tested for its maximum range on a single charge. Four vehicles produced the mileage results {260, 252, 254, 254}. Assuming mileage is normally distributed, test H₀: μ ≤ 250 versus H₁: μ is greater than 250 at α = 0.05. Use the one-tailed critical value t(0.05, 3) = 2.353.\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Because the population standard deviation is unknown and n = 4, use a one-sample t test. The sample mean is x̄ = 255 and s = 3.46, so t₀ = (255 − 250)/(3.46/√4) = 2.89. Since 2.89 exceeds 2.353, reject H₀; the data provide evidence that the average range exceeds 250 miles. [VI.B.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["z₀ = 2.89; reject H₀","z₀ = 2.89; do not reject H₀","t₀ = 2.89; reject H₀","t₀ = 2.89; do not reject H₀"],"stem":"A new electric vehicle is tested for its maximum range on a single charge. Four vehicles produced the mileage results {260, 252, 254, 254}. Assuming mileage is normally distributed, test H₀: μ ≤ 250 versus H₁: μ is greater than 250 at α = 0.05. Use the one-tailed critical value t(0.05, 3) = 2.353.","sub":"ana","why":"Because the population standard deviation is unknown and n = 4, use a one-sample t test. The sample mean is x̄ = 255 and s = 3.46, so t₀ = (255 − 250)/(3.46/√4) = 2.89. Since 2.89 exceeds 2.353, reject H₀; the data provide evidence that the average range exceeds 250 miles. [VI.B.5]"},"revision":"f95a7f6f37e32eb3535fd1e94fabf90a4848c8ee3f78c8ca6020a27af32c4b71"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-117":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"a specific combination of factor levels.\",\"an input to an experiment.\",\"the relationship between a factor and a response variable.\",\"an independent variable or assignable cause.\"],\"stem\":\"An experimental treatment is:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"An experimental treatment is a specific combination of factor levels. [VII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["a specific combination of factor levels.","an input to an experiment.","the relationship between a factor and a response variable.","an independent variable or assignable cause."],"stem":"An experimental treatment is:","sub":"imp","why":"An experimental treatment is a specific combination of factor levels. [VII.A.1]"},"revision":"fa017b1a02f62bc8c14899c513b31f4f23060e461a2e7e314dfb5d0aa3c6a205"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-118":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"A designed experiment can establish causality by changing inputs under controlled conditions.\",\"The number of samples in a designed experiment is unknown before the study is completed.\",\"Interactions cannot be estimated with a designed experiment.\",\"Ad hoc testing methods guarantee that model parameters can be estimated and statistically tested for significance.\"],\"stem\":\"How do designed experiments differ from trial-and-error, ad hoc testing methods?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"In contrast to trial-and-error methods, a designed experiment can establish causality by changing inputs under controlled conditions. [VII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["A designed experiment can establish causality by changing inputs under controlled conditions.","The number of samples in a designed experiment is unknown before the study is completed.","Interactions cannot be estimated with a designed experiment.","Ad hoc testing methods guarantee that model parameters can be estimated and statistically tested for significance."],"stem":"How do designed experiments differ from trial-and-error, ad hoc testing methods?","sub":"imp","why":"In contrast to trial-and-error methods, a designed experiment can establish causality by changing inputs under controlled conditions. [VII.A.1]"},"revision":"d13e26390184a4c70d4e49cf90270fa52ee718f3586acae42194de426f4dd6f8"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-119":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"1.5σ shift\",\"3.4 DPMO\",\"Y = f(X)\",\"OEE\"],\"stem\":\"Design of experiments (DoE) is most closely related to which Six Sigma concept?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Design of experiments is most closely aligned with the Six Sigma concept Y = f(X): controlled changes in the input factors X are used to quantify their effects on the response Y. [VII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["1.5σ shift","3.4 DPMO","Y = f(X)","OEE"],"stem":"Design of experiments (DoE) is most closely related to which Six Sigma concept?","sub":"imp","why":"Design of experiments is most closely aligned with the Six Sigma concept Y = f(X): controlled changes in the input factors X are used to quantify their effects on the response Y. [VII.A.1]"},"revision":"08693f9578fcc7c25b3bbc6c33dbd442d7a726328c5e3fb43ca1557521f8991e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-12":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Processes are system outputs.\",\"Systems are a series of interrelated processes.\",\"Systems are process inputs.\",\"Processes are a series of interrelated systems.\"],\"stem\":\"What is the relationship between a process and a system?\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"A system is a group of interdependent processes and people that together perform a common mission. [I.A.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Processes are system outputs.","Systems are a series of interrelated processes.","Systems are process inputs.","Processes are a series of interrelated systems."],"stem":"What is the relationship between a process and a system?","sub":"p1","why":"A system is a group of interdependent processes and people that together perform a common mission. [I.A.3]"},"revision":"f94c6a1de220ae8c0e372c5056301d4b59f4d36002989aff4b93214a88bd6bbe"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-120":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"the level of an experimental factor.\",\"an experimental treatment combination.\",\"the difference between an observation and its estimated value.\",\"the change in the response when the level of one or more factors is changed.\"],\"stem\":\"In a designed experiment, an effect is:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"An effect is the change in the response when the level of one or more factors is changed. [VII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["the level of an experimental factor.","an experimental treatment combination.","the difference between an observation and its estimated value.","the change in the response when the level of one or more factors is changed."],"stem":"In a designed experiment, an effect is:","sub":"imp","why":"An effect is the change in the response when the level of one or more factors is changed. [VII.A.1]"},"revision":"a36724e850244153af426917b56c511574b5b1cc7d22a37f2bf597c93e6e850c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-121":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"measurement mistakes made during the experimental run.\",\"errors in setting up the experimental treatments.\",\"variation in the response not accounted for by the experimental factors.\",\"mistakes made during the design phase of an experiment.\"],\"stem\":\"Experimental error is defined as:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Experimental error is defined as the variation in the response not accounted for by the experimental factors. [VII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["measurement mistakes made during the experimental run.","errors in setting up the experimental treatments.","variation in the response not accounted for by the experimental factors.","mistakes made during the design phase of an experiment."],"stem":"Experimental error is defined as:","sub":"imp","why":"Experimental error is defined as the variation in the response not accounted for by the experimental factors. [VII.A.1]"},"revision":"9b163a5f5a8f1211b3410ccabfef59f7bc9e56a578abe311a018446f0c3db6f3"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-122":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"mixture design.\",\"general factorial design.\",\"nested design.\",\"screening design.\"],\"stem\":\"An engineer is studying factors that are important in the annealing process. Annealing is performed at two different facilities, and each facility has three different ovens. Three batches will be run through each oven. The best experimental design for this situation is a:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"The best experimental design for this situation is a nested design because each facility has its own different ovens. [VII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["mixture design.","general factorial design.","nested design.","screening design."],"stem":"An engineer is studying factors that are important in the annealing process. Annealing is performed at two different facilities, and each facility has three different ovens. Three batches will be run through each oven. The best experimental design for this situation is a:","sub":"imp","why":"The best experimental design for this situation is a nested design because each facility has its own different ovens. [VII.A.1]"},"revision":"eb522bd2983275ac47947b9cae3be069062b20e5709e7800fb17c7946a1abfb2"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-123":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"replication.\",\"repetition.\",\"confounding.\",\"blocking.\"],\"stem\":\"During each experimental run, the factor settings are established once, then one unit is measured four consecutive times without resetting or rerandomizing the settings. This is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"The four measurements are repetitions within one unchanged experimental run. Replication would require independently resetting or rerandomizing the factor settings and repeating the treatment in a separate run. [VII.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["replication.","repetition.","confounding.","blocking."],"stem":"During each experimental run, the factor settings are established once, then one unit is measured four consecutive times without resetting or rerandomizing the settings. This is an example of:","sub":"imp","why":"The four measurements are repetitions within one unchanged experimental run. Replication would require independently resetting or rerandomizing the factor settings and repeating the treatment in a separate run. [VII.A.2]"},"revision":"5027d73813d13279797f531d7f2036a77b7f3fb14fbd9f92ac87da03c9ee5a9a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-124":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Blocking\",\"Confounding\",\"Replication\",\"Randomization\"],\"stem\":\"What experimental design technique would be used when an experiment may have to be conducted over several shifts?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"If an experiment may have to be conducted over several shifts, it is best to incorporate shifts as blocking variables. The blocks will account for any added variation introduced into the results due to the different conditions during the shifts. [VII.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Blocking","Confounding","Replication","Randomization"],"stem":"What experimental design technique would be used when an experiment may have to be conducted over several shifts?","sub":"imp","why":"If an experiment may have to be conducted over several shifts, it is best to incorporate shifts as blocking variables. The blocks will account for any added variation introduced into the results due to the different conditions during the shifts. [VII.A.2]"},"revision":"cc3f5ee8da8a08f3681d6fbaa85a5b099e00a6b1e2d14750bcd3bd75cf7ff343"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-125":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"randomization.\",\"orthogonal effects.\",\"replication.\",\"interaction.\"],\"stem\":\"Two or more factors, together, produce a result different than the sum of their separate effects. This is known as:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Interaction occurs when two or more factors, together, produce a result different than the sum of their separate effects. [VII.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["randomization.","orthogonal effects.","replication.","interaction."],"stem":"Two or more factors, together, produce a result different than the sum of their separate effects. This is known as:","sub":"imp","why":"Interaction occurs when two or more factors, together, produce a result different than the sum of their separate effects. [VII.A.2]"},"revision":"d9735daa3c89668f190adcf06263d23692a7ff121b39b618d4d19f1367b1a2f8"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-126":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Repetition reflects the sources of variability both within and between runs.\",\"Replication reflects the sources of variability both within and between runs.\",\"Repetition can only be used for fractional factorial designs.\",\"Replication can only be used for fractional factorial designs.\"],\"stem\":\"When conducting a designed experiment, what is the main difference between repetition and replication?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Replication reflects the sources of variability both within and between runs. [VII.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Repetition reflects the sources of variability both within and between runs.","Replication reflects the sources of variability both within and between runs.","Repetition can only be used for fractional factorial designs.","Replication can only be used for fractional factorial designs."],"stem":"When conducting a designed experiment, what is the main difference between repetition and replication?","sub":"imp","why":"Replication reflects the sources of variability both within and between runs. [VII.A.2]"},"revision":"0791367312beab9e342823a66ba4e96ee7979745a9c5ff7b620bec827358e228"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-127":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"confounded.\",\"biased.\",\"repeated.\",\"blocks.\"],\"stem\":\"An experimenter is investigating the performance of two injection molding machines. For the first machine, raw material from Supplier A is used to produce plastic switches. The second machine produces the switches using material from Supplier B. The factors machine and supplier are said to be:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"The factors machine and supplier are said to be confounded. Their separate effects on the response cannot be estimated. [VII.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["confounded.","biased.","repeated.","blocks."],"stem":"An experimenter is investigating the performance of two injection molding machines. For the first machine, raw material from Supplier A is used to produce plastic switches. The second machine produces the switches using material from Supplier B. The factors machine and supplier are said to be:","sub":"imp","why":"The factors machine and supplier are said to be confounded. Their separate effects on the response cannot be estimated. [VII.A.2]"},"revision":"019de61e7238e52dbd1a7f44b5d7e061a2fdeb303768ca0371f0486847eb6947"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-128":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"They are assumed to interact with each factor.\",\"They are not of direct experimental interest.\",\"They increase the number of experimental runs.\",\"They are indicative of a poorly designed experiment.\"],\"stem\":\"What is true about blocking variables in a designed experiment?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Blocking variables are not of experimental interest. They are incorporated into an experiment to account for any excess variability introduced by the blocks. [VII.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["They are assumed to interact with each factor.","They are not of direct experimental interest.","They increase the number of experimental runs.","They are indicative of a poorly designed experiment."],"stem":"What is true about blocking variables in a designed experiment?","sub":"imp","why":"Blocking variables are not of experimental interest. They are incorporated into an experiment to account for any excess variability introduced by the blocks. [VII.A.2]"},"revision":"8fa353db1926057b408992b0e702ac4e901959e8d17921378694596004dd89ee"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-129":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"minimize the effect of uncontrolled noise factors.\",\"incorporate variation into the responses.\",\"make running the experiment more difficult.\",\"account for a factor that adds variability to the response.\"],\"stem\":\"The main purpose of randomization in a designed experiment is to:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"The main purpose of randomization in a designed experiment is to minimize the effect of uncontrolled noise factors. [VII.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["minimize the effect of uncontrolled noise factors.","incorporate variation into the responses.","make running the experiment more difficult.","account for a factor that adds variability to the response."],"stem":"The main purpose of randomization in a designed experiment is to:","sub":"imp","why":"The main purpose of randomization in a designed experiment is to minimize the effect of uncontrolled noise factors. [VII.A.2]"},"revision":"03e924ffa7eba0edf87246d8300b1e3c7c7a105b0d6cd72361c09137b0418e30"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-13":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Regulatory agencies\",\"R&D department\",\"Shareholders\",\"Suppliers\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following groups is an example of an internal stakeholder?\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"The R&D department is internal to the company. All other entities listed are possible stakeholders but are external to the organization. [I.A.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Regulatory agencies","R&D department","Shareholders","Suppliers"],"stem":"Which of the following groups is an example of an internal stakeholder?","sub":"p1","why":"The R&D department is internal to the company. All other entities listed are possible stakeholders but are external to the organization. [I.A.3]"},"revision":"4856e58fbac7d089e38e1e310f2db60a071986dea8a18906fb1477cadc3b5580"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-130":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Full factorial\",\"Fractional factorial\",\"Mixture\",\"Latin square\"],\"stem\":\"Resolution of an experiment is a concern for which types of designs?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Resolution is a concern for fractional factorial designs. [VII.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Full factorial","Fractional factorial","Mixture","Latin square"],"stem":"Resolution of an experiment is a concern for which types of designs?","sub":"imp","why":"Resolution is a concern for fractional factorial designs. [VII.A.2]"},"revision":"e5cac96212d4a38582469283ff9acc2ed69084737e2b4ed445c214fb07b82642"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-131":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Full factorial design\",\"Latin square design\",\"Screening design\",\"One factor at a time design\"],\"stem\":\"A new process is being investigated, and the team has identified 12 factors that may affect the response variable of tensile strength. Which experiment should the team plan?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"To reduce the set of candidate factors as efficiently as possible, the team should plan to run a screening design. [VII.A.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Full factorial design","Latin square design","Screening design","One factor at a time design"],"stem":"A new process is being investigated, and the team has identified 12 factors that may affect the response variable of tensile strength. Which experiment should the team plan?","sub":"imp","why":"To reduce the set of candidate factors as efficiently as possible, the team should plan to run a screening design. [VII.A.3]"},"revision":"589aa2e446be2f21a546197d6706ebf5a5b35294ceb8057ea5210fa6245e212d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-132":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"2\",\"10\",\"25\",\"32\"],\"stem\":\"What is the minimum number of runs needed to conduct a full-factorial experiment with five factors, at two levels each?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"A two-level full factorial with five factors requires 2⁵ = 32 runs. [VII.A.6]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["2","10","25","32"],"stem":"What is the minimum number of runs needed to conduct a full-factorial experiment with five factors, at two levels each?","sub":"imp","why":"A two-level full factorial with five factors requires 2⁵ = 32 runs. [VII.A.6]"},"revision":"6ff58c1e722530a7d4a8e495c6525439f9f47a607187993c20e70e70af9c3b60"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-133":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"one factor at a time and sequential design.\",\"all-in-one experiments and multitude of effects.\",\"sparsity of effects and sequential design.\",\"maximizing confounding effects and heredity of effects.\"],\"stem\":\"Some principles of designed experiments include:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Some principles of designed experiments include the sparsity of effects and sequential design. [VII.A.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["one factor at a time and sequential design.","all-in-one experiments and multitude of effects.","sparsity of effects and sequential design.","maximizing confounding effects and heredity of effects."],"stem":"Some principles of designed experiments include:","sub":"imp","why":"Some principles of designed experiments include the sparsity of effects and sequential design. [VII.A.3]"},"revision":"5bf68394e448fa41d28130c7b17090161c0e39498d444fb764ffc865bf413cf6"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-134":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Plackett-Burman\",\"General factorial\",\"Fractional factorial\",\"Mixture\"],\"stem\":\"If an experimenter wished to have two factors at two levels each, and one factor at three levels, which type of experimental design would be appropriate?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"A general factorial would be appropriate for a design with two factors at two levels each, and one factor at three levels. [VII.A.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Plackett-Burman","General factorial","Fractional factorial","Mixture"],"stem":"If an experimenter wished to have two factors at two levels each, and one factor at three levels, which type of experimental design would be appropriate?","sub":"imp","why":"A general factorial would be appropriate for a design with two factors at two levels each, and one factor at three levels. [VII.A.3]"},"revision":"09c2b4371179a083e8ec53188a85ce175fb7bb6fb63e41a0bffcb704c95bd607"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-135":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"each block contains all the treatments.\",\"each block is randomly assigned to a treatment.\",\"there is no restriction on randomization for blocks or treatments.\",\"blocks are treated as confounding effects.\"],\"stem\":\"A randomized block experiment is one in which:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"A randomized block experiment is one in which each block contains all the treatments. [VII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["each block contains all the treatments.","each block is randomly assigned to a treatment.","there is no restriction on randomization for blocks or treatments.","blocks are treated as confounding effects."],"stem":"A randomized block experiment is one in which:","sub":"imp","why":"A randomized block experiment is one in which each block contains all the treatments. [VII.A.4]"},"revision":"813c84191ad80d8535fa7ebb32399e11080ac51ab4efb35eea57232eb5e1ff01"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-136":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"there is one control factor and two blocks, all with the same number of levels.\",\"there are two control factors and one block, all with the same number of levels.\",\"blocking variables interact with other blocking variables.\",\"the factor of interest is confounded with at least one block.\"],\"stem\":\"A Latin square design is one in which:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"A Latin square design is one in which there is one control factor and two blocks, all with the same number of levels. [VII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["there is one control factor and two blocks, all with the same number of levels.","there are two control factors and one block, all with the same number of levels.","blocking variables interact with other blocking variables.","the factor of interest is confounded with at least one block."],"stem":"A Latin square design is one in which:","sub":"imp","why":"A Latin square design is one in which there is one control factor and two blocks, all with the same number of levels. [VII.A.4]"},"revision":"92bb4d71551875af575c01753e59fff22ac767a9cb6145881e9d3ab1e47b2f42"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-137":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Latin square\",\"Split plot\",\"Fractional factorial\",\"Mixture\"],\"stem\":\"An experiment is being conducted to optimize a packaging process. One factor at three levels and two blocking factors at three levels each will be used to run the experiment. The experimenter would most likely select what type of design for this experiment?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"A Latin square is appropriate because there is one treatment factor and two blocking factors, all at three levels. This produces a 3 × 3 Latin square with nine runs. [VII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Latin square","Split plot","Fractional factorial","Mixture"],"stem":"An experiment is being conducted to optimize a packaging process. One factor at three levels and two blocking factors at three levels each will be used to run the experiment. The experimenter would most likely select what type of design for this experiment?","sub":"imp","why":"A Latin square is appropriate because there is one treatment factor and two blocking factors, all at three levels. This produces a 3 × 3 Latin square with nine runs. [VII.A.4]"},"revision":"ffa982cd2b8711bbc5b4e8016362ff78ff00663f7e800df14f37318b9037444c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-138":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"main effects are confounded with other main effects.\",\"some main effects are confounded with two-factor interactions.\",\"some two-factor interactions are confounded with the mean.\",\"some main effects are confounded with the mean.\"],\"stem\":\"In a Resolution III experiment:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"In a Resolution III experiment, some main effects are confounded with two-factor interactions. [VII.A.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["main effects are confounded with other main effects.","some main effects are confounded with two-factor interactions.","some two-factor interactions are confounded with the mean.","some main effects are confounded with the mean."],"stem":"In a Resolution III experiment:","sub":"imp","why":"In a Resolution III experiment, some main effects are confounded with two-factor interactions. [VII.A.5]"},"revision":"609642e32624a751646fabd8c53d157f4543fef33b7fb7e98ea4a9c8964849ea"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-139":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Run a follow-up factorial experiment to de-alias the interaction effects.\",\"Run a one-factor-at-a-time experiment.\",\"Run a Plackett-Burman design.\",\"Add blocking variables to the design and rerun.\"],\"stem\":\"A fractional-factorial experiment shows that there is a significant two-factor interaction between factors A and B. However, this effect is confounded with the interaction of factors C and D. What might the experimenter do next?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"To de-alias confounded interactions in a fractional-factorial design, the experimenter can run a follow-up experiment such as a foldover design. [VII.A.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Run a follow-up factorial experiment to de-alias the interaction effects.","Run a one-factor-at-a-time experiment.","Run a Plackett-Burman design.","Add blocking variables to the design and rerun."],"stem":"A fractional-factorial experiment shows that there is a significant two-factor interaction between factors A and B. However, this effect is confounded with the interaction of factors C and D. What might the experimenter do next?","sub":"imp","why":"To de-alias confounded interactions in a fractional-factorial design, the experimenter can run a follow-up experiment such as a foldover design. [VII.A.5]"},"revision":"852dc139128b34cceb6a34f406cd8639f72d4df6a6e14717e835d3c2c04d4b20"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-14":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"stakeholder.\",\"shareholder.\",\"regulator.\",\"employee.\"],\"stem\":\"Any individual or group with an interest in the business is considered a/an:\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"A stakeholder is defined as anyone with an interest in an issue or anyone who can influence or be affected by an action or change. [II.A]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["stakeholder.","shareholder.","regulator.","employee."],"stem":"Any individual or group with an interest in the business is considered a/an:","sub":"p2","why":"A stakeholder is defined as anyone with an interest in an issue or anyone who can influence or be affected by an action or change. [II.A]"},"revision":"3b45a716245466813ade293285caa0d376e796630455b45b6ca948b8f1cfdb55"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-140":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"highly controllable.\",\"risk-based.\",\"the operator’s responsibility.\",\"management’s responsibility.\"],\"stem\":\"Common cause variation is considered to be the noise of the process. This type of variation is:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Common cause variation, the noise of the process, is considered to be management’s responsibility. [VIII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["highly controllable.","risk-based.","the operator’s responsibility.","management’s responsibility."],"stem":"Common cause variation is considered to be the noise of the process. This type of variation is:","sub":"con","why":"Common cause variation, the noise of the process, is considered to be management’s responsibility. [VIII.A.1]"},"revision":"162a025e0959197b826a31d51d9b31b7e0d72a7522d34dbdd78cbb1ba3d06018"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-141":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"a centerline.\",\"control limits.\",\"specification limits.\",\"subgroups.\"],\"stem\":\"Control charts are graphical tools used to monitor a process. Control charts generally do not contain or use:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Control charts use a centerline, control limits, and time-ordered subgroup data. Specification limits are customer or design requirements and are generally not part of the control chart. [VIII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["a centerline.","control limits.","specification limits.","subgroups."],"stem":"Control charts are graphical tools used to monitor a process. Control charts generally do not contain or use:","sub":"con","why":"Control charts use a centerline, control limits, and time-ordered subgroup data. Specification limits are customer or design requirements and are generally not part of the control chart. [VIII.A.1]"},"revision":"af54135aa7e6eade1a14ad1fa5c0d2ab2f43e44a2e136f72613606ca9eff3e9a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-142":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"map cause and effect.\",\"determine when to take action on a process.\",\"set specification limits to reasonable values.\",\"provide strategic metrics.\"],\"stem\":\"One of the purposes of statistical process control is to:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"One purpose of SPC is to distinguish routine variation from signals that require investigation, thereby determining when to take action on a process. [VIII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["map cause and effect.","determine when to take action on a process.","set specification limits to reasonable values.","provide strategic metrics."],"stem":"One of the purposes of statistical process control is to:","sub":"con","why":"One purpose of SPC is to distinguish routine variation from signals that require investigation, thereby determining when to take action on a process. [VIII.A.1]"},"revision":"d50d835f06c6a962f6fce864595be20976d37a2e13dbcdab9a11c5001ac25917"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-143":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"it differentiates between common and special causes of variation.\",\"over time, it can lead to overadjustments to the process.\",\"it signals when common cause variation is present.\",\"it can be used only for continuous data.\"],\"stem\":\"A primary feature of a statistical process control chart is that:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"A primary feature of a statistical process control chart is that it differentiates between common and special causes of variation. [VIII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["it differentiates between common and special causes of variation.","over time, it can lead to overadjustments to the process.","it signals when common cause variation is present.","it can be used only for continuous data."],"stem":"A primary feature of a statistical process control chart is that:","sub":"con","why":"A primary feature of a statistical process control chart is that it differentiates between common and special causes of variation. [VIII.A.1]"},"revision":"9a0f95faac506fb9c365f76f481581190541fcf791ef480e6777533deeb1c5b6"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-144":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"The process output is out of specification.\",\"The distribution of the process output has changed.\",\"Common cause variation is acting on the process.\",\"The process has too many defects.\"],\"stem\":\"An X̄-R chart signals an out-of-control condition. What can be said about the process?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"An X̄-R chart signals an out-of-control condition, which indicates that the distribution of the process output has changed. [VIII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["The process output is out of specification.","The distribution of the process output has changed.","Common cause variation is acting on the process.","The process has too many defects."],"stem":"An X̄-R chart signals an out-of-control condition. What can be said about the process?","sub":"con","why":"An X̄-R chart signals an out-of-control condition, which indicates that the distribution of the process output has changed. [VIII.A.1]"},"revision":"fea98dcbd6d4220cdec88b68322f35d533bed9d7fbc8f923900bc7eef627b716"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-145":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"the voice of the process.\",\"the special cause variation zone.\",\"the specification zone.\",\"the voice of the customer.\"],\"stem\":\"The area between the upper and lower control limits of a control chart is often referred to as:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"The area between the upper and lower control limits of a control chart is often referred to as the voice of the process. [VIII.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["the voice of the process.","the special cause variation zone.","the specification zone.","the voice of the customer."],"stem":"The area between the upper and lower control limits of a control chart is often referred to as:","sub":"con","why":"The area between the upper and lower control limits of a control chart is often referred to as the voice of the process. [VIII.A.1]"},"revision":"de839036fbabb0ad8bb6a92a38fbef83360e382b35a4907b6ade62efbfedadff"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-146":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"which variable is performing the best.\",\"which variable has the least impact on the output.\",\"which variable is a lagging indicator.\",\"contractual requirements from the customer.\"],\"stem\":\"The process variable to be selected for control charting is sometimes determined by:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"The process variable to be selected for control charting is sometimes determined by contractual requirements from the customer. [VIII.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["which variable is performing the best.","which variable has the least impact on the output.","which variable is a lagging indicator.","contractual requirements from the customer."],"stem":"The process variable to be selected for control charting is sometimes determined by:","sub":"con","why":"The process variable to be selected for control charting is sometimes determined by contractual requirements from the customer. [VIII.A.2]"},"revision":"270b1bc4ab308bffe89291263a5e49036c8704efb6a42bdb2c6d8f5e5c560e3e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-147":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"subgroup units should be as similar as possible.\",\"maximizing subgroup variation improves detection.\",\"subgroups should represent common and special causes of variation.\",\"subgroups represent the “between” variation in a control chart.\"],\"stem\":\"The idea behind rational subgroups is that:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"The idea behind rational subgrouping is that units within a subgroup should be as similar as possible. [VIII.A.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["subgroup units should be as similar as possible.","maximizing subgroup variation improves detection.","subgroups should represent common and special causes of variation.","subgroups represent the “between” variation in a control chart."],"stem":"The idea behind rational subgroups is that:","sub":"con","why":"The idea behind rational subgrouping is that units within a subgroup should be as similar as possible. [VIII.A.3]"},"revision":"a5da60304bb417896c00e9ea995dcb549652276b34a1c3784b99b0a3df60365e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-148":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Rational subgrouping will have to be considered.\",\"The tensile strength must be converted to pass/fail data.\",\"The specifications for tensile strength will have to be adjusted.\",\"No changes will be needed.\"],\"stem\":\"Pieces from five spot-welding stations flow onto a common conveyor belt. An operator chooses five consecutive pieces each hour to test tensile strength. How should the sampling procedure be reconsidered before using an X̄-R chart?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Rational subgrouping must be reconsidered because five consecutive pieces may come from different welding stations and therefore mix distinct process sources. [VIII.A.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Rational subgrouping will have to be considered.","The tensile strength must be converted to pass/fail data.","The specifications for tensile strength will have to be adjusted.","No changes will be needed."],"stem":"Pieces from five spot-welding stations flow onto a common conveyor belt. An operator chooses five consecutive pieces each hour to test tensile strength. How should the sampling procedure be reconsidered before using an X̄-R chart?","sub":"con","why":"Rational subgrouping must be reconsidered because five consecutive pieces may come from different welding stations and therefore mix distinct process sources. [VIII.A.3]"},"revision":"cef8f248e1af4d297946c773af7fa623a7e0a6dd68f6dcf9be7b8497e1f9f140"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-149":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"I-MR\",\"X̄-S\",\"np\",\"EWMA\"],\"stem\":\"A control chart will be created to monitor a process that has an automated vision system to measure height, the critical feature. A subgroup size of 15 will be used. Which of the following control charts is best suited for this application?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Use an X̄-S chart when measuring continuous variables with sample sizes greater than or equal to 10. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["I-MR","X̄-S","np","EWMA"],"stem":"A control chart will be created to monitor a process that has an automated vision system to measure height, the critical feature. A subgroup size of 15 will be used. Which of the following control charts is best suited for this application?","sub":"con","why":"Use an X̄-S chart when measuring continuous variables with sample sizes greater than or equal to 10. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"8d3ab5152e8377380b58e76e19932ff588133e55cff7342b32c5f57ed18094fd"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-15":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"company newsletter.\",\"company annual report.\",\"human resources function.\",\"communications management process.\"],\"stem\":\"Delivering the right level of information, to the right stakeholders, at the right time is part of the:\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"The communication management process ensures delivery of the right level of information to the right stakeholders at the right time. [II.A]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["company newsletter.","company annual report.","human resources function.","communications management process."],"stem":"Delivering the right level of information, to the right stakeholders, at the right time is part of the:","sub":"p2","why":"The communication management process ensures delivery of the right level of information to the right stakeholders at the right time. [II.A]"},"revision":"df2387792f297053c79954191a54c38def17471dbfbd55898373a0ba3860a5e3"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-150":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"p\",\"np\",\"I-MR\",\"c\"],\"stem\":\"A powder coating line will be monitored for quality. The number of defects per item will be counted using a constant sample size. Which of the following control charts is best suited for this application?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Use a c-chart when counting defects per sampling unit with constant sample size. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["p","np","I-MR","c"],"stem":"A powder coating line will be monitored for quality. The number of defects per item will be counted using a constant sample size. Which of the following control charts is best suited for this application?","sub":"con","why":"Use a c-chart when counting defects per sampling unit with constant sample size. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"8218477269c63c1c7e4cddcc2ef0927f60e753d2b82c8c2c1dfe044b92470b19"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-151":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following subgroup sizes for an X̄-R chart would most likely be used if measurement were costly?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"A subgroup size of 2 minimizes measurement cost while still permitting an X̄-R chart. A subgroup size of 1 requires an individuals and moving-range chart. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["1","2","3","4"],"stem":"Which of the following subgroup sizes for an X̄-R chart would most likely be used if measurement were costly?","sub":"con","why":"A subgroup size of 2 minimizes measurement cost while still permitting an X̄-R chart. A subgroup size of 1 requires an individuals and moving-range chart. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"f4d81eecbe74a589080e5868ee8872ce9c87dc3c930c9aef5f3678efe99ff55e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-152":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"easier to hand-calculate.\",\"more sensitive to variation.\",\"more accurate.\",\"useful for large subgroup sizes.\"],\"stem\":\"An R chart is used rather than an S chart because it is:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"An R chart is used rather than an S chart because it is easier to hand-calculate. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["easier to hand-calculate.","more sensitive to variation.","more accurate.","useful for large subgroup sizes."],"stem":"An R chart is used rather than an S chart because it is:","sub":"con","why":"An R chart is used rather than an S chart because it is easier to hand-calculate. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"daf5e374af6ea578eafcb9a99af6329e384dde167dbbe8595d7c74e42ccbf407"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-153":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"p\",\"Median\",\"EWMA\",\"X̄-R\"],\"stem\":\"If only one continuous measurement can be collected at a time, which chart can be used to monitor the process?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"An exponentially weighted moving average (EWMA) chart can monitor continuous observations collected one at a time. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["p","Median","EWMA","X̄-R"],"stem":"If only one continuous measurement can be collected at a time, which chart can be used to monitor the process?","sub":"con","why":"An exponentially weighted moving average (EWMA) chart can monitor continuous observations collected one at a time. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"cf96177f1ecdeacd28d2fb4b5f679d326c4b76d12bb97aab6452d1aba6835eaf"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-154":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Moving average\",\"Median\",\"S chart\",\"EWMA\"],\"stem\":\"Which chart is a user-friendly control chart for the operator?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"A median chart is user-friendly for the operator because it does not require any calculation. An odd sample size is used, and all sample values are plotted on the chart. The middle point, or median, is then circled. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Moving average","Median","S chart","EWMA"],"stem":"Which chart is a user-friendly control chart for the operator?","sub":"con","why":"A median chart is user-friendly for the operator because it does not require any calculation. An odd sample size is used, and all sample values are plotted on the chart. The middle point, or median, is then circled. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"55556a1c79542a01c8f035c8231e47a85572a8604d39f2bae2c3f11252a167b9"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-155":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"easier to understand.\",\"easier to create.\",\"a better measure of variation.\",\"based only on the subgroup’s minimum and maximum values.\"],\"stem\":\"An S chart might be used as a measure of dispersion rather than an R chart because it is:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"The standard deviation plotted in an S chart is a better measure of variation than the range. The range ignores the data between the extremes. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["easier to understand.","easier to create.","a better measure of variation.","based only on the subgroup’s minimum and maximum values."],"stem":"An S chart might be used as a measure of dispersion rather than an R chart because it is:","sub":"con","why":"The standard deviation plotted in an S chart is a better measure of variation than the range. The range ignores the data between the extremes. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"78a2bea5a7e1515357269014b031c126da202789bdd0cf1ea7faabda30e7650b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-156":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"The range is too difficult to calculate with large samples.\",\"The range is not robust against outliers.\",\"The range becomes too small as sample size increases.\",\"The range is unbiased.\"],\"stem\":\"Why is an R chart generally not recommended for subgroup sizes greater than 9?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"An R chart uses only the subgroup extremes, so it is sensitive to outliers and discards increasingly more information as subgroup size grows. For subgroup sizes greater than 9, an S chart is generally preferred. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["The range is too difficult to calculate with large samples.","The range is not robust against outliers.","The range becomes too small as sample size increases.","The range is unbiased."],"stem":"Why is an R chart generally not recommended for subgroup sizes greater than 9?","sub":"con","why":"An R chart uses only the subgroup extremes, so it is sensitive to outliers and discards increasingly more information as subgroup size grows. For subgroup sizes greater than 9, an S chart is generally preferred. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"99f866bf13f4bb3c62324c806a2b5add0b5529172a896f1c1cc0c1016e8bcd08"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-157":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"monitoring the count of nonconformities in a product.\",\"monitoring the fraction nonconforming.\",\"cases in which there are few opportunities for nonconformities to occur.\",\"continuous data.\"],\"stem\":\"A p-chart is used for:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"A p-chart is used for monitoring the fraction nonconforming. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["monitoring the count of nonconformities in a product.","monitoring the fraction nonconforming.","cases in which there are few opportunities for nonconformities to occur.","continuous data."],"stem":"A p-chart is used for:","sub":"con","why":"A p-chart is used for monitoring the fraction nonconforming. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"f16db8e7a99f08ec960a733636ad379d8b416b15ec37cffcc91e8552124a705c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-158":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"so that employees can understand it better.\",\"because of the difficulty of variable part-to-part size.\",\"so that an average subgroup size n can be used.\",\"so that the lower control limit will be eliminated.\"],\"stem\":\"A fraction nonconforming chart is frequently converted to a percent nonconforming chart:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"A fraction nonconforming chart is frequently converted to a percent nonconforming chart so that employees can understand it better. Percentages are often easier for people to evaluate than decimals or fractions. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["so that employees can understand it better.","because of the difficulty of variable part-to-part size.","so that an average subgroup size n can be used.","so that the lower control limit will be eliminated."],"stem":"A fraction nonconforming chart is frequently converted to a percent nonconforming chart:","sub":"con","why":"A fraction nonconforming chart is frequently converted to a percent nonconforming chart so that employees can understand it better. Percentages are often easier for people to evaluate than decimals or fractions. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"187fc75a3808941c2fc4394c37ae736257274525e2e6247f4e1f238fda80ba85"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-159":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Define, measure, analyze, improve, control\",\"Design, define, measure, analyze, validate\",\"Define, measure, analyze, design, verify\",\"Design, define, validate, and verify\"],\"stem\":\"Which sequence lists the phases of DMADV, a common Design for Six Sigma methodology?\",\"sub\":\"dfss\",\"why\":\"The DFSS phases are define, measure, analyze, design, and verify (DMADV). [IX.A]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Define, measure, analyze, improve, control","Design, define, measure, analyze, validate","Define, measure, analyze, design, verify","Design, define, validate, and verify"],"stem":"Which sequence lists the phases of DMADV, a common Design for Six Sigma methodology?","sub":"dfss","why":"The DFSS phases are define, measure, analyze, design, and verify (DMADV). [IX.A]"},"revision":"9f593a5fac331c4cb23542b69241392aed5408dcf8b67f4b202d859faa02af4a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-16":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Newsletter\",\"Annual report\",\"Quarterly sales meeting\",\"Announcements\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following forms of communication can be best used to acknowledge a key milestone?\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"Announcements, whether formal or informal, are communications used to acknowledge a key milestone. [II.A]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Newsletter","Annual report","Quarterly sales meeting","Announcements"],"stem":"Which of the following forms of communication can be best used to acknowledge a key milestone?","sub":"p2","why":"Announcements, whether formal or informal, are communications used to acknowledge a key milestone. [II.A]"},"revision":"b898989d9aeacf644710b2e0431e5ce810d68c48352bb6a54520f13c47cc7ec2"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-160":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"to eliminate waste.\",\"to perform kaizen events.\",\"to improve an existing process using DMAIC.\",\"to design new products and processes to meet Six Sigma quality objectives.\"],\"stem\":\"Design for Six Sigma (DFSS) is a methodology:\",\"sub\":\"dfss\",\"why\":\"DFSS is used to design new products and processes so that their outputs meet demanding quality objectives from the outset. [IX.A]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["to eliminate waste.","to perform kaizen events.","to improve an existing process using DMAIC.","to design new products and processes to meet Six Sigma quality objectives."],"stem":"Design for Six Sigma (DFSS) is a methodology:","sub":"dfss","why":"DFSS is used to design new products and processes so that their outputs meet demanding quality objectives from the outset. [IX.A]"},"revision":"fd5545cb3c623ae8bdb26e07cc12c3a749e9c1a0236597814112c530043473e2"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-161":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Control\",\"Measure\",\"Define\",\"Analyze\"],\"stem\":\"Which DMAIC phase is not a named phase in DMADV?\",\"sub\":\"dfss\",\"why\":\"DMADV consists of Define, Measure, Analyze, Design, and Verify. Control is not a named DMADV phase. [IX.A]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Control","Measure","Define","Analyze"],"stem":"Which DMAIC phase is not a named phase in DMADV?","sub":"dfss","why":"DMADV consists of Define, Measure, Analyze, Design, and Verify. Control is not a named DMADV phase. [IX.A]"},"revision":"6eec2bca5b816e5811af308d00adc9f165ec299a7f51fc923803ff40fdcd3a2e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-162":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"DMEDI begins by improving an existing measured process.\",\"DMEDI develops a new design, so no existing-process baseline is available.\",\"DMAIC uses QFD, but DMEDI does not.\",\"DMAIC cannot measure current process performance.\"],\"stem\":\"The Measure phase in DMEDI differs from the Measure phase in DMAIC because:\",\"sub\":\"dfss\",\"why\":\"DMEDI is used for a new design, so there is no existing-process baseline to measure. DMAIC, by contrast, measures the current performance of an existing process. [IX.A]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["DMEDI begins by improving an existing measured process.","DMEDI develops a new design, so no existing-process baseline is available.","DMAIC uses QFD, but DMEDI does not.","DMAIC cannot measure current process performance."],"stem":"The Measure phase in DMEDI differs from the Measure phase in DMAIC because:","sub":"dfss","why":"DMEDI is used for a new design, so there is no existing-process baseline to measure. DMAIC, by contrast, measures the current performance of an existing process. [IX.A]"},"revision":"543f2529acd9a59effb7783f757c7e079266cbff938044cd133b16c5f5ddf62f"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-163":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"maintain quality during every earlier phase.\",\"confirm that the completed design meets its specified requirements and performs as designed.\",\"confirm that design inputs describe an intended use.\",\"control every change made during design.\"],\"stem\":\"The V in DMADV stands for Verify, whose purpose is to:\",\"sub\":\"dfss\",\"why\":\"Verify confirms that the completed design meets its specified design requirements and performs as designed. [IX.A]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["maintain quality during every earlier phase.","confirm that the completed design meets its specified requirements and performs as designed.","confirm that design inputs describe an intended use.","control every change made during design."],"stem":"The V in DMADV stands for Verify, whose purpose is to:","sub":"dfss","why":"Verify confirms that the completed design meets its specified design requirements and performs as designed. [IX.A]"},"revision":"a1730bb989c0ee2ef90985456dd126fe564d7c0680c492c56ede3d1b2f67c606"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-164":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Both have a define phase.\",\"Both use benchmarking to measure current performance.\",\"Both have an explore phase.\",\"Both use simulations to verify design requirements.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following features is a similarity between DMAIC and DMEDI?\",\"sub\":\"dfss\",\"why\":\"Both DMAIC and DMEDI have a common define phase. [IX.A]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Both have a define phase.","Both use benchmarking to measure current performance.","Both have an explore phase.","Both use simulations to verify design requirements."],"stem":"Which of the following features is a similarity between DMAIC and DMEDI?","sub":"dfss","why":"Both DMAIC and DMEDI have a common define phase. [IX.A]"},"revision":"91a0dc8bec4331017e223e1a7b09ff4eafa23cdacb63f940522aeb8a713043eb"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-165":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"an industry term for Six Sigma.\",\"a variable that can be any constraint to the design process.\",\"an industry term for manufacturing.\",\"a Japanese symbol for zero defects.\"],\"stem\":\"The X in Design for X is:\",\"sub\":\"dfss\",\"why\":\"The X in Design for X is a variable that can be any constraint to the design process. [IX.B]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["an industry term for Six Sigma.","a variable that can be any constraint to the design process.","an industry term for manufacturing.","a Japanese symbol for zero defects."],"stem":"The X in Design for X is:","sub":"dfss","why":"The X in Design for X is a variable that can be any constraint to the design process. [IX.B]"},"revision":"5104f22995a1c59d20fd10ecc3dd037e9eabfb1e5861f77297c888ca3d593b13"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-166":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Control chart\",\"Measles chart\",\"Flowchart\",\"House of quality\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following quality tools is used to diagram a process?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Flowcharts are graphical representations of the steps in a process and are drawn to better understand processes. [V.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Control chart","Measles chart","Flowchart","House of quality"],"stem":"Which of the following quality tools is used to diagram a process?","sub":"mea","why":"Flowcharts are graphical representations of the steps in a process and are drawn to better understand processes. [V.A.2]"},"revision":"c3e941ea07fa005b102e03db9e11e985bd978c2e3a10817e4ee3922be22f5a7d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-167":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"SWOT analysis\",\"SMART goals\",\"Contingency planning\",\"Risk analysis\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following tools would be the most appropriate for crisis management?\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"Contingency planning (also called a plan B) is used for crisis management, business continuity, and asset security. [I.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["SWOT analysis","SMART goals","Contingency planning","Risk analysis"],"stem":"Which of the following tools would be the most appropriate for crisis management?","sub":"p1","why":"Contingency planning (also called a plan B) is used for crisis management, business continuity, and asset security. [I.A.4]"},"revision":"24f2b184ef98273b28fd365d685d903e7de4726ca1270d15f57b05618bfd4268"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-168":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Opportunities and strengths\",\"Weaknesses and threats\",\"Opportunities and threats\",\"Strengths and weaknesses\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following categories in a SWOT analysis are considered external?\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"Opportunities and threats are considered external to the company''s direct control. Strengths and weaknesses are identified with respect to the internal capabilities of an organization, while opportunities and threats look outside the organization to identify opportunities for the organization and threats to the organization. [I.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Opportunities and strengths","Weaknesses and threats","Opportunities and threats","Strengths and weaknesses"],"stem":"Which of the following categories in a SWOT analysis are considered external?","sub":"p1","why":"Opportunities and threats are considered external to the company''s direct control. Strengths and weaknesses are identified with respect to the internal capabilities of an organization, while opportunities and threats look outside the organization to identify opportunities for the organization and threats to the organization. [I.A.4]"},"revision":"5dd0f94351a9ce4ad237b695d72db3cafc9707862196a73afa1b12472dcc504f"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-169":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"A routine product-label update\",\"Loss of key personnel\",\"A planned shift rotation\",\"Normal preventive maintenance\"],\"stem\":\"Which is a typical business-continuity risk?\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"Business-continuity planning addresses disruptions such as loss of key personnel, sudden market changes, labor actions, data loss, or disasters. [I.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["A routine product-label update","Loss of key personnel","A planned shift rotation","Normal preventive maintenance"],"stem":"Which is a typical business-continuity risk?","sub":"p1","why":"Business-continuity planning addresses disruptions such as loss of key personnel, sudden market changes, labor actions, data loss, or disasters. [I.A.4]"},"revision":"455e51e7033f34d83bf031a9097af671ad666e7b2ee41efbbe5eb609beb4e2ca"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-17":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"High Influence and High Importance\",\"High Influence and Low Importance\",\"Low Influence and High Importance\",\"Low Influence and Low Importance\"],\"stem\":\"In the stakeholder influence–importance matrix used for this project, frontline workers have little formal decision authority, but their process knowledge and adoption are essential. How should they be classified?\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"The frontline workers would likely have low organizational influence but high importance to the project’s success because the change directly affects their work and depends on their knowledge and adoption. [II.A]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["High Influence and High Importance","High Influence and Low Importance","Low Influence and High Importance","Low Influence and Low Importance"],"stem":"In the stakeholder influence–importance matrix used for this project, frontline workers have little formal decision authority, but their process knowledge and adoption are essential. How should they be classified?","sub":"p2","why":"The frontline workers would likely have low organizational influence but high importance to the project’s success because the change directly affects their work and depends on their knowledge and adoption. [II.A]"},"revision":"35472a2195ba27eeb7e078e652c2dad43a19984e412c80b8f2bf2b9eb4f8e75b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-170":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"measured intangibles.\",\"false savings.\",\"imaginary outcomes.\",\"unmeasurable benefits.\"],\"stem\":\"Six Sigma projects can deliver nonfinancial benefits, such as increased customer satisfaction. These benefits are referred to as:\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"Nonfinancial benefits or soft savings such as increased customer satisfaction are referred to as measured intangibles. For example, customer satisfaction can be measured via net promoter scores, market share, or returns reports. [I.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["measured intangibles.","false savings.","imaginary outcomes.","unmeasurable benefits."],"stem":"Six Sigma projects can deliver nonfinancial benefits, such as increased customer satisfaction. These benefits are referred to as:","sub":"p1","why":"Nonfinancial benefits or soft savings such as increased customer satisfaction are referred to as measured intangibles. For example, customer satisfaction can be measured via net promoter scores, market share, or returns reports. [I.A.4]"},"revision":"e5cf5f78fed24df2238b6e28d583b30a0428c1c7d7769838ec91ac7b386ddc5d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-171":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Loss of key personnel\",\"Pandemics\",\"Product recalls\",\"On-the-job accidents\"],\"stem\":\"Under the guide’s contingency-planning categories, which event is listed specifically under asset security?\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"All of the options may be addressed in contingency plans, but product recalls would be addressed in an asset security contingency plan. [I.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Loss of key personnel","Pandemics","Product recalls","On-the-job accidents"],"stem":"Under the guide’s contingency-planning categories, which event is listed specifically under asset security?","sub":"p1","why":"All of the options may be addressed in contingency plans, but product recalls would be addressed in an asset security contingency plan. [I.A.4]"},"revision":"2d4b99b28b5c001d957195e089d8bf6cf483fa682c11d7e0a02d02580fda0d0f"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-172":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"increased customer satisfaction.\",\"documented scrap-cost reduction.\",\"increased booked profit.\",\"decreased rework expense.\"],\"stem\":\"Six Sigma projects may deliver soft savings such as:\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"Increased customer satisfaction is a soft benefit because it is valuable but is not directly booked as a current cash-flow saving. The other listed outcomes are explicitly monetized hard savings. [I.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["increased customer satisfaction.","documented scrap-cost reduction.","increased booked profit.","decreased rework expense."],"stem":"Six Sigma projects may deliver soft savings such as:","sub":"p1","why":"Increased customer satisfaction is a soft benefit because it is valuable but is not directly booked as a current cash-flow saving. The other listed outcomes are explicitly monetized hard savings. [I.A.4]"},"revision":"e8ee5ba288679f0cc6d9eaf9761406593ae081a23712166026639606ee56c9a9"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-173":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"ensures that their projects are aligned with the organization''s strategic goals and priorities, and removes organizational barriers.\",\"retains their regular position but is trained in the tools, methods, and skills necessary to conduct Six Sigma improvement projects.\",\"is typically assigned full-time to train and mentor Black Belts and align improvement projects with organizational strategy.\",\"is typically assigned full-time to train, mentor, and lead the strategy for chartering organizations'' strategic projects.\"],\"stem\":\"The term champion is usually associated with an individual who:\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"Champion is the Six Sigma role associated with a senior manager who ensures that his or her projects are aligned with the organization''s strategic goals and priorities, provides the Six Sigma team with resources, removes organizational barriers for the team, participates in project tollgate reviews, and essentially serves as the team''s backer. A champion is also known as a sponsor. [I.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["ensures that their projects are aligned with the organization''s strategic goals and priorities, and removes organizational barriers.","retains their regular position but is trained in the tools, methods, and skills necessary to conduct Six Sigma improvement projects.","is typically assigned full-time to train and mentor Black Belts and align improvement projects with organizational strategy.","is typically assigned full-time to train, mentor, and lead the strategy for chartering organizations'' strategic projects."],"stem":"The term champion is usually associated with an individual who:","sub":"p1","why":"Champion is the Six Sigma role associated with a senior manager who ensures that his or her projects are aligned with the organization''s strategic goals and priorities, provides the Six Sigma team with resources, removes organizational barriers for the team, participates in project tollgate reviews, and essentially serves as the team''s backer. A champion is also known as a sponsor. [I.B.1]"},"revision":"1a26f17ae95dadf03128d6cefa000b372cb26d5723421bfc4b0dad2fb4f5d489"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-174":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"retains their regular position, but is trained in the tools, methods, and skills necessary to conduct Six Sigma improvement projects.\",\"is typically assigned full-time to train, mentor, and lead improvement projects.\",\"is typically assigned full-time to train and mentor Black Belts and align improvement projects with organizational strategy.\",\"has the authority or ability to make changes in the process as required.\"],\"stem\":\"A process owner is an individual who:\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"Process owner is the Six Sigma role associated with an individual who coordinates the various functions and work activities at all levels of a process, has the authority or ability to make changes in the process as required, and manages the entire process cycle so as to ensure performance effectiveness. [I.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["retains their regular position, but is trained in the tools, methods, and skills necessary to conduct Six Sigma improvement projects.","is typically assigned full-time to train, mentor, and lead improvement projects.","is typically assigned full-time to train and mentor Black Belts and align improvement projects with organizational strategy.","has the authority or ability to make changes in the process as required."],"stem":"A process owner is an individual who:","sub":"p1","why":"Process owner is the Six Sigma role associated with an individual who coordinates the various functions and work activities at all levels of a process, has the authority or ability to make changes in the process as required, and manages the entire process cycle so as to ensure performance effectiveness. [I.B.1]"},"revision":"bc24bcb27c9272e311d2b2a7c9affeea7e53a50b8c14464261efcea322d8970b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-175":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Selecting the technical solution\",\"Vetting the project''s estimated savings\",\"Approving every project charter\",\"Developing the process-control plan\"],\"stem\":\"Which action is finance specifically responsible for when validating a Six Sigma project''s claimed financial benefits?\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"Finance validates the assumptions and estimated financial savings claimed for a Six Sigma project. [I.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Selecting the technical solution","Vetting the project''s estimated savings","Approving every project charter","Developing the process-control plan"],"stem":"Which action is finance specifically responsible for when validating a Six Sigma project''s claimed financial benefits?","sub":"p1","why":"Finance validates the assumptions and estimated financial savings claimed for a Six Sigma project. [I.B.1]"},"revision":"a68e5fc70172bf68c87b3dfabfe9de7199506c004a0256639125d707e9f344b3"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-176":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"retains their regular position but is trained in the tools, methods, and skills necessary to conduct Six Sigma improvement projects.\",\"is typically assigned full-time to train, mentor, and lead improvement projects.\",\"is typically assigned full-time to train and mentor Black Belts and align improvement projects with organizational strategy.\",\"has the authority or ability to make changes in the process as required.\"],\"stem\":\"The term Green Belt is usually associated with an individual who:\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"A Green Belt (GB) generally retains their regular position but is trained in the tools, methods, and skills necessary to conduct Six Sigma improvement projects either individually or as part of larger teams. [I.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["retains their regular position but is trained in the tools, methods, and skills necessary to conduct Six Sigma improvement projects.","is typically assigned full-time to train, mentor, and lead improvement projects.","is typically assigned full-time to train and mentor Black Belts and align improvement projects with organizational strategy.","has the authority or ability to make changes in the process as required."],"stem":"The term Green Belt is usually associated with an individual who:","sub":"p1","why":"A Green Belt (GB) generally retains their regular position but is trained in the tools, methods, and skills necessary to conduct Six Sigma improvement projects either individually or as part of larger teams. [I.B.1]"},"revision":"53650b6a921b7b755f217469fc4649731234ae6c0816944fd5fad45bdef955c5"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-177":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Black Belt\",\"Master Black Belt\",\"Manager\",\"Champion\"],\"stem\":\"Which Six Sigma role is called upon to remove roadblocks when implementing change?\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"A project champion is an upper-level manager who serves as a sounding board and can work to remove roadblocks that the improvement team encounters. [I.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Black Belt","Master Black Belt","Manager","Champion"],"stem":"Which Six Sigma role is called upon to remove roadblocks when implementing change?","sub":"p1","why":"A project champion is an upper-level manager who serves as a sounding board and can work to remove roadblocks that the improvement team encounters. [I.B.1]"},"revision":"82bf837e83001b1eeb7e2a8435d91f5fc6b410b8da53dbbdfb33c130be6b0f8f"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-178":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"provides enterprise-level technical leadership and mentors Black Belts as a Master Black Belt.\",\"retains a regular role while leading smaller improvement efforts as a Green Belt.\",\"is typically assigned full-time to lead improvement projects and train or mentor Green Belts.\",\"owns the process and has authority to sustain operational changes as the process owner.\"],\"stem\":\"The term Black Belt is usually associated with an individual who:\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"A Black Belt is typically assigned full-time to lead improvement projects and train or mentor Green Belts using structured methods such as DMAIC or DMADV. [I.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["provides enterprise-level technical leadership and mentors Black Belts as a Master Black Belt.","retains a regular role while leading smaller improvement efforts as a Green Belt.","is typically assigned full-time to lead improvement projects and train or mentor Green Belts.","owns the process and has authority to sustain operational changes as the process owner."],"stem":"The term Black Belt is usually associated with an individual who:","sub":"p1","why":"A Black Belt is typically assigned full-time to lead improvement projects and train or mentor Green Belts using structured methods such as DMAIC or DMADV. [I.B.1]"},"revision":"bee751aee53548ab9765c61bbc7284d5a341e3150c727e6c4e19094ae75ec7ea"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-179":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Executive\",\"Yellow belt\",\"Finance\",\"Coach\"],\"stem\":\"Which Six Sigma role is involved in giving on-the-spot training, advising team leaders, and helping a team prepare for tollgate meetings?\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"The Six Sigma role of coach is involved in giving on-the-spot training, advising team leaders, and helping a team prepare for tollgate meetings. [I.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Executive","Yellow belt","Finance","Coach"],"stem":"Which Six Sigma role is involved in giving on-the-spot training, advising team leaders, and helping a team prepare for tollgate meetings?","sub":"p1","why":"The Six Sigma role of coach is involved in giving on-the-spot training, advising team leaders, and helping a team prepare for tollgate meetings. [I.B.1]"},"revision":"e64bbef1e4020747e852f15bdc96adcbf9f979950cae0d650820f78e89bc96bf"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-18":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Keep informed to prevent sabotage\",\"Collaborate\",\"Protect and defend\",\"Do not expend resources\"],\"stem\":\"If a project stakeholder is deemed to have High Influence and Low Importance, what behavior approach should the project leader adopt for this stakeholder?\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"A stakeholder with high influence but low importance to project execution is often a senior administrator or finance representative. The recommended approach is to keep the stakeholder informed to prevent opposition or sabotage. [II.A]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Keep informed to prevent sabotage","Collaborate","Protect and defend","Do not expend resources"],"stem":"If a project stakeholder is deemed to have High Influence and Low Importance, what behavior approach should the project leader adopt for this stakeholder?","sub":"p2","why":"A stakeholder with high influence but low importance to project execution is often a senior administrator or finance representative. The recommended approach is to keep the stakeholder informed to prevent opposition or sabotage. [II.A]"},"revision":"93b1ce256a4f42aca2ce6e64f3942cadc6d437cf94ed923f7cd89709a7538647"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-180":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"retains their regular position but is trained in the tools, methods, and skills needed to conduct Six Sigma projects.\",\"is typically assigned full-time to lead individual improvement projects.\",\"is typically assigned full-time to train and mentor Black Belts and align improvement projects with organizational strategy.\",\"owns a process and has authority to make required process changes.\"],\"stem\":\"The term Master Black Belt is usually associated with an individual who:\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"A Master Black Belt is typically assigned full-time to train and mentor Black Belts and align the organization’s improvement-project portfolio with strategy. Master Black Belts may also authorize Green Belt and Black Belt certification. [I.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["retains their regular position but is trained in the tools, methods, and skills needed to conduct Six Sigma projects.","is typically assigned full-time to lead individual improvement projects.","is typically assigned full-time to train and mentor Black Belts and align improvement projects with organizational strategy.","owns a process and has authority to make required process changes."],"stem":"The term Master Black Belt is usually associated with an individual who:","sub":"p1","why":"A Master Black Belt is typically assigned full-time to train and mentor Black Belts and align the organization’s improvement-project portfolio with strategy. Master Black Belts may also authorize Green Belt and Black Belt certification. [I.B.1]"},"revision":"841a9c062a2025cf9f3ba7f9296b50bf86a2c9c0ef9e3ed7a1abb71ab49cc0f4"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-181":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Define, manage, and communicate\",\"Unfreeze, change, and refreeze\",\"Lock, communicate, and implement\",\"Plan, change, and act\"],\"stem\":\"Kurt Lewin proposed a change-management theory that includes which three stages?\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"Lewin''s three-stage change model is unfreeze, change, and refreeze. [I.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Define, manage, and communicate","Unfreeze, change, and refreeze","Lock, communicate, and implement","Plan, change, and act"],"stem":"Kurt Lewin proposed a change-management theory that includes which three stages?","sub":"p1","why":"Lewin''s three-stage change model is unfreeze, change, and refreeze. [I.B.3]"},"revision":"5fa575c18268a1db52d715c6cb4b406a858f28c2ad2