the process that transforms the inputs into the outputs. By understanding this relationship, an organization can predict and control process outputs based on its inputs. [I.A.1]"},"revision":"4cf06b5118cd79e1c3e936015039df2f2cba37c4e5d638359f2b4d18d6ddfb92"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-20":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"involved in relevant discussion and decisions.\",\"handled in a defensive way.\",\"informed of changes after the fact.\",\"trained as a Black Belt.\"],\"stem\":\"A stakeholder whose daily work routine will likely be affected by a project should be:\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"A stakeholder whose daily work will be affected by a Six Sigma project should be included in relevant discussions and decisions. [II.A]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["involved in relevant discussion and decisions.","handled in a defensive way.","informed of changes after the fact.","trained as a Black Belt."],"stem":"A stakeholder whose daily work routine will likely be affected by a project should be:","sub":"p2","why":"A stakeholder whose daily work will be affected by a Six Sigma project should be included in relevant discussions and decisions. [II.A]"},"revision":"04961d8377002fc46f05b10abc5846cc9ece61818fbd2946578e620b11c554d5"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-200":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Six Sigma improvement.\",\"a suboptimized system.\",\"increased failure rate.\",\"unbalanced scorecard.\"],\"stem\":\"A trucking company strives to improve its on-time delivery. In a staff meeting, it is reported that the on-time delivery rate has increased, and transportation costs have also increased due to trucks leaving the facility half-full. This is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"Concentrating solely on the on-time delivery rate is an example of a suboptimized system. When improvements are proposed, it is important to think systemically, so that optimizing one metric will not result in the degradation of another. [II.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Six Sigma improvement.","a suboptimized system.","increased failure rate.","unbalanced scorecard."],"stem":"A trucking company strives to improve its on-time delivery. In a staff meeting, it is reported that the on-time delivery rate has increased, and transportation costs have also increased due to trucks leaving the facility half-full. This is an example of:","sub":"p2","why":"Concentrating solely on the on-time delivery rate is an example of a suboptimized system. When improvements are proposed, it is important to think systemically, so that optimizing one metric will not result in the degradation of another. [II.C.1]"},"revision":"d172e6ea42b2941fa5475794e9f7a7d7b30a0496d33d8615b0d0b33a0e60532f"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-201":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"track cultural change in an organization.\",\"punish low performers.\",\"dictate social norms.\",\"increase regulatory compliance.\"],\"stem\":\"One of the functions of key behavior indicators is to:\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"Key behavior indicators (KBIs) track behaviors that contribute to high-level performance, including cultural change in an organization. [II.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["track cultural change in an organization.","punish low performers.","dictate social norms.","increase regulatory compliance."],"stem":"One of the functions of key behavior indicators is to:","sub":"p2","why":"Key behavior indicators (KBIs) track behaviors that contribute to high-level performance, including cultural change in an organization. [II.C.1]"},"revision":"b8788e371d97262588e1df27db851878ab3b0c67edbe4c5e7571af181d0f818a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-202":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"OKRs are used to reduce the workforce equitably.\",\"OKRs list objectives and the results required to achieve them.\",\"OKRs incorporate benchmarking metrics.\",\"OKRs cannot be used to track individual achievement.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following statements is true about objectives and key results (OKRs)?\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"OKRs list objectives for either an organization, a department, or an individual, and then list the results that will be required to achieve the objective. [II.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["OKRs are used to reduce the workforce equitably.","OKRs list objectives and the results required to achieve them.","OKRs incorporate benchmarking metrics.","OKRs cannot be used to track individual achievement."],"stem":"Which of the following statements is true about objectives and key results (OKRs)?","sub":"p2","why":"OKRs list objectives for either an organization, a department, or an individual, and then list the results that will be required to achieve the objective. [II.C.1]"},"revision":"a8036d4626528b46b2c1fda62717e098c0e5f3ac9a3a7eeef595a7d98c2a4c3d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-203":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Responses\",\"Count\"],\"rows\":[[\"9–10\",\"23\"],[\"7–8\",\"12\"],[\"5–6\",\"5\"],[\"3–4\",\"4\"],[\"0–2\",\"1\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"28.9%\",\"51.1%\",\"55.6%\",\"77.8%\"],\"stem\":\"A customer satisfaction survey asks how likely respondents are to recommend the product on the standard 0–10 Net Promoter Score scale, where 0 is not at all likely and 10 is extremely likely. Based on the results shown below, what is the net promoter score?\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"Promoters (scores 9–10) = 23. Detractors (scores 0–6) = 5 + 4 + 1 = 10. Total respondents = 23 + 12 + 5 + 4 + 1 = 45. NPS = (promoters − detractors)/total = (23 − 10)/45 = 28.9%. [II.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"columns":["Responses","Count"],"rows":[["9–10","23"],["7–8","12"],["5–6","5"],["3–4","4"],["0–2","1"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["28.9%","51.1%","55.6%","77.8%"],"stem":"A customer satisfaction survey asks how likely respondents are to recommend the product on the standard 0–10 Net Promoter Score scale, where 0 is not at all likely and 10 is extremely likely. Based on the results shown below, what is the net promoter score?","sub":"p2","why":"Promoters (scores 9–10) = 23. Detractors (scores 0–6) = 5 + 4 + 1 = 10. Total respondents = 23 + 12 + 5 + 4 + 1 = 45. NPS = (promoters − detractors)/total = (23 − 10)/45 = 28.9%. [II.C.1]"},"revision":"06deadeb05258a9b8a73c44585a327fb6be666d1e4f1d23877a8a9d030040f7b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-204":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"future sales.\",\"customer returns.\",\"warranty charges.\",\"customer retention.\"],\"stem\":\"A company monitors the number of hits on its website sales page. This metric is likely a leading indicator of:\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"The number of hits on the company''s sales page is likely a leading indicator of future sales. [II.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["future sales.","customer returns.","warranty charges.","customer retention."],"stem":"A company monitors the number of hits on its website sales page. This metric is likely a leading indicator of:","sub":"p2","why":"The number of hits on the company''s sales page is likely a leading indicator of future sales. [II.C.1]"},"revision":"9d5e5b30cf899c8e4908a144d100e92d15c00b7a3a95e8167c01af2280f15fb6"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-205":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Internal business processes perspective\",\"Learning and growth perspective\",\"Financial perspective\",\"Customer perspective\"],\"stem\":\"Which balanced-scorecard perspective measures the efficiency, quality, and reliability of the internal processes through which the organization creates value?\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"The internal-business-process perspective measures the efficiency, quality, and reliability of the processes used to create and deliver value. The customer perspective addresses the customer value proposition. [II.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Internal business processes perspective","Learning and growth perspective","Financial perspective","Customer perspective"],"stem":"Which balanced-scorecard perspective measures the efficiency, quality, and reliability of the internal processes through which the organization creates value?","sub":"p2","why":"The internal-business-process perspective measures the efficiency, quality, and reliability of the processes used to create and deliver value. The customer perspective addresses the customer value proposition. [II.C.1]"},"revision":"81259b32f2daf09318ca0b69f4969f51a3cc7d4194e9fc12aec581964ea28ce9"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-206":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Financial perspective\",\"Learning and growth perspective\",\"Internal business process perspective\",\"Customer perspective\"],\"stem\":\"Which perspective includes the capabilities and skills of an organization?\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"The learning-and-growth perspective includes the capabilities and skills of the organization and its people. [II.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Financial perspective","Learning and growth perspective","Internal business process perspective","Customer perspective"],"stem":"Which perspective includes the capabilities and skills of an organization?","sub":"p2","why":"The learning-and-growth perspective includes the capabilities and skills of the organization and its people. [II.C.1]"},"revision":"48ff070b123141d923bd59c75e68e522ea3c22ef6642cfc928a1e9031efbe9eb"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-207":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"$500\",\"$1,581\",\"$3,105\",\"$5,000\"],\"stem\":\"In five years, $5,000 will be available. Assuming annual compounding at 10% per year, to the nearest dollar, what is its present value today?\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"PV = $5,000/(1.10)⁵ = $3,104.61, which rounds to $3,105. [II.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["$500","$1,581","$3,105","$5,000"],"stem":"In five years, $5,000 will be available. Assuming annual compounding at 10% per year, to the nearest dollar, what is its present value today?","sub":"p2","why":"PV = $5,000/(1.10)⁵ = $3,104.61, which rounds to $3,105. [II.C.2]"},"revision":"68d31167c5ede5e27e253fc8ad086ffc54a587b3398225b29b3039af70c793a8"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-208":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.13\",\"7.5\",\"100,000\",\"750,000\"],\"stem\":\"The net present value (NPV) of a project''s cost is $100,000, and the NPV of its benefits is $750,000. What is the benefit-to-cost ratio?\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"Benefit-to-cost ratio = NPV of benefits/NPV of costs = $750,000/$100,000 = 7.5. [II.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.13","7.5","100,000","750,000"],"stem":"The net present value (NPV) of a project''s cost is $100,000, and the NPV of its benefits is $750,000. What is the benefit-to-cost ratio?","sub":"p2","why":"Benefit-to-cost ratio = NPV of benefits/NPV of costs = $750,000/$100,000 = 7.5. [II.C.2]"},"revision":"19152f275d8987c6252d897aac6df790edd4b4c7e318f4d1c50f81501b732445"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-209":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"appraisal costs.\",\"prevention costs.\",\"internal failure costs.\",\"external failure costs.\"],\"stem\":\"Costs associated with customer complaints are best characterized as:\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"Customer complaints occur after delivery and are external failure costs. The four cost-of-quality categories are prevention, appraisal, internal failure, and external failure. [II.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["appraisal costs.","prevention costs.","internal failure costs.","external failure costs."],"stem":"Costs associated with customer complaints are best characterized as:","sub":"p2","why":"Customer complaints occur after delivery and are external failure costs. The four cost-of-quality categories are prevention, appraisal, internal failure, and external failure. [II.C.2]"},"revision":"4f019c7dd01a2ff37a8dc7537c82ad10b4c5fd91b88014dd7bb77b40216cc5ff"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-21":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"keep the stakeholder from working on the team.\",\"assign the stakeholder to another department.\",\"cancel the project due to resistance.\",\"develop an action plan to reduce opposition and increase support.\"],\"stem\":\"A current attitude assessment for a stakeholder shows that they are suspicious of the project. The project leader should:\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"When a stakeholder is suspicious of a project, the project leader should develop an action plan that reduces opposition and increases support rather than excluding or avoiding the stakeholder. [II.A]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["keep the stakeholder from working on the team.","assign the stakeholder to another department.","cancel the project due to resistance.","develop an action plan to reduce opposition and increase support."],"stem":"A current attitude assessment for a stakeholder shows that they are suspicious of the project. The project leader should:","sub":"p2","why":"When a stakeholder is suspicious of a project, the project leader should develop an action plan that reduces opposition and increases support rather than excluding or avoiding the stakeholder. [II.A]"},"revision":"9292222aa1a9802d36fbd96e131298b323385155c0b303342e26dd53c639de22"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-210":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"$5,000\",\"$13,304\",\"$13,335\",\"$14,416\"],\"stem\":\"To the nearest dollar, what is the present value of a $15,000 cost incurred in three years at a continuously compounded annual rate of 4.0%?\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"With continuous compounding, PV = A exp(−rt) = $15,000 exp[−(0.04)(3)] = $13,303.81, which rounds to $13,304. [II.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["$5,000","$13,304","$13,335","$14,416"],"stem":"To the nearest dollar, what is the present value of a $15,000 cost incurred in three years at a continuously compounded annual rate of 4.0%?","sub":"p2","why":"With continuous compounding, PV = A exp(−rt) = $15,000 exp[−(0.04)(3)] = $13,303.81, which rounds to $13,304. [II.C.2]"},"revision":"c3276087cbac91237af3a8aaf20e2e6ca72f37ea17bfc4b7c5d413355683d89d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-211":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Year\",\"Cost\",\"Benefit\"],\"rows\":[[\"0\",\"$75,000\",\"$150,000\"],[\"1\",\"$15,000\",\"$45,000\"],[\"2\",\"$15,000\",\"$45,000\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"27.1%\",\"80.8%\",\"127.1%\",\"130.8%\"],\"stem\":\"Using the cash flows shown, calculate the discounted return on investment (ROI) at an annual discount rate of 5%. Define discounted ROI as the present value of net benefits divided by the present value of costs.\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"PV(costs) = 75,000 + 15,000/1.05 + 15,000/1.05² = $102,891. PV(net benefits) = 75,000 + 30,000/1.05 + 30,000/1.05² = $130,782. Discounted ROI = $130,782 / $102,891 = 1.271, or 127.1%. [II.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"chart":{"columns":["Year","Cost","Benefit"],"rows":[["0","$75,000","$150,000"],["1","$15,000","$45,000"],["2","$15,000","$45,000"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["27.1%","80.8%","127.1%","130.8%"],"stem":"Using the cash flows shown, calculate the discounted return on investment (ROI) at an annual discount rate of 5%. Define discounted ROI as the present value of net benefits divided by the present value of costs.","sub":"p2","why":"PV(costs) = 75,000 + 15,000/1.05 + 15,000/1.05² = $102,891. PV(net benefits) = 75,000 + 30,000/1.05 + 30,000/1.05² = $130,782. Discounted ROI = $130,782 / $102,891 = 1.271, or 127.1%. [II.C.2]"},"revision":"fba8aeb0d8a3c22bf4af034fe1a3b4128e16a3c5d68114e18d42a6a4de240d6e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-212":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"$258,621\",\"$285,440\",\"$288,000\",\"$288,294\"],\"stem\":\"To the nearest dollar, calculate the present value of a $300,000 payment that will occur at the end of Year 1, assuming an annual interest rate of 4%, compounded quarterly.\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"With quarterly compounding, the quarterly rate is 0.04/4 = 0.01 and there are four quarters. PV = $300,000/(1 + 0.01)⁴ = $288,294. [II.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["$258,621","$285,440","$288,000","$288,294"],"stem":"To the nearest dollar, calculate the present value of a $300,000 payment that will occur at the end of Year 1, assuming an annual interest rate of 4%, compounded quarterly.","sub":"p2","why":"With quarterly compounding, the quarterly rate is 0.04/4 = 0.01 and there are four quarters. PV = $300,000/(1 + 0.01)⁴ = $288,294. [II.C.2]"},"revision":"f997c721552f77e2c00de6b308a90c5b92bf9e6e05f811eabc96ee8d2a1992cc"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-213":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"27%\",\"37%\",\"73%\",\"270%\"],\"stem\":\"Product A sells for $55.50 and its total cost is $15.00. What is its gross margin percentage, defined as (selling price − total cost)/selling price × 100%?\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"Percentage margin = (revenue − cost)/revenue × 100% = ($55.50 − $15.00)/$55.50 × 100% = 72.97%, which rounds to 73%. [II.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["27%","37%","73%","270%"],"stem":"Product A sells for $55.50 and its total cost is $15.00. What is its gross margin percentage, defined as (selling price − total cost)/selling price × 100%?","sub":"p2","why":"Percentage margin = (revenue − cost)/revenue × 100% = ($55.50 − $15.00)/$55.50 × 100% = 72.97%, which rounds to 73%. [II.C.2]"},"revision":"d00b640aa295ac1d2db830c2624ae1a1c5139aac12260375278f4ad0f23f896d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-214":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"26%\",\"37.5%\",\"60%\",\"62.5%\"],\"stem\":\"A two-month improvement produced $40,000 in gross benefits and required a $25,000 implementation cost. Using simple ROI = (benefit − cost)/cost × 100%, what is the project ROI?\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"For this short-term project, simple ROI = (benefit − cost)/cost = ($40,000 − $25,000)/$25,000 = 0.60, or 60%. [II.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["26%","37.5%","60%","62.5%"],"stem":"A two-month improvement produced $40,000 in gross benefits and required a $25,000 implementation cost. Using simple ROI = (benefit − cost)/cost × 100%, what is the project ROI?","sub":"p2","why":"For this short-term project, simple ROI = (benefit − cost)/cost = ($40,000 − $25,000)/$25,000 = 0.60, or 60%. [II.C.2]"},"revision":"09674fd0acf29778c01cb0fca6ab5fc156ac3301d0f5228aae42993e4a6a2149"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-215":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"The use of too many charts\",\"Inadequate communication\",\"The use of SMART goals\",\"Lack of advanced statistical knowledge\"],\"stem\":\"What is one of the most frequently cited causes for team failure?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"Inadequate communication is one of the most frequently cited causes of team failure. [III.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["The use of too many charts","Inadequate communication","The use of SMART goals","Lack of advanced statistical knowledge"],"stem":"What is one of the most frequently cited causes for team failure?","sub":"tm","why":"Inadequate communication is one of the most frequently cited causes of team failure. [III.B.3]"},"revision":"4e6c19c3c89d665a255f23eeb512fa443b742004bf0fbe2c1ca909394241d317"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-216":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"is not necessary.\",\"should be detailed and lengthy.\",\"should occur only after project completion.\",\"can be delivered via a status report.\"],\"stem\":\"Communication outside the team:\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"Communication outside the team should occur regularly. It can take the form of a bulleted status report or presentation. Typical items include accomplishments, problems, issues or concerns, actions being taken, and 30-day plans. [III.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["is not necessary.","should be detailed and lengthy.","should occur only after project completion.","can be delivered via a status report."],"stem":"Communication outside the team:","sub":"tm","why":"Communication outside the team should occur regularly. It can take the form of a bulleted status report or presentation. Typical items include accomplishments, problems, issues or concerns, actions being taken, and 30-day plans. [III.B.3]"},"revision":"67bde598602069f77bfa5475f40aca01334a17f035c825e9eb2ccebd15b36adf"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-217":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Supporting\",\"Coaching\",\"Delegating\",\"Directing\"],\"stem\":\"Which leadership style provides clear instructions and combines high directive behavior with low supportive behavior?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"The directing style combines high directive behavior with low supportive behavior and suits an enthusiastic beginner who has low competence but high commitment. [III.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Supporting","Coaching","Delegating","Directing"],"stem":"Which leadership style provides clear instructions and combines high directive behavior with low supportive behavior?","sub":"tm","why":"The directing style combines high directive behavior with low supportive behavior and suits an enthusiastic beginner who has low competence but high commitment. [III.B.4]"},"revision":"adaed4529efa613b54b77e96c80a4f71164ba046c3299aaca6772c0dc3d328cb"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-218":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Delegating\",\"Supporting\",\"Directing\",\"Consulting\"],\"stem\":\"A leader who empowers process improvement team members to function independently is using which leadership style?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"The delegating leadership style is characterized by low directive and low supportive behavior. It is suited for individuals who possess high competence and high commitment. Such individuals are often described as self-reliant achievers. They are proficient in their task and are highly motivated about it. [III.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Delegating","Supporting","Directing","Consulting"],"stem":"A leader who empowers process improvement team members to function independently is using which leadership style?","sub":"tm","why":"The delegating leadership style is characterized by low directive and low supportive behavior. It is suited for individuals who possess high competence and high commitment. Such individuals are often described as self-reliant achievers. They are proficient in their task and are highly motivated about it. [III.B.4]"},"revision":"e51f46dfa6aeff86c6268a524be8ef2d9809b408a993e7f4f3a77f889a696bbe"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-219":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Delegating\",\"Facilitating\",\"Directing\",\"Supporting\"],\"stem\":\"What type of leadership is best suited for team members with average competence and moderate commitment?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"The supporting leadership style is characterized by low directive and high supportive behavior. It is suited for individuals who possess mid-competence and moderate commitment. Such individuals are often described as capable, but cautious, performers. They know their task but are not motivated about it. [III.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Delegating","Facilitating","Directing","Supporting"],"stem":"What type of leadership is best suited for team members with average competence and moderate commitment?","sub":"tm","why":"The supporting leadership style is characterized by low directive and high supportive behavior. It is suited for individuals who possess mid-competence and moderate commitment. Such individuals are often described as capable, but cautious, performers. They know their task but are not motivated about it. [III.B.4]"},"revision":"054fccb8d3e9edc376b7762541b211572b898fa15012d15c3fa1b5ecdb4bd9d2"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-22":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"apply statistical techniques to control a process.\",\"compare the current state to best-in-class processes or products.\",\"improve processes using teams of five to seven employees.\",\"restructure an entire organization and its processes.\"],\"stem\":\"Benchmarking can be used to:\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"Benchmarking compares an organization’s performance, processes, products, or practices with best-in-class examples and uses the findings to improve performance. [II.B]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["apply statistical techniques to control a process.","compare the current state to best-in-class processes or products.","improve processes using teams of five to seven employees.","restructure an entire organization and its processes."],"stem":"Benchmarking can be used to:","sub":"p2","why":"Benchmarking compares an organization’s performance, processes, products, or practices with best-in-class examples and uses the findings to improve performance. [II.B]"},"revision":"51d9fe2e5e1daedb315b6651abb38c45bb7b9f7f358b53ce7312c841886776cb"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-220":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Low directive and low supportive\",\"High directive and low supportive\",\"Low directive and high supportive\",\"High directive and high supportive\"],\"stem\":\"Which leadership characteristics are best used for team members with low competence but high commitment?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"For team members who have low competence but high commitment, a directing leadership style is best. This style is characterized by low supportive but high directive leadership. [III.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Low directive and low supportive","High directive and low supportive","Low directive and high supportive","High directive and high supportive"],"stem":"Which leadership characteristics are best used for team members with low competence but high commitment?","sub":"tm","why":"For team members who have low competence but high commitment, a directing leadership style is best. This style is characterized by low supportive but high directive leadership. [III.B.4]"},"revision":"09a0eb12b4f4ad41a62c0b6f3dd28ca700961e35bbd44f81028fe5e01020f052"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-221":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Coaches usually work with individuals to develop skills and abilities.\",\"Mentors usually work with teams to develop skills and abilities.\",\"Mentors help projects stay on track by providing resources for completion of team member assignments.\",\"Coaches provide guidance and direction on how to navigate organizational barriers.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following statements concerning the role of a coach or mentor is correct?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"Coaches work with individuals to develop skills and abilities. They also work with teams to help projects stay on track by providing resources for completion of team member assignments. Mentors work with individuals, not teams, and help mentees navigate organizational barriers and provide guidance and direction for an individual''s career. [III.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Coaches usually work with individuals to develop skills and abilities.","Mentors usually work with teams to develop skills and abilities.","Mentors help projects stay on track by providing resources for completion of team member assignments.","Coaches provide guidance and direction on how to navigate organizational barriers."],"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning the role of a coach or mentor is correct?","sub":"tm","why":"Coaches work with individuals to develop skills and abilities. They also work with teams to help projects stay on track by providing resources for completion of team member assignments. Mentors work with individuals, not teams, and help mentees navigate organizational barriers and provide guidance and direction for an individual''s career. [III.C.1]"},"revision":"fcb1880f474102dfd996a00025e38b15b0ee453f748dfc042bfb4cea76cb4251"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-222":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"whisperer.\",\"naysayer.\",\"dropout.\",\"team clown.\"],\"stem\":\"In a team meeting, one member continually has private conversations with the person next to them. This type of disruptive team member is known as the:\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"The whisperer has private conversations with the person next to them during meetings, distracting others and sometimes creating concern about side comments. [III.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["whisperer.","naysayer.","dropout.","team clown."],"stem":"In a team meeting, one member continually has private conversations with the person next to them. This type of disruptive team member is known as the:","sub":"tm","why":"The whisperer has private conversations with the person next to them during meetings, distracting others and sometimes creating concern about side comments. [III.C.1]"},"revision":"5240d23ce4791c34152c7fe3163ec92b0cc97c56265175bab26948d02efd9d66"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-223":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"whisperer.\",\"politician.\",\"storyteller.\",\"naysayer.\"],\"stem\":\"A team member who agrees with whomever they are currently speaking with and views decisions as a way to get ahead is known as the:\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"The politician sees every decision as a way to get ahead and may agree with whomever they are currently speaking with to curry favor. [III.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["whisperer.","politician.","storyteller.","naysayer."],"stem":"A team member who agrees with whomever they are currently speaking with and views decisions as a way to get ahead is known as the:","sub":"tm","why":"The politician sees every decision as a way to get ahead and may agree with whomever they are currently speaking with to curry favor. [III.C.1]"},"revision":"c82ad4f8b73bfdedf06af00fbe1b171ebf83321ec6c897eba1083db25890f455"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-224":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Participating in groupthink\",\"Airing old disputes\",\"Accepting opinions without data\",\"Arguing over who gets credit\"],\"stem\":\"A team leader seeks out dissenting views in a team meeting. The leader is likely trying to get the team to avoid which behavior?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"The leader is seeking out dissenting views to avoid groupthink. [III.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Participating in groupthink","Airing old disputes","Accepting opinions without data","Arguing over who gets credit"],"stem":"A team leader seeks out dissenting views in a team meeting. The leader is likely trying to get the team to avoid which behavior?","sub":"tm","why":"The leader is seeking out dissenting views to avoid groupthink. [III.C.1]"},"revision":"9b1270f0581f2e66202745e5127f6240089047b0ed65894691db508f118952a5"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-225":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"scope creep.\",\"running out of time.\",\"one person from dominating the discussion.\",\"accepting opinions without data.\"],\"stem\":\"A team leader goes around the conference table during a brainstorming session, asking each person for one comment. The leader is likely trying to avoid:\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"To avoid one person dominating the conversation, the team leader can use a \\\"round-robin\\\" brainstorming approach, in which each person is asked for one comment in turn. [III.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["scope creep.","running out of time.","one person from dominating the discussion.","accepting opinions without data."],"stem":"A team leader goes around the conference table during a brainstorming session, asking each person for one comment. The leader is likely trying to avoid:","sub":"tm","why":"To avoid one person dominating the conversation, the team leader can use a \"round-robin\" brainstorming approach, in which each person is asked for one comment in turn. [III.C.1]"},"revision":"bcb7507828d1c22f6a8209f5f17984f7e8febc2e4e30883951af605a198d4fcf"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-226":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Boomerang\",\"Redirect\",\"Idea floating\",\"Direct probe\"],\"stem\":\"A team gets off on a tangent when discussing an improvement option. The team facilitator can bring the team''s focus back by using which technique?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"wh