78b931"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-273":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"chart\":{\"cells\":[[\"blank\",\"large\",\"large\",\"small\"],[\"large\",\"blank\",\"blank\",\"large\"],[\"blank\",\"large\",\"large\",\"blank\"],[\"large\",\"small\",\"small\",\"large\"]],\"colGroups\":[{\"cols\":[\"1\",\"2\"],\"label\":\"Shipper\"},{\"cols\":[\"A\",\"B\"],\"label\":\"Part\"}],\"rowLabels\":[\"Division 1\",\"Division 2\",\"Customer 1\",\"Customer 2\"],\"type\":\"matrix-diagram\"},\"options\":[\"Part B is only produced by Division 2.\",\"Division 1 makes the highest volume of Part B.\",\"Customer 2 buys a large volume of Part B.\",\"Division 2 ships Part B using Shipper 2.\"],\"stem\":\"A manufacturer compares manufacturing sites, products, customers, and shipping companies in terms of volume using the matrix diagram below. Based on the matrix diagram, what can be said about Part B?\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"The matrix shows a large-volume relationship between Customer 2 and Part B. It also shows that Customer 1 has no relationship with Part B. [IV.D.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"chart":{"cells":[["blank","large","large","small"],["large","blank","blank","large"],["blank","large","large","blank"],["large","small","small","large"]],"colGroups":[{"cols":["1","2"],"label":"Shipper"},{"cols":["A","B"],"label":"Part"}],"rowLabels":["Division 1","Division 2","Customer 1","Customer 2"],"type":"matrix-diagram"},"options":["Part B is only produced by Division 2.","Division 1 makes the highest volume of Part B.","Customer 2 buys a large volume of Part B.","Division 2 ships Part B using Shipper 2."],"stem":"A manufacturer compares manufacturing sites, products, customers, and shipping companies in terms of volume using the matrix diagram below. Based on the matrix diagram, what can be said about Part B?","sub":"def","why":"The matrix shows a large-volume relationship between Customer 2 and Part B. It also shows that Customer 1 has no relationship with Part B. [IV.D.3]"},"revision":"7bfe575f6da924dac729e3625ddae4cb731aeb2de253de3ebd1e5ad78cc67bb7"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-274":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Roof-shaped matrix\",\"L-matrix\",\"T-shaped matrix\",\"X matrix\"],\"stem\":\"Which matrix relates two different sets of items separately to a common third set, without directly comparing the two outer sets with each other?\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"A T-shaped matrix relates one common set to each of two outer sets, without directly comparing the outer sets. [IV.D.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Roof-shaped matrix","L-matrix","T-shaped matrix","X matrix"],"stem":"Which matrix relates two different sets of items separately to a common third set, without directly comparing the two outer sets with each other?","sub":"def","why":"A T-shaped matrix relates one common set to each of two outer sets, without directly comparing the outer sets. [IV.D.3]"},"revision":"bc55d1499c0f98da2ea9f89186bcdedfc9a7069e956844814b02e727a752935b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-275":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Product\",\"Ease of Use (50%)\",\"Low Cost (25%)\",\"Time to Install (25%)\"],\"rows\":[[\"A\",\"4\",\"3\",\"1\"],[\"B\",\"3\",\"1\",\"2\"],[\"C\",\"1\",\"2\",\"4\"],[\"D\",\"2\",\"4\",\"3\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"Product A\",\"Product B\",\"Product C\",\"Product D\"],\"stem\":\"A prioritization matrix was created by a team to choose which software product to purchase. Each criterion was ranked from 1 = best to 4 = worst. Based on the completed matrix, which product should the team consider first?\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"Compute each weighted rank using the stated weights: A = 4(0.50) + 3(0.25) + 1(0.25) = 3.00; B = 3(0.50) + 1(0.25) + 2(0.25) = 2.25; C = 1(0.50) + 2(0.25) + 4(0.25) = 2.00; D = 2(0.50) + 4(0.25) + 3(0.25) = 2.75. Because rank 1 is best, the lowest total is preferred, so Product C should be considered first. [IV.D.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"chart":{"columns":["Product","Ease of Use (50%)","Low Cost (25%)","Time to Install (25%)"],"rows":[["A","4","3","1"],["B","3","1","2"],["C","1","2","4"],["D","2","4","3"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["Product A","Product B","Product C","Product D"],"stem":"A prioritization matrix was created by a team to choose which software product to purchase. Each criterion was ranked from 1 = best to 4 = worst. Based on the completed matrix, which product should the team consider first?","sub":"def","why":"Compute each weighted rank using the stated weights: A = 4(0.50) + 3(0.25) + 1(0.25) = 3.00; B = 3(0.50) + 1(0.25) + 2(0.25) = 2.25; C = 1(0.50) + 2(0.25) + 4(0.25) = 2.00; D = 2(0.50) + 4(0.25) + 3(0.25) = 2.75. Because rank 1 is best, the lowest total is preferred, so Product C should be considered first. [IV.D.4]"},"revision":"4fdbf1f335c885fbfc400bc5e70765a8a16f93352d193815323a10344f53fb91"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-276":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Activity\",\"Predecessor\",\"Time\"],\"rows\":[[\"A\",\"—\",\"5\"],[\"B\",\"—\",\"10\"],[\"C\",\"A\",\"8\"],[\"D\",\"B\",\"2\"],[\"E\",\"—\",\"15\"],[\"F\",\"C, D, E\",\"6\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"ACF\",\"BDF\",\"EF\",\"CE\"],\"stem\":\"Using the precedence table below, identify the activities on the critical path.\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"There are three paths through the network: ACF = 5+8+6 = 19 days, BDF = 10+2+6 = 18 days, and EF = 15+6 = 21 days. EF is the path with the longest duration and is therefore the critical path. [IV.D.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"chart":{"columns":["Activity","Predecessor","Time"],"rows":[["A","—","5"],["B","—","10"],["C","A","8"],["D","B","2"],["E","—","15"],["F","C, D, E","6"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["ACF","BDF","EF","CE"],"stem":"Using the precedence table below, identify the activities on the critical path.","sub":"def","why":"There are three paths through the network: ACF = 5+8+6 = 19 days, BDF = 10+2+6 = 18 days, and EF = 15+6 = 21 days. EF is the path with the longest duration and is therefore the critical path. [IV.D.5]"},"revision":"031d695d80cfe9a1b95fb1e18c5c2579dc7b3b267e543ccc37c64c073fcba156"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-277":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Activity\",\"Predecessor\",\"Time\"],\"rows\":[[\"A\",\"—\",\"5\"],[\"B\",\"—\",\"10\"],[\"C\",\"A\",\"8\"],[\"D\",\"B\",\"2\"],[\"E\",\"—\",\"15\"],[\"F\",\"C, D, E\",\"6\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"5\",\"8\",\"10\",\"13\"],\"stem\":\"Using the precedence table below, what is the early start time for Activity C?\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"Activity C''s only predecessor is Activity A, which runs from day 0 to day 5. The early start for Activity C is therefore equal to the early finish of Activity A, which is 5. [IV.D.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"columns":["Activity","Predecessor","Time"],"rows":[["A","—","5"],["B","—","10"],["C","A","8"],["D","B","2"],["E","—","15"],["F","C, D, E","6"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["5","8","10","13"],"stem":"Using the precedence table below, what is the early start time for Activity C?","sub":"def","why":"Activity C''s only predecessor is Activity A, which runs from day 0 to day 5. The early start for Activity C is therefore equal to the early finish of Activity A, which is 5. [IV.D.5]"},"revision":"d22385a81a8935e5729d32449d5d88b8c06f1ce3aadd621fcd91041d0c2ab03d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-278":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Activity\",\"Predecessor\",\"Time\"],\"rows\":[[\"A\",\"—\",\"5\"],[\"B\",\"—\",\"10\"],[\"C\",\"A\",\"8\"],[\"D\",\"B\",\"2\"],[\"E\",\"—\",\"15\"],[\"F\",\"C, D, E\",\"6\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"There is no effect on the finish date.\",\"The other activities can be completed early to maintain the schedule.\",\"Activity C will not be able to start on time.\",\"The project will finish at least five days late.\"],\"stem\":\"Using the precedence table below, Activity E is delayed by five days. What effect will this have on the overall project timeline?\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"Activity E sits on the critical path (EF, at 21 days). Delaying any activity on the critical path delays the entire project by the same amount, so the project will finish at least five days late even if every other activity stays on schedule. [IV.D.5]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"chart":{"columns":["Activity","Predecessor","Time"],"rows":[["A","—","5"],["B","—","10"],["C","A","8"],["D","B","2"],["E","—","15"],["F","C, D, E","6"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["There is no effect on the finish date.","The other activities can be completed early to maintain the schedule.","Activity C will not be able to start on time.","The project will finish at least five days late."],"stem":"Using the precedence table below, Activity E is delayed by five days. What effect will this have on the overall project timeline?","sub":"def","why":"Activity E sits on the critical path (EF, at 21 days). Delaying any activity on the critical path delays the entire project by the same amount, so the project will finish at least five days late even if every other activity stays on schedule. [IV.D.5]"},"revision":"81bc3623e6c6d44fd6596366362388ee4a12838deb40672f8e78902964e46f32"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-279":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"the expected overrun in the project timeline.\",\"the latest that activity can finish without delaying the project.\",\"the slack time for the activity.\",\"the critical path.\"],\"stem\":\"In an activity network diagram, a \\\"late finish\\\" value for an activity represents:\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"The late finish for an activity in an activity network diagram shows the latest that the activity can finish without delaying the project. For activities on the critical path, early finish and late finish values are equal. [IV.D.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["the expected overrun in the project timeline.","the latest that activity can finish without delaying the project.","the slack time for the activity.","the critical path."],"stem":"In an activity network diagram, a \"late finish\" value for an activity represents:","sub":"def","why":"The late finish for an activity in an activity network diagram shows the latest that the activity can finish without delaying the project. For activities on the critical path, early finish and late finish values are equal. [IV.D.5]"},"revision":"35528815d4cca8cf5afe8d0996f65aa9f3ac6097eac634153cdcdc7d9003f9e2"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-28":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"unfocused.\",\"process improvement.\",\"cross-functional.\",\"self-directed.\"],\"stem\":\"A company has formed a team that has members with diverse roles and skill sets. This type of team is best defined as:\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"Cross-functional teams involve representation from various groups likely to be impacted by the changes. [III.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["unfocused.","process improvement.","cross-functional.","self-directed."],"stem":"A company has formed a team that has members with diverse roles and skill sets. This type of team is best defined as:","sub":"tm","why":"Cross-functional teams involve representation from various groups likely to be impacted by the changes. [III.A.1]"},"revision":"9e7baecb9bae0689bec121ae4a45b5dcddf3f6c1c5a8f6dd16d3b8cd584e1ce1"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-280":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"the project completion time.\",\"zero.\",\"the longest activity in the path.\",\"the late start time of the first activity.\"],\"stem\":\"The slack time for all activities in the critical path is equal to:\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"By definition, all activities in the critical path have slack values equal to zero. If any of these activities takes longer to complete, the entire project will be delayed. [IV.D.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["the project completion time.","zero.","the longest activity in the path.","the late start time of the first activity."],"stem":"The slack time for all activities in the critical path is equal to:","sub":"def","why":"By definition, all activities in the critical path have slack values equal to zero. If any of these activities takes longer to complete, the entire project will be delayed. [IV.D.5]"},"revision":"d871f1b90c521840626f052d28f4874d43a9815275bfeaad02782d434410e7a9"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-281":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"FMEA.\",\"interrelationship digraph.\",\"affinity diagram.\",\"process decision program chart.\"],\"stem\":\"Which planning tool extends a tree diagram by branching from planned activities to possible problems and then to countermeasures?\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"The process decision program chart (PDPC) is used to list all the ways an activity can go wrong and then propose countermeasures in the event of a problem. [IV.D.6]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["FMEA.","interrelationship digraph.","affinity diagram.","process decision program chart."],"stem":"Which planning tool extends a tree diagram by branching from planned activities to possible problems and then to countermeasures?","sub":"def","why":"The process decision program chart (PDPC) is used to list all the ways an activity can go wrong and then propose countermeasures in the event of a problem. [IV.D.6]"},"revision":"651d9bec501eeb4c0a714d77b1dd15c181f7a076f5974cdc3235db97b17fb395"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-282":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"FMEA.\",\"tree diagram.\",\"fault tree analysis.\",\"cause and effect diagram.\"],\"stem\":\"A process decision program chart begins as a/an:\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"A process decision program chart begins as a tree diagram. The team then identifies what could go wrong at the third-level tasks and develops countermeasures. [IV.D.6]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["FMEA.","tree diagram.","fault tree analysis.","cause and effect diagram."],"stem":"A process decision program chart begins as a/an:","sub":"def","why":"A process decision program chart begins as a tree diagram. The team then identifies what could go wrong at the third-level tasks and develops countermeasures. [IV.D.6]"},"revision":"a0d38d1058249ed0b3fd1a5c83197036e3a4154bec0f2b3875ea0266dfb41dfd"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-283":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"drivers.\",\"outcomes.\",\"contingencies.\",\"correlated.\"],\"stem\":\"The nodes on an interrelationship digraph with the most outgoing arrows are considered:\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"The nodes with the most outgoing arrows are considered drivers or causes of the problem. The nodes with the most incoming arrows are considered outcomes or effects. [IV.D.7]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["drivers.","outcomes.","contingencies.","correlated."],"stem":"The nodes on an interrelationship digraph with the most outgoing arrows are considered:","sub":"def","why":"The nodes with the most outgoing arrows are considered drivers or causes of the problem. The nodes with the most incoming arrows are considered outcomes or effects. [IV.D.7]"},"revision":"5150727d5fc1221ec113b2f3472293698c41950de1f6f5544efdfad5324dfc6e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-284":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"3, 2, 4, 1\",\"2, 4, 1, 3\",\"4, 3, 2, 1\",\"2, 4, 3, 1\"],\"stem\":\"A Black Belt is preparing a simple random sample. List the steps, from first to last, that they must perform to take the sample. 1. Select the members of the population associated with the n random numbers. 2. Determine the sampling frame. 3. Generate a random sample of n different numbers between 1 and N. 4. Assign each member of the population a number from 1 to N.\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The steps to taking a simple random sample are as follows: determine the sampling frame; assign each member of the population a number from 1 to N; generate a random sample of n different numbers from 1 to N; and select the members of the population associated with the n random numbers. [V.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["3, 2, 4, 1","2, 4, 1, 3","4, 3, 2, 1","2, 4, 3, 1"],"stem":"A Black Belt is preparing a simple random sample. List the steps, from first to last, that they must perform to take the sample. 1. Select the members of the population associated with the n random numbers. 2. Determine the sampling frame. 3. Generate a random sample of n different numbers between 1 and N. 4. Assign each member of the population a number from 1 to N.","sub":"mea","why":"The steps to taking a simple random sample are as follows: determine the sampling frame; assign each member of the population a number from 1 to N; generate a random sample of n different numbers from 1 to N; and select the members of the population associated with the n random numbers. [V.B.3]"},"revision":"6f195680b204ebc9c7408464079608b50d217b8f9656f0126efc45c0a3b97b72"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-285":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Bias and precision\",\"Bias and linearity\",\"Stability and linearity\",\"Repeatability and precision\"],\"stem\":\"In measurement systems, the term accuracy encompasses which two components?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"In measurement systems analysis, the term accuracy encompasses both bias and linearity. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Bias and precision","Bias and linearity","Stability and linearity","Repeatability and precision"],"stem":"In measurement systems, the term accuracy encompasses which two components?","sub":"mea","why":"In measurement systems analysis, the term accuracy encompasses both bias and linearity. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"102e9801eb908db633e2a5ecfe281efffcb0b059344e0462548b09a498260a75"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-286":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"accuracy.\",\"reproducibility.\",\"accuracy and precision over time.\",\"repeatability over time.\"],\"stem\":\"The stability of a measurement system refers to its:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The stability of a measurement system refers to its accuracy and precision over time. The stability can be monitored using control charts. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["accuracy.","reproducibility.","accuracy and precision over time.","repeatability over time."],"stem":"The stability of a measurement system refers to its:","sub":"mea","why":"The stability of a measurement system refers to its accuracy and precision over time. The stability can be monitored using control charts. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"f8098fc39f65ae23fe68f7815fd8ad36693aed49fdf45667cbc6aa2f3540b31a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-287":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Stability\",\"Linearity\",\"Bias\",\"Repeatability\"],\"stem\":\"In measurement system analysis, which of the following measures is a component of precision?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Reproducibility and repeatability are both components of precision in a measurement system. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Stability","Linearity","Bias","Repeatability"],"stem":"In measurement system analysis, which of the following measures is a component of precision?","sub":"mea","why":"Reproducibility and repeatability are both components of precision in a measurement system. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"7629c78d2f4325f98857724a7bda021784251ab85784515440c8484f9895acba"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-288":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"The scale is not a precise instrument.\",\"The scale cannot discriminate between conforming and nonconforming assemblies.\",\"The scale is biased toward accepting defective assemblies.\",\"The scale is not a reproducible instrument.\"],\"stem\":\"A scale can weigh an assembly to the nearest pound. The weight specifications are 5 kg ± 0.6 kg. Use 1 lb = 0.454 kg and a 10:1 resolution-to-tolerance guideline. What can be said about the instrument?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The scale''s 1 lb resolution is about 0.454 kg, while the 1.2 kg tolerance width calls for much finer discrimination (about 0.12 kg under a 10:1 guideline). Measurements near 4.4 kg or 5.6 kg can round across a specification boundary, so the scale cannot reliably distinguish conforming from nonconforming assemblies. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["The scale is not a precise instrument.","The scale cannot discriminate between conforming and nonconforming assemblies.","The scale is biased toward accepting defective assemblies.","The scale is not a reproducible instrument."],"stem":"A scale can weigh an assembly to the nearest pound. The weight specifications are 5 kg ± 0.6 kg. Use 1 lb = 0.454 kg and a 10:1 resolution-to-tolerance guideline. What can be said about the instrument?","sub":"mea","why":"The scale''s 1 lb resolution is about 0.454 kg, while the 1.2 kg tolerance width calls for much finer discrimination (about 0.12 kg under a 10:1 guideline). Measurements near 4.4 kg or 5.6 kg can round across a specification boundary, so the scale cannot reliably distinguish conforming from nonconforming assemblies. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"546785259c28f5339b2254d8758b2046cc6abc33d417bff2562ac73e6b0315b5"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-289":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"The measurement system is stable.\",\"Measurement-system variability is too large relative to the tolerance.\",\"Product variability is too large.\",\"The measurement system is adequate for process control.\"],\"stem\":\"A precision-to-tolerance ratio is calculated to be 0.50. Under standard measurement-system acceptance guidelines, what conclusion is justified?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"A precision-to-tolerance ratio (PTR) is a measure of the capability of the measurement system. PTR values less than 0.10 are considered good, while values greater than 0.30 require improvement in the measurement system in terms of reducing its variability. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["The measurement system is stable.","Measurement-system variability is too large relative to the tolerance.","Product variability is too large.","The measurement system is adequate for process control."],"stem":"A precision-to-tolerance ratio is calculated to be 0.50. Under standard measurement-system acceptance guidelines, what conclusion is justified?","sub":"mea","why":"A precision-to-tolerance ratio (PTR) is a measure of the capability of the measurement system. PTR values less than 0.10 are considered good, while values greater than 0.30 require improvement in the measurement system in terms of reducing its variability. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"1fdb3c9016712ee79bf280d46911b2cfdc56956e5c09a1ecaac7082d8c9fe5da"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-29":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Facilitator\",\"Team leader\",\"Scribe\",\"Champion\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following individuals does not generally attend regular Six Sigma project team meetings?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"Team champions do not generally attend regular team meetings. [III.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Facilitator","Team leader","Scribe","Champion"],"stem":"Which of the following individuals does not generally attend regular Six Sigma project team meetings?","sub":"tm","why":"Team champions do not generally attend regular team meetings. [III.A.2]"},"revision":"84522aabd56966872e5690f113b275684c4ecb76c57af1623b79909f3a121f5b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-290":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"the square root of precision.\",\"the square of discrimination.\",\"the precision-to-tolerance ratio.\",\"the square of the fractional total gage R&R.\"],\"stem\":\"If total gage R&R is expressed as a fraction of total study variation, the measurement-system variance contribution is equal to:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Variance contribution is σ²(GRR)/σ²(total), the square of the fractional study-variation ratio σ(GRR)/σ(total). If gage R&R is expressed as a percentage, %Contribution = (%GRR)²/100. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["the square root of precision.","the square of discrimination.","the precision-to-tolerance ratio.","the square of the fractional total gage R&R."],"stem":"If total gage R&R is expressed as a fraction of total study variation, the measurement-system variance contribution is equal to:","sub":"mea","why":"Variance contribution is σ²(GRR)/σ²(total), the square of the fractional study-variation ratio σ(GRR)/σ(total). If gage R&R is expressed as a percentage, %Contribution = (%GRR)²/100. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"45c5e6f0ca3a0490b6c861b62a87c267826161b28dc31a15ef8cfe0158b77fbe"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-291":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"stability.\",\"linearity.\",\"bias.\",\"reproducibility.\"],\"stem\":\"If the bias of a measuring system increases across the measurement range, the measuring device is said to have poor:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"If the bias of a measuring system increases across the measurement range, it is said to have poor linearity. A system with good linearity will have a constant bias across the range of measurement. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["stability.","linearity.","bias.","reproducibility."],"stem":"If the bias of a measuring system increases across the measurement range, the measuring device is said to have poor:","sub":"mea","why":"If the bias of a measuring system increases across the measurement range, it is said to have poor linearity. A system with good linearity will have a constant bias across the range of measurement. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"36766a8b90077e5cb6466a47a4923f0688c8b7706d8ca70502a9cd57741573b7"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-292":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"chart\":{\"type\":\"precision-accuracy\"},\"options\":[\"Quadrant A\",\"Quadrant B\",\"Quadrant C\",\"Quadrant D\"],\"stem\":\"Which quadrant of the figure shows high precision but low accuracy?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The quadrant in the lower right (D) shows an example of high precision. It is consistent (tightly clustered) but not accurate since the points are not at the target. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"chart":{"type":"precision-accuracy"},"options":["Quadrant A","Quadrant B","Quadrant C","Quadrant D"],"stem":"Which quadrant of the figure shows high precision but low accuracy?","sub":"mea","why":"The quadrant in the lower right (D) shows an example of high precision. It is consistent (tightly clustered) but not accurate since the points are not at the target. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"cb85a53aad7ed958c2eb06d5cc851069f9d20e613e072dc84e4c24b512993d37"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-293":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"type\":\"precision-accuracy\"},\"options\":[\"Quadrant A\",\"Quadrant B\",\"Quadrant C\",\"Quadrant D\"],\"stem\":\"Which quadrant in the figure shows high accuracy and low precision?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The quadrant in the upper left (A) shows a system with high accuracy, since the points average out near the target, but low precision because their locations are not consistent. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"type":"precision-accuracy"},"options":["Quadrant A","Quadrant B","Quadrant C","Quadrant D"],"stem":"Which quadrant in the figure shows high accuracy and low precision?","sub":"mea","why":"The quadrant in the upper left (A) shows a system with high accuracy, since the points average out near the target, but low precision because their locations are not consistent. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"26a110f8e0cb9b5ba54faa137b63f57bd5afcd8072b543bae5d530f02c4ecf25"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-294":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"chart\":{\"type\":\"bias-diagram\"},\"options\":[\"Precision\",\"Stability\",\"Linear bias\",\"Repeatability\"],\"stem\":\"What is the measurement system concept depicted in the figure below?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The figure depicts the concept of linear bias: the average of measured values runs parallel to the true/reference value line but is consistently offset from it across the full range of measurement. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"chart":{"type":"bias-diagram"},"options":["Precision","Stability","Linear bias","Repeatability"],"stem":"What is the measurement system concept depicted in the figure below?","sub":"mea","why":"The figure depicts the concept of linear bias: the average of measured values runs parallel to the true/reference value line but is consistently offset from it across the full range of measurement. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"e8abab1d508667383079a67b4fc788aaf62e0642fdb0af5ec482af14c1656e46"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-295":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"13.5%\",\"27%\",\"37%\",\"75%\"],\"stem\":\"Using PTR = 6σ(total)/(USL − LSL), calculate the precision-to-tolerance ratio for specifications 10 ± 0.5 cm and σ(total) = 0.045 cm.\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"PTR = 6σ(total)/(USL − LSL) = 6(0.045)/(10.5 − 9.5) = 0.27, or 27%. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["13.5%","27%","37%","75%"],"stem":"Using PTR = 6σ(total)/(USL − LSL), calculate the precision-to-tolerance ratio for specifications 10 ± 0.5 cm and σ(total) = 0.045 cm.","sub":"mea","why":"PTR = 6σ(total)/(USL − LSL) = 6(0.045)/(10.5 − 9.5) = 0.27, or 27%. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"c9775dc9e8337b22f67268b3a297186a9f488ecb9dbb2292b28ecfce8a0d7cc6"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-296":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"2.44\",\"5.21\",\"5.96\",\"27.14\"],\"stem\":\"A measurement system has been evaluated with the following results: repeatability = 0.81 and reproducibility = 5.15. What is the gage R&R?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Gage R&R combines repeatability and reproducibility: GRR = √(EV² + AV²) = √(0.81² + 5.15²) = 5.21. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["2.44","5.21","5.96","27.14"],"stem":"A measurement system has been evaluated with the following results: repeatability = 0.81 and reproducibility = 5.15. What is the gage R&R?","sub":"mea","why":"Gage R&R combines repeatability and reproducibility: GRR = √(EV² + AV²) = √(0.81² + 5.15²) = 5.21. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"00c4af466c3dfccd2f6d31320c477a19b9d3f6897d3090e7b021297232762f33"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-297":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"1.40\",\"2.23\",\"3.23\",\"3.27\"],\"stem\":\"The gage R&R for a measurement system is equal to 3.45. The equipment variation is found to be equal to 1.22. What is the appraiser variation?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"GRR² = EV² + AV², so AV = √(GRR² − EV²) = √(3.45² − 1.22²) = 3.23. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["1.40","2.23","3.23","3.27"],"stem":"The gage R&R for a measurement system is equal to 3.45. The equipment variation is found to be equal to 1.22. What is the appraiser variation?","sub":"mea","why":"GRR² = EV² + AV², so AV = √(GRR² − EV²) = √(3.45² − 1.22²) = 3.23. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"77a2ce594240b149e9654ced19c23627b43367b73d7a99f81dac2d048ba23332"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-298":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"gage R&R.\",\"equipment variation.\",\"appraiser variation.\",\"precision-to-tolerance ratio.\"],\"stem\":\"The standard deviation due to repeatability is also known as:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Repeatability is also known as equipment variation. It measures the precision under conditions where independent measurement results are obtained with the same method on identical items by the same appraiser using the same equipment within a short period of time. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["gage R&R.","equipment variation.","appraiser variation.","precision-to-tolerance ratio."],"stem":"The standard deviation due to repeatability is also known as:","sub":"mea","why":"Repeatability is also known as equipment variation. It measures the precision under conditions where independent measurement results are obtained with the same method on identical items by the same appraiser using the same equipment within a short period of time. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"a8b5ef60db4ed86b469c029eeb116a405e81449b1e28e81396711ab068374191"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-299":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.79\",\"1.98\",\"3.96\",\"247.5\"],\"stem\":\"Ignoring integer truncation, use NDC = √2[σ(parts)/GRR]. If NDC = 7.00 and gage R&R = 0.40, what is σ(parts)?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Using NDC = √2[σ(parts)/GRR], σ(parts) = NDC × GRR/√2 = 7.00(0.40)/√2 = 1.98. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.79","1.98","3.96","247.5"],"stem":"Ignoring integer truncation, use NDC = √2[σ(parts)/GRR]. If NDC = 7.00 and gage R&R = 0.40, what is σ(parts)?","sub":"mea","why":"Using NDC = √2[σ(parts)/GRR], σ(parts) = NDC × GRR/√2 = 7.00(0.40)/√2 = 1.98. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"9af1521d11f7074cc983e4951b0b52e41073da7e1cba84ad4ea1be22b625ceb4"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"number of employees trained and number of concurrent projects.\",\"leadership, customer focus, and business results.\",\"number of statistical software licenses purchased and black belts trained.\",\"stock price and reductions in staff.\"],\"stem\":\"Categories that are typically evaluated in a Six Sigma maturity model include:\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"Typical categories addressed in a Six Sigma maturity model include Six Sigma leadership, customer focus, Six Sigma strategy, Six Sigma project management, evaluation and motivation, Six Sigma infrastructure, and business results. [I.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["number of employees trained and number of concurrent projects.","leadership, customer focus, and business results.","number of statistical software licenses purchased and black belts trained.","stock price and reductions in staff."],"stem":"Categories that are typically evaluated in a Six Sigma maturity model include:","sub":"p1","why":"Typical categories addressed in a Six Sigma maturity model include Six Sigma leadership, customer focus, Six Sigma strategy, Six Sigma project management, evaluation and motivation, Six Sigma infrastructure, and business results. [I.A.1]"},"revision":"3ffd6f273c8713b9f08caab54b4883aeb40359cdc848cf513180e481b1d428ad"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-30":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"maintaining and publishing meeting minutes.\",\"summarizing progress using visual aids.\",\"notifying the team leader or facilitator of the time remaining on each agenda item.\",\"working with the team leader and facilitator to move the team toward the objective.\"],\"stem\":\"The team coach for an improvement project is usually responsible for:\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"Coaches work with the team leader and facilitator to move the team toward the objective and help provide resources for completing team-member assignments. Additionally, they evaluate the team''s progress. [III.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["maintaining and publishing meeting minutes.","summarizing progress using visual aids.","notifying the team leader or facilitator of the time remaining on each agenda item.","working with the team leader and facilitator to move the team toward the objective."],"stem":"The team coach for an improvement project is usually responsible for:","sub":"tm","why":"Coaches work with the team leader and facilitator to move the team toward the objective and help provide resources for completing team-member assignments. Additionally, they evaluate the team''s progress. [III.A.2]"},"revision":"83141c4173f04cb0c061fdd5ad9801577c4f0328661a241aa6afd7c891195a7e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-300":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"1.45\",\"2.09\",\"2.17\",\"4.71\"],\"stem\":\"A single operator measures 30 parts two times each. The average range of the readings is 2.45 mm. For two trials, use K₁ = 0.8862. What is the standard deviation due to repeatability?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Equipment variation (EV), the standard deviation due to repeatability, is EV = R̄ × K₁. Therefore, EV = 2.45 × 0.8862 = 2.171 mm, which rounds to 2.17 mm. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["1.45","2.09","2.17","4.71"],"stem":"A single operator measures 30 parts two times each. The average range of the readings is 2.45 mm. For two trials, use K₁ = 0.8862. What is the standard deviation due to repeatability?","sub":"mea","why":"Equipment variation (EV), the standard deviation due to repeatability, is EV = R̄ × K₁. Therefore, EV = 2.45 × 0.8862 = 2.171 mm, which rounds to 2.17 mm. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"d5669968acae7edad92a9b690074382e4251d0040dbb0e14d3bca9d8a29beb46"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-301":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"1.03\",\"1.22\",\"1.49\",\"1.54\"],\"stem\":\"Two operators measure three parts two times each. Their average measurements are 45.67 cm and 47.54 cm, and equipment variation is 1.25 cm. For two operators, use K₂ = 0.7071. What is the appraiser variation?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Appraiser variation is the reproducibility component. Using AV = √[(Xdiff × K₂)² − EV²/(nr)], with Xdiff = 1.87, K₂ = 0.7071, EV = 1.25, n = 3 parts, and r = 2 trials, AV = √[(1.87 × 0.7071)² − 1.25²/(3 × 2)] = 1.22. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["1.03","1.22","1.49","1.54"],"stem":"Two operators measure three parts two times each. Their average measurements are 45.67 cm and 47.54 cm, and equipment variation is 1.25 cm. For two operators, use K₂ = 0.7071. What is the appraiser variation?","sub":"mea","why":"Appraiser variation is the reproducibility component. Using AV = √[(Xdiff × K₂)² − EV²/(nr)], with Xdiff = 1.87, K₂ = 0.7071, EV = 1.25, n = 3 parts, and r = 2 trials, AV = √[(1.87 × 0.7071)² − 1.25²/(3 × 2)] = 1.22. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"57dd75e6ea95c451df76603a12627aef6b5c09a23fa01ac978e07319edffd9fa"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-302":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Gage R&R and appraiser variation\",\"Part-to-part variation and gage R&R\",\"Equipment variation and gage R&R\",\"Part-to-part variation and repeatability\"],\"stem\":\"What components comprise total observed study variation?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Total observed study variation combines part-to-part variation with gage R&R. Gage R&R is the measurement-system component and combines repeatability and reproducibility. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Gage R&R and appraiser variation","Part-to-part variation and gage R&R","Equipment variation and gage R&R","Part-to-part variation and repeatability"],"stem":"What components comprise total observed study variation?","sub":"mea","why":"Total observed study variation combines part-to-part variation with gage R&R. Gage R&R is the measurement-system component and combines repeatability and reproducibility. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"49234c6b9f705141bd35150b06e2bcc5bb774d5a004ee099f44ea9f06a6c3993"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-303":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Percent study variation\",\"Repeatability\",\"Percent tolerance\",\"Reproducibility\"],\"stem\":\"ABC Manufacturing performed a gage R&R study on a pair of calipers used to measure the width of a part. The customer has since changed the specifications on the drawing for the width. What part of the gage R&R is likely affected when a new gage R&R study is performed?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The calipers used for measurement have not changed, so it is not expected that the components of gage R&R will be affected by the change in specifications. However, the percent tolerance ratio will be affected. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Percent study variation","Repeatability","Percent tolerance","Reproducibility"],"stem":"ABC Manufacturing performed a gage R&R study on a pair of calipers used to measure the width of a part. The customer has since changed the specifications on the drawing for the width. What part of the gage R&R is likely affected when a new gage R&R study is performed?","sub":"mea","why":"The calipers used for measurement have not changed, so it is not expected that the components of gage R&R will be affected by the change in specifications. However, the percent tolerance ratio will be affected. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"5b865ac989df18e50358b317cf1e3af9a993ef41db86adca741aca26f5f611e2"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-304":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"AIAG methodology\",\"ANOVA analysis\",\"Control chart method\",\"Percent agreement\"],\"stem\":\"What method can be used to assess a measurement system that uses pass/fail classification?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The percent agreement method can be used to assess a measurement system that uses pass/fail classification. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["AIAG methodology","ANOVA analysis","Control chart method","Percent agreement"],"stem":"What method can be used to assess a measurement system that uses pass/fail classification?","sub":"mea","why":"The percent agreement method can be used to assess a measurement system that uses pass/fail classification. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"2529effd77debde192728fbd8f87e483bbe555080bb53a393bd704d725eae45c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-305":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Repeat the gage R&R study using the same samples and operators.\",\"Reduce the number of samples.\",\"Reduce the number of operators.\",\"Review the gage for improvement.\"],\"stem\":\"A gage R&R study indicates that the number of distinct categories is less than five. Which of the following steps should be taken next?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"According to the Automotive Industry Action Group (AIAG), if the number of distinct categories is less than five, the instrument should be reviewed for improvement. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Repeat the gage R&R study using the same samples and operators.","Reduce the number of samples.","Reduce the number of operators.","Review the gage for improvement."],"stem":"A gage R&R study indicates that the number of distinct categories is less than five. Which of the following steps should be taken next?","sub":"mea","why":"According to the Automotive Industry Action Group (AIAG), if the number of distinct categories is less than five, the instrument should be reviewed for improvement. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"f87cc2abb9a3db3d34ce7599411ecad7e29fee1bd80aa50c09a311dc48818190"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-306":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Nominal\",\"Ordinal\",\"Interval\",\"Ratio\"],\"stem\":\"Appraisers classify each item as poor, fair, good, or excellent. What measurement scale is used for these ordered attribute ratings?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Poor, fair, good, and excellent are ordered categories, so this particular attribute agreement study uses an ordinal scale. Attribute agreement can also use nominal categories when no order exists. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Nominal","Ordinal","Interval","Ratio"],"stem":"Appraisers classify each item as poor, fair, good, or excellent. What measurement scale is used for these ordered attribute ratings?","sub":"mea","why":"Poor, fair, good, and excellent are ordered categories, so this particular attribute agreement study uses an ordinal scale. Attribute agreement can also use nominal categories when no order exists. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"d5167921e1dc671a6ba7d348913f149b932d1dffae3f3dcb6f4bd78a9cef89a0"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-307":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"each appraiser versus standard and part variation.\",\"all appraisers versus the standard and between appraiser agreement.\",\"within appraiser agreement and part variation.\",\"all appraisers versus standard and gage R&R.\"],\"stem\":\"An attribute agreement analysis can include output on:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The output for an attribute agreement analysis can include four components: each appraiser versus the standard, between appraisers, all appraisers versus the standard, and within appraiser. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["each appraiser versus standard and part variation.","all appraisers versus the standard and between appraiser agreement.","within appraiser agreement and part variation.","all appraisers versus standard and gage R&R."],"stem":"An attribute agreement analysis can include output on:","sub":"mea","why":"The output for an attribute agreement analysis can include four components: each appraiser versus the standard, between appraisers, all appraisers versus the standard, and within appraiser. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"1d16371d0209ee1e02e6fce72db8eb83d50acf0445dd8ce7c7af284aab6a3f2b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-308":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Bias\",\"Linearity\",\"Stability\",\"Reproducibility\"],\"stem\":\"A torque wrench is verified to be within specifications with a torque analyzer before every use at ABC Manufacturing. ABC wants to move to verifying the torque wrench once a week. What measurement systems analysis evaluation technique will ensure that the torque wrench will exhibit only common cause variation over the week?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Stability addresses the variation in a measurement system over time. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Bias","Linearity","Stability","Reproducibility"],"stem":"A torque wrench is verified to be within specifications with a torque analyzer before every use at ABC Manufacturing. ABC wants to move to verifying the torque wrench once a week. What measurement systems analysis evaluation technique will ensure that the torque wrench will exhibit only common cause variation over the week?","sub":"mea","why":"Stability addresses the variation in a measurement system over time. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"655d4fd1a88ff9e3dc570390198964fd9a2a5e9582842df26651a800c7107a51"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-309":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.091\",\"0.112\",\"0.302\",\"0.335\"],\"stem\":\"The range of weights for five parts is 0.75 grams. Use PV = R̄part × K₃ and K₃ = 0.4030 for five parts. What is the part variation?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Part variation is PV = R̄part × K₃ = 0.75 × 0.4030 = 0.302 grams. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.091","0.112","0.302","0.335"],"stem":"The range of weights for five parts is 0.75 grams. Use PV = R̄part × K₃ and K₃ = 0.4030 for five parts. What is the part variation?","sub":"mea","why":"Part variation is PV = R̄part × K₃ = 0.75 × 0.4030 = 0.302 grams. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"fb6763b63383749089c19b87937859dd0f490bffb417b1720abeb0c7bb3f42b9"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-31":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"coach.\",\"champion.\",\"leader.\",\"facilitator.\"],\"stem\":\"The person providing assistance when necessary to individual team members to ensure that action items are completed is the:\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"A coach helps provide resources for completion of team-member assignments. [III.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["coach.","champion.","leader.","facilitator."],"stem":"The person providing assistance when necessary to individual team members to ensure that action items are completed is the:","sub":"tm","why":"A coach helps provide resources for completion of team-member assignments. [III.A.2]"},"revision":"6395720f269f8dadbfd413c771de86eb0cfdc5d8d3eaad8602808def6c628d81"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-310":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"1.83\",\"2.49\",\"2.65\",\"3.35\"],\"stem\":\"What is the total observed study variation when part variation is 1.60 and gage R&R is 0.89?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Total observed study variation combines gage R&R and part-to-part variation: TV = √(GRR² + PV²) = √(0.89² + 1.60²) = 1.83. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["1.83","2.49","2.65","3.35"],"stem":"What is the total observed study variation when part variation is 1.60 and gage R&R is 0.89?","sub":"mea","why":"Total observed study variation combines gage R&R and part-to-part variation: TV = √(GRR² + PV²) = √(0.89² + 1.60²) = 1.83. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"f7b18890293d57b637ee0f9859678c3e4c1d2f47ad6a5f8771944700cea9386f"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-311":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"assessing the reliability of audit results.\",\"investigating where the data come from, who collects it, and how the data was generated.\",\"assessing the validity of reproducibility findings.\",\"investigating the database used to store measurement readings.\"],\"ste