 system is performing correctly.","vet the measurement standard against the equipment.","calculate the gage R&R of an instrument.","adjust measurement devices to reduce defect detection."],"stem":"The purpose of calibration is to:","sub":"mea","why":"The purpose of calibration is to ensure that the measurement system is performing correctly. [V.C.3]"},"revision":"5197fda4e7d351024e97f22065911b4d01f812edfd8659d14fd2f8c56c72b8dc"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-318":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"lack of precision.\",\"lack of physical ability.\",\"parallax effect.\",\"vibration.\"],\"stem\":\"An example of measurement error due to equipment is:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Causes of measurement error can be classified under equipment, methods, appraiser, environment, parts, and measurement. Lack of precision is a cause of equipment measurement error. [V.C.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["lack of precision.","lack of physical ability.","parallax effect.","vibration."],"stem":"An example of measurement error due to equipment is:","sub":"mea","why":"Causes of measurement error can be classified under equipment, methods, appraiser, environment, parts, and measurement. Lack of precision is a cause of equipment measurement error. [V.C.3]"},"revision":"425ecd63f7a73ad3ec0e96c1175cac84bc649867e398254ad4ead1ba55836d84"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-319":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"statistics.\",\"parameters.\",\"estimates.\",\"ordinal measures.\"],\"stem\":\"A one-time custom order of 75 commemorative coins was minted. The weight of each coin was recorded, and a Black Belt calculated the mean and standard deviation from the data. The calculated values are considered to be:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The Black Belt has weighed all the items in the population. The calculated values are thus considered population parameters. [V.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["statistics.","parameters.","estimates.","ordinal measures."],"stem":"A one-time custom order of 75 commemorative coins was minted. The weight of each coin was recorded, and a Black Belt calculated the mean and standard deviation from the data. The calculated values are considered to be:","sub":"mea","why":"The Black Belt has weighed all the items in the population. The calculated values are thus considered population parameters. [V.D.1]"},"revision":"c0aeacad11fdcb6ad88ba49e2b6f2db53f04c6e7a416173ac507f29de5756208"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-32":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"champion.\",\"sponsor.\",\"mentor.\",\"leader.\"],\"stem\":\"The individual responsible for organizing, hosting, and running the team meeting is the:\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"The team leader is responsible for organizing, hosting, and running the team meeting. [III.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["champion.","sponsor.","mentor.","leader."],"stem":"The individual responsible for organizing, hosting, and running the team meeting is the:","sub":"tm","why":"The team leader is responsible for organizing, hosting, and running the team meeting. [III.A.2]"},"revision":"6b129877b1cad579870c0d5422cdc74e60f3fe6e4f10e200d7ec41ee254c00a6"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-320":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"N\",\"n − 1\",\"N − 1\",\"√N\"],\"stem\":\"What is the denominator in the population variance formula?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The population variance is σ² = Σ(xᵢ − μ)²/N, where N is the population size. Therefore, N is the denominator. [V.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["N","n − 1","N − 1","√N"],"stem":"What is the denominator in the population variance formula?","sub":"mea","why":"The population variance is σ² = Σ(xᵢ − μ)²/N, where N is the population size. Therefore, N is the denominator. [V.D.1]"},"revision":"b9156641637d43768c751762481f27bc8538b6da281529a1bdf66de38e14ea62"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-321":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Option\",\"Preference\"],\"rows\":[[\"In-office full-time\",\"12%\"],[\"Hybrid\",\"45%\"],[\"Work from home full-time\",\"33%\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"proportions.\",\"means.\",\"intervals.\",\"ranges.\"],\"stem\":\"A survey reports percentages for three selected work-location response categories in the table below; 10% of responses are in other or unreported categories. The displayed percentages are considered to be:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Each displayed percentage is the proportion of respondents recorded in that category. The three displayed proportions total 90%; the stem explicitly accounts for the remaining 10%. [V.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"columns":["Option","Preference"],"rows":[["In-office full-time","12%"],["Hybrid","45%"],["Work from home full-time","33%"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["proportions.","means.","intervals.","ranges."],"stem":"A survey reports percentages for three selected work-location response categories in the table below; 10% of responses are in other or unreported categories. The displayed percentages are considered to be:","sub":"mea","why":"Each displayed percentage is the proportion of respondents recorded in that category. The three displayed proportions total 90%; the stem explicitly accounts for the remaining 10%. [V.D.1]"},"revision":"ebd5b60f42592de00ff0f557c6f001f2b232f15034f5ea4699f16edba4ae1b66"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-322":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Sample variance\",\"Sample range\",\"Sample mode\",\"Sample correlation\"],\"stem\":\"Which statistic is a measure of central tendency?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The sample mode is a statistic that is a measure of central tendency. [V.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Sample variance","Sample range","Sample mode","Sample correlation"],"stem":"Which statistic is a measure of central tendency?","sub":"mea","why":"The sample mode is a statistic that is a measure of central tendency. [V.D.1]"},"revision":"ea596a3360b7a616304e89797d78f1406cb8fe029f7ca2b78aba973748b5d10b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-323":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.225\",\"0.474\",\"1.006\",\"4.528\"],\"stem\":\"A random sample of n = 20 observations is taken from a population with μ = 34.5 and σ² = 4.5. What is the standard deviation of the sampling distribution of the sample mean?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The population standard deviation is σ = √4.5. The standard error of the mean is σ/√n = √4.5/√20 = 0.474. [V.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.225","0.474","1.006","4.528"],"stem":"A random sample of n = 20 observations is taken from a population with μ = 34.5 and σ² = 4.5. What is the standard deviation of the sampling distribution of the sample mean?","sub":"mea","why":"The population standard deviation is σ = √4.5. The standard error of the mean is σ/√n = √4.5/√20 = 0.474. [V.D.2]"},"revision":"8fccd98d2d8aef15cf9fb62aa1f936932ba0878f5e680614307d20a9ca60e34c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-324":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Bayes'' theorem\",\"Central limit theorem\",\"Gauss-Markov theorem\",\"Law of large numbers\"],\"stem\":\"What theorem explains why the sample mean of independent observations is often approximately normally distributed and therefore supports use of an X̄ chart?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The central limit theorem states that, for independent observations from a population with finite mean and variance, the sampling distribution of the sample mean approaches normality as the sample size increases. This supports plotting subgroup means on an X̄ chart even when the individual observations are not perfectly normal. [V.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Bayes'' theorem","Central limit theorem","Gauss-Markov theorem","Law of large numbers"],"stem":"What theorem explains why the sample mean of independent observations is often approximately normally distributed and therefore supports use of an X̄ chart?","sub":"mea","why":"The central limit theorem states that, for independent observations from a population with finite mean and variance, the sampling distribution of the sample mean approaches normality as the sample size increases. This supports plotting subgroup means on an X̄ chart even when the individual observations are not perfectly normal. [V.D.2]"},"revision":"fa5b4c74d030496063b47dfe56307277ea30041bb1c94f1e06b1328c5e010358"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-325":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Normal with mean 2.0 and variance 0.04\",\"Normal with mean 2.0 and variance 0.40\",\"Exponential with mean 200\",\"Normal with mean 200 and variance 400\"],\"stem\":\"One hundred independent observations are taken from an exponential distribution with mean 2.0 and variance 4.0. The observations are summed. What is the approximate distribution of the sum?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"By the central limit theorem, the sum is approximately normal with mean nμ = 200 and variance nσ² = 400. [V.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Normal with mean 2.0 and variance 0.04","Normal with mean 2.0 and variance 0.40","Exponential with mean 200","Normal with mean 200 and variance 400"],"stem":"One hundred independent observations are taken from an exponential distribution with mean 2.0 and variance 4.0. The observations are summed. What is the approximate distribution of the sum?","sub":"mea","why":"By the central limit theorem, the sum is approximately normal with mean nμ = 200 and variance nσ² = 400. [V.D.2]"},"revision":"da1980fde53db1b14432a7b5371541707bf0139cb9704b0c8bb55149d9afb2aa"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-326":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Normal\",\"Student’s t\",\"Exponential\",\"Lognormal\"],\"stem\":\"A random sample of n = 40 independent observations is taken from an exponential distribution with scale parameter β = 2. Which distribution approximates the sampling distribution of x̄?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"For an exponential distribution with scale β = 2, the population mean is 2 and variance is 4. With n = 40, the central limit theorem makes the sampling distribution of x̄ approximately normal, with mean 2 and variance 4/40 = 0.10. [V.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Normal","Student’s t","Exponential","Lognormal"],"stem":"A random sample of n = 40 independent observations is taken from an exponential distribution with scale parameter β = 2. Which distribution approximates the sampling distribution of x̄?","sub":"mea","why":"For an exponential distribution with scale β = 2, the population mean is 2 and variance is 4. With n = 40, the central limit theorem makes the sampling distribution of x̄ approximately normal, with mean 2 and variance 4/40 = 0.10. [V.D.2]"},"revision":"dd1eb4662731b2b66ec80d978c029706e33a7430c158dac7cf5520906332e755"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-327":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"kurtosis.\",\"skewness.\",\"variation.\",\"range.\"],\"stem\":\"The measure of the heaviness of a distribution''s tails is known as:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The measure of the heaviness of a distribution''s tails is its kurtosis. This measure is often mistakenly thought to represent the peakedness of a distribution. [V.D.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["kurtosis.","skewness.","variation.","range."],"stem":"The measure of the heaviness of a distribution''s tails is known as:","sub":"mea","why":"The measure of the heaviness of a distribution''s tails is its kurtosis. This measure is often mistakenly thought to represent the peakedness of a distribution. [V.D.3]"},"revision":"7aeb9210a1457e96e6292b26b356976cc73cebf9a9f8b959860fb4a2126484e1"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-328":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Bell-shaped\",\"Skewed to the right\",\"Skewed to the left\",\"Disjointed\"],\"stem\":\"The skewness of a distribution is equal to −2.3. What is the shape of the distribution?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"A negative value of skew implies that the distribution is skewed to the left. [V.D.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Bell-shaped","Skewed to the right","Skewed 