found with the addition rule. [V.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Multiplication rule","Mutually exclusive rule","Complement rule","Addition rule"],"stem":"Metal plates are inspected for two types of defects: burrs and scratches. A Black Belt calculates the probability of finding a burr or a scratch on a metal panel. Which probability rule would they use?","sub":"mea","why":"Let Event A = a burr and Event B = a scratch. The probability of A or B is found with the addition rule. [V.E.1]"},"revision":"20475beb2d780ded2a93d1b78ab321a1804f743e1223eef05da9f2ef5cc73a48"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-344":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"4\",\"12\",\"220\",\"1320\"],\"stem\":\"A project team consists of twelve members. How many ways can members be chosen for a three-member subcommittee?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Because order does not matter, use combinations: C(12,3) = 12!/[3!(12 − 3)!] = 220. [V.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["4","12","220","1320"],"stem":"A project team consists of twelve members. How many ways can members be chosen for a three-member subcommittee?","sub":"mea","why":"Because order does not matter, use combinations: C(12,3) = 12!/[3!(12 − 3)!] = 220. [V.E.1]"},"revision":"137b5881cbc88e114350ad9bd7c3038d69496671954677b7500ccbeb8838c605"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-345":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"14,190\",\"28,380\",\"42,570\",\"85,140\"],\"stem\":\"A sample of three numbers is selected from the numbers 1 to 45, inclusive. How many different combinations are possible?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Because order does not matter, use combinations: C(45,3) = 45!/[3!(45 − 3)!] = 14,190. [V.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["14,190","28,380","42,570","85,140"],"stem":"A sample of three numbers is selected from the numbers 1 to 45, inclusive. How many different combinations are possible?","sub":"mea","why":"Because order does not matter, use combinations: C(45,3) = 45!/[3!(45 − 3)!] = 14,190. [V.E.1]"},"revision":"1cf0673eb0845f7ca8b55767e1bdbbabb3acfcbc668c28bad02da9bb447d37d8"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-346":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"independent.\",\"mutually exclusive.\",\"multiplicative.\",\"empirical.\"],\"stem\":\"When the occurrence of one event negates the possibility of another event, the events are referred to as:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Mutually exclusive events cannot occur at the same time. A light switch is either on or off; it can''t be both. [V.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["independent.","mutually exclusive.","multiplicative.","empirical."],"stem":"When the occurrence of one event negates the possibility of another event, the events are referred to as:","sub":"mea","why":"Mutually exclusive events cannot occur at the same time. A light switch is either on or off; it can''t be both. [V.E.1]"},"revision":"c957ac4106347e334a0c196f0d25d3e4ea8ffa98144f157d45efa36d5a3962a2"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-347":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"P(A | B) = P(A)\",\"P(A | B) cannot be determined.\",\"P(A | B) = P(A) + P(B)\",\"P(A | B) = 0\"],\"stem\":\"If events A and B are independent, which statement is true about the conditional probability P(A | B)?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"For independent events, knowing that B occurred provides no information about A, so P(A | B) = P(A). [V.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["P(A | B) = P(A)","P(A | B) cannot be determined.","P(A | B) = P(A) + P(B)","P(A | B) = 0"],"stem":"If events A and B are independent, which statement is true about the conditional probability P(A | B)?","sub":"mea","why":"For independent events, knowing that B occurred provides no information about A, so P(A | B) = P(A). [V.E.1]"},"revision":"6583e03730375365ec05216c4ecbcd053db779476ec96f82e3d653da713823c4"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-348":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"For a given n and r, the number of combinations is generally larger than the number of permutations.\",\"Order is important for combinations but not for permutations.\",\"Order is important for permutations but not for combinations.\",\"For a given n and r, the number of permutations is always greater than the number of combinations.\"],\"stem\":\"What is the main difference between a permutation and a combination?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"For permutations, order matters; for combinations, it does not. For a given n and r, the number of permutations exceeds the number of combinations when r exceeds 1, while the counts are equal when r = 1. [V.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["For a given n and r, the number of combinations is generally larger than the number of permutations.","Order is important for combinations but not for permutations.","Order is important for permutations but not for combinations.","For a given n and r, the number of permutations is always greater than the number of combinations."],"stem":"What is the main difference between a permutation and a combination?","sub":"mea","why":"For permutations, order matters; for combinations, it does not. For a given n and r, the number of permutations exceeds the number of combinations when r exceeds 1, while the counts are equal when r = 1. [V.E.1]"},"revision":"f0c928070ecce9f5505ae6ca25cc76d6d6adf3d84d20ec9e248212e8d8a244d2"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-349":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"independent.\",\"multiplicative.\",\"dependent.\",\"conditional.\"],\"stem\":\"An event affects the probability of another event occurring. The events are referred to as:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"If Events A and B are dependent, the occurrence of Event A affects the probability of occurrence of Event B, and vice versa. [V.E.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["independent.","multiplicative.","dependent.","conditional."],"stem":"An event affects the probability of another event occurring. The events are referred to as:","sub":"mea","why":"If Events A and B are dependent, the occurrence of Event A affects the probability of occurrence of Event B, and vice versa. [V.E.1]"},"revision":"a665fe94112836a190dc538fe215f7a2da48f8fb7a942cf7f38c6e172801a492"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-35":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Process dynamics\",\"Team building\",\"Project management\",\"Program management\"],\"stem\":\"Which training topic most directly helps process-improvement team members understand group behavior and avoid personality conflicts and turf wars?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"Team-building training helps members understand how teams work and which individual behaviors advance progress, reducing personality conflicts and turf wars. [III.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Process dynamics","Team building","Project management","Program management"],"stem":"Which training topic most directly helps process-improvement team members understand group behavior and avoid personality conflicts and turf wars?","sub":"tm","why":"Team-building training helps members understand how teams work and which individual behaviors advance progress, reducing personality conflicts and turf wars. [III.A.4]"},"revision":"b887a42b68b88913b055f761b096c17230b241edea5cc06dbc8c2999d94bf28e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-350":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Binomial\",\"Poisson\",\"Normal\",\"Hypergeometric\"],\"stem\":\"An inspector sampled four circuit boards from a steady stream of production that is known to be 12% nonconforming. What distribution would they use to find the probability of selecting two nonconforming units in the sample?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Use a binomial model because each sampled board is classified as conforming or nonconforming, the nonconforming probability remains 0.12, and sampling from a steady production stream makes the trials reasonably independent. [V.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Binomial","Poisson","Normal","Hypergeometric"],"stem":"An inspector sampled four circuit boards from a steady stream of production that is known to be 12% nonconforming. What distribution would they use to find the probability of selecting two nonconforming units in the sample?","sub":"mea","why":"Use a binomial model because each sampled board is classified as conforming or nonconforming, the nonconforming probability remains 0.12, and sampling from a steady production stream makes the trials reasonably independent. [V.E.2]"},"revision":"e4c63442f09bafe688e92c232ba05d386a946587f8c67e756d517f17b526d020"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-351":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.082\",\"0.257\",\"0.287\",\"0.544\"],\"stem\":\"Assume calls arrive independently according to a Poisson process at a constant mean rate of 2.5 calls per minute. What is the probability of fewer than 2 calls in the next minute?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The count is Poisson with mean λ = 2.5. “Fewer than 2” means X ≤ 1, so P(X ≤ 1) = exp(−2.5)(1 + 2.5) = 0.2873. [V.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.082","0.257","0.287","0.544"],"stem":"Assume calls arrive independently according to a Poisson process at a constant mean rate of 2.5 calls per minute. What is the probability of fewer than 2 calls in the next minute?","sub":"mea","why":"The count is Poisson with mean λ = 2.5. “Fewer than 2” means X ≤ 1, so P(X ≤ 1) = exp(−2.5)(1 + 2.5) = 0.2873. [V.E.2]"},"revision":"d84a93698cfcef7c254b1b7733586608da2c5264f6ebd7068e08674f2fa256a6"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-352":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Hypergeometric\",\"Binomial\",\"Poisson\",\"Normal\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following distributions is continuous?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The normal distribution is continuous. The other distributions listed are discrete probability distributions. [V.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Hypergeometric","Binomial","Poisson","Normal"],"stem":"Which of the following distributions is continuous?","sub":"mea","why":"The normal distribution is continuous. The other distributions listed are discrete probability distributions. [V.E.2]"},"revision":"441d6ea642d2be3b75824d23e2450054353adb84a5e044ca637fb988ab2faaa2"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-353":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Hourly capacity x\",\"P(X ≤ x)\"],\"rows\":[[\"11\",\"0.9085\"],[\"12\",\"0.9496\"],[\"13\",\"0.9739\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"10\",\"11\",\"12\",\"13\"],\"stem\":\"An emergency department handles an average of 7.7 patients each hour on a Wednesday evening. Using the cumulative Poisson probabilities below, how many arrivals should the check-in desk plan for if it wants capacity to meet or exceed the 95th percentile?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"For X ~ Poisson(7.7), choose the smallest capacity x for which P(X ≤ x) is at least 0.9500. P(X ≤ 12) = 0.9496 is still below 0.9500, while P(X ≤ 13) = 0.9739 exceeds it. Therefore, the desk should plan for 13 arrivals per hour. [V.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"chart":{"columns":["Hourly capacity x","P(X ≤ x)"],"rows":[["11","0.9085"],["12","0.9496"],["13","0.9739"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["10","11","12","13"],"stem":"An emergency department handles an average of 7.7 patients each hour on a Wednesday evening. Using the cumulative Poisson probabilities below, how many arrivals should the check-in desk plan for if it wants capacity to meet or exceed the 95th percentile?","sub":"mea","why":"For X ~ Poisson(7.7), choose the smallest capacity x for which P(X ≤ x) is at least 0.9500. P(X ≤ 12) = 0.9496 is still below 0.9500, while P(X ≤ 13) = 0.9739 exceeds it. Therefore, the desk should plan for 13 arrivals per hour. [V.E.2]"},"revision":"45dd975643dd28166de4fe54e564178af25e8128f1c798d522d9f48f400f987a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-354":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Standard-normal value\",\"Cumulative probability\"],\"rows\":[[\"z = 1.75\",\"P(Z ≤ 1.75) = 0.9599\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"40\",\"42\",\"45\",\"50\"],\"stem\":\"A normally distributed process metric has mean 32.79 and standard deviation 4.12. Four percent of production is above the upper specification limit. Use P(Z ≤ 1.75) = 0.9599 ≈ 0.96. What is the upper specification limit?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The 96th percentile of the standard normal distribution is z ≈ 1.75. Converting to the process scale gives x = μ + zσ = 32.79 + 1.75(4.12) = 40.0. [V.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"columns":["Standard-normal value","Cumulative probability"],"rows":[["z = 1.75","P(Z ≤ 1.75) = 0.9599"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["40","42","45","50"],"stem":"A normally distributed process metric has mean 32.79 and standard deviation 4.12. Four percent of production is above the upper specification limit. Use P(Z ≤ 1.75) = 0.9599 ≈ 0.96. What is the upper specification limit?","sub":"mea","why":"The 96th percentile of the standard normal distribution is z ≈ 1.75. Converting to the process scale gives x = μ + zσ = 32.79 + 1.75(4.12) = 40.0. [V.E.2]"},"revision":"7a2db7d57ba48b72ff814364c20e5ed23de2fc2e1d24b22f7dc20b4bb889845b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-355":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Pr(X = 2)\",\"Pr(X ≤ 1)\",\"Pr(X ≤ 2)\",\"1 − Pr(X = 2)\"],\"stem\":\"Ten square yards of carpet are inspected for cosmetic defects. The probability of finding two or fewer defects can be expressed using which probability statement?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"For a discrete probability distribution, \\\"two or fewer\\\" includes 0, 1, and 2. Therefore, the probability statement is Pr(X ≤ 2). [V.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Pr(X = 2)","Pr(X ≤ 1)","Pr(X ≤ 2)","1 − Pr(X = 2)"],"stem":"Ten square yards of carpet are inspected for cosmetic defects. The probability of finding two or fewer defects can be expressed using which probability statement?","sub":"mea","why":"For a discrete probability distribution, \"two or fewer\" includes 0, 1, and 2. Therefore, the probability statement is Pr(X ≤ 2). [V.E.2]"},"revision":"b2bd255e4a72e7a084e5c20e282731ec509c389dd7982bb2517955f4b3c87adf"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-356":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"20.3%\",\"25.2%\",\"75.4%\",\"80.0%\"],\"stem\":\"A process output is normally distributed with μ = 53.7 inches and σ = 7.59 inches. Use the standard normal value Φ(0.83) = 0.7967. What percentage of product will be larger than 60 inches?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Standardize 60 inches: z = (60 − 53.7)/7.59 = 0.83. Thus P(X > 60) = 1 − Φ(0.83) = 1 − 0.7967 = 0.2033, or 20.3%. [V.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["20.3%","25.2%","75.4%","80.0%"],"stem":"A process output is normally distributed with μ = 53.7 inches and σ = 7.59 inches. Use the standard normal value Φ(0.83) = 0.7967. What percentage of product will be larger than 60 inches?","sub":"mea","why":"Standardize 60 inches: z = (60 − 53.7)/7.59 = 0.83. Thus P(X > 60) = 1 − Φ(0.83) = 1 − 0.7967 = 0.2033, or 20.3%. [V.E.2]"},"revision":"5b5d047c465b92d7a28e71ffda8592da556ce36e03a102a38a0d73d7350c0792"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-357":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"μ ± 1.5σ\",\"σ ± 1μ\",\"μ ± 1σ\",\"σ ± 1.5μ\"],\"stem\":\"For a normal distribution, a little more than two-thirds of the values lie between:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"For a normal distribution, approximately 68.3% of values lie within one standard deviation of the mean, or μ ± 1σ. [V.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["μ ± 1.5σ","σ ± 1μ","μ ± 1σ","σ ± 1.5μ"],"stem":"For a normal distribution, a little more than two-thirds of the values lie between:","sub":"mea","why":"For a normal distribution, approximately 68.3% of values lie within one standard deviation of the mean, or μ ± 1σ. [V.E.2]"},"revision":"225cf73269e166f7f8f362defa90673e47eec4ec26ed824c5a3feb577f2cbfbd"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-358":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.0746\",\"0.0105\",\"0.9885\",\"0.9990\"],\"stem\":\"Assume 10 independently sampled parts, each with constant defective probability p = 0.05. What is the probability of fewer than 3 defectives?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"This is a binomial problem because each part is classified as defective or nondefective, the defective probability is constant, and there is a fixed number of independent trials. Fewer than three defectives means Pr(X ≤ 2). With n = 10 and p = 0.05, sum Pr(X = 0) + Pr(X = 1) + Pr(X = 2) to obtain 0.9885. [V.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.0746","0.0105","0.9885","0.9990"],"stem":"Assume 10 independently sampled parts, each with constant defective probability p = 0.05. What is the probability of fewer than 3 defectives?","sub":"mea","why":"This is a binomial problem because each part is classified as defective or nondefective, the defective probability is constant, and there is a fixed number of independent trials. Fewer than three defectives means Pr(X ≤ 2). With n = 10 and p = 0.05, sum Pr(X = 0) + Pr(X = 1) + Pr(X = 2) to obtain 0.9885. [V.E.2]"},"revision":"7f5070b801b0b0b3a2cb1bfcc4b812da9b710cb7e59b17930f17145ae77fb3f8"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-359":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Line A\",\"Line B\",\"Both have equal yields.\",\"This cannot be determined from the information given.\"],\"stem\":\"Two manufacturing lines produce a bioplastic sheet with a tensile strength requirement of at least 9 MPa. Using the convention N(μ, σ²), Line A tensile strength follows N(12.5, 2.56) and Line B follows N(11.5, 6.25). Which line has a lower yield?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Yield is the percentage of product that meets specifications. For Line A, P(X ≥ 9) = P[Z ≥ (9 − 12.5)/√2.56] = P(Z ≥ −2.19). For Line B, P(X ≥ 9) = P[Z ≥ (9 − 11.5)/√6.25] = P(Z ≥ −1.00). Since P(Z ≥ −2.19) is greater than P(Z ≥ −1.00), Line A has the higher yield and Line B has the lower yield. [V.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Line A","Line B","Both have equal yields.","This cannot be determined from the information given."],"stem":"Two manufacturing lines produce a bioplastic sheet with a tensile strength requirement of at least 9 MPa. Using the convention N(μ, σ²), Line A tensile strength follows N(12.5, 2.56) and Line B follows N(11.5, 6.25). Which line has a lower yield?","sub":"mea","why":"Yield is the percentage of product that meets specifications. For Line A, P(X ≥ 9) = P[Z ≥ (9 − 12.5)/√2.56] = P(Z ≥ −2.19). For Line B, P(X ≥ 9) = P[Z ≥ (9 − 11.5)/√6.25] = P(Z ≥ −1.00). Since P(Z ≥ −2.19) is greater than P(Z ≥ −1.00), Line A has the higher yield and Line B has the lower yield. [V.E.2]"},"revision":"4948182aae956fe76039dc89a732c23e7388711ca8e27d5cd232c45c5bc6f95e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-36":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"A sponsor with a vested interest in the project\",\"A Yellow Belt in attendance at all meetings\",\"Required use of designed experiments\",\"Advanced statistical training for all team members\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following items can best contribute to team success?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"A sponsor with a vested interest in the project can help remove barriers and roadblocks, thereby setting the team up for success. [III.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["A sponsor with a vested interest in the project","A Yellow Belt in attendance at all meetings","Required use of designed experiments","Advanced statistical training for all team members"],"stem":"Which of the following items can best contribute to team success?","sub":"tm","why":"A sponsor with a vested interest in the project can help remove barriers and roadblocks, thereby setting the team up for success. [III.A.4]"},"revision":"deb4f8ac940e5f0457f75eb02f699658f6a0c682a1d16872f9c970a3494ec8d7"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-360":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Standard normal\",\"F\",\"Chi-square\",\"Student''s t\"],\"stem\":\"Which distribution is used to test goodness of fit, association, and the standard deviation of the normal distribution?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The chi-square distribution is used to test goodness of fit, association, and the standard deviation of the normal distribution. [V.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Standard normal","F","Chi-square","Student''s t"],"stem":"Which distribution is used to test goodness of fit, association, and the standard deviation of the normal distribution?","sub":"mea","why":"The chi-square distribution is used to test goodness of fit, association, and the standard deviation of the normal distribution. [V.E.2]"},"revision":"b581bde89d2c9b2af4d450454a82c42976fe90c448c75ae9e6ff836f8237f284"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-361":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Hypergeometric\",\"Poisson\",\"Normal\",\"Binomial\"],\"stem\":\"A sample is drawn without replacement from a finite population whose units are classified as conforming or nonconforming. Which distribution models the number of nonconforming units selected?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The hypergeometric distribution models the count of successes when sampling without replacement from a finite population. [V.E.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Hypergeometric","Poisson","Normal","Binomial"],"stem":"A sample is drawn without replacement from a finite population whose units are classified as conforming or nonconforming. Which distribution models the number of nonconforming units selected?","sub":"mea","why":"The hypergeometric distribution models the count of successes when sampling without replacement from a finite population. [V.E.3]"},"revision":"9aaff00eb218f8ef388b78f51c170dc6c3a8e3a13ef44b1aefcd8a10925bf7d2"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-362":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"2\",\"4\",\"5\",\"6\"],\"stem\":\"A bivariate normal distribution has how many parameters?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"A bivariate normal distribution has five parameters: two means (μ₁ and μ₂), two standard deviations (σ₁ and σ₂), and one correlation coefficient (ρ). [V.E.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["2","4","5","6"],"stem":"A bivariate normal distribution has how many parameters?","sub":"mea","why":"A bivariate normal distribution has five parameters: two means (μ₁ and μ₂), two standard deviations (σ₁ and σ₂), and one correlation coefficient (ρ). [V.E.3]"},"revision":"415555480ba2c4522933077cfcf19f972a34655d9b7ed2bf60862d8cdc00a71c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-363":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Exponential\",\"Normal\",\"Lognormal\",\"Chi-square\"],\"stem\":\"Which distribution is useful when modeling multiplicative data?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The lognormal is used to model multiplicative data. [V.E.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Exponential","Normal","Lognormal","Chi-square"],"stem":"Which distribution is useful when modeling multiplicative data?","sub":"mea","why":"The lognormal is used to model multiplicative data. [V.E.3]"},"revision":"94d2910c951d3cfe2771d2f12fcc72ee9cbc66aca7b44f3604a3d9131f5ba177"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-364":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Hypergeometric\",\"Binomial\",\"Poisson\",\"Normal\"],\"stem\":\"An inspection procedure calls for sampling 5 items from a lot of 25. If no defective items are found, then the lot is accepted and shipped. What distribution would best model the probability of acceptance for a lot?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The hypergeometric would be used to find Pr(X = 0) in this case because we are sampling from a finite population and classifying items as pass or fail. [V.E.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Hypergeometric","Binomial","Poisson","Normal"],"stem":"An inspection procedure calls for sampling 5 items from a lot of 25. If no defective items are found, then the lot is accepted and shipped. What distribution would best model the probability of acceptance for a lot?","sub":"mea","why":"The hypergeometric would be used to find Pr(X = 0) in this case because we are sampling from a finite population and classifying items as pass or fail. [V.E.3]"},"revision":"c5c1aaecd832b22ce9481e8cd4de2fec7d8f13ad989be4e7aa61e66b66d6f79d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-365":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Reliability data\",\"Messy data\",\"Correlated data\",\"Defect counts\"],\"stem\":\"The Weibull distribution is useful when modeling what type of data?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The Weibull distribution is useful when modeling reliability data. The bathtub curve can be modeled using three Weibull distributions with varying parameters. [V.E.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Reliability data","Messy data","Correlated data","Defect counts"],"stem":"The Weibull distribution is useful when modeling what type of data?","sub":"mea","why":"The Weibull distribution is useful when modeling reliability data. The bathtub curve can be modeled using three Weibull distributions with varying parameters. [V.E.3]"},"revision":"22d19d85e495f0d8c7ebf00afc668fc042487a74498d44f4d46238d50d4c8211"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-366":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Poisson\",\"Binomial\",\"Uniform\",\"Hypergeometric\"],\"stem\":\"As parts are made, they are placed in trays that hold 30 parts. If a tray contains 6 defective parts and 2 parts are sampled from the tray, what distribution would be used to calculate the probability that both will be defective parts?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The hypergeometric distribution is used when sampling from a finite population in which sample units are characterized as pass or fail. In this case, the hypergeometric parameters are N = 30, D = 6, and n = 2. [V.E.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Poisson","Binomial","Uniform","Hypergeometric"],"stem":"As parts are made, they are placed in trays that hold 30 parts. If a tray contains 6 defective parts and 2 parts are sampled from the tray, what distribution would be used to calculate the probability that both will be defective parts?","sub":"mea","why":"The hypergeometric distribution is used when sampling from a finite population in which sample units are characterized as pass or fail. In this case, the hypergeometric parameters are N = 30, D = 6, and n = 2. [V.E.3]"},"revision":"ef98857a884b14df07edbe7430f25f771b886fc1730e2a93f3f89f237486cd88"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-367":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"The probability that X is less than s is the same as the probability that X is greater than s.\",\"The probability that a unit will function for at least 100 more hours is the same whether it has already run for 1,000 or 2,000 hours.\",\"The probability that a unit will function for 1,000 hours is the same as the probability it will function for 2,000 hours.\",\"The probability that a unit will fail at time t equals 0.632.\"],\"stem\":\"Which statement is true concerning the memoryless property of the exponential distribution?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The exponential distribution is memoryless: the probability of surviving at least another s units does not depend on the elapsed lifetime t. Thus, P(X > t + s | X > t) = P(X > s). [V.E.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["The probability that X is less than s is the same as the probability that X is greater than s.","The probability that a unit will function for at least 100 more hours is the same whether it has already run for 1,000 or 2,000 hours.","The probability that a unit will function for 1,000 hours is the same as the probability it will function for 2,000 hours.","The probability that a unit will fail at time t equals 0.632."],"stem":"Which statement is true concerning the memoryless property of the exponential distribution?","sub":"mea","why":"The exponential distribution is memoryless: the probability of surviving at least another s units does not depend on the elapsed lifetime t. Thus, P(X > t + s | X > t) = P(X > s). [V.E.3]"},"revision":"5ef4497158ec587035dcea7d7de78fe553c512a20f2e4bcdb5a0e216a0b4662b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-368":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"They are equivalent if the shape parameter equals 1.\",\"They are equivalent if the scale parameter equals 1.\",\"Both have support from −∞ to ∞.\",\"The probability that X is less than the mean is always 0.632.\"],\"stem\":\"What is the connection between the Weibull and exponential distributions?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The Weibull distribution reduces to the exponential distribution when its shape parameter equals 1. [V.E.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["They are equivalent if the shape parameter equals 1.","They are equivalent if the scale parameter equals 1.","Both have support from −∞ to ∞.","The probability that X is less than the mean is always 0.632."],"stem":"What is the connection between the Weibull and exponential distributions?","sub":"mea","why":"The Weibull distribution reduces to the exponential distribution when its shape parameter equals 1. [V.E.3]"},"revision":"f09877d2bb0cc82121f77dbf56bc8777cf540d059765cbb75e463bf34e2fe93c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-369":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Decrease the variability of the process.\",\"Move the process mean to the target.\",\"Increase the width of the specification limits.\",\"Perform a Ppk analysis.\"],\"stem\":\"A normally distributed, in-control process output has a Cp = 1.75 and a Cpk = 1.0. What action should the team perform first to improve the process?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The process potential, or Cp value, shows that the process is capable. The Cp value is comparable to a 5.25 process sigma. The Cpk value is not equal to the Cp value, indicating that the process is not centered at target. The first step the team should take is to move the mean to target, thus making Cpk = Cp = 1.75. After that, the team can work on reducing variation to further improve the process. [V.F.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Decrease the variability of the process.","Move the process mean to the target.","Increase the width of the specification limits.","Perform a Ppk analysis."],"stem":"A normally distributed, in-control process output has a Cp = 1.75 and a Cpk = 1.0. What action should the team perform first to improve the process?","sub":"mea","why":"The process potential, or Cp value, shows that the process is capable. The Cp value is comparable to a 5.25 process sigma. The Cpk value is not equal to the Cp value, indicating that the process is not centered at target. The first step the team should take is to move the mean to target, thus making Cpk = Cp = 1.75. After that, the team can work on reducing variation to further improve the process. [V.F.1]"},"revision":"6de63a072a15f4d2c182c2a3b7588ad5f25257e7fbf625bc87448185258e5b3b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-37":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Monetary rewards\",\"Changes in project scope\",\"Too many resources\",\"Public expressions of appreciation\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following factors is considered a demotivating factor for team members?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"Changes in project scope can become a demotivating factor for a team. [III.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Monetary rewards","Changes in project scope","Too many resources","Public expressions of appreciation"],"stem":"Which of the following factors is considered a demotivating factor for team members?","sub":"tm","why":"Changes in project scope can become a demotivating factor for a team. [III.B.1]"},"revision":"394c168086cbff83327e0490d020fe2cb47296b1b2840b8a86867d297253242c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-370":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"6σ equals USL − LSL\",\"3σ equals USL − LSL\",\"6σ is less than USL − LSL\",\"6σ is greater than USL − LSL\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following cases would be considered the most desirable in terms of the relationship of the specification to the process spread?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Cp = (USL − LSL)/(6σ) is a larger-is-better index. A process spread 6σ that is smaller than the specification width provides the most desirable listed relationship. [V.F.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["6σ equals USL − LSL","3σ equals USL − LSL","6σ is less than USL − LSL","6σ is greater than USL − LSL"],"stem":"Which of the following cases would be considered the most desirable in terms of the relationship of the specification to the process spread?","sub":"mea","why":"Cp = (USL − LSL)/(6σ) is a larger-is-better index. A process spread 6σ that is smaller than the specification width provides the most desirable listed relationship. [V.F.1]"},"revision":"939a2cf8b0d510589dddd080293d8622208727f786ccbd651a06fe71328fd771"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-371":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"1.00\",\"1.50\",\"0.75\",\"0.30\"],\"stem\":\"Of the following Cp values, which index is the most desirable?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The Cp value is a larger-the-better metric. The most desirable Cp index from those listed is 1.50, which is corresponding to a short-term process sigma level of 4.5. [V.F.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["1.00","1.50","0.75","0.30"],"stem":"Of the following Cp values, which index is the most desirable?","sub":"mea","why":"The Cp value is a larger-the-better metric. The most desirable Cp index from those listed is 1.50, which is corresponding to a short-term process sigma level of 4.5. [V.F.1]"},"revision":"cd0db9207e474091667c3686c2d07d645652c8837257abd4a21a5772a82f0fdf"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-372":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"6σ is less than USL − LSL\",\"6σ equals USL − LSL\",\"6σ is greater than USL − LSL\",\"3σ equals USL − LSL\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following cases would result in a Cp index equal to 1?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Cp = (USL − LSL)/(6σ). Therefore, Cp = 1 when the process spread 6σ equals the specification width USL − LSL. [V.F.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["6σ is less than USL − LSL","6σ equals USL − LSL","6σ is greater than USL − LSL","3σ equals USL − LSL"],"stem":"Which of the following cases would result in a Cp index equal to 1?","sub":"mea","why":"Cp = (USL − LSL)/(6σ). Therefore, Cp = 1 when the process spread 6σ equals the specification width USL − LSL. [V.F.1]"},"revision":"a3a1d2fc9b9caccf3009f5a77bb4c13d35dfc54dd7bb05e77dbd170468b22d7e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-373":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"the voice of the customer.\",\"the voice of the process.\",\"the process precision.\",\"the process variability.\"],\"stem\":\"The numerator of the Cp formula is also referred to as:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The numerator of Cp, USL − LSL, is called the voice of the customer. The denominator, 6σ, is called the voice of the process. [V.F.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["the voice of the customer.","the voice of the process.","the process precision.","the process variability."],"stem":"The numerator of the Cp formula is also referred to as:","sub":"mea","why":"The numerator of Cp, USL − LSL, is called the voice of the customer. The denominator, 6σ, is called the voice of the process. [V.F.1]"},"revision":"60851103e319c5e741bb8be59f68d1dd4af113841fcc1e6db21a3db0ea105453"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-374":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"An error was made in the calculation.\",\"The process mean is outside the specification limits.\",\"The Cp value is less than 1.\",\"The process variation is too large.\"],\"stem\":\"What does a negative Cpk signify?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"A negative Cpk means the process mean lies outside a specification limit. Cpk = min[(USL − μ)/(3σ), (μ − LSL)/(3σ)], so one branch is negative when μ is above USL or below LSL. [V.F.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["An error was made in the calculation.","The process mean is outside the specification limits.","The Cp value is less than 1.","The process variation is too large."],"stem":"What does a negative Cpk signify?","sub":"mea","why":"A negative Cpk means the process mean lies outside a specification limit. Cpk = min[(USL − μ)/(3σ), (μ − LSL)/(3σ)], so one branch is negative when μ is above USL or below LSL. [V.F.1]"},"revision":"eb2008528541b8cf9dab22ae217a3d1377485955712a1ac0d7fd2e7bc8f95599"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-375":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"X̿\",\"904.5\"],[\"S̄\",\"9.9\"],[\"Subgroup size, n\",\"20\"],[\"Specification\",\"900 ± 50\"],[\"Bias-correction factor, c₄\",\"0.9869\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"1.55\",\"1.66\",\"1.68\",\"1.75\"],\"stem\":\"Using the process-data table and bias-correction factor shown below, what is the value of Cp?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Estimate σ as σ̂ = S̄/c₄ = 9.9/0.9869 = 10.03. Then Cp = (USL − LSL)/(6σ̂) = (950 − 850)/[6(10.03)] = 1.66. [V.F.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["X̿","904.5"],["S̄","9.9"],["Subgroup size, n","20"],["Specification","900 ± 50"],["Bias-correction factor, c₄","0.9869"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["1.55","1.66","1.68","1.75"],"stem":"Using the process-data table and bias-correction factor shown below, what is the value of Cp?","sub":"mea","why":"Estimate σ as σ̂ = S̄/c₄ = 9.9/0.9869 = 10.03. Then Cp = (USL − LSL)/(6σ̂) = (950 − 850)/[6(10.03)] = 1.66. [V.F.1]"},"revision":"a541b841c56a59a6ce29f925e9f665aea3167044a5a307581cb7d94206ce0c96"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-376":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"X̿\",\"904.5\"],[\"S̄\",\"9.9\"],[\"Subgroup size, n\",\"20\"],[\"Specification\",\"900 ± 50\"],[\"Bias-correction factor, c₄\",\"0.9869\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"10.03\",\"59.40\",\"60.19\",\"100.0\"],\"stem\":\"Using the process-data table shown below, what is the estimated natural tolerance width?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Estimate σ as σ̂ = S̄/c₄ = 9.9/0.9869 = 10.03. The natural tolerance width is 6σ̂ = 6(10.03) = 60.19. [V.F.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["X̿","904.5"],["S̄","9.9"],["Subgroup size, n","20"],["Specification","900 ± 50"],["Bias-correction factor, c₄","0.9869"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["10.03","59.40","60.19","100.0"],"stem":"Using the process-data table shown below, what is the estimated natural tolerance width?","sub":"mea","why":"Estimate σ as σ̂ = S̄/c₄ = 9.9/0.9869 = 10.03. The natural tolerance width is 6σ̂ = 6(10.03) = 60.19. [V.F.1]"},"revision":"1640e265f5d1fd364cb82bb77267c0a30f21e90ddfaee8ccdfd6cabb5421550b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-377":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"X̿\",\"23.5\"],[\"R̄\",\"1.5\"],[\"Subgroup size, n\",\"5\"],[\"LSL\",\"22.5\"],[\"Target\",\"24.0\"],[\"USL\",\"25.5\"],[\"d₂ for n = 5\",\"2.326\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"0.22\",\"0.52\",\"0.77\",\"1.03\"],\"stem\":\"A normally distributed process is stable on an X̄-R chart with X̿ = 23.5, R̄ = 1.5, and n = 5. The specifications are 24 ± 1.5. Using the constants shown below, what is the estimated Cpk?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Estimate σ as σ̂ = R̄/d₂ = 1.5/2.326 = 0.645. Cpk = min[(25.5 − 23.5)/(3×0.645), (23.5 − 22.5)/(3×0.645)] = min(1.03, 0.52) = 0.52. [V.F.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["X̿","23.5"],["R̄","1.5"],["Subgroup size, n","5"],["LSL","22.5"],["Target","24.0"],["USL","25.5"],["d₂ for n = 5","2.326"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["0.22","0.52","0.77","1.03"],"stem":"A normally distributed process is stable on an X̄-R chart with X̿ = 23.5, R̄ = 1.5, and n = 5. The specifications are 24 ± 1.5. Using the constants shown below, what is the estimated Cpk?","sub":"mea","why":"Estimate σ as σ̂ = R̄/d₂ = 1.5/2.326 = 0.645. Cpk = min[(25.5 − 23.5)/(3×0.645), (23.5 − 22.5)/(3×0.645)] = min(1.03, 0.52) = 0.52. [V.F.1]"},"revision":"1c578e8e8ecae146c6b00fbf32cf929017be3f385b7ab0fafa5546a140494df1"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-378":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"X̿\",\"23.5\"],[\"R̄\",\"1.5\"],[\"Subgroup size, n\",\"5\"],[\"LSL\",\"22.5\"],[\"Target\",\"24.0\"],[\"USL\",\"25.5\"],[\"d₂ for n = 5\",\"2.326\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"0.342\",\"0.572\",\"0.613\",\"3.78\"],\"stem\":\"Using the process data and d₂ constant shown in the table below, what is the estimated Cpm?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Estimate σ as σ̂ = R̄/d₂ = 1.5/2.326 = 0.645. Then Cpm = (USL − LSL)/[6√(σ̂² + (X̿ − T)²)] = 3.0/[6√(0.645² + (23.5 − 24.0)²)] = 0.613. [V.F.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["X̿","23.5"],["R̄","1.5"],["Subgroup size, n","5"],["LSL","22.5"],["Target","24.0"],["USL","25.5"],["d₂ for n = 5","2.326"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["0.342","0.572","0.613","3.78"],"stem":"Using the process data and d₂ constant shown in the table below, what is the estimated Cpm?","sub":"mea","why":"Estimate σ as σ̂ = R̄/d₂ = 1.5/2.326 = 0.645. Then Cpm = (USL − LSL)/[6√(σ̂² + (X̿ − T)²)] = 3.0/[6√(0.645² + (23.5 − 24.0)²)] = 0.613. [V.F.2]"},"revision":"3141e002d326eb5c96e41894d30e278bc49a8c16c9812f5ab95f2b65a99e8bd7"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-379":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"The process is capable and centered within the specification limits.\",\"The process is centered within the specification limits but is not capable.\",\"The process is not centered and not capable.\",\"The process is narrow enough to be capable but is not centered.\"],\"stem\":\"Using an organizational minimum capability criterion of Cp ≥ 1.33 and Cpk ≥ 1.33, which statement is true if Cpk = 0.85 and Cp = 1.10?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Both indices are below the stated 1.33 criterion, so the process is not capable. Because Cpk is less than Cp, the process is also off center. [V.F.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["The process is capable and centered within the specification limits.","The process is centered within the specification limits but is not capable.","The process is not centered and not capable.","The process is narrow enough to be capable but is not centered."],"stem":"Using an organizational minimum capability criterion of Cp ≥ 1.33 and Cpk ≥ 1.33, which statement is true if Cpk = 0.85 and Cp = 1.10?","sub":"mea","why":"Both indices are below the stated 1.33 criterion, so the process is not capable. Because Cpk is less than Cp, the process is also off center. [V.F.1]"},"revision":"403359477370bc99fd2ebbabf37c0edfadd3714b09d2f770c99b1229039b1cb7"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-38":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Absence of trust\",\"Fear of conflict\",\"Avoidance of accountability\",\"Lack of commitment\"],\"stem\":\"Lencioni describes five dysfunctions of a team. Which dysfunction is considered the primary inhibitor of a successful team?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"According to Lencioni, absence of trust is the primary dysfunction and a fundamental inhibitor of team success. Trust is the bedrock of a well-functioning team: members must feel comfortable sharing opinions and making mistakes. Without trust, the other dysfunctions cannot be overcome. [III.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Absence of trust","Fear of conflict","Avoidance of accountability","Lack of commitment"],"stem":"Lencioni describes five dysfunctions of a team. Which dysfunction is considered the primary inhibitor of a successful team?","sub":"tm","why":"According to Lencioni, absence of trust is the primary dysfunction and a fundamental inhibitor of team success. Trust is the bedrock of a well-functioning team: members must feel comfortable sharing opinions and making mistakes. Without trust, the other dysfunctions cannot be overcome. [III.B.1]"},"revision":"ce6373bb8cefb73a0752994ab1be122d5a985558ebb01cb1f73ecf61276e639c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-380":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"short-term capability.\",\"long-term capability.\",\"the 1.5 sigma shift.\",\"the process sigma.\"],\"stem\":\"The Cp, Cpk, and Cpm indices are used to characterize:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Cp, Cpk, and Cpm characterize short-term capability. Pp and Ppk characterize long-term process performance. [V.F.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["short-term capability.","long-term capability.","the 1.5 sigma shift.","the process sigma."],"stem":"The Cp, Cpk, and Cpm indices are used to characterize:","sub":"mea","why":"Cp, Cpk, and Cpm characterize short-term capability. Pp and Ppk characterize long-term process performance. [V.F.1]"},"revision":"66cd203ebdefc9173448de7f88320be846cbed9b90b683d7653f0c7c8c31d926"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-381":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"The location of the process mean relative to the specifications.\",\"The variability of the process.\",\"The distance between the process mean and the process target.\",\"The distance between the process variability and the specifications.\"],\"stem\":\"What information does the Cpm index incorporate that Cpk does not?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Cpm includes the distance between the process mean and the process target in its denominator. This distance is the process bias: Cpm = (USL − LSL)/[6√(σ² + (μ − T)²)]. [V.F.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["The location of the process mean relative to the specifications.","The variability of the process.","The distance between the process mean and the process target.","The distance between the process variability and the specifications."],"stem":"What information does the Cpm index incorporate that Cpk does not?","sub":"mea","why":"Cpm includes the distance between the process mean and the process target in its denominator. This distance is the process bias: Cpm = (USL − LSL)/[6√(σ² + (μ − T)²)]. [V.F.2]"},"revision":"12580f2a028423bb6606dc9f06f47c807c0f5d3bd40b8a16228b3731347e2e6d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-382":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.44\",\"0.75\",\"1.33\",\"1.50\"],\"stem\":\"A process is analyzed over five months: the average is 45.67 and the standard deviation is 0.75. Specifications are 44.0 to 50.0. What is the value of Pp?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Pp = (USL − LSL)/(6s) = (50 − 44)/(6 × 0.75) = 6/4.5 = 1.33. [V.F.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.44","0.75","1.33","1.50"],"stem":"A process is analyzed over five months: the average is 45.67 and the standard deviation is 0.75. Specifications are 44.0 to 50.0. What is the value of Pp?","sub":"mea","why":"Pp = (USL − LSL)/(6s) = (50 − 44)/(6 × 0.75) = 6/4.5 = 1.33. [V.F.2]"},"revision":"ca903804d4d90d15a58d81690ce17b92a2380df7f98df586609e0e3c207af781"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-383":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.37\",\"0.74\",\"1.35\",\"2.69\"],\"stem\":\"A process has an average of 45.67, a standard deviation of 0.75, and specifications from 44.0 to 50.0. What is the value of Ppk?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Ppk = min[(USL − x̄)/(3s), (x̄ − LSL)/(3s)] = min[(50 − 45.67)/2.25, (45.67 − 44)/2.25] = min[1.92, 0.74] = 0.74. [V.F.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.37","0.74","1.35","2.69"],"stem":"A process has an average of 45.67, a standard deviation of 0.75, and specifications from 44.0 to 50.0. What is the value of Ppk?","sub":"mea","why":"Ppk = min[(USL − x̄)/(3s), (x̄ − LSL)/(3s)] = min[(50 − 45.67)/2.25, (45.67 − 44)/2.25] = min[1.92, 0.74] = 0.74. [V.F.2]"},"revision":"81c390a05630130bd83b6b120c3c895492bf12254a6ec7d2657fcd930a00fbeb"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-384":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"1.50\",\"2.25\",\"3.00\",\"3.75\"],\"stem\":\"A process has Cp = 1.25. What is its corresponding short-term process sigma level?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The short-term process sigma level is 3Cp. Here, 3(1.25) = 3.75. [V.F.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["1.50","2.25","3.00","3.75"],"stem":"A process has Cp = 1.25. What is its corresponding short-term process sigma level?","sub":"mea","why":"The short-term process sigma level is 3Cp. Here, 3(1.25) = 3.75. [V.F.2]"},"revision":"08955aa0788b454de49c2aa3a8bd022b51417feee6a6805b3198b215386188eb"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-385":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.00542\",\"0.00271\",\"0.01626\",\"0.04100\"],\"stem\":\"A process metric has specifications 0.20 ± 0.01. If Pp = 1.23, what is the process standard deviation?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Pp = (USL − LSL)/(6s). Therefore, 1.23 = (0.21 − 0.19)/(6s), so s = 0.02/[6(1.23)] = 0.00271. [V.F.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.00542","0.00271","0.01626","0.04100"],"stem":"A process metric has specifications 0.20 ± 0.01. If Pp = 1.23, what is the process standard deviation?","sub":"mea","why":"Pp = (USL − LSL)/(6s). Therefore, 1.23 = (0.21 − 0.19)/(6s), so s = 0.02/[6(1.23)] = 0.00271. [V.F.2]"},"revision":"20854896a5129d26f7f03b2486f40dc3f37bf3f18eb085c9dd5f4360c14c7ddb"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-386":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.97\",\"1.03\",\"1.07\",\"1.67\"],\"stem\":\"A capability study used n = 28 subgroups and produced Cpk = 1.35. Use z(0.05) = 1.645 and Cpk,L = Cpk − z(0.05)√[1/(9n) + Cpk²/(2n − 2)]. What is the 95% lower confidence bound on Cpk?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Cpk,L = 1.35 − 1.645√[1/(9×28) + 1.35²/(2×28 − 2)] = 1.0305, which rounds to 1.03. [V.F.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.97","1.03","1.07","1.67"],"stem":"A capability study used n = 28 subgroups and produced Cpk = 1.35. Use z(0.05) = 1.645 and Cpk,L = Cpk − z(0.05)√[1/(9n) + Cpk²/(2n − 2)]. What is the 95% lower confidence bound on Cpk?","sub":"mea","why":"Cpk,L = 1.35 − 1.645√[1/(9×28) + 1.35²/(2×28 − 2)] = 1.0305, which rounds to 1.03. [V.F.3]"},"revision":"8424c5068396b3587a69b69f2ed94d41198302659b6814484500a39db7f9dfb7"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-387":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"change the specifications to improve the capability.\",\"check the normality of the data.\",\"choose only the metrics that are performing best.\",\"use as few subgroups as possible.\"],\"stem\":\"One step in conducting a capability study is to:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"When conducting a process capability study, make sure the process is stable, use 25–30 subgroups for your dataset, and then check the normality of the data. If the data is not normal, a transformation can be used before calculating capability. [V.F.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["change the specifications to improve the capability.","check the normality of the data.","choose only the metrics that are performing best.","use as few subgroups as possible."],"stem":"One step in conducting a capability study is to:","sub":"mea","why":"When conducting a process capability study, make sure the process is stable, use 25–30 subgroups for your dataset, and then check the normality of the data. If the data is not normal, a transformation can be used before calculating capability. [V.F.3]"},"revision":"784d5ba273472cc78ed6aaf78b01ae44f5e389b4ea3cb5828d50aa21cb8b274b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-388":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.012\",\"0.020\",\"0.028\",\"0.040\"],\"stem\":\"Given the following information from an in-control p-chart, calculate the process capability: Σnp = 55 and Σn = 2,800.\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The process capability from a p-chart is reported as its centerline: p̄ = Σnp/Σn = 55/2,800 = 0.0196, which rounds to 0.020. [V.F.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.012","0.020","0.028","0.040"],"stem":"Given the following information from an in-control p-chart, calculate the process capability: Σnp = 55 and Σn = 2,800.","sub":"mea","why":"The process capability from a p-chart is reported as its centerline: p̄ = Σnp/Σn = 55/2,800 = 0.0196, which rounds to 0.020. [V.F.4]"},"revision":"b26a0828bd5312f19aba2955877d549ea11ded8e9c521ae98cefb21c43169c3c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-389":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"It is calculated using the same method as from a variables control chart.\",\"It is equal to the width of specification limits divided by the width of the control limits.\",\"It should be greater than 1.33.\",\"It is equal to the control chart average.\"],\"stem\":\"What statement is true about process capability calculated from an attributes control chart?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"For attribute control charts, process capability is represented by the chart’s average or centerline—for example, p̄, np̄, ū, or c̄. [V.F.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["It is calculated using the same method as from a variables control chart.","It is equal to the width of specification limits divided by the width of the control limits.","It should be greater than 1.33.","It is equal to the control chart average."],"stem":"What statement is true about process capability calculated from an attributes control chart?","sub":"mea","why":"For attribute control charts, process capability is represented by the chart’s average or centerline—for example, p̄, np̄, ū, or c̄. [V.F.4]"},"revision":"13461ad6fcd3c621b9fdb40028db50f9ae7ce0fb3d58b468f75bbfc3634ed4e9"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-39":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Inattention to team results and lack of success\",\"Fear of conflict and absence of trust\",\"Avoidance of accountability and absence of trust\",\"Lack of success and fear of conflict\"],\"stem\":\"According to Lencioni, before lack of commitment can be addressed, which dysfunctions must be overcome?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"According to Lencioni, fear of conflict and absence of trust must be addressed before lack of commitment can be overcome. [III.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Inattention to team results and lack of success","Fear of conflict and absence of trust","Avoidance of accountability and absence of trust","Lack of success and fear of conflict"],"stem":"According to Lencioni, before lack of commitment can be addressed, which dysfunctions must be overcome?","sub":"tm","why":"According to Lencioni, fear of conflict and absence of trust must be addressed before lack of commitment can be overcome. [III.B.1]"},"revision":"59530c6a815cc2b61da875bf333bf3254220c65ac637ffd596d2a9ca4b583e24"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-390":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"0.03\",\"0.06\",\"3.0\",\"6.0\"],\"stem\":\"Given the following information from an in-control np chart, calculate the process capability. Σ(np) = 120, n = 200, and the number of subgroups is 20.\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"For an np chart, process capability is reported as the centerline: n̄p = Σ(np)/k = 120/20 = 6.0 defectives per subgroup. [V.F.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["0.03","0.06","3.0","6.0"],"stem":"Given the following information from an in-control np chart, calculate the process capability. Σ(np) = 120, n = 200, and the number of subgroups is 20.","sub":"mea","why":"For an np chart, process capability is reported as the centerline: n̄p = Σ(np)/k = 120/20 = 6.0 defectives per subgroup. [V.F.4]"},"revision":"34b3d7c7b891807022eee777816e426fc6e8d77fbd82c8127fafd55ed5869966"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-391":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Process\",\"Capability\"],\"rows\":[[\"A\",\"2.31\"],[\"B\",\"0.67\"],[\"C\",\"1.67\"],[\"D\",\"1.33\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"Process A\",\"Process B\",\"Process C\",\"Process D\"],\"stem\":\"Four stable processes are monitored using u-charts, with the capabilities shown below. Which process has the most desirable capability?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The process capability for a u-chart is expressed as the average number of defects per unit. Therefore, Process B has the most desirable capability. [V.F.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"chart":{"columns":["Process","Capability"],"rows":[["A","2.31"],["B","0.67"],["C","1.67"],["D","1.33"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["Process A","Process B","Process C","Process D"],"stem":"Four stable processes are monitored using u-charts, with the capabilities shown below. Which process has the most desirable capability?","sub":"mea","why":"The process capability for a u-chart is expressed as the average number of defects per unit. Therefore, Process B has the most desirable capability. [V.F.4]"},"revision":"6a11a35f411f01fe561c36d2f38b3141ecfdfd248e55436d6ec2795d14e9e483"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-392":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.66\",\"1.52\",\"1.67\",\"2.52\"],\"stem\":\"A stable c-chart has 25 points plotted with the sum of defects equal to 38. What is the capability of the process?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"For a c-chart, the process capability is the average number of defects per sample: c̄ = total defects/number of samples = 38/25 = 1.52. [V.F.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.66","1.52","1.67","2.52"],"stem":"A stable c-chart has 25 points plotted with the sum of defects equal to 38. What is the capability of the process?","sub":"mea","why":"For a c-chart, the process capability is the average number of defects per sample: c̄ = total defects/number of samples = 38/25 = 1.52. [V.F.4]"},"revision":"8a452f218dab8900cdcf44807646cc408122b7793d84cb1e719fc6c2effbeca4"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-393":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Y = √X\",\"Y = ln(X)\",\"Y = X²\",\"Y = 1/X\"],\"stem\":\"What Box-Cox transformation is used to transform data from a Poisson to a normal distribution?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The square-root transformation is used to stabilize Poisson count data: Y = √X. [V.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Y = √X","Y = ln(X)","Y = X²","Y = 1/X"],"stem":"What Box-Cox transformation is used to transform data from a Poisson to a normal distribution?","sub":"mea","why":"The square-root transformation is used to stabilize Poisson count data: Y = √X. [V.F.5]"},"revision":"908b97a6b19ff0f30562994cc44b81427a12bf250a0e2e35204e50cd02145305"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-394":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Fisher information\",\"The central limit theorem\",\"Johnson transformation\",\"Gauss-Markov theorem\"],\"stem\":\"To calculate the process capability for nonnormal data, a Black Belt can first use which statistical tool to convert the data to the normal distribution?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The Johnson transformation is a technique to convert nonnormal data to a normal distribution. The process capability can then be calculated using the transformed data. [V.F.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Fisher information","The central limit theorem","Johnson transformation","Gauss-Markov theorem"],"stem":"To calculate the process capability for nonnormal data, a Black Belt can first use which statistical tool to convert the data to the normal distribution?","sub":"mea","why":"The Johnson transformation is a technique to convert nonnormal data to a normal distribution. The process capability can then be calculated using the transformed data. [V.F.5]"},"revision":"44cfc544cc66f8f096d542e226b93d50283ee5ca74d380c2705a9e9fe1b44924"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-395":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"the customers'' willingness to accept defects.\",\"the difference between the upper and lower specification limits.\",\"six times the specification width.\",\"six times the process standard deviation.\"],\"stem\":\"Tolerance is defined as:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The tolerance is the difference between the upper and lower specification limits. [V.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["the customers'' willingness to accept defects.","the difference between the upper and lower specification limits.","six times the specification width.","six times the process standard deviation."],"stem":"Tolerance is defined as:","sub":"mea","why":"The tolerance is the difference between the upper and lower specification limits. [V.F.6]"},"revision":"d0ea4e68ceeb8216377850faedeb76d4a226ea59d9ad93ca37fda6c5481861b1"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-396":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.0435%\",\"4.35%\",\"15%\",\"23%\"],\"stem\":\"A total of 345 units were inspected and 15 were found to be defective. What is the percentage defective?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Percentage defective = (defective units/total units) × 100% = (15/345) × 100% = 4.35%. [V.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.0435%","4.35%","15%","23%"],"stem":"A total of 345 units were inspected and 15 were found to be defective. What is the percentage defective?","sub":"mea","why":"Percentage defective = (defective units/total units) × 100% = (15/345) × 100% = 4.35%. [V.F.6]"},"revision":"ae95f71434eca7d2364fac6bc79bb9d1b564bd4d4cb9a38e96e0839c0d4902b4"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-397":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"119\",\"453\",\"839\",\"8,387\"],\"stem\":\"A total of 54,012 units were produced, and 453 were found to be inoperable. What is the ppm defective?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"PPM defective = (defective units/total units) × 1,000,000 = (453/54,012) × 1,000,000 = 8,387 ppm. [V.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["119","453","839","8,387"],"stem":"A total of 54,012 units were produced, and 453 were found to be inoperable. What is the ppm defective?","sub":"mea","why":"PPM defective = (defective units/total units) × 1,000,000 = (453/54,012) × 1,000,000 = 8,387 ppm. [V.F.6]"},"revision":"1479503565751bd7c964c7432f35ca4f22feb73266c8e2822558a38b1a30e03d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-398":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Defect Type\",\"Tally\"],\"rows\":[[\"A\",\"12\"],[\"B\",\"29\"],[\"C\",\"10\"],[\"D\",\"4\"],[\"Total\",\"55\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"0.026\",\"0.105\",\"37.96\",\"55\"],\"stem\":\"Units are each inspected for four types of defects. A total of 522 units were inspected with the results shown below. Calculate the defects per unit.\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"To calculate defects per unit, we sum the defects and divide by the total number of units inspected: dpu = total defects / total units = 55 / 522 = 0.105. [V.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"chart":{"columns":["Defect Type","Tally"],"rows":[["A","12"],["B","29"],["C","10"],["D","4"],["Total","55"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["0.026","0.105","37.96","55"],"stem":"Units are each inspected for four types of defects. A total of 522 units were inspected with the results shown below. Calculate the defects per unit.","sub":"mea","why":"To calculate defects per unit, we sum the defects and divide by the total number of units inspected: dpu = total defects / total units = 55 / 522 = 0.105. [V.F.6]"},"revision":"8dae7af75a823d6e4945eba5e9b68d687c82c4165534e6fcfd87ba61a25c57ee"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-399":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"6.8\",\"5,667\",\"11,333\",\"56,665\"],\"stem\":\"There are five different defect types that can be found on any one unit: weight, length, thickness, diameter, and tensile strength. Six hundred units were inspected, and 34 defects were found. What is the DPMO?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"There are 600 units and 5 defect opportunities per unit, so DPMO = 34/(600 × 5) × 1,000,000 = 11,333. [V.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["6.8","5,667","11,333","56,665"],"stem":"There are five different defect types that can be found on any one unit: weight, length, thickness, diameter, and tensile strength. Six hundred units were inspected, and 34 defects were found. What is the DPMO?","sub":"mea","why":"There are 600 units and 5 defect opportunities per unit, so DPMO = 34/(600 × 5) × 1,000,000 = 11,333. [V.F.6]"},"revision":"6fee81c47fdd67562934ddc4e8284086587daa407d89a0ead0c5f3d6bf1fcbf0"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-4":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"a philosophy, a methodology, and a statistical standard of quality.\",\"a method that uses new tools to solve process problems.\",\"a project approach that uses six steps to reduce defects.\",\"a quality method that emphasizes reducing waste.\"],\"stem\":\"Six Sigma can be thought of as:\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"Six Sigma is a philosophy, an improvement methodology using DMAIC, and a statistical standard of quality. Under the conventional long-term model with a 1.5σ mean shift, Six Sigma corresponds to about 3.4 defects per million opportunities. Lean, by contrast, emphasizes reducing waste. [I.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["a philosophy, a methodology, and a statistical standard of quality.","a method that uses new tools to solve process problems.","a project approach that uses six steps to reduce defects.","a quality method that emphasizes reducing waste."],"stem":"Six Sigma can be thought of as:","sub":"p1","why":"Six Sigma is a philosophy, an improvement methodology using DMAIC, and a statistical standard of quality. Under the conventional long-term model with a 1.5σ mean shift, Six Sigma corresponds to about 3.4 defects per million opportunities. Lean, by contrast, emphasizes reducing waste. [I.A.1]"},"revision":"a4a4a42cc8c53adc276674afb3fb393d9db76b252bd8c683fc3df5913184d78f"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-40":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Forming\",\"Performing\",\"Storming\",\"Norming\"],\"stem\":\"Team members working on a process improvement project struggle to understand the team''s goal and its meaning for them individually. The team is likely in which stage of team development?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"Forming is the stage in which members struggle to understand the team''s goal and what it means for them individually. [III.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Forming","Performing","Storming","Norming"],"stem":"Team members working on a process improvement project struggle to understand the team''s goal and its meaning for them individually. The team is likely in which stage of team development?","sub":"tm","why":"Forming is the stage in which members struggle to understand the team''s goal and what it means for them individually. [III.B.2]"},"revision":"15cb56d8d6e000768ca8b0faf0c53bbb883300d3b276ea96de4b4bc5c97e2d56"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-400":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.04\",\"0.673\",\"0.961\",\"Cannot be calculated from the information given\"],\"stem\":\"Assume the number of defects on a unit follows a Poisson distribution with a mean of 0.04 defects per unit. What is the first-pass yield (FPY)?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Using a Poisson model with DPU = 0.04, first-pass yield is the probability of zero defects: FPY = P(X = 0) = exp(−DPU) = exp(−0.04) = 0.961. [V.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.04","0.673","0.961","Cannot be calculated from the information given"],"stem":"Assume the number of defects on a unit follows a Poisson distribution with a mean of 0.04 defects per unit. What is the first-pass yield (FPY)?","sub":"mea","why":"Using a Poisson model with DPU = 0.04, first-pass yield is the probability of zero defects: FPY = P(X = 0) = exp(−DPU) = exp(−0.04) = 0.961. [V.F.6]"},"revision":"1954176ef28342432572ce94761e13f6974db478b111425508830f6d26a158fd"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-401":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"0.9863\",\"0.9883\",\"0.9923\",\"0.998\"],\"stem\":\"Three thousand parts are produced. Thirty-five were sent out for rework and six were scrapped. What is the first-pass yield?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"First-pass yield excludes units requiring rework or scrap: FPY = (3,000 − 35 − 6)/3,000 = 0.9863. [V.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["0.9863","0.9883","0.9923","0.998"],"stem":"Three thousand parts are produced. Thirty-five were sent out for rework and six were scrapped. What is the first-pass yield?","sub":"mea","why":"First-pass yield excludes units requiring rework or scrap: FPY = (3,000 − 35 − 6)/3,000 = 0.9863. [V.F.6]"},"revision":"54b35477cbfe5120368ffc6d34dc4b9bc48820a4610b81220040e7b18e512b10"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-402":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"0.912\",\"0.95\",\"0.97\",\"0.992\"],\"stem\":\"A process requires three steps operated in series. The first-pass yield (FPY) values for the steps are 0.99, 0.95, and 0.97. What is the rolled throughput yield (RTY)?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"For steps in series, RTY is the product of the first-pass yields: RTY = 0.99 × 0.95 × 0.97 = 0.912. [V.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["0.912","0.95","0.97","0.992"],"stem":"A process requires three steps operated in series. The first-pass yield (FPY) values for the steps are 0.99, 0.95, and 0.97. What is the rolled throughput yield (RTY)?","sub":"mea","why":"For steps in series, RTY is the product of the first-pass yields: RTY = 0.99 × 0.95 × 0.97 = 0.912. [V.F.6]"},"revision":"f399181338695c62bb92fc5f950d3a5e9b8694d8ed4a02e648bb8ed70629d5ae"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-403":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"95.45%\",\"93.32%\",\"99.34%\",\"99.73%\"],\"stem\":\"A 3-sigma process output is normally distributed and centered at target. If the mean shifts upward by 1.5σ, what is the resulting yield? Use Φ(1.5) = 0.9332 and Φ(−4.5) ≈ 0.\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"After a +1.5σ shift, the specification limits are −4.5σ and +1.5σ relative to the shifted mean. Yield = Φ(1.5) − Φ(−4.5) ≈ 0.9332, or 93.32%. [V.F.7]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["95.45%","93.32%","99.34%","99.73%"],"stem":"A 3-sigma process output is normally distributed and centered at target. If the mean shifts upward by 1.5σ, what is the resulting yield? Use Φ(1.5) = 0.9332 and Φ(−4.5) ≈ 0.","sub":"mea","why":"After a +1.5σ shift, the specification limits are −4.5σ and +1.5σ relative to the shifted mean. Yield = Φ(1.5) − Φ(−4.5) ≈ 0.9332, or 93.32%. [V.F.7]"},"revision":"b52154da61db10dc84fef13175fc144f2877e6b94ba2f49fee9ed7f6ff9dc568"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-404":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"is a snapshot of the process taken under ideal conditions.\",\"captures both within and between subgroup variation.\",\"does not need the process to be in control.\",\"offers the most realistic view of the actual process performance.\"],\"stem\":\"Short-term capability:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Short-term capability is measured with Cp, Cpk, and Cpm. These indices provide a snapshot of process capability under short-term conditions. [V.F.7]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["is a snapshot of the process taken under ideal conditions.","captures both within and between subgroup variation.","does not need the process to be in control.","offers the most realistic view of the actual process performance."],"stem":"Short-term capability:","sub":"mea","why":"Short-term capability is measured with Cp, Cpk, and Cpm. These indices provide a snapshot of process capability under short-term conditions. [V.F.7]"},"revision":"3c8b5c0c24897d480dde9f6db9e53a9ff72c1edaa49d87ac400e7af31f858ea5"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-405":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"can be expressed using the Cpm index.\",\"uses only within-subgroup variation.\",\"captures both within and between subgroup variation.\",\"is often larger in magnitude than short-term measures.\"],\"stem\":\"Long-term process performance:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Long-term performance indices such as Pp and Ppk use overall variation and therefore capture both within- and between-subgroup variation. Process stability is still important when interpreting any capability or performance index. [V.F.7]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["can be expressed using the Cpm index.","uses only within-subgroup variation.","captures both within and between subgroup variation.","is often larger in magnitude than short-term measures."],"stem":"Long-term process performance:","sub":"mea","why":"Long-term performance indices such as Pp and Ppk use overall variation and therefore capture both within- and between-subgroup variation. Process stability is still important when interpreting any capability or performance index. [V.F.7]"},"revision":"5696dfbac68dda18795ec3e5b833f6b15f1aedfb49dfa8eb693bfa926389243a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-406":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"chart\":{\"type\":\"scatter-quadrant\"},\"options\":[\"Figure A\",\"Figure B\",\"Figure C\",\"Figure D\"],\"stem\":\"The four scatter plots below all show the relationship between the same two variables under different conditions. Which figure demonstrates negative correlation?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"In Figure B, y decreases as x increases—the points trend downward from left to right, indicating a negative correlation. [VI.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"chart":{"type":"scatter-quadrant"},"options":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D"],"stem":"The four scatter plots below all show the relationship between the same two variables under different conditions. Which figure demonstrates negative correlation?","sub":"ana","why":"In Figure B, y decreases as x increases—the points trend downward from left to right, indicating a negative correlation. [VI.A.1]"},"revision":"df6f0694b0e9eddf303c96a9faf54a413d3198362f3c1cc9db4b5d469ca93066"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-407":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"chart\":{\"type\":\"scatter-quadrant\"},\"options\":[\"Figure A\",\"Figure B\",\"Figure C\",\"Figure D\"],\"stem\":\"The four scatter plots below show different relationships between two variables. Which figure demonstrates a nonlinear relationship?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"The points plotted in Figure D rise and then fall, suggesting a parabolic (nonlinear) relationship rather than a straight-line trend. [VI.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"chart":{"type":"scatter-quadrant"},"options":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D"],"stem":"The four scatter plots below show different relationships between two variables. Which figure demonstrates a nonlinear relationship?","sub":"ana","why":"The points plotted in Figure D rise and then fall, suggesting a parabolic (nonlinear) relationship rather than a straight-line trend. [VI.A.1]"},"revision":"b2cc698c29efb66713d8f84d1b8e40e41d1f7ace1a75303a8418703e8f698683"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-408":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Observation\",\"X (Time)\",\"Y (Strength)\",\"XY\",\"X²\",\"Y²\"],\"rows\":[[\"1\",\"0.5\",\"37.0\",\"18.5\",\"0.25\",\"1,369.00\"],[\"2\",\"1.5\",\"36.0\",\"54.0\",\"2.25\",\"1,296.00\"],[\"3\",\"2.0\",\"42.0\",\"84.0\",\"4.00\",\"1,764.00\"],[\"4\",\"2.5\",\"39.0\",\"97.5\",\"6.25\",\"1,521.00\"],[\"5\",\"3.0\",\"46.0\",\"138.0\",\"9.00\",\"2,116.00\"],[\"6\",\"3.0\",\"43.0