nt":{"answer":1,"options":["The response heard from market promotions","An expression of customer wants","The communication method used for mass marketing","A measure of the capability of a process"],"stem":"What is the voice of the customer?","sub":"def","why":"The voice of the customer (VOC) is an expression of what the customer wants. [IV.A.1]"},"revision":"23c05b3f830e1dc50a5ac867df8a7fc06126b9c5b67addcd9b88fd9bef40abb8"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-440":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Affinity diagram\",\"Fishbone diagram\",\"Pareto chart\",\"Process decision program chart\"],\"stem\":\"Which tool is based on the principle of \\\"the vital few and the useful many\\\"?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"The Pareto chart is based on the principle of the vital few and useful many, also known as the 80/20 rule. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Affinity diagram","Fishbone diagram","Pareto chart","Process decision program chart"],"stem":"Which tool is based on the principle of \"the vital few and the useful many\"?","sub":"ana","why":"The Pareto chart is based on the principle of the vital few and useful many, also known as the 80/20 rule. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"802fe7eed8dc74c7a0b4841bab0de1c72ab2fccde7554253857353d2d5400451"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-441":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Eight wastes\",\"5S\",\"SMED\",\"5 Whys\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following tools can be used to drill down to a root cause?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"The 5 Whys tool can be used to drill down to a root cause. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Eight wastes","5S","SMED","5 Whys"],"stem":"Which of the following tools can be used to drill down to a root cause?","sub":"ana","why":"The 5 Whys tool can be used to drill down to a root cause. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"9d2c5d6954440f2c8b27310b68a061fbc4f9d0c0e130c5d8e796311f8b18e676"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-442":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Decision tree\",\"Process flowchart\",\"Cause-and-effect diagram\",\"Check sheet\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following tools is used to provide visual identification of many potential causes of a problem?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"A cause-and-effect, or fishbone, diagram is used to provide visual identification of many potential causes of a problem. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Decision tree","Process flowchart","Cause-and-effect diagram","Check sheet"],"stem":"Which of the following tools is used to provide visual identification of many potential causes of a problem?","sub":"ana","why":"A cause-and-effect, or fishbone, diagram is used to provide visual identification of many potential causes of a problem. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"2e7dd50a5aaba67b293011daffe1774b3acb7c701ed8f87bb32d348e37e14b5b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-443":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"type\":\"fta-diagram\"},\"options\":[\"2 and 6\",\"3 and 4\",\"5\",\"7, 9, and 10\"],\"stem\":\"Using the fault-tree diagram below, which numbered symbols are AND gates?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Symbols 2 and 6 denote AND gates. The output for an AND gate occurs only if all inputs occur. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"type":"fta-diagram"},"options":["2 and 6","3 and 4","5","7, 9, and 10"],"stem":"Using the fault-tree diagram below, which numbered symbols are AND gates?","sub":"ana","why":"Symbols 2 and 6 denote AND gates. The output for an AND gate occurs only if all inputs occur. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"f7c9a0748bb6f19f6c18594f8078f61d395496b27bb742d0c375bdf1dd6bf4c3"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-444":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"type\":\"fta-diagram\"},\"options\":[\"1\",\"3\",\"4\",\"3 and 4\"],\"stem\":\"Using the fault-tree diagram below, which numbered symbol is the primary or head event?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Symbol 1 represents the primary, or head, event. This is the failure mode being investigated. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"type":"fta-diagram"},"options":["1","3","4","3 and 4"],"stem":"Using the fault-tree diagram below, which numbered symbol is the primary or head event?","sub":"ana","why":"Symbol 1 represents the primary, or head, event. This is the failure mode being investigated. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"f2937bad8446ce9609dadb334306fdf3bcfb42f68bdca264ed46d93c88d40e0c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-445":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Leftmost column.\",\"Rightmost column.\",\"Depends on the number of other items.\",\"The \\\"other\\\" column should not be used.\"],\"stem\":\"When constructing a Pareto diagram, where is the \\\"other\\\" category placed?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"In a Pareto chart, the \\\"Other\\\" column should appear as the last column. [VI.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Leftmost column.","Rightmost column.","Depends on the number of other items.","The \"other\" column should not be used."],"stem":"When constructing a Pareto diagram, where is the \"other\" category placed?","sub":"ana","why":"In a Pareto chart, the \"Other\" column should appear as the last column. [VI.D.2]"},"revision":"b05c58c0ccccbf6cb2cb97defdeb2f321cab1656e590d2f813310f0f53bc0a69"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-446":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Transportation\",\"Waiting\",\"Defects\",\"Overproduction\"],\"stem\":\"Producing 55 parts when the customer order is for 50 is an example of which of the Eight Wastes?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Producing more than current customer demand requires is overproduction. Overproduction and overprocessing are distinct forms of waste. [VI.D.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Transportation","Waiting","Defects","Overproduction"],"stem":"Producing 55 parts when the customer order is for 50 is an example of which of the Eight Wastes?","sub":"ana","why":"Producing more than current customer demand requires is overproduction. Overproduction and overprocessing are distinct forms of waste. [VI.D.3]"},"revision":"f0ce8f8fbcf6ce2829b2086b244314f44668edfe73000621554253601621e2e9"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-447":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Inventory\",\"Processing\",\"Waiting\",\"Transportation\"],\"stem\":\"ABC Manufacturing operates a machine shop using total productive maintenance. It receives an order for 50 parts. Because past defects and a long production lead time could prevent the order from being completed on time, the shop releases an order for 55 parts so that at least 50 good parts will be available. By reducing the long production lead time, ABC is actively eliminating which type of waste?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Reducing the long production lead time targets waiting waste. Producing 55 parts for demand of 50 is a separate overproduction issue. [VI.D.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Inventory","Processing","Waiting","Transportation"],"stem":"ABC Manufacturing operates a machine shop using total productive maintenance. It receives an order for 50 parts. Because past defects and a long production lead time could prevent the order from being completed on time, the shop releases an order for 55 parts so that at least 50 good parts will be available. By reducing the long production lead time, ABC is actively eliminating which type of waste?","sub":"ana","why":"Reducing the long production lead time targets waiting waste. Producing 55 parts for demand of 50 is a separate overproduction issue. [VI.D.3]"},"revision":"bfb8825b6d481ae8fac6d593a98f8e0b0f30956d86c36433bade2eb652c952b6"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-448":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Overprocessing\",\"Defects\",\"Transportation\",\"Inventory\"],\"stem\":\"ABC Manufacturing receives an order for 50 parts but releases 55 parts because it expects some units will be scrapped or require rework before shipment. Which waste is the extra production intended to hedge against?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"The extra units are released specifically to cover anticipated scrap or rework, so the underlying waste being hedged against is defects. [VI.D.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Overprocessing","Defects","Transportation","Inventory"],"stem":"ABC Manufacturing receives an order for 50 parts but releases 55 parts because it expects some units will be scrapped or require rework before shipment. Which waste is the extra production intended to hedge against?","sub":"ana","why":"The extra units are released specifically to cover anticipated scrap or rework, so the underlying waste being hedged against is defects. [VI.D.3]"},"revision":"d20d960c64efc2cfa9faa30c289464958f8d089ba9fa42a8964bc3a88ca86878"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-449":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Time\",\"Defects\",\"Transportation\",\"Overprocessing\"],\"stem\":\"In an effort to detect a critical defect, quality inspectors are asked to perform a 100% inspection. This activity is an example of which type of waste?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"One hundred percent human inspection is an example of overprocessing waste. [VI.D.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Time","Defects","Transportation","Overprocessing"],"stem":"In an effort to detect a critical defect, quality inspectors are asked to perform a 100% inspection. This activity is an example of which type of waste?","sub":"ana","why":"One hundred percent human inspection is an example of overprocessing waste. [VI.D.3]"},"revision":"01d19d0c3921ec85f6bbcd760958a90ccec7c26aec3bf2c266dd2e63082eb9df"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-45":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"champions.\",\"facilitators.\",\"internal customers.\",\"external customers.\"],\"stem\":\"A hospital IT department is implementing a new electronic medical records system that will make it easier for nurses to complete their reports. The nurses can be thought of as the IT department''s:\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"The nurses are the IT department''s internal customers since both parties are in the same organization. [IV.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["champions.","facilitators.","internal customers.","external customers."],"stem":"A hospital IT department is implementing a new electronic medical records system that will make it easier for nurses to complete their reports. The nurses can be thought of as the IT department''s:","sub":"def","why":"The nurses are the IT department''s internal customers since both parties are in the same organization. [IV.A.1]"},"revision":"3af01a5268ff553feafd529d180feddbbd454f04c211c4fb42b113c51af0e9ed"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-450":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"chart\":{\"type\":\"interaction-plot-3\"},\"options\":[\"AB and BC\",\"AB and AC\",\"AC and BC\",\"All three pairs\"],\"stem\":\"A Black Belt conducted a three-factor, two-level full-factorial experiment. The interaction plots are shown below. The ANOVA table identified two significant two-factor interactions. Which factor pairs interact?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"The lines for AB and AC are not parallel, indicating interaction. The lines for BC are approximately parallel, indicating no material BC interaction. [VII.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"chart":{"type":"interaction-plot-3"},"options":["AB and BC","AB and AC","AC and BC","All three pairs"],"stem":"A Black Belt conducted a three-factor, two-level full-factorial experiment. The interaction plots are shown below. The ANOVA table identified two significant two-factor interactions. Which factor pairs interact?","sub":"imp","why":"The lines for AB and AC are not parallel, indicating interaction. The lines for BC are approximately parallel, indicating no material BC interaction. [VII.A.2]"},"revision":"de086fb6620032a5cde07e5c1d0b181eb0bd7d94c049c945680991e3c48e8396"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-451":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"chart\":{\"type\":\"interaction-plot-3\"},\"options\":[\"The response does not change.\",\"The response increases.\",\"The response decreases.\",\"The response cannot be determined using the graphs.\"],\"stem\":\"Using the interaction plots below, if Factor A is at its low level (−1), what happens to the mean response when Factor C changes from low (−1, solid circles) to high (+1, dashed squares)?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"In the A×C panel at A = −1, the solid-circle line for C = −1 is near 45 and the dashed-square line for C = +1 is near 24. The mean response therefore decreases. [VII.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"chart":{"type":"interaction-plot-3"},"options":["The response does not change.","The response increases.","The response decreases.","The response cannot be determined using the graphs."],"stem":"Using the interaction plots below, if Factor A is at its low level (−1), what happens to the mean response when Factor C changes from low (−1, solid circles) to high (+1, dashed squares)?","sub":"imp","why":"In the A×C panel at A = −1, the solid-circle line for C = −1 is near 45 and the dashed-square line for C = +1 is near 24. The mean response therefore decreases. [VII.A.2]"},"revision":"3669894780469bbdb4e6b5fc20ece0288dd4a438432abedcb737c294aca5163d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-452":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"main effects are confounded with other main effects.\",\"main effects are confounded with two-factor interactions.\",\"some two-factor interactions are confounded with other two-factor interactions.\",\"main effects are confounded with the mean.\"],\"stem\":\"In a resolution IV experiment:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"In a Resolution IV experiment, some two-factor interactions are confounded with other two-factor interactions. [VII.A.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["main effects are confounded with other main effects.","main effects are confounded with two-factor interactions.","some two-factor interactions are confounded with other two-factor interactions.","main effects are confounded with the mean."],"stem":"In a resolution IV experiment:","sub":"imp","why":"In a Resolution IV experiment, some two-factor interactions are confounded with other two-factor interactions. [VII.A.5]"},"revision":"6a727829f24d7919fe95dbc3548b97c059701287bc27573c8b625611e1cd01b1"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-453":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"8\",\"9\",\"15\",\"16\"],\"stem\":\"How many runs are required for a 2⁴⁻¹ experiment?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"A 2⁴⁻¹ experiment is a half-fraction of a 2⁴ experiment, so it requires 2⁴/2 = 16/2 = 8 runs. [VII.A.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["8","9","15","16"],"stem":"How many runs are required for a 2⁴⁻¹ experiment?","sub":"imp","why":"A 2⁴⁻¹ experiment is a half-fraction of a 2⁴ experiment, so it requires 2⁴/2 = 16/2 = 8 runs. [VII.A.5]"},"revision":"c52652db4edb939e526e208e924a1e4412721cc80ab59bd1f7a46ae1393d2994"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-454":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"2\",\"4\",\"16\",\"48\"],\"stem\":\"How many fewer runs would a 2⁶⁻² experiment require than a 2⁶ full-factorial experiment?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"A 2⁶ full factorial requires 64 runs. The quarter-fraction 2⁶⁻² requires 2⁴ = 16 runs, so it requires 64 − 16 = 48 fewer runs. [VII.A.5]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["2","4","16","48"],"stem":"How many fewer runs would a 2⁶⁻² experiment require than a 2⁶ full-factorial experiment?","sub":"imp","why":"A 2⁶ full factorial requires 64 runs. The quarter-fraction 2⁶⁻² requires 2⁴ = 16 runs, so it requires 64 − 16 = 48 fewer runs. [VII.A.5]"},"revision":"83fc9bd4b0ff381de5930a945026ed8852768f7de8e4e27f5967e003efaab837"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-455":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Sparsity of effects\",\"Replication\",\"Randomization\",\"Blocking\"],\"stem\":\"What design of experiments principle can be used to justify running a fractional factorial experiment instead of a full-factorial?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"The principle of sparsity of effects can be used to justify running a fractional factorial experiment instead of a full-factorial. [VII.A.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Sparsity of effects","Replication","Randomization","Blocking"],"stem":"What design of experiments principle can be used to justify running a fractional factorial experiment instead of a full-factorial?","sub":"imp","why":"The principle of sparsity of effects can be used to justify running a fractional factorial experiment instead of a full-factorial. [VII.A.5]"},"revision":"aaa919a5b64f037a41c3014417691cacf54f5ff347df84e6903c91622973ea6c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-456":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"−12.4 + √MSE\",\"−6.2\",\"0\",\"−12.4\"],\"stem\":\"In a two-level full-factorial design, the effect of Factor A is −12.4. What is the corresponding coefficient for Factor A in the coded regression model?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"In a coded two-level factorial model, a factor''s coefficient is one-half of its effect. Therefore, the coefficient for Factor A is −12.4/2 = −6.2. [VII.A.6]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["−12.4 + √MSE","−6.2","0","−12.4"],"stem":"In a two-level full-factorial design, the effect of Factor A is −12.4. What is the corresponding coefficient for Factor A in the coded regression model?","sub":"imp","why":"In a coded two-level factorial model, a factor''s coefficient is one-half of its effect. Therefore, the coefficient for Factor A is −12.4/2 = −6.2. [VII.A.6]"},"revision":"cd79d4c0fb98282313d2db896577a24b1ee31f5294a82125d7ffda7eb20f9f23"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-457":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"The model is insignificant.\",\"There is an interaction between factors A and B.\",\"The model needed a blocking factor.\",\"The experimental error is quite large.\"],\"stem\":\"A 2² full-factorial experiment is run with three replicates. The contour plot of continuous Factors A and B against the estimated response displays curved lines. What does this plot indicate?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Curved lines on a contour plot indicate that there is an interaction between factors. [VII.A.6]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["The model is insignificant.","There is an interaction between factors A and B.","The model needed a blocking factor.","The experimental error is quite large."],"stem":"A 2² full-factorial experiment is run with three replicates. The contour plot of continuous Factors A and B against the estimated response displays curved lines. What does this plot indicate?","sub":"imp","why":"Curved lines on a contour plot indicate that there is an interaction between factors. [VII.A.6]"},"revision":"93fc3f395663733c7ec7623e8fc3fedacb533974746f0a7366ed8645c34e991e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-458":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"significant confounding.\",\"the factor has a large effect on the response.\",\"curvature in the model.\",\"a large effect on the error term.\"],\"stem\":\"In a designed experiment, a main effect plot with a steep slope indicates:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"In a designed experiment, a main effect plot with a steep slope indicates that the factor has a large effect on the response. [VII.A.6]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["significant confounding.","the factor has a large effect on the response.","curvature in the model.","a large effect on the error term."],"stem":"In a designed experiment, a main effect plot with a steep slope indicates:","sub":"imp","why":"In a designed experiment, a main effect plot with a steep slope indicates that the factor has a large effect on the response. [VII.A.6]"},"revision":"096f31a71554e846eea30c23bfcbdf71fe7c13ed0a246c256dae76de7b0d681c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-459":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"strong interaction effects between the factors.\",\"an error made in charting.\",\"confounding effects between the factors.\",\"cancellation of the effects of the factors.\"],\"stem\":\"A two-factor interaction graph from a full-factorial experiment displays two lines that cross. This is an indication of:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"If a two-factor interaction graph from a full-factorial experiment displays two lines that cross, it is an indication of a strong interaction effect between the factors. [VII.A.6]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["strong interaction effects between the factors.","an error made in charting.","confounding effects between the factors.","cancellation of the effects of the factors."],"stem":"A two-factor interaction graph from a full-factorial experiment displays two lines that cross. This is an indication of:","sub":"imp","why":"If a two-factor interaction graph from a full-factorial experiment displays two lines that cross, it is an indication of a strong interaction effect between the factors. [VII.A.6]"},"revision":"dd46e33d8470762385592b7b8e9733398cb2194f3ee1fec056c8b1c5932e8238"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-46":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"reliable but not valid.\",\"reliable and valid.\",\"unreliable but valid.\",\"unreliable and not valid.\"],\"stem\":\"A data-collection instrument produces highly repeatable results, but its average reading has a consistent offset from a known reference. It is:\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"Highly repeatable readings demonstrate reliability. A consistent offset from the known reference is bias, so the instrument is not valid or accurate. [IV.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["reliable but not valid.","reliable and valid.","unreliable but valid.","unreliable and not valid."],"stem":"A data-collection instrument produces highly repeatable results, but its average reading has a consistent offset from a known reference. It is:","sub":"def","why":"Highly repeatable readings demonstrate reliability. A consistent offset from the known reference is bias, so the instrument is not valid or accurate. [IV.A.2]"},"revision":"421367079816b40da40c7cff4f6323685fa98815ebaa9a5c1f73bf9e74d9d825"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-460":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Allows for a cubic term to be fit in the model\",\"Eliminates the need for replication.\",\"Allows the experimenter to test whether there is curvature in the model\",\"Increases the resolution of the design\"],\"stem\":\"What is one advantage of adding center points to a factorial experiment?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"One advantage of adding center points to a factorial design is that it allows the experimenter to test whether there is curvature (a squared term) in the model. [VII.A.6]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Allows for a cubic term to be fit in the model","Eliminates the need for replication.","Allows the experimenter to test whether there is curvature in the model","Increases the resolution of the design"],"stem":"What is one advantage of adding center points to a factorial experiment?","sub":"imp","why":"One advantage of adding center points to a factorial design is that it allows the experimenter to test whether there is curvature (a squared term) in the model. [VII.A.6]"},"revision":"af3da19e1d483b0db4d5b84648ce106484ac74102cb7cc4d08521204655810d6"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-461":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Main effects\",\"Interaction effects\",\"Confounding effects\",\"Resolution effects\"],\"stem\":\"When analyzing the results of a 2ᵏ full-factorial experiment, which effects should be examined first in the ANOVA table?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Interaction effects should be examined before interpreting main effects in a 2ᵏ factorial experiment because a significant interaction changes how the main effects should be understood. [VII.A.6]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Main effects","Interaction effects","Confounding effects","Resolution effects"],"stem":"When analyzing the results of a 2ᵏ full-factorial experiment, which effects should be examined first in the ANOVA table?","sub":"imp","why":"Interaction effects should be examined before interpreting main effects in a 2ᵏ factorial experiment because a significant interaction changes how the main effects should be understood. [VII.A.6]"},"revision":"fef22476b1ebe06bf5d83aa66e14cb5332ff0e8d0a483f67be581a4f70d2be0d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-462":{"canonicalCont