s work activities in terms of:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Standard work describes work activities in terms of human motion. Each work activity is precisely described, including specifying cycle time, takt time, task sequence, and minimum inventory needed. [VII.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["product specifications.","machine downtime.","human motion.","continuous flow."],"stem":"Standard work describes work activities in terms of:","sub":"imp","why":"Standard work describes work activities in terms of human motion. Each work activity is precisely described, including specifying cycle time, takt time, task sequence, and minimum inventory needed. [VII.B.1]"},"revision":"6109c29f0ee77bc815d0ab21808f2922a8618f62b83f4cbbaf11f5e1a26276a7"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-47":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Listening posts\",\"Customer specifications\",\"Customer expectations\",\"Closed-loop corrective actions\"],\"stem\":\"What collective term describes customer-information channels such as interviews, suggestion boxes, focus groups, and surveys?\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"Interviews, suggestion boxes, focus groups, and surveys are all examples of customer data collection mechanisms, or listening posts. [IV.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Listening posts","Customer specifications","Customer expectations","Closed-loop corrective actions"],"stem":"What collective term describes customer-information channels such as interviews, suggestion boxes, focus groups, and surveys?","sub":"def","why":"Interviews, suggestion boxes, focus groups, and surveys are all examples of customer data collection mechanisms, or listening posts. [IV.A.2]"},"revision":"0cee62c954bc99d16a84752eebec9a287952e60bea5ce6068af95b5a8612d98c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-470":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"mistake-proofing.\",\"a bottleneck.\",\"a pull system.\",\"heijunka.\"],\"stem\":\"The shopping tab on a website will not allow the user to continue to the next screen until a correct credit card number is entered. This is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"The shopping tab on a website will not allow the user to continue to the next screen until a correct credit card number is entered. This is an example of mistake-proofing. [VII.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["mistake-proofing.","a bottleneck.","a pull system.","heijunka."],"stem":"The shopping tab on a website will not allow the user to continue to the next screen until a correct credit card number is entered. This is an example of:","sub":"imp","why":"The shopping tab on a website will not allow the user to continue to the next screen until a correct credit card number is entered. This is an example of mistake-proofing. [VII.B.1]"},"revision":"d15da76a89cacf38be5ca02d916914213a45c56c9c3957bd46d3d22ee65541cb"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-471":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"heijunka.\",\"poka-yoke.\",\"kanban.\",\"theory of constraints.\"],\"stem\":\"Building a new fixture that prevents a metal part from being placed into a drilling machine upside down is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Building a new fixture that prevents a metal part from being placed into a drilling machine upside down is an example of poka-yoke, or mistake-proofing. [VII.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["heijunka.","poka-yoke.","kanban.","theory of constraints."],"stem":"Building a new fixture that prevents a metal part from being placed into a drilling machine upside down is an example of:","sub":"imp","why":"Building a new fixture that prevents a metal part from being placed into a drilling machine upside down is an example of poka-yoke, or mistake-proofing. [VII.B.1]"},"revision":"c360aead92029e63b2379835c3e8b7706c1b510ac267aa5e59c837799ac22c80"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-472":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"a push system.\",\"a pull system.\",\"heijunka.\",\"one-piece flow.\"],\"stem\":\"A company uses forecast demand to level both production volume and product mix into a smoothed schedule. This is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Heijunka levels production volume and product mix over time to meet demand while reducing unevenness. A forecast-driven schedule without leveling would simply describe a push system. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["a push system.","a pull system.","heijunka.","one-piece flow."],"stem":"A company uses forecast demand to level both production volume and product mix into a smoothed schedule. This is an example of:","sub":"imp","why":"Heijunka levels production volume and product mix over time to meet demand while reducing unevenness. A forecast-driven schedule without leveling would simply describe a push system. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"7ace4a3ee4f90ff0399bab61085ac630819b122c5062ae6cacf84e0f93f35021"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-473":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"moving internal work to external work.\",\"single-piece flow.\",\"batch processing.\",\"statistical analysis.\"],\"stem\":\"With the SMED technique, emphasis is placed on:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"With the SMED technique, emphasis is placed on moving internal work to external work. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["moving internal work to external work.","single-piece flow.","batch processing.","statistical analysis."],"stem":"With the SMED technique, emphasis is placed on:","sub":"imp","why":"With the SMED technique, emphasis is placed on moving internal work to external work. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"d4ad3596cd9b7fa6efe2fe570cf2350eedf58e4c824854b819ceae7b7e6e8cce"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-474":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"push and pull systems.\",\"transportation and motion.\",\"volume leveling and product-mix leveling.\",\"defect detection and mistake-proofing.\"],\"stem\":\"Two types of heijunka include:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"The two primary forms of heijunka are volume leveling and product-mix leveling. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["push and pull systems.","transportation and motion.","volume leveling and product-mix leveling.","defect detection and mistake-proofing."],"stem":"Two types of heijunka include:","sub":"imp","why":"The two primary forms of heijunka are volume leveling and product-mix leveling. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"e82f68fd46c223bd17d487729b54960daddd8dd9375cd3fc97c892bc9ce23ddc"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-475":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.48 min/piece\",\"2.4 min/piece\",\"25 pieces/min\",\"625 pieces/shift\"],\"stem\":\"A customer order for 1,000 quarters is due in five days. ABC runs one continuous eight-hour shift per day. What is the takt time?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Takt time equals available production time divided by customer demand. Available time = 5 days × 8 hr/day × 60 min/hr = 2,400 minutes. Takt time = 2,400 minutes/1,000 pieces = 2.4 min/piece. [V.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.48 min/piece","2.4 min/piece","25 pieces/min","625 pieces/shift"],"stem":"A customer order for 1,000 quarters is due in five days. ABC runs one continuous eight-hour shift per day. What is the takt time?","sub":"mea","why":"Takt time equals available production time divided by customer demand. Available time = 5 days × 8 hr/day × 60 min/hr = 2,400 minutes. Takt time = 2,400 minutes/1,000 pieces = 2.4 min/piece. [V.A.1]"},"revision":"0b4cd128d1fd3981ac7d317e0a2d5f896c0c607f29f05a013864724726c602ee"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-476":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Poka-yoke\",\"SMED\",\"5S\",\"Standard work\"],\"stem\":\"Which Lean tool reduces setup or changeover downtime by converting as many activities as possible from internal setup to external setup?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"SMED reduces changeover time by converting internal setup work, which requires stopped equipment, into external setup work that can be completed while equipment is operating. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Poka-yoke","SMED","5S","Standard work"],"stem":"Which Lean tool reduces setup or changeover downtime by converting as many activities as possible from internal setup to external setup?","sub":"imp","why":"SMED reduces changeover time by converting internal setup work, which requires stopped equipment, into external setup work that can be completed while equipment is operating. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"c733b356af26d057d1f9a262d9cbd61e7eb4389cc25a22ea96c0d427d44c369e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-477":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"volume leveling and continuous improvement.\",\"product-mix leveling and theory of constraints.\",\"product-mix leveling and volume leveling.\",\"kaizen and the eight wastes.\"],\"stem\":\"Heijunka is a technique that addresses:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Heijunka reduces production unevenness through two complementary dimensions: product-mix leveling and volume leveling. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["volume leveling and continuous improvement.","product-mix leveling and theory of constraints.","product-mix leveling and volume leveling.","kaizen and the eight wastes."],"stem":"Heijunka is a technique that addresses:","sub":"imp","why":"Heijunka reduces production unevenness through two complementary dimensions: product-mix leveling and volume leveling. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"03e9049d49a91f7f5163e0434b68c18d2c6619836df1385ccab54a0be13729c5"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-478":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"kaizen is used for short-term fixes.\",\"a kaizen blitz rarely is an effective way to improve a process.\",\"kaizen is focused on a specific area of production.\",\"a kaizen blitz has a schedule and a deadline.\"],\"stem\":\"The main difference between kaizen and a kaizen blitz is that:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Kaizen is a term that means gradual unending improvement. A kaizen blitz is a small, intensive team project occurring over a few days to a week. [VII.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["kaizen is used for short-term fixes.","a kaizen blitz rarely is an effective way to improve a process.","kaizen is focused on a specific area of production.","a kaizen blitz has a schedule and a deadline."],"stem":"The main difference between kaizen and a kaizen blitz is that:","sub":"imp","why":"Kaizen is a term that means gradual unending improvement. A kaizen blitz is a small, intensive team project occurring over a few days to a week. [VII.B.3]"},"revision":"65b7684347ddc6471c5ae773062c080a4790cc0f986ac9c992f96a32386ddbfc"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-479":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"identify, subordinate, elevate, exploit, repeat.\",\"identify, elevate, exploit, subordinate, repeat.\",\"identify, subordinate, exploit, elevate, repeat.\",\"identify, exploit, subordinate, elevate, repeat.\"],\"stem\":\"The five steps in the theory of constraints are, in order:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"The five steps of Goldratt''s theory of constraints are, in order: 1. Identify the bottleneck, or constraining process. 2. Exploit the process by using kaizen or other methods to improve throughput on the constraining process. 3. Subordinate, or adjust, the rates of the other processes in the chain to match that of the constraining process. 4. Elevate, or extensively revise, the constraining process to improve the throughput rate. 5. Repeat the steps to find the new constraint. [VII.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["identify, subordinate, elevate, exploit, repeat.","identify, elevate, exploit, subordinate, repeat.","identify, subordinate, exploit, elevate, repeat.","identify, exploit, subordinate, elevate, repeat."],"stem":"The five steps in the theory of constraints are, in order:","sub":"imp","why":"The five steps of Goldratt''s theory of constraints are, in order: 1. Identify the bottleneck, or constraining process. 2. Exploit the process by using kaizen or other methods to improve throughput on the constraining process. 3. Subordinate, or adjust, the rates of the other processes in the chain to match that of the constraining process. 4. Elevate, or extensively revise, the constraining process to improve the throughput rate. 5. Repeat the steps to find the new constraint. [VII.B.4]"},"revision":"fe1c4d59c216e8aae5995b634623a962469709ac9e28d75676d3c7e2d9bb0c3f"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-48":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"The company complaint database\",\"The company transaction database\",\"Public social-media comments and independent industry reports\",\"The company warranty database\"],\"stem\":\"Which source of customer information is generated outside the organization?\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"Public customer-generated media and independent industry reports originate outside the organization. Complaint, transaction, and warranty databases are company-owned internal records. [IV.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["The company complaint database","The company transaction database","Public social-media comments and independent industry reports","The company warranty database"],"stem":"Which source of customer information is generated outside the organization?","sub":"def","why":"Public customer-generated media and independent industry reports originate outside the organization. Complaint, transaction, and warranty databases are company-owned internal records. [IV.A.2]"},"revision":"67005a09af4b708f26f12272b410dd9e5152f4754df610f737f055a4edcd2084"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-480":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"Shift length\",\"8 hours\"],[\"Lunch break\",\"30 min\"],[\"Planned maintenance\",\"15 min\"],[\"Downtime (broken fitting)\",\"48 min\"],[\"Downtime (waiting for material)\",\"14 min\"],[\"Good pieces produced\",\"7,000\"],[\"Scrap/rework pieces\",\"195\"],[\"Planned output at ideal rate\",\"10,000 pieces per 435 planned minutes\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"0.4206\",\"0.8575\",\"0.8708\",\"0.9609\"],\"stem\":\"For the injection-molding shift summarized below, what is the calculated availability?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Planned production time = 480 − 30 − 15 = 435 minutes. Operating time = 435 − 48 − 14 = 373 minutes. Availability = 373/435 = 0.8575. [VII.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["Shift length","8 hours"],["Lunch break","30 min"],["Planned maintenance","15 min"],["Downtime (broken fitting)","48 min"],["Downtime (waiting for material)","14 min"],["Good pieces produced","7,000"],["Scrap/rework pieces","195"],["Planned output at ideal rate","10,000 pieces per 435 planned minutes"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["0.4206","0.8575","0.8708","0.9609"],"stem":"For the injection-molding shift summarized below, what is the calculated availability?","sub":"imp","why":"Planned production time = 480 − 30 − 15 = 435 minutes. Operating time = 435 − 48 − 14 = 373 minutes. Availability = 373/435 = 0.8575. [VII.B.4]"},"revision":"110786a31e58dc8f3c8f3c8f51f25c8b0dba3c2e25c5fe64d3c8d6bb7630e64a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-481":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"Shift length\",\"8 hours\"],[\"Lunch break\",\"30 min\"],[\"Planned maintenance\",\"15 min\"],[\"Downtime (broken fitting)\",\"48 min\"],[\"Downtime (waiting for material)\",\"14 min\"],[\"Good pieces produced\",\"7,000\"],[\"Scrap/rework pieces\",\"195\"],[\"Planned output at ideal rate\",\"10,000 pieces per 435 planned minutes\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"0.7000\",\"0.7195\",\"0.8391\",\"0.9450\"],\"stem\":\"For the injection-molding shift summarized below, what is the OEE performance factor?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Planned production time is 480 − 30 − 15 = 435 minutes, and operating time is 435 − 48 − 14 = 373 minutes. The actual rate is 7,195/373 pieces per minute and the ideal rate is 10,000/435 pieces per minute. Performance = (7,195/373)/(10,000/435) = 0.8391. [VII.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["Shift length","8 hours"],["Lunch break","30 min"],["Planned maintenance","15 min"],["Downtime (broken fitting)","48 min"],["Downtime (waiting for material)","14 min"],["Good pieces produced","7,000"],["Scrap/rework pieces","195"],["Planned output at ideal rate","10,000 pieces per 435 planned minutes"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["0.7000","0.7195","0.8391","0.9450"],"stem":"For the injection-molding shift summarized below, what is the OEE performance factor?","sub":"imp","why":"Planned production time is 480 − 30 − 15 = 435 minutes, and operating time is 435 − 48 − 14 = 373 minutes. The actual rate is 7,195/373 pieces per minute and the ideal rate is 10,000/435 pieces per minute. Performance = (7,195/373)/(10,000/435) = 0.8391. [VII.B.4]"},"revision":"b27c578b11238faf2ba60d5ee12b90c996f5d6e9a92b5b82a94d5a23da70be53"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-482":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"Shift length\",\"8 hours\"],[\"Lunch break\",\"30 min\"],[\"Planned maintenance\",\"15 min\"],[\"Downtime (broken fitting)\",\"48 min\"],[\"Downtime (waiting for material)\",\"14 min\"],[\"Good pieces produced\",\"7,000\"],[\"Scrap/rework pieces\",\"195\"],[\"Planned output at ideal rate\",\"10,000 pieces per 435 planned minutes\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"0.7000\",\"0.8575\",\"0.9721\",\"0.9729\"],\"stem\":\"For the injection-molding shift summarized below, what is the OEE quality factor?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Quality is the ratio of good pieces to total pieces produced. Quality = good pieces / total pieces = 7,000 / (7,000 + 195) = 7,000 / 7,195 = 0.9729. [VII.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["Shift length","8 hours"],["Lunch break","30 min"],["Planned maintenance","15 min"],["Downtime (broken fitting)","48 min"],["Downtime (waiting for material)","14 min"],["Good pieces produced","7,000"],["Scrap/rework pieces","195"],["Planned output at ideal rate","10,000 pieces per 435 planned minutes"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["0.7000","0.8575","0.9721","0.9729"],"stem":"For the injection-molding shift summarized below, what is the OEE quality factor?","sub":"imp","why":"Quality is the ratio of good pieces to total pieces produced. Quality = good pieces / total pieces = 7,000 / (7,000 + 195) = 7,000 / 7,195 = 0.9729. [VII.B.4]"},"revision":"8a439f8faf8bb2b23bda9597fca9d1bddf0976213b982b17fba5519c09ab988a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-483":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"Shift length\",\"8 hours\"],[\"Lunch break\",\"30 min\"],[\"Planned maintenance\",\"15 min\"],[\"Downtime (broken fitting)\",\"48 min\"],[\"Downtime (waiting for material)\",\"14 min\"],[\"Good pieces produced\",\"7,000\"],[\"Scrap/rework pieces\",\"195\"],[\"Planned output at ideal rate\",\"10,000 pieces per 435 planned minutes\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"Increase the availability of the process.\",\"Increase the quality of the process.\",\"Decrease the number of days before the product is due.\",\"Increase the performance of the process.\"],\"stem\":\"For the injection-molding shift summarized below, assume availability, performance, or quality could each be raised by exactly one percentage point. Which listed action would produce the greatest increase in OEE?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Availability = 373/435 = 0.8575, performance = 0.8391, and quality = 7,000/7,195 = 0.9729. Because performance is the lowest OEE factor, improving process performance is the best listed action. [VII.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["Shift length","8 hours"],["Lunch break","30 min"],["Planned maintenance","15 min"],["Downtime (broken fitting)","48 min"],["Downtime (waiting for material)","14 min"],["Good pieces produced","7,000"],["Scrap/rework pieces","195"],["Planned output at ideal rate","10,000 pieces per 435 planned minutes"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["Increase the availability of the process.","Increase the quality of the process.","Decrease the number of days before the product is due.","Increase the performance of the process."],"stem":"For the injection-molding shift summarized below, assume availability, performance, or quality could each be raised by exactly one percentage point. Which listed action would produce the greatest increase in OEE?","sub":"imp","why":"Availability = 373/435 = 0.8575, performance = 0.8391, and quality = 7,000/7,195 = 0.9729. Because performance is the lowest OEE factor, improving process performance is the best listed action. [VII.B.4]"},"revision":"d563fb384bc3c7efac296faa72caddfe3c5cf43722cec8fac688ec5f5502ce29"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-484":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"simulation.\",\"pilot run.\",\"prototype.\",\"model.\"],\"stem\":\"An evaluation method for a set of solutions that involves manufacturing a small run of production-equivalent parts is known as a:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"A pilot run evaluates a solution by manufacturing a small run of production-equivalent parts. [VII.C]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["simulation.","pilot run.","prototype.","model."],"stem":"An evaluation method for a set of solutions that involves manufacturing a small run of production-equivalent parts is known as a:","sub":"imp","why":"A pilot run evaluates a solution by manufacturing a small run of production-equivalent parts. [VII.C]"},"revision":"c8f62083dff790e3af7db64284970c76043fd9f5e4e7a386b9ec801b09aa9447"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-485":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"pilot run.\",\"prototype.\",\"model.\",\"simulation.\"],\"stem\":\"An evaluation method for a set of solutions that involves using software to evaluate options is a:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"A simulation is an evaluation method for a set of solutions that involves using software to test the performance of the options. [VII.C]\"}","content":{"answ