R̄ = 16.25.\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"For subgroup size n = 7, D₃ = 0.076. Therefore, LCLᵣ = D₃R̄ = 0.076(16.25) = 1.235. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["X̿","345.50"],["R̄","16.25"],["n","7"],["D₃ for n = 7","0.076"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["0","1.235","9.441","12.350"],"stem":"Calculate the lower control limit of an R chart using X̿ = 345.50, subgroup size n = 7, and R̄ = 16.25.","sub":"con","why":"For subgroup size n = 7, D₃ = 0.076. Therefore, LCLᵣ = D₃R̄ = 0.076(16.25) = 1.235. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"5f97941bcaa6b8491841336328ee3ec2e1f8611e57fee76ad7e016ea9d5b9edd"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-492":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"X̿\",\"29.87\"],[\"S̄\",\"3.55\"],[\"n\",\"16\"],[\"A₃\",\"0.763\"],[\"B₃\",\"0.448\"],[\"B₄\",\"1.552\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"[1.232, 5.868]\",\"[1.289, 5.811]\",\"[1.590, 5.510]\",\"[1.519, 5.581]\"],\"stem\":\"Using the X̄-S chart summary and constants below, calculate the lower and upper control limits for the S chart.\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"For n = 16, B₃ = 0.448 and B₄ = 1.552. LCL = B₃S̄ = 0.448(3.55) = 1.590, and UCL = B₄S̄ = 1.552(3.55) = 5.510. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["X̿","29.87"],["S̄","3.55"],["n","16"],["A₃","0.763"],["B₃","0.448"],["B₄","1.552"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["[1.232, 5.868]","[1.289, 5.811]","[1.590, 5.510]","[1.519, 5.581]"],"stem":"Using the X̄-S chart summary and constants below, calculate the lower and upper control limits for the S chart.","sub":"con","why":"For n = 16, B₃ = 0.448 and B₄ = 1.552. LCL = B₃S̄ = 0.448(3.55) = 1.590, and UCL = B₄S̄ = 1.552(3.55) = 5.510. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"90f4fe6358c57a0882b31c2482e2a2b1c4235c2504b74de386341c008208e44a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-493":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"X̿\",\"29.87\"],[\"S̄\",\"3.55\"],[\"n\",\"16\"],[\"A₃\",\"0.763\"],[\"B₃\",\"0.448\"],[\"B₄\",\"1.552\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"[27.16, 32.58]\",\"[29.11, 29.93]\",\"[29.19, 30.55]\",\"[29.70, 30.04]\"],\"stem\":\"Using the X̄-S chart summary and constants below, calculate the lower and upper control limits for the X̄ chart.\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"For n = 16, A₃ = 0.763. LCL = X̿ − A₃S̄ = 29.87 − 0.763(3.55) = 27.16, and UCL = X̿ + A₃S̄ = 32.58. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["X̿","29.87"],["S̄","3.55"],["n","16"],["A₃","0.763"],["B₃","0.448"],["B₄","1.552"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["[27.16, 32.58]","[29.11, 29.93]","[29.19, 30.55]","[29.70, 30.04]"],"stem":"Using the X̄-S chart summary and constants below, calculate the lower and upper control limits for the X̄ chart.","sub":"con","why":"For n = 16, A₃ = 0.763. LCL = X̿ − A₃S̄ = 29.87 − 0.763(3.55) = 27.16, and UCL = X̿ + A₃S̄ = 32.58. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"c7e44cacd0a494316d44cc932688f16a3cb412972c16717f0295a33cb16953da"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-494":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"[0, 36.29]\",\"[24.05, 29.91]\",\"[24.27, 29.69]\",\"[26.20, 27.76]\"],\"stem\":\"A single sample was measured each hour for 25 hours: ΣX = 674.50 and the sum of the 24 moving ranges is 24.40. For an individuals chart based on two-point moving ranges, use E₂ = 2.660. What are the individuals-chart control limits?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"X̄ = 674.50/25 = 26.98 and MR̄ = 24.40/24 = 1.017. The limits are X̄ ± E₂MR̄ = 26.98 ± 2.660(1.017) = 26.98 ± 2.71 = [24.27, 29.69]. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["[0, 36.29]","[24.05, 29.91]","[24.27, 29.69]","[26.20, 27.76]"],"stem":"A single sample was measured each hour for 25 hours: ΣX = 674.50 and the sum of the 24 moving ranges is 24.40. For an individuals chart based on two-point moving ranges, use E₂ = 2.660. What are the individuals-chart control limits?","sub":"con","why":"X̄ = 674.50/25 = 26.98 and MR̄ = 24.40/24 = 1.017. The limits are X̄ ± E₂MR̄ = 26.98 ± 2.660(1.017) = 26.98 ± 2.71 = [24.27, 29.69]. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"fe010cef8c600a505f3dfaeb8a4a3f193cd65b099ed6f1f0e7b932bd664e0793"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-495":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"[0, 2.71]\",\"[0, 3.32]\",\"[0.47, 1.57]\",\"[0.57, 1.46]\"],\"stem\":\"For 25 hourly individuals, the sum of readings is 674.50 and the sum of the 24 moving ranges is 24.40. For a moving range of two observations, use D₃ = 0 and D₄ = 3.267. What are the control limits of the moving range chart?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"MR̄ = 24.40/24 = 1.017. LCL = D₃MR̄ = 0, and UCL = D₄MR̄ = 3.267 × 1.017 = 3.32. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["[0, 2.71]","[0, 3.32]","[0.47, 1.57]","[0.57, 1.46]"],"stem":"For 25 hourly individuals, the sum of readings is 674.50 and the sum of the 24 moving ranges is 24.40. For a moving range of two observations, use D₃ = 0 and D₄ = 3.267. What are the control limits of the moving range chart?","sub":"con","why":"MR̄ = 24.40/24 = 1.017. LCL = D₃MR̄ = 0, and UCL = D₄MR̄ = 3.267 × 1.017 = 3.32. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"8ddddabea4073bec120a7f24ce08a4b9d558e64052603ae29708aa02c7660946"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-496":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"To determine the average quality level\",\"To discover how many defects are in each product\",\"To track defects per unit\",\"To track the between-sample variability of measurements\"],\"stem\":\"What is one purpose of a p-chart?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"One purpose of a p-chart is to determine the average quality level of a product. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["To determine the average quality level","To discover how many defects are in each product","To track defects per unit","To track the between-sample variability of measurements"],"stem":"What is one purpose of a p-chart?","sub":"con","why":"One purpose of a p-chart is to determine the average quality level of a product. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"7e9c4da59104be184bac7a044616cd78bf03ccbc0ee9f180ac915c8160de49f6"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-497":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"changing the product.\",\"using the average subgroup size.\",\"using a c-chart.\",\"measuring fewer items.\"],\"stem\":\"A p-chart has subgroup sizes that vary only moderately. The burden of calculating a separate pair of control limits for every subgroup can be minimized by:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"When p-chart subgroup sizes vary only moderately, using the average subgroup size provides one approximate pair of control limits and reduces calculation burden. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["changing the product.","using the average subgroup size.","using a c-chart.","measuring fewer items."],"stem":"A p-chart has subgroup sizes that vary only moderately. The burden of calculating a separate pair of control limits for every subgroup can be minimized by:","sub":"con","why":"When p-chart subgroup sizes vary only moderately, using the average subgroup size provides one approximate pair of control limits and reduces calculation burden. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"6e14f8bececbfe2c3fca8c1269051d5bd71fbcc067ba1deb4a5652db5075b5c3"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-498":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Plot as calculated.\",\"Set the lower limit to zero.\",\"Recalculate; an error has been made.\",\"Use a u-chart.\"],\"stem\":\"What action should be taken if the lower limit of a c-chart is negative?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"If the lower limit of a c-chart is negative, set the lower limit to zero. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Plot as calculated.","Set the lower limit to zero.","Recalculate; an error has been made.","Use a u-chart."],"stem":"What action should be taken if the lower limit of a c-chart is negative?","sub":"con","why":"If the lower limit of a c-chart is negative, set the lower limit to zero. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"af2b2762350807a48b92c6976b05eaa14f9e66b212582c790fe84a3d61da354d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-499":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"count of nonconformities.\",\"rate of defectives per sample.\",\"count of defectives per unit.\",\"rate of nonconformities per unit.\"],\"stem\":\"A u-chart measures the:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"A u-chart measures the rate of nonconformities (defects) per unit. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["count of nonconformities.","rate of defectives per sample.","count of defectives per unit.","rate of nonconformities per unit."],"stem":"A u-chart measures the:","sub":"con","why":"A u-chart measures the rate of nonconformities (defects) per unit. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"5f3cc36c6afa41b77df1ca54fc197c9ff3d53c9499f2c0555603e5060f33160c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-5":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"reducing the risk of external audit findings.\",\"aiming for 3.4 defects per million opportunities.\",\"identifying the value from the customers’ point of view.\",\"creating organized warehouses to store excess inventory.\"],\"stem\":\"One of the five pillars, or principles, of lean is:\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"Identifying the value from the customers’ point of view is a pillar of the lean approach. The five pillars are (1) specify value from the customers’ point of view, (2) identify the value stream, (3) create flow, (4) pull from the customer, and (5) seek perfection. [I.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["reducing the risk of external audit findings.","aiming for 3.4 defects per million opportunities.","identifying the value from the customers’ point of view.","creating organized warehouses to store excess inventory."],"stem":"One of the five pillars, or principles, of lean is:","sub":"p1","why":"Identifying the value from the customers’ point of view is a pillar of the lean approach. The five pillars are (1) specify value from the customers’ point of view, (2) identify the value stream, (3) create flow, (4) pull from the customer, and (5) seek perfection. [I.A.1]"},"revision":"022c02e4c2abdb3827e144245e54603b229067a6138914d373042d11ea87d5d0"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-50":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Quality function deployment\",\"Kano model\",\"Process decision program chart\",\"SIPOC diagram\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following tools captures requirements for inputs and outputs from a process?\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"A SIPOC diagram has columns for suppliers, inputs, process steps, outputs, and customers. The I and O columns list the requirements for inputs and outputs of a process, respectively. [IV.A.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Quality function deployment","Kano model","Process decision program chart","SIPOC diagram"],"stem":"Which of the following tools captures requirements for inputs and outputs from a process?","sub":"def","why":"A SIPOC diagram has columns for suppliers, inputs, process steps, outputs, and customers. The I and O columns list the requirements for inputs and outputs of a process, respectively. [IV.A.3]"},"revision":"a11ad23225fbb29db4e871eccbe7805015c39c8307ac477b4f6729eee9ac5909"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-500":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"The number of surface defects in lots of 10 stainless steel panels will be tracked.\",\"The number of nonconforming light bulbs in a constant sample size of 300 bulbs will be tracked.\",\"The fraction of defective printed circuit boards produced each shift will be tracked. The number of boards produced per shift can vary from 400 to 500, depending on what type of chip is being manufactured.\",\"The average number of flaws per computer produced will be tracked. Random samples of 20–30 computers are checked each hour.\"],\"stem\":\"A p-chart would be best to use in which of the following situations?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"The p-chart would be best for tracking the fraction of defective printed circuit boards produced each shift in which there is a varying sample size. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["The number of surface defects in lots of 10 stainless steel panels will be tracked.","The number of nonconforming light bulbs in a constant sample size of 300 bulbs will be tracked.","The fraction of defective printed circuit boards produced each shift will be tracked. The number of boards produced per shift can vary from 400 to 500, depending on what type of chip is being manufactured.","The average number of flaws per computer produced will be tracked. Random samples of 20–30 computers are checked each hour."],"stem":"A p-chart would be best to use in which of the following situations?","sub":"con","why":"The p-chart would be best for tracking the fraction of defective printed circuit boards produced each shift in which there is a varying sample size. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"7929642b1f234733b9bd887885545f1b47b1863d862fce372d416bcae28d684c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-501":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"The number of surface defects found on stainless steel panels will be tracked. Sample sizes are constant.\",\"The number of nonconforming light bulbs in a constant sample size of 300 bulbs will be tracked.\",\"The fraction of defective printed circuit boards produced each shift will be tracked. The number of boards produced per shift can vary from 400 to 500, depending on what type of chip is being manufactured.\",\"The average number of flaws per computer produced will be tracked. Random samples of 20–30 computers are checked each hour.\"],\"stem\":\"A c-chart would be best in which situation?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"A c-chart monitors the count of nonconformities in a constant inspection unit or constant area of opportunity, such as surface defects on equally sized panels. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["The number of surface defects found on stainless steel panels will be tracked. Sample sizes are constant.","The number of nonconforming light bulbs in a constant sample size of 300 bulbs will be tracked.","The fraction of defective printed circuit boards produced each shift will be tracked. The number of boards produced per shift can vary from 400 to 500, depending on what type of chip is being manufactured.","The average number of flaws per computer produced will be tracked. Random samples of 20–30 computers are checked each hour."],"stem":"A c-chart would be best in which situation?","sub":"con","why":"A c-chart monitors the count of nonconformities in a constant inspection unit or constant area of opportunity, such as surface defects on equally sized panels. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"ed2589c170d21169ccfc33e5c830d0765dcbd642013166a774b50e134745ade8"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-502":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"controlling the number of nonconformities in a product.\",\"controlling the fraction nonconforming in a lot.\",\"cases where there are few opportunities for a nonconformity to occur.\",\"controlling the number of nonconforming products.\"],\"stem\":\"An np-chart is used for:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"An np-chart is used for controlling the number of nonconforming products. The np-chart uses a constant sample size. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["controlling the number of nonconformities in a product.","controlling the fraction nonconforming in a lot.","cases where there are few opportunities for a nonconformity to occur.","controlling the number of nonconforming products."],"stem":"An np-chart is used for:","sub":"con","why":"An np-chart is used for controlling the number of nonconforming products. The np-chart uses a constant sample size. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"d63bc3ab484ac2584cd93fd2ea6c6573cd68c63b150f1f437bac005223f96560"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-503":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"number of nonconforming units.\",\"rate of defects per unit.\",\"count of nonconformities.\",\"variation within subgroups.\"],\"stem\":\"A c-chart tracks the:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"A c-chart tracks the count of nonconformities. Sample sizes are constant. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["number of nonconforming units.","rate of defects per unit.","count of nonconformities.","variation within subgroups."],"stem":"A c-chart tracks the:","sub":"con","why":"A c-chart tracks the count of nonconformities. Sample sizes are constant. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"5c45a4593194e06a5a3ede905137b1dc7dac974248c5aa3aaba853df8a210d4f"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-504":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"subgroup sizes of 5.\",\"25 subgroups.\",\"100 samples.\",\"three days of production.\"],\"stem\":\"Trial control limits are generally calculated based on at least:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Trial control limits are generally calculated based on at least 25 subgroups. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["subgroup sizes of 5.","25 subgroups.","100 samples.","three days of production."],"stem":"Trial control limits are generally calculated based on at least:","sub":"con","why":"Trial control limits are generally calculated based on at least 25 subgroups. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"cdc18e13877b7e0c012c8b6a8c80186d856ef344883b8d42b0bc6def3873a36b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-505":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Variables charts are more informative.\",\"Attributes charts provide more detailed data.\",\"Attributes charts use physical part measurements.\",\"Variables charts track count data.\"],\"stem\":\"What is a difference between an attributes control chart and a variables control chart?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Variables charts that use continuous data are more informative than attributes control charts, which are based on discrete data. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Variables charts are more informative.","Attributes charts provide more detailed data.","Attributes charts use physical part measurements.","Variables charts track count data."],"stem":"What is a difference between an attributes control chart and a variables control chart?","sub":"con","why":"Variables charts that use continuous data are more informative than attributes control charts, which are based on discrete data. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"83c8e8bdd0b6faf1eb62f442d1ccb755a2d7f21ade3e3e1229adb69a557bcdfc"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-506":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"The centerline value plus or minus the mean.\",\"The centerline value plus or minus the range.\",\"The centerline value plus or minus three times the standard deviation.\",\"The centerline value plus or minus a percentage of the average value.\"],\"stem\":\"How are the control limits for an attributes chart calculated?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Control limits for attributes charts are calculated using the centerline plus or minus three times the standard deviation. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["The centerline value plus or minus the mean.","The centerline value plus or minus the range.","The centerline value plus or minus three times the standard deviation.","The centerline value plus or minus a percentage of the average value."],"stem":"How are the control limits for an attributes chart calculated?","sub":"con","why":"Control limits for attributes charts are calculated using the centerline plus or minus three times the standard deviation. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"850e5b1a9b4bff7f617a58c34f1d08476c1d38ea30a2da395c3d226960800690"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-507":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0\",\"0.0007\",\"0.0196\",\"0.5893\"],\"stem\":\"Across 30 subgroups, an attributes chart''s underlying data totals Σnp = 55 and Σn = 2,800. What is the centerline of the p-chart?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"The p-chart centerline is total defectives divided by total inspected: p̄ = Σnp/Σn = 55/2,800 = 0.0196. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0","0.0007","0.0196","0.5893"],"stem":"Across 30 subgroups, an attributes chart''s underlying data totals Σnp = 55 and Σn = 2,800. What is the centerline of the p-chart?","sub":"con","why":"The p-chart centerline is total defectives divided by total inspected: p̄ = Σnp/Σn = 55/2,800 = 0.0196. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"02d4c9a4b7a8ac9cd06e154cf51d2d33b0c16549a2b5d71d968ad8287675d139"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-508":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"[0, 0.0086]\",\"[0, 0.0627]\",\"[0.0118, 0.0275]\",\"[0.4540, 0.6570]\"],\"stem\":\"Across 30 subgroups, an attributes chart''s underlying data totals Σnp = 55 and Σn = 2,800. Calculate the control limits of the p-chart using the average sample size.\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"The average sample size is n̄ = 2,800/30 = 93.3, and p̄ = 55/2,800 = 0.0196. The limits are p̄ ± 3√[p̄(1 − p̄)/n̄] = 0.0196 ± 3√[(0.0196)(0.9804)/93.3] = 0.0196 ± 0.0431. The negative lower limit is set to 0, giving [0, 0.0627]. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["[0, 0.0086]","[0, 0.0627]","[0.0118, 0.0275]","[0.4540, 0.6570]"],"stem":"Across 30 subgroups, an attributes chart''s underlying data totals Σnp = 55 and Σn = 2,800. Calculate the control limits of the p-chart using the average sample size.","sub":"con","why":"The average sample size is n̄ = 2,800/30 = 93.3, and p̄ = 55/2,800 = 0.0196. The limits are p̄ ± 3√[p̄(1 − p̄)/n̄] = 0.0196 ± 3√[(0.0196)(0.9804)/93.3] = 0.0196 ± 0.0431. The negative lower limit is set to 0, giving [0, 0.0627]. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"2b6c434a3f8cc3207391926f3714aa0a348799d575eb99c184ccd88611fc9272"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-509":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.016\",\"0.390\",\"3.120\",\"5.791\"],\"stem\":\"An np-chart is built from 25 subgroups of n = 200 units each, with a total of 78 nonconforming units found across all subgroups. What is the centerline of the np-chart?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"The centerline is total nonconforming divided by number of subgroups: 78 / 25 = 3.12. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.016","0.390","3.120","5.791"],"stem":"An np-chart is built from 25 subgroups of n = 200 units each, with a total of 78 nonconforming units found across all subgroups. What is the centerline of the np-chart?","sub":"con","why":"The centerline is total nonconforming divided by number of subgroups: 78 / 25 = 3.12. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"5a610094e444a58ec6e848464dba8c957e8ce8ad639264bb16ddd7a339213333"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-51":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"natural process limits.\",\"critical-to-quality attributes.\",\"Taguchi''s loss function.\",\"the voice of the customer.\"],\"stem\":\"Specifications most accurately represent:\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"Specifications most accurately reflect the customer requirements, or the voice of the customer. [IV.A.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["natural process limits.","critical-to-quality attributes.","Taguchi''s loss function.","the voice of the customer."],"stem":"Specifications most accurately represent:","sub":"def","why":"Specifications most accurately reflect the customer requirements, or the voice of the customer. [IV.A.3]"},"revision":"4f2e1c2776a750addd32dcff17bcfc56cf639c0a9c1b01a8084ec401a75d8a1f"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-510":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"[0, 1.30]\",\"[0, 7.26]\",\"[0, 8.38]\",\"[2.46, 3.78]\"],\"stem\":\"An np-chart is based on 78 nonconforming units across 25 subgroups, each containing 200 units. What are the lower and upper control limits of the np-chart?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"p̄ = 78/(25 × 200) = 0.0156 and np̄ = 3.12. The limits are np̄ ± 3√[np̄(1 − p̄)] = 3.12 ± 5.30. Set the negative lower limit to 0, giving [0, 8.38]. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["[0, 1.30]","[0, 7.26]","[0, 8.38]","[2.46, 3.78]"],"stem":"An np-chart is based on 78 nonconforming units across 25 subgroups, each containing 200 units. What are the lower and upper control limits of the np-chart?","sub":"con","why":"p̄ = 78/(25 × 200) = 0.0156 and np̄ = 3.12. The limits are np̄ ± 3√[np̄(1 − p̄)] = 3.12 ± 5.30. Set the negative lower limit to 0, giving [0, 8.38]. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"27e669f47ad30d0ae8ef9c59d1aa47fa21a57899982423d61d613116a2089439"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-511":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Binomial\",\"Poisson\",\"Geometric\",\"Normal\"],\"stem\":\"Which distribution is used to calculate the control limits of the np-chart?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"The np-chart is based on the binomial distribution. There is a constant number of independent trials, there are only two possible classifications for each unit in a sample, and the probability of a defective is constant from trial to trial. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Binomial","Poisson","Geometric","Normal"],"stem":"Which distribution is used to calculate the control limits of the np-chart?","sub":"con","why":"The np-chart is based on the binomial distribution. There is a constant number of independent trials, there are only two possible classifications for each unit in a sample, and the probability of a defective is constant from trial to trial. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"a39afa99752c64728e28f21c8d280d8d9ccda72a282f55e333a8931145d7d48a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-512":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"[0, 1.32]\",\"[0, 2.69]\",\"[0, 5.22]\",\"[0, 9.65]\"],\"stem\":\"Each hour, 10 units are sampled and inspected for defects. After 25 time periods, the total number of defects equals 38. Using this information, calculate the control limits for a c-chart.\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"The average count is c̄ = 38/25 = 1.52. Therefore, LCL = c̄ − 3√c̄ = 1.52 − 3.70 = −2.18, which is set to 0, and UCL = c̄ + 3√c̄ = 1.52 + 3.70 = 5.22. The control limits are [0, 5.22]. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["[0, 1.32]","[0, 2.69]","[0, 5.22]","[0, 9.65]"],"stem":"Each hour, 10 units are sampled and inspected for defects. After 25 time periods, the total number of defects equals 38. Using this information, calculate the control limits for a c-chart.","sub":"con","why":"The average count is c̄ = 38/25 = 1.52. Therefore, LCL = c̄ − 3√c̄ = 1.52 − 3.70 = −2.18, which is set to 0, and UCL = c̄ + 3√c̄ = 1.52 + 3.70 = 5.22. The control limits are [0, 5.22]. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"d29bc11f54b992c2221388074b91adfe007d6dd436839906827bc122f71099cd"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-513":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Normal\",\"Poisson\",\"Uniform\",\"Student''s t\"],\"stem\":\"Which distribution is used to calculate the control limits of a c-chart?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"A c-chart uses the Poisson model to count nonconformities in a constant inspection unit or constant area of opportunity. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Normal","Poisson","Uniform","Student''s t"],"stem":"Which distribution is used to calculate the control limits of a c-chart?","sub":"con","why":"A c-chart uses the Poisson model to count nonconformities in a constant inspection unit or constant area of opportunity. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"1fcf23dff530a1581a587a21d55e27fb76091d20e0991d9134a3469f3d1adef5"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-514":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"0.006\",\"0.155\",\"3.85\",\"9.60\"],\"stem\":\"A u-chart is used to monitor defects per unit. Across 25 subgroups, a total of 240 defects were found in 1,550 units inspected. What is the centerline of the u-chart?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"The u-chart centerline is the average defects per unit: ū = 240/1,550 = 0.15484, which rounds to 0.155. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["0.006","0.155","3.85","9.60"],"stem":"A u-chart is used to monitor defects per unit. Across 25 subgroups, a total of 240 defects were found in 1,550 units inspected. What is the centerline of the u-chart?","sub":"con","why":"The u-chart centerline is the average defects per unit: ū = 240/1,550 = 0.15484, which rounds to 0.155. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"791b7a26a129d1920494a93d7f84165d53776af60145ca81a5bc78a53dcec16a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-515":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"[0, 0.059]\",\"[0.131, 0.177]\",\"[0, 0.419]\",\"[0.124, 0.184]\"],\"stem\":\"A u-chart has ū = 240/1,550 defects per unit. What are the lower and upper control limits for a subgroup of n = 20 units?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Using the unrounded ū = 240/1,550 = 0.15484, the limits are ū ± 3√(ū/20) = 0.15484 ± 0.26397. Set the negative lower limit to 0; UCL = 0.41880, giving [0, 0.419]. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["[0, 0.059]","[0.131, 0.177]","[0, 0.419]","[0.124, 0.184]"],"stem":"A u-chart has ū = 240/1,550 defects per unit. What are the lower and upper control limits for a subgroup of n = 20 units?","sub":"con","why":"Using the unrounded ū = 240/1,550 = 0.15484, the limits are ū ± 3√(ū/20) = 0.15484 ± 0.26397. Set the negative lower limit to 0; UCL = 0.41880, giving [0, 0.419]. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"fdaa0656b21f7c0ede75904bbd2e587aac51bb67b5d982d7d94a6e009228b88a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-516":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"six consecutive increasing or decreasing points.\",\"a point out of control.\",\"three consecutive points above or below the control limits.\",\"a change in control limits.\"],\"stem\":\"A trend signal on a control chart refers to:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"A trend signal is a sequence of consecutive points that steadily increase or steadily decrease; this source uses a six-point rule. A run is the more general sequence of consecutive points sharing a property. [VIII.A.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["six consecutive increasing or decreasing points.","a point out of control.","three consecutive points above or below the control limits.","a change in control limits."],"stem":"A trend signal on a control chart refers to:","sub":"con","why":"A trend signal is a sequence of consecutive points that steadily increase or steadily decrease; this source uses a six-point rule. A run is the more general sequence of consecutive points sharing a property. [VIII.A.5]"},"revision":"dbc644844c7143686173322435a881043ae90d96def46b85dec9b4bf6167a102"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-517":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"periodic shifts on an attributes chart.\",\"a random pattern on an attributes chart.\",\"variable control limits on an attributes chart.\",\"negative lower control limits.\"],\"stem\":\"Two inspectors alternate on a fixed schedule, and they use different criteria for classifying an item as defective. What pattern can this create on an attributes chart?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"When inspectors with different criteria alternate on a regular schedule, their systematic classification differences can create a periodic shift in the chart. [VIII.A.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["periodic shifts on an attributes chart.","a random pattern on an attributes chart.","variable control limits on an attributes chart.","negative lower control limits."],"stem":"Two inspectors alternate on a fixed schedule, and they use different criteria for classifying an item as defective. What pattern can this create on an attributes chart?","sub":"con","why":"When inspectors with different criteria alternate on a regular schedule, their systematic classification differences can create a periodic shift in the chart. [VIII.A.5]"},"revision":"bd79067e72adc7ff7424b55f811ec2cd73d20ba368ef3c15c3cdc9fd1571dc78"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-518":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"a decrease in process variability.\",\"a decrease in sensitivity.\",\"an increase in Type I errors.\",\"an increase in Type II errors.\"],\"stem\":\"Applying all eight Shewhart rules to determine out-of-control conditions on a variables control chart can result in:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Applying all eight Shewhart rules to determine out-of-control conditions on a chart can result in an increase in Type I errors, also known as false alarms. [VIII.A.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["a decrease in process variability.","a decrease in sensitivity.","an increase in Type I errors.","an increase in Type II errors."],"stem":"Applying all eight Shewhart rules to determine out-of-control conditions on a variables control chart can result in:","sub":"con","why":"Applying all eight Shewhart rules to determine out-of-control conditions on a chart can result in an increase in Type I errors, also known as false alarms. [VIII.A.5]"},"revision":"07d3c64995b94d2417a5b0703a840dfd9eb22b801da1557a003868dba2a916ed"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-519":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"0.00195\",\"0.00270\",\"0.00450\",\"0.50000\"],\"stem\":\"Assume successive in-control X̄-chart points are independent and equally likely to fall above or below the centerline. What is the probability that nine consecutive points fall above the centerline?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"For a symmetric in-control chart, the chance that a point is above the centerline is 0.50. The probability that nine consecutive points are above it is (0.50)⁹ = 0.00195, an unlikely in-control pattern. [VIII.A.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["0.00195","0.00270","0.00450","0.50000"],"stem":"Assume successive in-control X̄-chart points are independent and equally likely to fall above or below the centerline. What is the probability that nine consecutive points fall above the centerline?","sub":"con","why":"For a symmetric in-control chart, the chance that a point is above the centerline is 0.50. The probability that nine consecutive points are above it is (0.50)⁹ = 0.00195, an unlikely in-control pattern. [VIII.A.5]"},"revision":"ca768f3b6265dda180e5c526e47f2e19eb9127dae42295cb8860bb7bbfd2eb21"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-52":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"low customer dissatisfaction if not met, but high customer satisfaction if met.\",\"high customer dissatisfaction if not met, and high customer satisfaction if met.\",\"high customer dissatisfaction if not met, but neutral customer satisfaction if met.\",\"low customer dissatisfaction if not met, and neutral customer satisfaction if met.\"],\"stem\":\"In the Kano model, expected requirements are those that will cause:\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"In Kano analysis, expected requirements are known as must-be requirements. Not meeting these requirements will result in high customer dissatisfaction. However, meeting minimum requirements will neither please nor displease the customer. [IV.A.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["low customer dissatisfaction if not met, but high customer satisfaction if met.","high customer dissatisfaction if not met, and high customer satisfaction if met.","high customer dissatisfaction if not met, but neutral customer satisfaction if met.","low customer dissatisfaction if not met, and neutral customer satisfaction if met."],"stem":"In the Kano model, expected requirements are those that will cause:","sub":"def","why":"In Kano analysis, expected requirements are known as must-be requirements. Not meeting these requirements will result in high customer dissatisfaction. However, meeting minimum requirements will neither please nor displease the customer. [IV.A.3]"},"revision":"0c958c585dedb3cbd0988c99b79e12c24c56035c558321e141266a16b892bc42"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-520":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Tool wear\",\"A mean shift\",\"Stratification\",\"Excessive tampering\"],\"stem\":\"A control chart shows 14 points in a row alternating up and down. What is a possible cause of this out-of-control pattern?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Having 14 points in a row alternating up and down could be caused by excessive tampering by operators who adjust the machine when a point is above the centerline, and then again when a point is below the centerline, even though the points are in control. [VIII.A.5]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Tool wear","A mean shift","Stratification","Excessive tampering"],"stem":"A control chart shows 14 points in a row alternating up and down. What is a possible cause of this out-of-control pattern?","sub":"con","why":"Having 14 points in a row alternating up and down could be caused by excessive tampering by operators who adjust the machine when a point is above the centerline, and then again when a point is below the centerline, even though the points are in control. [VIII.A.5]"},"revision":"85f81bbbf48c84aba316418817949b6416c6d654ed3f92cc40d187df122d6375"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-521":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"lean principles with push systems.\",\"kanban with the Six Big Losses.\",\"continuous improvement and quality function deployment.\",\"maintenance procedures with continuous improvement initiatives.\"],\"stem\":\"Total productive maintenance combines:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Total productive maintenance combines maintenance procedures with continuous improvement initiatives. [VIII.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["lean principles with push systems.","kanban with the Six Big Losses.","continuous improvement and quality function deployment.","maintenance procedures with continuous improvement initiatives."],"stem":"Total productive maintenance combines:","sub":"con","why":"Total productive maintenance combines maintenance procedures with continuous improvement initiatives. [VIII.B.1]"},"revision":"dddc2e0437e247fece0c59883eae5419a42ca3f18daa9ac2a3e2eef8ec2737ba"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-522":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"A vibration alarm triggers an immediate bearing inspection.\",\"A failed pump is repaired after it stops operating.\",\"A root-cause investigation begins after repeated unscheduled failures.\",\"An automobile receives an oil change every 5,000 miles.\"],\"stem\":\"Which situation is routine periodic maintenance at a standard usage interval that was not selected from component failure-history analysis or a measured operating condition?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Changing automobile oil every 5,000 miles is periodic preventive maintenance because the work is scheduled at a routine usage interval rather than triggered by condition or failure. [VIII.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["A vibration alarm triggers an immediate bearing inspection.","A failed pump is repaired after it stops operating.","A root-cause investigation begins after repeated unscheduled failures.","An automobile receives an oil change every 5,000 miles."],"stem":"Which situation is routine periodic maintenance at a standard usage interval that was not selected from component failure-history analysis or a measured operating condition?","sub":"con","why":"Changing automobile oil every 5,000 miles is periodic preventive maintenance because the work is scheduled at a routine usage interval rather than triggered by condition or failure. [VIII.B.1]"},"revision":"4161da03ff8d84b05570aa84f5b05811e0e21e85546cae50b5b9ef299f704b13"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-523":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"To perform maintenance in a timely manner\",\"To replace preventive maintenance with run-to-failure maintenance.\",\"To ensure that there are no unplanned delays in production\",\"To ensure that proper production planning has occurred prior to the issuing of material\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following best describes the goal of total productive maintenance (TPM)?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Total productive maintenance (TPM) uses preventive maintenance throughout equipment life to maximize equipment effectiveness and avoid production interruptions. [VIII.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["To perform maintenance in a timely manner","To replace preventive maintenance with run-to-failure maintenance.","To ensure that there are no unplanned delays in production","To ensure that proper production planning has occurred prior to the issuing of material"],"stem":"Which of the following best describes the goal of total productive maintenance (TPM)?","sub":"con","why":"Total productive maintenance (TPM) uses preventive maintenance throughout equipment life to maximize equipment effectiveness and avoid production interruptions. [VIII.B.1]"},"revision":"c4f766a4780b0de58302002534cb8fb709be5c225712c385044a61bcdb44107a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-524":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"condition-based maintenance.\",\"periodic maintenance.\",\"reliability-based maintenance.\",\"maintenance analytics.\"],\"stem\":\"Failure-history analysis shows truck-engine timing belts fail between 60,000 and 80,000 miles. The company deliberately derives a 50,000-mile replacement threshold from that failure distribution rather than using a routine manufacturer service interval. Which maintenance strategy most specifically describes this decision?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Changing the timing belt before its mean time to failure is an example of reliability-based maintenance. [VIII.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["condition-based maintenance.","periodic maintenance.","reliability-based maintenance.","maintenance analytics."],"stem":"Failure-history analysis shows truck-engine timing belts fail between 60,000 and 80,000 miles. The company deliberately derives a 50,000-mile replacement threshold from that failure distribution rather than using a routine manufacturer service interval. Which maintenance strategy most specifically describes this decision?","sub":"con","why":"Changing the timing belt before its mean time to failure is an example of reliability-based maintenance. [VIII.B.1]"},"revision":"44aa063efcd5d4c795b8fcf8dc613740669e9ff6d20aa877263dc8dab04fd377"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-525":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"maintenance analytics.\",\"visual controls.\",\"reliability-based maintenance.\",\"process capability.\"],\"stem\":\"A continuous improvement project team began using SPC to track the diameter of a drilled hole in a part. After several tool change cycles, the continuous improvement team noticed that the SPC chart consistently went out of control at part number 190. Changing the preventive maintenance cycle from 200 parts to being triggered when the SPC chart goes out of control would be an example of the use of:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Using the recurring SPC signal to determine when the cutting tool should be changed is maintenance analytics: maintenance timing is based on process-sensing data. [VIII.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["maintenance analytics.","visual controls.","reliability-based maintenance.","process capability."],"stem":"A continuous improvement project team began using SPC to track the diameter of a drilled hole in a part. After several tool change cycles, the continuous improvement team noticed that the SPC chart consistently went out of control at part number 190. Changing the preventive maintenance cycle from 200 parts to being triggered when the SPC chart goes out of control would be an example of the use of:","sub":"con","why":"Using the recurring SPC signal to determine when the cutting tool should be changed is maintenance analytics: maintenance timing is based on process-sensing data. [VIII.B.1]"},"revision":"5e67c820ab8085c9554bc2cfa75af343cb91ce1137feff4cb2f5bba1666622f2"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-526":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Condition-based\",\"Preventive\",\"Reliability-based\",\"Corrective\"],\"stem\":\"During pollen season, air filters must be changed more often. Because the service interval changes in response to an environmental operating condition rather than a fixed calendar or usage interval, which maintenance approach is being used?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Changing air filters more often during pollen season is an example of condition-based maintenance. [VIII.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Condition-based","Preventive","Reliability-based","Corrective"],"stem":"During pollen season, air filters must be changed more often. Because the service interval changes in response to an environmental operating condition rather than a fixed calendar or usage interval, which maintenance approach is being used?","sub":"con","why":"Changing air filters more often during pollen season is an example of condition-based maintenance. [VIII.B.1]"},"revision":"2f4fec80610fe973eac5117d2c11c495f379aa11dce962362efe07a882c4197f"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-527":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"maintenance analytics.\",\"reliability-based maintenance.\",\"process capability.\",\"visual controls.\"],\"stem\":\"The refill line on a lubricant container is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"The refill line on the lubricant container is an example of visual controls. [VIII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["maintenance analytics.","reliability-based maintenance.","process capability.","visual controls."],"stem":"The refill line on a lubricant container is an example of:","sub":"con","why":"The refill line on the lubricant container is an example of visual controls. [VIII.B.2]"},"revision":"b0ec2e3201e761cc10d3d7b53e57b59dbf77085c308ce4fce84bd24fb60f0494"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-528":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"perform another kaizen event.\",\"perform a gage R&R study.\",\"buy a more precise measuring device.\",\"reduce machine downtime.\"],\"stem\":\"After a kaizen event greatly reduces observed process variation, the team must verify that the measurement system is adequate relative to the now-smaller process variation. Which study should the team perform next?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"When process variation is reduced, measurement error may become a larger share of the observed variation. A gage R&R study checks whether the measurement system remains adequate for the improved process. [VIII.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["perform another kaizen event.","perform a gage R&R study.","buy a more precise measuring device.","reduce machine downtime."],"stem":"After a kaizen event greatly reduces observed process variation, the team must verify that the measurement system is adequate relative to the now-smaller process variation. Which study should the team perform next?","sub":"co