[["A","±0.5"],["B","±0.2"],["C","±0.3"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["±0.62","±1.0","±2.0","±6.8"],"stem":"Using the component tolerance table below, calculate the assembly''s statistical ± tolerance (half-width) by root-sum-square.","sub":"dfss","why":"Statistical tolerancing combines independent half-widths by root-sum-square: ±√(0.5² + 0.2² + 0.3²) = ±√0.38 = ±0.62. [IX.C]"},"revision":"9d1ffd7dfbea3dfa98ba50edeca17c006a5be269c3cd633a0aeca40b6a503e5e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-542":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"combines cost, target, and variation into one metric.\",\"can only be used for robust designs.\",\"is only concerned with production losses in dollars due to waste of raw material.\",\"is a step-function with steps occurring at the specification limits.\"],\"stem\":\"Taguchi''s loss function:\",\"sub\":\"dfss\",\"why\":\"Taguchi''s loss function combines cost, target, and variation into one metric. [IX.C]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["combines cost, target, and variation into one metric.","can only be used for robust designs.","is only concerned with production losses in dollars due to waste of raw material.","is a step-function with steps occurring at the specification limits."],"stem":"Taguchi''s loss function:","sub":"dfss","why":"Taguchi''s loss function combines cost, target, and variation into one metric. [IX.C]"},"revision":"5a1db3eae5557ee173a52fb8b694004a167648491e2915cbab1d4b464611ed73"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-543":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Variance is best.\",\"Larger is better.\",\"Smaller is better.\",\"Nominal is best.\"],\"stem\":\"What is the most common loss function used for symmetric two-sided tolerances?\",\"sub\":\"dfss\",\"why\":\"The nominal-is-best loss function is most commonly used for two-sided symmetric tolerances. [IX.C]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Variance is best.","Larger is better.","Smaller is better.","Nominal is best."],"stem":"What is the most common loss function used for symmetric two-sided tolerances?","sub":"dfss","why":"The nominal-is-best loss function is most commonly used for two-sided symmetric tolerances. [IX.C]"},"revision":"644adae842ac16118922ddeaa0785fa7d8d68e57f45a6f22c66d4404807446fc"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-544":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"tolerance design.\",\"parameter design.\",\"Taguchi array.\",\"system design.\"],\"stem\":\"Designing around the use of inferior-grade, low-cost components and raw materials in the design process is referred to as:\",\"sub\":\"dfss\",\"why\":\"Parameter design makes a product or process more robust to variation from inferior-grade, low-cost components and raw materials. [IX.C]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["tolerance design.","parameter design.","Taguchi array.","system design."],"stem":"Designing around the use of inferior-grade, low-cost components and raw materials in the design process is referred to as:","sub":"dfss","why":"Parameter design makes a product or process more robust to variation from inferior-grade, low-cost components and raw materials. [IX.C]"},"revision":"f32a9abbd9bb44fcc2e49d1006d5699f7de7d0fb627ed25788d9701433645005"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-545":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"tolerance design.\",\"parameter design.\",\"Taguchi arrays.\",\"system design.\"],\"stem\":\"The process of selectively upgrading components to eliminate excessive variation is known as:\",\"sub\":\"dfss\",\"why\":\"Tolerance design is the process of selectively upgrading components to eliminate excessive variation. [IX.C]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["tolerance design.","parameter design.","Taguchi arrays.","system design."],"stem":"The process of selectively upgrading components to eliminate excessive variation is known as:","sub":"dfss","why":"Tolerance design is the process of selectively upgrading components to eliminate excessive variation. [IX.C]"},"revision":"85aba0f7f1d985fcd2aba5f5b87d7dbc30ed5981de8ec2dd09009e0621287662"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-546":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"\",\"Part A\",\"Part B\",\"Part C\"],\"rows\":[[\"LSL\",\"0.750\",\"0.355\",\"1.230\"],[\"USL\",\"0.795\",\"0.360\",\"1.280\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"[2.285, 2.485]\",\"[2.318, 2.452]\",\"[2.335, 2.385]\",\"[2.351, 2.419]\"],\"stem\":\"Using the statistical tolerancing method, calculate the lower and upper specifications of the stacked assembly shown below.\",\"sub\":\"dfss\",\"why\":\"The nominal values and half-width tolerances are A = 0.7725 ± 0.0225, B = 0.3575 ± 0.0025, and C = 1.255 ± 0.025. The statistical stack is √(0.0225² + 0.0025² + 0.025²) = 0.034, and the nominal total is 0.7725 + 0.3575 + 1.255 = 2.385. Thus the limits are [2.385 − 0.034, 2.385 + 0.034] = [2.351, 2.419]. [IX.C]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"chart":{"columns":["","Part A","Part B","Part C"],"rows":[["LSL","0.750","0.355","1.230"],["USL","0.795","0.360","1.280"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["[2.285, 2.485]","[2.318, 2.452]","[2.335, 2.385]","[2.351, 2.419]"],"stem":"Using the statistical tolerancing method, calculate the lower and upper specifications of the stacked assembly shown below.","sub":"dfss","why":"The nominal values and half-width tolerances are A = 0.7725 ± 0.0225, B = 0.3575 ± 0.0025, and C = 1.255 ± 0.025. The statistical stack is √(0.0225² + 0.0025² + 0.025²) = 0.034, and the nominal total is 0.7725 + 0.3575 + 1.255 = 2.385. Thus the limits are [2.385 − 0.034, 2.385 + 0.034] = [2.351, 2.419]. [IX.C]"},"revision":"8af9456601ea06fd4233134db7bf765a3c9114e2b35adcabfab6cdab99c38724"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-547":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Input\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"Sample size, n\",\"15\"],[\"Sample mean, x̄\",\"34.56\"],[\"Sample standard deviation, s\",\"2.34\"],[\"Confidence\",\"90%\"],[\"Population coverage\",\"95%\"],[\"Two-sided tolerance factor, K\",\"2.706\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"[29.220, 39.900]\",\"[30.51