cifications, detection, and risk plans","Gage R&R, diagnosis, and risk plans","Monitoring, detection, diagnosis, and a reaction plan"],"stem":"An effective control plan contains which elements?","sub":"con","why":"An effective control plan defines monitoring, detection, diagnosis, and a reaction plan. [VIII.C.2]"},"revision":"2466730b328b462ae6b53b1f79889d937634903d59010f9fc81be5815b56636c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-552":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Learning creates potential; performance is the realization of the potential.\",\"Learning creates behavior change; performance creates potential.\",\"Learning is the realization of potential; performance occurs in the classroom.\",\"Learning is the realization of training; performance shows training gaps.\"],\"stem\":\"What is the difference between learning and performance?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"Learning and performance differ in that learning creates potential, and performance is the realization of that potential. [III.D.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Learning creates potential; performance is the realization of the potential.","Learning creates behavior change; performance creates potential.","Learning is the realization of potential; performance occurs in the classroom.","Learning is the realization of training; performance shows training gaps."],"stem":"What is the difference between learning and performance?","sub":"tm","why":"Learning and performance differ in that learning creates potential, and performance is the realization of that potential. [III.D.2]"},"revision":"7ce45e195b3628621708d195361fba274130a1543400344bf93c49732d7ef344"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-553":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Input\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"Sample size, n\",\"50\"],[\"Sample mean, x̄\",\"1.50\"],[\"Sample standard deviation, s\",\"0.20\"],[\"Confidence\",\"95%\"],[\"Population coverage\",\"99%\"],[\"Two-sided tolerance factor, K\",\"3.117\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"[0.877, 2.123]\",\"[0.929, 2.071]\",\"[0.986, 2.014]\",\"[1.046, 1.954]\"],\"stem\":\"A normally distributed process output has an average of 1.50 and a sample standard deviation of 0.20. If 50 parts are produced, what is the tolerance interval that will cover at least 99% of the distribution with 95% confidence?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"For n = 50, 95% confidence, and 99% population coverage, the two-sided tolerance factor is K = 3.117. The interval is x̄ ± Ks = 1.50 ± 3.117(0.20) = [0.877, 2.123]. [VI.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"columns":["Input","Value"],"rows":[["Sample size, n","50"],["Sample mean, x̄","1.50"],["Sample standard deviation, s","0.20"],["Confidence","95%"],["Population coverage","99%"],["Two-sided tolerance factor, K","3.117"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["[0.877, 2.123]","[0.929, 2.071]","[0.986, 2.014]","[1.046, 1.954]"],"stem":"A normally distributed process output has an average of 1.50 and a sample standard deviation of 0.20. If 50 parts are produced, what is the tolerance interval that will cover at least 99% of the distribution with 95% confidence?","sub":"ana","why":"For n = 50, 95% confidence, and 99% population coverage, the two-sided tolerance factor is K = 3.117. The interval is x̄ ± Ks = 1.50 ± 3.117(0.20) = [0.877, 2.123]. [VI.B.4]"},"revision":"d8d520a2cc63dfb2dee311e5250c1a29d7a5c6eae69a88734ad83990381f40c0"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-554":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Part\",\"Dimension\"],\"rows\":[[\"A\",\"1.20 ± 0.05\"],[\"B\",\"2.50 ± 0.10\"],[\"C\",\"2.0 ± 0.50\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"±0.20\",\"±0.65\",\"±1.30\",\"±5.70\"],\"stem\":\"Using conventional worst-case tolerancing, calculate the assembly''s resulting ± tolerance (half-width).\",\"sub\":\"dfss\",\"why\":\"Conventional worst-case tolerance is the sum of the component half-widths: ±(0.05 + 0.10 + 0.50) = ±0.65. The full tolerance width is 1.30. [IX.C]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"chart":{"columns":["Part","Dimension"],"rows":[["A","1.20 ± 0.05"],["B","2.50 ± 0.10"],["C","2.0 ± 0.50"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["±0.20","±0.65","±1.30","±5.70"],"stem":"Using conventional worst-case tolerancing, calculate the assembly''s resulting ± tolerance (half-width).","sub":"dfss","why":"Conventional worst-case tolerance is the sum of the component half-widths: ±(0.05 + 0.10 + 0.50) = ±0.65. The full tolerance width is 1.30. [IX.C]"},"revision":"0a7dd4abf494c01b5cb0754f533e747aa1fdbea33e7ef46628c2337fae1b3c6c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-555":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"The team is asked to give specific information, and then the leader makes the final decision.\",\"The team is informed of the situation and each member''s opinion is asked for individually. The group is not brought together. The leader then makes the final decision.\",\"The leader works with the team to reach group consensus.\",\"The leader uses information they already have and makes the decision without input from the team.\"],\"stem\":\"In the consultative C1 decision style, a leader seeks individual opinions without convening the group. Which statement best describes this approach?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"With the consultative decision-making approach, the team is informed of the situation and each member''s opinion is asked for individually. The group is not brought together. The leader then makes the final decision. [III.C.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["The team is asked to give specific information, and then the leader makes the final decision.","The team is informed of the situation and each member''s opinion is asked for individually. The group is not brought together. The leader then makes the final decision.","The leader works with the team to reach group consensus.","The leader uses information they already have and makes the decision without input from the team."],"stem":"In the consultative C1 decision style, a leader seeks individual opinions without convening the group. Which statement best describes this approach?","sub":"tm","why":"With the consultative decision-making approach, the team is informed of the situation and each member''s opinion is asked for individually. The group is not brought together. The leader then makes the final decision. [III.C.3]"},"revision":"7861f42003cc142ef6aaa88eda1d37ee4ed3cbd22d93a12b92e83bacfb637620"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-556":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"design a training plan.\",\"develop the curriculum.\",\"assess the need for the training.\",\"hire a certified trainer.\"],\"stem\":\"An organization wishes to provide training to improve employee morale. The first step in developing a training program is to:\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"The first step in developing a training plan should be to assess the need for training. [III.D.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["design a training plan.","develop the curriculum.","assess the need for the training.","hire a certified trainer."],"stem":"An organization wishes to provide training to improve employee morale. The first step in developing a training program is to:","sub":"tm","why":"The first step in developing a training plan should be to assess the need for training. [III.D.1]"},"revision":"1250dff197cbd3a4a9997bcd08493bed6f7cbd96160356a0c223087ac5dfbbbb"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-557":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"periodic maintenance.\",\"corrective maintenance.\",\"reliability-based maintenance.\",\"condition-based maintenance.\"],\"stem\":\"A maintenance technician reviews the preventive maintenance checklists and notices that the lubricant level of a CNC milling machine is below the refill line. The technician then fills the tank with more lubricant. Refilling the lubricant is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Refilling the lubricant is an example of condition-based maintenance, in which maintenance tasks are performed based on operating conditions. [VIII.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["periodic maintenance.","corrective maintenance.","reliability-based maintenance.","condition-based maintenance."],"stem":"A maintenance technician reviews the preventive maintenance checklists and notices that the lubricant level of a CNC milling machine is below the refill line. The technician then fills the tank with more lubricant. Refilling the lubricant is an example of:","sub":"con","why":"Refilling the lubricant is an example of condition-based maintenance, in which maintenance tasks are performed based on operating conditions. [VIII.B.1]"},"revision":"a78eea6457ac245322fa1a713890d82283ba2860b3c118ebded65960c4aa3903"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-558":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Cross-directional\",\"Self-directed\",\"Virtual\",\"Informal\"],\"stem\":\"Which type of improvement team is least affected by geography?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"Virtual teams are least affected by geography. [III.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Cross-directional","Self-directed","Virtual","Informal"],"stem":"Which type of improvement team is least affected by geography?","sub":"tm","why":"Virtual teams are least affected by geography. [III.A.1]"},"revision":"586a2390b7f4d66ca7b6aa5c438d11815f981467a7af421dc489f318c0d93570"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-559":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"all responses are equivalent.\",\"the number of runs for each factor level is equal.\",\"there are an even number of factors.\",\"the factors are set to the same levels.\"],\"stem\":\"A balanced experimental design is one in which:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"A balanced experimental design has the same number of runs at each factor level. [VII.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["all responses are equivalent.","the number of runs for each factor level is equal.","there are an even number of factors.","the factors are set to the same levels."],"stem":"A balanced experimental design is one in which:","sub":"imp","why":"A balanced experimental design has the same number of runs at each factor level. [VII.A.2]"},"revision":"06da24966d8026536559a275ce781c524f3e9d918ce94e2d197e0636fc301d21"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-56":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Customer satisfaction will increase.\",\"Customer satisfaction will decrease.\",\"Customer satisfaction will remain steady.\",\"Customers will express delight.\"],\"stem\":\"Consistently meeting must-be requirements has what effect on customer satisfaction?\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"Consistently meeting minimum or must-be requirements will cause satisfaction to remain steady. Customer satisfaction will decrease if the must-be requirement is not met, but it will remain neutral if it is met. [IV.A.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Customer satisfaction will increase.","Customer satisfaction will decrease.","Customer satisfaction will remain steady.","Customers will express delight."],"stem":"Consistently meeting must-be requirements has what effect on customer satisfaction?","sub":"def","why":"Consistently meeting minimum or must-be requirements will cause satisfaction to remain steady. Customer satisfaction will decrease if the must-be requirement is not met, but it will remain neutral if it is met. [IV.A.3]"},"revision":"37a31c3bcee5f2e97fefb8fa19a8360478c7b3a143e86ee3ce78be7b48b00527"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-560":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"a bottleneck.\",\"overprocessing waste.\",\"work in progress (WIP).\",\"work in queue (WIQ).\"],\"stem\":\"Material that has entered the process but has not reached the output or finished stage can be defined as:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Work in progress (WIP) is the material that has entered the process but has not reached the output or finished stage. Work in queue is a component of WIP. [V.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["a bottleneck.","overprocessing waste.","work in progress (WIP).","work in queue (WIQ)."],"stem":"Material that has entered the process but has not reached the output or finished stage can be defined as:","sub":"mea","why":"Work in progress (WIP) is the material that has entered the process but has not reached the output or finished stage. Work in queue is a component of WIP. [V.A.1]"},"revision":"aa9b7fd8549941b790a76b9844ec2ed0a856734199e5749c088ae106dd642f21"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-561":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Activity\",\"Predecessor\",\"Time (days)\"],\"rows\":[[\"A\",\"—\",\"10\"],[\"B\",\"—\",\"5\"],[\"C\",\"A, B\",\"10\"],[\"D\",\"—\",\"5\"],[\"E\",\"D\",\"5\"],[\"F\",\"E\",\"5\"],[\"G\",\"C, F\",\"5\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"ABCG\",\"ACG\",\"BCG\",\"DEFG\"],\"stem\":\"Using the precedence table below, which activities make up the critical path?\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"There are three paths through the network: ACG = 10 + 10 + 5 = 25 days, BCG = 5 + 10 + 5 = 20 days, and DEFG = 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 20 days. The path with the longest duration is the critical path, which is ACG. [IV.D.5]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"chart":{"columns":["Activity","Predecessor","Time (days)"],"rows":[["A","—","10"],["B","—","5"],["C","A, B","10"],["D","—","5"],["E","D","5"],["F","E","5"],["G","C, F","5"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["ABCG","ACG","BCG","DEFG"],"stem":"Using the precedence table below, which activities make up the critical path?","sub":"def","why":"There are three paths through the network: ACG = 10 + 10 + 5 = 25 days, BCG = 5 + 10 + 5 = 20 days, and DEFG = 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 20 days. The path with the longest duration is the critical path, which is ACG. [IV.D.5]"},"revision":"5b4d646ae5c55ebe3a6d3f16eb56fefed53749a6dc2bf6733686d36f35931069"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-562":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Activity\",\"Predecessor\",\"Time (days)\"],\"rows\":[[\"A\",\"—\",\"10\"],[\"B\",\"—\",\"5\"],[\"C\",\"A, B\",\"10\"],[\"D\",\"—\",\"5\"],[\"E\",\"D\",\"5\"],[\"F\",\"E\",\"5\"],[\"G\",\"C, F\",\"5\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\