\"Design for Cost.\",\"Design for Manufacturability.\",\"Design for Test.\",\"Design for Service.\"],\"stem\":\"A new discount-retail product must meet a strict low target cost to be viable. Which Design for X method most directly addresses this objective?\",\"sub\":\"dfss\",\"why\":\"In this situation, the R&D department would likely use Design for Cost. [IX.B]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Design for Cost.","Design for Manufacturability.","Design for Test.","Design for Service."],"stem":"A new discount-retail product must meet a strict low target cost to be viable. Which Design for X method most directly addresses this objective?","sub":"dfss","why":"In this situation, the R&D department would likely use Design for Cost. [IX.B]"},"revision":"b072a47f1032802cb05201c45425fcf616022ee3002a838a9f01e8ad1e70bc06"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-614":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"0.40\",\"0.49\",\"0.60\",\"0.67\"],\"stem\":\"Assume the number of defects on a unit follows a Poisson distribution with a mean of 0.40 defects per unit. What is the first-pass yield (FPY)?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Assuming a Poisson defect count, first pass yield is the probability of zero defects: FPY = exp(−DPU) = exp(−0.40) = 0.67. [V.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["0.40","0.49","0.60","0.67"],"stem":"Assume the number of defects on a unit follows a Poisson distribution with a mean of 0.40 defects per unit. What is the first-pass yield (FPY)?","sub":"mea","why":"Assuming a Poisson defect count, first pass yield is the probability of zero defects: FPY = exp(−DPU) = exp(−0.40) = 0.67. [V.F.6]"},"revision":"1748ac9b2756a1113e6ebafd28dd9ae059f1eef8cd5e1b2817df3a9d01c901ec"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-615":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Performing\",\"Adjourning\",\"Norming\",\"Forming\"],\"stem\":\"Following turnover on a formerly high-performing team, the remaining and new members have resolved their initial conflicts and are now establishing shared norms, roles, and working relationships. At what stage is the team?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"When a high-performing team changes membership, it commonly regresses at least one development stage. In this scenario, the team is most likely to return to norming. [III.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Performing","Adjourning","Norming","Forming"],"stem":"Following turnover on a formerly high-performing team, the remaining and new members have resolved their initial conflicts and are now establishing shared norms, roles, and working relationships. At what stage is the team?","sub":"tm","why":"When a high-performing team changes membership, it commonly regresses at least one development stage. In this scenario, the team is most likely to return to norming. [III.B.2]"},"revision":"7a01004583c3e82940518318d36f61572539108096bf692b82662b616ddcdaa5"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-616":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Helps detect changes in the experimental environment over time\",\"Allows for a cubic term to be fit in the model\",\"Reduces the degrees of freedom of the error term\",\"Increases the resolution of the design\"],\"stem\":\"Besides testing for curvature, what is another advantage of adding center points to a factorial experiment?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Adding center points to a factorial design has many advantages. One of these is that it helps the analyst detect changes in the experimental environment over time. In addition, the incorporation of center points allows for testing for curvature in the model, adds to the degrees of freedom of the analysis, and gives an estimate of pure error. [VII.A.6]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Helps detect changes in the experimental environment over time","Allows for a cubic term to be fit in the model","Reduces the degrees of freedom of the error term","Increases the resolution of the design"],"stem":"Besides testing for curvature, what is another advantage of adding center points to a factorial experiment?","sub":"imp","why":"Adding center points to a factorial design has many advantages. One of these is that it helps the analyst detect changes in the experimental environment over time. In addition, the incorporation of center points allows for testing for curvature in the model, adds to the degrees of freedom of the analysis, and gives an estimate of pure error. [VII.A.6]"},"revision":"54d15a5bf702dbfc4db1dd24a6ec3c626d5c122140b89a755601af136cc0c48a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-617":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"low customer dissatisfaction if not met, but high customer satisfaction if met.\",\"high customer dissatisfaction if not met, but low customer satisfaction if met.\",\"no customer dissatisfaction if not met, but low customer satisfaction if met.\",\"no customer dissatisfaction if not met, but high customer satisfaction if met.\"],\"stem\":\"In the Kano model, delighter features are those that will cause:\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"Delighters in Kano analysis are not expected by the customer, so their absence will not adversely affect customer satisfaction. Adding a delighter will increase customer satisfaction. [IV.A.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["low customer dissatisfaction if not met, but high customer satisfaction if met.","high customer dissatisfaction if not met, but low customer satisfaction if met.","no customer dissatisfaction if not met, but low customer satisfaction if met.","no customer dissatisfaction if not met, but high customer satisfaction if met."],"stem":"In the Kano model, delighter features are those that will cause:","sub":"def","why":"Delighters in Kano analysis are not expected by the customer, so their absence will not adversely affect customer satisfaction. Adding a delighter will increase customer satisfaction. [IV.A.3]"},"revision":"2825d3f04354a98921496244091a095922794fc7e3518c841ce2bef71a36946c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-618":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Stratified sampling\",\"Convenience sampling\",\"Simple random sampling\",\"Systematic sampling\"],\"stem\":\"A Black Belt lists the vehicle identification number (VIN) of each car produced on the previous shift in a spreadsheet. They then create a macro that randomly selects 10 VINs for inspection. What type of sampling are they using?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Listing out the units in a population and then randomly choosing n units is an example of simple random sampling. [V.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Stratified sampling","Convenience sampling","Simple random sampling","Systematic sampling"],"stem":"A Black Belt lists the vehicle identification number (VIN) of each car produced on the previous shift in a spreadsheet. They then create a macro that randomly selects 10 VINs for inspection. What type of sampling are they using?","sub":"mea","why":"Listing out the units in a population and then randomly choosing n units is an example of simple random sampling. [V.B.3]"},"revision":"43a76c765ed6f68f42d423aa2d7fdd2cbb8fbe4828621645dc54aaac233a7090"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-619":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"X̄-S\",\"I-MR\",\"u\",\"p\"],\"stem\":\"Each hour, a varying number of circuit boards is inspected and each board is classified as defective or nondefective. Which chart should track the fraction defective over time?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"A p-chart monitors the fraction of nonconforming units and permits the inspected sample size to vary. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["X̄-S","I-MR","u","p"],"stem":"Each hour, a varying number of circuit boards is inspected and each board is classified as defective or nondefective. Which chart should track the fraction defective over time?","sub":"con","why":"A p-chart monitors the fraction of nonconforming units and permits the inspected sample size to vary. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"fa1cff953b81d7d027168bdb848584858dff317e39812899fd4dd600d2298b01"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-62":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":