 among technical requirements shown?\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"Pairwise relationships among technical requirements appear in the triangular roof, Area 9. [IV.A.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"chart":{"type":"house-of-quality"},"options":["Area 1","Area 2","Area 4","Area 9"],"stem":"Using the numbered house-of-quality diagram below, in which area are pairwise relationships among technical requirements shown?","sub":"def","why":"Pairwise relationships among technical requirements appear in the triangular roof, Area 9. [IV.A.3]"},"revision":"95270c1e131808175ec07032b1538a33afc7da207db8908c9814d1cd19b22e17"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-622":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"the hidden factory.\",\"poor workmanship.\",\"lean implementation.\",\"total productive maintenance.\"],\"stem\":\"Increased cycle times, reduced throughput, and increased work in process are symptoms of:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Symptoms of the hidden factory include increased cycle times, reduced throughput, and increased work in process. [V.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["the hidden factory.","poor workmanship.","lean implementation.","total productive maintenance."],"stem":"Increased cycle times, reduced throughput, and increased work in process are symptoms of:","sub":"mea","why":"Symptoms of the hidden factory include increased cycle times, reduced throughput, and increased work in process. [V.A.1]"},"revision":"8824f8a6eae82a404e5ab41ce42032f2ff41acd1be85f02d176fd321ecd42c0b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-623":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"8\",\"12\",\"16\",\"112\"],\"stem\":\"A 2⁷⁻³ fractional-factorial experiment has how many fewer runs than a 2⁷ full-factorial experiment?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"A 2⁷ full factorial requires 128 runs. The 2⁷⁻³ fraction requires 2⁴ = 16 runs, so it requires 128 − 16 = 112 fewer runs. [VII.A.5]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["8","12","16","112"],"stem":"A 2⁷⁻³ fractional-factorial experiment has how many fewer runs than a 2⁷ full-factorial experiment?","sub":"imp","why":"A 2⁷ full factorial requires 128 runs. The 2⁷⁻³ fraction requires 2⁴ = 16 runs, so it requires 128 − 16 = 112 fewer runs. [VII.A.5]"},"revision":"3caaee6bff23550c3dad396f51f326f6f3f77e11f96d78026dc0dbf88e4aeaf0"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-624":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Define, measure, analyze, design, optimize, and verify.\",\"Design, measure, analyze, define, originate, validate.\",\"Define, measure, analyze, design, optimize, and validate.\",\"Design, measure, analyze, define, originate, verify.\"],\"stem\":\"The Design for Six Sigma procedure DMADOV stands for:\",\"sub\":\"dfss\",\"why\":\"The Design for Six Sigma procedure DMADOV stands for define, measure, analyze, design, optimize, and verify. [IX.A]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Define, measure, analyze, design, optimize, and verify.","Design, measure, analyze, define, originate, validate.","Define, measure, analyze, design, optimize, and validate.","Design, measure, analyze, define, originate, verify."],"stem":"The Design for Six Sigma procedure DMADOV stands for:","sub":"dfss","why":"The Design for Six Sigma procedure DMADOV stands for define, measure, analyze, design, optimize, and verify. [IX.A]"},"revision":"64c5b4d07a0b8580a95ce28c9158416601810c3a9442abd7e2196d0fbb08db3e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-625":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"samples may become excessively large.\",\"there is a lack of objectivity.\",\"bias may result if units display a natural cyclic pattern.\",\"identifying units to sample may be difficult.\"],\"stem\":\"A disadvantage of systematic sampling is that:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"A disadvantage of systematic sampling is that bias may result if units display a natural cyclic pattern. The systematic samples may capture the same position on the cycle each time. [V.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["samples may become excessively large.","there is a lack of objectivity.","bias may result if units display a natural cyclic pattern.","identifying units to sample may be difficult."],"stem":"A disadvantage of systematic sampling is that:","sub":"mea","why":"A disadvantage of systematic sampling is that bias may result if units display a natural cyclic pattern. The systematic samples may capture the same position on the cycle each time. [V.B.3]"},"revision":"e3d844517b51ae16402c34c704c12ffc6a21aa3bde2bbde38a47cc9b23ba7d18"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-626":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"40.16\",\"49.00\",\"67.24\",\"69.83\"],\"stem\":\"A process output is normally distributed with a mean of 53.70 and standard deviation of 8.23. Use the standard normal lower-tail value z = −1.645 for 5%. What lower specification limit will result in 5% of the units falling below the specification?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Convert the 5th-percentile z value to the process scale: LSL = μ + zσ = 53.70 − 1.645(8.23) = 40.16. [V.E.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["40.16","49.00","67.24","69.83"],"stem":"A process output is normally distributed with a mean of 53.70 and standard deviation of 8.23. Use the standard normal lower-tail value z = −1.645 for 5%. What lower specification limit will result in 5% of the units falling below the specification?","sub":"mea","why":"Convert the 5th-percentile z value to the process scale: LSL = μ + zσ = 53.70 − 1.645(8.23) = 40.16. [V.E.2]"},"revision":"d7145d3a41d2225d57ef091dbc1927bcb645974b6fa0ee379af1ec6c54aaad08"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-627":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"cash flow.\",\"cycle time.\",\"employee satisfaction.\",\"defect rates.\"],\"stem\":\"A common metric in the learning and growth perspective of a balanced scorecard is:\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"Employee satisfaction is a common metric in the learning and growth perspective of a balanced scorecard. [II.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["cash flow.","cycle time.","employee satisfaction.","defect rates."],"stem":"A common metric in the learning and growth perspective of a balanced scorecard is:","sub":"p2","why":"Employee satisfaction is a common metric in the learning and growth perspective of a balanced scorecard. [II.C.1]"},"revision":"b295df792b1054ff36db80471d7993974736b0062499dfce02e051b80e62b6ac"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-628":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"5 subgroups.\",\"25 subgroups.\",\"15 subgroups.\",\"50 subgroups.\"],\"stem\":\"Trial control limits are calculated based on at least:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Trial control limits for an SPC chart are calculated based on at least 25 subgroups. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["5 subgroups.","25 subgroups.","15 subgroups.","50 subgroups."],"stem":"Trial control limits are calculated based on at least:","sub":"con","why":"Trial control limits for an SPC chart are calculated based on at least 25 subgroups. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"255f85a794c12874bce46ef9b5d489abf035d3470a3dd49bef24d95aa9cc14e1"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-629":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"24\",\"25\",\"418\",\"419\"],\"stem\":\"Using the guide''s finite-population approximation n = N/(1 + NE²), where N = 1,264 customers and E = 0.04, what minimum number of responses is required?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Apply the finite-population sample-size formula n = N/(1 + NE²): n = 1,264/[1 + 1,264(0.04)²] = 418.21. Round up to ensure the margin-of-error requirement, giving 419. [V.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["24","25","418","419"],"stem":"Using the guide''s finite-population approximation n = N/(1 + NE²), where N = 1,264 customers and E = 0.04, what minimum number of responses is required?","sub":"mea","why":"Apply the finite-population sample-size formula n = N/(1 + NE²): n = 1,264/[1 + 1,264(0.04)²] = 418.21. Round up to ensure the margin-of-error requirement, giving 419. [V.B.4]"},"revision":"74cb413ca8994060fd7dc97c1714854c677fb16e5c93bde5a22bdbc027ced442"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-63":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Dimensional method\",\"Functional method\",\"SMART method\",\"PERT method\"],\"stem\":\"Which project scoping method emphasizes the relationship between process inputs and outputs?\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"The functional method for characterizing a project scope emphasizes the relationship between input and output variables. This is also referred to as Y = f(X). [IV.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Dimensional method","Functional method","SMART method","PERT method"],"stem":"Which project scoping method emphasizes the relationship between process inputs and outputs?","sub":"def","why":"The functional method for characterizing a project scope emphasizes the relationship between input and output variables. This is also referred to as Y = f(X). [IV.B.3]"},"revision":"982ab34e83f0ba293afac5d9f811b5edea88ed2361fd287c597daa171787ab1d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-630":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Standard deviation\",\"Range\",\"Mode\",\"None is acceptable.\"],\"stem\":\"What is an acceptable measure of dispersion to use for data that is on the nominal measurement scale?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"There is no accepted measure of dispersion for data on the nominal measurement scale because nominal categories have no meaningful order. The mode may be used as a measure of central tendency. [V.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Standard deviation","Range","Mode","None is acceptable."],"stem":"What is an acceptable measure of dispersion to use for data that is on the nominal measurement scale?","sub":"mea","why":"There is no accepted measure of dispersion for data on the nominal measurement scale because nominal categories have no meaningful order. The mode may be used as a measure of central tendency. [V.B.2]"},"revision":"dfa1f256a9752edd248fa36a2e744fb1cb56efcebf4a52f4d605b038339b0ab1"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-631":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"may give competitors access to sensitive data.\",\"is limited by the company''s culture, norms, and history.\",\"always eliminates competition among departments.\",\"depends on data that is difficult to collect.\"],\"stem\":\"One of the drawbacks of internal benchmarking is that it:\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"One of the drawbacks of internal benchmarking is that it is limited by the company''s culture, norms, and history. [II.B]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["may give competitors access to sensitive data.","is limited by the company''s culture, norms, and history.","always eliminates competition among departments.","depends on data that is difficult to collect."],"stem":"One of the drawbacks of internal benchmarking is that it:","sub":"p2","why":"One of the drawbacks of internal benchmarking is that it is limited by the company''s culture, norms, and history. [II.B]"},"revision":"31a00fd50546b7e8257a3ee2ffec87cd886f9293c6cfd998c757541f72c59106"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-632":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"producing defects.\",\"increasing cycle time.\",\"creating inventory.\",\"reducing availability.\"],\"stem\":\"Heijunka methods offer a tradeoff between leveling production and:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Heijunka methods offer a tradeoff between leveling production and creating inventory. [VII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["producing defects.","increasing cycle time.","creating inventory.","reducing availability."],"stem":"Heijunka methods offer a tradeoff between leveling production and:","sub":"imp","why":"Heijunka methods offer a tradeoff between leveling production and creating inventory. [VII.B.2]"},"revision":"11fac293099de179033cab333095f5a95aba9e3af8a1a7ebcf6d0b5a4607b1e8"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-633":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"0.9605\",\"0.9615\",\"0.9685\",\"0.9915\"],\"stem\":\"Two thousand parts were produced. Sixty-two were sent off for rework and 17 were scrapped. What is the first pass yield?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"First-pass yield excludes units requiring rework or scrap: FPY = (2,000 − 62 − 17)/2,000 = 0.9605. [V.F.6]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["0.9605","0.9615","0.9685","0.9915"],"stem":"Two thousand parts were produced. Sixty-two were sent off for rework and 17 were scrapped. What is the first pass yield?","sub":"mea","why":"First-pass yield excludes units requiring rework or scrap: FPY = (2,000 − 62 − 17)/2,000 = 0.9605. [V.F.6]"},"revision":"7fce944e178f8707eda88bdfcd9ababdce3596178b56576e9fb6df57183a7644"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-634":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Responsible\",\"Accountable\",\"Consulted\",\"Informed\"],\"stem\":\"Which RACI role is ultimately answerable for the result and has final approval authority?\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"Accountable is ultimately answerable for the result and provides final approval; Responsible performs the work. [IV.C.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Responsible","Accountable","Consulted","Informed"],"stem":"Which RACI role is ultimately answerable for the result and has final approval authority?","sub":"def","why":"Accountable is ultimately answerable for the result and provides final approval; Responsible performs the work. [IV.C.4]"},"revision":"00ec451f59ab30aa6a92c42acebe55533d57317928895e3e94be3950599a7f70"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-635":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"0.84\",\"1.19\",\"2.49\",\"6.20\"],\"stem\":\"A gage R&R study was conducted on a tensile tester. The equipment variation is equal to 2.01, and the gage R&R equals 3.20. What is the value of the reproducibility?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Gage R&R combines equipment variation and appraiser variation: GRR² = EV² + AV². Therefore, AV = √(3.20² − 2.01²) = √6.1999 = 2.49. [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["0.84","1.19","2.49","6.20"],"stem":"A gage R&R study was conducted on a tensile tester. The equipment variation is equal to 2.01, and the gage R&R equals 3.20. What is the value of the reproducibility?","sub":"mea","why":"Gage R&R combines equipment variation and appraiser variation: GRR² = EV² + AV². Therefore, AV = √(3.20² − 2.01²) = √6.1999 = 2.49. [V.C.1]"},"revision":"8ee65afe2c8bc90f92d64741bcadf6b8c672464b7e020261de768d5bdd1c8319"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-636":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Nominal\",\"Interval\",\"Ratio\",\"Ordinal\"],\"stem\":\"Temperature recorded in degrees Celsius is an example of which scale of measurement?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Temperature measured in either Celsius or Fahrenheit is an example of an interval measurement scale. The values can be ordered, and the difference between values is meaningful, but there is no true zero. [V.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Nominal","Interval","Ratio","Ordinal"],"stem":"Temperature recorded in degrees Celsius is an example of which scale of measurement?","sub":"mea","why":"Temperature measured in either Celsius or Fahrenheit is an example of an interval measurement scale. The values can be ordered, and the difference between values is meaningful, but there is no true zero. [V.B.2]"},"revision":"93e9318b4c13021751efd9bc73a21dbfbc734e5f81cafd0cd4d9d3392519ea20"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-637":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Simple random sampling\",\"Block sampling\",\"Systematic sampling\",\"Stratified sampling\"],\"stem\":\"A quality inspector chooses every 10th pallet and then samples from the first layer of the chosen pallet. What type of sampling scheme is being used?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Choosing every 10th pallet and then sampling from the first layer of the pallet is an example of systematic sampling. [V.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Simple random sampling","Block sampling","Systematic sampling","Stratified sampling"],"stem":"A quality inspector chooses every 10th pallet and then samples from the first layer of the chosen pallet. What type of sampling scheme is being used?","sub":"mea","why":"Choosing every 10th pallet and then sampling from th