075. What is the interpretation of the p-value?","sub":"ana","why":"A p-value is calculated under the assumption that H₀ is true. It is the probability of observing the observed result, or a more extreme result. A small p-value provides evidence against H₀; it does not prove that H₁ is correct. [VI.B.1]"},"revision":"57622f565a30393d737f372041be4caf44b325d302b6480926d0303a29564895"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-648":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"It applies to quality characteristics that are continuous measures.\",\"It gives a poor overall quality picture.\",\"It can be used to track defects.\",\"It can be used for varying sample sizes.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following statements concerning the p-chart is true?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"The p-chart can be used for tracking defectives when sample size varies. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["It applies to quality characteristics that are continuous measures.","It gives a poor overall quality picture.","It can be used to track defects.","It can be used for varying sample sizes."],"stem":"Which of the following statements concerning the p-chart is true?","sub":"con","why":"The p-chart can be used for tracking defectives when sample size varies. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"2ed35bd60de9cef4ae23462db8968da893c7b502afa385ca7763863b2678aed0"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-649":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Coaching\",\"Delegating\",\"Directing\",\"Supporting\"],\"stem\":\"Which leadership approach is best used for team members with medium competence but low commitment?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"For team members with moderate competence but low commitment, the team leader should use the coaching approach. [III.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Coaching","Delegating","Directing","Supporting"],"stem":"Which leadership approach is best used for team members with medium competence but low commitment?","sub":"tm","why":"For team members with moderate competence but low commitment, the team leader should use the coaching approach. [III.B.4]"},"revision":"b8024727644fae73e52cd5011403fe948cc62c3f3738d312f91a19ba78b1652a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-65":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"The goal statement meets all SMART criteria.\",\"The goal statement is missing relevance.\",\"The goal statement is missing a time-bound deadline.\",\"The goal statement is not measurable.\"],\"stem\":\"A Six Sigma team is pursuing a project to increase throughput on an injection-molding line. Throughput was formerly 600 pieces per hour but has dropped to 500 pieces per hour. Evaluate this SMART goal: “Increase throughput to 600 pieces per hour.”\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"SMART goals are specific, measurable, actionable, relevant, and time-bound. “Increase throughput to 600 pieces per hour” gives a measurable target but no deadline for achieving it. [IV.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["The goal statement meets all SMART criteria.","The goal statement is missing relevance.","The goal statement is missing a time-bound deadline.","The goal statement is not measurable."],"stem":"A Six Sigma team is pursuing a project to increase throughput on an injection-molding line. Throughput was formerly 600 pieces per hour but has dropped to 500 pieces per hour. Evaluate this SMART goal: “Increase throughput to 600 pieces per hour.”","sub":"def","why":"SMART goals are specific, measurable, actionable, relevant, and time-bound. “Increase throughput to 600 pieces per hour” gives a measurable target but no deadline for achieving it. [IV.B.4]"},"revision":"2dab3c81f2566aa879fedc982660618cc7195071d7ef0ff6a007c6ed8a0b2845"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-650":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"chart\":{\"type\":\"precision-accuracy\"},\"options\":[\"Quadrant A\",\"Quadrant B\",\"Quadrant C\",\"Quadrant D\"],\"stem\":\"Which quadrant of the figure shows low precision and low accuracy?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Quadrant C displays low precision (scattered points) and low accuracy (not centered on the target). [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"chart":{"type":"precision-accuracy"},"options":["Quadrant A","Quadrant B","Quadrant C","Quadrant D"],"stem":"Which quadrant of the figure shows low precision and low accuracy?","sub":"mea","why":"Quadrant C displays low precision (scattered points) and low accuracy (not centered on the target). [V.C.1]"},"revision":"10b31af0d988eb93d6611f733aa6303b6e043b65b65ba4ab1ec0bc4379d83668"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-651":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"chart\":{\"type\":\"precision-accuracy\"},\"options\":[\"Quadrant A\",\"Quadrant B\",\"Quadrant C\",\"Quadrant D\"],\"stem\":\"Which quadrant in the figure shows high precision and low accuracy?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Quadrant D displays high precision (tightly clustered points) and low accuracy (off the target). [V.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"chart":{"type":"precision-accuracy"},"options":["Quadrant A","Quadrant B","Quadrant C","Quadrant D"],"stem":"Which quadrant in the figure shows high precision and low accuracy?","sub":"mea","why":"Quadrant D displays high precision (tightly clustered points) and low accuracy (off the target). [V.C.1]"},"revision":"1a77914b642d8ff67284d2e4d61f3c4113bd991dcb30f46724e437a210560546"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-652":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Availability, performance, and quality\",\"Availability, cycle time, and gage R&R\",\"Takt time, inventory, and defects\",\"Work in process, cycle time, and quality\"],\"stem\":\"OEE is a metric that combines which three measures?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Overall equipment effectiveness combines availability, performance, and quality: OEE = A × P × Q. [VII.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Availability, performance, and quality","Availability, cycle time, and gage R&R","Takt time, inventory, and defects","Work in process, cycle time, and quality"],"stem":"OEE is a metric that combines which three measures?","sub":"imp","why":"Overall equipment effectiveness combines availability, performance, and quality: OEE = A × P × Q. [VII.B.4]"},"revision":"a8ad3b12a7acf600ae9de2f142edd4f800a0d5511668459314d1ae69ad5ecb02"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-653":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Source\",\"Sum of Squares\",\"df\",\"Mean Square\",\"F-statistic\"],\"rows\":[[\"Factor A\",\"344\",\"3\",\"114.67\",\"7.45\"],[\"Factor B\",\"234\",\"4\",\"58.5\",\"3.80\"],[\"AB Interaction\",\"120\",\"12\",\"10\",\"?\"],[\"Error\",\"77\",\"5\",\"15.4\",\"\"],[\"Total\",\"775\",\"24\",\"\",\"\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"0.65\",\"1.54\",\"2.23\",\"2.86\"],\"stem\":\"Given the two-way ANOVA table below, what is the F-statistic for the AB interaction?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"For the interaction, df(A×B) = df(A) × df(B) = 3 × 4 = 12, so MS(A×B) = SS(A×B)/df(A×B) = 120/12 = 10. The error mean square is MS(error) = 77/5 = 15.4. Therefore, F(A×B) = MS(A×B)/MS(error) = 10/15.4 = 0.65. [VI.B.6]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"columns":["Source","Sum of Squares","df","Mean Square","F-statistic"],"rows":[["Factor A","344","3","114.67","7.45"],["Factor B","234","4","58.5","3.80"],["AB Interaction","120","12","10","?"],["Error","77","5","15.4",""],["Total","775","24","",""]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["0.65","1.54","2.23","2.86"],"stem":"Given the two-way ANOVA table below, what is the F-statistic for the AB interaction?","sub":"ana","why":"For the interaction, df(A×B) = df(A) × df(B) = 3 × 4 = 12, so MS(A×B) = SS(A×B)/df(A×B) = 120/12 = 10. The error mean square is MS(error) = 77/5 = 15.4. Therefore, F(A×B) = MS(A×B)/MS(error) = 10/15.4 = 0.65. [VI.B.6]"},"revision":"6808011dc03d112db95b269a83e183e663e636b845acc797719c1bc6a34f49d1"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-654":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"noncritical items.\",\"bottleneck items.\",\"strategic items.\",\"leverage items.\"],\"stem\":\"In a supplier risk analysis, items that are classified as low supply-risk but high profit-impact are considered:\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"In a supplier risk analysis, items that are classified as low supply-risk but high profit-impact are considered leverage items. [VI.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["noncritical items.","bottleneck items.","strategic items.","leverage items."],"stem":"In a supplier risk analysis, items that are classified as low supply-risk but high profit-impact are considered:","sub":"ana","why":"In a supplier risk analysis, items that are classified as low supply-risk but high profit-impact are considered leverage items. [VI.C.1]"},"revision":"b17389628795f74db0be845a2d318698be29466f2e7df37dbdcf492cae46a994"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-655":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Six Sigma projects cost more to implement.\",\"In a Six Sigma project, the solution is not known in advance.\",\"Six Sigma projects use teams.\",\"Six Sigma projects use Gantt charts to track progress.\"],\"stem\":\"What sets Six Sigma projects apart from other types of projects?\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"In a Six Sigma project, the solution is not known in advance. This can add complexity to the project management effort. [I.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Six Sigma projects cost more to implement.","In a Six Sigma project, the solution is not known in advance.","Six Sigma projects use teams.","Six Sigma projects use Gantt charts to track progress."],"stem":"What sets Six Sigma projects apart from other types of projects?","sub":"p1","why":"In a Six Sigma project, the solution is not known in advance. This can add complexity to the project management effort. [I.A.2]"},"revision":"944c09244b514504b77706f512f87642ef7d733d85388d4a0400fd22df4e1617"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-656":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Availability\",\"Performance\",\"Quality\",\"Kaizen\"],\"stem\":\"SMED can directly help increase which component of OEE?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"SMED, or quick changeover, reduces setup time and therefore increases equipment availability. [VII.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Availability","Performance","Quality","Kaizen"],"stem":"SMED can directly help increase which component of OEE?","sub":"imp","why":"SMED, or quick changeover, reduces setup time and therefore increases equipment availability. [VII.B.4]"},"revision":"6e7efe30ee3c3bcbb88134bec003b972a71bf33203cdf3d70fa033644b2c3610"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-657":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"X̿\",\"904.0\"],[\"S̄\",\"32.5\"],[\"n\",\"20\"],[\"A₃ for n = 20\",\"0.680\"],[\"B₄ for n = 20\",\"1.490\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"909.9\",\"920.9\",\"926.1\",\"952.4\"],\"stem\":\"Calculate the upper control limit for the X̄ chart in an X̄-S chart using X̿ = 904.0, S̄ = 32.5, and subgroup size n = 20.\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"For subgroup size n = 20, A₃ = 0.680. The X̄-chart upper control limit is UCL = X̿ + A₃S̄ = 904.0 + 0.680(32.5) = 926.1. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["X̿","904.0"],["S̄","32.5"],["n","20"],["A₃ for n = 20","0.680"],["B₄ for n = 20","1.490"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["909.9","920.9","926.1","952.4"],"stem":"Calculate the upper control limit for the X̄ chart in an X̄-S chart using X̿ = 904.0, S̄ = 32.5, and subgroup size n = 20.","sub":"con","why":"For subgroup size n = 20, A₃ = 0.680. The X̄-chart upper control limit is UCL = X̿ + A₃S̄ = 904.0 + 0.680(32.5) = 926.1. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"0ec8ad1aa92bb43448dc8314e0688cd08fc2cf7d641cb56fcf72519351c10469"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-658":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"z₀ = −1.32, critical z = −1.645; do not reject H₀.\",\"z₀ = −1.32, critical z = −1.960; reject H₀.\",\"z₀ = −1.65, critical z = −1.645; reject H₀.\",\"z₀ = −0.42, critical z = −1.645; do not reject H₀.\"],\"stem\":\"Before an improvement project, the defective rate for a milling process was 4.9%. After the changes, 1,000 parts were run and 40 were defective (4.0%). Using the normal approximation, test H₀: p = 0.049 versus H₁: p is less than 0.049 at α = 0.05. Use critical z = −1.645.\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"The normal approximation is valid because np₀ = 49 and n(1 − p₀) = 951 are both at least 10. z₀ = (0.040 − 0.049)/√[0.049(0.951)/1,000] = −1.32. Because −1.32 is not below the lower-tail critical value −1.645, do not reject H₀; there is insufficient evidence that the defective rate decreased. [VI.B.5]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["z₀ = −1.32, critical z = −1.645; do not reject H₀.","z₀ = −1.32, critical z = −1.960; reject H₀.","z₀ = −1.65, critical z = −1.645; reject H₀.","z₀ = −0.42, critical z = −1.645; do not reject H₀."],"stem":"Before an improvement project, the defective rate for a milling process was 4.9%. After the changes, 1,000 parts were run and 40 were defective (4.0%). Using the normal approximation, test H₀: p = 0.049 versus H₁: p is less than 0.049 at α = 0.05. Use critical z = −1.645.","sub":"ana","why":"The normal approximation is valid because np₀ = 49 and n(1 − p₀) = 951 are both at least 10. z₀ = (0.040 − 0.049)/√[0.049(0.951)/1,000] = −1.32. Because −1.32 is not below the lower-tail critical value −1.645, do not reject H₀; there is insufficient evidence that the defective rate decreased. [VI.B.5]"},"revision":"ea22e2b734419299f1a36ad744f4a4bac97b914836166e88fff7fceb0dd4c465"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-659":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Target, terminate, treat, or transfer\",\"Tolerate, terminate, treat, or transfer\",\"Temper, treat, transfer, or tighten\",\"Tolerate, tailor, transfer, or treat\"],\"stem\":\"Once an organization identifies a risk, it can choose one of which four actions?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Once an organization identifies a risk, it can choose to tolerate, terminate, treat, or transfer the risk. [VI.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Target, terminate, treat, or transfer","Tolerate, terminate, treat, or transfer","Temper, treat, transfer, or tighten","Tolerate, tailor, transfer, or treat"],"stem":"Once an organization identifies a risk, it can choose one of which four actions?","sub":"ana","why":"Once an organization identifies a risk, it can choose to tolerate, terminate, treat, or transfer the risk. [VI.C.1]"},"revision":"43b8fc8faee8facc716ed087a31c1dc0a0a495ea3800b78c881c328a6dddc8ff"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-66":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Work in queue (WIQ) and work being processed\",\"Finished goods and customer returns\",\"Finished goods and material not yet released to the process\",\"Work in queue (WIQ) and finished goods ready for shipment\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following items are components of work in progress (WIP)?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Work in progress (WIP) includes material that has entered the process but has not reached the finished-output stage. It includes both work in queue (WIQ) and work being processed. [V.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Work in queue (WIQ) and work being processed","Finished goods and customer returns","Finished goods and material not yet released to the process","Work in queue (WIQ) and finished goods ready for shipment"],"stem":"Which of the following items are components of work in progress (WIP)?","sub":"mea","why":"Work in progress (WIP) includes material that has entered the process but has not reached the finished-output stage. It includes both work in queue (WIQ) and work being processed. [V.A.1]"},"revision":"a872c3e906c7fbbf140740089fbc7e3e22a73ff2e05f4c874b208433b4de8120"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-660":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Opportunities and strengths\",\"Weaknesses and threats\",\"Opportunities and threats\",\"Strengths and weaknesses\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following categories in a SWOT analysis are considered internal to the organization?\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"The acronym SWOT stands for strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats. The categories that are considered internal to the organization are strengths and weaknesses. [I.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Opportunities and strengths","Weaknesses and threats","Opportunities and threats","Strengths and weaknesses"],"stem":"Which of the following categories in a SWOT analysis are considered internal to the organization?","sub":"p1","why":"The acronym SWOT stands for strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats. The categories that are considered internal to the organization are strengths and weaknesses. [I.A.4]"},"revision":"5a096b9fbe5bc4db6bb0e28cc2447ac0a10fa1fd70ac33726510dac2d543de83"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-661":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"\",\"Part A\",\"Part B\",\"Part C\"],\"rows\":[[\"Last year''s percentage\",\"25%\",\"50%\",\"25%\"],[\"Last month''s observed units\",\"340\",\"600\",\"360\"],[\"Last month''s expected units\",\"325.0\",\"650.0\",\"325.0\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"χ² = 8.308; χ² critical value = 11.070\",\"χ² = 8.231; χ² critical value = 7.378\",\"χ² = 8.308; χ² critical value = 5.991\",\"χ² = 8.231; χ² critical value = 12.833\"],\"stem\":\"A job shop makes three metal parts. Test whether last month''s product mix differed from last year''s percentages using the table below. At α = 0.05 with df = 2, use χ² critical value = 5.991. What are the test statistic and critical value?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Under H₀, the historical mix applies, so the expected counts are 325, 650, and 325. The goodness-of-fit statistic is χ² = (340 − 325)²/325 + (600 − 650)²/650 + (360 − 325)²/325 = 8.308. With 2 degrees of freedom, χ² critical value = 5.991 at α = 0.05. Because 8.308 > 5.991, reject H₀; the product mix has changed. [VI.B.7]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"chart":{"columns":["","Part A","Part B","Part C"],"rows":[["Last year''s percentage","25%","50%","25%"],["Last month''s observed units","340","600","360"],["Last month''s expected units","325.0","650.0","325.0"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["χ² = 8.308; χ² critical value = 11.070","χ² = 8.231; χ² critical value = 7.378","χ² = 8.308; χ² critical value = 5.991","χ² = 8.231; χ² critical value = 12.833"],"stem":"A job shop makes three metal parts. Test whether last month''s product mix differed from last year''s percentages using the table below. At α = 0.05 with df = 2, use χ² critical value = 5.991. What are the test statistic and critical value?","sub":"ana","why":"Under H₀, the historical mix applies, so the expected counts are 325, 650, and 325. The goodness-of-fit statistic is χ² = (340 − 325)²/325 + (600 − 650)²/650 + (360 − 325)²/325 = 8.308. With 2 degrees of freedom, χ² critical value = 5.991 at α = 0.05. Because 8.308 > 5.991, reject H₀; the product mix has changed. [VI.B.7]"},"revision":"43507ae25a59a1282519bafba1a9da59f1e172d8652e2f3fb987c62426e4857a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-662":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"affinity diagram.\",\"Gantt chart.\",\"FMEA.\",\"control plan.\"],\"stem\":\"Work breakdown structures serve as inputs into tools such as the:\",\"sub\":\"def\",\"why\":\"A work breakdown structure serves as an input to a Gantt chart. [IV.C.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["affinity diagram.","Gantt chart.","FMEA.","control plan."],"stem":"Work breakdown structures serve as inputs into tools such as the:","sub":"def","why":"A work breakdown structure serves as an input to a Gantt chart. [IV.C.3]"},"revision":"4047babdbc6e6d4f950d7a5d05f9f3881137dcb0a867e13d2435b54bfb77f874"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-663":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"some main effects are confounded with two-factor interactions.\",\"some main effects are confounded with three-factor interactions.\",\"some two-factor interactions are confounded with three-factor interactions.\",\"some two-factor interactions are confounded with the mean.\"],\"stem\":\"In a Resolution V experiment:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"In a Resolution V design, some two-factor interactions are confounded with three-factor interactions. [VII.A.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["some main effects are confounded with two-factor interactions.","some main effects are confounded with three-factor interactions.","some two-factor interactions are confounded with three-factor interactions.","some two-factor interactions are confounded with the mean."],"stem":"In a Resolution V experiment:","sub":"imp","why":"In a Resolution V design, some two-factor interactions are confounded with three-factor interactions. [VII.A.5]"},"revision":"2a7050476f67d3ade3702d685856a3f8496019b6d9090fd99f213b0c4edbbb10"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-664":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Delegating\",\"Coaching\",\"Directing\",\"Supporting\"],\"stem\":\"Which leadership style is best used for a team member who has low competence but high commitment?\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"For team members with low competence but high commitment, the directing leadership approach is best. [III.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Delegating","Coaching","Directing","Supporting"],"stem":"Which leadership style is best used for a team member who has low competence but high commitment?","sub":"tm","why":"For team members with low competence but high commitment, the directing leadership approach is best. [III.B.4]"},"revision":"6c5a9cf62581bfbf00442ae87527bf16a37fbff3b59c539f8eb7d2d5f4ad874c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-665":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Breakdowns and setup and changeover time\",\"Idling and minor stoppages\",\"Startup losses\",\"Defects, rework, and startup losses\"],\"stem\":\"Which pair of the Six Big Losses is associated with equipment performance?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"The losses of idling and minor stoppages are associated with equipment performance. [VII.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Breakdowns and setup and changeover time","Idling and minor stoppages","Startup losses","Defects, rework, and startup losses"],"stem":"Which pair of the Six Big Losses is associated with equipment performance?","sub":"imp","why":"The losses of idling and minor stoppages are associated with equipment performance. [VII.B.4]"},"revision":"6932fd9e900428a3eee1219ae97f11b5c5aa977d27e5d2637375ad2c5c5b89c6"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-666":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Continuously hunt for network intrusions.\",\"Update and upgrade all software immediately.\",\"Leverage modern hardware security features.\",\"Transition to multifactor authentication.\"],\"stem\":\"Which listed cybersecurity strategy directly searches for active network intrusions rather than hardening systems or strengthening authentication?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"An organization may continuously hunt for network intrusions to respond to a cybersecurity threat. [VI.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Continuously hunt for network intrusions.","Update and upgrade all software immediately.","Leverage modern hardware security features.","Transition to multifactor authentication."],"stem":"Which listed cybersecurity strategy directly searches for active network intrusions rather than hardening systems or strengthening authentication?","sub":"ana","why":"An organization may continuously hunt for network intrusions to respond to a cybersecurity threat. [VI.C.1]"},"revision":"61a391b6ef7a6978defa6a22e94a9f43d75b84f78d138990dda2ef936f19f76f"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-667":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"t₀ = 1.13; do not reject H₀.\",\"z₀ = 1.13; do not reject H₀.\",\"t₀ = 2.52; reject H₀.\",\"z₀ = 2.52; reject H₀.\"],\"stem\":\"A new electric vehicle is evaluated for its maximum range on a single charge. Five vehicles produced the mileage results {265, 252, 255, 260, 251}. Assuming mileage is normally distributed, test H₀: μ ≤ 250 versus H₁: μ is greater than 250 at α = 0.05. Use the one-tailed critical value t(0.05, 4) = 2.132.\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Because the population standard deviation is unknown and n = 5, use a one-sample Student''s t test. The sample mean is x̄ = 256.60 and the sample standard deviation is s = 5.86, so t₀ = (256.60 − 250)/(5.86/√5) = 2.52. Since 2.52 exceeds 2.132, reject H₀; the data provide evidence that the average range exceeds 250 miles. [VI.B.5]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["t₀ = 1.13; do not reject H₀.","z₀ = 1.13; do not reject H₀.","t₀ = 2.52; reject H₀.","z₀ = 2.52; reject H₀."],"stem":"A new electric vehicle is evaluated for its maximum range on a single charge. Five vehicles produced the mileage results {265, 252, 255, 260, 251}. Assuming mileage is normally distributed, test H₀: μ ≤ 250 versus H₁: μ is greater than 250 at α = 0.05. Use the one-tailed critical value t(0.05, 4) = 2.132.","sub":"ana","why":"Because the population standard deviation is unknown and n = 5, use a one-sample Student''s t test. The sample mean is x̄ = 256.60 and the sample standard deviation is s = 5.86, so t₀ = (256.60 − 250)/(5.86/√5) = 2.52. Since 2.52 exceeds 2.132, reject H₀; the data provide evidence that the average range exceeds 250 miles. [VI.B.5]"},"revision":"3c179fcb869c0bdfd0df0f43aca7d5f61497d90504135cee243d33cbc4a60fe1"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-668":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"The alpha risk will increase.\",\"The beta risk will increase.\",\"The capability index will decrease.\",\"Special cause variation will decrease.\"],\"stem\":\"What might be the effect of using 2σ control limits on an X̄ chart?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Narrowing X̄-chart limits from 3σ to 2σ increases the false-alarm probability α from about 0.0027 to 0.0455. The in-control average run length is ARL₀ = 1/α, so it falls from about 1/0.0027 = 370 subgroups to 1/0.0455 = 22 subgroups. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["The alpha risk will increase.","The beta risk will increase.","The capability index will decrease.","Special cause variation will decrease."],"stem":"What might be the effect of using 2σ control limits on an X̄ chart?","sub":"con","why":"Narrowing X̄-chart limits from 3σ to 2σ increases the false-alarm probability α from about 0.0027 to 0.0455. The in-control average run length is ARL₀ = 1/α, so it falls from about 1/0.0027 = 370 subgroups to 1/0.0455 = 22 subgroups. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"9efd8b1ccb3b31b86705595c00e57beec5e8e157968c40c1094ffe8882882ede"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-669":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"The null hypothesis is true with probability 0.675.\",\"The probability of the alternative hypothesis being true is 0.325.\",\"There is insufficient evidence to reject the null hypothesis.\",\"There is sufficient evidence to accept the null hypothesis.\"],\"stem\":\"A Black Belt conducts a hypothesis test using a Student’s t statistic and α = 0.05. The p-value is 0.325. Which statement is true?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Because p = 0.325 is greater than α = 0.05, there is insufficient evidence to reject H₀. A p-value does not give the probability that either hypothesis is true, and failing to reject H₀ is not the same as accepting or proving it. [VI.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["The null hypothesis is true with probability 0.675.","The probability of the alternative hypothesis being true is 0.325.","There is insufficient evidence to reject the null hypothesis.","There is sufficient evidence to accept the null hypothesis."],"stem":"A Black Belt conducts a hypothesis test using a Student’s t statistic and α = 0.05. The p-value is 0.325. Which statement is true?","sub":"ana","why":"Because p = 0.325 is greater than α = 0.05, there is insufficient evidence to reject H₀. A p-value does not give the probability that either hypothesis is true, and failing to reject H₀ is not the same as accepting or proving it. [VI.B.1]"},"revision":"a397bb6b164055bcff59c59c1dce925fec63a93272e43c2c8d2108f48db3e411"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-67":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"cycle time.\",\"setup time.\",\"touch time.\",\"takt time.\"],\"stem\":\"The time during which work is actively performed on a unit at a process step is defined as:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Touch time is the time during which work is actively performed on a unit. It should not be confused with the time required for a process cycle or with total end-to-end lead time. [V.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["cycle time.","setup time.","touch time.","takt time."],"stem":"The time during which work is actively performed on a unit at a process step is defined as:","sub":"mea","why":"Touch time is the time during which work is actively performed on a unit. It should not be confused with the time required for a process cycle or with total end-to-end lead time. [V.A.1]"},"revision":"02b17b41f702c99e9387574099b677ee6d2846e0b0d0f0a29dc260d0f0717cae"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-670":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"21\",\"25\",\"34\",\"48\"],\"stem\":\"After adjustments to a packaging line, a Six Sigma professional wants a 95% confidence interval for the mean fill weight with a margin of error of 1 g. The historical process standard deviation is σ = 3.5 g. Use z* = 1.96. What minimum sample size is required?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Use n = (z*σ/E)² = (1.96 × 3.5/1)² = 47.06. Round up to the next whole observation, giving n = 48. [VI.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["21","25","34","48"],"stem":"After adjustments to a packaging line, a Six Sigma professional wants a 95% confidence interval for the mean fill weight with a margin of error of 1 g. The historical process standard deviation is σ = 3.5 g. Use z* = 1.96. What minimum sample size is required?","sub":"ana","why":"Use n = (z*σ/E)² = (1.96 × 3.5/1)² = 47.06. Round up to the next whole observation, giving n = 48. [VI.B.3]"},"revision":"86784a53421cb34df5d30d011dd68a6175fe6f588f9d56809da98302b199e613"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-671":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"The team is consulted to obtain specific information and then the leader makes the final decision.\",\"The team is informed of the situation and each member''s opinion is asked for individually. The group is not brought together. The leader then makes the final decision.\",\"The leader works with the team to reach a group consensus.\",\"The team is brought together to discuss the issue and make suggestions, and the leader then makes the final decision.\"],\"stem\":\"In an autocratic (A2) decision-making approach:\",\"sub\":\"tm\",\"why\":\"In an autocratic (A2) decision-making approach, the team is consulted to obtain specific information and then the leader makes the final decision. [III.C.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["The team is consulted to obtain specific information and then the leader makes the final decision.","The team is informed of the situation and each member''s opinion is asked for individually. The group is not brought together. The leader then makes the final decision.","The leader works with the team to reach a group consensus.","The team is brought together to discuss the issue and make suggestions, and the leader then makes the final decision."],"stem":"In an autocratic (A2) decision-making approach:","sub":"tm","why":"In an autocratic (A2) decision-making approach, the team is consulted to obtain specific information and then the leader makes the final decision. [III.C.3]"},"revision":"2f2ebfe36c9cffc9401f0b876b05404d9697d5a89abc70fa7030408eb468d074"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-672":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"3.61\",\"6.67\",\"6.85\",\"30.61\"],\"stem\":\"The 95% confidence interval [12.5, 18.5] was calculated for a population mean with known standard deviation σ and sample size n = 20. Use z* = 1.96. What is σ?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"The margin of error is E = (18.5 − 12.5)/2 = 3.0. From E = z*σ/√n, σ = 3.0√20/1.96 = 6.85. [VI.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["3.61","6.67","6.85","30.61"],"stem":"The 95% confidence interval [12.5, 18.5] was calculated for a population mean with known standard deviation σ and sample size n = 20. Use z* = 1.96. What is σ?","sub":"ana","why":"The margin of error is E = (18.5 − 12.5)/2 = 3.0. From E = z*σ/√n, σ = 3.0√20/1.96 = 6.85. [VI.B.4]"},"revision":"dd9bf4f192ebec4a19acc379884f41f334969e825db4005d3708c88133b92d17"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-673":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"the number of defective units.\",\"the number of defects.\",\"the number of standard deviations.\",\"the defect rate per unit.\"],\"stem\":\"A u-chart tracks:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"A u-chart tracks the defect rate per unit. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["the number of defective units.","the number of defects.","the number of standard deviations.","the defect rate per unit."],"stem":"A u-chart tracks:","sub":"con","why":"A u-chart tracks the defect rate per unit. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"eb82b6585d70f6bf2f9b5d9f64c314f761b75709d82d357d73fc46a4d69af8d0"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-674":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"2\",\"12\",\"16\",\"48\"],\"stem\":\"A process step in a PFMEA has an RPN of 96, a severity rating of 8, and an occurrence rating of 6. What is the detection rating?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"RPN = Severity × Occurrence × Detection. Therefore, Detection = RPN ÷ (Severity × Occurrence) = 96 ÷ (8 × 6) = 2. [VI.C.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["2","12","16","48"],"stem":"A process step in a PFMEA has an RPN of 96, a severity rating of 8, and an occurrence rating of 6. What is the detection rating?","sub":"ana","why":"RPN = Severity × Occurrence × Detection. Therefore, Detection = RPN ÷ (Severity × Occurrence) = 96 ÷ (8 × 6) = 2. [VI.C.2]"},"revision":"cbb2b5ba346f9d54be658de8f3e63fa6cdf2907e2f1c365023e7815c407c84c3"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-675":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"visual management.\",\"kanban.\",\"theory of constraints.\",\"SMED.\"],\"stem\":\"Schedule boards, signage, and color-coded bins are examples of:\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"Schedule boards, signage, and color-coded bins are examples of visual management. [VIII.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["visual management.","kanban.","theory of constraints.","SMED."],"stem":"Schedule boards, signage, and color-coded bins are examples of:","sub":"con","why":"Schedule boards, signage, and color-coded bins are examples of visual management. [VIII.B.2]"},"revision":"c18cf9abfcd6a53ab2ae2507cc38984e513ac65761e3dbf85708bab6a72a80fd"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-676":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Collaborate\",\"Engage to prevent sabotage\",\"Protect, defend, and give voice\",\"Monitor\"],\"stem\":\"If a project stakeholder is deemed to have low influence and high importance, what behavior approach should the project leader adopt for this stakeholder?\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"If a project stakeholder is deemed to have low influence and high importance, the project leader should adopt the \\\"protect, defend, and give voice\\\" approach. [II.A]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Collaborate","Engage to prevent sabotage","Protect, defend, and give voice","Monitor"],"stem":"If a project stakeholder is deemed to have low influence and high importance, what behavior approach should the project leader adopt for this stakeholder?","sub":"p2","why":"If a project stakeholder is deemed to have low influence and high importance, the project leader should adopt the \"protect, defend, and give voice\" approach. [II.A]"},"revision":"2c4268d2675d96b648f7bcd7c7c767191487b229aa2f0a8eaef31719c5c75922"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-677":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"5\",\"7\"],\"stem\":\"Which subgroup size would not be used for an X̄-R chart?\",\"sub\":\"con\",\"why\":\"An X̄-R chart requires more than one observation per subgroup, commonly n = 2 to 10. For n = 1, use an I-MR chart or another appropriate individuals chart. [VIII.A.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["1","2","5","7"],"stem":"Which subgroup size would not be used for an X̄-R chart?","sub":"con","why":"An X̄-R chart requires more than one observation per subgroup, commonly n = 2 to 10. For n = 1, use an I-MR chart or another appropriate individuals chart. [VIII.A.4]"},"revision":"b21750b321a6cbbee3da383fe11bd0ff5f8c22b9abc90441b2e20eec40f465fe"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-678":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"set noise factors to minimize the effects of control factors.\",\"set control factors to minimize the effects of noise factors.\",\"set noise factors to optimal levels.\",\"identify noise factors and eliminate them.\"],\"stem\":\"Robust design methods aim to:\",\"sub\":\"dfss\",\"why\":\"Robust design aims to set control factors to minimize the effects of noise factors. [IX.C]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["set noise factors to minimize the effects of control factors.","set control factors to minimize the effects of noise factors.","set noise factors to optimal levels.","identify noise factors and eliminate them."],"stem":"Robust design methods aim to:","sub":"dfss","why":"Robust design aims to set control factors to minimize the effects of noise factors. [IX.C]"},"revision":"8818b353a781b2d186603a3f0149cda97c64e35785876443f09b0aeccd8b2b4c"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-679":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Defects and skills\",\"Overproduction and inventory\",\"Waiting and transportation\",\"Defects and motion\"],\"stem\":\"A production supervisor runs a punch press line for a few extra hours to create backup stock \\\"just in case.\\\" What types of waste have been created?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Running the line a few extra hours to build up inventory would create both overproduction and inventory waste. [VI.D.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Defects and skills","Overproduction and inventory","Waiting and transportation","Defects and motion"],"stem":"A production supervisor runs a punch press line for a few extra hours to create backup stock \"just in case.\" What types of waste have been created?","sub":"ana","why":"Running the line a few extra hours to build up inventory would create both overproduction and inventory waste. [VI.D.3]"},"revision":"44aa92b1cae3ab999e90efe8b4ddc0ac9037fac9aeceba2c3e137bdc524a14d3"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-68":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"touch time.\",\"cycle time.\",\"takt time.\",\"throughput.\"],\"stem\":\"The amount of output that passes through a process in a specified period is defined as:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The amount of output that passes through a process in a specified period is throughput. [V.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["touch time.","cycle time.","takt time.","throughput."],"stem":"The amount of output that passes through a process in a specified period is defined as:","sub":"mea","why":"The amount of output that passes through a process in a specified period is throughput. [V.A.1]"},"revision":"1d44bccc462e14abbc2c05a5fdc923e891961a300785101b6f8c4cae03e6af32"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-680":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"0.9125\",\"0.9315\",\"0.9375\",\"0.9733\"],\"stem\":\"On an eight-hour shift, a process runs a total of 438 minutes due to a scheduled 30-minute shutdown for lunch and 12 minutes of downtime due to a broken clamp. What is the availability of the process?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Availability equals actual operating time divided by planned operating time. The 30-minute lunch shutdown is planned. Actual operating time = (8 × 60) − 30 − 12 = 438 minutes; planned operating time = (8 × 60) − 30 = 450 minutes. Availability = 438/450 = 0.9733. [VII.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["0.9125","0.9315","0.9375","0.9733"],"stem":"On an eight-hour shift, a process runs a total of 438 minutes due to a scheduled 30-minute shutdown for lunch and 12 minutes of downtime due to a broken clamp. What is the availability of the process?","sub":"imp","why":"Availability equals actual operating time divided by planned operating time. The 30-minute lunch shutdown is planned. Actual operating time = (8 × 60) − 30 − 12 = 438 minutes; planned operating time = (8 × 60) − 30 = 450 minutes. Availability = 438/450 = 0.9733. [VII.B.4]"},"revision":"44ff56fc44347e87ab736d3f20eda8e96672235c14840dfb2a6c5c781d301d0e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-681":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"266\",\"267\",\"1067\",\"1068\"],\"stem\":\"A quality engineer will test a simple random sample of lightbulbs from a population large enough to ignore the finite-population correction. What minimum sample size is required to estimate the defective percentage with a 3% margin of error at 95% confidence? No prior estimate is available; use p = 0.50 and z* = 1.96.\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"With no prior estimate, p = 0.50 gives the most conservative sample size. n = 1.96²(0.50)(0.50)/0.03² = 1,067.11, so the minimum whole-number sample that meets the margin of error is 1,068. [VI.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["266","267","1067","1068"],"stem":"A quality engineer will test a simple random sample of lightbulbs from a population large enough to ignore the finite-population correction. What minimum sample size is required to estimate the defective percentage with a 3% margin of error at 95% confidence? No prior estimate is available; use p = 0.50 and z* = 1.96.","sub":"ana","why":"With no prior estimate, p = 0.50 gives the most conservative sample size. n = 1.96²(0.50)(0.50)/0.03² = 1,067.11, so the minimum whole-number sample that meets the margin of error is 1,068. [VI.B.3]"},"revision":"1f62bf150018eb121e8068859e8bdd9ea57eb3a42c52b25dafbc55d581a8e229"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-682":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"physical model.\",\"pilot run.\",\"prototype.\",\"simulation.\"],\"stem\":\"An evaluation method that uses a three-dimensional miniature version of a solution is known as a:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"The evaluation method that uses a three-dimensional miniature version of a solution is known as a physical model. [VII.C]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["physical model.","pilot run.","prototype.","simulation."],"stem":"An evaluation method that uses a three-dimensional miniature version of a solution is known as a:","sub":"imp","why":"The evaluation method that uses a three-dimensional miniature version of a solution is known as a physical model. [VII.C]"},"revision":"04b1085acdb798e0e55468abcd15769e4e5671291cfda9628ae5ba91157d48a4"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-683":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"−5.72\",\"2.86\",\"5.72\",\"11.44\"],\"stem\":\"In a two-level full-factorial design, the effect of Factor A is equal to 5.72. What is the corresponding coefficient for Factor A in the coded regression model?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"The coefficient in the coded regression model for a full-factorial experiment will be one-half the factor effect. Here, 5.72 ÷ 2 = 2.86. [VII.A.6]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["−5.72","2.86","5.72","11.44"],"stem":"In a two-level full-factorial design, the effect of Factor A is equal to 5.72. What is the corresponding coefficient for Factor A in the coded regression model?","sub":"imp","why":"The coefficient in the coded regression model for a full-factorial experiment will be one-half the factor effect. Here, 5.72 ÷ 2 = 2.86. [VII.A.6]"},"revision":"142afb37ee40d473d28dd48365b60aa190d8c8cdf87ec6e5288de68c76366a47"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-684":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Financial perspective\",\"Learning and growth perspective\",\"Internal business processes perspective\",\"Customer perspective\"],\"stem\":\"In a balanced scorecard, which perspective includes the capabilities and skills of an organization?\",\"sub\":\"p2\",\"why\":\"In a balanced scorecard, the learning and growth perspective includes the capabilities and skills of an organization. [II.C.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Financial perspective","Learning and growth perspective","Internal business processes perspective","Customer perspective"],"stem":"In a balanced scorecard, which perspective includes the capabilities and skills of an organization?","sub":"p2","why":"In a balanced scorecard, the learning and growth perspective includes the capabilities and skills of an organization. [II.C.1]"},"revision":"a68b8794ab762e5c9b7c2244f6366fcc25799810c5c5263770d7277fc0fa2ab9"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-685":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Confounding\",\"Replication\",\"Randomization\",\"Blocking\"],\"stem\":\"What experimental design technique would be used when an experiment will have to be conducted over the course of two days?\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"If an experiment must be conducted over two days, the analyst should use blocking to account for any additional variability introduced by different conditions day to day. [VII.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Confounding","Replication","Randomization","Blocking"],"stem":"What experimental design technique would be used when an experiment will have to be conducted over the course of two days?","sub":"imp","why":"If an experiment must be conducted over two days, the analyst should use blocking to account for any additional variability introduced by different conditions day to day. [VII.A.2]"},"revision":"787695a0a84fe5231e3ed62383277e2e98200f8cd12fbddda3a894656bbbd56a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-686":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"mistake-proofing.\",\"a bottleneck.\",\"a pull system.\",\"heijunka.\"],\"stem\":\"A university institutes two-factor authentication for logging into its systems. This is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"Two-factor authentication is an example of mistake-proofing. [VII.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["mistake-proofing.","a bottleneck.","a pull system.","heijunka."],"stem":"A university institutes two-factor authentication for logging into its systems. This is an example of:","sub":"imp","why":"Two-factor authentication is an example of mistake-proofing. [VII.