rate at which the process must produce units to meet customer demand. [V.A.1]"},"revision":"1d74ae125c7db0a8a184494f3cda2b554b6a9bc671443630f66db27139e08f5f"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-690":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"designing in self-diagnostics.\",\"using self-locating fixtures.\",\"minimizing life cycle cost.\",\"using exotic materials for manufacturing.\"],\"stem\":\"Which design feature most directly improves fault diagnosis and serviceability?\",\"sub\":\"dfss\",\"why\":\"Design for Maintainability includes features such as built-in self-diagnostics, which make faults easier to detect and service. [IX.B]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["designing in self-diagnostics.","using self-locating fixtures.","minimizing life cycle cost.","using exotic materials for manufacturing."],"stem":"Which design feature most directly improves fault diagnosis and serviceability?","sub":"dfss","why":"Design for Maintainability includes features such as built-in self-diagnostics, which make faults easier to detect and service. [IX.B]"},"revision":"2538acab79908bd534ecc34f39c4205a0d9847912f57a5cbaba7e6af094532f1"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-691":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"3, 1, 5, 2, 4\",\"3, 2, 5, 1, 4\",\"3, 1, 2, 5, 4\",\"3, 5, 2, 1, 4\"],\"stem\":\"Put the theory-of-constraints improvement steps in order: (1) Exploit; (2) Elevate; (3) Identify; (4) Repeat; (5) Subordinate.\",\"sub\":\"imp\",\"why\":\"The steps in the theory of constraints are, in order, identify, exploit, subordinate, elevate, and repeat. [VII.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["3, 1, 5, 2, 4","3, 2, 5, 1, 4","3, 1, 2, 5, 4","3, 5, 2, 1, 4"],"stem":"Put the theory-of-constraints improvement steps in order: (1) Exploit; (2) Elevate; (3) Identify; (4) Repeat; (5) Subordinate.","sub":"imp","why":"The steps in the theory of constraints are, in order, identify, exploit, subordinate, elevate, and repeat. [VII.B.4]"},"revision":"6f6af40ed69b1cddcaef1258d43a1aa9a8a1e5a22470f6