ers responding with 7 or 8 are:","sub":"p2","why":"In Net Promoter Score classification, respondents who select 7 or 8 are passives: generally satisfied but not enthusiastic promoters. [II.C.1]"},"revision":"8d5f81ebd41f8495bdf477770192ec732cf79013bf07ac5021384897fefd5ad9"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-694":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Launch\",\"Culture transformation\",\"Scale and replication\",\"Institutionalization\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following terms is considered the fourth stage in a Six Sigma maturity model?\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"The fourth stage in a Six Sigma maturity model is institutionalization. The five stages include (1) launch, (2) early success, (3) scale and replication, (4) institutionalization, and (5) culture transformation. [I.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Launch","Culture transformation","Scale and replication","Institutionalization"],"stem":"Which of the following terms is considered the fourth stage in a Six Sigma maturity model?","sub":"p1","why":"The fourth stage in a Six Sigma maturity model is institutionalization. The five stages include (1) launch, (2) early success, (3) scale and replication, (4) institutionalization, and (5) culture transformation. [I.A.1]"},"revision":"1227f78d657f379359ffd82398a73b0166ff3b610f220193072070a3a31ff3a3"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-7":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Launch\",\"Culture transformation\",\"Scale and replication\",\"Institutionalization\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following terms is associated with the third stage in a Six Sigma maturity model?\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"Scale and replication. The five stages of Six Sigma maturity are (1) launch, (2) early success, (3) scale and replication, (4) institutionalization, and (5) culture transformation. [I.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Launch","Culture transformation","Scale and replication","Institutionalization"],"stem":"Which of the following terms is associated with the third stage in a Six Sigma maturity model?","sub":"p1","why":"Scale and replication. The five stages of Six Sigma maturity are (1) launch, (2) early success, (3) scale and replication, (4) institutionalization, and (5) culture transformation. [I.A.1]"},"revision":"6b9a206e69a49bccfa6dded56c18adea2fe9225639f7a64babdd3ff4aafc6df0"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-70":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"work in queue (WIQ).\",\"work in progress (WIP).\",\"one of the eight wastes.\",\"a value-added activity.\"],\"stem\":\"A process step changes the form, fit, or function of a product, is required by the customer, and is performed correctly the first time. This step is classified as:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"A value-added activity changes the product or service in a way the customer requires, and it is performed correctly the first time. [V.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["work in queue (WIQ).","work in progress (WIP).","one of the eight wastes.","a value-added activity."],"stem":"A process step changes the form, fit, or function of a product, is required by the customer, and is performed correctly the first time. This step is classified as:","sub":"mea","why":"A value-added activity changes the product or service in a way the customer requires, and it is performed correctly the first time. [V.A.1]"},"revision":"7e0765a44c8181ce4095990b6abb2fd93189c1a48303b924c8d4bf7ff7145c1f"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-71":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"value-added time.\",\"cycle time.\",\"takt time.\",\"setup time.\"],\"stem\":\"The time from when a hospital patient is discharged until the room is ready for the next patient is known as:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The interval from patient discharge until the room is ready for the next patient is setup, or changeover, time. In manufacturing, it is the interval from the last unit of one job to the first good unit of the next job produced at full speed. [V.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["value-added time.","cycle time.","takt time.","setup time."],"stem":"The time from when a hospital patient is discharged until the room is ready for the next patient is known as:","sub":"mea","why":"The interval from patient discharge until the room is ready for the next patient is setup, or changeover, time. In manufacturing, it is the interval from the last unit of one job to the first good unit of the next job produced at full speed. [V.A.1]"},"revision":"e7d73012b020ee9fab2ec8f841a5947d6b71a87a01cbb7b24dfb57d45411bc84"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-72":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"hidden factory.\",\"process map.\",\"swim-lane chart.\",\"process performance.\"],\"stem\":\"The unplanned rework, workarounds, inspections, and corrective activities that consume process capacity without adding customer value are collectively called the:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The hidden factory consists of unplanned work, such as rework, workarounds, inspection, and correction, that consumes resources without adding customer value. [V.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["hidden factory.","process map.","swim-lane chart.","process performance."],"stem":"The unplanned rework, workarounds, inspections, and corrective activities that consume process capacity without adding customer value are collectively called the:","sub":"mea","why":"The hidden factory consists of unplanned work, such as rework, workarounds, inspection, and correction, that consumes resources without adding customer value. [V.A.1]"},"revision":"ee4f227d9672de36d44b95809fc1a8968344ed25146abac9240e45d294b09106"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-73":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"scatter plot.\",\"why-why diagram.\",\"process flow chart.\",\"interrelationship digraph.\"],\"stem\":\"A chart that graphically represents the steps needed to approve a loan application is referred to as a:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"A process flow chart uses standard symbols to show process steps and their sequence. [V.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["scatter plot.","why-why diagram.","process flow chart.","interrelationship digraph."],"stem":"A chart that graphically represents the steps needed to approve a loan application is referred to as a:","sub":"mea","why":"A process flow chart uses standard symbols to show process steps and their sequence. [V.A.2]"},"revision":"14f5b9a753ee45a90e6756ba8c6e1fa01f25b2ba2bee13614504518288a2400e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-74":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Stakeholders, current state, and action plans\",\"WIP, changeover times, and inventory\",\"Defect locations and error types\",\"Material and worker flow and distances traveled\"],\"stem\":\"A value stream map (VSM) is a detailed process map that shows what types of information?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"A value stream map (VSM) shows process steps in sequence. It may include cycle and changeover times, staffing, WIP, inventory, packaging sizes, scrap rates, value-added and non-value-added activities, and changeovers. [V.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Stakeholders, current state, and action plans","WIP, changeover times, and inventory","Defect locations and error types","Material and worker flow and distances traveled"],"stem":"A value stream map (VSM) is a detailed process map that shows what types of information?","sub":"mea","why":"A value stream map (VSM) shows process steps in sequence. It may include cycle and changeover times, staffing, WIP, inventory, packaging sizes, scrap rates, value-added and non-value-added activities, and changeovers. [V.A.2]"},"revision":"152d0003f50dd4d20088f9d4686e9a090dc9af095a31c22f9bfedb8ffd0c132d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-75":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"be printed on A3-sized paper.\",\"provide only a broad overview of the process.\",\"encourage improvisation.\",\"include labeled graphics.\"],\"stem\":\"Work instructions should ideally:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Work instructions should include labeled graphics; required parts, materials, tools, fixtures, and personnel; safety and defect risks; the exact sequence of steps; and a document and revision number. [V.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["be printed on A3-sized paper.","provide only a broad overview of the process.","encourage improvisation.","include labeled graphics."],"stem":"Work instructions should ideally:","sub":"mea","why":"Work instructions should include labeled graphics; required parts, materials, tools, fixtures, and personnel; safety and defect risks; the exact sequence of steps; and a document and revision number. [V.A.2]"},"revision":"e0ec4f77452528ba42a50226719fad103ad21db1a07e5cc5935c27f6d7460b35"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-76":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Value stream map.\",\"Spaghetti diagram.\",\"Gemba walk.\",\"Process map.\"],\"stem\":\"A team would like to track the physical path that parts travel from arrival at the dock through the manufacturing process. Which graphical tool is the best choice?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"A spaghetti diagram shows how parts or people physically move through a process. A gemba walk may be used to collect observations for the diagram, but it is not itself a graphical tool. [V.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Value stream map.","Spaghetti diagram.","Gemba walk.","Process map."],"stem":"A team would like to track the physical path that parts travel from arrival at the dock through the manufacturing process. Which graphical tool is the best choice?","sub":"mea","why":"A spaghetti diagram shows how parts or people physically move through a process. A gemba walk may be used to collect observations for the diagram, but it is not itself a graphical tool. [V.A.2]"},"revision":"f83de99715666ae038a77de4eab2f94d67f9ab9a8657037a48f64b836d88dd35"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-77":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"brainstorm ideas to improve the process.\",\"go on a gemba walk.\",\"hold stakeholders accountable.\",\"create a future-state map.\"],\"stem\":\"Before creating a process map, a team should:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Before mapping a process, the team should walk the actual process (conduct a gemba walk) and speak with the people who perform the work. [V.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["brainstorm ideas to improve the process.","go on a gemba walk.","hold stakeholders accountable.","create a future-state map."],"stem":"Before creating a process map, a team should:","sub":"mea","why":"Before mapping a process, the team should walk the actual process (conduct a gemba walk) and speak with the people who perform the work. [V.A.2]"},"revision":"a5c11bb789c98607c155aa638a4d985ce20d34d201bde3c2b064ec2dd0ae102f"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-78":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"swim-lane chart.\",\"value stream map.\",\"spaghetti diagram.\",\"SIPOC diagram.\"],\"stem\":\"A flow chart that shows handoffs and changes of control across departments is known as a:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"A swim-lane chart uses lanes for roles, functions, or departments and shows responsibilities, handoffs, and cross-functional transitions. [V.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["swim-lane chart.","value stream map.","spaghetti diagram.","SIPOC diagram."],"stem":"A flow chart that shows handoffs and changes of control across departments is known as a:","sub":"mea","why":"A swim-lane chart uses lanes for roles, functions, or departments and shows responsibilities, handoffs, and cross-functional transitions. [V.A.2]"},"revision":"7cfcdc94d7a752634c1cfb32d5cbfb9142b90b2d121be73b28f7b41541e9f5fd"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-79":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Weight.\",\"Length.\",\"Volume.\",\"Defect type.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following characteristics is considered qualitative?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Defect type is qualitative because it classifies observations into nominal categories; arithmetic differences or averages of category labels are not meaningful. [V.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Weight.","Length.","Volume.","Defect type."],"stem":"Which of the following characteristics is considered qualitative?","sub":"mea","why":"Defect type is qualitative because it classifies observations into nominal categories; arithmetic differences or averages of category labels are not meaningful. [V.B.1]"},"revision":"335fcea9141dbb9c72b8075ca10bf1ae5cfaa908adc19194af3b270499724978"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-8":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"all belts have undergone training.\",\"projects are geared toward low-hanging fruit.\",\"DMAIC is consistently used to solve problems.\",\"Six Sigma is ingrained into the organization’s culture.\"],\"stem\":\"During the early-success phase of a Six Sigma implementation:\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"In the early-success phase of Six Sigma implementation, projects are geared toward low-hanging fruit. These early successes demonstrate proof of concept and prepare the organization for the scale and replication phase. [I.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["all belts have undergone training.","projects are geared toward low-hanging fruit.","DMAIC is consistently used to solve problems.","Six Sigma is ingrained into the organization’s culture."],"stem":"During the early-success phase of a Six Sigma implementation:","sub":"p1","why":"In the early-success phase of Six Sigma implementation, projects are geared toward low-hanging fruit. These early successes demonstrate proof of concept and prepare the organization for the scale and replication phase. [I.A.1]"},"revision":"8ea42a720e61f8385de4dde3def5967f980f449b99e15ce36bb73bd447c5dffd"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-80":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"The weight of a raw material.\",\"The number of defects per unit.\",\"The output voltage of a transformer.\",\"The cycle time for a filling operation.\"],\"stem\":\"Which of the following measurements is considered discrete data?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"The number of defects per unit is a count and therefore discrete data. Discrete data takes values from a finite or countably infinite set, such as the nonnegative integers. [V.B.1]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["The weight of a raw material.","The number of defects per unit.","The output voltage of a transformer.","The cycle time for a filling operation."],"stem":"Which of the following measurements is considered discrete data?","sub":"mea","why":"The number of defects per unit is a count and therefore discrete data. Discrete data takes values from a finite or countably infinite set, such as the nonnegative integers. [V.B.1]"},"revision":"0e83b33276aab4f0c49a39e7aee12a4abebe234e1054720e510161e49155114d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-81":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Ordinal\",\"Interval\",\"Ratio\",\"Nominal\"],\"stem\":\"Data collected on which measurement scale is classified into categories with no order implied?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Nominal data can be classified into categories, but there is no implied order. Numerical values used only as labels, such as identification numbers, are also nominal. Sums or averages of nominal labels are not meaningful. [V.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Ordinal","Interval","Ratio","Nominal"],"stem":"Data collected on which measurement scale is classified into categories with no order implied?","sub":"mea","why":"Nominal data can be classified into categories, but there is no implied order. Numerical values used only as labels, such as identification numbers, are also nominal. Sums or averages of nominal labels are not meaningful. [V.B.2]"},"revision":"7bc57d34950e6d45956afca7d1e974e4bac5dd32a6d4feed8765988be6df6033"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-82":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Interval\",\"Ratio\",\"Ordinal\",\"Nominal\"],\"stem\":\"On which measurement scale can data be ranked, even though precise differences between ranks are not defined?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Ordinal data has intrinsic order, but differences between ranks are not necessarily equal. For example, runners may finish first in 30 minutes, second in 31 minutes, and third in 45 minutes. [V.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Interval","Ratio","Ordinal","Nominal"],"stem":"On which measurement scale can data be ranked, even though precise differences between ranks are not defined?","sub":"mea","why":"Ordinal data has intrinsic order, but differences between ranks are not necessarily equal. For example, runners may finish first in 30 minutes, second in 31 minutes, and third in 45 minutes. [V.B.2]"},"revision":"e2d5bd652c8081be8cf453e9fb333b359cf164ad1b1dca878a9a734997edac9e"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-83":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Interval\",\"Ratio\",\"Ordinal\",\"Nominal\"],\"stem\":\"Which measurement scale has meaningful, equal differences and an absolute zero?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Ratio data has meaningful, equidistant differences and an absolute zero, so ratios are meaningful. For example, if Part A weighs 3 kg and Part B weighs 6 kg, Part B weighs twice as much as Part A. [V.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Interval","Ratio","Ordinal","Nominal"],"stem":"Which measurement scale has meaningful, equal differences and an absolute zero?","sub":"mea","why":"Ratio data has meaningful, equidistant differences and an absolute zero, so ratios are meaningful. For example, if Part A weighs 3 kg and Part B weighs 6 kg, Part B weighs twice as much as Part A. [V.B.2]"},"revision":"229ed53acec0de2a5155f9ecbd6fef941cbb83589d9367347bae1294a8806a6b"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-84":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Standard deviation\",\"Interquartile range\",\"Mode\",\"None is acceptable\"],\"stem\":\"What is an acceptable measure of dispersion to use for data that is on the ordinal measurement scale?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"For ordinal data, acceptable measures of dispersion include the range and interquartile range. [V.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Standard deviation","Interquartile range","Mode","None is acceptable"],"stem":"What is an acceptable measure of dispersion to use for data that is on the ordinal measurement scale?","sub":"mea","why":"For ordinal data, acceptable measures of dispersion include the range and interquartile range. [V.B.2]"},"revision":"929e68c4c31b1a1300bf5e0805ecb2198ca79d68c78d68fbcbd1d19b2b711312"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-85":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Nominal\",\"Ordinal\",\"Interval\",\"Ratio\"],\"stem\":\"A company creates a customer-satisfaction survey using a five-point Likert scale. Which measurement scale is associated with this type of data?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"A Likert response scale is ordinal: responses are ordered, but the difference between adjacent categories is not guaranteed to be equal. [V.B.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Nominal","Ordinal","Interval","Ratio"],"stem":"A company creates a customer-satisfaction survey using a five-point Likert scale. Which measurement scale is associated with this type of data?","sub":"mea","why":"A Likert response scale is ordinal: responses are ordered, but the difference between adjacent categories is not guaranteed to be equal. [V.B.2]"},"revision":"b2f666d7fed9d991252513b88c41595e922ddac491fd32e5d1ee8d056474d222"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-86":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Double sampling\",\"Simple random sampling\",\"Stratified sampling\",\"Multiple sampling\"],\"stem\":\"An injection-molding process has a four-cavity mold running in one press. What sampling method is most appropriate to monitor this process?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Sampling from every cavity ensures that each cavity is represented. Treating the cavities as strata is stratified sampling. [V.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Double sampling","Simple random sampling","Stratified sampling","Multiple sampling"],"stem":"An injection-molding process has a four-cavity mold running in one press. What sampling method is most appropriate to monitor this process?","sub":"mea","why":"Sampling from every cavity ensures that each cavity is represented. Treating the cavities as strata is stratified sampling. [V.B.3]"},"revision":"c93729a03670d96a886d83e769121806d02f73576cca54d3d6d64e2016d60356"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-87":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"recall bias.\",\"self-selection bias.\",\"survivorship bias.\",\"exclusion bias.\"],\"stem\":\"An internet poll reveals that respondents prefer Product A over Product B. This result is likely not representative of the population due to:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Self-selection bias occurs when individuals decide whether to participate. Those who choose to respond are often not representative of the population as a whole. [V.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["recall bias.","self-selection bias.","survivorship bias.","exclusion bias."],"stem":"An internet poll reveals that respondents prefer Product A over Product B. This result is likely not representative of the population due to:","sub":"mea","why":"Self-selection bias occurs when individuals decide whether to participate. Those who choose to respond are often not representative of the population as a whole. [V.B.3]"},"revision":"d5a6f754057d09948f240656bbf1b49155dc6443f84d01b6207d22bd8ee987f4"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-88":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Simple random sampling\",\"Systematic sampling\",\"Convenience sampling\",\"Stratified sampling\"],\"stem\":\"A quality inspector chooses every 10th assembly coming off the final production line for testing. What type of sampling scheme is being used?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Choosing every nth item in a sequence is systematic sampling. [V.B.3]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Simple random sampling","Systematic sampling","Convenience sampling","Stratified sampling"],"stem":"A quality inspector chooses every 10th assembly coming off the final production line for testing. What type of sampling scheme is being used?","sub":"mea","why":"Choosing every nth item in a sequence is systematic sampling. [V.B.3]"},"revision":"99ed928dab94f4b81a61870febae37d54e595c515e3a33479fe0d4b3a0d386b9"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-89":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"28\",\"29\",\"609\",\"610\"],\"stem\":\"A company sends a survey to its 2,400 customers. Using the finite-population planning formula n = N/(1 + NE²), what is the minimum number of responses required for E = 3.5%?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Using n = N/(1 + NE²), n = 2,400/[1 + 2,400(0.035)²] = 609.14. Round up to ensure the requirement, giving 610 responses. [V.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["28","29","609","610"],"stem":"A company sends a survey to its 2,400 customers. Using the finite-population planning formula n = N/(1 + NE²), what is the minimum number of responses required for E = 3.5%?","sub":"mea","why":"Using n = N/(1 + NE²), n = 2,400/[1 + 2,400(0.035)²] = 609.14. Round up to ensure the requirement, giving 610 responses. [V.B.4]"},"revision":"be1686157886949f0b527a24ec10d40005532951b9699b08964b9e7da0d9b2d4"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-9":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"use all seven of the basic quality tools.\",\"involve a designed experiment.\",\"be completed within two months.\",\"involve frontline workers affected by the project.\"],\"stem\":\"Six Sigma projects should:\",\"sub\":\"p1\",\"why\":\"Six Sigma projects should involve the frontline workers of the process being improved. These workers are stakeholders in the project and have key insights into the process. Their support is needed for any improvements to stick. [I.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["use all seven of the basic quality tools.","involve a designed experiment.","be completed within two months.","involve frontline workers affected by the project."],"stem":"Six Sigma projects should:","sub":"p1","why":"Six Sigma projects should involve the frontline workers of the process being improved. These workers are stakeholders in the project and have key insights into the process. Their support is needed for any improvements to stick. [I.A.2]"},"revision":"1149c3dc0371fb5092de2f7d65eee31d98457b51647dd97d949242a5f7a66952"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-90":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"To transform the data into a normal distribution\",\"To put different data sets on a common measurement scale\",\"To ensure that units are assigned to data values\",\"To detect sampling bias\"],\"stem\":\"What is the purpose of z-score standardization (called normalization in this guide)?\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"Z-score standardization places different data sets on a common unitless scale with mean 0 and standard deviation 1. It does not transform a nonnormal distribution into a normal distribution. [V.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["To transform the data into a normal distribution","To put different data sets on a common measurement scale","To ensure that units are assigned to data values","To detect sampling bias"],"stem":"What is the purpose of z-score standardization (called normalization in this guide)?","sub":"mea","why":"Z-score standardization places different data sets on a common unitless scale with mean 0 and standard deviation 1. It does not transform a nonnormal distribution into a normal distribution. [V.B.4]"},"revision":"fe2e8f40738ea4402bcdd2d705a68c4f6cf4109e58be1edb67586c1ea310cd9a"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-91":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Gantt chart.\",\"fishbone diagram.\",\"check sheet.\",\"Y-matrix.\"],\"stem\":\"A useful tool for keeping a tally of events is known as a:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"A check sheet is one of the seven basic quality tools. It is used to tally occurrences of defined events or categories, often over time. [V.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Gantt chart.","fishbone diagram.","check sheet.","Y-matrix."],"stem":"A useful tool for keeping a tally of events is known as a:","sub":"mea","why":"A check sheet is one of the seven basic quality tools. It is used to tally occurrences of defined events or categories, often over time. [V.B.4]"},"revision":"d4169fb336fc223ebfbc14370e7db76f64ab0ac8bd2e570d63e3418dd6d78559"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-92":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"capture data across several complete seasonal cycles.\",\"use as short a collection period as possible.\",\"ignore seasonality.\",\"collect data during only one short, supposedly representative period.\"],\"stem\":\"A data-collection plan for seasonal data should:\",\"sub\":\"mea\",\"why\":\"For seasonal data, the collection window should span several complete seasonal cycles. Regular sampling is useful, but a short window can miss the seasonal pattern. [V.B.4]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["capture data across several complete seasonal cycles.","use as short a collection period as possible.","ignore seasonality.","collect data during only one short, supposedly representative period."],"stem":"A data-collection plan for seasonal data should:","sub":"mea","why":"For seasonal data, the collection window should span several complete seasonal cycles. Regular sampling is useful, but a short window can miss the seasonal pattern. [V.B.4]"},"revision":"5d52880b8a4efe230e448f6b4b3f6693ab25e2f3069c47cfaf4b097d1f2ffa82"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-93":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"t = 1.91\",\"t = 2.03\",\"t = 2.39\",\"t = 3.39\"],\"stem\":\"For testing H₀: ρ = 0 with a sample correlation r = 0.56 and n = 10 paired observations, what is the value of the t statistic?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"t = r√(n−2)/√(1−r²) = 0.56√8/√(1−0.56²) = 1.91. [VI.A.1]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["t = 1.91","t = 2.03","t = 2.39","t = 3.39"],"stem":"For testing H₀: ρ = 0 with a sample correlation r = 0.56 and n = 10 paired observations, what is the value of the t statistic?","sub":"ana","why":"t = r√(n−2)/√(1−r²) = 0.56√8/√(1−0.56²) = 1.91. [VI.A.1]"},"revision":"f660328887e3f27240db5a9c557debcea86fa5f34a0870a268f8f6053c714608"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-94":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"If ŷ increases by 1 unit, x will increase by the amount of the slope.\",\"If x increases by 1 unit, ŷ will change by the amount of the slope.\",\"If x equals 0, ŷ will equal the slope.\",\"An increase in x will cause ŷ to increase.\"],\"stem\":\"What is the interpretation of the slope in a simple linear regression?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"The slope is the expected change in the predicted response for a one-unit increase in x. The sign of the slope determines whether the predicted response rises or falls. [VI.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["If ŷ increases by 1 unit, x will increase by the amount of the slope.","If x increases by 1 unit, ŷ will change by the amount of the slope.","If x equals 0, ŷ will equal the slope.","An increase in x will cause ŷ to increase."],"stem":"What is the interpretation of the slope in a simple linear regression?","sub":"ana","why":"The slope is the expected change in the predicted response for a one-unit increase in x. The sign of the slope determines whether the predicted response rises or falls. [VI.A.2]"},"revision":"47ca5b058b911b5e76dcf8ea696949e928bee05073996a199c26a237aad92027"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-95":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"The regression model is underestimating the observation.\",\"The regression model is overestimating the observation.\",\"The regression model has a negative slope.\",\"The regression model is overestimating the input variable.\"],\"stem\":\"A simple linear regression model returns a negative residual for a particular value of X. What must be true about the model?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"A residual is e = y − ŷ. If e is negative, then ŷ > y, so the model is overestimating the observed response. [VI.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["The regression model is underestimating the observation.","The regression model is overestimating the observation.","The regression model has a negative slope.","The regression model is overestimating the input variable."],"stem":"A simple linear regression model returns a negative residual for a particular value of X. What must be true about the model?","sub":"ana","why":"A residual is e = y − ŷ. If e is negative, then ŷ > y, so the model is overestimating the observed response. [VI.A.2]"},"revision":"024d2d2e96c5328300a56bfa14a3b9ec2bddb5ed3b7717821e6dcb91d7babb7d"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-96":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Y is independent and normally distributed with a constant mean and standard deviation.\",\"Errors are independent and normally distributed with mean 0 and constant variance σ².\",\"X and Y are normally distributed and dependent.\",\"Errors are dependent and normally distributed with mean 0 and variance σ²/n.\"],\"stem\":\"What are the assumptions for simple linear regression?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Simple linear regression assumes independent errors with mean 0 and constant variance σ²; normal errors are required for exact small-sample inference. Residual plots are used to assess these assumptions. [VI.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Y is independent and normally distributed with a constant mean and standard deviation.","Errors are independent and normally distributed with mean 0 and constant variance σ².","X and Y are normally distributed and dependent.","Errors are dependent and normally distributed with mean 0 and variance σ²/n."],"stem":"What are the assumptions for simple linear regression?","sub":"ana","why":"Simple linear regression assumes independent errors with mean 0 and constant variance σ²; normal errors are required for exact small-sample inference. Residual plots are used to assess these assumptions. [VI.A.2]"},"revision":"1bb95c7f6a798a2ba3f4233ae519ef624c175dce35cca6c6d28651a0bb6446a9"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-97":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"X = y\",\"X = 0\",\"X = x̄\",\"X = b₁\"],\"stem\":\"A 95% confidence interval on the response of a regression model will be at its narrowest point when which statement is true?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"The confidence interval for the mean response is narrowest at X = x̄ because the leverage term (X − x̄)² is then zero. [VI.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["X = y","X = 0","X = x̄","X = b₁"],"stem":"A 95% confidence interval on the response of a regression model will be at its narrowest point when which statement is true?","sub":"ana","why":"The confidence interval for the mean response is narrowest at X = x̄ because the leverage term (X − x̄)² is then zero. [VI.A.2]"},"revision":"e8da0e3a1731dc26c9c709fa841b37308359a35042821e5109ec24b783bebed1"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-98":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"At any given X, the confidence interval will be wider than the prediction interval.\",\"The confidence and prediction intervals will be equal at X = x̄.\",\"Both the confidence and prediction intervals will get wider as x deviates from its average value.\",\"The width of the prediction interval is independent of the value of x.\"],\"stem\":\"For a simple linear regression model, an analyst calculates 95% confidence and prediction intervals. What is true about these intervals?\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Both intervals are narrowest at x̄ and widen as x moves away from the average observed x. A prediction interval is also wider than the corresponding confidence interval because it includes individual-response variation. [VI.A.2]\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["At any given X, the confidence interval will be wider than the prediction interval.","The confidence and prediction intervals will be equal at X = x̄.","Both the confidence and prediction intervals will get wider as x deviates from its average value.","The width of the prediction interval is independent of the value of x."],"stem":"For a simple linear regression model, an analyst calculates 95% confidence and prediction intervals. What is true about these intervals?","sub":"ana","why":"Both intervals are narrowest at x̄ and widen as x moves away from the average observed x. A prediction interval is also wider than the corresponding confidence interval because it includes individual-response variation. [VI.A.2]"},"revision":"e4297f001edc625935b5e92f9118158bb5e5817117df81a4b358f198d5d7a159"},"cssbb:set-3:legacy-99":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"develop a linear combination of input variables that acts to separate the data into two or more classes.\",\"detect small differences in the accuracy of gages.\",\"reduce the dimensionality of a data set when inputs are correlated.\",\"categorize brainstormed ideas into natural groupings.\"],\"stem\":\"The purpose of discriminant analysis is to:\",\"sub\":\"ana\",\"why\":\"Discriminant analysis develops one or more linear combinations of predictors that separate observations into two or more predefined classes. [VI.A.3]\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["develop a linear combination of input variables that acts to separate the data into two or more classes.","detect small differences in the accuracy of gages.","reduce the dimensionality of a data set when inputs are correlated.","categorize brainstormed ideas into natural groupings."],"stem":"The purpose of discriminant analysis is to:","sub":"ana","why":"Discriminant analysis develops one or more linear combinations of predictors that separate observations into two or more predefined classes. [VI.A.3]"},"revision":"55a62317446c4e4a910d4147e4efef26ead93f73c25e4ee1f89dfa4901494e43"}},"examId":"cssbb","provenance":{"disclosure":"This site preset scores all 165 questions and allows 270 minutes. ASQ computer delivery has 165 items (150 scored), 258 exam minutes and a 270-minute appointment. The site timer is a practice preset, not the official exam clock.","officialComputer":{"appointmentMinutes":270,"examMinutes":258,"items":165,"per