ess of creating value without waste</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 17 (print p. 7), I.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"429701e978dbe9d449b9ae1ff18af812aa14ed065927f085af738d526b321a21"},"cssgb:set-2:source-118:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.1\",\"options\":[\"Value for the customer\",\"Cost of poor quality\",\"Ishikawa diagram\",\"Taguchi loss function\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":118,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":7,\"sourceQuestion\":18,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following concepts is associated with the Toyota Production System House?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"This should be an easy answer as the other options are separate tools and not directly associated with the Toyota Production System House. <b>A. Value for the customer</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 18 (print p. 7), I.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"I.B.1","options":["Value for the customer","Cost of poor quality","Ishikawa diagram","Taguchi loss function"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":118,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":7,"sourceQuestion":18,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which of the following concepts is associated with the Toyota Production System House?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"This should be an easy answer as the other options are separate tools and not directly associated with the Toyota Production System House. <b>A. Value for the customer</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 18 (print p. 7), I.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"38e5249a66ecf1bef4a7ecdce087686936febb65e042164ac07788763afc6584"},"cssgb:set-2:source-119:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.1\",\"options\":[\"Building a culture of stopping to fix problems to get quality right the first time\",\"Encouraging employees to use the A3 methodology to sell their Six Sigma ideas to their Champions\",\"Allowing employees to go-see and report their understandings back to management so that it stays out of the way\",\"Teaching the management team to manage by wandering around\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":119,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":8,\"sourceQuestion\":19,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following actions is a principle of “The Toyota Way”?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"Of the four possibilities here, only a drives to remove waste, which is one of the keys of lean. <b>A. Building a culture of stopping to fix problems to get quality right the first time</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 19 (print p. 8), I.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"I.B.1","options":["Building a culture of stopping to fix problems to get quality right the first time","Encouraging employees to use the A3 methodology to sell their Six Sigma ideas to their Champions","Allowing employees to go-see and report their understandings back to management so that it stays out of the way","Teaching the management team to manage by wandering around"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":119,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":8,"sourceQuestion":19,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which of the following actions is a principle of “The Toyota Way”?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"Of the four possibilities here, only a drives to remove waste, which is one of the keys of lean. <b>A. Building a culture of stopping to fix problems to get quality right the first time</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 19 (print p. 8), I.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"14fb46b2899a3c433d719018c41cbc6fa00a8e4c488f6527b07ed3956680b608"},"cssgb:set-2:source-11:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"II.E.1\",\"options\":[\"defects per time period, defects per opportunity, mean time between failures, and mean time to repair.\",\"defects in a process mean, defects in the capacity ratio, and sigma levels.\",\"rolled throughput yield, a mean divided by sigma, and defects per time period.\",\"defects per unit, parts per million, rolled throughput yield, sigma levels, and process capability indices.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":11,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":151,\"sourceQuestion\":11,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"Process performance is a measure of how the process is executed against an established goal. The process performance measures usually include:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Defects per unit. Defects per million, rolled throughput yield, sigma levels, and capability are used to describe process performance. <b>D. defects per unit, parts per million, rolled throughput yield, sigma levels, and process capability indices.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 11 (print p. 151), II.E.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"II.E.1","options":["defects per time period, defects per opportunity, mean time between failures, and mean time to repair.","defects in a process mean, defects in the capacity ratio, and sigma levels.","rolled throughput yield, a mean divided by sigma, and defects per time period.","defects per unit, parts per million, rolled throughput yield, sigma levels, and process capability indices."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":11,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":151,"sourceQuestion":11,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"Process performance is a measure of how the process is executed against an established goal. The process performance measures usually include:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Defects per unit. Defects per million, rolled throughput yield, sigma levels, and capability are used to describe process performance. <b>D. defects per unit, parts per million, rolled throughput yield, sigma levels, and process capability indices.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 11 (print p. 151), II.E.1.</span>"},"revision":"61720c268391e268633f3436f054c2acfeb19d6e823aaef9ffb1239074e01486"},"cssgb:set-2:source-120:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.1\",\"options\":[\"accelerating value-added functions.\",\"reducing or eliminating non-value-added functions.\",\"developing new manufacturing facilities with faster production lines.\",\"decreasing the prototype design phase.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":120,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":8,\"sourceQuestion\":20,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Studies have shown that an overwhelming percentage of lead time is non-value added, yet, over the years, efforts to decrease lead time have often focused on:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"Typically, working to accelerate value-added functions has been the drive for managers who direct the teams to work on those areas. <b>A. accelerating value-added functions.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 20 (print p. 8), I.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"I.B.1","options":["accelerating value-added functions.","reducing or eliminating non-value-added functions.","developing new manufacturing facilities with faster production lines.","decreasing the prototype design phase."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":120,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":8,"sourceQuestion":20,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Studies have shown that an overwhelming percentage of lead time is non-value added, yet, over the years, efforts to decrease lead time have often focused on:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"Typically, working to accelerate value-added functions has been the drive for managers who direct the teams to work on those areas. <b>A. accelerating value-added functions.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 20 (print p. 8), I.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"ee19d4032cb29b50fa4e57ca9a6712c77c05e6ebc6bfbbf1382c5f0994a27d04"},"cssgb:set-2:source-121:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.2\",\"options\":[\"can eliminate the defect count found in many processes.\",\"can fix prior mistakes in the organization process.\",\"ensures a reduction of more than 80 percent of efficiency issues.\",\"can help improve the efficiency and management of operations.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":121,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":8,\"sourceQuestion\":21,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"5S is a workplace organization method that:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"Note that even with the drive to improve the efficiency of operations, most attempts at sustaining 5S do not work well for organizations. <b>D. can help improve the efficiency and management of operations.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 21 (print p. 8), I.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"I.B.2","options":["can eliminate the defect count found in many processes.","can fix prior mistakes in the organization process.","ensures a reduction of more than 80 percent of efficiency issues.","can help improve the efficiency and management of operations."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":121,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":8,"sourceQuestion":21,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"5S is a workplace organization method that:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"Note that even with the drive to improve the efficiency of operations, most attempts at sustaining 5S do not work well for organizations. <b>D. can help improve the efficiency and management of operations.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 21 (print p. 8), I.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"4356660b3ef8671eb8ff77d6b63a55fd52cac84bcc73de2a006fcfe6a546b9ff"},"cssgb:set-2:source-122:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.1\",\"options\":[\"label unused items for the archives.\",\"remove unneeded items.\",\"clean up any mess.\",\"find a place for each item.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":122,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":8,\"sourceQuestion\":22,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The 5S step of sort means:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"In the 5S sort step, unneeded items are removed from the area. <b>B. remove unneeded items.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 22 (print p. 8), I.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"I.B.1","options":["label unused items for the archives.","remove unneeded items.","clean up any mess.","find a place for each item."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":122,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":8,"sourceQuestion":22,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The 5S step of sort means:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"In the 5S sort step, unneeded items are removed from the area. <b>B. remove unneeded items.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 22 (print p. 8), I.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"7891d4024e79010e93ccb340a5449f32ddcc473ab72a08eec376b9bad791eacb"},"cssgb:set-2:source-123:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.2\",\"options\":[\"Sort\",\"Sustain\",\"Shine\",\"Standardize\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":123,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":8,\"sourceQuestion\":23,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A team working in a lab cleans the hot plates and testing equipment in the steam baths. Which phase of 5S is it likely in?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"In the 5S shine step, the work area and equipment are cleaned. <b>C. Shine</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 23 (print p. 8), I.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"I.B.2","options":["Sort","Sustain","Shine","Standardize"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":123,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":8,"sourceQuestion":23,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A team working in a lab cleans the hot plates and testing equipment in the steam baths. Which phase of 5S is it likely in?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"In the 5S shine step, the work area and equipment are cleaned. <b>C. Shine</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 23 (print p. 8), I.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"bbe3b6d6021dc5573cc00b4efd963ba3772d868950ad51229cec5b7dbd707f98"},"cssgb:set-2:source-124:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.2\",\"options\":[\"creating checklists, standards, and work instructions to keep the work area in a clean and orderly condition.\",\"writing work procedures, posting OSHA standards, and writing flowcharts.\",\"creating strategic plans and delivering all-employee monthly standardized broadcasts about workplace cleanliness.\",\"creating work instructions and posting them in all areas with cleaning supplies.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":124,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":9,\"sourceQuestion\":24,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The 5S step of standardize involves:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"In the 5S standardize step, the team creates checklists, standards, and work instructions to keep the work area clean and orderly. <b>A. creating checklists, standards, and work instructions to keep the work area in a clean and orderly condition.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 24 (print p. 9), I.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"I.B.2","options":["creating checklists, standards, and work instructions to keep the work area in a clean and orderly condition.","writing work procedures, posting OSHA standards, and writing flowcharts.","creating strategic plans and delivering all-employee monthly standardized broadcasts about workplace cleanliness.","creating work instructions and posting them in all areas with cleaning supplies."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":124,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":9,"sourceQuestion":24,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The 5S step of standardize involves:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"In the 5S standardize step, the team creates checklists, standards, and work instructions to keep the work area clean and orderly. <b>A. creating checklists, standards, and work instructions to keep the work area in a clean and orderly condition.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 24 (print p. 9), I.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"fa63ab5de9a5e86933cf57190d0c62893627d6ec55f10e3b0070cb8f6c9e4c11"},"cssgb:set-2:source-125:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.1\",\"options\":[\"training and testing of all the 5S steps.\",\"management support and employee empowerment.\",\"additional training in accident reduction.\",\"engaging management in the cost reductions effected with 5S.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":125,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":9,\"sourceQuestion\":25,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The 5S step of sustain involves:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"In the 5S sustain step, management gives its support, and employees are empowered to maintain the gains achieved. <b>B. management support and employee empowerment.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 25 (print p. 9), I.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"I.B.1","options":["training and testing of all the 5S steps.","management support and employee empowerment.","additional training in accident reduction.","engaging management in the cost reductions effected with 5S."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":125,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":9,"sourceQuestion":25,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The 5S step of sustain involves:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"In the 5S sustain step, management gives its support, and employees are empowered to maintain the gains achieved. <b>B. management support and employee empowerment.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 25 (print p. 9), I.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"9d2609aabe0e37a17c85f793255ffd1b8c44bd5a18dc7131e9d7c59bbec32ec6"},"cssgb:set-2:source-126:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.1\",\"options\":[\"a problem-solving methodology applied to reduce energy usage.\",\"a problem-solving methodology that focuses on the weakest link in a chain of processes.\",\"a decision-making tool that focuses on prioritization of weak links.\",\"an analysis tool that uncovers covert processes.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":126,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":9,\"sourceQuestion\":26,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The theory of constraints is:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"The theory of constraints (TOC) is a problem-solving tool that focuses on the bottlenecks/weakest links in a process. <b>B. a problem-solving methodology that focuses on the weakest link in a chain of processes.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 26 (print p. 9), I.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"I.B.1","options":["a problem-solving methodology applied to reduce energy usage.","a problem-solving methodology that focuses on the weakest link in a chain of processes.","a decision-making tool that focuses on prioritization of weak links.","an analysis tool that uncovers covert processes."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":126,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":9,"sourceQuestion":26,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The theory of constraints is:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"The theory of constraints (TOC) is a problem-solving tool that focuses on the bottlenecks/weakest links in a process. <b>B. a problem-solving methodology that focuses on the weakest link in a chain of processes.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 26 (print p. 9), I.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"0778c5abcb1d6784b84123a441181b0a02638c804f5a652f930358d214e63e06"},"cssgb:set-2:source-127:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.1\",\"options\":[\"reduced waste.\",\"customer satisfaction.\",\"perfection.\",\"one-piece flow.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":127,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":9,\"sourceQuestion\":27,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The ultimate goal of lean is:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"While reduced waste, customer satisfaction, and one-piece flow are intermediate goals, the end goal of lean is perfection. <b>C. perfection.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 27 (print p. 9), I.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"I.B.1","options":["reduced waste.","customer satisfaction.","perfection.","one-piece flow."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":127,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":9,"sourceQuestion":27,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The ultimate goal of lean is:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"While reduced waste, customer satisfaction, and one-piece flow are intermediate goals, the end goal of lean is perfection. <b>C. perfection.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 27 (print p. 9), I.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"31623cd65c6c6c5cc80f528838a0d612d26647e4edfc250c2d709c18fea050df"},"cssgb:set-2:source-128:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.2\",\"options\":[\"an open system.\",\"a multiple-path production stream.\",\"a value stream.\",\"a transformation concept.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":128,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":9,\"sourceQuestion\":28,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The series of activities that an organization performs, such as ordering, designing, producing, and delivering products and services, is known as:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"A value stream is a series of activities that an organization performs, such as ordering, designing, producing, and delivering products and services. Value stream maps are constructed for the as-is (current), could-be, and should-be states. <b>C. a value stream.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 28 (print p. 9), I.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"I.B.2","options":["an open system.","a multiple-path production stream.","a value stream.","a transformation concept."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":128,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":9,"sourceQuestion":28,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The series of activities that an organization performs, such as ordering, designing, producing, and delivering products and services, is known as:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"A value stream is a series of activities that an organization performs, such as ordering, designing, producing, and delivering products and services. Value stream maps are constructed for the as-is (current), could-be, and should-be states. <b>C. a value stream.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 28 (print p. 9), I.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"eb61659e6fb1d4601cf0aadd11bb20fec29059d5b89bbda5fa287e5c09cdd44f"},"cssgb:set-2:source-129:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.2\",\"chart\":{\"type\":\"vsm-symbol\"},\"options\":[\"Supermarket\",\"Production kanban\",\"Customer\",\"Safety or buffer stock\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":129,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":10,\"sourceQuestion\":29,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The symbol below is used on a value stream map to denote which of the following items?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"The symbol below denotes a production kanban in a value stream map. <b>B. Production kanban</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 29 (print p. 10), I.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"I.B.2","chart":{"type":"vsm-symbol"},"options":["Supermarket","Production kanban","Customer","Safety or buffer stock"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":129,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":10,"sourceQuestion":29,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The symbol below is used on a value stream map to denote which of the following items?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"The symbol below denotes a production kanban in a value stream map. <b>B. Production kanban</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 29 (print p. 10), I.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"73812855c10f322e9cbfe5f43acb3a173f8c1da45cc9eb9f82c9712bfb30590d"},"cssgb:set-2:source-12:10hzvwm":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"I.C.3\",\"options\":[\"detection.\",\"occurrence.\",\"severity.\",\"FMEA.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":12,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":151,\"sourceQuestion\":12,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"The likelihood that current controls will prevent a failure from reaching the customer is called:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"The likelihood that current controls will prevent a failure from reaching the customer is called detection. <b>A. detection.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 12 (print p. 151), I.C.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"I.C.3","options":["detection.","occurrence.","severity.","FMEA."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":12,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":151,"sourceQuestion":12,"sourceSection":"I. Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"The likelihood that current controls will prevent a failure from reaching the customer is called:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"The likelihood that current controls will prevent a failure from reaching the customer is called detection. <b>A. detection.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 12 (print p. 151), I.C.3.</span>"},"revision":"6e2b8e08402c8c47072ebd06e81e041c6bf40d0e88fe2eac5da0a925af738c11"},"cssgb:set-2:source-130:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.2\",\"options\":[\"non-value added.\",\"value added.\",\"voice of the customer.\",\"kanban.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":130,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":10,\"sourceQuestion\":30,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"On a value-stream map, inspections would be classified as:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"Inspections are a form of overprocessing waste classified as non-value added activities on a value stream map. <b>A. non-value added.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 30 (print p. 10), I.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"I.B.2","options":["non-value added.","value added.","voice of the customer.","kanban."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":130,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":10,"sourceQuestion":30,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"On a value-stream map, inspections would be classified as:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"Inspections are a form of overprocessing waste classified as non-value added activities on a value stream map. <b>A. non-value added.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 30 (print p. 10), I.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"f9936bcddd4992a86ce685f81a43ab6096111a727ca10c2d9898722b9c647f7c"},"cssgb:set-2:source-131:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.2\",\"options\":[\"the cost of quality.\",\"waste.\",\"constraints.\",\"the value chain.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":131,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":10,\"sourceQuestion\":31,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Unused space, defects, and rework are examples of:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"Unused space, defects, and rework are all examples of waste. <b>B. waste.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 31 (print p. 10), I.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"I.B.2","options":["the cost of quality.","waste.","constraints.","the value chain."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":131,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":10,"sourceQuestion":31,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Unused space, defects, and rework are examples of:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"Unused space, defects, and rework are all examples of waste. <b>B. waste.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 31 (print p. 10), I.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"b10a82aaea9c11b93661b9d2d949675cea569654c31e78514f3f65439b926199"},"cssgb:set-2:source-132:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.1\",\"options\":[\"takt.\",\"a pull system.\",\"one-piece flow.\",\"single-minute exchange of dies (SMED).\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":132,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":10,\"sourceQuestion\":32,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The method that sets the pace of industrial manufacturing lines so that production cycle times can be matched to customer demand rates is called:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"Takt is a German word meaning to set a pace. It is a method that sets the pace of industrial manufacturing lines so that production cycle times can be matched to customer demand rates. <b>A. takt.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 32 (print p. 10), I.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"I.B.1","options":["takt.","a pull system.","one-piece flow.","single-minute exchange of dies (SMED)."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":132,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":10,"sourceQuestion":32,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The method that sets the pace of industrial manufacturing lines so that production cycle times can be matched to customer demand rates is called:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"Takt is a German word meaning to set a pace. It is a method that sets the pace of industrial manufacturing lines so that production cycle times can be matched to customer demand rates. <b>A. takt.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 32 (print p. 10), I.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"e029aa58d198b81325202fc24211f5fd1477bc588b0c617559299e3d19c64b73"},"cssgb:set-2:source-133:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.1\",\"options\":[\"0.67\",\"1.5\",\"40\",\"320\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":133,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":10,\"sourceQuestion\":33,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A customer requires 320 units per day. The plant operates continuously for one eight-hour shift. What is the required takt time in minutes per unit?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"Takt time is the rate in time per unit at which the process must complete units to meet customer demand. It is calculated as the ratio of the total time available divided by the number of units the customer requires. Here, we have eight hours of time, which equals 480 minutes, and a customer demand of 320 units per day. Takt time = available production time / customer demand = 480 / 320 = 1.5 minutes per unit. <b>B. 1.5</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 33 (print p. 10), I.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"I.B.1","options":["0.67","1.5","40","320"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":133,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":10,"sourceQuestion":33,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A customer requires 320 units per day. The plant operates continuously for one eight-hour shift. What is the required takt time in minutes per unit?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"Takt time is the rate in time per unit at which the process must complete units to meet customer demand. It is calculated as the ratio of the total time available divided by the number of units the customer requires. Here, we have eight hours of time, which equals 480 minutes, and a customer demand of 320 units per day. Takt time = available production time / customer demand = 480 / 320 = 1.5 minutes per unit. <b>B. 1.5</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 33 (print p. 10), I.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"0f09a74910b8abc98f1f45e82cb42e8717ef56f74c648e96874a852082abde37"},"cssgb:set-2:source-134:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.1\",\"options\":[\"Cause-and-effect diagram\",\"Root cause analysis\",\"Gemba walk\",\"Takt time analysis\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":134,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":11,\"sourceQuestion\":34,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following tools can be used during the improvement phase of the DMAIC model to evaluate workflow?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"Takt time analysis can be used during the improvement phase to evaluate workflow. <b>D. Takt time analysis</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 34 (print p. 11), I.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"I.B.1","options":["Cause-and-effect diagram","Root cause analysis","Gemba walk","Takt time analysis"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":134,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":11,"sourceQuestion":34,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which of the following tools can be used during the improvement phase of the DMAIC model to evaluate workflow?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"Takt time analysis can be used during the improvement phase to evaluate workflow. <b>D. Takt time analysis</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 34 (print p. 11), I.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"1121b7d31576c6816da0e906742053c2a7c00277cb8eaed04422266680a2a73a"},"cssgb:set-2:source-135:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.1\",\"options\":[\"a storage setting with little or no manufacturing material inventory on hand at the site.\",\"the rate at which you need to complete a product to meet customer demand.\",\"a holding place of manufacturing material inventory with little or no incoming inspection.\",\"an optimal material requirement planning system for a manufacturing process.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":135,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":11,\"sourceQuestion\":35,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Just-in-time (JIT) manufacturing can be described as:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"Just-in-Time (JIT) is an optimal material requirement planning system for a manufacturing process. Holding place and optimal materials are counter to lean methodologies and are not correct here. <b>D. an optimal material requirement planning system for a manufacturing process.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 35 (print p. 11), I.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"I.B.1","options":["a storage setting with little or no manufacturing material inventory on hand at the site.","the rate at which you need to complete a product to meet customer demand.","a holding place of manufacturing material inventory with little or no incoming inspection.","an optimal material requirement planning system for a manufacturing process."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":135,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":11,"sourceQuestion":35,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Just-in-time (JIT) manufacturing can be described as:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"Just-in-Time (JIT) is an optimal material requirement planning system for a manufacturing process. Holding place and optimal materials are counter to lean methodologies and are not correct here. <b>D. an optimal material requirement planning system for a manufacturing process.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 35 (print p. 11), I.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"8a39d94f15fcc9c79af89bbc0c827ae82f7cbffd25563ad16b0f173c70153b72"},"cssgb:set-2:source-136:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.1\",\"options\":[\"Variable change and TRIZ\",\"Just-in-time and jidoka\",\"Kaizen and trend\",\"Validation and jidoka\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":136,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":11,\"sourceQuestion\":36,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following methods comprise the two pillars of the Toyota Production System (TPS)?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"The two pillars of the Toyota Production System (TPS) are just-in-time and jidoka, or autonomation. <b>B. Just-in-time and jidoka</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 36 (print p. 11), I.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"I.B.1","options":["Variable change and TRIZ","Just-in-time and jidoka","Kaizen and trend","Validation and jidoka"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":136,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":11,"sourceQuestion":36,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which of the following methods comprise the two pillars of the Toyota Production System (TPS)?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"The two pillars of the Toyota Production System (TPS) are just-in-time and jidoka, or autonomation. <b>B. Just-in-time and jidoka</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 36 (print p. 11), I.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"6d5918827d691c187d66841b5c2baf315a154e3706a728fd10d88be1833946cd"},"cssgb:set-2:source-137:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.1\",\"options\":[\"graphic chart that illustrates the pace of product flow.\",\"graphic tool intended to show the flow or path that people, products, or paperwork take during normal operations.\",\"chart showing the critical path for people and products.\",\"messy roadmap that shows flow or paths of multiple employees, materials, and paperwork for manufacturing processes.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":137,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":11,\"sourceQuestion\":37,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A spaghetti diagram is best described as a:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"A spaghetti diagram is best described as a graphic tool intended to show the flow or path that people, products, or paperwork take during the course of normal operations. Some might argue d is the correct answer, but there may not be multiple people or processes involved. <b>B. graphic tool intended to show the flow or path that people, products, or paperwork take during normal operations.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 37 (print p. 11), I.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"I.B.1","options":["graphic chart that illustrates the pace of product flow.","graphic tool intended to show the flow or path that people, products, or paperwork take during normal operations.","chart showing the critical path for people and products.","messy roadmap that shows flow or paths of multiple employees, materials, and paperwork for manufacturing processes."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":137,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":11,"sourceQuestion":37,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A spaghetti diagram is best described as a:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"A spaghetti diagram is best described as a graphic tool intended to show the flow or path that people, products, or paperwork take during the course of normal operations. Some might argue d is the correct answer, but there may not be multiple people or processes involved. <b>B. graphic tool intended to show the flow or path that people, products, or paperwork take during normal operations.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 37 (print p. 11), I.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"fc18aaff5f0c9d4d6c2135c8052a65bae6149fca01a51f79cf43ebc098cbce0f"},"cssgb:set-2:source-138:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.1\",\"options\":[\"It shows the shape of a data distribution.\",\"It makes inefficiencies in layout and process flow evident.\",\"It identifies the root causes of a problem.\",\"It identifies the variability inherent in a process.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":138,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":11,\"sourceQuestion\":38,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following statements describes a benefit of a spaghetti diagram?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"A spaghetti diagram makes layout and process flow inefficiencies evident. <b>B. It makes inefficiencies in layout and process flow evident.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 38 (print p. 11), I.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"I.B.1","options":["It shows the shape of a data distribution.","It makes inefficiencies in layout and process flow evident.","It identifies the root causes of a problem.","It identifies the variability inherent in a process."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":138,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":11,"sourceQuestion":38,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which of the following statements describes a benefit of a spaghetti diagram?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"A spaghetti diagram makes layout and process flow inefficiencies evident. <b>B. It makes inefficiencies in layout and process flow evident.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 38 (print p. 11), I.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"71e53a62e44b418bcceaafca5fc6a903db20483c2e301c02c5ccb5035cfa4900"},"cssgb:set-2:source-139:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.1\",\"options\":[\"Kaizen event\",\"Kaizen blitz\",\"Kaikaku\",\"Kanban\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":139,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":12,\"sourceQuestion\":39,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is the Japanese term in lean that means breakthrough improvement or big change?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"Kaikaku is a breakthrough improvement. Kaizen refers to small event changes, and both events and blitzes are used for small changes. <b>C. Kaikaku</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 39 (print p. 12), I.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"I.B.1","options":["Kaizen event","Kaizen blitz","Kaikaku","Kanban"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":139,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":12,"sourceQuestion":39,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is the Japanese term in lean that means breakthrough improvement or big change?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"Kaikaku is a breakthrough improvement. Kaizen refers to small event changes, and both events and blitzes are used for small changes. <b>C. Kaikaku</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 39 (print p. 12), I.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"58ddcfcff2f73af33c87fe36c70144d03a2ef9218f4e3c7e9af1e075a410bd33"},"cssgb:set-2:source-13:10hzvwm":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.2\",\"chart\":{\"type\":\"vsm-supermarket\"},\"options\":[\"Safety or buffer stock\",\"Production kanban\",\"Inventory\",\"Supermarket\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":13,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":151,\"sourceQuestion\":13,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"What does the following symbol on a value stream map represent?\",\"sub\":\"c