m collaborates with designers to ensure that final design outputs include which of the desired attributes?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"Cost, test, and maintainability would be the three primary areas the design engineers need to include in their plans for the new product. Some other elements of the distractors may come into play as we move to sustainability, but a is the only fully correct answer here. <b>A. Design for cost, design for test, and design for maintainability</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 40 (print p. 12), I.C.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"I.C.1","options":["Design for cost, design for test, and design for maintainability","Design for ability, design for cost, and risk reduction","Design for repeatability, design for waste prevention, and design for reproducibility","Design for recyclability, design for gage repeatability and reproducibility (GR&R), and design for feasibility"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":140,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":12,"sourceQuestion":40,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The Six Sigma team collaborates with designers to ensure that final design outputs include which of the desired attributes?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"Cost, test, and maintainability would be the three primary areas the design engineers need to include in their plans for the new product. Some other elements of the distractors may come into play as we move to sustainability, but a is the only fully correct answer here. <b>A. Design for cost, design for test, and design for maintainability</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 40 (print p. 12), I.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"dfed9b8492b5ee74e1cfc5541546ebe1c9239a9ebb5e14270d4d52433212c3ee"},"cssgb:set-2:source-141:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"I.C.1\",\"options\":[\"functional inspection.\",\"first-article inspection.\",\"100 percent inspection.\",\"audit.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":141,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":12,\"sourceQuestion\":41,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"An onsite verification activity to ensure compliance with requirements is known as a(n):\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"An onsite verification activity to ensure compliance with requirements is known as an audit. <b>D. audit.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 41 (print p. 12), I.C.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"I.C.1","options":["functional inspection.","first-article inspection.","100 percent inspection.","audit."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":141,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":12,"sourceQuestion":41,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"An onsite verification activity to ensure compliance with requirements is known as a(n):","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"An onsite verification activity to ensure compliance with requirements is known as an audit. <b>D. audit.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 41 (print p. 12), I.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"9ce574c8324552035f07fbd6aa3ef298750848e75c8c9a5399efc956a72af8ed"},"cssgb:set-2:source-142:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent"