ive evidence, that specified requirements have been met. <b>A. confirmation, through the provision of objective evidence, that specified requirements have been fulfilled.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 42 (print p. 12), I.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"df3381877814e39806d5d685bc9d2f1ec7ac4f939f29164033e180e974683ed6"},"cssgb:set-2:source-143:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"I.C.1\",\"options\":[\"evaluating a process or product and determining what might cause it to fail.\",\"making educated assessments of potential failures.\",\"confirmation, via objective evidence, that the requirements for a specific intended use or application have been fulfilled.\",\"proof of concept at the final design stage that confirms the initiative.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":143,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":13,\"sourceQuestion\":43,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Validation can be defined as:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"Validation is the confirmation, through objective evidence, that the requirements for a specific intended use or application have been met. <b>C. confirmation, via objective evidence, that the requirements for a specific intended use or application have been fulfilled.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 43 (print p. 13), I.C.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"I.C.1","options":["evaluating a process or product and determining what might cause it to fail.","making educated assessments of potential failures.","confirmation, via objective evidence, that the requirements for a specific intended use or application have been fulfilled.","proof of concept at the final design stage that confirms the initiative."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":143,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":13,"sourceQuestion":43,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Validation can be defined as:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"Validation is the confirmation, through objective evidence, that the requirements for a specific intended use or application have been met. <b>C. confirmation, via objective evidence, that the requirements for a specific intended use or application have been fulfilled.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 43 (print p. 13), I.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"bd27a4ac00f685cc3807b391234987438fee4d256736fd87d16a1e46bb11c9b0"},"cssgb:set-2:source-144:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"I.C.2\",\"options\":[\"robustness.\",\"prevention.\",\"risk.\",\"opportunity.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":144,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":13,\"sourceQuestion\":44,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The primary purpose of a failure mode and effects analysis is the study of:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"FMEA is primarily a risk tool to ensure customer satisfaction. Other risk tools are the strengths-weaknesses-opportunities-threats (SWOT) and PESTLE. <b>C. risk.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 44 (print p. 13), I.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"I.C.2","options":["robustness.","prevention.","risk.","opportunity."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":144,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":13,"sourceQuestion":44,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The primary purpose of a failure mode and effects analysis is the study of:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"FMEA is primarily a risk tool to ensure customer satisfaction. Other risk tools are the strengths-weaknesses-opportunities-threats (SWOT) and PESTLE. <b>C. risk.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 44 (print p. 13), I.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"a85993ed5b6046edba008891a12fb882b31e4442e76a12bf93ded4dc1b5c17a0"},"cssgb:set-2:source-145:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"I.C.2\",\"options\":[\"schedule the milestones of a project.\",\"utilize spreadsheet software programs.\",\"use design experts in the process of FMEA.\",\"predict the highest risk of things that could go wrong.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":145,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":13,\"sourceQuestion\":45,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The fundamental reason for using an FMEA is to:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"FMEA is used to predict states with the highest risk based on severity, probability of occurrence, and probability of detection. Common forms are the design FMEA, process FMEA, system FMEA, or machinery FMEA. <b>D. predict the highest risk of things that could go wrong.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 45 (print p. 13), I.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"I.C.2","options":["schedule the milestones of a project.","utilize spreadsheet software programs.","use design experts in the process of FMEA.","predict the highest risk of things that could go wrong."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":145,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":13,"sourceQuestion":45,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The fundamental reason for using an FMEA is to:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"FMEA is used to predict states with the highest risk based on severity, probability of occurrence, and probability of detection. Common forms are the design FMEA, process FMEA, system FMEA, or machinery FMEA. <b>D. predict the highest risk of things that could go wrong.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 45 (print p. 13), I.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"9c4ba5b4489c2f48538ca57e987a7859c8fe0c3a9866809bb06a40d83459e053"},"cssgb:set-2:source-146:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"I.C.2\",\"options\":[\"Ignoring the risk due to its complexity\",\"Implementing corrective actions to reduce the risk\",\"Delegating responsibility to another department\",\"Reducing the scope of the process to avoid the risk\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":146,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":13,\"sourceQuestion\":46,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is the typical action taken when a high-risk priority number (RPN) is identified during a process FMEA?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"When a high RPN is identified, the primary action is implementing corrective actions to reduce the risk associated with the failure mode. <b>B. Implementing corrective actions to reduce the risk</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 46 (print p. 13), I.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"I.C.2","options":["Ignoring the risk due to its complexity","Implementing corrective actions to reduce the risk","Delegating responsibility to another department","Reducing the scope of the process to avoid the risk"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":146,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":13,"sourceQuestion":46,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is the typical action taken when a high-risk priority number (RPN) is identified during a process FMEA?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"When a high RPN is identified, the primary action is implementing corrective actions to reduce the risk associated with the failure mode. <b>B. Implementing corrective actions to reduce the risk</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 46 (print p. 13), I.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"d1fa521ee8126f78bf5a5db308c0b2eb88e7bfa0faafb9cc084c0fac57759e69"},"cssgb:set-2:source-147:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"I.C.2\",\"options\":[\"12\",\"15\",\"35\",\"105\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":147,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":13,\"sourceQuestion\":47,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A line item in an FMEA has a severity of 7, an occurrence score of 5, and a detection score of 3. What is the RPN?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"RPN = S × O × D = 7 × 5 × 3 = 105. <b>D. 105</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 47 (print p. 13), I.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"I.C.2","options":["12","15","35","105"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":147,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":13,"sourceQuestion":47,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A line item in an FMEA has a severity of 7, an occurrence score of 5, and a detection score of 3. What is the RPN?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"RPN = S × O × D = 7 × 5 × 3 = 105. <b>D. 105</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 47 (print p. 13), I.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"395fd8dc52acf51af8046337bb9ca195f9b7b74a2ee3cb185351bfbdf5d23cc7"},"cssgb:set-2:source-148:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"I.C.2\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Row\",\"Sev. (Current)\",\"Occ. (Current)\",\"Det. (Current)\",\"RPN (Current)\",\"Sev. (After)\",\"Occ. (After)\",\"Det. (After)\",\"RPN (After)\"],\"rows\":[[\"1\",\"3\",\"5\",\"10\",\"150\",\"3\",\"5\",\"4\",\"60\"],[\"2\",\"5\",\"10\",\"3\",\"150\",\"3\",\"5\",\"3\",\"45\"],[\"3\",\"10\",\"3\",\"5\",\"150\",\"10\",\"3\",\"1\",\"30\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"The RPNs are the same; no special effort is necessary.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":148,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":14,\"sourceQuestion\":48,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"On which row of the FMEA should a process improvement team focus their efforts for the current state of the process?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"If there is a tie in RPNs, the team should compare the severity ratings and act on the row with the highest severity score. Here, Row 3 shows a severity of 10. This should be the team’s focus. <b>C. 3</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 48 (print p. 14), I.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"I.C.2","chart":{"columns":["Row","Sev. (Current)","Occ. (Current)","Det. (Current)","RPN (Current)","Sev. (After)","Occ. (After)","Det. (After)","RPN (After)"],"rows":[["1","3","5","10","150","3","5","4","60"],["2","5","10","3","150","3","5","3","45"],["3","10","3","5","150","10","3","1","30"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["1","2","3","The RPNs are the same; no special effort is necessary."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":148,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":14,"sourceQuestion":48,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"On which row of the FMEA should a process improvement team focus their efforts for the current state of the process?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"If there is a tie in RPNs, the team should compare the severity ratings and act on the row with the highest severity score. Here, Row 3 shows a severity of 10. This should be the team’s focus. <b>C. 3</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 48 (print p. 14), I.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"61dda5e17cf880fe82937dda007f9b6c0e4106ac46d530b537d0368ae3fa2e72"},"cssgb:set-2:source-149:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"I.C.2\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Row\",\"Sev. (Current)\",\"Occ. (Current)\",\"Det. (Current)\",\"RPN (Current)\",\"Sev. (After)\",\"Occ. (After)\",\"Det. (After)\",\"RPN (After)\"],\"rows\":[[\"1\",\"3\",\"5\",\"10\",\"150\",\"3\",\"5\",\"4\",\"60\"],[\"2\",\"5\",\"10\",\"3\",\"150\",\"3\",\"5\",\"3\",\"45\"],[\"3\",\"10\",\"3\",\"5\",\"150\",\"10\",\"3\",\"1\",\"30\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"2, 3, 1\",\"3, 1, 2\",\"3, 2, 1\",\"The RPNs are the same, so no ranking is needed.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":149,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":14,\"sourceQuestion\":49,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Give the rows of the current state of the process in order of highest to lowest risk.\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"Since the RPNs are tied, we rank the risks of the rows by severity. Row 3 has the highest severity, followed by Row 2, then Row 1. <b>C. 3, 2, 1</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 49 (print p. 14), I.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"I.C.2","chart":{"columns":["Row","Sev. (Current)","Occ. (Current)","Det. (Current)","RPN (Current)","Sev. (After)","Occ. (After)","Det. (After)","RPN (After)"],"rows":[["1","3","5","10","150","3","5","4","60"],["2","5","10","3","150","3","5","3","45"],["3","10","3","5","150","10","3","1","30"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["2, 3, 1","3, 1, 2","3, 2, 1","The RPNs are the same, so no ranking is needed."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":149,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":14,"sourceQuestion":49,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Give the rows of the current state of the process in order of highest to lowest risk.","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"Since the RPNs are tied, we rank the risks of the rows by severity. Row 3 has the highest severity, followed by Row 2, then Row 1. <b>C. 3, 2, 1</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 49 (print p. 14), I.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"3a08d2461933afed473836fc38968d1088a67fc8cf2b6b2b876c791279c3eaa7"},"cssgb:set-2:source-14:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"II.D\",\"options\":[\"all suspicious outlying effects.\",\"factors in a production cycle.\",\"the use of endcaps in marketing.\",\"positive effects as well as undesirable ones.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":14,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":151,\"sourceQuestion\":14,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"Cause-and-effect diagrams can be used to analyze:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Also used as a risk tool, the C&amp;E is not bound by the negative or positive and can be used to help understand the process. <b>D. positive effects as well as undesirable ones.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 14 (print p. 151), II.D.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"II.D","options":["all suspicious outlying effects.","factors in a production cycle.","the use of endcaps in marketing.","positive effects as well as undesirable ones."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":14,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":151,"sourceQuestion":14,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"Cause-and-effect diagrams can be used to analyze:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Also used as a risk tool, the C&amp;E is not bound by the negative or positive and can be used to help understand the process. <b>D. positive effects as well as undesirable ones.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 14 (print p. 151), II.D.</span>"},"revision":"477447283ffa37d1180520bf0d9026b33a0a15eeb1d52d9c31d60ef57cb43d90"},"cssgb:set-2:source-150:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"I.C.2\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Row\",\"Sev. (Current)\",\"Occ. (Current)\",\"Det. (Current)\",\"RPN (Current)\",\"Sev. (After)\",\"Occ. (After)\",\"Det. (After)\",\"RPN (After)\"],\"rows\":[[\"1\",\"3\",\"5\",\"10\",\"150\",\"3\",\"5\",\"4\",\"60\"],[\"2\",\"5\",\"10\",\"3\",\"150\",\"3\",\"5\",\"3\",\"45\"],[\"3\",\"10\",\"3\",\"5\",\"150\",\"10\",\"3\",\"1\",\"30\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"Row 1\",\"Row 2\",\"Row 3\",\"None; the risk has been mitigated.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":150,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":14,\"sourceQuestion\":50,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"After action has been taken, which row now shows the highest risk?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"The RPN of Row 1 is now the highest risk with RPN = 60. <b>A. Row 1</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 50 (print p. 14), I.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"I.C.2","chart":{"columns":["Row","Sev. (Current)","Occ. (Current)","Det. (Current)","RPN (Current)","Sev. (After)","Occ. (After)","Det. (After)","RPN (After)"],"rows":[["1","3","5","10","150","3","5","4","60"],["2","5","10","3","150","3","5","3","45"],["3","10","3","5","150","10","3","1","30"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["Row 1","Row 2","Row 3","None; the risk has been mitigated."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":150,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":14,"sourceQuestion":50,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"After action has been taken, which row now shows the highest risk?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"The RPN of Row 1 is now the highest risk with RPN = 60. <b>A. Row 1</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 50 (print p. 14), I.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"8568aaca618062c1ba60e023148a83439d23e631f6edfbaa07680ac32711e4fd"},"cssgb:set-2:source-151:epnw8v":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"I.C.3\",\"options\":[\"prototype brainstorm.\",\"product design.\",\"product packaging.\",\"design engineer training.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":151,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":14,\"sourceQuestion\":51,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The focus of a design FMEA is:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"The design FMEA (DFMEA) looks at a product design. <b>B. product design.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 51 (print p. 14), I.C.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"I.C.3","options":["prototype brainstorm.","product design.","product packaging.","design engineer training."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter1.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":151,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":14,"sourceQuestion":51,"sourceSection":"I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The focus of a design FMEA is:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"The design FMEA (DFMEA) looks at a product design. <b>B. product design.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A I. Overview: Six Sigma and the Organization, Question 51 (print p. 14), I.C.3.</span>"},"revision":"065345de4424b764a58031f7670b69cbe62b61c2371e2d8cefcd027f6f13eb5b"},"cssgb:set-2:source-152:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"II.A.1\",\"options\":[\"problem or issue to be worked on.\",\"desired project outcome.\",\"budgetary constraints.\",\"solution to the problem.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":152,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":21,\"sourceQuestion\":1,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The define phase of the DMAIC model serves two primary purposes, which are to define the project management process and the:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"In the define phase of a Six Sigma project, the project management process and the problem or issue to be worked on are defined. <b>A. problem or issue to be worked on.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 1 (print p. 21), II.A.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"II.A.1","options":["problem or issue to be worked on.","desired project outcome.","budgetary constraints.","solution to the problem."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":152,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":21,"sourceQuestion":1,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The define phase of the DMAIC model serves two primary purposes, which are to define the project management process and the:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"In the define phase of a Six Sigma project, the project management process and the problem or issue to be worked on are defined. <b>A. problem or issue to be worked on.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 1 (print p. 21), II.A.1.</span>"},"revision":"dbbf123f5726d51980166056fabccee0fc99f393281fd386527b042461972a24"},"cssgb:set-2:source-153:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"II.A.1\",\"options\":[\"be certain to include other critical organizational goals.\",\"identify the cost reduction terms first.\",\"always select the projects with known solutions.\",\"establish a set of criteria for project selection.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":153,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":22,\"sourceQuestion\":2,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The project selection group (Master Black Belts, Black Belts, Key Executives, and Champions) should:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Establishing a set of criteria for project selection is the standard for selecting Six Sigma projects. <b>D. establish a set of criteria for project selection.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 2 (print p. 22), II.A.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"II.A.1","options":["be certain to include other critical organizational goals.","identify the cost reduction terms first.","always select the projects with known solutions.","establish a set of criteria for project selection."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":153,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":22,"sourceQuestion":2,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The project selection group (Master Black Belts, Black Belts, Key Executives, and Champions) should:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Establishing a set of criteria for project selection is the standard for selecting Six Sigma projects. <b>D. establish a set of criteria for project selection.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 2 (print p. 22), II.A.1.</span>"},"revision":"4bbe3a9dd2252207aa68441d6ab6fcda5565b8e16ad103c4bd34750de8c8e465"},"cssgb:set-2:source-154:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.A.2\",\"options\":[\"environmental challenges.\",\"boundaries.\",\"history.\",\"business plan.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":154,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":22,\"sourceQuestion\":3,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"When planning to study a process or system, it is very important to first identify the process:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"The first step in any process work is to identify the boundaries for the process that you intend to investigate. Then go to Gemba—go and see what is actually being done. <b>B. boundaries.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 3 (print p. 22), II.A.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.A.2","options":["environmental challenges.","boundaries.","history.","business plan."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":154,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":22,"sourceQuestion":3,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"When planning to study a process or system, it is very important to first identify the process:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"The first step in any process work is to identify the boundaries for the process that you intend to investigate. Then go to Gemba—go and see what is actually being done. <b>B. boundaries.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 3 (print p. 22), II.A.2.</span>"},"revision":"0b47094f14c455c2c582906d64f0b959402d6f0db05cb944dd1b2aed411f0d49"},"cssgb:set-2:source-155:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"II.A.2\",\"options\":[\"an effective business plan.\",\"part of a good strategic plan.\",\"critical analysis.\",\"systems thinking.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":155,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":22,\"sourceQuestion\":4,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Recognizing that if one process factor is changed or influenced in some particular way, then something different might happen downstream in the process is commonly referred to as:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Systems thinking is defined as recognizing that if one process factor is changed or influenced in some particular way, then something different might happen downstream in the process. <b>D. systems thinking.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 4 (print p. 22), II.A.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"II.A.2","options":["an effective business plan.","part of a good strategic plan.","critical analysis.","systems thinking."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":155,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":22,"sourceQuestion":4,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Recognizing that if one process factor is changed or influenced in some particular way, then something different might happen downstream in the process is commonly referred to as:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Systems thinking is defined as recognizing that if one process factor is changed or influenced in some particular way, then something different might happen downstream in the process. <b>D. systems thinking.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 4 (print p. 22), II.A.2.</span>"},"revision":"7cd73bee1370196ca9d999f47799e52c07143dcd1cb22f969fab87e3469716d5"},"cssgb:set-2:source-156:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"II.A.3\",\"options\":[\"Internal\",\"Competitive\",\"Functional\",\"Collaborative\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":156,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":22,\"sourceQuestion\":5,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Going to a local fast-food restaurant to understand how its processes work would be an example of which type of benchmarking?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Understanding how processes work is an example of functional benchmarking. <b>C. Functional</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 5 (print p. 22), II.A.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.A.3","options":["Internal","Competitive","Functional","Collaborative"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":156,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":22,"sourceQuestion":5,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Going to a local fast-food restaurant to understand how its processes work would be an example of which type of benchmarking?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Understanding how processes work is an example of functional benchmarking. <b>C. Functional</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 5 (print p. 22), II.A.3.</span>"},"revision":"b332cc16e2ce9939f609bf31cae72df83c5f3b26d2d50cd95c18ab106faa90b4"},"cssgb:set-2:source-157:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"II.A.3\",\"options\":[\"internal, external, and subgroup.\",\"team, individual, competitive, and extreme.\",\"internal, competitive, functional, and collaborative.\",\"external, community, and cultural.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":157,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":22,\"sourceQuestion\":6,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The following are types of benchmarking:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Common types of benchmarking include internal, competitive, functional, and collaborative. <b>C. internal, competitive, functional, and collaborative.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 6 (print p. 22), II.A.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.A.3","options":["internal, external, and subgroup.","team, individual, competitive, and extreme.","internal, competitive, functional, and collaborative.","external, community, and cultural."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":157,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":22,"sourceQuestion":6,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The following are types of benchmarking:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Common types of benchmarking include internal, competitive, functional, and collaborative. <b>C. internal, competitive, functional, and collaborative.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 6 (print p. 22), II.A.3.</span>"},"revision":"df68b0df508a00910c2351baec10121ae9cfe1f2db54731b6d19871f833b6b03"},"cssgb:set-2:source-158:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"II.A.4\",\"options\":[\"SIPOC chart\",\"Cause-and-effect matrix\",\"Scatter diagram\",\"Critical to quality tree\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":158,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":23,\"sourceQuestion\":7,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which tool helps a team to quickly familiarize themselves with the process at an organizational level and visually understand the scope of the project?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"A SIPOC chart lists suppliers, inputs, process steps, outputs, and customers at a high level. It helps the team understand the process at an organizational level, as well as the scope of the project. <b>A. SIPOC chart</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 7 (print p. 23), II.A.4.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"II.A.4","options":["SIPOC chart","Cause-and-effect matrix","Scatter diagram","Critical to quality tree"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":158,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":23,"sourceQuestion":7,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which tool helps a team to quickly familiarize themselves with the process at an organizational level and visually understand the scope of the project?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"A SIPOC chart lists suppliers, inputs, process steps, outputs, and customers at a high level. It helps the team understand the process at an organizational level, as well as the scope of the project. <b>A. SIPOC chart</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 7 (print p. 23), II.A.4.</span>"},"revision":"82d6eaab20f763e94b7b09bef47d328ddeee3f721e65585ed9b09b0b2369e74c"},"cssgb:set-2:source-159:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"II.A.5\",\"options\":[\"have a vested interest in the process and/or its products and outputs.\",\"are responsible for adding more funding to the project as needed.\",\"meet with the Six Sigma team to report on the progress of the project’s effectiveness.\",\"are always subject matter experts within the project’s scope.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":159,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":23,\"sourceQuestion\":8,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Stakeholders (e.g., customers, suppliers, employees, investors) generally:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Another term from ISO MSS is interested parties, with stakeholder being one of these. Thus, stakeholders are interested in the outcomes of the Six Sigma team. <b>A. have a vested interest in the process and/or its products and outputs.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 8 (print p. 23), II.A.5.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"II.A.5","options":["have a vested interest in the process and/or its products and outputs.","are responsible for adding more funding to the project as needed.","meet with the Six Sigma team to report on the progress of the project’s effectiveness.","are always subject matter experts within the project’s scope."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":159,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":23,"sourceQuestion":8,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Stakeholders (e.g., customers, suppliers, employees, investors) generally:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Another term from ISO MSS is interested parties, with stakeholder being one of these. Thus, stakeholders are interested in the outcomes of the Six Sigma team. <b>A. have a vested interest in the process and/or its products and outputs.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 8 (print p. 23), II.A.5.</span>"},"revision":"2282cb1e42ad8c1a530e3af348f30d98850e2166664881e08ae192b57a0f47dc"},"cssgb:set-2:source-15:10hzvwm":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.2\",\"options\":[\"Sort, set in order, shine, standardize, and sustain\",\"Set in order, strategic catalog, shine wax parts, and sustain gains\",\"Situate in alphabetical order, select each part, and set into appropriate containers\",\"Strategize and plan, select dirty and damaged items, sanitize with organic cleansers, sterile dry, and situate into storage\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":15,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":152,\"sourceQuestion\":15,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"5S is one of the first tools to apply in the path to achieving lean enterprise organizations. The traditional sequence for 5S includes which of the following?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"The 5S steps include sort, set in order, shine, standardize, and sustain. <b>A. Sort, set in order, shine, standardize, and sustain</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 15 (print p. 152), I.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"I.B.2","options":["Sort, set in order, shine, standardize, and sustain","Set in order, strategic catalog, shine wax parts, and sustain gains","Situate in alphabetical order, select each part, and set into appropriate containers","Strategize and plan, select dirty and damaged items, sanitize with organic cleansers, sterile dry, and situate into storage"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":15,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":152,"sourceQuestion":15,"sourceSection":"I. Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"5S is one of the first tools to apply in the path to achieving lean enterprise organizations. The traditional sequence for 5S includes which of the following?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"The 5S steps include sort, set in order, shine, standardize, and sustain. <b>A. Sort, set in order, shine, standardize, and sustain</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 15 (print p. 152), I.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"62d46f274d3a1821e06f933ff60ebb65d5401de0ddcc84ddbd4d95db7fc09a9e"},"cssgb:set-2:source-160:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.A.5\",\"options\":[\"subject matter experts.\",\"process owners.\",\"top management.\",\"stakeholders.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":160,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":23,\"sourceQuestion\":9,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Those who have responsibility for the execution and implementation of specific tasks or activities being worked on are:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Process owners have responsibility for the execution and implementation of specific tasks or activities being worked on. <b>B. process owners.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 9 (print p. 23), II.A.5.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.A.5","options":["subject matter experts.","process owners.","top management.","stakeholders."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":160,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":23,"sourceQuestion":9,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Those who have responsibility for the execution and implementation of specific tasks or activities being worked on are:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Process owners have responsibility for the execution and implementation of specific tasks or activities being worked on. <b>B. process owners.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 9 (print p. 23), II.A.5.</span>"},"revision":"ceabe2ad6dade00dd9f75b1fab7d5556255ee21634446eda8c2cda1438e5bb98"},"cssgb:set-2:source-161:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.B.1\",\"options\":[\"internal customer.\",\"external customer.\",\"process owner.\",\"champion.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":161,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":23,\"sourceQuestion\":10,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A human resources department is addressing its onboarding process to eliminate redundant steps. For this project, a new hire would be considered a(n):\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"The human resource employees would be internal process customers, and the new hires would be considered external customers of the process. <b>B. external customer.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 10 (print p. 23), II.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.B.1","options":["internal customer.","external customer.","process owner.","champion."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":161,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":23,"sourceQuestion":10,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A human resources department is addressing its onboarding process to eliminate redundant steps. For this project, a new hire would be considered a(n):","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"The human resource employees would be internal process customers, and the new hires would be considered external customers of the process. <b>B. external customer.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 10 (print p. 23), II.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"b1ef744a32cc775e1a0430fe42fffc8acbb2eac2e69606244b600393a6cd7e84"},"cssgb:set-2:source-162:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.B.2\",\"options\":[\"observation, cause-and-effect diagram, and Pareto charts.\",\"surveys, quality function deployment (QFD), interviews, and focus groups.\",\"phone surveys, mailed questionnaires, online surveys, and FMEAs.\",\"secret shopper, random selection X-ray scans, and SPC.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":162,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":23,\"sourceQuestion\":11,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"There are several tools for gathering customer data, including:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Surveys, quality function deployment (QFD), interviews, and focus groups are all tools for gathering voice of the customer data. <b>B. surveys, quality function deployment (QFD), interviews, and focus groups.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 11 (print p. 23), II.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.B.2","options":["observation, cause-and-effect diagram, and Pareto charts.","surveys, quality function deployment (QFD), interviews, and focus groups.","phone surveys, mailed questionnaires, online surveys, and FMEAs.","secret shopper, random selection X-ray scans, and SPC."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":162,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":23,"sourceQuestion":11,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"There are several tools for gathering customer data, including:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Surveys, quality function deployment (QFD), interviews, and focus groups are all tools for gathering voice of the customer data. <b>B. surveys, quality function deployment (QFD), interviews, and focus groups.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 11 (print p. 23), II.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"d3c08ebf02e4e2ed3298bc48fa53b3573b28caddd23a135383ce06d21e07e6d4"},"cssgb:set-2:source-163:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"II.B.2\",\"options\":[\"Randomly selected large groups for interviews\",\"Randomly selected large groups for surveys\",\"Randomly selected small groups for mailed questionnaires\",\"Small focus groups\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":163,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":24,\"sourceQuestion\":12,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Statistically, what is the most valid procedure for collecting customer data?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Remember that reasonably large groups of customers are needed to obtain complete and accurate data on each topic selected; surveys cannot do this. To improve accuracy, increase the sample size. <b>A. Randomly selected large groups for interviews</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 12 (print p. 24), II.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"II.B.2","options":["Randomly selected large groups for interviews","Randomly selected large groups for surveys","Randomly selected small groups for mailed questionnaires","Small focus groups"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":163,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":24,"sourceQuestion":12,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Statistically, what is the most valid procedure for collecting customer data?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Remember that reasonably large groups of customers are needed to obtain complete and accurate data on each topic selected; surveys cannot do this. To improve accuracy, increase the sample size. <b>A. Randomly selected large groups for interviews</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 12 (print p. 24), II.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"6e9449dba3648904dead7af2f8586aab10a9d5ddf5abddb65bf4a48ad6e14234"},"cssgb:set-2:source-164:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.B.3\",\"options\":[\"design and redesign.\",\"quality.\",\"linkages.\",\"product planning.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":164,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":24,\"sourceQuestion\":13,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The primary matrix used in quality function deployment (QFD) to translate customer needs into technical requirements is called the house of:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"The House of Quality is the primary matrix used within QFD to translate customer needs into technical requirements. It is not another name for the entire QFD method. <b>B. quality.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 13 (print p. 24), II.B.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.B.3","options":["design and redesign.","quality.","linkages.","product planning."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":164,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":24,"sourceQuestion":13,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The primary matrix used in quality function deployment (QFD) to translate customer needs into technical requirements is called the house of:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"The House of Quality is the primary matrix used within QFD to translate customer needs into technical requirements. It is not another name for the entire QFD method. <b>B. quality.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 13 (print p. 24), II.B.3.</span>"},"revision":"6d189f12249c002805735f68876df6a5a79aa1d802d0c0356369db81e6cdde5d"},"cssgb:set-2:source-165:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"II.B.3\",\"options\":[\"PDCA\",\"Spaghetti diagram\",\"Ishikawa chart\",\"Kano model\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":165,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":24,\"sourceQuestion\":14,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which graphical tool best shows the relationship between product features and customer satisfaction?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"A Kano model is a graphical tool for showing the relationship between product features and customer satisfaction. <b>D. Kano model</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 14 (print p. 24), II.B.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"II.B.3","options":["PDCA","Spaghetti diagram","Ishikawa chart","Kano model"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":165,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":24,"sourceQuestion":14,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which graphical tool best shows the relationship between product features and customer satisfaction?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"A Kano model is a graphical tool for showing the relationship between product features and customer satisfaction. <b>D. Kano model</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 14 (print p. 24), II.B.3.</span>"},"revision":"41cbbde5eacd58a12a489f53b981248c76359594cb88eb80243fa92d18a49798"},"cssgb:set-2:source-166:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.B.3\",\"options\":[\"determine takt time.\",\"discover customer needs and wants.\",\"improve throughput.\",\"move teams from storming to performing.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":166,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":24,\"sourceQuestion\":15,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The primary focus of a Kano analysis is to:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"The primary focus of Kano analysis is to discover customer needs and wants. <b>B. discover customer needs and wants.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 15 (print p. 24), II.B.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.B.3","options":["determine takt time.","discover customer needs and wants.","improve throughput.","move teams from storming to performing."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":166,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":24,"sourceQuestion":15,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The primary focus of a Kano analysis is to:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"The primary focus of Kano analysis is to discover customer needs and wants. <b>B. discover customer needs and wants.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 15 (print p. 24), II.B.3.</span>"},"revision":"09b3025de5099173889273a9c2db897558db0a57dd749f54599c67e40eb18f97"},"cssgb:set-2:source-167:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"II.B.3\",\"options\":[\"source dysfunctional/service fully functional.\",\"delighters/satisfiers.\",\"dissatisfaction/satisfaction.\",\"one-dimensional versus two-dimensional.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":167,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":24,\"sourceQuestion\":16,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The traditional Kano model y-axis shows:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"In the traditional Kano model, the vertical axis represents customer dissatisfaction through customer satisfaction. <b>C. dissatisfaction/satisfaction.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 16 (print p. 24), II.B.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.B.3","options":["source dysfunctional/service fully functional.","delighters/satisfiers.","dissatisfaction/satisfaction.","one-dimensional versus two-dimensional."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":167,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":24,"sourceQuestion":16,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The traditional Kano model y-axis shows:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"In the traditional Kano model, the vertical axis represents customer dissatisfaction through customer satisfaction. <b>C. dissatisfaction/satisfaction.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 16 (print p. 24), II.B.3.</span>"},"revision":"d97612878aeb6435e888e11c37767c6a344ab0b47f93c06c9321b4d174a632b7"},"cssgb:set-2:source-168:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"II.B.3\",\"options\":[\"Basic needs\",\"Performance needs\",\"Delighters\",\"Indifferent needs\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":168,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":25,\"sourceQuestion\":17,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"In the Kano model, which category represents features that, when present, lead to higher customer satisfaction, but when absent, do not result in dissatisfaction?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Delighters are features that, when present, exceed customer expectations and lead to higher satisfaction levels, but their absence doesn’t result in dissatisfaction. <b>C. Delighters</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 17 (print p. 25), II.B.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.B.3","options":["Basic needs","Performance needs","Delighters","Indifferent needs"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":168,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":25,"sourceQuestion":17,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"In the Kano model, which category represents features that, when present, lead to higher customer satisfaction, but when absent, do not result in dissatisfaction?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Delighters are features that, when present, exceed customer expectations and lead to higher satisfaction levels, but their absence doesn’t result in dissatisfaction. <b>C. Delighters</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 17 (print p. 25), II.B.3.</span>"},"revision":"7b72b8418da76e0eb857181b8dcbf372280ceb02859b10cc5a1add390a91ad3a"},"cssgb:set-2:source-169:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.B.3\",\"options\":[\"the customer will be delighted with the product.\",\"the longer the battery life, the happier the customer will be.\",\"the customer is indifferent to the length of battery life.\",\"increasing battery life will not improve customer satisfaction.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":169,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":25,\"sourceQuestion\":18,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A Kano analysis shows that battery life is a one-dimensional requirement. This means that:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Customer satisfaction increases as a product or service delivers more of a one-dimensional requirement. <b>B. the longer the battery life, the happier the customer will be.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 18 (print p. 25), II.B.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.B.3","options":["the customer will be delighted with the product.","the longer the battery life, the happier the customer will be.","the customer is indifferent to the length of battery life.","increasing battery life will not improve customer satisfaction."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":169,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":25,"sourceQuestion":18,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A Kano analysis shows that battery life is a one-dimensional requirement. This means that:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Customer satisfaction increases as a product or service delivers more of a one-dimensional requirement. <b>B. the longer the battery life, the happier the customer will be.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 18 (print p. 25), II.B.3.</span>"},"revision":"11a960501c0cc0f944d4f3db8b3e1f4f79547b4a5936e1e02aaae50ff32ceefb"},"cssgb:set-2:source-16:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"V.A.2\",\"options\":[\"6\",\"12\",\"32\",\"64\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":16,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":152,\"sourceQuestion\":16,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"A half-fraction of a 2⁶ experiment has how many runs?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"A half-fraction uses 2^(k−1) runs. With k = 6 factors, 2^(6−1) = 2⁵ = 32 runs. <b>C. 32</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 16 (print p. 152), V.A.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"V.A.2","options":["6","12","32","64"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":16,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":152,"sourceQuestion":16,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"A half-fraction of a 2⁶ experiment has how many runs?","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"A half-fraction uses 2^(k−1) runs. With k = 6 factors, 2^(6−1) = 2⁵ = 32 runs. <b>C. 32</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 16 (print p. 152), V.A.2.</span>"},"revision":"02301ec8ac341a8037f1fb81eee683d27576b237c662a365def17cd1eca00eca"},"cssgb:set-2:source-170:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"II.B.3\",\"options\":[\"customers.\",\"suppliers.\",\"the management team.\",\"process.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":170,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":25,\"sourceQuestion\":19,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Critical-to-X factors found in Six Sigma should be focused on:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Customer, always. There are several critical-to-X factors, and all of them will end up with a focus on customers of the products, processes, or services. <b>A. customers.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 19 (print p. 25), II.B.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"II.B.3","options":["customers.","suppliers.","the management team.","process."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":170,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":25,"sourceQuestion":19,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Critical-to-X factors found in Six Sigma should be focused on:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Customer, always. There are several critical-to-X factors, and all of them will end up with a focus on customers of the products, processes, or services. <b>A. customers.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 19 (print p. 25), II.B.3.</span>"},"revision":"103bf87503b717a17b6a05f43a2e5a804eda2a9dab11a3306e98fee99afdcf92"},"cssgb:set-2:sourc