,\"sourcePrintPage\":25,\"sourceQuestion\":21,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The term coined in the lean approach/lean thinking that focuses on reducing cycle time and waste is called the:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"The agile approach focuses on reducing cycle time and waste. <b>D. agile approach.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 21 (print p. 25), II.C.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"II.C.1","options":["Ishikawa diagram.","Kano model.","loss function.","agile approach."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":172,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":25,"sourceQuestion":21,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The term coined in the lean approach/lean thinking that focuses on reducing cycle time and waste is called the:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"The agile approach focuses on reducing cycle time and waste. <b>D. agile approach.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 21 (print p. 25), II.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"b07d9365114c5bfc66386a24e9c820cc44edcb4c2ddd440b26bb6205eeda815b"},"cssgb:set-2:source-173:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"II.C.1\",\"options\":[\"information processing, communication, resource negotiations, securing commitments, incremental planning, assuring measurable milestones, and facilitating top management involvement.\",\"communication, statistical process control, risk-based thinking, problem-solving, resource negotiations, facilitation skills, and team negotiating skills.\",\"information processing, decision-making, legal analysis, and securing commitments.\",\"information processing, advance product quality planning, risk-b