sure phase of a Six Sigma project. <b>B. A process map</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 30 (print p. 27), II.C.6.</span>"},"revision":"bde9b1c1dcc48902f1b711dfe2bbfac8750d495acdb70ef8f56de6747c1d6c99"},"cssgb:set-2:source-182:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"II.C.6\",\"options\":[\"Root cause analysis\",\"Control plan\",\"Pilot test results\",\"Process map\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":182,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":28,\"sourceQuestion\":31,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which document would be most appropriate to present in a tollgate meeting in the analyze phase of a Six Sigma project?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Root cause analysis is a key deliverable for the analyze phase of a Six Sigma project. <b>A. Root cause analysis</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 31 (print p. 28), II.C.6.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"II.C.6","options":["Root cause analysis","Control plan","Pilot test results","Process map"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":182,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":28,"sourceQuestion":31,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which document would be most appropriate to present in a tollgate meeting in the analyze phase of a Six Sigma project?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Root cause analysis is a key deliverable for the analyze phase of a Six Sigma project. <b>A. Root cause analysis</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 31 (print p. 28), II.C.6.</span>"},"revision":"3e384806a778f3116d68dfa54fe11d56aa73ffa1bb8da1463c4468b17f019bd5"},"cssgb:set-2:source-183:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.C.6\",\"options\":[\"Force field analysis\",\"Tollgates\",\"Check sheets\",\"Plan-do-check-act\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":183,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":28,\"sourceQuestion\":32,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which project-governance mechanism is used to confirm at the end of each phase that a project remains on track for its intended outcomes?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Tollgates are formal reviews conducted at phase boundaries to confirm that required work is complete and the project should continue. Advanced product quality planning (APQP) may use gate reviews, but APQP and tollgates are not synonyms. <b>B. Tollgates</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 32 (print p. 28), II.C.6.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.C.6","options":["Force field analysis","Tollgates","Check sheets","Plan-do-check-act"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":183,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":28,"sourceQuestion":32,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which project-governance mechanism is used to confirm at the end of each phase that a project remains on track for its intended outcomes?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Tollgates are formal reviews conducted at phase boundaries to confirm that required work is complete and the project should continue. Advanced product quality planning (APQP) may use gate reviews, but APQP and tollgates are not synonyms. <b>B. Tollgates</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 32 (print p. 28), II.C.6.</span>"},"revision":"27c654b6cef1dd911ca79973721ed00a5b5c763f79118f6814da1e6e265f6188"},"cssgb:set-2:source-184:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"II.C.7\",\"options\":[\"all internal and external customers contribute safety data prior to the first risk analysis meeting.\",\"warranty and reliability data are treated as the most important inputs.\",\"all appropriate organizations are represented during risk analysis meetings, with all aspects of potential risk considered.\",\"risk mitigation and verification are performed only at the final stages of the analysis.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":184,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":28,\"sourceQuestion\":33,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Successful risk analysis depends, in part, on ensuring that:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Successful risk analysis depends, in part, on having all appropriate organizations represented in the risk analysis meetings. As many interested parties as possible need to be engaged in the process. This includes internal and external customers. <b>C. all appropriate organizations are represented during risk analysis meetings, with all aspects of potential risk considered.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 33 (print p. 28), II.C.7.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.C.7","options":["all internal and external customers contribute safety data prior to the first risk analysis meeting.","warranty and reliability data are treated as the most important inputs.","all appropriate organizations are represented during risk analysis meetings, with all aspects of potential risk considered.","risk mitigation and verification are performed only at the final stages of the analysis."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":184,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":28,"sourceQuestion":33,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Successful risk analysis depends, in part, on ensuring that:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Successful risk analysis depends, in part, on having all appropriate organizations represented in the risk analysis meetings. As many interested parties as possible need to be engaged in the process. This includes internal and external customers. <b>C. all appropriate organizations are represented during risk analysis meetings, with all aspects of potential risk considered.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 33 (print p. 28), II.C.7.</span>"},"revision":"06d95be0a5f07495843c455e4d8f3c42a3c15232d0b5e6c1567744c6039a1ae6"},"cssgb:set-2:source-185:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.C.7\",\"options\":[\"Monitoring normal supply chain issues for day-to-day operations\",\"Ensuring that operations can continue in adverse conditions\",\"Dealing with a design complaint from a key customer during the launch of a product\",\"Finding failure modes for customer complaints\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":185,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":28,\"sourceQuestion\":34,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which scenario is business continuity designed to deal with?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Business continuity is a design for when things do not go as planned (for instance, like what happened during the worldwide pandemic). It is designed to ensure that operations can continue in adverse conditions. <b>B. Ensuring that operations can continue in adverse conditions</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 34 (print p. 28), II.C.7.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.C.7","options":["Monitoring normal supply chain issues for day-to-day operations","Ensuring that operations can continue in adverse conditions","Dealing with a design complaint from a key customer during the launch of a product","Finding failure modes for customer complaints"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":185,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":28,"sourceQuestion":34,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which scenario is business continuity designed to deal with?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Business continuity is a design for when things do not go as planned (for instance, like what happened during the worldwide pandemic). It is designed to ensure that operations can continue in adverse conditions. <b>B. Ensuring that operations can continue in adverse conditions</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 34 (print p. 28), II.C.7.</span>"},"revision":"3b670c4928caadc72bf64f3d58b2bc0c2894204777245ea5e80f7403306eb149"},"cssgb:set-2:source-186:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"II.C.7\",\"options\":[\"the natural boundaries of a process within specified confidence levels.\",\"a systematized group of activities to recognize and evaluate the potential failure of a product or process.\",\"a characteristic of a product or service that is essential to ensure customer satisfaction.\",\"coordinated activities to direct and control an organization concerning risk.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":186,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":29,\"sourceQuestion\":35,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Risk management is defined in the ISO 31000:2018 as:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"There is a series of ISO risk documents in the ISO 31000 series. ISO 31010 lists a number of risk registers that can be very useful during Six Sigma projects. <b>D. coordinated activities to direct and control an organization concerning risk.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 35 (print p. 29), II.C.7.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"II.C.7","options":["the natural boundaries of a process within specified confidence levels.","a systematized group of activities to recognize and evaluate the potential failure of a product or process.","a characteristic of a product or service that is essential to ensure customer satisfaction.","coordinated activities to direct and control an organization concerning risk."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":186,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":29,"sourceQuestion":35,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Risk management is defined in the ISO 31000:2018 as:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"There is a series of ISO risk documents in the ISO 31000 series. ISO 31010 lists a number of risk registers that can be very useful during Six Sigma projects. <b>D. coordinated activities to direct and control an organization concerning risk.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 35 (print p. 29), II.C.7.</span>"},"revision":"19cd4ceff2397fa9b1a409a19a280e4da1091b98f75f55efcecadb3ac16d4d74"},"cssgb:set-2:source-187:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.C.7\",\"options\":[\"Implementing a test of the chosen risk management technique(s)\",\"Monitoring the implemented techniques for adequacy of protection\",\"Assessing the effectiveness of the technique and making necessary adjustments\",\"Identifying and defining its potential exposures to loss\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":187,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":29,\"sourceQuestion\":36,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Risk management can be defined using the PDCA process. Which item is part of Act?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"In the act phase, we monitor the implemented techniques for adequacy of protection. <b>B. Monitoring the implemented techniques for adequacy of protection</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 36 (print p. 29), II.C.7.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.C.7","options":["Implementing a test of the chosen risk management technique(s)","Monitoring the implemented techniques for adequacy of protection","Assessing the effectiveness of the technique and making necessary adjustments","Identifying and defining its potential exposures to loss"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":187,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":29,"sourceQuestion":36,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Risk management can be defined using the PDCA process. Which item is part of Act?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"In the act phase, we monitor the implemented techniques for adequacy of protection. <b>B. Monitoring the implemented techniques for adequacy of protection</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 36 (print p. 29), II.C.7.</span>"},"revision":"1bb987290c3fbc7b40c9ab24129f6364e5c2c47b4f42eb3c0058b135a5ed23f0"},"cssgb:set-2:source-188:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"II.C.8\",\"options\":[\"the charter grand finale.\",\"an independent third-party review.\",\"Six Sigma team project termination.\",\"the project closure.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":188,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":29,\"sourceQuestion\":37,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The final step that is performed to prove that the project met established goals and objectives and that all documentation was completed and stored is called:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"The project closure could include things like an A3 and other pertinent information from the project to demonstrate that goals have been met. <b>D. the project closure.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 37 (print p. 29), II.C.8.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"II.C.8","options":["the charter grand finale.","an independent third-party review.","Six Sigma team project termination.","the project closure."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":188,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":29,"sourceQuestion":37,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The final step that is performed to prove that the project met established goals and objectives and that all documentation was completed and stored is called:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"The project closure could include things like an A3 and other pertinent information from the project to demonstrate that goals have been met. <b>D. the project closure.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 37 (print p. 29), II.C.8.</span>"},"revision":"15df4920628f5e508b7abb8c135351d4a6a77ae30747c7e0fa05c1a7c75b76cb"},"cssgb:set-2:source-189:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"II.C.8\",\"options\":[\"identify and share lessons learned with the organization.\",\"justify the cost of the project.\",\"develop a control plan and return the process to the process owner.\",\"design for Six Sigma.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":189,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":29,\"sourceQuestion\":38,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"One activity of project closure is to:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"When closing the project, the team should identify lessons learned and share them with the rest of the organization. <b>A. identify and share lessons learned with the organization.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 38 (print p. 29), II.C.8.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"II.C.8","options":["identify and share lessons learned with the organization.","justify the cost of the project.","develop a control plan and return the process to the process owner.","design for Six Sigma."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":189,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":29,"sourceQuestion":38,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"One activity of project closure is to:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"When closing the project, the team should identify lessons learned and share them with the rest of the organization. <b>A. identify and share lessons learned with the organization.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 38 (print p. 29), II.C.8.</span>"},"revision":"2faab20db5efa83f7bef58461161efb720ca0f305ea35f1db93b0796a61ebbb5"},"cssgb:set-2:source-18:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"2.92\",\"4.51\",\"5.42\",\"26.50\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":18,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":152,\"sourceQuestion\":18,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"Find the critical value for an F statistic with 3 and 30 degrees of freedom and α = 0.01.\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"For numerator df = 3, denominator df = 30, and α = 0.01, the upper-tail F critical value is approximately 4.51. <b>B. 4.51</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 18 (print p. 152), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["2.92","4.51","5.42","26.50"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":18,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":152,"sourceQuestion":18,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"Find the critical value for an F statistic with 3 and 30 degrees of freedom and α = 0.01.","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"For numerator df = 3, denominator df = 30, and α = 0.01, the upper-tail F critical value is approximately 4.51. <b>B. 4.51</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 18 (print p. 152), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"0d75060fa28817584d8a56800c6c834ab81aa3efaf81e4f8d7295aa2f5f5468d"},"cssgb:set-2:source-190:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"II.D\",\"options\":[\"Activity network diagram\",\"Cause-and-effect diagram\",\"Pareto chart\",\"Control chart\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":190,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":29,\"sourceQuestion\":39,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which tool graphically shows the interdependence between tasks while highlighting key tasks, time to accomplishment, flow paths, and so on?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"An activity network diagram shows the interdependence between tasks while highlighting key tasks, time to accomplishment, flow paths, and so on. <b>A. Activity network diagram</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 39 (print p. 29), II.D.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"II.D","options":["Activity network diagram","Cause-and-effect diagram","Pareto chart","Control chart"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":190,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":29,"sourceQuestion":39,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which tool graphically shows the interdependence between tasks while highlighting key tasks, time to accomplishment, flow paths, and so on?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"An activity network diagram shows the interdependence between tasks while highlighting key tasks, time to accomplishment, flow paths, and so on. <b>A. Activity network diagram</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 39 (print p. 29), II.D.</span>"},"revision":"bdda4e6f2017dc346411c14773a093ca5fee0961271277deb407d93cf9e12f76"},"cssgb:set-2:source-191:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.D\",\"options\":[\"Early executive adventure\",\"Advance quality planning\",\"Association matrix\",\"The seven-step improvement model\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":191,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":30,\"sourceQuestion\":40,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What important planning tool is founded on the premise that solid planning helps prevent undesirable surprises and saves valuable resources?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"The AQP was renamed APQP for the automotive industry to highlight that the design/product engineers need to be involved during the initial planning to help prevent surprises. <b>B. Advance quality planning</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 40 (print p. 30), II.D.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.D","options":["Early executive adventure","Advance quality planning","Association matrix","The seven-step improvement model"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":191,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":30,"sourceQuestion":40,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What important planning tool is founded on the premise that solid planning helps prevent undesirable surprises and saves valuable resources?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"The AQP was renamed APQP for the automotive industry to highlight that the design/product engineers need to be involved during the initial planning to help prevent surprises. <b>B. Advance quality planning</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 40 (print p. 30), II.D.</span>"},"revision":"88bae22a193e851be409a7e234a0deb0549d801d7c79af31f40de712c502ff8e"},"cssgb:set-2:source-192:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"II.D\",\"options\":[\"Venn diagram\",\"Fishbone diagram\",\"Affinity diagram\",\"Cause-and-effect diagram\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":192,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":30,\"sourceQuestion\":41,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which tool is used to produce many possible answers to an open-ended question?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"The affinity diagram is used to produce and categorize as many possible answers to an open-ended question. <b>C. Affinity diagram</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 41 (print p. 30), II.D.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.D","options":["Venn diagram","Fishbone diagram","Affinity diagram","Cause-and-effect diagram"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":192,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":30,"sourceQuestion":41,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which tool is used to produce many possible answers to an open-ended question?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"The affinity diagram is used to produce and categorize as many possible answers to an open-ended question. <b>C. Affinity diagram</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 41 (print p. 30), II.D.</span>"},"revision":"076bccd72a745f973169b3b7682d45923afad39ee3606b1f5adf394c7ac6eb10"},"cssgb:set-2:source-193:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.D\",\"options\":[\"satisfaction, work, output, and target.\",\"strength, weakness, opportunity, and threat.\",\"solution, work, overall, and test.\",\"severity, worst, operating, and time.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":193,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":30,\"sourceQuestion\":42,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A SWOT analysis consists of:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"A SWOT analysis consists of identifying the internal strengths and weaknesses of an organization, as well as outside opportunities and threats. <b>B. strength, weakness, opportunity, and threat.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 42 (print p. 30), II.D.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.D","options":["satisfaction, work, output, and target.","strength, weakness, opportunity, and threat.","solution, work, overall, and test.","severity, worst, operating, and time."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":193,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":30,"sourceQuestion":42,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A SWOT analysis consists of:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"A SWOT analysis consists of identifying the internal strengths and weaknesses of an organization, as well as outside opportunities and threats. <b>B. strength, weakness, opportunity, and threat.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 42 (print p. 30), II.D.</span>"},"revision":"5deced542aef16ccc668e7053785e508b620db63728736796a42f308f7fc8da8"},"cssgb:set-2:source-194:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"II.D\",\"options\":[\"how many internal and external audits have previously been conducted.\",\"whether all the important rules are in writing.\",\"whether the employees have been coached in advance.\",\"what the standards or criteria are to be audited against.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":194,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":30,\"sourceQuestion\":43,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The first thing to know when planning an audit is:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"The first step in planning an audit is to identify the desired requirements of the standard to be reviewed. A good reference here is ISO 19011 for conducting audits. <b>D. what the standards or criteria are to be audited against.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 43 (print p. 30), II.D.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"II.D","options":["how many internal and external audits have previously been conducted.","whether all the important rules are in writing.","whether the employees have been coached in advance.","what the standards or criteria are to be audited against."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":194,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":30,"sourceQuestion":43,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The first thing to know when planning an audit is:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"The first step in planning an audit is to identify the desired requirements of the standard to be reviewed. A good reference here is ISO 19011 for conducting audits. <b>D. what the standards or criteria are to be audited against.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 43 (print p. 30), II.D.</span>"},"revision":"6110701b268427ecf5d4453bd8226402bc3b5195890cb2637894330283f67ea0"},"cssgb:set-2:source-195:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.D\",\"options\":[\"the lessons learned fact sheet.\",\"another system.\",\"all prototype production.\",\"the deployment of first production.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":195,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":30,\"sourceQuestion\":44,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Benchmarking is the process of looking at one system(s) and applying the concepts observed to:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Benchmarking is the process of looking at one system and applying the concepts observed to another system. Getting people to observe one thing and then apply it in another area, which could be different from what was initially observed, is often a challenge. <b>B. another system.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 44 (print p. 30), II.D.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.D","options":["the lessons learned fact sheet.","another system.","all prototype production.","the deployment of first production."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":195,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":30,"sourceQuestion":44,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Benchmarking is the process of looking at one system(s) and applying the concepts observed to:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Benchmarking is the process of looking at one system and applying the concepts observed to another system. Getting people to observe one thing and then apply it in another area, which could be different from what was initially observed, is often a challenge. <b>B. another system.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 44 (print p. 30), II.D.</span>"},"revision":"c11bd919302985084079fe6fa1da3d6e477c00b4fd55e05fd78e536ee55b1be4"},"cssgb:set-2:source-196:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.D\",\"options\":[\"activities and diagrams.\",\"materials, services, people, decisions, time, and process measurements.\",\"activities, stakeholders, strategic plan, and risk analysis.\",\"aspects, impacts, hazardous analysis, safety concerns, and environmental risks.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":196,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":31,\"sourceQuestion\":45,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A flowchart is a picture of the separate steps of a process in sequential order, including:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"A flowchart is a display of the separate steps in a process shown in sequential order, including materials, services, people, decisions, time, and process measurements. <b>B. materials, services, people, decisions, time, and process measurements.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 45 (print p. 31), II.D.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.D","options":["activities and diagrams.","materials, services, people, decisions, time, and process measurements.","activities, stakeholders, strategic plan, and risk analysis.","aspects, impacts, hazardous analysis, safety concerns, and environmental risks."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":196,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":31,"sourceQuestion":45,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A flowchart is a picture of the separate steps of a process in sequential order, including:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"A flowchart is a display of the separate steps in a process shown in sequential order, including materials, services, people, decisions, time, and process measurements. <b>B. materials, services, people, decisions, time, and process measurements.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 45 (print p. 31), II.D.</span>"},"revision":"9345204c994d432fc00f9efacd8df762a0c3fd2d166ae1edb549d9ad9e89c6d2"},"cssgb:set-2:source-197:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.D\",\"options\":[\"developed, exercised, data collected, and data analyzed.\",\"identified, conducted, data collected, and data analyzed.\",\"identified, trained, interviewed, and surveys analyzed.\",\"identified, confirmed, coached, and data collected.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":197,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":31,\"sourceQuestion\":46,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Once focus group planning is completed, the focus groups can be:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Once the focus group planning is complete, the next steps are to identify participants, conduct the focus group, and collect and analyze the resulting data. <b>B. identified, conducted, data collected, and data analyzed.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 46 (print p. 31), II.D.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.D","options":["developed, exercised, data collected, and data analyzed.","identified, conducted, data collected, and data analyzed.","identified, trained, interviewed, and surveys analyzed.","identified, confirmed, coached, and data collected."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":197,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":31,"sourceQuestion":46,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Once focus group planning is completed, the focus groups can be:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Once the focus group planning is complete, the next steps are to identify participants, conduct the focus group, and collect and analyze the resulting data. <b>B. identified, conducted, data collected, and data analyzed.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 46 (print p. 31), II.D.</span>"},"revision":"d42566526d5236707a233b28974efc3e8be7241bf3e412ff2401bb4160c3e939"},"cssgb:set-2:source-198:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"II.D\",\"options\":[\"To create flowcharts of the entire project process\",\"To visualize the relationships between different process steps\",\"To identify potential risks and develop contingency plans\",\"To analyze process variations using statistical techniques\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":198,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":31,\"sourceQuestion\":47,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is the main purpose of using process decision program charts (PDPCs) in Six Sigma projects?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"PDPC is used to systematically identify potential risks in a process, assess their impact, and develop contingency plans to address them. <b>C. To identify potential risks and develop contingency plans</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 47 (print p. 31), II.D.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.D","options":["To create flowcharts of the entire project process","To visualize the relationships between different process steps","To identify potential risks and develop contingency plans","To analyze process variations using statistical techniques"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":198,"sourceP