:"cssgb-define","why":"SWOT lists the strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats of an organization to assess its competitive position. <b>A. SWOT.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 48 (print p. 31), II.D.</span>"},"revision":"c1a06a6c6b16ab7320f1cf6a0b27b7f2e24dafe5db657e95a92eebecca3a96b5"},"cssgb:set-2:source-19:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"V.C.1\",\"options\":[\"training and reinforcement of proper work instructions.\",\"limiting or restricting ways to complete a task.\",\"adherence to process control charts.\",\"constant supervision and rapid intervention.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":19,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":152,\"sourceQuestion\":19,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"Mistake-proofing is achieved by:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"Mistake-proofing is achieved by limiting or restricting ways to complete a task to ensure accuracy and compliance. <b>B. limiting or restricting ways to complete a task.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 19 (print p. 152), V.C.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"V.C.1","options":["training and reinforcement of proper work instructions.","limiting or restricting ways to complete a task.","adherence to process control charts.","constant supervision and rapid intervention."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":19,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":152,"sourceQuestion":19,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"Mistake-proofing is achieved by:","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"Mistake-proofing is achieved by limiting or restricting ways to complete a task to ensure accuracy and compliance. <b>B. limiting or restricting ways to complete a task.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 19 (print p. 152), V.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"47730ae41b94d027e64aefaf43fef5141d904d469b43dea88ba8b2430b537783"},"cssgb:set-2:source-1:10hzvwm":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"I.A.1\",\"options\":[\"W. Edwards Deming\",\"Joseph M. Juran\",\"Kaoru Ishikawa\",\"Walter A. Shewhart\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":1,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":149,\"sourceQuestion\":1,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"Who is the quality guru who created the 14 Points and the 7 Deadly Diseases?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"These are Deming’s works. <b>A. W. Edwards Deming</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 1 (print p. 149), I.A.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"I.A.1","options":["W. Edwards Deming","Joseph M. Juran","Kaoru Ishikawa","Walter A. Shewhart"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":1,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":149,"sourceQuestion":1,"sourceSection":"I. Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"Who is the quality guru who created the 14 Points and the 7 Deadly Diseases?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"These are Deming’s works. <b>A. W. Edwards Deming</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 1 (print p. 149), I.A.1.</span>"},"revision":"18cb7ca67af114f7bd4532fcd596b5ec616c6e72d42ff53c2c31fc6765295af5"},"cssgb:set-2:source-200:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"II.E.1\",\"options\":[\"Internal, external, supplier, warranty\",\"Internal, external, appraisal, rework\",\"Internal, external, customer, design\",\"Internal, external, appraisal, prevention\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":200,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":31,\"sourceQuestion\":49,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The four elements of the Cost of Quality are:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"The Cost of Quality comprises four costs: internal, external, appraisal, and prevention. <b>D. Internal, external, appraisal, prevention</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 49 (print p. 31), II.E.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"II.E.1","options":["Internal, external, supplier, warranty","Internal, external, appraisal, rework","Internal, external, customer, design","Internal, external, appraisal, prevention"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":200,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":31,"sourceQuestion":49,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The four elements of the Cost of Quality are:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"The Cost of Quality comprises four costs: internal, external, appraisal, and prevention. <b>D. Internal, external, appraisal, prevention</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 49 (print p. 31), II.E.1.</span>"},"revision":"fc4f987504ac4ee16e669f438c6b974e4bdc3317425460f1aed8b00f34223679"},"cssgb:set-2:source-201:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.E.1\",\"options\":[\"Internal\",\"External\",\"Appraisal\",\"Prevention\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":201,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":32,\"sourceQuestion\":50,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Warranty costs are an example of which Cost of Quality?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Warranty costs are an example of an external quality cost. The external costs of quality are those defects that the customer sees. <b>B. External</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 50 (print p. 32), II.E.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.E.1","options":["Internal","External","Appraisal","Prevention"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":201,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":32,"sourceQuestion":50,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Warranty costs are an example of which Cost of Quality?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Warranty costs are an example of an external quality cost. The external costs of quality are those defects that the customer sees. <b>B. External</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 50 (print p. 32), II.E.1.</span>"},"revision":"0e0b1ecf5274bf197159e949f8593bbbc3d4ab068b42be32f1860ba637f7ca8f"},"cssgb:set-2:source-202:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"II.E.1\",\"options\":[\"DPO\",\"DPU\",\"DPMO\",\"RTY\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":202,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":32,\"sourceQuestion\":51,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which index is used to identify the total number of defects/deficiencies that have occurred and standardized to a common total number of possibilities?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Defects per million opportunities are used to identify the total number of defects/deficiencies that have occurred, standardized to a common total number of possibilities. <b>C. DPMO</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 51 (print p. 32), II.E.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.E.1","options":["DPO","DPU","DPMO","RTY"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":202,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":32,"sourceQuestion":51,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which index is used to identify the total number of defects/deficiencies that have occurred and standardized to a common total number of possibilities?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Defects per million opportunities are used to identify the total number of defects/deficiencies that have occurred, standardized to a common total number of possibilities. <b>C. DPMO</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 51 (print p. 32), II.E.1.</span>"},"revision":"56514b6364a95c6ee78c517246c449b86bf5a4b179b961c35f552df894ec1033"},"cssgb:set-2:source-203:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"II.E.1\",\"options\":[\"DPO\",\"DPU\",\"DPMO\",\"RTY\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":203,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":32,\"sourceQuestion\":52,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which index is used to identify the first pass rate through a series of processes?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Rolled throughput yield is used to identify the first pass rate through a series of processes. <b>D. RTY</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 52 (print p. 32), II.E.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"II.E.1","options":["DPO","DPU","DPMO","RTY"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":203,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":32,"sourceQuestion":52,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which index is used to identify the first pass rate through a series of processes?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Rolled throughput yield is used to identify the first pass rate through a series of processes. <b>D. RTY</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 52 (print p. 32), II.E.1.</span>"},"revision":"895f5fd29f233fb2b8aa5f8a1f3dca768e7ac98915ea0e528856f8a17ad9b478"},"cssgb:set-2:source-204:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"II.E.1\",\"options\":[\"0.002\",\"0.006\",\"2000\",\"6000\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":204,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":32,\"sourceQuestion\":53,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A ring gauge is used to check the diameter of a metal shaft in three places. An inspector tests 1000 shafts and finds six defects. What is the DPMO?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Each shaft has three defect opportunities, so DPMO = 6 / (1000 × 3) × 10⁶ = 2000. <b>C. 2000</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 53 (print p. 32), II.E.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.E.1","options":["0.002","0.006","2000","6000"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":204,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":32,"sourceQuestion":53,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A ring gauge is used to check the diameter of a metal shaft in three places. An inspector tests 1000 shafts and finds six defects. What is the DPMO?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Each shaft has three defect opportunities, so DPMO = 6 / (1000 × 3) × 10⁶ = 2000. <b>C. 2000</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 53 (print p. 32), II.E.1.</span>"},"revision":"fdf9b22c807bf0d397e18fa7d43178dc138cfa94fb71290cc2d7cfe1f6f6ab33"},"cssgb:set-2:source-205:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"II.E.1\",\"options\":[\"2.9%\",\"0.97%\",\"90%\",\"97%\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":205,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":32,\"sourceQuestion\":54,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Calculate the rolled throughput yield of a drilling process with three consecutive machine steps that have yields of 98%, 96%, and 96%, respectively.\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"To calculate rolled throughput yield, we multiply the individual machine yields: RTY = 0.98 × 0.96 × 0.96 = 0.903 × 100% ≈ 90%. <b>C. 90%</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 54 (print p. 32), II.E.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.E.1","options":["2.9%","0.97%","90%","97%"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":205,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":32,"sourceQuestion":54,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Calculate the rolled throughput yield of a drilling process with three consecutive machine steps that have yields of 98%, 96%, and 96%, respectively.","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"To calculate rolled throughput yield, we multiply the individual machine yields: RTY = 0.98 × 0.96 × 0.96 = 0.903 × 100% ≈ 90%. <b>C. 90%</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 54 (print p. 32), II.E.1.</span>"},"revision":"2f2dde0f51f7fd84f1e1740603b5c532248271f5fb6477c2d2447b4b5cd79094"},"cssgb:set-2:source-206:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.E.1\",\"options\":[\"2 parts per billion.\",\"3.4 parts per million.\",\"34 parts per million.\",\"2700 parts per million.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":206,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":33,\"sourceQuestion\":55,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The defect level of a 6σ-level process, after a 1.5σ shift, is equal to:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"For a normally distributed process output, a 6σ-level process, after a 1.5σ shift in the mean, results in a defect level of 3.4 parts per million. <b>B. 3.4 parts per million.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 55 (print p. 33), II.E.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.E.1","options":["2 parts per billion.","3.4 parts per million.","34 parts per million.","2700 parts per million."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":206,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":33,"sourceQuestion":55,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The defect level of a 6σ-level process, after a 1.5σ shift, is equal to:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"For a normally distributed process output, a 6σ-level process, after a 1.5σ shift in the mean, results in a defect level of 3.4 parts per million. <b>B. 3.4 parts per million.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 55 (print p. 33), II.E.1.</span>"},"revision":"c6f42fd0915137fd51dd21011f7667713e1ceb069009c014b7ca97c7cf5dadd3"},"cssgb:set-2:source-207:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"II.E.2\",\"options\":[\"Efficient dissemination of top-level decisions\",\"Quick decision-making at the senior management level\",\"Enhanced employee engagement and empowerment\",\"Facilitation of direct communication between peers\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":207,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":33,\"sourceQuestion\":56,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is a key advantage of bottom-up communication in organizations?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Bottom-up communication enhances employee engagement and empowerment by allowing employees to share their ideas, concerns, and feedback directly with higher-level management. <b>C. Enhanced employee engagement and empowerment</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 56 (print p. 33), II.E.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.E.2","options":["Efficient dissemination of top-level decisions","Quick decision-making at the senior management level","Enhanced employee engagement and empowerment","Facilitation of direct communication between peers"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":207,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":33,"sourceQuestion":56,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is a key advantage of bottom-up communication in organizations?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Bottom-up communication enhances employee engagement and empowerment by allowing employees to share their ideas, concerns, and feedback directly with higher-level management. <b>C. Enhanced employee engagement and empowerment</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 56 (print p. 33), II.E.2.</span>"},"revision":"6fcce9c774b11df877c72709783964791817ff208c6034302a7750b7c964dbb3"},"cssgb:set-2:source-208:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.E.2\",\"options\":[\"lateral flow.\",\"downward flow.\",\"diagonal flow.\",\"matrix flow.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":208,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":33,\"sourceQuestion\":57,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Flow utilized when top management, or the executive sponsor of the project, is providing instruction, communicating policies, or providing feedback on project performance is called:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Downward flow, also known as top-down flow, occurs when top management, or the project’s executive sponsor, provides instruction, communicates policies, or provides feedback on project performance. <b>B. downward flow.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 57 (print p. 33), II.E.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.E.2","options":["lateral flow.","downward flow.","diagonal flow.","matrix flow."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":208,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":33,"sourceQuestion":57,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Flow utilized when top management, or the executive sponsor of the project, is providing instruction, communicating policies, or providing feedback on project performance is called:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Downward flow, also known as top-down flow, occurs when top management, or the project’s executive sponsor, provides instruction, communicates policies, or provides feedback on project performance. <b>B. downward flow.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 57 (print p. 33), II.E.2.</span>"},"revision":"71130b4ab4153917f602ccb6c0235e94e0fb72f0e5294cfce7f3007be677f087"},"cssgb:set-2:source-209:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.E.2\",\"options\":[\"To convey performance feedback from supervisors to employees\",\"To share information and collaborate among peers or colleagues\",\"To deliver strategic directives from top management\",\"To provide opportunities for employees to voice concerns to management\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":209,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":33,\"sourceQuestion\":58,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is the primary purpose of horizontal communication in organizations?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Horizontal communication serves to share information, collaborate, and exchange ideas among peers or colleagues at the same organizational level. <b>B. To share information and collaborate among peers or colleagues</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 58 (print p. 33), II.E.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.E.2","options":["To convey performance feedback from supervisors to employees","To share information and collaborate among peers or colleagues","To deliver strategic directives from top management","To provide opportunities for employees to voice concerns to management"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":209,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":33,"sourceQuestion":58,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is the primary purpose of horizontal communication in organizations?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Horizontal communication serves to share information, collaborate, and exchange ideas among peers or colleagues at the same organizational level. <b>B. To share information and collaborate among peers or colleagues</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 58 (print p. 33), II.E.2.</span>"},"revision":"0b0153e77b774d7b62050d8df7cf3a9547e6b3ab9b2684aef13c94f8b921010f"},"cssgb:set-2:source-20:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"II.F.1\",\"options\":[\"get to know each other.\",\"voice their ideas.\",\"resolve their conflicts.\",\"realize their interdependence.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":20,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":153,\"sourceQuestion\":20,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"When a team is in the performing growth stage, team members:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"In the performing stage, team members realize that they are interdependent. The team focuses on the project goal. <b>D. realize their interdependence.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 20 (print p. 153), II.F.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"II.F.1","options":["get to know each other.","voice their ideas.","resolve their conflicts.","realize their interdependence."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":20,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":153,"sourceQuestion":20,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"When a team is in the performing growth stage, team members:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"In the performing stage, team members realize that they are interdependent. The team focuses on the project goal. <b>D. realize their interdependence.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 20 (print p. 153), II.F.1.</span>"},"revision":"1b834bfb048548541d0ef47faca24652a3ee14871025420df711f42baf89b47b"},"cssgb:set-2:source-210:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.F.1\",\"options\":[\"Forming, challenging, testing, and agreeing.\",\"Forming, storming, norming, and performing.\",\"Circling, advancing, organizing, and accomplishing.\",\"Honeymooning, presenting, advocating, and completing.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":210,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":33,\"sourceQuestion\":59,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Teams typically go through the following sequence or stages of evolution:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Teams typically go through the forming, storming, norming, and performing stages of evolution. This has been shown time and time again and has been well documented in many references. Also, remember that as new team members join the group, the process may need to start over. <b>B. Forming, storming, norming, and performing.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 59 (print p. 33), II.F.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.F.1","options":["Forming, challenging, testing, and agreeing.","Forming, storming, norming, and performing.","Circling, advancing, organizing, and accomplishing.","Honeymooning, presenting, advocating, and completing."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":210,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":33,"sourceQuestion":59,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Teams typically go through the following sequence or stages of evolution:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Teams typically go through the forming, storming, norming, and performing stages of evolution. This has been shown time and time again and has been well documented in many references. Also, remember that as new team members join the group, the process may need to start over. <b>B. Forming, storming, norming, and performing.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 59 (print p. 33), II.F.1.</span>"},"revision":"1a24bb5dd224f4451348e61e541a02fff3c38264eef775e87008aff803db25bf"},"cssgb:set-2:source-211:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.F.1\",\"options\":[\"team members getting to know each other.\",\"team members voicing their opinions.\",\"team members resolving their conflicts.\",\"team members realizing interdependence.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":211,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":34,\"sourceQuestion\":60,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A function of team storming includes:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Team members voicing their opinions, and often disagreeing, is an example of team storming behavior. <b>B. team members voicing their opinions.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 60 (print p. 34), II.F.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.F.1","options":["team members getting to know each other.","team members voicing their opinions.","team members resolving their conflicts.","team members realizing interdependence."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":211,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":34,"sourceQuestion":60,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A function of team storming includes:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Team members voicing their opinions, and often disagreeing, is an example of team storming behavior. <b>B. team members voicing their opinions.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 60 (print p. 34), II.F.1.</span>"},"revision":"aebfe6ac9d8afbd13182f408d61fe5cc5c6a2be34e8e3635be87f2a24793dfe3"},"cssgb:set-2:source-212:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"II.F.1\",\"options\":[\"negative issues within the organizational culture.\",\"a lack of training in how to be a team member.\",\"a team leader who refused to lead the team.\",\"team members with too many distractions and a lack of commitment.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":212,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":34,\"sourceQuestion\":61,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Negative team dynamics are often reflective of:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Negative team dynamics are often reflective of negative issues within the organizational culture. <b>A. negative issues within the organizational culture.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 61 (print p. 34), II.F.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"II.F.1","options":["negative issues within the organizational culture.","a lack of training in how to be a team member.","a team leader who refused to lead the team.","team members with too many distractions and a lack of commitment."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":212,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":34,"sourceQuestion":61,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Negative team dynamics are often reflective of:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Negative team dynamics are often reflective of negative issues within the organizational culture. <b>A. negative issues within the organizational culture.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 61 (print p. 34), II.F.1.</span>"},"revision":"f6b4878ecce60e79eea8fbe7e212204150f6ddad4a61102aaf19cbe45d8c0e19"},"cssgb:set-2:source-213:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"II.F.1\",\"options\":[\"enforce discipline.\",\"encourage mutual respect.\",\"use the devil’s advocate approach.\",\"bring in independent, outside members to the meeting.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":213,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":34,\"sourceQuestion\":62,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"One way to reduce groupthink in a team is to:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"A countermeasure to groupthink, in which there is no public disagreement, is to bring in independent, outside members to the team meeting. <b>D. bring in independent, outside members to the meeting.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 62 (print p. 34), II.F.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"II.F.1","options":["enforce discipline.","encourage mutual respect.","use the devil’s advocate approach.","bring in independent, outside members to the meeting."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":213,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":34,"sourceQuestion":62,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"One way to reduce groupthink in a team is to:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"A countermeasure to groupthink, in which there is no public disagreement, is to bring in independent, outside members to the team meeting. <b>D. bring in independent, outside members to the meeting.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 62 (print p. 34), II.F.1.</span>"},"revision":"e48888328394ab8538819e3f1bc31fb363559856f37fc16bdf438d6c0f3971f6"},"cssgb:set-2:source-214:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"II.F.1\",\"options\":[\"the amount of feedback built into the system.\",\"the depth of historical background attributed to it.\",\"the responsibility of business unit leaders for profit and loss.\",\"the structure it demands of the organization.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":214,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":34,\"sourceQuestion\":63,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"One factor that has helped Six Sigma be successful is:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"The structure that Six Sigma demands on the organization has helped it be successful. This will be true as long as the management team is engaged and active. Remember that Deming identified management as 85 percent of the problem in many organizations, and this is where part of the discipline is needed. <b>D. the structure it demands of the organization.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 63 (print p. 34), II.F.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"II.F.1","options":["the amount of feedback built into the system.","the depth of historical background attributed to it.","the responsibility of business unit leaders for profit and loss.","the structure it demands of the organization."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":214,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":34,"sourceQuestion":63,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"One factor that has helped Six Sigma be successful is:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"The structure that Six Sigma demands on the organization has helped it be successful. This will be true as long as the management team is engaged and active. Remember that Deming identified management as 85 percent of the problem in many organizations, and this is where part of the discipline is needed. <b>D. the structure it demands of the organization.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 63 (print p. 34), II.F.1.</span>"},"revision":"6ede3bb5e089f4e7bde23c3055f3ed5f79a71530f0c84c5f44c971419625399c"},"cssgb:set-2:source-215:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"II.F.2\",\"options\":[\"process owner.\",\"Project Champion.\",\"Black Belt.\",\"Green Belt.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":215,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":34,\"sourceQuestion\":64,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"An individual trained in Six Sigma methodologies, statistical tools, basic financial tools, change management, risk assessment, and project management is a:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"A Black Belt is trained in Six Sigma methodologies, statistical tools, basic financial tools, change management, risk assessment, and project management. <b>C. Black Belt.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 64 (print p. 34), II.F.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.F.2","options":["process owner.","Project Champion.","Black Belt.","Green Belt."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":215,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":34,"sourceQuestion":64,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"An individual trained in Six Sigma methodologies, statistical tools, basic financial tools, change management, risk assessment, and project management is a:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"A Black Belt is trained in Six Sigma methodologies, statistical tools, basic financial tools, change management, risk assessment, and project management. <b>C. Black Belt.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 64 (print p. 34), II.F.2.</span>"},"revision":"346464e8057603ff8c5764980b550d5009f76c99435dcd27c76fff3130f9ca21"},"cssgb:set-2:source-216:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"II.F.2\",\"options\":[\"retains their regular position but is trained in the tools, methods, and skills necessary to conduct Six Sigma improvement projects.\",\"is assigned full-time to train, mentor, and lead improvement projects.\",\"is assigned full-time to train, mentor, and lead the strategy for chartering the organization’s improvement projects.\",\"has the authority or ability to make changes in the process as required.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":216,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":35,\"sourceQuestion\":65,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The Master Black Belt is usually associated with an individual who typically:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"A Master Black Belt is a Six Sigma role associated with an individual typically assigned to train, mentor, and lead the strategy for chartering the organization’s improvement projects full-time. <b>C. is assigned full-time to train, mentor, and lead the strategy for chartering the organization’s improvement projects.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 65 (print p. 35), II.F.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.F.2","options":["retains their regular position but is trained in the tools, methods, and skills necessary to conduct Six Sigma improvement projects.","is assigned full-time to train, mentor, and lead improvement projects.","is assigned full-time to train, mentor, and lead the strategy for chartering the organization’s improvement projects.","has the authority or ability to make changes in the process as required."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":216,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":35,"sourceQuestion":65,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The Master Black Belt is usually associated with an individual who typically:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"A Master Black Belt is a Six Sigma role associated with an individual typically assigned to train, mentor, and lead the strategy for chartering the organization’s improvement projects full-time. <b>C. is assigned full-time to train, mentor, and lead the strategy for chartering the organization’s improvement projects.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 65 (print p. 35), II.F.2.</span>"},"revision":"5f14a592b54fb5644eb4722989329bc285e625aa9c5be959dc9f37bcb3245993"},"cssgb:set-2:source-217:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.F.2\",\"options\":[\"registration, accreditation, conformance, and independence.\",\"responsible, accountable, consulted, and informed.\",\"reliable, act, check, and improve.\",\"reliability, availability, consistency, and improvement.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":217,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":35,\"sourceQuestion\":66,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"RACI commonly stands for:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"RACI stands for responsible, accountable, consulted, and informed. <b>B. responsible, accountable, consulted, and informed.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 66 (print p. 35), II.F.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.F.2","options":["registration, accreditation, conformance, and independence.","responsible, accountable, consulted, and informed.","reliable, act, check, and improve.","reliability, availability, consistency, and improvement."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":217,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":35,"sourceQuestion":66,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"RACI commonly stands for:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"RACI stands for responsible, accountable, consulted, and informed. <b>B. responsible, accountable, consulted, and informed.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 66 (print p. 35), II.F.2.</span>"},"revision":"579d90aa767ed482255198d170230f0ebcde7576755a6591cea547a39dbc3b65"},"cssgb:set-2:source-218:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"II.F.2\",\"options\":[\"support.\",\"staff.\",\"senior SME.\",\"safety.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":218,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":35,\"sourceQuestion\":67,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The added role found in RASCI, which is a sister to the RACI matrix, is for the role of:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"A support person can include any number of personnel, and this is the best answer here, as it covers what the team may need. <b>A. support.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 67 (print p. 35), II.F.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"II.F.2","options":["support.","staff.","senior SME.","safety."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":218,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":35,"sourceQuestion":67,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The added role found in RASCI, which is a sister to the RACI matrix, is for the role of:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"A support person can include any number of personnel, and this is the best answer here, as it covers what the team may need. <b>A. support.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 67 (print p. 35), II.F.2.</span>"},"revision":"3d2429fcb013a8f9ad965c050239e4989c1fcb5dcd8b8a001005d4b8104796bd"},"cssgb:set-2:source-219:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"II.F.2\",\"options\":[\"Responsible\",\"Accountable\",\"Consulted\",\"Informed\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":219,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":35,\"sourceQuestion\":68,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"In which role in the RACI model must a decision be discussed with the individual before a final decision is made?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"The RACI model is a matrix that lists stakeholders and tasks, indicating who is responsible, accountable, consulted, and informed for each task. A stakeholder in the “consulted” role must be asked about a decision before the final decision is made. <b>C. Consulted</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 68 (print p. 35), II.F.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.F.2","options":["Responsible","Accountable","Consulted","Informed"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":219,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":35,"sourceQuestion":68,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"In which role in the RACI model must a decision be discussed with the individual before a final decision is made?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"The RACI model is a matrix that lists stakeholders and tasks, indicating who is responsible, accountable, consulted, and informed for each task. A stakeholder in the “consulted” role must be asked about a decision before the final decision is made. <b>C. Consulted</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 68 (print p. 35), II.F.2.</span>"},"revision":"957068fb5b2ed7456b2c5de1dc1dab208078aebb3b59b3214251cec0bc25a9f2"},"cssgb:set-2:source-21:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"V.C.2\",\"options\":[\"the completion of the machine changes.\",\"the first good piece of another product.\",\"the last piece of the other product.\",\"the next changeover cycle.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":21,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":153,\"sourceQuestion\":21,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"For quick changeovers, setup time is defined as the time from the last piece of one product to:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"Setup time is defined as the time between the last piece of one product to the first good piece of another product, with the process running at full speed. <b>B. the first good piece of another product.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 21 (print p. 153), V.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"V.C.2","options":["the completion of the machine changes.","the first good piece of another product.","the last piece of the other product.","the next changeover cycle."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":21,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":153,"sourceQuestion":21,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"For quick changeovers, setup time is defined as the time from the last piece of one product to:","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"Setup time is defined as the time between the last piece of one product to the first good piece of another product, with the process running at full speed. <b>B. the first good piece of another product.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 21 (print p. 153), V.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"1a9828d705990c238c21aaa362981b270f185b4da932cb32f66c52a04d10227d"},"cssgb:set-2:source-220:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"II.F.2\",\"options\":[\"PFMEA.\",\"SWOT.\",\"PESTLE.\",\"RACI.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS