onclusion regarding the topic at hand is called:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"In consensus decision-making, the team works together to come to a conclusion regarding the topic at hand. <b>C. consensus decision-making.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 74 (print p. 37), II.F.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.F.3","options":["fact-based decision-making.","top-down decision-making.","consensus decision-making.","brainstorming."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":225,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":37,"sourceQuestion":74,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The approach that allows a team to work through a process to come to a conclusion regarding the topic at hand is called:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"In consensus decision-making, the team works together to come to a conclusion regarding the topic at hand. <b>C. consensus decision-making.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 74 (print p. 37), II.F.3.</span>"},"revision":"355f650d814b3cef12a9f8237018a74256813c00aad990c2523e20bf85e80758"},"cssgb:set-2:source-226:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"II.F.4\",\"options\":[\"share lessons learned.\",\"incorporate the results in the annual report.\",\"hold a focus group.\",\"benchmark against a competitor.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":226,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":37,\"sourceQuestion\":75,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"One way a team can communicate its project results to the organization is to:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"The project team can communicate their results by sharing lessons learned with the organization. <b>A. share lessons learned.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 75 (print p. 37), II.F.4.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"II.F.4","options":["share lessons learned.","incorporate the results in the annual report.","hold a focus group.","benchmark against a competitor."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":226,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":37,"sourceQuestion":75,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"One way a team can communicate its project results to the organization is to:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"The project team can communicate their results by sharing lessons learned with the organization. <b>A. share lessons learned.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A II. Define Phase, Question 75 (print p. 37), II.F.4.</span>"},"revision":"1cad76d7a3d3bf691a728944f7b28e1e58f4657cab08c976cdf8aece1be585a6"},"cssgb:set-2:source-227:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.A\",\"options\":[\"process map.\",\"spaghetti diagram.\",\"Pareto chart.\",\"activity network diagram.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":227,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":45,\"sourceQuestion\":1,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A graphic that shows a set of interrelated activities that transforms inputs into outputs is an:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"A process map shows a set of interrelated activities that transform inputs into outputs. <b>A. process map.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 1 (print p. 45), III.A.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.A","options":["process map.","spaghetti diagram.","Pareto chart.","activity network diagram."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":227,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":45,"sourceQuestion":1,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A graphic that shows a set of interrelated activities that transforms inputs into outputs is an:","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"A process map shows a set of interrelated activities that transform inputs into outputs. <b>A. process map.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 1 (print p. 45), III.A.</span>"},"revision":"46e6eced86aaca9d17ad4ba8d790e6b1c18999156c613dedcefc95779ef7c24a"},"cssgb:set-2:source-228:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"III.A\",\"options\":[\"Build the sequence of the process, putting everything into the appropriate order.\",\"Determine the various steps in the process through team brainstorming or walking the process.\",\"Draw the flowchart using the appropriate mapping symbols.\",\"Create the boundaries of the process that we intend to flowchart.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":228,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":47,\"sourceQuestion\":2,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What should be the first step in creating a process flowchart?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The first step in creating a process map is to identify the boundaries of the process of interest. The other three steps will be taken as you progress through the project. <b>D. Create the boundaries of the process that we intend to flowchart.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 2 (print p. 47), III.A.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"III.A","options":["Build the sequence of the process, putting everything into the appropriate order.","Determine the various steps in the process through team brainstorming or walking the process.","Draw the flowchart using the appropriate mapping symbols.","Create the boundaries of the process that we intend to flowchart."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":228,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":47,"sourceQuestion":2,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What should be the first step in creating a process flowchart?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The first step in creating a process map is to identify the boundaries of the process of interest. The other three steps will be taken as you progress through the project. <b>D. Create the boundaries of the process that we intend to flowchart.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 2 (print p. 47), III.A.</span>"},"revision":"ed93a9737665634ab48f103846666cfefdc0eb1ccb2b49ace747a14a3378340b"},"cssgb:set-2:source-229:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.A\",\"options\":[\"why it is done (business reason, purpose).\",\"how it is done (step by step).\",\"when it is done (trigger).\",\"where it is done (location/process step).\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":229,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":47,\"sourceQuestion\":3,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"One of the differences between a work instruction and a procedure is that the work instruction identifies:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Work instructions (WIs) are more granular descriptions of a process. In addition to the information contained in procedures, WIs also include who does what and how it is done. <b>B. how it is done (step by step).</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 3 (print p. 47), III.A.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.A","options":["why it is done (business reason, purpose).","how it is done (step by step).","when it is done (trigger).","where it is done (location/process step)."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":229,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":47,"sourceQuestion":3,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"One of the differences between a work instruction and a procedure is that the work instruction identifies:","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Work instructions (WIs) are more granular descriptions of a process. In addition to the information contained in procedures, WIs also include who does what and how it is done. <b>B. how it is done (step by step).</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 3 (print p. 47), III.A.</span>"},"revision":"957c28d44e0450bc0d79a27cc8895887f5454c839bb5b17559e6fa5a5a774bac"},"cssgb:set-2:source-22:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"options\":[\"Confidence level, significance level\",\"Mean squares, F-statistic\",\"Readings, levels, measurements\",\"Between treatment, within treatment, total\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":22,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":153,\"sourceQuestion\":22,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"The one-way ANOVA table includes which sources of variation?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"The sources of variation included as part of an ANOVA table are between treatment, within treatment, and total. <b>D. Between treatment, within treatment, total</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 22 (print p. 153), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","options":["Confidence level, significance level","Mean squares, F-statistic","Readings, levels, measurements","Between treatment, within treatment, total"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":22,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":153,"sourceQuestion":22,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"The one-way ANOVA table includes which sources of variation?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"The sources of variation included as part of an ANOVA table are between treatment, within treatment, and total. <b>D. Between treatment, within treatment, total</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 22 (print p. 153), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"8b5c28b82936044dd0aa63cac0b61e389e978db39188f73b8eb78327a0d94e46"},"cssgb:set-2:source-230:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"III.A\",\"options\":[\"Work instruction\",\"Product map\",\"FMEA\",\"Method\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":230,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":47,\"sourceQuestion\":4,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is the term for an unwritten process that must be followed consistently?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"A method is an unwritten process that must be followed consistently. Both the FMEA and WI are written documented processes. The product map is not a tool used in SS. <b>D. Method</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 4 (print p. 47), III.A.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"III.A","options":["Work instruction","Product map","FMEA","Method"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":230,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":47,"sourceQuestion":4,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is the term for an unwritten process that must be followed consistently?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"A method is an unwritten process that must be followed consistently. Both the FMEA and WI are written documented processes. The product map is not a tool used in SS. <b>D. Method</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 4 (print p. 47), III.A.</span>"},"revision":"5efc13c689f39f1f86cd1019fba8e1c846e5cbda377a60a1ce0bc36456b247fc"},"cssgb:set-2:source-231:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"III.A\",\"options\":[\"relationship diagram.\",\"SIPOC chart.\",\"measles chart.\",\"spaghetti chart.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":231,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":47,\"sourceQuestion\":5,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A graphical tool that shows the physical location of defects is known as a:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Measles charts show the physical location of defects. <b>C. measles chart.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 5 (print p. 47), III.A.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"III.A","options":["relationship diagram.","SIPOC chart.","measles chart.","spaghetti chart."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":231,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":47,"sourceQuestion":5,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A graphical tool that shows the physical location of defects is known as a:","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Measles charts show the physical location of defects. <b>C. measles chart.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 5 (print p. 47), III.A.</span>"},"revision":"9109bd6eee2578cf42e6e10a11c577cc63d0372a642d06194d64d42831e521f4"},"cssgb:set-2:source-232:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"III.A\",\"options\":[\"Design of experiments\",\"Affinity diagram\",\"Scatter diagram\",\"Pareto chart\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":232,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":47,\"sourceQuestion\":6,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What tool helps us visualize the items charted as “vital few” and “trivial many” using the 80–20 rule?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The Pareto chart helps narrow the team’s focus by differentiating between the vital few and trivial many causes of a problem. This principle is also called the 80–20 rule. <b>D. Pareto chart</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 6 (print p. 47), III.A.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"III.A","options":["Design of experiments","Affinity diagram","Scatter diagram","Pareto chart"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":232,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":47,"sourceQuestion":6,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What tool helps us visualize the items charted as “vital few” and “trivial many” using the 80–20 rule?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The Pareto chart helps narrow the team’s focus by differentiating between the vital few and trivial many causes of a problem. This principle is also called the 80–20 rule. <b>D. Pareto chart</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 6 (print p. 47), III.A.</span>"},"revision":"45e34fbeb48fe588996632c77b2119a5168e97b83c29273694199a7d060bbbe3"},"cssgb:set-2:source-233:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.B.1\",\"options\":[\"1/6\",\"1/3\",\"1/2\",\"2/3\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":233,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":48,\"sourceQuestion\":7,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is the probability of rolling a 2 in a single toss of a standard six-sided die?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The six sides have equal probability. Thus P(X= 2) = 1/6. <b>A. 1/6</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 7 (print p. 48), III.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.B.1","options":["1/6","1/3","1/2","2/3"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":233,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":48,"sourceQuestion":7,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is the probability of rolling a 2 in a single toss of a standard six-sided die?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The six sides have equal probability. Thus P(X= 2) = 1/6. <b>A. 1/6</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 7 (print p. 48), III.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"5a73f8aff37941860c56ccac51ec8815698785e6b256310732eac03156e9ea66"},"cssgb:set-2:source-234:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.B.1\",\"options\":[\"P(A) + P(B).\",\"P(A) + P(B) − P(A & B).\",\"P(A) × P(B|A).\",\"P(A & B) ÷ P(A).\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":234,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":48,\"sourceQuestion\":8,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The general addition rule of probability states that P(A or B) =\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The probability of Event A or B is found using the addition rule: P(A) + P(B) − P(A &amp; B). <b>B. P(A) + P(B) − P(A &amp; B).</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 8 (print p. 48), III.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.B.1","options":["P(A) + P(B).","P(A) + P(B) − P(A & B).","P(A) × P(B|A).","P(A & B) ÷ P(A)."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":234,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":48,"sourceQuestion":8,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The general addition rule of probability states that P(A or B) =","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The probability of Event A or B is found using the addition rule: P(A) + P(B) − P(A &amp; B). <b>B. P(A) + P(B) − P(A &amp; B).</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 8 (print p. 48), III.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"5dc0298c3223aa9ece6f9cf4f143f211f12958a4e19755f6e900b8d755386297"},"cssgb:set-2:source-235:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.B.1\",\"options\":[\"1/6\",\"1/3\",\"1/2\",\"2/3\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":235,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":48,\"sourceQuestion\":9,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"When throwing a standard six-sided die, what is the probability of getting a 4 or a 6?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Using the addition rule, P(4 or 6) = P(4) + P(6) − P(4 &amp; 6) = 1/6 + 1/6 – 0 = 1/3. <b>B. 1/3</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 9 (print p. 48), III.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.B.1","options":["1/6","1/3","1/2","2/3"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":235,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":48,"sourceQuestion":9,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"When throwing a standard six-sided die, what is the probability of getting a 4 or a 6?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Using the addition rule, P(4 or 6) = P(4) + P(6) − P(4 &amp; 6) = 1/6 + 1/6 – 0 = 1/3. <b>B. 1/3</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 9 (print p. 48), III.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"c022a74b925eb7c8c4e157e328325e6eac249a1d5c95c0d0bd789248b94afb0a"},"cssgb:set-2:source-236:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.B.1\",\"options\":[\"P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B)\",\"P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B)\",\"P(A and B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B)\",\"P(A or B) = P(A) × P(B) − P(A ∩ B)\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":236,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":48,\"sourceQuestion\":10,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is the formula for the probability of Event A or B occurring when the events are mutually exclusive?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Using the general addition rule, P(A) + P(B) − P(A &amp; B). If Events A and B are mutually exclusive, P(A and B) = 0. Therefore, the formula reduces to P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B). <b>A. P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B)</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 10 (print p. 48), III.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.B.1","options":["P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B)","P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B)","P(A and B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B)","P(A or B) = P(A) × P(B) − P(A ∩ B)"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":236,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":48,"sourceQuestion":10,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is the formula for the probability of Event A or B occurring when the events are mutually exclusive?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Using the general addition rule, P(A) + P(B) − P(A &amp; B). If Events A and B are mutually exclusive, P(A and B) = 0. Therefore, the formula reduces to P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B). <b>A. P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B)</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 10 (print p. 48), III.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"006964159470d932e2c9c5407c26520fbdf60ca3c035aaccc7947066023ac72e"},"cssgb:set-2:source-237:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.B.1\",\"options\":[\"0.008\",\"0.05\",\"0.20\",\"0.42\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":237,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":48,\"sourceQuestion\":11,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"There are two extrusion machines that produce plastic parts. Forty percent of the parts are produced on Machine A. The probability that a randomly chosen part is made on Machine A and is defective is 0.02. What is the probability that a part is defective given that it is made on Machine A?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Use conditional probability: P(Defective | Machine A) = P(Machine A and Defective) / P(Machine A) = 0.02 / 0.40 = 0.05. <b>B. 0.05</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 11 (print p. 48), III.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.B.1","options":["0.008","0.05","0.20","0.42"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":237,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":48,"sourceQuestion":11,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"There are two extrusion machines that produce plastic parts. Forty percent of the parts are produced on Machine A. The probability that a randomly chosen part is made on Machine A and is defective is 0.02. What is the probability that a part is defective given that it is made on Machine A?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Use conditional probability: P(Defective | Machine A) = P(Machine A and Defective) / P(Machine A) = 0.02 / 0.40 = 0.05. <b>B. 0.05</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 11 (print p. 48), III.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"6edef369d7e39434397aa81e65b850b3dbd1ed742eeced0e8dae2aebb9a6ee36"},"cssgb:set-2:source-238:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.B.1\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Complaint type\",\"Frequency\"],\"rows\":[[\"Missing connectors\",\"32\"],[\"Wrong connectors\",\"15\"],[\"Directions not clear\",\"20\"],[\"Shipping damage\",\"23\"],[\"Lost shipment\",\"10\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"15/47\",\"15/100\",\"32/100\",\"47/100\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":238,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":49,\"sourceQuestion\":12,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is the probability that a randomly chosen complaint concerns wrong connectors?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"There are a total of 100 complaints. Fifteen concerned wrong connectors. The probability that a randomly chosen complaint is a wrong connector complaint is 15/100. <b>B. 15/100</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 12 (print p. 49), III.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.B.1","chart":{"columns":["Complaint type","Frequency"],"rows":[["Missing connectors","32"],["Wrong connectors","15"],["Directions not clear","20"],["Shipping damage","23"],["Lost shipment","10"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["15/47","15/100","32/100","47/100"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":238,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":49,"sourceQuestion":12,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is the probability that a randomly chosen complaint concerns wrong connectors?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"There are a total of 100 complaints. Fifteen concerned wrong connectors. The probability that a randomly chosen complaint is a wrong connector complaint is 15/100. <b>B. 15/100</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 12 (print p. 49), III.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"9542a2d47da6441f8adb2c11f464ee2be23bc04da393641afb0e2b1f6191095f"},"cssgb:set-2:source-239:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"III.B.1\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Complaint type\",\"Frequency\"],\"rows\":[[\"Missing connectors\",\"32\"],[\"Wrong connectors\",\"15\"],[\"Directions not clear\",\"20\"],[\"Shipping damage\",\"23\"],[\"Lost shipment\",\"10\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"10/100\",\"23/100\",\"33/100\",\"33/67\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":239,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":49,\"sourceQuestion\":13,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is the probability that a complaint involves a shipping concern?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"There are two types of shipping complaints: shipping damage and lost shipment. The total number of these complaints is 23 + 10 = 33. Therefore, P(Shipping complaint) = 33/100. <b>C. 33/100</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 13 (print p. 49), III.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"III.B.1","chart":{"columns":["Complaint type","Frequency"],"rows":[["Missing connectors","32"],["Wrong connectors","15"],["Directions not clear","20"],["Shipping damage","23"],["Lost shipment","10"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["10/100","23/100","33/100","33/67"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":239,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":49,"sourceQuestion":13,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is the probability that a complaint involves a shipping concern?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"There are two types of shipping complaints: shipping damage and lost shipment. The total number of these complaints is 23 + 10 = 33. Therefore, P(Shipping complaint) = 33/100. <b>C. 33/100</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 13 (print p. 49), III.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"b34c59f81bb9944679d7f5e9ea8024f473fc5babc9e0cfb4e2924299b27aef07"},"cssgb:set-2:source-23:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.D.4\",\"options\":[\"Stem-and-leaf\",\"Scatter diagram\",\"Pareto chart\",\"Run chart\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":23,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":153,\"sourceQuestion\":23,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"Which graphical tool provides a pictorial view of numerical data about location and spread?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"A stem-and-leaf plot shows the shape, center, and spread of the data while still preserving the original data values. <b>A. Stem-and-leaf</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 23 (print p. 153), III.D.4.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.D.4","options":["Stem-and-leaf","Scatter diagram","Pareto chart","Run chart"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":23,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":153,"sourceQuestion":23,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"Which graphical tool provides a pictorial view of numerical data about location and spread?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"A stem-and-leaf plot shows the shape, center, and spread of the data while still preserving the original data values. <b>A. Stem-and-leaf</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 23 (print p. 153), III.D.4.</span>"},"revision":"44b5b2b4e4d3c178d667d045cb93843edf6e3f17fa125067d29dc91f43c91f9e"},"cssgb:set-2:source-240:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"III.B.1\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Complaint type\",\"Frequency\"],\"rows\":[[\"Missing connectors\",\"32\"],[\"Wrong connectors\",\"15\"],[\"Directions not clear\",\"20\"],[\"Shipping damage\",\"23\"],[\"Lost shipment\",\"10\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"32/100\",\"15/32\",\"32/47\",\"47/100\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":240,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":49,\"sourceQuestion\":14,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is the probability that a connector complaint concerns missing connectors?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The probability that a connector complaint concerns missing connectors is found using the conditional probability formula. Let P(Connector complaint) = P(A) and P(Missing connector) = P(B). We then can calculate: P(Missing | Connector) = 32 / 47. <b>C. 32/47</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 14 (print p. 49), III.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"III.B.1","chart":{"columns":["Complaint type","Frequency"],"rows":[["Missing connectors","32"],["Wrong connectors","15"],["Directions not clear","20"],["Shipping damage","23"],["Lost shipment","10"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["32/100","15/32","32/47","47/100"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":240,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":49,"sourceQuestion":14,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is the probability that a connector complaint concerns missing connectors?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The probability that a connector complaint concerns missing connectors is found using the conditional probability formula. Let P(Connector complaint) = P(A) and P(Missing connector) = P(B). We then can calculate: P(Missing | Connector) = 32 / 47. <b>C. 32/47</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 14 (print p. 49), III.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"f23cfa135a22d9ecf9d4d4006e99ada911918d77b936e94b75e84b46f38f8285"},"cssgb:set-2:source-241:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"III.B.1\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"5\",\"3003\",\"360\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":241,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":49,\"sourceQuestion\":15,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"There are 15 inspection reports stored in a file folder. How many ways can five reports be chosen for an audit?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Five reports will be chosen from 15, in no particular order. We can use the combination formula to solve using n = 15 and r = 5. C(15,5) = 15! / [5!(15−5)!] = 3003. <b>C. 3003</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 15 (print p. 49), III.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"III.B.1","options":["3","5","3003","360"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":241,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":49,"sourceQuestion":15,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"There are 15 inspection reports stored in a file folder. How many ways can five reports be chosen for an audit?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Five reports will be chosen from 15, in no particular order. We can use the combination formula to solve using n = 15 and r = 5. C(15,5) = 15! / [5!(15−5)!] = 3003. <b>C. 3003</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 15 (print p. 49), III.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"8bb25cd4d6995c70767c7a2b259c328c84438f0a06aefd4136c49cc346f5b3cb"},"cssgb:set-2:source-242:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"III.B.1\",\"options\":[\"3\",\"60\",\"1140\",\"6840\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":242,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":50,\"sourceQuestion\":16,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"How many ways can a group of 20 candidates be assigned to the chair, vice-chair, and secretary positions of a volunteer organization?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Three candidates will be chosen for different positions, so order matters here. Using the permutation formula with n = 20 and r = 3 yields: P(20,3) = 20! / (20−3)! = 6840. <b>D. 6840</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 16 (print p. 50), III.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"III.B.1","options":["3","60","1140","6840"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":242,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":50,"sourceQuestion":16,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"How many ways can a group of 20 candidates be assigned to the chair, vice-chair, and secretary positions of a volunteer organization?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Three candidates will be chosen for different positions, so order matters here. Using the permutation formula with n = 20 and r = 3 yields: P(20,3) = 20! / (20−3)! = 6840. <b>D. 6840</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 16 (print p. 50), III.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"b0fad371ae33886da856575becf9f84e61cfa9c5ce23742a15ae056952132103"},"cssgb:set-2:source-243:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"III.B.2\",\"options\":[\"theory of constraints.\",\"Z distribution.\",\"Poisson distribution.\",\"central limit theorem.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":243,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":50,\"sourceQuestion\":17,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The distribution of averages tends to be normal, even when the distribution from which the averages are computed is not normal. This statement is a consequence of the:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The central limit states that averages tend to be normally distributed, even if the original distribution is not normally distributed. <b>D. central limit theorem.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 17 (print p. 50), III.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"III.B.2","options":["theory of constraints.","Z distribution.","Poisson distribution.","central limit theorem."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":243,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":50,"sourceQuestion":17,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The distribution of averages tends to be normal, even when the distribution from which the averages are computed is not normal. This statement is a consequence of the:","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The central limit states that averages tend to be normally distributed, even if the original distribution is not normally distributed. <b>D. central limit theorem.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 17 (print p. 50), III.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"14bbe8f519d2034f6a9354c9d4096b74d967f4d7098934d715915f1c930fbfbe"},"cssgb:set-2:source-244:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"III.B.2\",\"options\":[\"Exponential with μ = 10, σ = 10\",\"Normal with μ = 10, σ = 0.278\",\"Normal with μ = 10, σ = 1.667\",\"Normal with μ = 10, σ = 10\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":244,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":50,\"sourceQuestion\":18,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Samples of size 36 are taken from a skewed population with a mean equal to 10.0 and a standard deviation equal to 10.0. The sample averages will follow which distribution?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"This is an application of the central limit theorem. The sampling distribution of the mean is approximately normal with mean μ = 10 and standard error σ/√n = 10/√36 = 1.667. <b>C. Normal with μ = 10, σ = 1.667</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 18 (print p. 50), III.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"III.B.2","options":["Exponential with μ = 10, σ = 10","Normal with μ = 10, σ = 0.278","Normal with μ = 10, σ = 1.667","Normal with μ = 10, σ = 10"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":244,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":50,"sourceQuestion":18,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Samples of size 36 are taken from a skewed population with a mean equal to 10.0 and a standard deviation equal to 10.0. The sample averages will follow which distribution?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"This is an application of the central limit theorem. The sampling distribution of the mean is approximately normal with mean μ = 10 and standard error σ/√n = 10/√36 = 1.667. <b>C. Normal with μ = 10, σ = 1.667</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 18 (print p. 50), III.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"5568c21f047a5aa6b5cbd12290b59317834d33ab61730c133d2c74cab063adba"},"cssgb:set-2:source-245:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.B.2\",\"options\":[\"departures from normality.\",\"departures from special cause variation.\",\"alpha risk.\",\"the central limit theorem.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":245,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":50,\"sourceQuestion\":19,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Since control charts like X-bar and R charts and X-bar and s charts are plotted with averages of the individual readings, the charts are fairly robust against:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Since control charts like X-bar and R charts and X-bar and s charts are plotted with averages of the individual readings, the charts are fairly robust against departures from normality. This is a consequence of the central limit theorem. <b>A. departures from normality.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 19 (print p. 50), III.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.B.2","options":["departures from normality.","departures from special cause variation.","alpha risk.","the central limit theorem."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":245,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":50,"sourceQuestion":19,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Since control charts like X-bar and R charts and X-bar and s charts are plotted with averages of the individual readings, the charts are fairly robust against:","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Since control charts like X-bar and R charts and X-bar and s charts are plotted with averages of the individual readings, the charts are fairly robust against departures from normality. This is a consequence of the central limit theorem. <b>A. departures from normality.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 19 (print p. 50), III.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"a1e35f794086295d67bc13a10297fe7d120955be8fd3bc61f28ca66ef2d9b12a"},"cssgb:set-2:source-246:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"III.B\",\"options\":[\"Parameter\",\"Statistic\",\"Population\",\"Sample\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":246,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":50,\"sourceQuestion\":20,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"In a statistical study, what refers to the collection of all items or data under consideration?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The population is the collection of all items under consideration. <b>C. Population</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 20 (print p. 50), III.B.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"III.B","options":["Parameter","Statistic","Population","Sample"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":246,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":50,"sourceQuestion":20,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"In a statistical study, what refers to the collection of all items or data under consideration?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The population is the collection of all items under consideration. <b>C. Population</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 20 (print p. 50), III.B.</span>"},"revision":"34028dcfc20f309af6078af43fd5c2c56f124599209b91dc117b60053143318e"},"cssgb:set-2:source-247:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"III.B\",\"options\":[\"sample sizes.\",\"probabilities.\",\"sample statistics.\",\"population parameters.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":247,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":51,\"sourceQuestion\":21,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Greek letters are used to denote:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Greek letters denote population parameters. Latin letters are generally used to denote sample statistics and, sometimes, parameters. <b>D. population parameters.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 21 (print p. 51), III.B.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"III.B","options":["sample sizes.","probabilities.","sample statistics.","population parameters."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":247,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":51,"sourceQuestion":21,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Greek letters are used to denote:","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Greek letters denote population parameters. Latin letters are generally used to denote sample statistics and, sometimes, parameters. <b>D. population parameters.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 21 (print p. 51), III.B.</span>"},"revision":"d6d8be98f2da24e4e2618b97181f6b2727d840aadda324cfe516e8af17cc6278"},"cssgb:set-2:source-248:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"Normal\",\"Chi-square\",\"Binomial\",\"Poisson\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":248,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":51,\"sourceQuestion\":22,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The density function f(x) = [1/(σ√(2π))]e^−[(x−μ)²/(2σ²)] is used to model which distribution?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The normal probability density function is f(x) = [1 / (σ√(2π))]e^−[(x−μ)²/(2σ²)]. <b>A. Normal</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 22 (print p. 51), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["Normal","Chi-square","Binomial","Poisson"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":248,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":51,"sourceQuestion":22,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The density function f(x) = [1/(σ√(2π))]e^−[(x−μ)²/(2σ²)] is used to model which distribution?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The normal probability density function is f(x) = [1 / (σ√(2π))]e^−[(x−μ)²/(2σ²)]. <b>A. Normal</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 22 (print p. 51), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"42821d332a2b88580fdb04c3bac078bd62dd26868b86d2d2fb1c5df82ef2d86a"},"cssgb:set-2:source-249:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"Normal\",\"Chi-square\",\"Binomial\",\"Poisson\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":249,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":51,\"sourceQuestion\":23,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which distribution is modeled by the probability mass function P(X = x) = λˣe^−λ/x!?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The Poisson probability mass function is P(X = x) = λˣe^−λ / x!. <b>D. Poisson</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 23 (print p. 51), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["Normal","Chi-square","Binomial","Poisson"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":249,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":51,"sourceQuestion":23,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which distribution is modeled by the probability mass function P(X = x) = λˣe^−λ/x!?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The Poisson probability mass function is P(X = x) = λˣe^−λ / x!. <b>D. Poisson</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 23 (print p. 51), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"ef6c7027d014d122923e1e3795e09e7db1be0e9b9d09a9101ce43e559eb7d60c"},"cssgb:set-2:source-24:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Measure\",\"Length\",\"Weight\",\"Strength\"],\"rows\":[[\"Historical %\",\"50%\",\"25%\",\"25%\"],[\"Last month\",\"60\",\"35\",\"45\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"The test statistic = 3.889, and the critical value = 5.991.\",\"The test statistic = 4.286, and the critical value = 5.991.\",\"The test statistic = 3.889, and the critical value = 7.815.\",\"The test statistic = 4.286, and the critical value = 7.815.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":24,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":153,\"sourceQuestion\":24,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"A Green Belt performs a chi-square goodness-of-fit test with alpha = 0.05 to determine whether the distribution of defect types last month differed from the historical percentages. Given the data table below, which statement is true?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"The expected counts are 70, 35, and 35. χ² = (60−70)²/70 + (35−35)²/35 + (45−35)²/35 = 4.286. With df = 3−1 = 2 and α = 0.05, the critical value is 5.991. <b>B. The test statistic = 4.286, and the critical value = 5.991.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 24 (print p. 153), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","chart":{"columns":["Measure","Length","Weight","Strength"],"rows":[["Historical %","50%","25%","25%"],["Last month","60","35","45"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["The test statistic = 3.889, and the critical value = 5.991.","The test statistic = 4.286, and the critical value = 5.991.","The test statistic = 3.889, and the critical value = 7.815.","The test statistic = 4.286, and the critical value = 7.815."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":24,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":153,"sourceQuestion":24,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"A Green Belt performs a chi-square goodness-of-fit test with alpha = 0.05 to determine whether the distribution of defect types last month differed from the historical percentages. Given the data table below, which statement is true?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"The expected counts are 70, 35, and 35. χ² = (60−70)²/70 + (35−35)²/35 + (45−35)²/35 = 4.286. With df = 3−1 = 2 and α = 0.05, the critical value is 5.991. <b>B. The test statistic = 4.286, and the critical value = 5.991.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 24 (print p. 153), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"6b0973876436262bd19a6383b0bea6cc4a18cf4b4c4a56b345d31fd2a86c0e91"},"cssgb:set-2:source-250:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"σ²\",\"λ\",\"λ²\",\"e−λ\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":250,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":51,\"sourceQuestion\":24,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is the variance of the Poisson distribution?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The Poisson distribution’s mean and variance are equal to λ. <b>B. λ</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 24 (print p. 51), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["σ²","λ","λ²","e−λ"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":250,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":51,"sourceQuestion":24,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is the variance of the Poisson distribution?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The Poisson distribution’s mean and variance are equal to λ. <b>B. λ</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 24 (print p. 51), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"e849e4c094dfaa780cf691a3ea3fa2e54ebdac8598fc855b7e29455127cffbcf"},"cssgb:set-2:source-251:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"Normal\",\"Poisson\",\"Student’s t\",\"Chi-square\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":251,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":52,\"sourceQuestion\":25,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which distribution requires two parameters to fully describe it?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The normal distribution is fully described by two parameters: μ and σ². <b>A. Normal</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 25 (print p. 52), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["Normal","Poisson","Student’s t","Chi-square"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":251,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":52,"sourceQuestion":25,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which distribution requires two parameters to fully describe it?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The normal distribution is fully described by two parameters: μ and σ². <b>A. Normal</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 25 (print p. 52), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"044a30a83d1b76670d7b730109d61bc259c34dd5d7b73d4bc3fb1bc6ebc5d7bb"},"cssgb:set-2:source-252:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"Normal\",\"Binomial\",\"Poisson\",\"Chi-square\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":252,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":52,\"sourceQuestion\":26,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which distribution would be used to model the number of surface defects found on samples of 10 stainless steel panels?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The Poisson distribution is used to count defects per unit. The number of defects has no natural upper limit. <b>C. Poisson</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 26 (print p. 52), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["Normal","Binomial","Poisson","Chi-square"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":252,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":52,"sourceQuestion":26,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which distribution would be used to model the number of surface defects found on samples of 10 stainless steel panels?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The Poisson distribution is used to count defects per unit. The number of defects has no natural upper limit. <b>C. Poisson</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 26 (print p. 52), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"a0639564bfd90b71c78387fe39c773cd72876394669b4903f89f8508e7474cc2"},"cssgb:set-2:source-253:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"Normal\",\"Binomial\",\"Poisson\",\"Student’s t\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":253,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":52,\"sourceQuestion\":27,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which distribution would be used to find the probability that three defective units will be found in a sample of 20 units?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The binomial distribution is used to find the probability of x defectives in a sample of n. <b>B. Binomial</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 27 (print p. 52), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["Normal","Binomial","Poisson","Student’s t"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":253,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":52,"sourceQuestion":27,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which distribution would be used to find the probability that three defective units will be found in a sample of 20 units?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The binomial distribution is used to find the probability of x defectives in a sample of n. <b>B. Binomial</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 27 (print p. 52), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"579ded3076cba7d5150a9a6dcf7666ad2d3341b5cd138c50e6bbe99c72f16fbc"},"cssgb:set-2:source-254:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"Student’s t\",\"Binomial\",\"Chi-square\",\"F\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":254,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":52,\"sourceQuestion\":28,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which sampling distribution is symmetric with heavier tails than a normal distribution?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The Student’s t distribution is symmetric and has a heavier tail than the normal distribution. As the sample size n increases, the tails of the Student’s t distribution become less heavy, and the distribution looks more and more normal. <b>A. Student’s t</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 28 (print p. 52), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["Student’s t","Binomial","Chi-square","F"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":254,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":52,"sourceQuestion":28,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which sampling distribution is symmetric with heavier tails than a normal distribution?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The Student’s t distribution is symmetric and has a heavier tail than the normal distribution. As the sample size n increases, the tails of the Student’s t distribution become less heavy, and the distribution looks more and more normal. <b>A. Student’s t</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 28 (print p. 52), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"6b76f03d5e7130531e900c18dfa9b5ee5a924baa64f6995a4a63d3064bfcdcb1"},"cssgb:set-2:source-255:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"Normal\",\"Chi-square\",\"Student’s t\",\"F\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":255,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":52,\"sourceQuestion\":29,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which distribution is used to test the ratio of two variances?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"We test the ratio of two variances using the F distribution. <b>D. F</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 29 (print p. 52), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["Normal","Chi-square","Student’s t","F"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":255,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":52,"sourceQuestion":29,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which distribution is used to test the ratio of two variances?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"We test the ratio of two variances using the F distribution. <b>D. F</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 29 (print p. 52), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"b97f1262b32a2d53d82b230d8f10b65c6622052848a496d572e34885e2ef817b"},"cssgb:set-2:source-256:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"Normal\",\"Chi-square\",\"Student’s t\",\"F\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":256,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":53,\"sourceQuestion\":30,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which distribution is used to test a standard deviation against a standard?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The chi-square distribution is used to test a variance or standard deviation against a standard. <b>B. Chi-square</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 30 (print p. 53), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["Normal","Chi-square","Student’s t","F"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":256,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":53,"sourceQuestion":30,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which distribution is used to test a standard deviation against a standard?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The chi-square distribution is used to test a variance or standard deviation against a standard. <b>B. Chi-square</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 30 (print p. 53), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"84769cde6c259f4f64c2beaaeee88a474a599ea7309107f859d63aacf3f39d4e"},"cssgb:set-2:source-257:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"0.4096\",\"0.3543\",\"0.3281\",\"0.2916\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":257,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":53,\"sourceQuestion\":31,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A sample of size six is randomly selected from a batch with a 10% defective rate. Find the probability that the sample has exactly one defective.\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"For X ~ Binomial(n = 6, p = 0.10), P(X = 1) = C(6,1)(0.10)(0.90)⁵ = 0.354294, which rounds to 0.3543. The book''s printed key says 0.3281 even though its displayed calculation gives 0.3543; the student-facing key is corrected here. <b>B. 0.3543</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 31 (print p. 53), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["0.4096","0.3543","0.3281","0.2916"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":257,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":53,"sourceQuestion":31,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A sample of size six is randomly selected from a batch with a 10% defective rate. Find the probability that the sample has exactly one defective.","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"For X ~ Binomial(n = 6, p = 0.10), P(X = 1) = C(6,1)(0.10)(0.90)⁵ = 0.354294, which rounds to 0.3543. The book''s printed key says 0.3281 even though its displayed calculation gives 0.3543; the student-facing key is corrected here. <b>B. 0.3543</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 31 (print p. 53), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"026a7aeda59d1894fda6f09d1587df8edaace218daa2e69c7434d94224993ed9"},"cssgb:set-2:source-258:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"0.0905\",\"0.3679\",\"0.7358\",\"0.9953\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":258,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":53,\"sourceQuestion\":32,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"An electronic component manufacturer has an average defect rate of 0.01 per unit. What is the probability that an inspector finds one or fewer defects in a sample of 100 units?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"This is an application of the Poisson distribution in which we are counting defects per unit. If there are an average of 0.01 defects per unit, we should expect (0.01)(100) = 1 defect per 100 units. We can use the Poisson distribution with λ = 1. To find the probability of finding one or zero defects in 100 units: P(X ≤ 1) = e⁻¹ + e⁻¹ = 0.7358. <b>C. 0.7358</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 32 (print p. 53), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["0.0905","0.3679","0.7358","0.9953"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":258,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":53,"sourceQuestion":32,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"An electronic component manufacturer has an average defect rate of 0.01 per unit. What is the probability that an inspector finds one or fewer defects in a sample of 100 units?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"This is an application of the Poisson distribution in which we are counting defects per unit. If there are an average of 0.01 defects per unit, we should expect (0.01)(100) = 1 defect per 100 units. We can use the Poisson distribution with λ = 1. To find the probability of finding one or zero defects in 100 units: P(X ≤ 1) = e⁻¹ + e⁻¹ = 0.7358. <b>C. 0.7358</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 32 (print p. 53), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"87af156c891bdfd79ff4993217c03113210b8d2247522b640c27ddfc600ab791"},"cssgb:set-2:source-259:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"Z\",\"F\",\"t\",\"χ²\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":259,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":53,\"sourceQuestion\":33,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A normal distribution can be standardized using which transformation?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The Z transformation standardizes any normal distribution with a mean μ and variance σ² into a standard normal with mean equal to 0 and variance equal to 1. Note: Z = (X−μ) / σ. <b>A. Z</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 33 (print p. 53), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["Z","F","t","χ²"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":259,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":53,"sourceQuestion":33,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A normal distribution can be standardized using which transformation?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The Z transformation standardizes any normal distribution with a mean μ and variance σ² into a standard normal with mean equal to 0 and variance equal to 1. Note: Z = (X−μ) / σ. <b>A. Z</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 33 (print p. 53), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"19accc53fe05b9c0407ffdb726f23541d083172afeab83fbf0146cfbce6aff31"},"cssgb:set-2:source-25:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"VI.A.3\",\"options\":[\"1\",\"2\",\"3\",\"4\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":25,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":154,\"sourceQuestion\":25,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following subgroup sizes for an X-bar and R chart would most likely be used if measurement were costly?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"If measurement were costly, it is likely that a sample size of 2 would be used for the X-bar and R chart. A sample size equal to 1 must be plotted using an XmR chart. <b>B. 2</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 25 (print p. 154), VI.A.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"VI.A.3","options":["1","2","3","4"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":25,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":154,"sourceQuestion":25,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"Which of the following subgroup sizes for an X-bar and R chart would most likely be used if measurement were costly?","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"If measurement were costly, it is likely that a sample size of 2 would be used for the X-bar and R chart. A sample size equal to 1 must be plotted using an XmR chart. <b>B. 2</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 25 (print p. 154), VI.A.3.</span>"},"revision":"c362510a346b8cda1c07eea73eabe55f7226157dc6667bc819dd993c0b1bcb13"},"cssgb:set-2:source-260:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"Poisson\",\"Student’s t\",\"Binomial\",\"F\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":260,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":53,\"sourceQuestion\":34,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following probability distributions has negative values?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Among the choices, only the Student’s t distribution has negative values. <b>B. Student’s t</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 34 (print p. 53), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["Poisson","Student’s t","Binomial","F"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":260,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":53,"sourceQuestion":34,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which of the following probability distributions has negative values?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Among the choices, only the Student’s t distribution has negative values. <b>B. Student’s t</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 34 (print p. 53), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"e57774272f330f55717d4032ad9c2ad3a7c1dfc4d22656705f9be496836b0e22"},"cssgb:set-2:source-261:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"0.1455\",\"0.1524\",\"0.8500\",\"0.8545\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":261,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":54,\"sourceQuestion\":35,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Find the area under the standard normal curve between 1.00 standard deviation and 2.50 standard deviations from the mean.\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Using a standard normal table, P(1.00 &lt; Z &lt; 2.50) = P(Z &lt; 2.50) − P(Z &lt; 1.00) = 0.99379 − 0.84134 = 0.15245, which rounds to 0.1524. <b>B. 0.1524</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 35 (print p. 54), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["0.1455","0.1524","0.8500","0.8545"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":261,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":54,"sourceQuestion":35,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Find the area under the standard normal curve between 1.00 standard deviation and 2.50 standard deviations from the mean.","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Using a standard normal table, P(1.00 &lt; Z &lt; 2.50) = P(Z &lt; 2.50) − P(Z &lt; 1.00) = 0.99379 − 0.84134 = 0.15245, which rounds to 0.1524. <b>B. 0.1524</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 35 (print p. 54), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"9dbe9f82ebdf70b9ee9f1495ea635162eac899265c2d9e91d25d148bd7984964"},"cssgb:set-2:source-262:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"6.68%\",\"44.04%\",\"55.96%\",\"93.32%\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":262,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":54,\"sourceQuestion\":36,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The weights of soup cans follow a normal distribution with a mean of 16.0 oz and a standard deviation of 0.005 oz. What percentage of cans weigh more than 16.0075 oz?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The weight variable follows a N(16.0, 0.005²) distribution. To find P(X &gt; 16.0075), we will use the standard normal distribution. Z = (16.0075−16.0) / 0.005 = 1.5. Using a cumulative standard normal table, we find P(Z &gt; 1.5) = 1− P(Z &lt; 1.5) = (1 − 0.9332) = 0.0668 = 6.68%. Note that the standard normal table in Appendix F of The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Handbook gives values for P(Z &gt; z), so the answer can be taken directly from the table. <b>A. 6.68%</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 36 (print p. 54), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["6.68%","44.04%","55.96%","93.32%"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":262,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":54,"sourceQuestion":36,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The weights of soup cans follow a normal distribution with a mean of 16.0 oz and a standard deviation of 0.005 oz. What percentage of cans weigh more than 16.0075 oz?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The weight variable follows a N(16.0, 0.005²) distribution. To find P(X &gt; 16.0075), we will use the standard normal distribution. Z = (16.0075−16.0) / 0.005 = 1.5. Using a cumulative standard normal table, we find P(Z &gt; 1.5) = 1− P(Z &lt; 1.5) = (1 − 0.9332) = 0.0668 = 6.68%. Note that the standard normal table in Appendix F of The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Handbook gives values for P(Z &gt; z), so the answer can be taken directly from the table. <b>A. 6.68%</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 36 (print p. 54), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"48143e5d12fd644f351bf2dd76609cad3fdb7c77a50328cccec7ae637c33aa52"},"cssgb:set-2:source-263:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"0.0918\",\"0.9053\",\"0.9082\",\"0.9970\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":263,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":54,\"sourceQuestion\":37,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Find P (−1.33 < Z < 2.75).\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Using a standard normal table, P(−1.33 &lt; Z &lt; 2.75) = 0.99702 − 0.09176 = 0.90526, which rounds to 0.9053. <b>B. 0.9053</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 37 (print p. 54), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["0.0918","0.9053","0.9082","0.9970"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":263,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":54,"sourceQuestion":37,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Find P (−1.33 < Z < 2.75).","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Using a standard normal table, P(−1.33 &lt; Z &lt; 2.75) = 0.99702 − 0.09176 = 0.90526, which rounds to 0.9053. <b>B. 0.9053</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 37 (print p. 54), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"0208b0889fcea02710ba5d4decb1f35d045b8c103bd505f96eb73d902e523f89"},"cssgb:set-2:source-264:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"0.3085\",\"0.5000\",\"0.6915\",\"0.9915\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":264,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":54,\"sourceQuestion\":38,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Find the probability that Z ≥ 0.50.\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"To find P(Z ≥ 0.50), we can find 1− P(Z &lt; 0.50) using the standard normal table, in which 1− P(Z &lt; 0.50) = 1− 0.69146 = 0.30854. Note that the standard normal table in Appendix F of The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Handbook gives values for P(Z &gt; z), so the answer can be taken directly from the table. <b>A. 0.3085</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 38 (print p. 54), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["0.3085","0.5000","0.6915","0.9915"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":264,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":54,"sourceQuestion":38,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Find the probability that Z ≥ 0.50.","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"To find P(Z ≥ 0.50), we can find 1− P(Z &lt; 0.50) using the standard normal table, in which 1− P(Z &lt; 0.50) = 1− 0.69146 = 0.30854. Note that the standard normal table in Appendix F of The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Handbook gives values for P(Z &gt; z), so the answer can be taken directly from the table. <b>A. 0.3085</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 38 (print p. 54), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"9b126f7f8580a15ef6c6b47e025f0de18ba6f8b16cc3a6bb3290ea1efe96e84d"},"cssgb:set-2:source-265:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"0.711\",\"9.488\",\"11.070\",\"12.592\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":265,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":54,\"sourceQuestion\":39,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Find the critical value for the chi-square distribution with 5 degrees of freedom and α = 0.05.\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Using the chi-square table, we look up the value associated with 5 degrees of freedom and α = 0.05 to get 11.070. Appendix I in The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Handbook can be used as a reference. <b>C. 11.070</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 39 (print p. 54), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["0.711","9.488","11.070","12.592"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":265,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":54,"sourceQuestion":39,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Find the critical value for the chi-square distribution with 5 degrees of freedom and α = 0.05.","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Using the chi-square table, we look up the value associated with 5 degrees of freedom and α = 0.05 to get 11.070. Appendix I in The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Handbook can be used as a reference. <b>C. 11.070</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 39 (print p. 54), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"cbbaa0e775a318c007a4c57c63643fb1c0c342fe824e20af7737e2019aa60dbe"},"cssgb:set-2:source-266:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"2.365\",\"2.447\",\"3.707\",\"3.499\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":266,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":55,\"sourceQuestion\":40,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Find the critical value for a Student’s t statistic with 7 degrees of freedom and α = 0.025.\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Using the Student’s t table, we look up the value associated with 7 degrees of freedom and α = 0.025 to get 2.365. Appendix L in The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Handbook can be used as a reference. <b>A. 2.365</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 40 (print p. 55), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["2.365","2.447","3.707","3.499"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":266,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":55,"sourceQuestion":40,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Find the critical value for a Student’s t statistic with 7 degrees of freedom and α = 0.025.","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Using the Student’s t table, we look up the value associated with 7 degrees of freedom and α = 0.025 to get 2.365. Appendix L in The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Handbook can be used as a reference. <b>A. 2.365</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 40 (print p. 55), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"43d871f5f6830ac87641c510d05ac23ec87e85efbe85ade0f906482c2d4bddfd"},"cssgb:set-2:source-267:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.C\",\"options\":[\"1.72\",\"2.01\",\"2.07\",\"2.70\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":267,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":55,\"sourceQuestion\":41,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Find the critical value for an F statistic with 20 and 25 degrees of freedom and α = 0.05.\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Using the F table, we look up the value associated with 20 and 25 degrees of freedom and α = 0.05 to get 2.01. Appendix G in The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Handbook can be used as a reference. <b>B. 2.01</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 41 (print p. 55), III.C.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.C","options":["1.72","2.01","2.07","2.70"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":267,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":55,"sourceQuestion":41,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Find the critical value for an F statistic with 20 and 25 degrees of freedom and α = 0.05.","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Using the F table, we look up the value associated with 20 and 25 degrees of freedom and α = 0.05 to get 2.01. Appendix G in The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Handbook can be used as a reference. <b>B. 2.01</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 41 (print p. 55), III.C.</span>"},"revision":"6e07fa75a56e6f37b2829e0e3691fa3aec3b7f0ad9b555e1e008e3807b117603"},"cssgb:set-2:source-268:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.D.1\",\"options\":[\"Ratio\",\"Interval\",\"Ordinal\",\"Nominal\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":268,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":55,\"sourceQuestion\":42,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which measurement scale has meaningful intervals and data that can be ordered but no meaningful zero point?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Data on the interval scale have intrinsic numerical meaning, can be ordered, and the intervals between values are meaningful. However, interval data does not have a true zero point. Examples include temperature in Fahrenheit and Celsius, and calendar years. <b>B. Interval</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 42 (print p. 55), III.D.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.D.1","options":["Ratio","Interval","Ordinal","Nominal"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":268,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":55,"sourceQuestion":42,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which measurement scale has meaningful intervals and data that can be ordered but no meaningful zero point?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Data on the interval scale have intrinsic numerical meaning, can be ordered, and the intervals between values are meaningful. However, interval data does not have a true zero point. Examples include temperature in Fahrenheit and Celsius, and calendar years. <b>B. Interval</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 42 (print p. 55), III.D.1.</span>"},"revision":"1f27fb16a49a47be3befc1854cd753e093f1ef2a650c60204e16e35ca56bd306"},"cssgb:set-2:source-269:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"III.D.1\",\"options\":[\"Ratio\",\"Interval\",\"Ordinal\",\"Nominal\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":269,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":55,\"sourceQuestion\":43,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Employees are issued identification numbers based on a combination of numbers and letters. The IDs are an example of which measurement scale?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Data on the nominal scale have no intrinsic numerical meaning and no ordering scheme. Often, nominal data is made up of words or numerical sequences that serve as labels, such as social security numbers or employee IDs. <b>D. Nominal</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 43 (print p. 55), III.D.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"III.D.1","options":["Ratio","Interval","Ordinal","Nominal"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":269,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":55,"sourceQuestion":43,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Employees are issued identification numbers based on a combination of numbers and letters. The IDs are an example of which measurement scale?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Data on the nominal scale have no intrinsic numerical meaning and no ordering scheme. Often, nominal data is made up of words or numerical sequences that serve as labels, such as social security numbers or employee IDs. <b>D. Nominal</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 43 (print p. 55), III.D.1.</span>"},"revision":"7cfa18a59c5e1064e44b137f3fc56a7789b8d1e5d43d0ab1df4fd201390c6523"},"cssgb:set-2:source-26:10hzvwm":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"I.A.2\",\"options\":[\"voice of the customer.\",\"value stream mapping.\",\"kaizen blitz.\",\"process FMEA.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":26,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":154,\"sourceQuestion\":26,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"Warranty data, complaint logs, and focus groups are inputs for:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"The voice of the customer uses warranty data, complaint logs, focus groups, and other customer-facing sources of data. <b>A. voice of the customer.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 26 (print p. 154), I.A.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"I.A.2","options":["voice of the customer.","value stream mapping.","kaizen blitz.","process FMEA."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":26,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":154,"sourceQuestion":26,"sourceSection":"I. Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"Warranty data, complaint logs, and focus groups are inputs for:","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"The voice of the customer uses warranty data, complaint logs, focus groups, and other customer-facing sources of data. <b>A. voice of the customer.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 26 (print p. 154), I.A.2.</span>"},"revision":"6c87fb13959382d8bf0293b246cd92ec399218985968f77ec75c9f9effa7f21b"},"cssgb:set-2:source-270:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"III.D.1\",\"options\":[\"Ratio\",\"Interval\",\"Ordinal\",\"Nominal\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":270,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":55,\"sourceQuestion\":44,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"In which measurement scale do values have an order, but differences between adjacent values are indeterminate?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"In the ordinal measurement scale, data have inherent numerical value and can be ordered, but the difference between values is not necessarily equal. Ranks are an example of the ordinal measurement scale. <b>C. Ordinal</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 44 (print p. 55), III.D.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"III.D.1","options":["Ratio","Interval","Ordinal","Nominal"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":270,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":55,"sourceQuestion":44,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"In which measurement scale do values have an order, but differences between adjacent values are indeterminate?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"In the ordinal measurement scale, data have inherent numerical value and can be ordered, but the difference between values is not necessarily equal. Ranks are an example of the ordinal measurement scale. <b>C. Ordinal</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 44 (print p. 55), III.D.1.</span>"},"revision":"31ecd6f2bbb52e1056539ed52683f169af4ced5a8d9c800143aadb2bb16b4bbe"},"cssgb:set-2:source-271:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.D.1\",\"options\":[\"Ratio\",\"Interval\",\"Ordinal\",\"Nominal\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":271,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":56,\"sourceQuestion\":45,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which measurement scale has intervals between adjacent scale values that are equal with respect to the attribute being measured and a true zero?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The ratio measurement scale has inherent numerical value, can be ordered, has known intervals between data points, and has a true zero. Examples are weights, length, costs, or pressure readings. <b>A. Ratio</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 45 (print p. 56), III.D.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.D.1","options":["Ratio","Interval","Ordinal","Nominal"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":271,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":56,"sourceQuestion":45,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which measurement scale has intervals between adjacent scale values that are equal with respect to the attribute being measured and a true zero?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The ratio measurement scale has inherent numerical value, can be ordered, has known intervals between data points, and has a true zero. Examples are weights, length, costs, or pressure readings. <b>A. Ratio</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 45 (print p. 56), III.D.1.</span>"},"revision":"63d51a1161efa80e2b96171a4ab0eae63fe2d14f574c402d6ebbed6b3c2d5710"},"cssgb:set-2:source-272:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.D.1\",\"options\":[\"Ratio\",\"Interval\",\"Ordinal\",\"Nominal\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":272,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":56,\"sourceQuestion\":46,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Temperature measured in Fahrenheit is an example of which measure scale?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Temperature measured in degrees Fahrenheit is an example of the interval scale. The values have intrinsic numerical meaning, can be ordered, and the intervals between values are meaningful. However, interval data does not have a true zero point. Both the Fahrenheit and the Celsius scales have arbitrary zero points. <b>B. Interval</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 46 (print p. 56), III.D.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.D.1","options":["Ratio","Interval","Ordinal","Nominal"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":272,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":56,"sourceQuestion":46,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Temperature measured in Fahrenheit is an example of which measure scale?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Temperature measured in degrees Fahrenheit is an example of the interval scale. The values have intrinsic numerical meaning, can be ordered, and the intervals between values are meaningful. However, interval data does not have a true zero point. Both the Fahrenheit and the Celsius scales have arbitrary zero points. <b>B. Interval</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 46 (print p. 56), III.D.1.</span>"},"revision":"98eb547436b2a2589985100f9b8caed49a852100516b5c3aae4aa787d157b287"},"cssgb:set-2:source-273:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.D.1\",\"options\":[\"Discrete\",\"Continuous\",\"Locational\",\"Categorical\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":273,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":56,\"sourceQuestion\":47,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"When setting up a data collection scheme for a project, which data set will be the most useful for measuring sensitivity to process changes?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Continuous data contains the most information and is more sensitive to process changes than discrete data. <b>B. Continuous</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 47 (print p. 56), III.D.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.D.1","options":["Discrete","Continuous","Locational","Categorical"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":273,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":56,"sourceQuestion":47,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"When setting up a data collection scheme for a project, which data set will be the most useful for measuring sensitivity to process changes?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Continuous data contains the most information and is more sensitive to process changes than discrete data. <b>B. Continuous</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 47 (print p. 56), III.D.1.</span>"},"revision":"7ef5596606adaa41e233a132512b3d4fde6e7f4e3a8c621db3f40e686373afd8"},"cssgb:set-2:source-274:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"III.D.1\",\"options\":[\"Smaller\",\"Equal\",\"Larger\",\"Cannot determine from the information given\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":274,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":56,\"sourceQuestion\":48,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Compared to continuous data, discrete data requires what sample size to achieve the same Type II error?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Discrete data do not contain as much information as continuous data. To achieve the same Type II error, a larger sample size must be collected compared to continuous data. <b>C. Larger</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 48 (print p. 56), III.D.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"III.D.1","options":["Smaller","Equal","Larger","Cannot determine from the information given"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":274,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":56,"sourceQuestion":48,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Compared to continuous data, discrete data requires what sample size to achieve the same Type II error?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Discrete data do not contain as much information as continuous data. To achieve the same Type II error, a larger sample size must be collected compared to continuous data. <b>C. Larger</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 48 (print p. 56), III.D.1.</span>"},"revision":"71f07565b05729497b7cfa2e64f8eab1a20e0888b4882ba9de31603e8a3453cd"},"cssgb:set-2:source-275:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.D.2\",\"options\":[\"What will be measured, training plan, who will measure, and the sampling plan\",\"What will be measured, how it will be measured, who will measure, and the sampling plan\",\"What will be measured, measurement cost, who will measure, and the sampling plan\",\"What will be measured, process flow, who will measure, and the control plan\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":275,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":56,\"sourceQuestion\":49,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"In developing a data collection plan, what elements are needed?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"A data collection plan will include the elements: what will be measured, how it will be measured, who will measure, and the sampling plan. <b>B. What will be measured, how it will be measured, who will measure, and the sampling plan</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 49 (print p. 56), III.D.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.D.2","options":["What will be measured, training plan, who will measure, and the sampling plan","What will be measured, how it will be measured, who will measure, and the sampling plan","What will be measured, measurement cost, who will measure, and the sampling plan","What will be measured, process flow, who will measure, and the control plan"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":275,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":56,"so