","29","23","10","11"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["18.4","19.6","19.8","20.6"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":304,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":63,"sourceQuestion":78,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is the average reading for appraiser A2?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Calculate only the line for A2 to find the average of the five parts measured = 19.8. <b>C. 19.8</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 78 (print p. 63), III.E.</span>"},"revision":"6d1d159980ade75bce15473300d5ec9b895747296f7f536f2d41a89e581c9bd5"},"cssgb:set-2:source-305:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.E\",\"options\":[\"stability.\",\"linearity.\",\"bias.\",\"reproducibility.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":305,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":64,\"sourceQuestion\":79,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"If the bias of a measurement system increases across the measurement range, the measuring device is said to have poor:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"If the bias of a measurement system increases across the measurement range, the measuring device is said to have poor linearity. A system with good linearity will have a constant bias across the range of the measurement. <b>B. linearity.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 79 (print p. 64), III.E.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.E","options":["stability.","linearity.","bias.","reproducibility."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":305,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":64,"sourceQuestion":79,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"If the bias of a measurement system increases across the measurement range, the measuring device is said to have poor:","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"If the bias of a measurement system increases across the measurement range, the measuring device is said to have poor linearity. A system with good linearity will have a constant bias across the range of the measurement. <b>B. linearity.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 79 (print p. 64), III.E.</span>"},"revision":"95a523e5253da9a21a2c5fe53c0f234c6321aafa6242d71124d363861b706dff"},"cssgb:set-2:source-306:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.E\",\"options\":[\"2.44\",\"5.21\",\"5.96\",\"27.14\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":306,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":64,\"sourceQuestion\":80,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A measurement system has been evaluated with the following results: repeatability = 0.81 and reproducibility = 5.15. What is the gage R&R?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"To calculate gage R&amp;R, we use: GR&amp;R = √(repeatability² + reproducibility²) = √(0.81² + 5.15²) = 5.21. <b>B. 5.21</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 80 (print p. 64), III.E.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.E","options":["2.44","5.21","5.96","27.14"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":306,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":64,"sourceQuestion":80,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A measurement system has been evaluated with the following results: repeatability = 0.81 and reproducibility = 5.15. What is the gage R&R?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"To calculate gage R&amp;R, we use: GR&amp;R = √(repeatability² + reproducibility²) = √(0.81² + 5.15²) = 5.21. <b>B. 5.21</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 80 (print p. 64), III.E.</span>"},"revision":"f70977840c140b11d94ab8f6e40b022a85cdcb3dbf2ae4a5e770f3f7ff3e8cfb"},"cssgb:set-2:source-307:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.E\",\"options\":[\"The measurement system is stable.\",\"The variability of the measurement system is too large.\",\"The variability of the products is too large.\",\"The specification range is too small.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":307,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":64,\"sourceQuestion\":81,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A precision-to-tolerance ratio is calculated to be 0.50. What can be said about the measurement system?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"A precision-to-tolerance ratio (PTR) is a measure of the capability of the measurement system. PTR values less than 0.10 are considered good, while values greater than 0.30 require improvement in the measurement system in terms of reducing its variability. <b>B. The variability of the measurement system is too large.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 81 (print p. 64), III.E.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.E","options":["The measurement system is stable.","The variability of the measurement system is too large.","The variability of the products is too large.","The specification range is too small."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":307,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":64,"sourceQuestion":81,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A precision-to-tolerance ratio is calculated to be 0.50. What can be said about the measurement system?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"A precision-to-tolerance ratio (PTR) is a measure of the capability of the measurement system. PTR values less than 0.10 are considered good, while values greater than 0.30 require improvement in the measurement system in terms of reducing its variability. <b>B. The variability of the measurement system is too large.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 81 (print p. 64), III.E.</span>"},"revision":"5c64734cde35ae60e2950ee6ec8d80401ae7b01b1e4a7dcb337e3b7a6af8b0ba"},"cssgb:set-2:source-308:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"III.E\",\"chart\":{\"type\":\"bias-diagram\"},\"options\":[\"Precision\",\"Stability\",\"Linear bias\",\"Repeatability\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":308,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":64,\"sourceQuestion\":82,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is the measurement system concept depicted in the figure below?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The figure depicts the concept of linear bias, in which the measured values are consistently biased across the range of measurement. <b>C. Linear bias</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 82 (print p. 64), III.E.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"III.E","chart":{"type":"bias-diagram"},"options":["Precision","Stability","Linear bias","Repeatability"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":308,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":64,"sourceQuestion":82,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is the measurement system concept depicted in the figure below?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The figure depicts the concept of linear bias, in which the measured values are consistently biased across the range of measurement. <b>C. Linear bias</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 82 (print p. 64), III.E.</span>"},"revision":"cdbdf381e1320672cd304f7f47588967575a5fc71f1c3ca2d5201c089e258301"},"cssgb:set-2:source-309:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.E\",\"options\":[\"Gage R&R and appraiser variation\",\"Part-to-part variation and gage R&R\",\"Equipment variation and gage R&R\",\"Part-to-part variation and repeatability\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":309,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":65,\"sourceQuestion\":83,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which components combine to form total observed variation in a Gage R&R study?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Total observed variation combines part-to-part variation with gage repeatability and reproducibility (GR&amp;R). GR&amp;R alone represents the measurement-system contribution. <b>B. Part-to-part variation and gage R&amp;R</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 83 (print p. 65), III.E.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.E","options":["Gage R&R and appraiser variation","Part-to-part variation and gage R&R","Equipment variation and gage R&R","Part-to-part variation and repeatability"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":309,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":65,"sourceQuestion":83,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which components combine to form total observed variation in a Gage R&R study?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Total observed variation combines part-to-part variation with gage repeatability and reproducibility (GR&amp;R). GR&amp;R alone represents the measurement-system contribution. <b>B. Part-to-part variation and gage R&amp;R</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 83 (print p. 65), III.E.</span>"},"revision":"9c6fe0c48e2cc2c3a9a895602858de0a32e2ae8f4c33fb6d8baa6cee5a45131a"},"cssgb:set-2:source-30:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.E\",\"options\":[\"altering the samples during the study.\",\"using parts that cover the range of the gage.\",\"using overtrained inspectors.\",\"using parts that cover the specification range.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":30,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":155,\"sourceQuestion\":30,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"When conducting an MSA study, a common error made while performing GR&R is:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"A common error made while performing a gage R&amp;R study is altering the samples during the study. <b>A. altering the samples during the study.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 30 (print p. 155), III.E.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.E","options":["altering the samples during the study.","using parts that cover the range of the gage.","using overtrained inspectors.","using parts that cover the specification range."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":30,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":155,"sourceQuestion":30,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"When conducting an MSA study, a common error made while performing GR&R is:","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"A common error made while performing a gage R&amp;R study is altering the samples during the study. <b>A. altering the samples during the study.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 30 (print p. 155), III.E.</span>"},"revision":"973a6872dfa41188948a14b8375f0938cb6d1ab2e6e51e28f0afbb722735f04a"},"cssgb:set-2:source-310:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"III.E\",\"options\":[\"AIAG methodology\",\"ANOVA analysis\",\"Control chart method\",\"Percent agreement\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":310,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":65,\"sourceQuestion\":84,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What method can be used to assess a measurement system that uses pass/fail classification?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Percent agreement can be used to assess a measurement system that uses pass/fail classification. <b>D. Percent agreement</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 84 (print p. 65), III.E.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"III.E","options":["AIAG methodology","ANOVA analysis","Control chart method","Percent agreement"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":310,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":65,"sourceQuestion":84,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What method can be used to assess a measurement system that uses pass/fail classification?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Percent agreement can be used to assess a measurement system that uses pass/fail classification. <b>D. Percent agreement</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 84 (print p. 65), III.E.</span>"},"revision":"3ce3ceaf716dea50782c47d13bbe249b934b85e7f765f97ea9d303da73f76a6f"},"cssgb:set-2:source-311:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.E\",\"options\":[\"each appraiser versus standard and part variation.\",\"all appraisers versus the standard and between-appraiser agreement.\",\"within-appraiser agreement and part variation.\",\"all appraisers versus standard, and gage R&R.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":311,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":65,\"sourceQuestion\":85,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"An attribute agreement analysis can include output on:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The output for an attribute agreement analysis can include four components: each appraiser versus the standard, between appraisers, all appraisers versus the standard, and within appraiser. <b>B. all appraisers versus the standard and between-appraiser agreement.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 85 (print p. 65), III.E.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.E","options":["each appraiser versus standard and part variation.","all appraisers versus the standard and between-appraiser agreement.","within-appraiser agreement and part variation.","all appraisers versus standard, and gage R&R."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":311,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":65,"sourceQuestion":85,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"An attribute agreement analysis can include output on:","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The output for an attribute agreement analysis can include four components: each appraiser versus the standard, between appraisers, all appraisers versus the standard, and within appraiser. <b>B. all appraisers versus the standard and between-appraiser agreement.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 85 (print p. 65), III.E.</span>"},"revision":"b50dce867c2b2a58c9bee62125f489279df4ca73075d27d2f7e4f97cf746ff91"},"cssgb:set-2:source-312:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.E\",\"options\":[\"1.83\",\"2.49\",\"2.65\",\"3.35\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":312,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":65,\"sourceQuestion\":86,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is the total observed variation when part-to-part variation is 1.60 and gage R&R is 0.89?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Total observed variation combines part-to-part variation (PV) with gage repeatability and reproducibility (GR&amp;R): TV = √(PV² + GR&amp;R²) = √(1.60² + 0.89²) = 1.83. <b>A. 1.83</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 86 (print p. 65), III.E.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.E","options":["1.83","2.49","2.65","3.35"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":312,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":65,"sourceQuestion":86,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is the total observed variation when part-to-part variation is 1.60 and gage R&R is 0.89?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Total observed variation combines part-to-part variation (PV) with gage repeatability and reproducibility (GR&amp;R): TV = √(PV² + GR&amp;R²) = √(1.60² + 0.89²) = 1.83. <b>A. 1.83</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 86 (print p. 65), III.E.</span>"},"revision":"39be15e7d8254c7d91b34c9142da128728ff66869b46d15d58b570247a0435d0"},"cssgb:set-2:source-313:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.E\",\"options\":[\"ensure that the measurement system is performing correctly.\",\"vet the measurement standard against the equipment.\",\"calculate the gage R&R of an instrument.\",\"adjust measurement devices to reduce defect detection.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":313,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":65,\"sourceQuestion\":87,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The purpose of calibration is to:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The purpose of calibration is to ensure that the measurement system is performing correctly. <b>A. ensure that the measurement system is performing correctly.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 87 (print p. 65), III.E.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.E","options":["ensure that the measurement system is performing correctly.","vet the measurement standard against the equipment.","calculate the gage R&R of an instrument.","adjust measurement devices to reduce defect detection."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":313,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":65,"sourceQuestion":87,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The purpose of calibration is to:","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The purpose of calibration is to ensure that the measurement system is performing correctly. <b>A. ensure that the measurement system is performing correctly.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 87 (print p. 65), III.E.</span>"},"revision":"fa217503d0c723c05a3da314c97b67cf496a344ede504bad208e77a9f8ad6a13"},"cssgb:set-2:source-314:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.E\",\"options\":[\"using parts that cover the range of the gage.\",\"not randomizing the samples during measurement.\",\"using overtrained inspectors.\",\"using parts that cover the specification range.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":314,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":66,\"sourceQuestion\":88,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"During a Gage R&R study in which the same sample parts are measured repeatedly, which practice is a common measurement-system-analysis error?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"When conducting an MSA study, a common error made while performing GR&amp;R is not randomizing the samples during measurement. <b>B. not randomizing the samples during measurement.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 88 (print p. 66), III.E.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.E","options":["using parts that cover the range of the gage.","not randomizing the samples during measurement.","using overtrained inspectors.","using parts that cover the specification range."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":314,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":66,"sourceQuestion":88,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"During a Gage R&R study in which the same sample parts are measured repeatedly, which practice is a common measurement-system-analysis error?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"When conducting an MSA study, a common error made while performing GR&amp;R is not randomizing the samples during measurement. <b>B. not randomizing the samples during measurement.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 88 (print p. 66), III.E.</span>"},"revision":"5cc987ca4ee2a15d415266daaaf4c8d7216e1711308b2616d3eb9583fe49a722"},"cssgb:set-2:source-315:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"III.E\",\"options\":[\"Measurand\",\"Calibration\",\"Measurement system error\",\"Measurement correlation\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":315,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":66,\"sourceQuestion\":89,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is the term for measurements taken simultaneously with multiple measurement devices of the same type for parts coming from multiple manufacturing streams?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Measurement correlation is used when measurements are taken simultaneously with multiple measurement devices of the same type for parts coming from multiple streams of manufacturing. <b>D. Measurement correlation</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 89 (print p. 66), III.E.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"III.E","options":["Measurand","Calibration","Measurement system error","Measurement correlation"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":315,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":66,"sourceQuestion":89,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is the term for measurements taken simultaneously with multiple measurement devices of the same type for parts coming from multiple manufacturing streams?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Measurement correlation is used when measurements are taken simultaneously with multiple measurement devices of the same type for parts coming from multiple streams of manufacturing. <b>D. Measurement correlation</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 89 (print p. 66), III.E.</span>"},"revision":"1989af42a076c994a138b8fa9fc63d55b6248b9db736f9375def5ea9e5a6e4a3"},"cssgb:set-2:source-316:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.F.1\",\"options\":[\"out of spec.\",\"capable.\",\"Six Sigma capable.\",\"centered on target.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":316,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":66,\"sourceQuestion\":90,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"When the process variation is significantly smaller than the width of the specification, the process is said to be:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"When the process variation is significantly smaller than the width of the specification, the process is said to be capable. <b>B. capable.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 90 (print p. 66), III.F.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.F.1","options":["out of spec.","capable.","Six Sigma capable.","centered on target."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":316,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":66,"sourceQuestion":90,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"When the process variation is significantly smaller than the width of the specification, the process is said to be:","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"When the process variation is significantly smaller than the width of the specification, the process is said to be capable. <b>B. capable.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 90 (print p. 66), III.F.1.</span>"},"revision":"4b45e593e5e4458dcba39c3c8eb71876e32c7180948177309be7bd6c660a3ee2"},"cssgb:set-2:source-317:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.F.1\",\"options\":[\"±3 σ.\",\"±4 σ.\",\"±5 σ.\",\"±6 σ.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":317,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":66,\"sourceQuestion\":91,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The traditional definition of natural process limits is:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"Traditionally, natural process limits are defined as ±3 σ. For a normal distribution, this width maps to 99.73% of the distribution. <b>A. ±3 σ.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A III. Measure Phase, Question 91 (print p. 66), III.F.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.F.1","options":["±3 σ.","±4 σ.","±5 σ.","±6 σ."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter3.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":317,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":66,"sourceQuestion":91,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The traditional definition of natural process limits is:","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"Traditionally, natural process limits are defined as ±3 σ. F