A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":87,"sourceQuestion":21,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The diagram below shows the critical region for a two-tailed hypothesis test. The total value of the shaded areas is equal to:","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"The value of each shaded area is 0.025 for a two-tailed test, for a total of 0.05. <b>C. 0.05.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 21 (print p. 87), IV.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"27cf96fcea6f8d376ae86cd3bbe04e3effd6133a8626ec96f79914f129b03695"},"cssgb:set-2:source-351:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.1\",\"options\":[\"Increasing sample size\",\"Decreasing alpha level\",\"Increasing variability\",\"Decreasing the difference to detect\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":351,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":88,\"sourceQuestion\":22,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which action can decrease the potential of a Type II error?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"To reduce the potential of a Type II error, we can increase the sample size in our study. <b>A. Increasing sample size</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 22 (print p. 88), IV.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"IV.B.1","options":["Increasing sample size","Decreasing alpha level","Increasing variability","Decreasing the difference to detect"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":351,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":88,"sourceQuestion":22,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which action can decrease the potential of a Type II error?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"To reduce the potential of a Type II error, we can increase the sample size in our study. <b>A. Increasing sample size</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 22 (print p. 88), IV.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"0ea2fc0e3a6dbf5d723d05064a653f65825e1af9f61776826475f302280b82e0"},"cssgb:set-2:source-352:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.1\",\"options\":[\"Type I error increases.\",\"Type II error decreases.\",\"Type I error decreases.\",\"Power decreases.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":352,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":88,\"sourceQuestion\":23,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"In hypothesis testing, when the null hypothesis is false, as the effect size increases:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"If the null hypothesis is false, the alternative hypothesis is true. If the effect size is large, we are more likely to (correctly) reject the null. This means that Type II error decreases as the effect size increases. Note that if the alternative hypothesis is true, the only error we can make is a Type II. The source repeats choice B as choice D; choice D is normalized here to a distinct false distractor. <b>B. Type II error decreases.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 23 (print p. 88), IV.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"IV.B.1","options":["Type I error increases.","Type II error decreases.","Type I error decreases.","Power decreases."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":352,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":88,"sourceQuestion":23,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"In hypothesis testing, when the null hypothesis is false, as the effect size increases:","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"If the null hypothesis is false, the alternative hypothesis is true. If the effect size is large, we are more likely to (correctly) reject the null. This means that Type II error decreases as the effect size increases. Note that if the alternative hypothesis is true, the only error we can make is a Type II. The source repeats choice B as choice D; choice D is normalized here to a distinct false distractor. <b>B. Type II error decreases.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 23 (print p. 88), IV.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"fe3b84df9ab85ff37e2f7c77fbc14aaf30764781da1478c89a62c20d5bf39da8"},"cssgb:set-2:source-353:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.1\",\"options\":[\"power.\",\"significance level.\",\"confidence level.\",\"effect size.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":353,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":88,\"sourceQuestion\":24,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The complement of Type II error is:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"Power is equal to (1−β), so it is the complement of Type II error. <b>A. power.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 24 (print p. 88), IV.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"IV.B.1","options":["power.","significance level.","confidence level.","effect size."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":353,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":88,"sourceQuestion":24,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The complement of Type II error is:","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"Power is equal to (1−β), so it is the complement of Type II error. <b>A. power.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 24 (print p. 88), IV.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"5f927aa78a934e129010d41e1c2d02d97d51f825ce7746861d4eb1e03d9bf544"},"cssgb:set-2:source-354:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.1\",\"options\":[\"increase\",\"decrease\",\"not affect\",\"eliminate\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":354,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":88,\"sourceQuestion\":25,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"When the null hypothesis is true, changing the significance level from 0.05 to 0.01 will ________ the probability of a Type I error.\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"If the null hypothesis is true, the only type of error we can make is a Type I or alpha error. If the significance level is changed from 0.05 to 0.01, more evidence will be required to reject the null. Therefore, the probability of a Type I error will decrease. <b>B. decrease</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 25 (print p. 88), IV.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"IV.B.1","options":["increase","decrease","not affect","eliminate"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":354,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":88,"sourceQuestion":25,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"When the null hypothesis is true, changing the significance level from 0.05 to 0.01 will ________ the probability of a Type I error.","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"If the null hypothesis is true, the only type of error we can make is a Type I or alpha error. If the significance level is changed from 0.05 to 0.01, more evidence will be required to reject the null. Therefore, the probability of a Type I error will decrease. <b>B. decrease</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 25 (print p. 88), IV.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"3ba2297c3006d71d2201a9c972b9ad44e56bbcf25b0e7e6da7006a0c033d54b7"},"cssgb:set-2:source-355:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.1\",\"options\":[\"24\",\"47\",\"236\",\"55,320\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":355,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":88,\"sourceQuestion\":26,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Calculate the sample size needed to assure a margin of error of 10 for a 95% two-sided confidence interval on the mean. The population variance is equal to 1200.\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"We are given E = 10 and σ² = 1200. Let Z = 1.96 (based on a 95% two-sided confidence interval). Then, using the formula for sample size: n = Z²σ²/E² = 1.96²(1200)/10² = 46.10, rounded up to 47. Note that we always round the result up to the next highest integer value. <b>B. 47</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 26 (print p. 88), IV.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"IV.B.1","options":["24","47","236","55,320"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":355,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":88,"sourceQuestion":26,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Calculate the sample size needed to assure a margin of error of 10 for a 95% two-sided confidence interval on the mean. The population variance is equal to 1200.","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"We are given E = 10 and σ² = 1200. Let Z = 1.96 (based on a 95% two-sided confidence interval). Then, using the formula for sample size: n = Z²σ²/E² = 1.96²(1200)/10² = 46.10, rounded up to 47. Note that we always round the result up to the next highest integer value. <b>B. 47</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 26 (print p. 88), IV.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"c4b92027e18bb874d2b3e6fd443e81895dfd1efa61e51fc2537257fa0c0ca24b"},"cssgb:set-2:source-356:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.1\",\"options\":[\"is rejected when it is true.\",\"is not rejected when it is true.\",\"is rejected when it is false.\",\"is not rejected when it is false.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":356,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":89,\"sourceQuestion\":27,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A Type II or beta error occurs when the null hypothesis:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"A Type II (beta) error occurs when the null hypothesis is false but the test fails to reject it. <b>D. is not rejected when it is false.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 27 (print p. 89), IV.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"IV.B.1","options":["is rejected when it is true.","is not rejected when it is true.","is rejected when it is false.","is not rejected when it is false."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":356,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":89,"sourceQuestion":27,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A Type II or beta error occurs when the null hypothesis:","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"A Type II (beta) error occurs when the null hypothesis is false but the test fails to reject it. <b>D. is not rejected when it is false.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 27 (print p. 89), IV.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"19c95c78dfa3d94d60c48882ce0060b585945955f1a6c2129e7edffb4dfdd1cc"},"cssgb:set-2:source-357:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"options\":[\"H₀: μd = 0 vs. H₁: μd ≠ 0\",\"H₀: μ₁ − μ₂ = 0 vs. H₁: μ₁ − μ₂ ≠ 0\",\"H₀: μ₁ − μ₂ ≠ 0 vs. H₁: μ₁ − μ₂ = 0\",\"H₀: x̄₁−x̄₂ = 0 vs. H₁: x̄₁−x̄₂ ≠ 0\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":357,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":89,\"sourceQuestion\":28,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following sets of hypotheses is appropriate to test the difference between two means?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"The null hypothesis contains equality, and hypotheses are expressed in terms of population parameters. To compare two population means, use H₀: μ₁ − μ₂ = 0 versus H₁: μ₁ − μ₂ ≠ 0. <b>B. H₀: μ₁ − μ₂ = 0 vs. H₁: μ₁ − μ₂ ≠ 0</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 28 (print p. 89), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","options":["H₀: μd = 0 vs. H₁: μd ≠ 0","H₀: μ₁ − μ₂ = 0 vs. H₁: μ₁ − μ₂ ≠ 0","H₀: μ₁ − μ₂ ≠ 0 vs. H₁: μ₁ − μ₂ = 0","H₀: x̄₁−x̄₂ = 0 vs. H₁: x̄₁−x̄₂ ≠ 0"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":357,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":89,"sourceQuestion":28,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which of the following sets of hypotheses is appropriate to test the difference between two means?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"The null hypothesis contains equality, and hypotheses are expressed in terms of population parameters. To compare two population means, use H₀: μ₁ − μ₂ = 0 versus H₁: μ₁ − μ₂ ≠ 0. <b>B. H₀: μ₁ − μ₂ = 0 vs. H₁: μ₁ − μ₂ ≠ 0</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 28 (print p. 89), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"eec9ae29677d523a72c957a218a0cf3b0e9eef8c6a90e67bb790d452996b480c"},"cssgb:set-2:source-358:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"options\":[\"the null hypothesis is rejected.\",\"the null hypothesis is accepted.\",\"the null hypothesis is not rejected.\",\"the null hypothesis is significant.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":358,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":89,\"sourceQuestion\":29,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"If the ratio of two sample variances from normal distributions exceeds the critical value of F at a defined level of significance:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"The null hypothesis rejection area is equal to or greater than the critical value of F at a defined significance level, so if the test statistic is greater than the critical value, the null hypothesis is rejected. <b>A. the null hypothesis is rejected.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 29 (print p. 89), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","options":["the null hypothesis is rejected.","the null hypothesis is accepted.","the null hypothesis is not rejected.","the null hypothesis is significant."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":358,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":89,"sourceQuestion":29,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"If the ratio of two sample variances from normal distributions exceeds the critical value of F at a defined level of significance:","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"The null hypothesis rejection area is equal to or greater than the critical value of F at a defined significance level, so if the test statistic is greater than the critical value, the null hypothesis is rejected. <b>A. the null hypothesis is rejected.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 29 (print p. 89), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"380eeb0bb8b3869d57ff8665c6ba83e5d689eb1fe02af3b8f4896d2594bf981e"},"cssgb:set-2:source-359:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"options\":[\"N\",\"n − 1\",\"k − 1\",\"k − 2\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":359,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":89,\"sourceQuestion\":30,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"With n observations from a random sample, each of which could be classified into exactly one of k categories, how many degrees of freedom does a goodness-of-fit test with specified probabilities have?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"For a chi-squared goodness-of-fit test, there will be k–1 degrees of freedom, where k is the number of categories. <b>C. k − 1</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 30 (print p. 89), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","options":["N","n − 1","k − 1","k − 2"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":359,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":89,"sourceQuestion":30,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"With n observations from a random sample, each of which could be classified into exactly one of k categories, how many degrees of freedom does a goodness-of-fit test with specified probabilities have?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"For a chi-squared goodness-of-fit test, there will be k–1 degrees of freedom, where k is the number of categories. <b>C. k − 1</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 30 (print p. 89), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"a5e031cde5a5fc30f5b0ebc98c21d9f2ff8ee67ea4f735f579ecfda4fb602128"},"cssgb:set-2:source-35:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"VI.C.2\",\"options\":[\"spreadsheets filled with numbers.\",\"pictures, colors, shapes.\",\"written procedures.\",\"instructions from supervisors.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":35,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":156,\"sourceQuestion\":35,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"A visual workplace supports visual devices that are easily understood and accessed by all through the use of:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"Visual devices do not require detailed text or content but rely on pictures, colors, and shapes. <b>B. pictures, colors, shapes.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 35 (print p. 156), VI.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"VI.C.2","options":["spreadsheets filled with numbers.","pictures, colors, shapes.","written procedures.","instructions from supervisors."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":35,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":156,"sourceQuestion":35,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"A visual workplace supports visual devices that are easily understood and accessed by all through the use of:","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"Visual devices do not require detailed text or content but rely on pictures, colors, and shapes. <b>B. pictures, colors, shapes.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 35 (print p. 156), VI.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"5c0227c593aff8e16ba72155114621ef20a7b9ba6de39d48c94ca8a6873552fa"},"cssgb:set-2:source-360:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.1\",\"options\":[\"false claim.\",\"result being statistically insignificant.\",\"result being statistically insignificant but practically significant.\",\"result being statistically significant but not practically significant.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":360,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":89,\"sourceQuestion\":31,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A training company has shown that its study program results in a significantly higher mean score on a 100-point certification exam than those who did not use the program. Further investigation shows that the mean score for those who used the training program was 80.4, compared to 80.2 for those who did not. This is an example of a:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"This is an example of a result that is statistically significant but not practically significant. <b>D. result being statistically significant but not practically significant.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 31 (print p. 89), IV.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"IV.B.1","options":["false claim.","result being statistically insignificant.","result being statistically insignificant but practically significant.","result being statistically significant but not practically significant."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":360,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":89,"sourceQuestion":31,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A training company has shown that its study program results in a significantly higher mean score on a 100-point certification exam than those who did not use the program. Further investigation shows that the mean score for those who used the training program was 80.4, compared to 80.2 for those who did not. This is an example of a:","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"This is an example of a result that is statistically significant but not practically significant. <b>D. result being statistically significant but not practically significant.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 31 (print p. 89), IV.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"752b175c31f268f3f1a4e90b119ab2db0394b9387899c38ca46ba2543752dd3f"},"cssgb:set-2:source-361:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.1\",\"options\":[\"t₀.₀₀₅,₉ = 3.250\",\"t₀.₀₀₅, ₈ = 3.355\",\"t₀.₀₁, ₉ = 2.821\",\"t₀.₀₁, ₈ = 2.896.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":361,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":90,\"sourceQuestion\":32,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A two-sided hypothesis test on two means is being conducted. Each sample comes from a symmetric distribution, and the sample size of each group is equal to 5. The variances of the groups are assumed to be equal. What is the critical value for the test if α = 0.01?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"The degrees of freedom are equal to 5 + 5–2 = 8. Since the test is two-sided, we use α/2 = 0.01/2 = 0.005. From the Student’s t table, the critical value for t(0.005, 8) = 3.355. <b>B. t₀.₀₀₅, ₈ = 3.355</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 32 (print p. 90), IV.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"IV.B.1","options":["t₀.₀₀₅,₉ = 3.250","t₀.₀₀₅, ₈ = 3.355","t₀.₀₁, ₉ = 2.821","t₀.₀₁, ₈ = 2.896."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":361,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":90,"sourceQuestion":32,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A two-sided hypothesis test on two means is being conducted. Each sample comes from a symmetric distribution, and the sample size of each group is equal to 5. The variances of the groups are assumed to be equal. What is the critical value for the test if α = 0.01?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"The degrees of freedom are equal to 5 + 5–2 = 8. Since the test is two-sided, we use α/2 = 0.01/2 = 0.005. From the Student’s t table, the critical value for t(0.005, 8) = 3.355. <b>B. t₀.₀₀₅, ₈ = 3.355</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 32 (print p. 90), IV.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"83b5cc528d92c382243b046eecff8623569cbc904702920fb42d5aa54cc84903"},"cssgb:set-2:source-362:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"options\":[\"z₀ = 2.89; reject H₀.\",\"z₀ = 2.89; do not reject H₀.\",\"t₀ = 2.89; reject H₀.\",\"t₀ = 2.89; do not reject H₀.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":362,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":90,\"sourceQuestion\":33,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A new electric vehicle is tested for its maximum range under a single charge. Four vehicles were tested on a track with the following mileage results: {260, 252, 254, 254}. Assuming mileage is normally distributed, test whether the average range is at least 250 miles using an alpha = 0.05.\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"We set up the one-sided hypotheses for the mean: H₀: μ = 250 H₁: μ &gt; 250 Because we do not know the population standard deviation, we will use the Student’s t distribution for this test. x̄ = 255, s = 3.46, and t₀ = (255−250)/(3.46/√4) = 2.89. We will reject H₀ if t₀ &gt; t(0.05, 3) = 2.353. Since 2.89 &gt; 2.353, we reject H₀ and conclude that the average mileage is greater than 250. <b>C. t₀ = 2.89; reject H₀.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 33 (print p. 90), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","options":["z₀ = 2.89; reject H₀.","z₀ = 2.89; do not reject H₀.","t₀ = 2.89; reject H₀.","t₀ = 2.89; do not reject H₀."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":362,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":90,"sourceQuestion":33,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A new electric vehicle is tested for its maximum range under a single charge. Four vehicles were tested on a track with the following mileage results: {260, 252, 254, 254}. Assuming mileage is normally distributed, test whether the average range is at least 250 miles using an alpha = 0.05.","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"We set up the one-sided hypotheses for the mean: H₀: μ = 250 H₁: μ &gt; 250 Because we do not know the population standard deviation, we will use the Student’s t distribution for this test. x̄ = 255, s = 3.46, and t₀ = (255−250)/(3.46/√4) = 2.89. We will reject H₀ if t₀ &gt; t(0.05, 3) = 2.353. Since 2.89 &gt; 2.353, we reject H₀ and conclude that the average mileage is greater than 250. <b>C. t₀ = 2.89; reject H₀.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 33 (print p. 90), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"ce6de97389f13064283f60659d37f888272ffd0bc6b5433250dbcede851d592f"},"cssgb:set-2:source-363:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"options\":[\"Z₀ = –1.93, the defective rate has not significantly improved.\",\"Z₀ = –1.93, the defective rate has significantly improved.\",\"t₀ = –1.93, the defective rate has not significantly improved.\",\"t₀ = –1.93, the defective rate has significantly improved.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":363,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":90,\"sourceQuestion\":34,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A process is running at a 5.0% defective rate. In a pilot test of a potential improvement, 225 items were processed with 5 defectives. Has the improvement significantly improved the defective rate? Test at α = 0.05.\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"This is a one-sided test since we are trying to prove that the improvement decreases the defect rate. The hypotheses we are testing are: H₀: p = 0.05 H₁: p &lt; 0.05 We note that n = 225, p₀ = 0.05, and p̂ = 0.022 and use the normal approximation method: z₀ = (0.022−0.05)/√[0.05(1−0.05)/225] = −1.93. We reject H₀ if z₀ &lt;–z₀.₀₅. From the table, we find –z₀.₀₅ =–1.645. Since –1.93 is less than –1.645, we reject H₀. The improvement has reduced the rate of defects. <b>B. Z₀ = –1.93, the defective rate has significantly improved.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 34 (print p. 90), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","options":["Z₀ = –1.93, the defective rate has not significantly improved.","Z₀ = –1.93, the defective rate has significantly improved.","t₀ = –1.93, the defective rate has not significantly improved.","t₀ = –1.93, the defective rate has significantly improved."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":363,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":90,"sourceQuestion":34,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A process is running at a 5.0% defective rate. In a pilot test of a potential improvement, 225 items were processed with 5 defectives. Has the improvement significantly improved the defective rate? Test at α = 0.05.","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"This is a one-sided test since we are trying to prove that the improvement decreases the defect rate. The hypotheses we are testing are: H₀: p = 0.05 H₁: p &lt; 0.05 We note that n = 225, p₀ = 0.05, and p̂ = 0.022 and use the normal approximation method: z₀ = (0.022−0.05)/√[0.05(1−0.05)/225] = −1.93. We reject H₀ if z₀ &lt;–z₀.₀₅. From the table, we find –z₀.₀₅ =–1.645. Since –1.93 is less than –1.645, we reject H₀. The improvement has reduced the rate of defects. <b>B. Z₀ = –1.93, the defective rate has significantly improved.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 34 (print p. 90), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"8acbc552b98795ecf4d92d7260b3dd1964566f510ded78ffef94e1b00a3ed6af"},"cssgb:set-2:source-364:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"options\":[\"79.88 ≤ μ ≤ 80.12.\",\"79.02 ≤ μ ≤ 80.98.\",\"78.04 ≤ μ ≤ 81.96.\",\"76.08 ≤ μ ≤ 83.92.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":364,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":90,\"sourceQuestion\":35,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"When calculating the 95 percent confidence interval for the mean of a sample of 256 customers, the average order is $80, and the population standard deviation is $16, so the range of the interval is:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"The sample mean is 80, the population standard deviation is equal to 16, and n = 256. Using the confidence interval formula for the mean with a known variance: 80 ± 1.96(16/√256) = [78.04, 81.96]. <b>C. 78.04 ≤ μ ≤ 81.96.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 35 (print p. 90), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","options":["79.88 ≤ μ ≤ 80.12.","79.02 ≤ μ ≤ 80.98.","78.04 ≤ μ ≤ 81.96.","76.08 ≤ μ ≤ 83.92."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":364,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":90,"sourceQuestion":35,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"When calculating the 95 percent confidence interval for the mean of a sample of 256 customers, the average order is $80, and the population standard deviation is $16, so the range of the interval is:","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"The sample mean is 80, the population standard deviation is equal to 16, and n = 256. Using the confidence interval formula for the mean with a known variance: 80 ± 1.96(16/√256) = [78.04, 81.96]. <b>C. 78.04 ≤ μ ≤ 81.96.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 35 (print p. 90), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"55b697d045fb73e8846985098b90293b3ec59fa3ebfd299ecade9a6105eff0a1"},"cssgb:set-2:source-365:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"options\":[\"Student’s t-test\",\"Fisher’s exact test\",\"Chi-squared test\",\"ANOVA\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":365,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":91,\"sourceQuestion\":36,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"To compare the means of three or more groups, which of the following tests is the most appropriate?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"To compare the means of three or more groups, we use analysis of variance (ANOVA). <b>D. ANOVA</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 36 (print p. 91), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","options":["Student’s t-test","Fisher’s exact test","Chi-squared test","ANOVA"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":365,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":91,"sourceQuestion":36,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"To compare the means of three or more groups, which of the following tests is the most appropriate?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"To compare the means of three or more groups, we use analysis of variance (ANOVA). <b>D. ANOVA</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 36 (print p. 91), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"a8aac225e339caf992a0c5b33984fc20c30b362bbe714d39ebac399bc0f75916"},"cssgb:set-2:source-366:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"options\":[\"Chi-square\",\"Poisson\",\"Binomial\",\"Standard normal\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":366,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":91,\"sourceQuestion\":37,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A confidence interval on the standard deviation of a normal distribution uses critical values from which distribution?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"The chi-square distribution is used to test a standard deviation or variance from a normal distribution. <b>A. Chi-square</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 37 (print p. 91), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","options":["Chi-square","Poisson","Binomial","Standard normal"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":366,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":91,"sourceQuestion":37,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A confidence interval on the standard deviation of a normal distribution uses critical values from which distribution?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"The chi-square distribution is used to test a standard deviation or variance from a normal distribution. <b>A. Chi-square</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 37 (print p. 91), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"bb8f0a857db0f6d113d40066b7d89a6c6a4be9001eb2b0ece77ef8b29a850d96"},"cssgb:set-2:source-367:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"options\":[\"0.33 ± 1.645(0.047).\",\"0.33 ± 1.645(0.0022).\",\"0.33 ± 1.96(0.047).\",\"0.33 ± 1.96(0.0022).\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":367,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":91,\"sourceQuestion\":38,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"When calculating a 90 percent confidence interval for a proportion with a sample size of 100 and a sample proportion of 0.33, the interval is calculated using:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"For a two-sided 90% confidence interval, z* = 1.645. The estimated standard error is √[0.33(1−0.33)/100] ≈ 0.047, so the interval is calculated as 0.33 ± 1.645(0.047). <b>A. 0.33 ± 1.645(0.047).</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 38 (print p. 91), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","options":["0.33 ± 1.645(0.047).","0.33 ± 1.645(0.0022).","0.33 ± 1.96(0.047).","0.33 ± 1.96(0.0022)."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":367,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":91,"sourceQuestion":38,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"When calculating a 90 percent confidence interval for a proportion with a sample size of 100 and a sample proportion of 0.33, the interval is calculated using:","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"For a two-sided 90% confidence interval, z* = 1.645. The estimated standard error is √[0.33(1−0.33)/100] ≈ 0.047, so the interval is calculated as 0.33 ± 1.645(0.047). <b>A. 0.33 ± 1.645(0.047).</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 38 (print p. 91), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"f25289d4bcec2e9671f06e09c88fca92b7e7ff32922aaaacb06772def0fa32ee"},"cssgb:set-2:source-368:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Machine\",\"n\",\"s²\"],\"rows\":[[\"1\",\"20\",\"4.55\"],[\"2\",\"15\",\"1.55\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"F₀ = 2.94, Fcrit = 1.92, Reject H₀.\",\"F₀ = 2.94, Fcrit = 1.97, Reject H₀.\",\"F₀ = 2.94, Fcrit = 2.33, Reject H₀.\",\"F₀ = 2.94, Fcrit = 2.40, Reject H₀.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":368,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":91,\"sourceQuestion\":39,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A Six Sigma Green Belt is studying the output of two milling machines. Assuming the output of each machine follows a normal distribution, test whether there is a significant difference in the variances of the two machines at α = 0.10. Use the information in the following table.\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"The wording asks whether the variances differ, so this is a two-sided F test: H₀: σ₁² = σ₂² versus H₁: σ₁² ≠ σ₂². F₀ = 4.55/1.55 = 2.94. With df₁ = 19, df₂ = 14, and α/2 = 0.05 in the upper tail, Fcrit ≈ 2.40, so reject H₀. The book uses the one-sided α = 0.10 value 1.97; the student-facing key is corrected to match the two-sided question. <b>D. F₀ = 2.94, Fcrit = 2.40, Reject H₀.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 39 (print p. 91), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","chart":{"columns":["Machine","n","s²"],"rows":[["1","20","4.55"],["2","15","1.55"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["F₀ = 2.94, Fcrit = 1.92, Reject H₀.","F₀ = 2.94, Fcrit = 1.97, Reject H₀.","F₀ = 2.94, Fcrit = 2.33, Reject H₀.","F₀ = 2.94, Fcrit = 2.40, Reject H₀."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":368,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":91,"sourceQuestion":39,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A Six Sigma Green Belt is studying the output of two milling machines. Assuming the output of each machine follows a normal distribution, test whether there is a significant difference in the variances of the two machines at α = 0.10. Use the information in the following table.","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"The wording asks whether the variances differ, so this is a two-sided F test: H₀: σ₁² = σ₂² versus H₁: σ₁² ≠ σ₂². F₀ = 4.55/1.55 = 2.94. With df₁ = 19, df₂ = 14, and α/2 = 0.05 in the upper tail, Fcrit ≈ 2.40, so reject H₀. The book uses the one-sided α = 0.10 value 1.97; the student-facing key is corrected to match the two-sided question. <b>D. F₀ = 2.94, Fcrit = 2.40, Reject H₀.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 39 (print p. 91), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"eedea212b0ae5beff856b88aadb8e0354aa64df517f89d9bdff9a0bc9bc4679c"},"cssgb:set-2:source-369:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Participant\",\"Pre-test\",\"Post-test\"],\"rows\":[[\"1\",\"55\",\"80\"],[\"2\",\"67\",\"70\"],[\"3\",\"80\",\"90\"],[\"4\",\"79\",\"83\"],[\"5\",\"84\",\"88\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"H₀: μ₁ = μ₂ vs. H₁: μ₁ ≠ μ₂\",\"H₀: μ₁ − μ₂ = 0 vs. H₁: μ₁ − μ₂ < 0\",\"H₀: μD = 0 vs. H₁: μD ≠ 0\",\"H₀: μD = 0 vs. H₁: μD < 0\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":369,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":92,\"sourceQuestion\":40,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Participants took the same test before and after training; define each paired difference as pre-test minus post-test. The instructor wants to determine whether training improved scores. What is the appropriate set of hypotheses for the test?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"Because each paired difference is defined as pre-test minus post-test, improvement produces negative differences. Use the paired hypotheses H₀: μD = 0 versus H₁: μD &lt; 0. <b>D. H₀: μD = 0 vs. H₁: μD &lt; 0</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 40 (print p. 92), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","chart":{"columns":["Participant","Pre-test","Post-test"],"rows":[["1","55","80"],["2","67","70"],["3","80","90"],["4","79","83"],["5","84","88"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["H₀: μ₁ = μ₂ vs. H₁: μ₁ ≠ μ₂","H₀: μ₁ − μ₂ = 0 vs. H₁: μ₁ − μ₂ < 0","H₀: μD = 0 vs. H₁: μD ≠ 0","H₀: μD = 0 vs. H₁: μD < 0"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":369,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":92,"sourceQuestion":40,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Participants took the same test before and after training; define each paired difference as pre-test minus post-test. The instructor wants to determine whether training improved scores. What is the appropriate set of hypotheses for the test?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"Because each paired difference is defined as pre-test minus post-test, improvement produces negative differences. Use the paired hypotheses H₀: μD = 0 versus H₁: μD &lt; 0. <b>D. H₀: μD = 0 vs. H₁: μD &lt; 0</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 40 (print p. 92), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"4d626e3dcab882e0e066bdb57db4c895040281440f12d36a4e959aff049ef1e8"},"cssgb:set-2:source-36:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Source\",\"SS\",\"DF\",\"MS\",\"F\"],\"rows\":[[\"Machines\",\"456.7\",\"4\",\"\",\"\"],[\"Error\",\"123.8\",\"24\",\"\",\"\"],[\"Total\",\"580.5\",\"28\",\"\",\"\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"0.61\",\"0.79\",\"3.69\",\"22.13\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":36,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":156,\"sourceQuestion\":36,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"What is the value of the F statistic in the table?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"MS(machine) = 456.7/4 = 114.175 and MS(error) = 123.8/24 = 5.1583. Therefore F = 114.175/5.1583 = 22.13. <b>D. 22.13</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 36 (print p. 156), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","chart":{"columns":["Source","SS","DF","MS","F"],"rows":[["Machines","456.7","4","",""],["Error","123.8","24","",""],["Total","580.5","28","",""]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["0.61","0.79","3.69","22.13"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":36,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":156,"sourceQuestion":36,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"What is the value of the F statistic in the table?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"MS(machine) = 456.7/4 = 114.175 and MS(error) = 123.8/24 = 5.1583. Therefore F = 114.175/5.1583 = 22.13. <b>D. 22.13</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 36 (print p. 156), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"48d53e82e82069ec5b8cc8dea1214ca0ce63870969ca3747a770780d778b46d4"},"cssgb:set-2:source-370:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Participant\",\"Pre-test\",\"Post-test\"],\"rows\":[[\"1\",\"55\",\"80\"],[\"2\",\"67\",\"70\"],[\"3\",\"80\",\"90\"],[\"4\",\"79\",\"83\"],[\"5\",\"84\",\"88\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"t₀ = −0.99\",\"t₀ = −1.44\",\"t₀ = −1.99\",\"t₀ = −2.22\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":370,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":92,\"sourceQuestion\":41,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Participants took the same test before and after training; define each difference as pre-test minus post-test. What is the value of the test statistic?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"We first calculate the mean difference and the standard deviation of the differences using the table: Participant Pre-test Post-test Difference 1 55 80 −25 2 67 70 −3 3 80 90 −10 4 79 83 −4 5 84 88 −4 Mean difference −9.2 Std deviation 9.257 Then we calculate: t₀ = (−9.2−0)/(9.26/√5) = −2.22. <b>D. t₀ = −2.22</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 41 (print p. 92), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","chart":{"columns":["Participant","Pre-test","Post-test"],"rows":[["1","55","80"],["2","67","70"],["3","80","90"],["4","79","83"],["5","84","88"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["t₀ = −0.99","t₀ = −1.44","t₀ = −1.99","t₀ = −2.22"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":370,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":92,"sourceQuestion":41,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Participants took the same test before and after training; define each difference as pre-test minus post-test. What is the value of the test statistic?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"We first calculate the mean difference and the standard deviation of the differences using the table: Participant Pre-test Post-test Difference 1 55 80 −25 2 67 70 −3 3 80 90 −10 4 79 83 −4 5 84 88 −4 Mean difference −9.2 Std deviation 9.257 Then we calculate: t₀ = (−9.2−0)/(9.26/√5) = −2.22. <b>D. t₀ = −2.22</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 41 (print p. 92), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"d8ff897a615036ca62679278e5f9a7afd37bb4f6f3f73d7d0d33d30ef464a44a"},"cssgb:set-2:source-371:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Participant\",\"Pre-test\",\"Post-test\"],\"rows\":[[\"1\",\"55\",\"80\"],[\"2\",\"67\",\"70\"],[\"3\",\"80\",\"90\"],[\"4\",\"79\",\"83\"],[\"5\",\"84\",\"88\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"t = 1.812\",\"Z = 1.860\",\"Z= 2.015\",\"t = −2.132\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":371,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":92,\"sourceQuestion\":42,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Participants took the same test before and after training; define each paired difference as pre-test minus post-test. The alternative is that training improves scores. What is the critical value for the test, assuming α = 0.05?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"There are 5 paired differences, so df = 4. Because differences are defined as pre-test minus post-test, improvement is a left-tailed alternative. For α = 0.05, the critical value is −2.132; the observed t₀ = −2.22 falls below it, so reject H₀. <b>D. t = −2.132</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 42 (print p. 92), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","chart":{"columns":["Participant","Pre-test","Post-test"],"rows":[["1","55","80"],["2","67","70"],["3","80","90"],["4","79","83"],["5","84","88"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["t = 1.812","Z = 1.860","Z= 2.015","t = −2.132"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":371,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":92,"sourceQuestion":42,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Participants took the same test before and after training; define each paired difference as pre-test minus post-test. The alternative is that training improves scores. What is the critical value for the test, assuming α = 0.05?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"There are 5 paired differences, so df = 4. Because differences are defined as pre-test minus post-test, improvement is a left-tailed alternative. For α = 0.05, the critical value is −2.132; the observed t₀ = −2.22 falls below it, so reject H₀. <b>D. t = −2.132</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 42 (print p. 92), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"44d6c0e8726b42218f0893df70682054ed88b6dfb78d83624da178d12537af65"},"cssgb:set-2:source-372:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Source\",\"SS\",\"df\",\"MS\",\"F\"],\"rows\":[[\"Supplier\",\"360\",\"4\",\"\",\"\"],[\"Error\",\"400\",\"20\",\"\",\"\"],[\"Total\",\"760\",\"23\",\"\",\"\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"H₀: µ₁ = µ₂ = µ₃ = µ₄ vs. H₁: µ₁ ≠ µ₂ ≠ µ₃ ≠ µ₄\",\"H₀: µ₁ = µ₂ = µ₃ = µ₄ vs. H₁: At least one mean is not equal to the rest.\",\"H₀: µ₁ = µ₂ = µ₃ = µ₄ = µ₅ vs. H₁: µ₁ ≠ µ₂ ≠ µ₃ ≠ µ₄ ≠ µ₅\",\"H₀: µ₁ = µ₂ = µ₃ = µ₄ = µ₅ vs. H₁: At least one mean is not equal to the rest.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":372,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":93,\"sourceQuestion\":43,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A Green Belt is comparing five suppliers using the ANOVA table shown. Which set of hypotheses is correct for the analysis?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"Since the degrees of freedom for Supplier are equal to 4, we know we have 5 means that we are comparing. The alternative hypothesis for ANOVA is that at least one mean is not equal to the rest. <b>D. H₀: µ₁ = µ₂ = µ₃ = µ₄ = µ₅ vs. H₁: At least one mean is not equal to the rest.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 43 (print p. 93), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","chart":{"columns":["Source","SS","df","MS","F"],"rows":[["Supplier","360","4","",""],["Error","400","20","",""],["Total","760","23","",""]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["H₀: µ₁ = µ₂ = µ₃ = µ₄ vs. H₁: µ₁ ≠ µ₂ ≠ µ₃ ≠ µ₄","H₀: µ₁ = µ₂ = µ₃ = µ₄ vs. H₁: At least one mean is not equal to the rest.","H₀: µ₁ = µ₂ = µ₃ = µ₄ = µ₅ vs. H₁: µ₁ ≠ µ₂ ≠ µ₃ ≠ µ₄ ≠ µ₅","H₀: µ₁ = µ₂ = µ₃ = µ₄ = µ₅ vs. H₁: At least one mean is not equal to the rest."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":372,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":93,"sourceQuestion":43,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A Green Belt is comparing five suppliers using the ANOVA table shown. Which set of hypotheses is correct for the analysis?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"Since the degrees of freedom for Supplier are equal to 4, we know we have 5 means that we are comparing. The alternative hypothesis for ANOVA is that at least one mean is not equal to the rest. <b>D. H₀: µ₁ = µ₂ = µ₃ = µ₄ = µ₅ vs. H₁: At least one mean is not equal to the rest.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 43 (print p. 93), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"c5da766987882c72d84a2543e85102cddc4abcf460d3139e0e692470e9090a55"},"cssgb:set-2:source-373:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Source\",\"SS\",\"df\",\"MS\",\"F\"],\"rows\":[[\"Supplier\",\"360\",\"4\",\"\",\"\"],[\"Error\",\"400\",\"20\",\"\",\"\"],[\"Total\",\"760\",\"23\",\"\",\"\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"0.22\",\"0.90\",\"4.50\",\"9.00\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":373,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":93,\"sourceQuestion\":44,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A Green Belt is comparing five suppliers using the ANOVA table shown. What is the calculated value of the F statistic?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"From the displayed ANOVA table, MSsupplier = 360/4 = 90 and MSerror = 400/20 = 20. The F statistic is 90/20 = 4.50. <b>C. 4.50</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 44 (print p. 93), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","chart":{"columns":["Source","SS","df","MS","F"],"rows":[["Supplier","360","4","",""],["Error","400","20","",""],["Total","760","23","",""]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["0.22","0.90","4.50","9.00"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":373,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":93,"sourceQuestion":44,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A Green Belt is comparing five suppliers using the ANOVA table shown. What is the calculated value of the F statistic?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"From the displayed ANOVA table, MSsupplier = 360/4 = 90 and MSerror = 400/20 = 20. The F statistic is 90/20 = 4.50. <b>C. 4.50</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 44 (print p. 93), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"81503703aeff94b020ade9eded3bb339160e8c609f6fc60871b9253bfa18acb1"},"cssgb:set-2:source-374:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Source\",\"SS\",\"df\",\"MS\",\"F\"],\"rows\":[[\"Supplier\",\"360\",\"4\",\"\",\"\"],[\"Error\",\"400\",\"20\",\"\",\"\"],[\"Total\",\"760\",\"23\",\"\",\"\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"2.25\",\"3.8\",\"4.43\",\"14.02\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":374,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":93,\"sourceQuestion\":45,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A Green Belt is comparing five suppliers using the ANOVA table shown. What is the critical value from the F table if α = 0.01?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"The critical value F(4, 20, 0.01) = 4.43 can be found using the statistical tables. <b>C. 4.43</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 45 (print p. 93), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","chart":{"columns":["Source","SS","df","MS","F"],"rows":[["Supplier","360","4","",""],["Error","400","20","",""],["Total","760","23","",""]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["2.25","3.8","4.43","14.02"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":374,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":93,"sourceQuestion":45,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A Green Belt is comparing five suppliers using the ANOVA table shown. What is the critical value from the F table if α = 0.01?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"The critical value F(4, 20, 0.01) = 4.43 can be found using the statistical tables. <b>C. 4.43</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 45 (print p. 93), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"7510f2ee325d99cf1fc25d8095b9bb61ed7f22c683add1c55fb70e1af4ea6602"},"cssgb:set-2:source-375:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Source\",\"SS\",\"df\",\"MS\",\"F\"],\"rows\":[[\"Supplier\",\"360\",\"4\",\"\",\"\"],[\"Error\",\"400\",\"20\",\"\",\"\"],[\"Total\",\"760\",\"23\",\"\",\"\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"Fcalc > Fcrit, Reject H₀.\",\"Fcalc < Fcrit, Reject H₀.\",\"Fcalc > Fcrit, Do not reject H₀.\",\"Fcalc < Fcrit, Do not reject H₀.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":375,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":93,\"sourceQuestion\":46,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A Green Belt is comparing five suppliers using the ANOVA table shown. What is the conclusion for the statistical test?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"The calculated F = 4.50 is greater than the critical value F(4, 20, 0.01) = 4.43. Therefore Fcalc &gt; Fcrit, so reject H₀ and conclude that at least one supplier mean differs. <b>A. Fcalc &gt; Fcrit, Reject H₀.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 46 (print p. 93), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","chart":{"columns":["Source","SS","df","MS","F"],"rows":[["Supplier","360","4","",""],["Error","400","20","",""],["Total","760","23","",""]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["Fcalc > Fcrit, Reject H₀.","Fcalc < Fcrit, Reject H₀.","Fcalc > Fcrit, Do not reject H₀.","Fcalc < Fcrit, Do not reject H₀."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":375,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":93,"sourceQuestion":46,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A Green Belt is comparing five suppliers using the ANOVA table shown. What is the conclusion for the statistical test?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"The calculated F = 4.50 is greater than the critical value F(4, 20, 0.01) = 4.43. Therefore Fcalc &gt; Fcrit, so reject H₀ and conclude that at least one supplier mean differs. <b>A. Fcalc &gt; Fcrit, Reject H₀.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 46 (print p. 93), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"1522b4091f04b1652afc047a39e80f2f7c0fcb8fd96be6fe1c3fd22167f28279"},"cssgb:set-2:source-376:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Defect\",\"Historical probability\",\"Observed\",\"Expected\"],\"rows\":[[\"Paint run\",\"0.16\",\"27\",\"33.28\"],[\"Paint blister\",\"0.28\",\"60\",\"58.24\"],[\"Decal crooked\",\"0.42\",\"100\",\"87.36\"],[\"Door cracked\",\"0.14\",\"21\",\"29.12\"],[\"Total\",\"\",\"208\",\"208.00\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"−0.29\",\"0.46\",\"5.33\",\"6.25\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":376,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":94,\"sourceQuestion\":47,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A Green Belt is testing whether current defect frequencies differ from the historical probabilities shown. What is the calculated value of the chi-square statistic?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"To calculate the chi-square statistic, we use: χ² = Σ[(Oᵢ−Eᵢ)²/Eᵢ] = 5.331. <b>C. 5.33</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 47 (print p. 94), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","chart":{"columns":["Defect","Historical probability","Observed","Expected"],"rows":[["Paint run","0.16","27","33.28"],["Paint blister","0.28","60","58.24"],["Decal crooked","0.42","100","87.36"],["Door cracked","0.14","21","29.12"],["Total","","208","208.00"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["−0.29","0.46","5.33","6.25"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":376,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":94,"sourceQuestion":47,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A Green Belt is testing whether current defect frequencies differ from the historical probabilities shown. What is the calculated value of the chi-square statistic?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"To calculate the chi-square statistic, we use: χ² = Σ[(Oᵢ−Eᵢ)²/Eᵢ] = 5.331. <b>C. 5.33</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 47 (print p. 94), IV.B.2.</span>"},"r