"A Green Belt is testing whether current defect frequencies differ from the historical probabilities shown. What is the critical value of the chi-square goodness of fit test at α = 0.05?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"With four categories, df = k − 1 = 3. A chi-square goodness-of-fit test is right-tailed, and the α = 0.05 critical value is 7.815. <b>B. 7.815</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 48 (print p. 94), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"e39a038106235a1396d0f2be170523b921e1266e08f713863ad4c63361b8c41b"},"cssgb:set-2:source-378:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Defect\",\"Historical probability\",\"Observed\",\"Expected\"],\"rows\":[[\"Paint run\",\"0.16\",\"27\",\"33.28\"],[\"Paint blister\",\"0.28\",\"60\",\"58.24\"],[\"Decal crooked\",\"0.42\",\"100\",\"87.36\"],[\"Door cracked\",\"0.14\",\"21\",\"29.12\"],[\"Total\",\"\",\"208\",\"208.00\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"Reject H₀, defect percentages have changed.\",\"Do not reject H₀, defect percentages have not changed.\",\"Reject H₀, defect percentages have not changed.\",\"Accept H₀, defect percentages have not changed.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":378,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":94,\"sourceQuestion\":49,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A Green Belt is testing whether current defect frequencies differ from the historical probabilities shown. What conclusion can be stated, assuming α = 0.05?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"Since the calculated chi-square value of 5.33 is not greater than the critical value of 7.815, we do not reject H₀. There is insufficient evidence to claim that the percentages have changed. <b>B. Do not reject H₀, defect percentages have not changed.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 49 (print p. 94), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","chart":{"columns":["Defect","Historical probability","Observed","Expected"],"rows":[["Paint run","0.16","27","33.28"],["Paint blister","0.28","60","58.24"],["Decal crooked","0.42","100","87.36"],["Door cracked","0.14","21","29.12"],["Total","","208","208.00"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["Reject H₀, defect percentages have changed.","Do not reject H₀, defect percentages have not changed.","Reject H₀, defect percentages have not changed.","Accept H₀, defect percentages have not changed."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":378,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":94,"sourceQuestion":49,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A Green Belt is testing whether current defect frequencies differ from the historical probabilities shown. What conclusion can be stated, assuming α = 0.05?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"Since the calculated chi-square value of 5.33 is not greater than the critical value of 7.815, we do not reject H₀. There is insufficient evidence to claim that the percentages have changed. <b>B. Do not reject H₀, defect percentages have not changed.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 49 (print p. 94), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"d616f977114348d650e4cb0a3664a8692d89d03c6be8ab4412da689c451cca1a"},"cssgb:set-2:source-379:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"options\":[\"association between two classifications.\",\"difference in population means.\",\"difference in population variances.\",\"difference in the observed frequency compared to historical proportions.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":379,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":94,\"sourceQuestion\":50,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Contingency tables use the chi-square procedure to test whether there is a(n):\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"A contingency table shows two or more categorical variables with counts in each cell. The statistical test associated with a contingency table is the chi-square procedure. We test whether there is an association between the two categorical variables. For example, we could study whether defect types are associated with shift. <b>A. association between two classifications.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 50 (print p. 94), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","options":["association between two classifications.","difference in population means.","difference in population variances.","difference in the observed frequency compared to historical proportions."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":379,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":94,"sourceQuestion":50,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Contingency tables use the chi-square procedure to test whether there is a(n):","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"A contingency table shows two or more categorical variables with counts in each cell. The statistical test associated with a contingency table is the chi-square procedure. We test whether there is an association between the two categorical variables. For example, we could study whether defect types are associated with shift. <b>A. association between two classifications.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 50 (print p. 94), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"23199eff285a8a322f4d13961b6ca250b1cd31f4c6f45255f914dd3aae759641"},"cssgb:set-2:source-37:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Source\",\"SS\",\"DF\",\"MS\",\"F\"],\"rows\":[[\"Machines\",\"456.7\",\"4\",\"\",\"\"],[\"Error\",\"123.8\",\"24\",\"\",\"\"],[\"Total\",\"580.5\",\"28\",\"\",\"\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"FCalc > F(24, 4, 0.05), Reject H0.\",\"FCalc < F(4, 24, 0.95), Accept H0.\",\"FCalc > F(4, 24, 0.05), Reject H0.\",\"FCalc < F(4, 24, 0.05), Accept H0.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":37,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":156,\"sourceQuestion\":37,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"If the test were conducted at an alpha level of 0.05, which of the following statements is the correct conclusion?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"The calculated F = 22.13 exceeds F(4,24,0.05) ≈ 2.78, so reject H₀ and conclude that at least one machine mean differs. <b>C. FCalc &gt; F(4, 24, 0.05), Reject H0.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 37 (print p. 156), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","chart":{"columns":["Source","SS","DF","MS","F"],"rows":[["Machines","456.7","4","",""],["Error","123.8","24","",""],["Total","580.5","28","",""]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["FCalc > F(24, 4, 0.05), Reject H0.","FCalc < F(4, 24, 0.95), Accept H0.","FCalc > F(4, 24, 0.05), Reject H0.","FCalc < F(4, 24, 0.05), Accept H0."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":37,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":156,"sourceQuestion":37,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"If the test were conducted at an alpha level of 0.05, which of the following statements is the correct conclusion?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"The calculated F = 22.13 exceeds F(4,24,0.05) ≈ 2.78, so reject H₀ and conclude that at least one machine mean differs. <b>C. FCalc &gt; F(4, 24, 0.05), Reject H0.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 37 (print p. 156), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"90343bd55e9baf67da1f9cfc93ea89626533991ff32c92625d98b7b5a260ecb8"},"cssgb:set-2:source-380:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"options\":[\"Poisson\",\"Chi-square\",\"Normal\",\"Student’s t\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":380,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":94,\"sourceQuestion\":51,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which distribution is used for a hypothesis test on a proportion?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"For the hypothesis test on proportions, we often use a normal approximation. <b>C. Normal</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 51 (print p. 94), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","options":["Poisson","Chi-square","Normal","Student’s t"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":380,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":94,"sourceQuestion":51,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which distribution is used for a hypothesis test on a proportion?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"For the hypothesis test on proportions, we often use a normal approximation. <b>C. Normal</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 51 (print p. 94), IV.B.2.</span