nal efficiency prior to rolling out an organizational change. <b>C. Proactive: Operational efficiency prior to rolling out an organizational change</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 52 (print p. 94), IV.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"0188362317677b24b157a1831fe08a580bee62e9e1390528b4483c8ad71ebd5f"},"cssgb:set-2:source-382:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"IV.C.1\",\"options\":[\"customer perception prior to a product or service launch.\",\"customer satisfaction after having launched a product/service.\",\"operational efficiency after the organizational change.\",\"results of the last internal management system audit.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":382,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":95,\"sourceQuestion\":53,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A proactive approach to identifying performance gaps when an organization wants to identify opportunities for improvement would be to examine:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"The question asks for a proactive approach, which indicates something prior to a launch. <b>A. customer perception prior to a product or service launch.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 53 (print p. 95), IV.C.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"IV.C.1","options":["customer perception prior to a product or service launch.","customer satisfaction after having launched a product/service.","operational efficiency after the organizational change.","results of the last internal management system audit."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":382,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":95,"sourceQuestion":53,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A proactive approach to identifying performance gaps when an organization wants to identify opportunities for improvement would be to examine:","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"The question asks for a proactive approach, which indicates something prior to a launch. <b>A. customer perception prior to a product or service launch.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 53 (print p. 95), IV.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"53759e7ae12789b326138c80d864f93e13e8b2685fa5f02d8e36826528195bf0"},"cssgb:set-2:source-383:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"IV.C.1\",\"options\":[\"Measure\",\"Analyze\",\"Improve\",\"Control\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":383,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":95,\"sourceQuestion\":54,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A gap analysis in Six Sigma is most useful during which phase?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"Gap analysis is most useful during the improve phase of a Six Sigma project. <b>C. Improve</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 54 (print p. 95), IV.C.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"IV.C.1","options":["Measure","Analyze","Improve","Control"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":383,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":95,"sourceQuestion":54,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A gap analysis in Six Sigma is most useful during which phase?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"Gap analysis is most useful during the improve phase of a Six Sigma project. <b>C. Improve</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 54 (print p. 95), IV.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"8bcacec19013b7c5644ba1ced8bbb52ea8abd9d4e3050183879a0efeb788b910"},"cssgb:set-2:source-384:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"IV.C.2\",\"options\":[\"Fault tree analysis is a top-down failure analysis in which a failure of a system is analyzed using a combined series of lower-level events, while Five Whys diagnoses the failure by asking Why repeatedly until the root cause is determined.\",\"Fault tree analysis is a bottom-up failure analysis in which a failure of lower-level events is analyzed using system failures, while Five Whys diagnoses the failure by repeatedly asking Why until the root cause is determined.\",\"Five Whys analysis is a top-down failure analysis in which a failure of a system is analyzed using a combined series of lower-level events, while fault tree analysis diagnoses the failure by asking Why repeatedly until the root cause is determined.\",\"The two terms are consistent and interchangeable, as both are applied toward root cause analysis.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":384,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":95,\"sourceQuestion\":55,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which statement correctly distinguishes fault tree analysis from Five Whys analysis?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"Fault tree analysis is a top-down failure analysis in which a failure of a system is analyzed using a combined series of lower-level events, while Five Whys diagnoses the failure by asking why repeatedly until the root cause is determined. <b>A. Fault tree analysis is a top-down failure analysis in which a failure of a system is analyzed using a combined series of lower-level events, while Five Whys diagnoses the failure by asking Why repeatedly until the root cause is determined.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 55 (print p. 95), IV.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"IV.C.2","options":["Fault tree analysis is a top-down failure analysis in which a failure of a system is analyzed using a combined series of lower-level events, while Five Whys diagnoses the failure by asking Why repeatedly until the root cause is determined.","Fault tree analysis is a bottom-up failure analysis in which a failure of lower-level events is analyzed using system failures, while Five Whys diagnoses the failure by repeatedly asking Why until the root cause is determined.","Five Whys analysis is a top-down failure analysis in which a failure of a system is analyzed using a combined series of lower-level events, while fault tree analysis diagnoses the failure by asking Why repeatedly until the root cause is determined.","The two terms are consistent and interchangeable, as both are applied toward root cause analysis."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":384,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":95,"sourceQuestion":55,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes fault tree analysis from Five Whys analysis?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"Fault tree analysis is a top-down failure analysis in which a failure of a system is analyzed using a combined series of lower-level events, while Five Whys diagnoses the failure by asking why repeatedly until the root cause is determined. <b>A. Fault tree analysis is a top-down failure analysis in which a failure of a system is analyzed using a combined series of lower-level events, while Five Whys diagnoses the failure by asking Why repeatedly until the root cause is determined.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 55 (print p. 95), IV.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"19411c602551ec0ff7b951867b01f35754ada6a783cc36406b9c1db9c978ba27"},"cssgb:set-2:source-385:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"IV.C.2\",\"options\":[\"FMEA.\",\"fault tree analysis.\",\"cause-and-effect diagram.\",\"root cause analysis.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":385,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":95,\"sourceQuestion\":56,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The analytical method that is mainly used in reliability engineering to understand how systems can fail is:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"Reliability engineers use fault tree diagrams and analysis to uncover actual and potential system errors. <b>B. fault tree analysis.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 56 (print p. 95), IV.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"IV.C.2","options":["FMEA.","fault tree analysis.","cause-and-effect diagram.","root cause analysis."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter4.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":385,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":95,"sourceQuestion":56,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The analytical method that is mainly used in reliability engineering to understand how systems can fail is:","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"Reliability engineers use fault tree diagrams and analysis to uncover actual and potential system errors. <b>B. fault tree analysis.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A IV. Analyze Phase, Question 56 (print p. 95), IV.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"3d69b30deab88987edb66ef9e7ebb3697fcb6b7