":["express factor levels in percentages adding up to 100 percent.","investigate the primary factor while controlling the interactions and effects of other variables.","increase the combinations of factors and levels required to be run.","reduce the combinations of factors and levels required to be run."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":402,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":110,"sourceQuestion":2,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A fractional factorial experiment is used to:","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"Fractional factorials reduce the combinations of factors and levels for experiments. <b>D. reduce the combinations of factors and levels required to be run.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 2 (print p. 110), V.A.1.</span>"},"revision":"e3dda52ecffa9b982b6c095318ac6169e1142f48d7e42c27754da9879460d013"},"cssgb:set-2:source-403:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"V.A.1\",\"options\":[\"special-cause or time-order variation.\",\"common cause variation.\",\"main effects.\",\"interaction effects.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":403,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":110,\"sourceQuestion\":3,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Randomizing experimental run order primarily helps prevent which influence from being systematically confounded with a factor effect?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"Randomizing run order distributes special-cause, time-order, and other lurking influences across factor settings so they do not systematically bias one estimated factor effect. Randomization does not remove the underlying variation. <b>A. special-cause or time-order variation.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-sou