69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"V.A.2\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Run\",\"A\",\"B\",\"C\",\"Y\"],\"rows\":[[\"1\",\"−\",\"−\",\"−\",\"10\"],[\"2\",\"+\",\"−\",\"−\",\"5\"],[\"3\",\"−\",\"+\",\"−\",\"5\"],[\"4\",\"+\",\"+\",\"−\",\"3\"],[\"5\",\"−\",\"−\",\"+\",\"2\"],[\"6\",\"+\",\"−\",\"+\",\"1\"],[\"7\",\"−\",\"+\",\"+\",\"7\"],[\"8\",\"+\",\"+\",\"+\",\"10\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"−1.25\",\"0.875\",\"1.25\",\"1.75\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":417,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":113,\"sourceQuestion\":17,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is the main effect of Factor B?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"The main effect for Factor B is calculated using: Effect B = mean(B high) − mean(B low) = 6.25 − 4.50 = 1.75. <b>D. 1.75</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 17 (print p. 113), V.A.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"V.A.2","chart":{"columns":["Run","A","B","C","Y"],"rows":[["1","−","−","−","10"],["2","+","−","−","5"],["3","−","+","−","5"],["4","+","+","−","3"],["5","−","−","+","2"],["6","+","−","+","1"],["7","−","+","+","7"],["8","+","+","+","10"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["−1.25","0.875","1.25","1.75"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":417,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":113,"sourceQuestion":17,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is the main effect of Factor B?","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"The main effect for Factor B is calculated using: Effect B = mean(B high) − mean(B low) = 6.25 − 4.50 = 1.75. <b>D. 1.75</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 17 (print p. 113), V.A.2.</span>"},"revision":"d0a548bc0818326ffb7b5170748a2aae1eebf04e9bac80a8b2fdf665caf28b54"},"cssgb:set-2:source-418:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"V.A.2\",\"options\":[\"there are no interaction effects.\",\"there are no main effects.\",\"main effects are present.\",\"interaction effects are present.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":418,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":113,\"sourceQuestion\":18,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The risks of confounding for a fractional factorial design are higher when:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"If interaction effects are present, there is more of a risk of confounding in a fractional factorial experiment. <b>D. interaction effects are present.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 18 (print p. 113), V.A.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"V.A.2","options":["there are no interaction effects.","there are no main effects.","main effects are present.","interaction effects are present."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":418,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":113,"sourceQuestion":18,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The risks of confounding for a fractional factorial design are higher when:","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"If interaction effects are present, there is more of a risk of confounding in a fractional factorial experiment. <b>D. interaction effects are present.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 18 (print p. 113), V.A.2.</span>"},"revision":"c5c4a8866970205cea408a55a3c02407f884b9d368f191b7e5c975c8a26b9ceb"},"cssgb:set-2:source-419:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"V.A.2\",\"chart\":{\"panels\":[{\"highLine\":[71,53],\"lowLine\":[63,38],\"title\":\"A × B\"},{\"highLine\":[60,41],\"lowLine\":[73,50],\"title\":\"A × C\"},{\"highLine\":[39,63],\"lowLine\":[62,62],\"title\":\"B × C\"}],\"type\":\"interaction-plot-3\"},\"options\":[\"Interaction AB\",\"Interaction AC\",\"Interaction BC\",\"Cannot be determined from the graph\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":419,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":114,\"sourceQuestion\":19,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The interaction plots for an experiment with three factors are shown below. Based on the graphs, which two-factor interaction is the strongest?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"The BC interaction is the strongest. The BC interaction plot shows lines that are the least parallel. Because there is a BC interaction, the effect of changing the B from the low to the high level cannot be determined unless the factor level setting of C is known, and vice versa. <b>C. Interaction BC</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 19 (print p. 114), V.A.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"V.A.2","chart":{"panels":[{"highLine":[71,53],"lowLine":[63,38],"title":"A × B"},{"highLine":[60,41],"lowLine":[73,50],"title":"A × C"},{"highLine":[39,63],"lowLine":[62,62],"title":"B × C"}],"type":"interaction-plot-3"},"options":["Interaction AB","Interaction AC","Interaction BC","Cannot be determined from the graph"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":419,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":114,"sourceQuestion":19,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The interaction plots for an experiment with three factors are shown below. Based on the graphs, which two-factor interaction is the strongest?","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"The BC interaction is the strongest. The BC interaction plot shows lines that are the least parallel. Because there is a BC interaction, the effect of changing the B from the low to the high level cannot be determined unless the factor level setting of C is known, and vice versa. <b>C. Interaction BC</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 19 (print p. 114), V.A.2.</span>"},"revision":"25e2c1e497b4bbe4c96232b3f237e1e087e3e1bd41456f62c6e359e0a4d6ca45"},"cssgb:set-2:source-41:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"IV.C.2\",\"options\":[\"manpower, materials, communication, weather, and executives.\",\"manpower, materials, methods, Mother Nature, management, and measurement.\",\"humans, time, machinery, environment, and coworkers.\",\"employees, material, methods, environment, supervisors, and calibration.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":41,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":157,\"sourceQuestion\":41,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"Process inputs for a fishbone diagram are traditionally recognized as:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"Fishbone, also known as cause-and-effect or Ishikawa, generally uses the 6Ms, which are manpower, materials, methods, Mother Nature, management, and measurement. Other input options include place, people, procedures, and policies. <b>B. manpower, materials, methods, Mother Nature, management, and measurement.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 41 (print p. 157), IV.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"IV.C.2","options":["manpower, materials, communication, weather, and executives.","manpower, materials, methods, Mother Nature, management, and measurement.","humans, time, machinery, environment, and coworkers.","employees, material, methods, environment, supervisors, and calibration."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":41,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":157,"sourceQuestion":41,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"Process inputs for a fishbone diagram are traditionally recognized as:","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"Fishbone, also known as cause-and-effect or Ishikawa, generally uses the 6Ms, which are manpower, materials, methods, Mother Nature, management, and measurement. Other input options include place, people, procedures, and policies. <b>B. manpower, materials, methods, Mother Nature, management, and measurement.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 41 (print p. 157), IV.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"1103a82f32ad7dd806d78a994bb4fb32b80322f5cd8d9b96ec2c25e579d1309b"},"cssgb:set-2:source-420:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"V.A.2\",\"chart\":{\"overall\":16.75,\"panels\":[{\"factor\":\"A\",\"high\":17.25,\"low\":16.25},{\"factor\":\"B\",\"high\":19.25,\"low\":14.25}],\"type\":\"main-effects-plot\"},\"options\":[\"The mean response will decrease by 5 units.\",\"The mean response will increase by 2.5 units.\",\"The mean response will increase by 5 units.\",\"The mean response will be dependent on the setting for Factor A.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":420,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":114,\"sourceQuestion\":20,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A two-factor experiment was conducted. The analysis sho