e concept toward completion and implementation","Advance Product Quality Planning: Potential fulfillment of the needs analysis within business costs and constraints"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":425,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":116,"sourceQuestion":25,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Select the option that correctly pairs the concept with its definition.","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"Try-storming simulations are exploratory tests to incrementally build the concept toward completion and implementation. <b>C. Try-storming simulations: Exploratory tests to incrementally build the concept toward completion and implementation</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 25 (print p. 116), V.B.</span>"},"revision":"b00bb9a4aedbe5e4ab77d2b11bbb56c332ed4dddd04a9b8731c37e4b780e67d8"},"cssgb:set-2:source-426:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"V.B\",\"options\":[\"Quickly simulating the most feasible ideas from the brainstorming session\",\"Critiquing the outputs of brainstorming and identifying potential defects and flaws for risk management\",\"Expanding the scope and reducing control of implementation activities\",\"Conducting extensive document reviews prior to simulation\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":426,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":116,\"sourceQuestion\":26,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Try-storming continues the work generated by brainstorming by which actions?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"In try-storming, the most feasible ideas from the brainstorming sessions are quickly simulated or developed for demonstration. <b>A. Quickly simulating the most feasible ideas from the brainstorming session</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 26 (print p. 116), V.B.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"V.B","options":["Quickly simulating the most feasible ideas from the brainstorming session","Critiquing the outputs of brainstorming and identifying potential defects and flaws for risk management","Expanding the scope and reducing control of implementation activities","Conducting extensive document reviews prior to simulation"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":426,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":116,"sourceQuestion":26,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Try-storming continues the work generated by brainstorming by which actions?","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"In try-storming, the most feasible ideas from the brainstorming sessions are quickly simulated or developed for demonstration. <b>A. Quickly simulating the most feasible ideas from the brainstorming session</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 26 (print p. 116), V.B.</span>"},"revision":"6ca9a45088fd4a8ebf68de562013fdec53e4a4b8009e6146dca43d7609948a7b"},"cssgb:set-2:source-427:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"V.B\",\"options\":[\"The proof of concepts will be delayed, affecting the potential progress and readiness for completion.\",\"The try-storming will need to be canceled, pending a resolution of the pilot test.\",\"The product malfunctions from previous versions could inhibit completion.\",\"New defects and maladies could impair the benefits of the Six Sigma project.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":427,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":116,\"sourceQuestion\":27,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What are the potential risks if the pilot test phase does not effectively restrict problematic solutions?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"The benefits of the Six Sigma project will be offset by the new risks, defects, and maladies introduced from the suboptimal implementation approach. <b>D. New defects and maladies could impair the benefits of the Six Sigma project.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 27 (print p. 116), V.B.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"V.B","options":["The proof of concepts will be delayed, affecting the potential progress and readiness for completion.","The try-storming will need to be canceled, pending a resolution of the pilot test.","The product malfunctions from previous versions could inhibit completion.","New defects and maladies could impair the benefits of the Six Sigma project."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":427,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":116,"sourceQuestion":27,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What are the potential risks if the pilot test phase does not effectively restrict problematic solutions?","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"The benefits of the Six Sigma project will be offset by the new risks, defects, and maladies introduced from the suboptimal implementation approach. <b>D. New defects and maladies could impair the benefits of the Six Sigma project.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 27 (print p. 116), V.B.</span>"},"revision":"55f22f4b142c66893210cec82e1c20913f32b86f36013c0b176fb1fdb4d2fc18"},"cssgb:set-2:source-428:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"V.C.1\",\"options\":[\"sequential; complete lean activities before starting Six Sigma.\",\"mutually exclusive; deploy either lean or Six Sigma activities, but not both.\",\"integrative; use both methods to reinforce and sustain improvements.\",\"antagonistic; using lean methods will reduce the effect of Six Sigma.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":428,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":117,\"sourceQuestion\":28,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The ideal relationship of lean and Six Sigma methods is best described as:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"The convergence of Lean approaches and Six Sigma methods will more efficiently deliver the intended benefits to cost, quality, delivery, customer satisfaction, and predictability. <b>C. integrative; use both methods to reinforce and sustain improvements.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 28 (print p. 117), V.C.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"V.C.1","options":["sequential; complete lean activities before starting Six Sigma.","mutually exclusive; deploy either lean or Six Sigma activities, but not both.","integrative; use both methods to reinforce and sustain improvements.","antagonistic; using lean methods will reduce the effect of Six Sigma."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":428,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":117,"sourceQuestion":28,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The ideal relationship of lean and Six Sigma methods is best described as:","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"The convergence of Lean approaches and Six Sigma methods will more efficiently deliver the intended benefits to cost, quality, delivery, customer satisfaction, and predictability. <b>C. integrative; use both methods to reinforce and sustain improvements.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 28 (print p. 117), V.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"0adf808a2210be8956e2ce1592aeab3aff2332599b93dce82eb207a1a918241d"},"cssgb:set-2:source-429:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"V.C.1\",\"options\":[\"dedicated full-time employees.\",\"large work areas.\",\"concurrent production of different models.\",\"the quantity of work-in-process (WIP) inventory.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":429,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":117,\"sourceQuestion\":29,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The best way to reduce large, segregating inventories and carrying costs is to reduce:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"Lean reduces the WIP inventory, which reduces segregating inventories and carrying costs. <b>D. the quantity of work-in-process (WIP) inventory.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 29 (print p. 117), V.C.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"V.C.1","options":["dedicated full-time employees.","large work areas.","concurrent production of different models.","the quantity of work-in-process (WIP) inventory."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":429,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":117,"sourceQuestion":29,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The best way to reduce large, segregating inventories and carrying costs is to reduce:","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"Lean reduces the WIP inventory, which reduces segregating inventories and carrying costs. <b>D. the quantity of work-in-process (WIP) inventory.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 29 (print p. 117), V.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"26b8a9a91f98496ab11e3dfa3c99132aa8af3f2cf4deafee549099ac00f18dea"},"cssgb:set-2:source-42:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"II.F.3\",\"options\":[\"a SWOT analysis.\",\"SMED.\",\"multi-voting.\",\"an ANOVA analysis.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":42,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":157,\"sourceQuestion\":42,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"A common way for a Green Belt team to make a team decision is through:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"The best choice here is multi-voting, the most common for decision-making. <b>C. multi-voting.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 42 (print p. 157), II.F.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.F.3","options":["a SWOT analysis.","SMED.","multi-voting.","an ANOVA analysis."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":42,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":157,"sourceQuestion":42,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"A common way for a Green Belt team to make a team decision is through:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"The best choice here is multi-voting, the most common for decision-making. <b>C. multi-voting.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 42 (print p. 157), II.F.3.</span>"},"revision":"c83c273e6d365956a65329d7fbe2fdf40d84645bc1d773834e5715deaef451e6"},"cssgb:set-2:source-430:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"V.C.1\",\"options\":[\"track process outc