estion\":31,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which of the following tools is used to develop standard work?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"A production capacity chart is used to develop standard work. <b>A. Production capacity chart</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 31 (print p. 117), V.C.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"V.C.1","options":["Production capacity chart","Kanban","Control charts","5S"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":431,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":117,"sourceQuestion":31,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which of the following tools is used to develop standard work?","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"A production capacity chart is used to develop standard work. <b>A. Production capacity chart</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 31 (print p. 117), V.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"0ac6a79b904a0625c32995081c68f1a2ed97dcf66bb6cd71fb674c94a2af3d8a"},"cssgb:set-2:source-432:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"V.C.1\",\"options\":[\"mistake-proofing.\",\"a pull system.\",\"standard work.\",\"SMED.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":432,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":117,\"sourceQuestion\":32,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"On a production line, a kanban square is used to signal when another part is needed. This is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"Producing a piece only when it is needed by the next machine is an example of a pull system. <b>B. a pull system.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 32 (print p. 117), V.C.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"V.C.1","options":["mistake-proofing.","a pull system.","standard work.","SMED."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":432,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":117,"sourceQuestion":32,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"On a production line, a kanban square is used to signal when another part is needed. This is an example of:","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"Producing a piece only when it is needed by the next machine is an example of a pull system. <b>B. a pull system.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 32 (print p. 117), V.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"517a8fdd5dd169299d9cbdfe2243f2af047c5dc393a75a278337db3147de343f"},"cssgb:set-2:source-433:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"V.C.1\",\"options\":[\"Retrain the operator\",\"Employ total productive maintenance\",\"Mistake-proof the machine\",\"Use a pull system for the process\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":433,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":118,\"sourceQuestion\":33,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"An operator is tasked with placing workpieces into a milling machine. Once every so often, the operator places a piece into the machine upside down, resulting in a shutdown. What is the best way to address this problem?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"The best way to address human errors that occur at random intervals is to mistake-proof the process. A fixture on the milling machine could be redesigned so that the piece will not fit if it is placed in the machine upside-down. <b>C. Mistake-proof the machine</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 33 (print p. 118), V.C.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"V.C.1","options":["Retrain the operator","Employ total productive maintenance","Mistake-proof the machine","Use a pull system for the process"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":433,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":118,"sourceQuestion":33,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"An operator is tasked with placing workpieces into a milling machine. Once every so often, the operator places a piece into the machine upside down, resulting in a shutdown. What is the best way to address this problem?","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"The best way to address human errors that occur at random intervals is to mistake-proof the process. A fixture on the milling machine could be redesigned so that the piece will not fit if it is placed in the machine upside-down. <b>C. Mistake-proof the machine</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 33 (print p. 118), V.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"67be381c315690a180d891746ef74380136208d76716fa52612eb1d612d47b59"},"cssgb:set-2:source-434:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"V.C.1\",\"options\":[\"waste, or muda.\",\"capacity.\",\"value-added activities.\",\"Kano categories.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":434,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":118,\"sourceQuestion\":34,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Overproduction, excess motion, waiting, inventory level excess movement, defect correction, excess processing, and lost 