 eliminate waste (muda), unevenness (mura), and unreasonableness (muri) are known as:","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"Countermeasure activities eliminate waste (muda), unevenness (mura), and unreasonableness (muri). <b>A. countermeasure activities.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 37 (print p. 118), V.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"6aef0a8e05b9562ae53b8edd5ce29930acdccfe8a60605e776100a52595b1ade"},"cssgb:set-2:source-438:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"V.C.1\",\"options\":[\"shifted process average.\",\"reduced process variation.\",\"reduced cycle time.\",\"reduced process complexity.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":438,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":119,\"sourceQuestion\":38,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The crossover of Lean-Six Sigma objectives between traditional Six Sigma and traditional Lean is:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"The crossover of Lean Six Sigma, between traditional Lean and traditional Six Sigma, is reduced process variation. <b>B. reduced process variation.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 38 (print p. 119), V.C.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"V.C.1","options":["shifted process average.","reduced process variation.","reduced cycle time.","reduced process complexity."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":438,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":119,"sourceQuestion":38,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The crossover of Lean-Six Sigma objectives between traditional Six Sigma and traditional Lean is:","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"The crossover of Lean Six Sigma, between traditional Lean and traditional Six Sigma, is reduced process variation. <b>B. reduced process variation.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 38 (print p. 119), V.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"e3bc521998375a03ffdf3d4952cee09413ee5e601b6e23f5a8aa032f9d0bfbde"},"cssgb:set-2:source-439:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"V.C.1\",\"options\":[\"a pull system.\",\"the visual factory.\",\"kanban.\",\"total productive maintenance.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":439,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":119,\"sourceQuestion\":39,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The process that manages scheduling and material flow so that overproduction and excess inventories are eliminated is known as:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"A pull system is a process used to manage scheduling and material flow to avoid overproduction and excess inventories. <b>A. a pull system.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 39 (print p. 119), V.C.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"V.C.1","options":["a pull system.","the visual factory.","kanban.","total productive maintenance."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":439,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":119,"sourceQuestion":39,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The process that manages scheduling and material flow so that overproduction and excess inventories are eliminated is known as:","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"A pull system is a process used to manage scheduling and material flow to avoid overproduction and excess inventories. <b>A. a pull system.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 39 (print p. 119), V.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"e87a49985e0b4d85d1e2cbbbb1a7ba105e413fbed373a601d74647db92139d73"},"cssgb:set-2:source-43:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B1\",\"options\":[\"Accept H0 since p < α.\",\"Reject H0 since p < α.\",\"Do not reject H0 since 2p > α.\",\"It depends on the type of test conducted.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":43,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":158,\"sourceQuestion\":43,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"A hypothesis test yields a p-value equal to 0.035. If the test is conducted at α = 0.05, what is the conclusion?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"Because p = 0.035 is less than α = 0.05, reject the null hypothesis. <b>B. Reject H0 since p &lt; α.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 43 (print p. 158), IV.B1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"IV.B1","options":["Accept H0 since p < α.","Reject H0 since p < α.","Do not reject H0 since 2p > α.","It depends on the type of test conducted."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":43,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":158,"sourceQuestion":43,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"A hypothesis test yields a p-value equal to 0.035. If the test is conducted at α = 0.05, what is the conclusion?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"Because p = 0.035 is less than α = 0.05, reject the null hypothesis. <b>B. Reject H0 since p &lt; α.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 43 (print p. 158), IV.B1.</span>"},"revision":"1b305465f55041847e32833c6e89b2746e3ab545d579116f405ee3d10e68b08a"},"cssgb:set-2:source-440:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"V.C.2\",\"options\":[\"Economic order quantity (EOQ) amortizes the costs of long setups\",\"Creates bottlenecks, drives up lot sizes, and reduces flexibility\",\"Mitigates the hidden costs of producing and transporting large amounts of inventory\",\"Reduces the time available to set up and produce a wider variety of products and services\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":440,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":119,\"sourceQuestion\":40,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which advantages justify the importance of quick changeover or single-minute exchange of dies (SMED) implementation?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"Quick changeover, or SMED, mitigates the hidden costs of producing and transporting large amounts of inventory. <b>C. Mitigates the hidden costs of producing and transporting large amounts of inventory</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 40 (print p. 119), V.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"V.C.2","options":["Economic order quantity (EOQ) amortizes the costs of long setups","Creates bottlenecks, drives up lot sizes, and reduces flexibility","Mitigates the hidden costs of producing and transporting large amounts of inventory","Reduces the time available to set up and produce a wider variety of products and services"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":440,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":119,"sourceQuestion":40,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Which advantages justify the importance of quick changeover or single-minute exchange of dies (SMED) implementation?","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"Quick changeover, or SMED, mitigates the hidden costs of producing and transporting large amounts of inventory. <b>C. Mitigates the hidden costs of producing and transporting large amounts of inventory</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 40 (print p. 119), V.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"e5cd7f7fb889276c2d809aaa347063d0e139847702353cbcc44765c141d1cb67"},"cssgb:set-2:source-441:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"V.C.2\",\"options\":[\"Tools, fasteners, and placement\",\"Bottlenecks, lot sizes, and flexibility\",\"Inventory, shipment quantities, and receiving methods\",\"Six Sigma practices for define, measure, analyze, improve, and control phases\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":441,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":119,\"sourceQuestion\":41,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What are examples of items that could be standardized to reduce internal setup times?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"The following are items to consider when focusing on improving internal setup times: standardized tools, fasteners, and placement. <b>A. Tools, fasteners, and placement</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A V. Improve Phase, Question 41 (print p. 119), V.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"V.C.2","options":["Tools, fasteners, and placement","Bottlenecks, lot sizes, and flexibility","Inventory, shipment quantities, and receiving methods","Six Sigma practices for define, measure, analyze, improve, and control phases"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter5.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":441,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintP