sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"An R chart is used rather than an S chart because it is:","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"An R chart is used rather than an S chart because it is easier to hand-calculate. <b>A. easier to hand-calculate.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 12 (print p. 128), VI.A.3.</span>"},"revision":"e6cb6c09c9a4649ee53adef7b14ad8d254ec0f3c3e37286a4b12829dfc10c8d7"},"cssgb:set-2:source-457:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"VI.A.3\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"X̿\",\"90.475\"],[\"R̄\",\"12.440\"],[\"Subgroup size, n\",\"6\"],[\"A₂\",\"0.483\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"84.4665\",\"96.4835\",\"97.6529\",\"108.2270\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":457,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":128,\"sourceQuestion\":13,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Calculate the upper control limit of an X-bar chart using the following information:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"To calculate the upper control limit of the X-bar chart, we need to look up the table value A₂, which is based on the subgroup size, n. Then we use: UCL = X̿ + A₂R̄ = 90.475 + 0.483(12.440) = 96.4835. <b>B. 96.4835</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 13 (print p. 128), VI.A.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"VI.A.3","chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["X̿","90.475"],["R̄","12.440"],["Subgroup size, n","6"],["A₂","0.483"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["84.4665","96.4835","97.6529","108.2270"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":457,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":128,"sourceQuestion":13,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Calculate the upper control limit of an X-bar chart using the following information:","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"To calculate the upper control limit of the X-bar chart, we need to look up the table value A₂, which is based on the subgroup size, n. Then we use: UCL = X̿ + A₂R̄ = 90.475 + 0.483(12.440) = 96.4835. <b>B. 96.4835</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 13 (print p. 128), VI.A.3.</span>"},"revision":"01fe937119447682a190fca1969f3ebb5cdbde0f9d98b1d18a8c431ab58c87cb"},"cssgb:set-2:source-458:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"VI.A.3\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"X̿\",\"29.87\"],[\"S̄\",\"3.55\"],[\"Subgroup size, n\",\"16\"],[\"A₃\",\"0.763\"],[\"B₃\",\"0.448\"],[\"B₄\",\"1.552\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"[1.232, 5.868]\",\"[1.289, 5.811]\",\"[1.590, 5.510]\",\"[1.519, 5.581]\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":458,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":129,\"sourceQuestion\":14,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Calculate the upper and lower control limits for the S chart.\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"To calculate an S chart’s upper and lower control limits, we need to look up two table values, B₃ and B₄, based on the subgroup size, n. Then we use: LCL = B₃S̄ = 0.448(3.55) = 1.590; UCL = B₄S̄ = 1.552(3.55) = 5.510. <b>C. [1.590, 5.510]</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 14 (print p. 129), VI.A.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"VI.A.3","chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["X̿","29.87"],["S̄","3.55"],["Subgroup size, n","16"],["A₃","0.763"],["B₃","0.448"],["B₄","1.552"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["[1.232, 5.868]","[1.289, 5.811]","[1.590, 5.510]","[1.519, 5.581]"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":458,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":129,"sourceQuestion":14,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Calculate the upper and lower control limits for the S chart.","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"To calculate an S chart’s upper and lower control limits, we need to look up two table values, B₃ and B₄, based on the subgroup size, n. Then we use: LCL = B₃S̄ = 0.448(3.55) = 1.590; UCL = B₄S̄ = 1.552(3.55) = 5.510. <b>C. [1.590, 5.510]</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 14 (print p. 129), VI.A.3.</span>"},"revision":"7ca37a8250be324571d7eb623a871a7231f1936e18ff36d594e026b7087a85a0"},"cssgb:set-2:source-459:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"VI.A.3\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"X̿\",\"29.87\"],[\"S̄\",\"3.55\"],[\"Subgroup size, n\",\"16\"],[\"A₃\",\"0.763\"],[\"B₃\",\"0.448\"],[\"B₄\",\"1.552\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"[27.16, 32.58]\",\"[29.11, 29.93]\",\"[29.19, 30.55]\",\"[29.70, 30.04]\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":459,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":129,\"sourceQuestion\":15,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Calculate the upper and lower control limits for the X-bar chart.\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"To calculate the control limits of the X-bar chart with n = 16, we find the table value A₃ = 0.763 based on n. Then X̄-chart limits = 29.87 ± 0.763(3.55) = [27.16, 32.58]. <b>A. [27.16, 32.58]</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 15 (print p. 129), VI.A.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"VI.A.3","chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["X̿","29.87"],["S̄","3.55"],["Subgroup size, n","16"],["A₃","0.763"],["B₃","0.448"],["B₄","1.552"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["[27.16, 32.58]","[29.11, 29.93]","[29.19, 30.55]","[29.70, 30.04]"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":459,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":129,"sourceQuestion":15,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Calculate the upper and lower control limits for the X-bar chart.","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"To calculate the control limits of the X-bar chart with n = 16, we find the table value A₃ = 0.763 based on n. Then X̄-chart limits = 29.87 ± 0.763(3.55) = [27.16, 32.58]. <b>A. [27.16, 32.58]</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 15 (print p. 129), VI.A.3.</span>"},"revision":"c5552aa612a3a80cd66948085b513bb966668fb3d16fed2cb320deb52d12c076"},"cssgb:set-2:source-45:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.D.2\",\"options\":[\"random sampling.\",\"sequential sampling.\",\"stratified sampling.\",\"biased sampling.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":45,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":158,\"sourceQuestion\":45,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"Sampling from a lot in which each unit has an equal probability of getting chosen is known as:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"In random sampling, each unit of the population has an equal chance of being chosen for the sample. <b>A. random sampling.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 45 (print p. 158), III.D.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.D.2","options":["random sampling.","sequential sampling.","stratified sampling.","biased sampling."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":45,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":158,"sourceQuestion":45,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"Sampling from a lot in which each unit has an equal probability of getting chosen is known as:","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"In random sampling, each unit of the population has an equal chance of being chosen for the sample. <b>A. random sampling.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 45 (print p. 158), III.D.2.</span>"},"revision":"477b4f2c640af72ef0f7c7f739f695cd1906cce0eb3f946342626697513dd864"},"cssgb:set-2:source-460:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"VI.A.3\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"ΣXᵢ\",\"674.50\"],[\"Number of observations, k\",\"25\"],[\"Σ moving ranges\",\"24.40\"],[\"Moving-range period, n\",\"2\"],[\"E₂\",\"2.660\"],[\"D₃\",\"0\"],[\"D₄\",\"3.267\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"[0, 36.29]\",\"[24.05, 29.91]\",\"[24.27, 29.69]\",\"[26.20, 27.76]\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":460,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":129,\"sourceQuestion\":16,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What are the control limits of the individual chart?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"We note that the number of samples is k = 25 and the period for the moving average is n = 2. We first calculate X̄ = 674.50/25 = 26.98 and MR̄ = 24.40/(25−1) = 1.017. We find the table value E₂ for n = 2 to be 2.660. Then Individual-chart limits = 26.98 ± 2.660(1.017) = [24.27, 29.69]. <b>C. [24.27, 29.69]</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 16 (print p. 129), VI.A.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"VI.A.3","chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["ΣXᵢ","674.50"],["Number of observations, k","25"],["Σ moving ranges","24.40"],["Moving-range period, n","2"],["E₂","2.660"],["D₃","0"],["D₄","3.267"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["[0, 36.29]","[24.05, 29.91]","[24.27, 29.69]","[26.20, 27.76]"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":460,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":129,"sourceQuestion":16,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What are the control limits of the individual chart?","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"We note that the number of samples is k = 25 and the period for the moving average is n = 2. We first calculate X̄ = 674.50/25 = 26.98 and MR̄ = 24.40/(25−1) = 1.017. We find the table value E₂ for n = 2 to be 2.660. Then Individual-chart limits = 26.98 ± 2.660(1.017) = [24.27, 29.69]. <b>C. [24.27, 29.69]</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 16 (print p. 129), VI.A.3.</span>"},"revision":"14c623f344d20460b1c18cb48e76bd4fa0db01864f2cbebba66c50e94c5f16d8"},"cssgb:set-2:source-461:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"VI.A.3\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"ΣXᵢ\",\"674.50\"],[\"Number of observations, k\",\"25\"],[\"Σ moving ranges\",\"24.40\"],[\"Moving-range period, n\",\"2\"],[\"E₂\",\"2.660\"],[\"D₃\",\"0\"],[\"D₄\",\"3.267\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"[0, 2.71]\",\"[0, 3.32]\",\"[0.47, 1.57]\",\"[0.57, 1.46]\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":461,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":129,\"sourceQuestion\":17,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What are the control limits of the moving range chart?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"To find the control limits for the moving range chart with period n = 2, we look up table values D₃ = 0 and D₄ = 3.267 based on n. Then Moving-range limits = [D₃MR̄, D₄MR̄] = [0, 3.267(1.017)] = [0, 3.32]. <b>B. [0, 3.32]</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 17 (print p. 129), VI.A.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"VI.A.3","chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["ΣXᵢ","674.50"],["Number of observations, k","25"],["Σ moving ranges","24.40"],["Moving-range period, n","2"],["E₂","2.660"],["D₃","0"],["D₄","3.267"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["[0, 2.71]","[0, 3.32]","[0.47, 1.57]","[0.57, 1.46]"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":461,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":129,"sourceQuestion":17,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What are the control limits of the moving range chart?","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"To find the control limits for the moving range chart with period n = 2, we look up table values D₃ = 0 and D₄ = 3.267 based on n. Then Moving-range limits = [D₃MR̄, D₄MR̄] = [0, 3.267(1.017)] = [0, 3.32]. <b>B. [0, 3.32]</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 17 (print p. 129), VI.A.3.</span>"},"revision":"133b182f1a1c308628c15c9fce2017caf5abe8edc60e7ada600de98416b06ddd"},"cssgb:set-2:source-462:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"VI.A.3\",\"options\":[\"monitoring the count of nonconformities in a product.\",\"monitoring the fraction nonconforming.\",\"cases in which there are few opportunities for nonconformities to occur.\",\"continuous data.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":462,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":130,\"sourceQuestion\":18,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A p-chart is used for:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"A p-chart is used to monitor the fraction nonconforming. <b>B. monitoring the fraction nonconforming.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 18 (print p. 130), VI.A.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"VI.A.3","options":["monitoring the count of nonconformities in a product.","monitoring the fraction nonconforming.","cases in which there are few opportunities for nonconformities to occur.","continuous data."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":462,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":130,"sourceQuestion":18,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A p-chart is used for:","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"A p-chart is used to monitor the fraction nonconforming. <b>B. monitoring the fraction nonconforming.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 18 (print p. 130), VI.A.3.</span>"},"revision":"b754231217e7b6fdaa9435aa988e2c68fd9e366436505ee0519caf87d5c3a4b0"},"cssgb:set-2:source-463:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"VI.A.3\",\"options\":[\"Plot as calculated.\",\"Set the lower limit to zero.\",\"Recalculate. An error has been made.\",\"Use a u-chart.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":463,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":130,\"sourceQuestion\":19,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What action should be taken if the lower limit of a c-chart is negative?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"If the lower limit of a c-chart is negative, set the lower limit to zero. <b>B. Set the lower limit to zero.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 19 (print p. 130), VI.A.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"VI.A.3","options":["Plot as calculated.","Set the lower limit to zero.","Recalculate. An error has been made.","Use a u-chart."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":463,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":130,"sourceQuestion":19,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What action should be taken if the lower limit of a c-chart is negative?","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"If the lower limit of a c-chart is negative, set the lower limit to zero. <b>B. Set the lower limit to zero.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 19 (print p. 130), VI.A.3.</span>"},"revision":"0f01a540e110d2cecab406c44672f373797c8effeec1144626ce39997636561f"},"cssgb:set-2:source-464:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"VI.A.3\",\"options\":[\"count of nonconformities.\",\"rate of defectives per sample.\",\"count of defectives per unit.\",\"rate of nonconformities per unit.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":464,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":130,\"sourceQuestion\":20,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A u-chart measures the:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"A u-chart measures the rate of nonconformities (defects) per unit. <b>D. rate of nonconformities per unit.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 20 (print p. 130), VI.A.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"VI.A.3","options":["count of nonconformities.","rate of defectives per sample.","count of defectives per unit.","rate of nonconformities per unit."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":464,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":130,"sourceQuestion":20,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A u-chart measures the:","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"A u-chart measures the rate of nonconformities (defects) per unit. <b>D. rate of nonconformities per unit.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 20 (print p. 130), VI.A.3.</span>"},"revision":"6143753dfc04d577c2e87d3a71d89ccd8527c1a40bb31fdfe169920557368440"},"cssgb:set-2:source-465:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"VI.A.3\",\"options\":[\"The number of defects per washing machine produced will be tracked.\",\"The number of nonconforming light bulbs in a constant sample size of 300 bulbs will be tracked.\",\"The fraction of defective printed circuit boards produced each shift will be tracked. The number of boards produced per shift can vary from 400 to 500, depending on the type of chip being manufactured.\",\"The average number of flaws per computer produced will be tracked. Random samples of 20–30 computers are checked each hour.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":465,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":130,\"sourceQuestion\":21,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A c control chart would be best to use in which of the following situations?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"A c-chart monitors the count of nonconformities per inspection unit when the opportunity area is constant; here, each washing machine is one constant inspection unit. <b>A. The number of defects per washing machine produced will be tracked.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 21 (print p. 130), VI.A.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"VI.A.3","options":["The number of defects per washing machine produced will be tracked.","The number of nonconforming light bulbs in a constant sample size of 300 bulbs will be tracked.","The fraction of defective printed circuit boards produced each shift will be tracked. The number of boards produced per shift can vary from 400 to 500, depending on the type of chip being manufactured.","The average number of flaws per computer produced will be tracked. Random samples of 20–30 computers are checked each hour."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":465,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":130,"sourceQuestion":21,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A c control chart would be best to use in which of the following situations?","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"A c-chart monitors the count of nonconformities per inspection unit when the opportunity area is constant; here, each washing machine is one constant inspection unit. <b>A. The number of defects per washing machine produced will be tracked.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 21 (print p. 130), VI.A.3.</span>"},"revision":"90b5ee7da829e2f1577deeebb8281dbdfb7b400c2991466051212d19d6b41746"},"cssgb:set-2:source-466:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"VI.A.3\",\"options\":[\"the number of nonconformities in a product.\",\"the fraction nonconforming in a lot.\",\"cases where there are few opportunities for a nonconformity to occur.\",\"the number of nonconforming products.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":466,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":130,\"sourceQuestion\":22,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"An np-chart is used for monitoring:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"An np-chart is used to monitor the number of nonconforming products. The np-chart uses a constant sample size. <b>D. the number of nonconforming products.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 22 (print p. 130), VI.A.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"VI.A.3","options":["the number of nonconformities in a product.","the fraction nonconforming in a lot.","cases where there are few opportunities for a nonconformity to occur.","the number of nonconforming products."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":466,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":130,"sourceQuestion":22,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"An np-chart is used for monitoring:","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"An np-chart is used to monitor the number of nonconforming products. The np-chart uses a constant sample size. <b>D. the number of nonconforming products.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 22 (print p. 130), VI.A.3.</span>"},"revision":"7e5c5b10f4597871ceebd7f7d4403ac07ab5eca49b1bb66638d02d502b3d5d75"},"cssgb:set-2:source-467:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"VI.A.3\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"Total nonconforming, Σnp\",\"55\"],[\"Total inspected, Σn\",\"2800\"],[\"Number of subgroups, k\",\"30\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"0\",\"0.0007\",\"0.0196\",\"0.5893\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":467,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":131,\"sourceQuestion\":23,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is the centerline of the p-chart?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"To find the average proportion defective, we use p̄ = Σnp / Σn = 55/2800 = 0.0196. <b>C. 0.0196</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 23 (print p. 131), VI.A.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"VI.A.3","chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["Total nonconforming, Σnp","55"],["Total inspected, Σn","2800"],["Number of subgroups, k","30"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["0","0.0007","0.0196","0.5893"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":467,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":131,"sourceQuestion":23,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is the centerline of the p-chart?","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"To find the average proportion defective, we use p̄ = Σnp / Σn = 55/2800 = 0.0196. <b>C. 0.0196</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 23 (print p. 131), VI.A.3.</span>"},"revision":"dd16b61764d618a5188372ae5f89a6325a41f44f28e57b29b26c08d32ff96cdb"},"cssgb:set-2:source-468:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"VI.A.3\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"Total nonconforming, Σnp\",\"55\"],[\"Total inspected, Σn\",\"2800\"],[\"Number of subgroups, k\",\"30\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"[0, 0.0086]\",\"[0, 0.0627]\",\"[0.0118, 0.0275]\",\"[0.4540, 0.6570]\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":468,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":131,\"sourceQuestion\":24,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Calculate the control limits of the p-chart using the average sample size.\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"To find the average n, we use n̄ = Σn/k = 2800/30 = 93.3. Then, we can calculate the control limits of the p-chart: p-chart limits = p̄ ± 3√[p̄(1−p̄)/n̄] = [0, 0.0627]. <b>B. [0, 0.0627]</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 24 (print p. 131), VI.A.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"VI.A.3","chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["Total nonconforming, Σnp","55"],["Total inspected, Σn","2800"],["Number of subgroups, k","30"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["[0, 0.0086]","[0, 0.0627]","[0.0118, 0.0275]","[0.4540, 0.6570]"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":468,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":131,"sourceQuestion":24,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Calculate the control limits of the p-chart using the average sample size.","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"To find the average n, we use n̄ = Σn/k = 2800/30 = 93.3. Then, we can calculate the control limits of the p-chart: p-chart limits = p̄ ± 3√[p̄(1−p̄)/n̄] = [0, 0.0627]. <b>B. [0, 0.0627]</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 24 (print p. 131), VI.A.3.</span>"},"revision":"b7fcc17948242bb6c718140778101576d529de2e4c2cc334f26893fb82823a92"},"cssgb:set-2:source-469:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"VI.A.3\",\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Parameter\",\"Value\"],\"rows\":[[\"Total nonconforming, Σnp\",\"78\"],[\"Number of subgroups, k\",\"25\"],[\"Constant subgroup size, n\",\"200\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"0.016\",\"0.390\",\"3.120\",\"5.791\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":469,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":131,\"sourceQuestion\":25,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"What is the centerline of the np-chart?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"To find the centerline of the np-chart, we use np̄ = Σnp/k = 78/25 = 3.120. <b>C. 3.120</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 25 (print p. 131), VI.A.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"VI.A.3","chart":{"columns":["Parameter","Value"],"rows":[["Total nonconforming, Σnp","78"],["Number of subgroups, k","25"],["Constant subgroup size, n","200"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["0.016","0.390","3.120","5.791"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":469,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":131,"sourceQuestion":25,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"What is the centerline of the np-chart?","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"To find the centerline of the np-chart, we use np̄ = Σnp/k = 78/25 = 3.120. <b>C. 3.120</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 25 (print p. 131), VI.A.3.</span>"},"revision":"7818efe73d9708b8d022928723e49d4c108d52e812353ca2620e5295f28113ea"},"cssgb:set-2:source-46:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"VI.B.1\",\"options\":[\"R