kReference\":\"VI.B.4\",\"options\":[\"audit findings (objective evidence and observations) are compared to audit criteria (expected references and behavior), leading to audit conclusions.\",\"audit criteria (objective evidence and observations) are compared to audit findings (expected references and behavior), leading to audit conclusions.\",\"audit conclusions (objective evidence and observations) are compared to audit findings (expected references and behavior), leading to audit criteria.\",\"audit conclusions (objective evidence and observations) are compared to audit criteria (expected references and behavior), leading to audit findings.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":493,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":136,\"sourceQuestion\":49,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"The correct representation of audit conclusions, audit criteria, and audit findings is:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"Audit findings (objective evidence and observations) are compared to audit criteria (expected references and behavior), leading to audit conclusions. <b>A. audit findings (objective evidence and observations) are compared to audit criteria (expected references and behavior), leading to audit conclusions.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 49 (print p. 136), VI.B.4.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"VI.B.4","options":["audit findings (objective evidence and observations) are compared to audit criteria (expected references and behavior), leading to audit conclusions.","audit criteria (objective evidence and observations) are compared to audit findings (expected references and behavior), leading to audit conclusions.","audit conclusions (objective evidence and observations) are compared to audit findings (expected references and behavior), leading to audit criteria.","audit conclusions (objective evidence and observations) are compared to audit criteria (expected references and behavior), leading to audit findings."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":493,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":136,"sourceQuestion":49,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"The correct representation of audit conclusions, audit criteria, and audit findings is:","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"Audit findings (objective evidence and observations) are compared to audit criteria (expected references and behavior), leading to audit conclusions. <b>A. audit findings (objective evidence and observations) are compared to audit criteria (expected references and behavior), leading to audit conclusions.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 49 (print p. 136), VI.B.4.</span>"},"revision":"e01e175540bc9a5dd6aba3ec4b8f1919d0df4e9e6863c3f6ee0ec24686c39520"},"cssgb:set-2:source-494:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"VI.B.5\",\"options\":[\"Act\",\"Check/study\",\"Do\",\"Plan\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":494,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":136,\"sourceQuestion\":50,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"At what stage of the PDCA cycle is the process or problem selected for improvement described?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"At the plan stage, the process or problem that is selected for improvement is described, and the improvement goals and objectives are defined. <b>D. Plan</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 50 (print p. 136), VI.B.5.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"VI.B.5","options":["Act","Check/study","Do","Plan"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":494,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":136,"sourceQuestion":50,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"At what stage of the PDCA cycle is the process or problem selected for improvement described?","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"At the plan stage, the process or problem that is selected for improvement is described, and the improvement goals and objectives are defined. <b>D. Plan</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 50 (print p. 136), VI.B.5.</span>"},"revision":"001ee3439ee9038e247e430edeb2fe95faf747910e9072f3b4141e3c432c24f2"},"cssgb:set-2:source-495:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"VI.B.5\",\"options\":[\"“Act” maps to the control phase. Actions include implementing solutions and preventing recurrence.\",\"“Check” maps to the measure phase. Actions include defining the problem and interim containment.\",\"“Do” maps to the define phase. Actions include identifying the problem and using the team approach.\",\"“Plan” maps to the analyze and improve phases. Actions include defining the root cause and developing solutions.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":495,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":137,\"sourceQuestion\":51,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Which option correctly shows the relationship between the phase of PDCA and DMAIC and the corresponding actions?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"The PDCA act stage is mapped to control, prioritizing implementing the solutions and preventing recurrence. <b>A. “Act” maps to the control phase. Actions include implementi