\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Operators can enhance TPM through:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"Preventing, correcting, and measuring deterioration will enhance TPM. <b>C. inspection and collection of data to monitor and measure deterioration.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 55 (print p. 137), VI.C.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"VI.C.1","options":["equipment management and prevention of contamination.","planned and preventive maintenance of equipment.","inspection and collection of data to monitor and measure deterioration.","training and accountability for operating equipment."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":499,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":137,"sourceQuestion":55,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Operators can enhance TPM through:","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"Preventing, correcting, and measuring deterioration will enhance TPM. <b>C. inspection and collection of data to monitor and measure deterioration.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 55 (print p. 137), VI.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"3acf86b79de2ff8b453bcec05c976b03b0b9e0a97faf61372ef239c20a4dd10f"},"cssgb:set-2:source-49:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"II.E.1\",\"options\":[\"DPO\",\"DPU\",\"DPMO\",\"RTY\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":49,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":159,\"sourceQuestion\":49,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"What index is calculated as the total number of defects/deficiencies divided by the total number of products produced?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Defects per unit is DPU = total defects divided by total units produced. <b>B. DPU</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 49 (print p. 159), II.E.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"II.E.1","options":["DPO","DPU","DPMO","RTY"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":49,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":159,"sourceQuestion":49,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"What index is calculated as the total number of defects/deficiencies divided by the total number of products produced?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Defects per unit is DPU = total defects divided by total units produced. <b>B. DPU</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 49 (print p. 159), II.E.1.</span>"},"revision":"3be7c862fed8b1feb4f38842273d0afb70468c13f1eae8be028bb26c251a9522"},"cssgb:set-2:source-4:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"VI.A.3\",\"options\":[\"Tracking the number of surface defects in lots of 10 stainless steel panels\",\"Tracking the number of nonconforming light bulbs in a constant sample size of 300 bulbs\",\"Tracking the fraction of defective printed circuit boards produced each shift when the number of boards produced per shift can vary from 400 to 500, depending on what type of chip is being manufactured\",\"Tracking the average number of flaws per computer produced when random samples of 20–30 computers need to be checked each hour\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":4,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":149,\"sourceQuestion\":4,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"A p-chart would be best to use in which of the following situati