sourcePrintPage":138,"sourceQuestion":58,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Visual factory models support cross-trained personnel and mixed-model schedules by communicating:","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"The visual factory strives to make problems visible, notify employees of current operating conditions, and communicate process goals. <b>A. process goals, operating conditions, and known problems.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 58 (print p. 138), VI.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"429078bc542e94f2d82aeb8a7f38ce142d474198042797fe5e4507288175fc5a"},"cssgb:set-2:source-503:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"VI.C.2\",\"options\":[\"schedule boards.\",\"problems and employee notifications.\",\"maintenance and sustainability.\",\"task performance and inspection methods.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":503,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":138,\"sourceQuestion\":59,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"A single-point lesson relies on visual displays for:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"A single-point lesson shows task performance and inspection of a product or service. <b>D. task performance and inspection methods.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 59 (print p. 138), VI.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"VI.C.2","options":["schedule boards.","problems and employee notifications.","maintenance and sustainability.","task performance and inspection methods."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":503,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":138,"sourceQuestion":59,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"A single-point lesson relies on visual displays for:","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"A single-point lesson shows task performance and inspection of a product or service. <b>D. task performance and inspection methods.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 59 (print p. 138), VI.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"a42420c35b82756591f2f254c9cd82ef0d87f6320a2b2dfd82f3a2690376e0f3"},"cssgb:set-2:source-504:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"VI.C.2\",\"options\":[\"Alarm lights, depleted stock, visual defects, and safety displays\",\"Pictures, colors, and shapes\",\"Written procedures and work instructions\",\"Spreadsheets filled with numbers\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":504,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":139,\"sourceQuestion\":60,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Employees and management must respond to which of these active visual signals in the workplace?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"Visual signals include alarm lights, depleted stock, visual defects, and safety displays that warn against entry into a danger zone without protection. <b>A. Alarm lights, depleted stock, visual defects, and safety displays</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 60 (print p. 139), VI.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"VI.C.2","options":["Alarm lights, depleted stock, visual defects, and safety displays","Pictures, colors, and shapes","Written procedures and work instructions","Spreadsheets filled with numbers"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":504,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":139,"sourceQuestion":60,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Employees and management must respond to which of these active visual signals in the workplace?","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"Visual signals include alarm lights, depleted stock, visual defects, and safety displays that warn against entry into a danger zone without protection. <b>A. Alarm lights, depleted stock, visual defects, and safety displays</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 60 (print p. 139), VI.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"784d672fa97b74b79b55f61c6355f74b6f936475fcfe5514157b284bdaa14046"},"cssgb:set-2:source-505:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"VI.C.2\",\"options\":[\"Reduces the need for direct monitoring but enables responses based on human judgment\",\"Reduces the need for headcount, enabling right-sizing of surplus factory staff\",\"Reduces common cause variation\",\"Increases the need for direct monitoring and multi-process handling\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":505,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":139,\"sourceQuestion\":61,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"Jidoka or autonomation integrates automation with human intelligence to provide which of the following advantages?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"The integration of automation with human intelligence reduces the need for direct monitoring but enables responses based on human judgment. <b>A. Reduces the need for direct monitoring but enables responses based on human judgment</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 61 (print p. 139), VI.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"VI.C.2","options":["Reduces the need for direct monitoring but enables responses based on human judgment","Reduces the need for headcount, enabling right-sizing of surplus factory staff","Reduces common cause variation","Increases the need for direct monitoring and multi-process handling"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":505,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":139,"sourceQuestion":61,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"Jidoka or autonomation integrates automation with human intelligence to provide which of the following advantages?","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"The integration of automation with human intelligence reduces the need for direct monitoring but enables responses based on human judgment. <b>A. Reduces the need for direct monitoring but enables responses based on human judgment</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 61 (print p. 139), VI.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"cf079d1e40ede66af837807b6f570766a805a6c0158808d8402f567596d66437"},"cssgb:set-2:source-506:uwm1ss":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"VI.C.2\",\"options\":[\"just in time.\",\"andon.\",\"kaizen.\",\"predictive maintenance.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":506,\"sourcePart\":\"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK\",\"sourcePrintPage\":139,\"sourceQuestion\":62,\"sourceSection\":\"VI. Control Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book BoK Sample Question\",\"stem\":\"At a grocery store, each checkout aisle has a light indicating whether it is open or closed, or if the cashier is waiting for price information. This is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-control\",\"why\":\"Having visual signals such as lights that show the status of machines or processes is known as andon. <b>B. andon.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 62 (print p. 139), VI.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"VI.C.2","options":["just in time.","andon.","kaizen.","predictive maintenance."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/chapter6.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":506,"sourcePart":"Part A: Sample Questions by BoK","sourcePrintPage":139,"sourceQuestion":62,"sourceSection":"VI. Control Phase","sourceType":"Book BoK Sample Question","stem":"At a grocery store, each checkout aisle has a light indicating whether it is open or closed, or if the cashier is waiting for price information. This is an example of:","sub":"cssgb-control","why":"Having visual signals such as lights that show the status of machines or processes is known as andon. <b>B. andon.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part A VI. Control Phase, Question 62 (print p. 139), VI.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"5393ab936daa56ad39b1512d8fcab23a96f7cac0db3e4e2c49712484d7b82080"},"cssgb:set-2:source-50:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.1\",\"options\":[\"a paired t test.\",\"statistical versus practical significance.\",\"proactive problem-solving.\",\"design of experiments.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":50,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":159,\"sourceQuestion\":50,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"The specifications for the length of a new metal part are 34.0 ± 0.50 cm. Samples of size 1000 are taken on each of two shifts, resulting in mean lengths equal to 34.005 and 34.012 cm, respectively. A statistical test of the difference between mean lengths is significant at p = 0.000. This is an example of:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"The mean difference is statistically detectable because the samples are very large, but 0.007 cm is negligible relative to the ±0.50 cm specification. This distinguishes statistical from practical significance. <b>B. statistical versus practical significance.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 50 (print p. 159), IV.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"IV.B.1","options":["a paired t test.","statistical versus practical significance.","proactive problem-solving.","design of experiments."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":50,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":159,"sourceQuestion":50,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"The specifications for the length of a new metal part are 34.0 ± 0.50 cm. Samples of size 1000 are taken on each of two shifts, resulting in mean lengths equal to 34.005 and 34.012 cm, respectively. A statistical test of the difference between mean lengths is significant at p = 0.000. This is an example of:","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"The mean difference is statistically detectable because the samples are very large, but 0.007 cm is negligible relative to the ±0.50 cm specification. This distinguishes statistical from practical significance. <b>B. statistical versus practical significance.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 50 (print p. 159), IV.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"91a3e98db94e5ba00e05e7125513e273c64cf943f314c7074605bfc2d24cfd5f"},"cssgb:set-2:source-51:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"V.A.1\",\"options\":[\"always contains blocking variables.\",\"tests all combinations of factor levels.\",\"expresses factor levels as percentages that add to 100%.\",\"has two-factor interaction confounded with main effects.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":51,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":159,\"sourceQuestion\":51,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"A full factorial experiment:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"In a full factorial experiment, all possible factor-level combinations are tested. <b>B. tests all combinations of factor levels.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 51 (print p. 159), V.A.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"V.A.1","options":["always contains blocking variables.","tests all combinations of factor levels.","expresses factor levels as percentages that add to 100%.","has two-factor interaction confounded with main effects."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":51,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":159,"sourceQuestion":51,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"A full factorial experiment:","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"In a full factorial experiment, all possible factor-level combinations are tested. <b>B. tests all combinations of factor levels.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 51 (print p. 159), V.A.1.</span>"},"revision":"eb8c515ff1a340cd5b611e4bcb0be3f141576ed1926b0c70604979c0524a7c85"},"cssgb:set-2:source-52:10hzvwm":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"I.B.2\",\"options\":[\"Stakeholders, current state, and action plan\",\"WIP, changeover times, and inventory\",\"Defect locations and error types\",\"Material and worker flow and distances traveled\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":52,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":160,\"sourceQuestion\":52,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"A value stream map (VSM) is a type of detailed process map that shows what types of information?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"A value stream map (VSM) is a detailed process map that shows all the steps in sequence in a value stream. Information gathered and displayed on a VSM can include cycle time, changeover time, number of people, WIP, inventory packaging sizes, scrap rates, value-added versus non-value-added activities, and number of changeovers. <b>B. WIP, changeover times, and inventory</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 52 (print p. 160), I.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"I.B.2","options":["Stakeholders, current state, and action plan","WIP, changeover times, and inventory","Defect locations and error types","Material and worker flow and distances traveled"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":52,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":160,"sourceQuestion":52,"sourceSection":"I. Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"A value stream map (VSM) is a type of detailed process map that shows what types of information?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"A value stream map (VSM) is a detailed process map that shows all the steps in sequence in a value stream. Information gathered and displayed on a VSM can include cycle time, changeover time, number of people, WIP, inventory packaging sizes, scrap rates, value-added versus non-value-added activities, and number of changeovers. <b>B. WIP, changeover times, and inventory</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 52 (print p. 160), I.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"6a7796f50d48043bb582c26dbcac7fa39f445d0ecdee570311cafc2ac2bd10bc"},"cssgb:set-2:source-53:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.D.3\",\"options\":[\"Mean\",\"Median\",\"Mode\",\"Range\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":53,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":160,\"sourceQuestion\":53,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"When referencing descriptive statistics, what term defines the value that is the middle of a sorted list of data?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The median of a sample is the middle value of the sorted data list. By definition, the median is the second quartile, or the 50th percentile. <b>B. Median</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 53 (print p. 160), III.D.3.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.D.3","options":["Mean","Median","Mode","Range"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":53,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":160,"sourceQuestion":53,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"When referencing descriptive statistics, what term defines the value that is the middle of a sorted list of data?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The median of a sample is the middle value of the sorted data list. By definition, the median is the second quartile, or the 50th percentile. <b>B. Median</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 53 (print p. 160), III.D.3.</span>"},"revision":"b2b7681bd34b691f244053a56e048f18f0b0cb6ee8d8a9d9eaebc3ac3643d99c"},"cssgb:set-2:source-54:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"II.B.1\",\"options\":[\"the complexity of technical requirements.\",\"parts planning.\",\"customer satisfaction at an acceptable cost.\",\"the known values of a product.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":54,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":160,\"sourceQuestion\":54,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"QFD is applied to improve:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"The focus of quality function deployment is on customers and their satisfaction. <b>C. customer satisfaction at an acceptable cost.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 54 (print p. 160), II.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.B.1","options":["the complexity of technical requirements.","parts planning.","customer satisfaction at an acceptable cost.","the known values of a product."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":54,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":160,"sourceQuestion":54,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"QFD is applied to improve:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"The focus of quality function deployment is on customers and their satisfaction. <b>C. customer satisfaction at an acceptable cost.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 54 (print p. 160), II.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"662f4b138a0d4aa458aabe412292b4c8739e5fb4ac08d581161e62e73ad2a2c2"},"cssgb:set-2:source-55:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"V.C.2\",\"options\":[\"countermeasure activities.\",\"quick changeover.\",\"the theory of constraints.\",\"mistake-proofing.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":55,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":160,\"sourceQuestion\":55,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"The technique that can significantly reduce the time taken to switch from producing one product to producing another is called:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"Quick changeover, or single minute exchange of dies (SMED), is a technique that can significantly reduce the time taken to switch from producing one product to producing another. <b>B. quick changeover.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 55 (print p. 160), V.C.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"V.C.2","options":["countermeasure activities.","quick changeover.","the theory of constraints.","mistake-proofing."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":55,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":160,"sourceQuestion":55,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"The technique that can significantly reduce the time taken to switch from producing one product to producing another is called:","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"Quick changeover, or single minute exchange of dies (SMED), is a technique that can significantly reduce the time taken to switch from producing one product to producing another. <b>B. quick changeover.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 55 (print p. 160), V.C.2.</span>"},"revision":"4dc174379afcd3de33729aa3eef33bfb3986c604e7bde04202b270ccfa71a6cd"},"cssgb:set-2:source-56:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"IV.A.2\",\"options\":[\"as x decreases, y tends to decrease.\",\"as x decreases, y will be negative.\",\"as x increases, y tends to decrease.\",\"as x increases, y tends to increase.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":56,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":160,\"sourceQuestion\":56,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"The correlation coefficient between two quantitative variables is equal to −0.75. This means that:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"A negative correlation means the variables t