sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"By concentrating solely on speed, the process might be suboptimized if rework then increases. <b>A. Rework increases.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 77 (print p. 165), II.C.4.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"II.C.4","options":["Rework increases.","Throughput increases.","Defects decrease.","Costs decrease."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":77,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":165,"sourceQuestion":77,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"A team succeeds in speeding up a process. What might be an unintended negative consequence?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"By concentrating solely on speed, the process might be suboptimized if rework then increases. <b>A. Rework increases.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 77 (print p. 165), II.C.4.</span>"},"revision":"3be4a404e4bd35d193c055ef267ceb508f0abda93e36abd176507c3177d93059"},"cssgb:set-2:source-78:1up69ca":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"V.C.1\",\"options\":[\"Standard work\",\"Control charts\",\"Process capability\",\"Pull systems\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":78,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":166,\"sourceQuestion\":78,\"sourceSection\":\"V. Improve Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"Which tool is used for waste elimination?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-improve\",\"why\":\"Pull systems are a lean tool used for waste elimination. <b>D. Pull systems</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 78 (print p. 166), V.C.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"V.C.1","options":["Standard work","Control charts","Process capability","Pull systems"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":78,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":166,"sourceQuestion":78,"sourceSection":"V. Improve Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"Which tool is used for waste elimination?","sub":"cssgb-improve","why":"Pull systems are a lean tool used for waste elimination. <b>D. Pull systems</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 78 (print p. 166), V.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"8c1af02b9413ba677de4fadd3d3b5278c55a569ebc983fbddd38da4cb5442670"},"cssgb:set-2:source-79:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.B.1\",\"options\":[\"P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B)\",\"P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B)\",\"P(A and B) = P(A) + P(B)\",\"P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B)\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":79,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":166,\"sourceQuestion\":79,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"What is the formula for the probability of two events occurring when the events are independent?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"For independent events, P(B|A) = P(B), so the multiplication rule becomes P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). <b>B. P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B)</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 79 (print p. 166), III.B.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"bokReference":"III.B.1","options":["P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B)","P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B)","P(A and B) = P(A) + P(B)","P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B)"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":79,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":166,"sourceQuestion":79,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"What is the formula for the probability of two events occurring when the events are independent?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"For independent events, P(B|A) = P(B), so the multiplication rule becomes P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). <b>B. P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B)</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 79 (print p. 166), III.B.1.</span>"},"revision":"3429270483e02860719ba8500d486a327aea2eab8095183ed5c078e278b23a2f"},"cssgb:set-2:source-7:sebhe3":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"IV.B.2\",\"options\":[\"Reject or fail to reject\",\"Reject or accept\",\"Accept\",\"Accept or fail to reject\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":7,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":150,\"sourceQuestion\":7,\"sourceSection\":\"IV. Analyze Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"What action can we take on a null hypothesis?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-analyze\",\"why\":\"A null hypothesis can only be rejected or fail to be rejected; it cannot be accepted because of a lack of evidence to reject it. <b>A. Reject or fail to reject</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 7 (print p. 150), IV.B.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"IV.B.2","options":["Reject or fail to reject","Reject or accept","Accept","Accept or fail to reject"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":7,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":150,"sourceQuestion":7,"sourceSection":"IV. Analyze Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"What action can we take on a null hypothesis?","sub":"cssgb-analyze","why":"A null hypothesis can only be rejected or fail to be rejected; it cannot be accepted because of a lack of evidence to reject it. <b>A. Reject or fail to reject</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 7 (print p. 150), IV.B.2.</span>"},"revision":"0dbcc1ff488920471edf9bc2a8ebdf1a52cf64b1c2b1400924be161542a644c3"},"cssgb:set-2:source-80:10hzvwm":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"I.A.2\",\"options\":[\"They push the team to achieve stretch goals.\",\"They set up five-year project targets for after completion of the DMAIC process.\",\"They are written in the control phase of a project.\",\"They help prevent scope creep across the DMAIC process.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":80,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":166,\"sourceQuestion\":80,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"Which statement describes how SMART goals are used in a Six Sigma project?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"SMART goals can keep the team focused to avoid project scope creep. <b>D. They help prevent scope creep across the DMAIC process.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 80 (print p. 166), I.A.2.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"I.A.2","options":["They push the team to achieve stretch goals.","They set up five-year project targets for after completion of the DMAIC process.","They are written in the control phase of a project.","They help prevent scope creep across the DMAIC process."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":80,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":166,"sourceQuestion":80,"sourceSection":"I. Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"Which statement describes how SMART goals are used in a Six Sigma project?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"SMART goals can keep the team focused to avoid project scope creep. <b>D. They help prevent scope creep across the DMAIC process.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 80 (print p. 166), I.A.2.</span>"},"revision":"34de4598f5479051c12c1de2a4a31a9a3a556080d31e59c34b5b8d748899c4b9"},"cssgb:set-2:source-81:10hzvwm":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"I.C.1\",\"options\":[\"Stated goals\",\"Product potential\",\"Methodologies and measurements\",\"Optimal design\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":81,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":166,\"sourceQuestion\":81,\"sourceSection\":\"I. Six Sigma and the Organization\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"Verification and validation are used to compare results against which of the following factors?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-org\",\"why\":\"The project’s stated goals are what the verification and validation (V&amp;V) should focus on. Verification is assessment to specifications. Validation is demonstrated through actual usage in intended operating environments. <b>A. Stated goals</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 81 (print p. 166), I.C.1.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"I.C.1","options":["Stated goals","Product potential","Methodologies and measurements","Optimal design"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":81,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":166,"sourceQuestion":81,"sourceSection":"I. Six Sigma and the Organization","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"Verification and validation are used to compare results against which of the following factors?","sub":"cssgb-org","why":"The project’s stated goals are what the verification and validation (V&amp;V) should focus on. Verification is assessment to specifications. Validation is demonstrated through actual usage in intended operating environments. <b>A. Stated goals</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 81 (print p. 166), I.C.1.</span>"},"revision":"f5a875efadc669df72779ac982a95f9bb6a453589c4b364d0fb0a11dfcfc18f0"},"cssgb:set-2:source-82:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"bokReference\":\"III.B\",\"options\":[\"Parameter\",\"Statistic\",\"Population\",\"Sample\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":82,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":166,\"sourceQuestion\":82,\"sourceSection\":\"III. Measure Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"What is a numerical value that provides information about the entire system being studied?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-measure\",\"why\":\"The entire system being studied is the population. Populations are described using parameters. Samples are described using statistics. <b>A. Parameter</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 82 (print p. 166), III.B.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"bokReference":"III.B","options":["Parameter","Statistic","Population","Sample"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":82,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":166,"sourceQuestion":82,"sourceSection":"III. Measure Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"What is a numerical value that provides information about the entire system being studied?","sub":"cssgb-measure","why":"The entire system being studied is the population. Populations are described using parameters. Samples are described using statistics. <b>A. Parameter</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 82 (print p. 166), III.B.</span>"},"revision":"2cba3279aaf5478a50f64d202565ce4b9d571079f23cfc4a5c4fd6420dfc9031"},"cssgb:set-2:source-83:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"bokReference\":\"II.A.5\",\"options\":[\"subject matter experts.\",\"process owners.\",\"top management.\",\"stakeholders.\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":83,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":167,\"sourceQuestion\":83,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"Those who have a vested interest in the process and/or its products and outputs are:\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Interested parties is another name for stakeholders. <b>D. stakeholders.</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 83 (print p. 167), II.A.5.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"bokReference":"II.A.5","options":["subject matter experts.","process owners.","top management.","stakeholders."],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":83,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":167,"sourceQuestion":83,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"Those who have a vested interest in the process and/or its products and outputs are:","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Interested parties is another name for stakeholders. <b>D. stakeholders.</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 83 (print p. 167), II.A.5.</span>"},"revision":"ca0642b7f0b3592028e50d689a5594efcc71c6cbbc4301dd4dd0bfa41db0e562"},"cssgb:set-2:source-84:1f5xhgp":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"bokReference\":\"II.D\",\"options\":[\"Rapid collection of large-scale quantitative data\",\"Anonymity of participant responses\",\"In-depth qualitative insights and group dynamics\",\"Automation of data analysis\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":84,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":167,\"sourceQuestion\":84,\"sourceSection\":\"II. Define Phase\",\"sourceType\":\"Book Practice Exam\",\"stem\":\"What is the main advantage of using focus groups to gather customer feedback?\",\"sub\":\"cssgb-define\",\"why\":\"Focus groups excel in providing in-depth qualitative insights by facilitating discussions among participants, allowing exploration of perceptions, opinions, and group dynamics. <b>C. In-depth qualitative insights and group dynamics</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 84 (print p. 167), II.D.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"bokReference":"II.D","options":["Rapid collection of large-scale quantitative data","Anonymity of participant responses","In-depth qualitative insights and group dynamics","Automation of data analysis"],"sourceEpubFile":"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml","sourceGlobalQuestion":84,"sourcePart":"Part B: Practice Exam","sourcePrintPage":167,"sourceQuestion":84,"sourceSection":"II. Define Phase","sourceType":"Book Practice Exam","stem":"What is the main advantage of using focus groups to gather customer feedback?","sub":"cssgb-define","why":"Focus groups excel in providing in-depth qualitative insights by facilitating discussions among participants, allowing exploration of perceptions, opinions, and group dynamics. <b>C. In-depth qualitative insights and group dynamics</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The ASQ Certified Six Sigma Green Belt Study Guide (2024), Part B Practice Exam, Question 84 (print p. 167), II.D.</span>"},"revision":"2b796eca39a388b983bcdb39cdcc517698a65628d519f706e66316dc7aefe132"},"cssgb:set-2:source-85:5m2iwb":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"bokReference\":\"III.E\",\"options\":[\"13.5%\",\"27%\",\"37%\",\"75%\"],\"sourceEpubFile\":\"OEBPS/xhtml/part2.xhtml\",\"sourceGlobalQuestion\":85,\"sourcePart\":\"Part B: Practice Exam\",\"sourcePrintPage\":167,\"sourceQue