me":"V. Coaching and Mentoring Responsibilities","w":9}],"weight":9},{"domain":"mbbanalytics","subs":[{"id":"mbb-analytics","lesson":"/lessons/introduction-to-design-of-experiment-doe","lessonName":"Design of Experiments","name":"VI. Advanced Data Management and Analytic Methods","w":37}],"weight":37}],"fullExamQuestionsBySet":{"1":100,"2":175,"3":175,"mix":100},"gradingPolicyVersion":"single-select-v1","masteryPolicyVersion":"baseline-confidence-v1","minutes":150,"questions":100,"siteTargetBps":7000,"timingPolicyVersion":"site-proportional-v1","title":"Master Black Belt — Exam Simulation"},"configVersion":"2c2b8866712a23e45ac5b2f1053c1bdbeda98ed9503bd7b7f564fe791aef0c79","contents":{"mbb:set-1:source-1":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"A set point, a feedback report, and a project sponsor\",\"A sensor, a specification limit, and a capability index\",\"A controller, a control chart, and a risk register\",\"A sensor/transmitter, a controller, and a final control element\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"D\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":1,\"stem\":\"Which three physical elements form the core of a basic feedback-control loop?\",\"sub\":\"mbb-analytics\",\"why\":\"The sensor or transmitter measures the process, the controller compares and decides, and the final control element changes the process input. <b>D. A sensor/transmitter, a controller, and a final control element</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 1; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["A set point, a feedback report, and a project sponsor","A sensor, a specification limit, and a capability index","A controller, a control chart, and a risk register","A sensor/transmitter, a controller, and a final control element"],"sourceAnswer":"D","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":1,"stem":"Which three physical elements form the core of a basic feedback-control loop?","sub":"mbb-analytics","why":"The sensor or transmitter measures the process, the controller compares and decides, and the final control element changes the process input. <b>D. A sensor/transmitter, a controller, and a final control element</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 1; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"0f091357e3e9d14dab56d9d9e713aafdf237d74eb025ee96ffb673c981ce7808"},"mbb:set-1:source-10":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"When seasonal fluctuations grow in direct proportion to the series level\",\"When seasonal fluctuations have roughly constant absolute magnitude as the series level changes\",\"Only when the series has no trend and no seasonality\",\"Whenever the autocorrelation at lag 1 is exactly zero\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"B\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":10,\"stem\":\"When is an additive decomposition model appropriate for a time series?\",\"sub\":\"mbb-analytics\",\"why\":\"Additive decomposition is appropriate when seasonal effects are expressed in roughly constant units. When seasonal amplitude scales with the level, a multiplicative model is generally more appropriate. <b>B. When seasonal fluctuations have roughly constant absolute magnitude as the series level changes</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 10; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["When seasonal fluctuations grow in direct proportion to the series level","When seasonal fluctuations have roughly constant absolute magnitude as the series level changes","Only when the series has no trend and no seasonality","Whenever the autocorrelation at lag 1 is exactly zero"],"sourceAnswer":"B","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":10,"stem":"When is an additive decomposition model appropriate for a time series?","sub":"mbb-analytics","why":"Additive decomposition is appropriate when seasonal effects are expressed in roughly constant units. When seasonal amplitude scales with the level, a multiplicative model is generally more appropriate. <b>B. When seasonal fluctuations have roughly constant absolute magnitude as the series level changes</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 10; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"fee8e6adf84844c1c93670c978c8bce27cf2e870be3a9f8136f82f961c340798"},"mbb:set-1:source-100":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Inadequate communication\",\"An overly detailed agenda\",\"Too many documented team norms\",\"Excessive use of neutral facilitation\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"A\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":100,\"stem\":\"Across team-failure modes, which foundational weakness most often allows attendance, commitment, and support problems to persist unresolved?\",\"sub\":\"mbb-coaching\",\"why\":\"Communication is the mechanism for surfacing expectations, commitments, conflicts, and support needs; when it fails, the other team problems remain hidden or unresolved. <b>A. Inadequate communication</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 100; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Inadequate communication","An overly detailed agenda","Too many documented team norms","Excessive use of neutral facilitation"],"sourceAnswer":"A","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":100,"stem":"Across team-failure modes, which foundational weakness most often allows attendance, commitment, and support problems to persist unresolved?","sub":"mbb-coaching","why":"Communication is the mechanism for surfacing expectations, commitments, conflicts, and support needs; when it fails, the other team problems remain hidden or unresolved. <b>A. Inadequate communication</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 100; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"5c5d446ac041967d4b0816b17edb83d13fcec3588a5dc0fe9846b8c1664f2c3c"},"mbb:set-1:source-11":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"funding, leadership, customers\",\"people, culture, customers\",\"leadership, people, funding\",\"technology, people, infrastructure\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"D\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":11,\"stem\":\"Three potential barriers to a successful Lean Six Sigma implementation include\",\"sub\":\"mbb-org\",\"why\":\"Technology limitations, human adoption barriers, and inadequate infrastructure can each prevent a deployment from being accepted, supported, or sustained. <b>D. technology, people, infrastructure</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 11; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["funding, leadership, customers","people, culture, customers","leadership, people, funding","technology, people, infrastructure"],"sourceAnswer":"D","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":11,"stem":"Three potential barriers to a successful Lean Six Sigma implementation include","sub":"mbb-org","why":"Technology limitations, human adoption barriers, and inadequate infrastructure can each prevent a deployment from being accepted, supported, or sustained. <b>D. technology, people, infrastructure</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 11; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"c8e7805e80ceb26e38db13428778d2ec3d1af34bf8992316e4ae45e94a3f0c07"},"mbb:set-1:source-12":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Throttle change, rewards and recognition, goals and objectives, communications plan\",\"Modifications to the performance appraisal system, enhanced goals and objectives\",\"Defined scope, communications plan, modifications to the performance appraisal system\",\"Designated change agents, goals and objectives, modifications to the performance appraisal system\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"A\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":12,\"stem\":\"What should be considered when planning for change?\",\"sub\":\"mbb-org\",\"why\":\"Pacing the change, aligning rewards, setting clear objectives, and communicating consistently are complementary controls in a practical change plan. <b>A. Throttle change, rewards and recognition, goals and objectives, communications plan</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 12; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Throttle change, rewards and recognition, goals and objectives, communications plan","Modifications to the performance appraisal system, enhanced goals and objectives","Defined scope, communications plan, modifications to the performance appraisal system","Designated change agents, goals and objectives, modifications to the performance appraisal system"],"sourceAnswer":"A","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":12,"stem":"What should be considered when planning for change?","sub":"mbb-org","why":"Pacing the change, aligning rewards, setting clear objectives, and communicating consistently are complementary controls in a practical change plan. <b>A. Throttle change, rewards and recognition, goals and objectives, communications plan</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 12; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"778249a9f496211e9e169177dac29f0f3734a6d8fe549fd608964c2d4626e8a9"},"mbb:set-1:source-13":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"The perceived probability that effort will produce the required performance\",\"The perceived probability that performance will lead to an outcome\",\"The amount of authority delegated to a self-directed team\",\"The value or attractiveness an individual assigns to a possible outcome or reward\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"D\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":13,\"stem\":\"In expectancy theory, what does valence mean?\",\"sub\":\"mbb-org\",\"why\":\"Valence is the personal value attached to an outcome. Expectancy links effort to performance, while instrumentality links performance to the outcome. <b>D. The value or attractiveness an individual assigns to a possible outcome or reward</b> <span cl