 project sponsor","it adheres to the requirements of the Sarbanes–Oxley Act","it limits the time requirements of functional group member participation"],"sourceAnswer":"A","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":65,"stem":"A project plan and completion date is important because","sub":"mbb-portfolio","why":"Reliable plans and completion dates let portfolio leaders forecast resource demand, sequence dependencies, and compare progress across concurrent projects. <b>A. it facilitates the portfolio management process</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 65; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"3bdbae1f57abdb08a0c9cc5b638512a003ac9bfc1de43e4dfdc18aec15b243d8"},"mbb:set-1:source-66":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"whether conflicts exist with other projects\",\"whether resources have been allocated to move to the next phase\",\"whether there is the right level of engagement from stakeholders\",\"whether to broaden the scope\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"D\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":66,\"stem\":\"One question project sponsors should not ask during tollgate review is\",\"sub\":\"mbb-coaching\",\"why\":\"A tollgate should confirm evidence, resources, stakeholder engagement, and conflicts before advancing. Broadening scope at the gate invites scope creep and should require explicit rechartering. <b>D. whether to broaden the scope</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 66; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["whether conflicts exist with other projects","whether resources have been allocated to move to the next phase","whether there is the right level of engagement from stakeholders","whether to broaden the scope"],"sourceAnswer":"D","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":66,"stem":"One question project sponsors should not ask during tollgate review is","sub":"mbb-coaching","why":"A tollgate should confirm evidence, resources, stakeholder engagement, and conflicts before advancing. Broadening scope at the gate invites scope creep and should require explicit rechartering. <b>D. whether to broaden the scope</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 66; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"eea1658a75327486775abb50cb289264ec240625eaecfc291ae8e1afb4fc4b12"},"mbb:set-1:source-67":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Rely on certification authority and avoid disagreement\",\"Use one standard presentation style regardless of audience\",\"Escalate every difference to the deployment leader\",\"Influence without authority, adapt communication and interaction styles, and handle conflict constructively\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"E\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":67,\"stem\":\"Which capabilities should an MBB use when giving feedback to champions or senior executives?\",\"sub\":\"mbb-coaching\",\"why\":\"Executive coaching requires influence, audience-aware communication, adaptable interaction, and constructive conflict management. <b>D. Influence without authority, adapt communication and interaction styles, and handle conflict constructively</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 67; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Rely on certification authority and avoid disagreement","Use one standard presentation style regardless of audience","Escalate every difference to the deployment leader","Influence without authority, adapt communication and interaction styles, and handle conflict constructively"],"sourceAnswer":"E","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":67,"stem":"Which capabilities should an MBB use when giving feedback to champions or senior executives?","sub":"mbb-coaching","why":"Executive coaching requires influence, audience-aware communication, adaptable interaction, and constructive conflict management. <b>D. Influence without authority, adapt communication and interaction styles, and handle conflict constructively</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 67; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"45bf3e56875282c7abdac971ba6a15e45acb13d98dd04b8a23ba838748733625"},"mbb:set-1:source-68":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Forming\",\"Storming\",\"Performing\",\"Learning\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"D\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":68,\"stem\":\"Which item is not a recognized stage in Tuckman’s team-development model?\",\"sub\":\"mbb-coaching\",\"why\":\"Tuckman’s model includes forming, storming, norming, performing, and later adjourning. Learning is not a named stage. <b>D. Learning</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 68; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Forming","Storming","Performing","Learning"],"sourceAnswer":"D","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":68,"stem":"Which item is not a recognized stage in Tuckman’s team-development model?","sub":"mbb-coaching","why":"Tuckman’s model includes forming, storming, norming, performing, and later adjourning. Learning is not a named stage. <b>D. Learning</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 68; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"2730401a205bfb3b19cb4ccc65a3d0abf083a4fa1566df24f963131c99cdafce"},"mbb:set-1:source-69":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"provide visibility of the mentee to the remainder of the organization\",\"eliminate organizational obstacles to career progression\",\"provide guidance, wisdom, and a possible road map to career advancement\",\"enhance the existing skills and capabilities of the mentee\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"C\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":69,\"stem\":\"The role of the mentor is to\",\"sub\":\"mbb-coaching\",\"why\":\"Mentoring is a longer-term developmental relationship that shares judgment, guidance, and career perspective; coaching is typically more immediate and performance-specific. <b>C. provide guidance, wisdom, and a possible road map to career advancement</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 69; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["provide visibility of the mentee to the remainder of the organization","eliminate organizational obstacles to career progression","provide guidance, wisdom, and a possible road map to career advancement","enhance the existing skills and capabilities of the mentee"],"sourceAnswer":"C","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":69,"stem":"The role of the mentor is to","sub":"mbb-coaching","why":"Mentoring is a longer-term developmental relationship that shares judgment, guidance, and career perspective; coaching is typically more immediate and performance-specific. <b>C. provide guidance, wisdom, and a possible road map to career advancement</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 69; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"1c61ddea8e3b89754bc78622a41939a44485fdaf3614cb68b2b5a2bf8164bce2"},"mbb:set-1:source-7":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"DMAIC takes too long to complete\",\"the engineering development organization is resistant\",\"the cost of DMAIC exceeds the cost of project management\",\"the solution to the problem is known\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"D\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":7,\"stem\":\"In the general sense, project management is used when\",\"sub\":\"mbb-portfolio\",\"why\":\"General project management is appropriate when the desired deliverable or solution path is sufficiently known; DMAIC is designed for performance problems whose causes and solution are not yet known. <b>D. the solution to the problem is known</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 7; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["DMAIC takes too long to complete","the engineering development organization is resistant","the cost of DMAIC exceeds the cost of project management","the solution to the problem is known"],"sourceAnswer":"D","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":7,"stem":"In the general sense, project management is used when","sub":"mbb-portfolio","why":"General project management is appropriate when the desired deliverable or solution path is sufficiently known; DMAIC is designed for performance problems whose causes and solution are not yet known. <b>D. the solution to the problem is known</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 7; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"f955b9fe9a507a70b0971c5125449f8b5e5d9d44e6be0898219d37468688e414"},"mbb:set-1:source-70":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"the project leader should call in a professional facilitator\",\"the team coach should intervene\",\"the team should stop all action and immediately begin a force field analysis on the problem\",\"the Lean Six Sigma deployment leader should consider replacing the Black Belt or project leader with a stronger, more experienced leader\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"B\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":70,\"stem\":\"If team failure is imminent,\",\"sub\":\"mbb-coaching\",\"why\":\"When team failure is imminent, the coach should intervene promptly to diagnose the failure mode, restore productive norms, and escalate only when the team cannot recover safely. <b>B. the team coach should intervene</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 70; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["the project leader should call in a professional facilitator","the team coach should intervene","the team should stop all action and immediately begin a force field analysis on the problem","the Lean Six Sigma deployment leader should consider replacing the Black Belt or project leader with a stronger, more experienced leader"],"sourceAnswer":"B","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":70,"stem":"If team failure is imminent,","sub":"mbb-coaching","why":"When team failure is imminent, the coach should intervene promptly to diagnose the failure mode, restore productive norms, and escalate only when the team cannot recover safely. <b>B. the team coach should intervene</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 70; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"52e5e4de42ee689377cc0ec634ae23a100fec370026485270f72e7b828b250d5"},"mbb:set-1:source-71":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"executives become more active, visible, engaged, communicative, and knowledgeable\",\"employee demoralization and frustration and the eventual demise of the Lean Six Sigma initiative\",\"executives become more enthusiastic and begin to take on additional projects even though they may be at the Green Belt level\",\"employees see their executives as committed nonetheless and rally their support\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"B\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":71,\"stem\":\"When organizations allow executives to achieve belts with less effort than others in the organization, the following effect can occur:\",\"sub\":\"mbb-org\",\"why\":\"A lower certification standard for executives undermines procedural fairness and leadership credibility, which can demoralize employees and weaken the entire deployment. <b>B. employee demoralization and frustration and the eventual demise of the Lean Six Sigma initiative</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 71; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["executives become more active, visible, engaged, communicative, and knowledgeable","employee demoralization and frustration and the eventual demise of the Lean Six Sigma initiative","executives become more enthusiastic and begin to take on additional projects even though they may be at the Green Belt level","employees see their executives as committed nonetheless and rally their support"],"sourceAnswer":"B","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":71,"stem":"When organizations allow executives to achieve belts with less effort than others in the organization, the following effect can occur:","sub":"mbb-org","why":"A lower certification standard for executives undermines procedural fairness and leadership credibility, which can demoralize employees and weaken the entire deployment. <b>B. employee demoralization and frustration and the eventual demise of the Lean Six Sigma initiative</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 71; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"62167a6b070a9327f9f861ff0244de86e30593003320e0b007cd0e625f18548b"},"mbb:set-1:source-72":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"the cumulative stack-up effect tolerance intervals have on the component confidence interval\",\"the inherent limitations suppliers have when establishing original component specifications\",\"the psychological tolerance limit organizations face when confronted with an excessive amount of change in too short a period of time\",\"the cumulative stack-up effect subcomponent specifications have on the total component specifications\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"D\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":72,\"stem\":\"In tolerance stack-up analysis, the concept of propagation of errors addresses\",\"sub\":\"mbb-analytics\",\"why\":\"Propagation of error quantifies how uncertainty in component dimensions or inputs combines to affect the variation or tolerance of the assembled output. <b>D. the cumulative stack-up effect subcomponent specifications have on the total component specifications</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 72; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["the cumulative stack-up effect tolerance intervals have on the component confidence interval","the inherent limitations suppliers have when establishing original component specifications","the psychological tolerance limit organizations face when confronted with an excessive amount of change in too short a period of time","the cumulative stack-up effect subcomponent specifications have on the total component specifications"],"sourceAnswer":"D","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":72,"stem":"In tolerance stack-up analysis, the concept of propagation of errors addresses","sub":"mbb-analytics","why":"Propagation of error quantifies how uncertainty in component dimensions or inputs combines to affect the variation or tolerance of the assembled output. <b>D. the cumulative stack-up effect subcomponent specifications have on the total component specifications</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 72; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"e9dc1794c1f9a64809e6c715b07e9639442b8e736eeec04e6e87dde6601a8447"},"mbb:set-1:source-73":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"Transformational\",\"Autocratic\",\"Directive coaching\",\"Transactional\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"D\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":73,\"stem\":\"A leader clarifies tasks and responsibilities, supplies needed resources, and links performance to agreed rewards or corrective action. Which leadership style is this?\",\"sub\":\"mbb-org\",\"why\":\"Transactional leadership manages performance through clear roles, resources, monitoring, and explicit exchanges of rewards or corrective action. <b>D. Transactional</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 73; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["Transformational","Autocratic","Directive coaching","Transactional"],"sourceAnswer":"D","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":73,"stem":"A leader clarifies tasks and responsibilities, supplies needed resources, and links performance to agreed rewards or corrective action. Which leadership style is this?","sub":"mbb-org","why":"Transactional leadership manages performance through clear roles, resources, monitoring, and explicit exchanges of rewards or corrective action. <b>D. Transactional</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 73; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"b76a620d2e1d416d0bcc0c134efc4408cf207cfa4b64d9681b77aa83078c2c16"},"mbb:set-1:source-74":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"Cp, Cpk, and a variables gage R&R study only\",\"A continuous-data control chart and process capability indices only\",\"Percent agreement, kappa, Kendall-type concordance, and intraclass correlation for ordered ratings\",\"Ordinary least squares with no rater or item effects\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"C\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":74,\"stem\":\"Which set contains methods that can be appropriate for an attribute or discrete measurement-system study, depending on the scale and design?\",\"sub\":\"mbb-analytics\",\"why\":\"Current ASQ MBB guidance includes percent agreement, kappa, Kendall, and ICC among methods for discrete measurement systems. The scale, raters, items, and intended agreement definition determine the appropriate statistic. <b>C. Percent agreement, kappa, Kendall-type concordance, and intraclass correlation for ordered ratings</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 74; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["Cp, Cpk, and a variables gage R&R study only","A continuous-data control chart and process capability indices only","Percent agreement, kappa, Kendall-type concordance, and intraclass correlation for ordered ratings","Ordinary least squares with no rater or item effects"],"sourceAnswer":"C","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":74,"stem":"Which set contains methods that can be appropriate for an attribute or discrete measurement-system study, depending on the scale and design?","sub":"mbb-analytics","why":"Current ASQ MBB guidance includes percent agreement, kappa, Kendall, and ICC among methods for discrete measurement systems. The scale, raters, items, and intended agreement definition determine the appropriate statistic. <b>C. Percent agreement, kappa, Kendall-type concordance, and intraclass correlation for ordered ratings</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 74; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"cdba8572963b157d6413c5926ef21a785aecd9854b32b901598ab844964ca0d6"},"mbb:set-1:source-75":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"Accept as-is because the overall kappa equals 0.80\",\"Require application-specific review because at least one category is between 0.60 and 0.80\",\"Reject and redesign because at least one category is below 0.80\",\"No decision can be made because category kappas must all equal the overall kappa\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"B\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":75,\"stem\":\"A team classifies an attribute measurement system using this rule: accept as-is only if every category kappa is at least 0.80; reject and redesign if any category kappa is below 0.60; otherwise require application-specific review. With Koverall = 0.80 and category kappas 0.74, 0.78, and 0.86, what is the decision?\",\"sub\":\"mbb-analytics\",\"why\":\"The overall value does not override category performance. The lowest category kappa is 0.74: it is not below the rejection threshold, but it fails the all-categories acceptance threshold, so the stated rule requires application-specific review. <b>B. Require application-specific review because at least one category is between 0.60 and 0.80</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 75; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["Accept as-is because the overall kappa equals 0.80","Require application-specific review because at least one category is between 0.60 and 0.80","Reject and redesign because at least one category is below 0.80","No decision can be made because category kappas must all equal the overall kappa"],"sourceAnswer":"B","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":75,"stem":"A team classifies an attribute measurement system using this rule: accept as-is only if every category kappa is at least 0.80; reject and redesign if any category kappa is below 0.60; otherwise require application-specific review. With Koverall = 0.80 and category kappas 0.74, 0.78, and 0.86, what is the decision?","sub":"mbb-analytics","why":"The overall value does not override category performance. The lowest category kappa is 0.74: it is not below the rejection threshold, but it fails the all-categories acceptance threshold, so the stated rule requires application-specific review. <b>B. Require application-specific review because at least one category is between 0.60 and 0.80</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 75; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"831ab1e11a5ea860c59063842dec982fa2b833d427984bc3a3f8d62030865409"},"mbb:set-1:source-76":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"chart\":{\"columns\":[\"Assumption\",\"Tool 1\",\"Tool 2\"],\"rows\":[[\"Units\",\"Independent\",\"Independent\"],[\"Classification scale\",\"Nominal categories\",\"Ordered, equally spaced ratings\"],[\"Category use\",\"Some categories may occur more often\",\"Ranges such as −2, −1, 0, 1, 2\"],[\"Raters\",\"Classify independently\",\"Classify independently\"],[\"Categories\",\"Mutually exclusive and exhaustive\",\"Mutually exclusive and exhaustive\"],[\"Decision impact\",\"Misclassification distance not specified\",\"Misclassification consequences matter\"]],\"type\":\"data-table\"},\"options\":[\"Tool 1 = kappa; Tool 2 = intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC)\",\"Tool 1 = ICC; Tool 2 = percent agreement\",\"Tool 1 = ICC; Tool 2 = kappa\",\"Tool 1 = percent agreement; Tool 2 = variables gage R&R\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"A\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":76,\"stem\":\"Using the assumptions table below, which analysis is the best match for Tool 1 and Tool 2?\",\"sub\":\"mbb-analytics\",\"why\":\"Tool 1 describes nominal classifications by independent raters, which calls for kappa. Tool 2 describes ordered, equally spaced ratings where misclassification distance matters, which supports an ICC analysis. <b>A. Tool 1 = kappa; Tool 2 = intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC)</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 76; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"chart":{"columns":["Assumption","Tool 1","Tool 2"],"rows":[["Units","Independent","Independent"],["Classification scale","Nominal categories","Ordered, equally spaced ratings"],["Category use","Some categories may occur more often","Ranges such as −2, −1, 0, 1, 2"],["Raters","Classify independently","Classify independently"],["Categories","Mutually exclusive and exhaustive","Mutually exclusive and exhaustive"],["Decision impact","Misclassification distance not specified","Misclassification consequences matter"]],"type":"data-table"},"options":["Tool 1 = kappa; Tool 2 = intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC)","Tool 1 = ICC; Tool 2 = percent agreement","Tool 1 = ICC; Tool 2 = kappa","Tool 1 = percent agreement; Tool 2 = variables gage R&R"],"sourceAnswer":"A","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":76,"stem":"Using the assumptions table below, which analysis is the best match for Tool 1 and Tool 2?","sub":"mbb-analytics","why":"Tool 1 describes nominal classifications by independent raters, which calls for kappa. Tool 2 describes ordered, equally spaced ratings where misclassification distance matters, which supports an ICC analysis. <b>A. Tool 1 = kappa; Tool 2 = intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC)</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 76; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"f590011825a71955ef1e34979e059c9b68bd1656cbccee7767475bf9c72b2a33"},"mbb:set-1:source-77":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Investigate and improve the measurement system\",\"Accept the measurement system without further review\",\"Reject it as automatically unusable because every ICC is below 0.75\",\"Average the ICCs and ignore the individual estimates\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"A\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":77,\"stem\":\"A team uses this screening rule for six ICC estimates: all values at or above 0.75 are acceptable; any value from 0.40 to below 0.75 requires investigation and improvement; a value below 0.40 is unacceptable. The ICCs are 0.51, 0.62, 0.49, 0.64, 0.55, and 0.60. What is the decision?\",\"sub\":\"mbb-analytics\",\"why\":\"Every reported ICC lies between 0.40 and 0.75, so the supplied decision rule places the system in the investigate-and-improve band. The rule—not an unstated universal threshold—determines the answer. <b>A. Investigate and improve the measurement system</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 77; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Investigate and improve the measurement system","Accept the measurement system without further review","Reject it as automatically unusable because every ICC is below 0.75","Average the ICCs and ignore the individual estimates"],"sourceAnswer":"A","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":77,"stem":"A team uses this screening rule for six ICC estimates: all values at or above 0.75 are acceptable; any value from 0.40 to below 0.75 requires investigation and improvement; a value below 0.40 is unacceptable. The ICCs are 0.51, 0.62, 0.49, 0.64, 0.55, and 0.60. What is the decision?","sub":"mbb-analytics","why":"Every reported ICC lies between 0.40 and 0.75, so the supplied decision rule places the system in the investigate-and-improve band. The rule—not an unstated universal threshold—determines the answer. <b>A. Investigate and improve the measurement system</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 77; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"da595e4ce32338190bbb11c2eb8aa87dd24521f5b012b0c37d1cadd7bb3cb0e8"},"mbb:set-1:source-78":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"Assess the current level of institutional capability and guide progression toward more mature, sustained practices\",\"Calculate the short-term Cp and Cpk of one production characteristic\",\"Replace strategy deployment with a certification count\",\"Prove that every business unit must use the same intervention sequence\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"A\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":78,\"stem\":\"What is the primary purpose of an organizational maturity model in an improvement deployment?\",\"sub\":\"mbb-org\",\"why\":\"A maturity model provides a staged view of institutional capability, helping leaders assess current state, identify gaps, and plan progression toward sustained transformation. <b>A. Assess the current level of institutional capability and guide progression toward more mature, sustained practices</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 78; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["Assess the current level of institutional capability and guide progression toward more mature, sustained practices","Calculate the short-term Cp and Cpk of one production characteristic","Replace strategy deployment with a certification count","Prove that every business unit must use the same intervention sequence"],"sourceAnswer":"A","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":78,"stem":"What is the primary purpose of an organizational maturity model in an improvement deployment?","sub":"mbb-org","why":"A maturity model provides a staged view of institutional capability, helping leaders assess current state, identify gaps, and plan progression toward sustained transformation. <b>A. Assess the current level of institutional capability and guide progression toward more mature, sustained practices</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 78; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"19985ee5e271adcafbb4dcdafe1c4f6d002e9f1d9278a2d3674f8569fff6ebfb"},"mbb:set-1:source-79":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"ACF, MRDR, LDR, MAPE\",\"MAD, ACF\",\"MSD, MAPE, ACF\",\"MAD, MSD, MAPE\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"D\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":79,\"stem\":\"Measures of the accuracy of a fitted time series include\",\"sub\":\"mbb-analytics\",\"why\":\"MAD summarizes absolute errors, MSD summarizes squared errors, and MAPE expresses absolute error relative to actual values; all three assess fitted or forecast accuracy. <b>D. MAD, MSD, MAPE</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 79; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":3,"options":["ACF, MRDR, LDR, MAPE","MAD, ACF","MSD, MAPE, ACF","MAD, MSD, MAPE"],"sourceAnswer":"D","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":79,"stem":"Measures of the accuracy of a fitted time series include","sub":"mbb-analytics","why":"MAD summarizes absolute errors, MSD summarizes squared errors, and MAPE expresses absolute error relative to actual values; all three assess fitted or forecast accuracy. <b>D. MAD, MSD, MAPE</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 79; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"42317e2335ca83947c6be690ae2b163e9418751c1361ee584d415dfc78be5699"},"mbb:set-1:source-8":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"competitive pressures and market volatility undermine risk mitigation plans\",\"something did or did not occur\",\"the expected value computation resulted in an unacceptably high dollar value\",\"competitive pressure and market dynamics fell into the lower right quadrant of the Kano model\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"B\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":8,\"stem\":\"Risk is the probability of not achieving a project goal, schedule, budget, or resource target because\",\"sub\":\"mbb-portfolio\",\"why\":\"Risk arises from uncertain events or conditions—something may happen or fail to happen—and their consequences for objectives such as scope, time, cost, or resources. <b>B. something did or did not occur</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 8; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["competitive pressures and market volatility undermine risk mitigation plans","something did or did not occur","the expected value computation resulted in an unacceptably high dollar value","competitive pressure and market dynamics fell into the lower right quadrant of the Kano model"],"sourceAnswer":"B","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":8,"stem":"Risk is the probability of not achieving a project goal, schedule, budget, or resource target because","sub":"mbb-portfolio","why":"Risk arises from uncertain events or conditions—something may happen or fail to happen—and their consequences for objectives such as scope, time, cost, or resources. <b>B. something did or did not occur</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 8; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"c9d5949a99eb806456d3079a016a13f41a733be36f43496883504bd6b05f7264"},"mbb:set-1:source-80":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":2,\"options\":[\"when the ACF of the time-series data is zero at regular periodic intervals\",\"in the area of the curve where the Ljung–Box statistic exceeds the chi-square statistics\",\"when the mean, variance, and autocorrelation structure are constant over the entire range of the data\",\"only at the zero points of the fitted line\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"C\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":80,\"stem\":\"Time-series data are assumed to be stationary. Stationarity occurs\",\"sub\":\"mbb-analytics\",\"why\":\"Weak stationarity requires a constant mean and variance and an autocovariance structure that depends on lag rather than calendar time. <b>C. when the mean, variance, and autocorrelation structure are constant over the entire range of the data</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 80; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":2,"options":["when the ACF of the time-series data is zero at regular periodic intervals","in the area of the curve where the Ljung–Box statistic exceeds the chi-square statistics","when the mean, variance, and autocorrelation structure are constant over the entire range of the data","only at the zero points of the fitted line"],"sourceAnswer":"C","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":80,"stem":"Time-series data are assumed to be stationary. Stationarity occurs","sub":"mbb-analytics","why":"Weak stationarity requires a constant mean and variance and an autocovariance structure that depends on lag rather than calendar time. <b>C. when the mean, variance, and autocorrelation structure are constant over the entire range of the data</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 80; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"2b8f7df74b1efae97bfbb0768d7ce10c06ed1a183d27325476344b29129d5478"},"mbb:set-1:source-81":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"options\":[\"It supplies scope, schedule, resource, risk, communication, and governance controls around the technical improvement work\",\"It replaces DMAIC whenever the root cause is unknown\",\"It guarantees every phase will finish in the same amount of time\",\"It removes the need for a sponsor once the charter is approved\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"A\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":81,\"stem\":\"Why is project-management discipline important when applying DMAIC?\",\"sub\":\"mbb-portfolio\",\"why\":\"DMAIC provides the improvement logic; project management controls the work needed to deliver it through planning, coordination, risk management, reporting, and governance. <b>A. It supplies scope, schedule, resource, risk, communication, and governance controls around the technical improvement work</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 81; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"options":["It supplies scope, schedule, resource, risk, communication, and governance controls around the technical improvement work","It replaces DMAIC whenever the root cause is unknown","It guarantees every phase will finish in the same amount of time","It removes the need for a sponsor once the charter is approved"],"sourceAnswer":"A","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":81,"stem":"Why is project-management discipline important when applying DMAIC?","sub":"mbb-portfolio","why":"DMAIC provides the improvement logic; project management controls the work needed to deliver it through planning, coordination, risk management, reporting, and governance. <b>A. It supplies scope, schedule, resource, risk, communication, and governance controls around the technical improvement work</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 81; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"ded25c773bddc6b2f91b78988839da1e2ad299da5fe9a4c5336a8add98157a31"},"mbb:set-1:source-82":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":1,\"options\":[\"A variance-stabilizing transformation only\",\"Smoothing the series\",\"Standardizing every value independently\",\"Deleting every large residual\"],\"sourceAnswer\":\"B\",\"sourceAssessment\":\"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook\",\"sourceQuestion\":82,\"stem\":\"Which technique can make the underlying pattern in a noisy time series easier to see?\",\"sub\":\"mbb-analytics\",\"why\":\"Smoothing reduces short-term noise so level, trend, or seasonal structure is easier to see. It does not justify deleting special causes or outliers without investigation. <b>B. Smoothing the series</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 82; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":1,"options":["A variance-stabilizing transformation only","Smoothing the series","Standardizing every value independently","Deleting every large residual"],"sourceAnswer":"B","sourceAssessment":"Simulated Examination Questions for Parts I–VI","sourceDocument":"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook","sourceQuestion":82,"stem":"Which technique can make the underlying pattern in a noisy time series easier to see?","sub":"mbb-analytics","why":"Smoothing reduces short-term noise so level, trend, or seasonal structure is easier to see. It does not justify deleting special causes or outliers without investigation. <b>B. Smoothing the series</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Source: The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook, simulated examination, Question 82; normalized for four-choice delivery.</span>"},"revision":"afbcabf810413a12e1545c105afa96d0e9756b3d0037bb4c47a318b96a154af4"},"mbb:set-1:source-83":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":3,\"options\":[\"A binary response with a logit link and binomial variance\",\"Count responses whose variance must equal the mean\",\"Correlated residuals with an unspecified nonlinear mean function\",\"A line