ues for the remaining scope, which diagnosis and estimate are most defensible? Round indices to two decimals and EAC to the nearest $0.01M only at the end.","sub":"mbb-portfolio","trap":"Do not swap CPI and SPI or round CPI before forecasting. A revised forecast is not automatic permission to reset the baseline.","visual":{"altText":"An earned-value table shows BAC 1.2 million dollars, PV 600 thousand, EV 480 thousand, and AC 550 thousand.","answerCueAudit":true,"breakpointsValidated":[],"datasetRef":"test-bank-assets/mbb-160/batch-07/datasets.json#mbb:set-2:original-161","interactionPurpose":"","specRef":"test-bank-assets/mbb-160/batch-07/visual-specs.json#mbb:set-2:original-161","staticAssetRef":"test-bank-assets/mbb-160/batch-07/static-fallbacks.html#mbb-set-2-original-161","type":"data-table","validationRef":"test-bank-assets/mbb-160/batch-07/validation.json#mbb:set-2:original-161"},"why":"CPI=EV/AC=480/550=0.872727… and SPI=EV/PV=480/600=0.80. Using the unrounded CPI, EAC=BAC/CPI=$1.20M×550/480=$1.375M, or $1.38M at the requested precision. Cost variance is −$70,000 and schedule variance is −$120,000 in earned-value units. Update the forecast and investigate recovery options; an adverse forecast is not authority to erase the approved baseline. <b>B. CPI=0.87 and SPI=0.80; forecast about $1.38M and require recovery analysis before rebaselining</b> <span class=\"tb-source-ref\">Concept reference: Kubiak (2012), Chapter 14 - Project Oversight, pp. 217–218.</span>"},"revision":"445f1a736c6b4b722cad87b5e388e1183adac57167db90d2d2369583395ee05d"},"mbb:set-2:original-162":{"canonicalContent":"{\"answer\":0,\"assumptions\":[\"The 70% already spent cannot be recovered by continuing or stopping.\",\"The regulatory change is a supplied scenario condition, not legal advice about a real jurisdiction.\"],\"batch\":7,\"bok\":{\"code\":\"III.B.4\",\"domain\":\"III. Project Portfolio Management\",\"subdomain\":\"B. Project Portfolio Infrastructure and Management\",\"topic\":\"Evidence-based project termination\"},\"cognitive\":\"Evaluate\",\"difficulty\":\"Very Hard\",\"distractors\":[\"Correct. Forward consequences determine the choice while irrecoverable historical spending is excluded.\",\"Percent spent does not override a material change in the future value of the project.\",\"Holding scarce resources indefinitely needs a justified option value rather than an assumption.\",\"Relabeling eliminated benefits does not create an actual financial result.\"],\"estimatedMinutes\":3,\"formula\":null,\"industry\":\"Finance and insurance\",\"keywords\":[\"project termination\",\"sunk cost\",\"opportunity cost\",\"incremental value\",\"portfolio governance\"],\"optionRationales\":[\"Correct. Forward consequences determine the choice while irrecoverable historical spending is excluded.\",\"Percent spent does not override a material change in the future value of the project.\",\"Holding scarce resources indefinitely needs a justified option value rather than an assumption.\",\"Relabeling eliminated benefits does not create an actual financial result.\"],\"options\":[\"Compare future incremental value, costs, risks and opportunity costs, including closure obligations\",\"Continue because passing the budget midpoint protects a project from new evidence\",\"Suspend indefinitely while reserving all specialists without valuing that option\",\"Finish the old scope and relabel eliminated benefits as realized cost avoidance\"],\"quantitative\":false,\"questionType\":\"Portfolio, finance, and risk scenario\",\"sourceDocument\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook (Kubiak, 2012)\",\"sourcePages\":\"223–224\",\"sourceSection\":\"Chapter 15 - Project Management Infrastructure\",\"sources\":[{\"chapter\":\"Chapter 15 - Project Management Infrastructure\",\"document\":\"The Certified Six Sigma Master Black Belt Handbook (Kubiak, 2012)\",\"id\":\"S1\",\"pages\":\"223–224\",\"section\":\"Evidence-based project termination\",\"verificationScope\":\"Concept/topic locator. Case-specific reasoning is audit analysis; not every page of an inherited range has been independently verified.\"}],\"stem\":\"A project has spent 70% of its budget on irrecoverable work. A stipulated regulatory change eliminates most expected benefit, but the sponsor says stopping would waste the investment. What should govern the decision?\",\"sub\":\"mbb-portfolio\",\"trap\":\"Irrecoverable past spending is sunk, but future shutdown costs, recoveries and obligations still matter.\",\"why\":\"A uses the consequences that differ between continuing, modifying and stopping. The already spent, explicitly irrecoverable amount is sunk. Future termination costs, contractual obligations, recoveries and alternative uses of resources still belong in the comparison. The question does not supply enough future economics to declare termination automatically correct; it identifies the appropriate decision basis. <b>A. Compare future incremental value, costs, risks and opportunity costs, including closure obligations</b> <span class=\\\"tb-source-ref\\\">Concept reference: Kubiak (2012), Chapter 15 - Project Management Infrastructure, pp. 223–224.</span>\"}","content":{"answer":0,"assumptions":["The 70% already spent cannot be recovered by continuing or stopping.","The regulatory change is a supplied scenario condition, not legal advice about a real jurisdiction."],"batch":7,"bok":{"code":"III.B.4","domain":"III. Project Portfolio Management","subdomain":"B. Project Portfolio Infrastructure and Management","topic":"Evidence-based project termination"},"cognitive":"Evaluate","difficulty":"Very Hard","distractors":["Correct. Forward consequences determine the choice while irrecoverable historical spending is excluded.","Percent spent does not override a material change in the future value of the project.","Holding scarce resources indefinitely needs a justified option value rather than an assumption.","Relabeling eliminated benefits does not create an actual 